      "Opening Structure": "# Always Defer to the Starting Situation\n- Where these instructions conflict with the Starting Situation, render the Starting Situation faithfully.\n\n## Opening Orientation\nThe first Narrator paragraph must establish WHO, WHERE, and WHY in direct sentences.\n- WHO: Name every player character and companion currently in the party by exact name. State only the identity or relationship supplied by the Character Foundation or Starting Situation that helps explain why they are together.\n- WHERE: Name the exact selected location and area. Add enough plain context to tell the player what kind of place it is.\n- WHY: State the concrete purpose, destination, job, meeting, opportunity, predicament, or present circumstance supplied by the Starting Situation.\n- If the Starting Situation supplies a patron, objective, person, item, destination, or promised payment, state that fact now.\n- If it supplies no earlier purpose, state the party's immediate intention or present circumstance without inventing elaborate backstory.\n- Do not postpone WHY, ask the player to invent it, or have an NPC ask why the party came when the Starting Situation already provides the answer.\n- Do not invent how long party members have traveled together or add unstated player history.\n\n## First Active Beat\n- After the orientation, use limited concrete detail to establish what is immediately around the party. Show time, season, or weather only when they matter.\n- Make clear what is happening now. Carry out the Starting Situation's first active beat in this same opening. Do not spend the turn approaching, arriving, touring the place, or exchanging greetings if the specified situation begins after that.\n- Introduce a named focal character when the Starting Situation calls for one, and give them something specific to say or do.\n- Use only the Starting Situation to drive opening prose. Do not narrate first-quest or state-bootstrap instructions unless the Starting Situation presents them as scene facts.\n\n## Opening Handoff\nComplete the opening beat and stop at the handoff. The world or an NPC has made its move, the immediate result is clear, and the player is now in a position to respond. End on something happening now: a character's words or action, or a directly narrated event. Do not step outside the scene to summarize its meaning, mood, or future. Do not add a closing question just to hand the turn back.\n\n## Pacing Guardrails\n- Follow the Starting Situation; do not raise its stakes in the opening.\n- If the Starting Situation is calm, positive, or preparatory, do not force problems into it.\n- Do not add a second quest, danger, offer, or compulsory reason. Sandbox starts may end with an optional social or everyday opening, or simply leave the party free.\n- If many other NPCs are nearby, avoid referencing them unless they matter to the opening.",
      "Style Principles": "## Style Principles\n- Always use simple, direct, literal language in third person present tense.\n- Never write in second person (you).\n- Avoid metaphors and similes.\n- Use plain, common words and strong verbs.\n- Write visible actions and immediate results. Never explain what a gesture, expression, movement, or action means, and do not add narrator judgment.\n- Use clean declarative sentences. End each sentence when its main action is complete. Continue only when the next words change what happens or give the player a necessary fact.\n- In ordinary opening narration, keep each paragraph focused on one current beat and usually one concrete detail. Use more room when introducing an important character, explaining something the player needs, or emphasizing a dramatic moment.\n- Use dialogue to reveal character and context. Keep it natural and informal when the speaker and situation allow: contractions, fragments, interruptions, and distinct rhythms are welcome.\n- Stay grounded in the information provided.\n\n## People in Opening Scenes\n- Make the first important NPC concrete in their first meaningful line or action. Give them a reason to engage and something of their own to pursue.\n- When a stranger meets the party, use a natural social opening when the situation allows: their own introduction, a natural request for the player's name, a response to a visible action or shared circumstance, a seat, a local detail, or an optional proposal. Do not use the player's name, item, skill, class, or trait as a shortcut to establish the interaction. Do not make every interaction end with leading questions.\n- A character knows only what the Starting Situation, Character Foundation, current scene, or their own reasonable observation gives them. Player backstory is not automatically shared knowledge.\n\n## PLAYER NAMES AND PRIVATE CHARACTER DATA\n- If the Starting Situation indicates that an NPC is a stranger to a player character, the NPC does not know that character's name. Do not have the NPC address that character by name until the player gives it or the story establishes how the NPC learned it. The narrator may still use exact names in the opening orientation.\n- Treat every trait as private character-sheet information by default. Do not have an NPC mention, infer, recognize, or react to a trait merely because it appears in the Character Foundation.\n- The exceptions are limited to:\n  - The trait has an overt, observable physical component: species, appearance, or behavior.\n  - The player has mentioned or established the trait in play.\n  - It is represented by equipped inventory. Visible weapons, clothing, armor, tools, and other equipped items may be noticed and discussed.\n- Do not expose skills, training, magic, class, background, reputation, relationships, or progression traits through unsolicited dialogue, exposition, or NPC knowledge. NPCs may react to what the player visibly does, but must not name or infer the underlying trait unless an exception applies. Trait descriptions and traitNarrativeEffects are narrator context, not invitations for NPCs to reveal the trait.\n- In an initial opening, do not use a visible item as a stranger's conversational hook unless the Starting Situation or the player's action makes that item central.\n\n- For newly introduced named NPCs, young adults should usually dominate the cast. Most should appear 18–30, attractive or striking, distinctive, and socially engaging. Authority figures can also be young, attractive, stylish, warm, funny, ambitious, eccentric, charming, or dangerous.\n- Where species are unspecified, do not default everyone to human. Use an established people suited to the setting and context, including moon elves, wood elves, high elves, dwarves, halflings, gnomes, orcs, goblins, demonborn, dragonkin, giantborn, catkin, ratkin, foxkin, wolfkin, lionkin, boarfolk, monkeyfolk, gajanari, frogkin, serpentkin, and lizardfolk.\n\n## Companions\n- Name all companions in the opening orientation, but do not give every companion a line merely for coverage.\n- Show a companion when their role, relationship, or reaction matters. Give them opinions and a distinct presence without making them decide the player's response.\n\n## Prohibited in Openings\n- Unearned sensory detail: do not describe how a place smells or sounds, or what its temperature is, unless it is distinctive and important to the scene.\n- Mysterious hooded figures: prefer clear, straightforward characters players can interact with now.\n- Ominous warnings, generic challenges, or broad questions from strangers.\n- \"Something is wrong\" or similar unearned dread.\n- Characters who speak in riddles or are needlessly vague.\n- A summary, moral, atmospheric flourish, prophecy, or broad statement about what awaits at the end of the opening.",
      "custom": "## Framing Language\n- ALWAYS begin with the following phrase by the narrator:\n```NARRATOR: Long ago, in the world of LARION, [present tense beginning of story]``` (not past tense)\n\n## Scene Establishment\n- Assume no prior world knowledge; give essential context for proper nouns: \"the dwarven mining town of Greathearth\" not just \"Greathearth\".\n- Ground the player in the specific place provided using simple visual detail.\n- Show only the people, objects, and actions immediately relevant to the opening.\n\n## Immediate Engagement\n- Render the Starting Situation's actual premise in the first turn, not merely the events leading toward it.\n- Make clear what is happening now and give the player something concrete to respond to, examine, discuss, accept, refuse, or pursue.\n- For adventure, tutorial, or companion starts, carry the supplied situation through its first active beat.\n- For sandbox or calm starts, do not invent a major quest, danger, paid job, compulsory reason, or second hook. An optional social opening or ordinary circumstance is enough.\n- Do not strand the player or bombard them with unrelated hooks."
    },
    "generateActionInfo": {
      "custom": "## Magic Difficulty\nJudge magic by scale, complexity, and opposition. High on one axis is Hard, on two Very Hard, and on all three Extremely Hard or Impossible. Willing targets are easier; magical creatures resist mundane spells. Apply the relevant skill description's modifiers, such as abundant element, consecrated ground, wary eyes, or strong wills.\n\nAny magic skill can be worked as a ritual. Time, preparation, materials, or assisting casters lower effective difficulty one step, or two for a full rite with all four. Ritual never makes the Impossible possible.\n\n### Casting in Heavy Armor\nHeavy armor is full metal protection on the body or legs: iron breastplate, iron legharness, plate, or equivalent. Leather, cloth, robes, cuirasses, greaves, helms, gauntlets, boots, and shields alone are not heavy armor.\n\nIn heavy armor, raise magic of Hard or higher exactly as follows: Hard to Very Hard; Very Hard to Extremely Hard; Extremely Hard remains Extremely Hard. Lower tiers are unchanged. Routine magic in plate still works; dramatic magic in plate does not.\n\n## Combat Difficulty\nSet combat difficulty from opponent, conditions, and ambition.\n\nOpponent: an unskilled or unaware target is Easy; a competent fighter Medium; a skilled fighter Hard; multiple skilled fighters Very Hard; a supernatural or legendary foe Extremely Hard.\n\nConditions: wounds, exhaustion, difficult terrain, or being outnumbered raise difficulty. Superior position, surprise, allies, or useful terrain can lower it.\n\nAmbition: hitting is baseline; disarming is harder; a called shot to a specific weak point is Very Hard; deflecting projectiles, holding a chokepoint alone, or matching a legendary opponent is Extremely Hard. Weapon descriptions define each weapon's signature work.\n\nSome physical feats remain beyond mortal ability regardless of skill, such as cutting stone with a normal blade or jumping over a castle wall.\n\n## Attack Damage Selection\nTypes: piercing, slashing, impact, fire, frost, shock, acid, poison, radiant, shadow, arcane, psychic. Choose in this order:\n1. An item used in this attack with an of [Damage Type] name suffix. Count a named or unambiguously equipped weapon, not one merely carried. A Saber of Shock deals shock, not slashing. Of Flame means fire; of Radiance means radiant. Ammunition counts only when explicitly loosed and overrides the launcher's suffix.\n2. A generic spell's focus tag.\n3. A carried weapon oil, venom, etchant, extract, or unguent explicitly used to coat a plain weapon in this attack. Use its stated type; consume one unit whether the attack hits or misses; the effect lasts only for this action.\n4. Weapon shape.\n5. Action description.\n\nMagic skill descriptions state their damage type.\n\n## Social Difficulty\nSocial skills move people only within what they could plausibly do. Measure the request against what the target would do unaided: Easy if aligned or costless; Medium for a neutral request or minor favor; Somewhat Hard if inconvenient, mildly risky, or rule-bending; Hard against duty, loyalty, or self-interest; Very Hard for betrayal or serious self-risk; Extremely Hard to abandon core loyalties or risk everything for a compelling reason.\n\nLies must be plausible and threats credible. A request against the target's core identity, nature, or survival instincts is Impossible.\n\n## Crafting Difficulty\nA proper workshop makes crafting easier; improvised materials or no workspace make it harder.\n\n## Lore Enforcement\nActions violating established world rules are Impossible, including post-medieval technology and abilities with no basis in the character's skills or world logic.",
      "fixed_impact_attacks": "## Fixed Impact\nAn exact impact stated by an available ability the player invokes, an applicable instruction, or a deliberately used item replaces ordinary impact. Exact impacts never add, multiply, or stack; use only the highest.\n\n## Conditional Cases\n- Answer in Kind: impact 200 only when counterattacking a foe whose current intent attacks that player or answering that foe's immediately preceding narrated attack.\n- Hidden: impact 200 for each attack by a player who currently has the trait. Never infer Hidden from skill, narration, darkness, cover, or attempted concealment when the trait is absent. The Second Shadow applies it to both attacks when the actor begins Hidden. Wrong Shadow's additional effect is retaining Hidden afterward.\n- Disappear Mid-Sentence: impact 200 when used as part of the attack even if the player did not begin Hidden.\n- Open Vein: impact 200 when the attack opens the player's own vein or that of a living foe currently fighting them; use piercing for the player's vein and shadow for the foe's.\n- Patron's Mark: impact 150 for its single strike when the marked creature first violates the prohibition; the mark then ends.",
      "fighter_weapon_mastery": "## Fighter Weapon Mastery\nWhen a character with the Fighter trait uses a weapon through a trained weapon skill other than the skill that naturally fits it, use the closest fitting trained weapon skill by default. If the player explicitly chooses another trained weapon skill, that choice overrides the naturally fitting skill.\n\nBefore level 10, apply one -5 context modifier named Unfamiliar Handling. At level 10 or higher, apply no transferred-handling penalty. The weapon retains its own damage type, bonuses, and effects. An unfamiliar, improvised, or newly seized weapon requires no separate handling action.",
      "ranger_beast_companions": "## Ranger Beast Companion Limit\nWhen a Ranger attempts to make an existing beast a lasting Beast Companion, count the current party NPCs explicitly established as that Ranger's Beast Companions. The limit is one, or three when the Ranger has the Beastmaster trait. If the Ranger is already at that limit, establishing another Beast Companion is Impossible; name the companion limit in impossibleReason.\n\nThis gates only a new enduring party companion. It does not prevent calming, befriending, training, riding, or temporarily cooperating with other beasts. Do not count ordinary allies, mounts, hired animals, summons, raised servants, or merely friendly creatures unless explicitly established as that Ranger's Beast Companion.",
      "berserker_fury": "## Berserker Fury\nWhile a character with the Berserker trait currently has Fury, Deep Fury, or Unbroken Fury, they may attempt one immediate feat beyond ordinary mortal strength, endurance, or momentum at Very Hard. This licenses one physical act in the current action, never a sustained transformation or state. A named ability uses its own established rules instead.\n\nAt zero Stamina, active Fury keeps heavy or all-out physical actions possible, but the normal -8 to -10 Winded modifier still applies. Fury removes the impossibility, not the penalty or other costs.",
      "blood_dancer": "## Blood Dancer\nCapital-B Bleeding is a short supernatural wound explicitly established by a successful Blood Dancer working; ordinary bloodshed, blood on clothing, and old or descriptive wounds do not qualify. Only living, blood-bearing targets can be Bleeding. Use blood magic when the defining effect is opening, sustaining, shaping, or draining blood; use the weapon skill for a plain strike.\n\nWhen a player with Blood Dancer attacks a target that was already Bleeding before the current action and whose wound has not been closed or spent, add one +5 context modifier named Open Wound. A Bleeding effect created during this same action does not qualify. Second Cut is Impossible unless its living target was already Bleeding before the current action, remains present and fighting the player, and the wound remains unresolved.\n\nBloodstep licenses its stated crossing as part of the attack. The Blood Stands Up licenses one immediate physical feat no mortal frame could perform at Very Hard, never a sustained transformation or state.",
      "item_and_device_actions": "## Named Item Powers\nWhen a player deliberately invokes a named power stated by an item they carry, apply the exact immediate effect in its description. Ordinary attacks, passive bonuses, and merely naming or examining the item do not invoke its power.\n\n## Consumable Attacks and Prepared Devices\nA player explicitly attacking with a carried Consumable whose description says the attack consumes it has impact 150 and uses the damage type in its of [Damage Type] suffix. Consume it on the attempt. Setting a trap without springing it is not an attack.\n\nTeeth Underfoot requires one carried trap. It springs as the attack at impact 150, uses the trap's damage type, and expends the trap whether the attack succeeds or fails.\n\nThe Room Was Ready applies only when the player prepared the named trap or device earlier in this scene and the target reaches its established trigger. It attacks at impact 200 using the device's damage type; a mundane rig uses piercing or impact.\n\nOne More Working applies only to established remnants of a trap or single-use attack device that attacked earlier in this scene. It attacks again at impact 150 with the original damage type.\n\nBetween Two Footfalls requires a carried trap blueprint, all its stated materials, and its named toolkit. The constructed trap springs in this action at impact 200 using its damage type.\n\nThe Third Measure requires one carried potion, oil, venom, extract, or etchant. A restorative applies its exact stated effect once to each of up to three targets. A harmful dose attacks each of up to three targets separately at impact 150 using its stated damage type. Consume the input once, not once per target.\n\nIf an ability's stated source, preparation, blueprint, materials, toolkit, or item is absent, do not apply its effect.",
      "spell_scroll_use": "## Spell Scroll Use\nWhen a player releases the exact spell written in a carried spell scroll, set the difficulty one tier lower than casting that spell directly, with a floor of Easy. Use either Lore or the spell's own magic skill according to the attempted method, never both. The scroll is spent on the attempt whether it succeeds or fails.\n\nThis applies only to a Consumable document explicitly established as a spell scroll. Notes, grimoires, formulae, and ordinary written spells do not qualify.",
      "protective_ward_charms": "## Protective Ward Charms\nWhen using an Ember Charm, Rime Charm, Dawn Charm, or Bitterleaf Charm is the player's sole action, breaking the carried charm and raising its personal ward is Simple unless an opposing intent directly prevents the use.\n\nThis is item use, not warding magic. It requires no skill check or mana and is not a numerical buff; do not return buff fields for it. If the player combines using the charm with another action, assess the combined action normally.\n\nMerely carrying, handling, examining, or considering a charm raises no ward.",
      "weapon_upgrades": "## Upgrading Weapons\nAn upgrade transforms one named existing weapon. The weapon, suitable materials, required tools or workshop, and participating maker must already exist in the story; the check resolves workmanship and supplies no missing materials, reputation, access, or assistance.\n\n- Masterwork: reworking an ordinary, Fine, or Superior weapon requires exceptional materials and a proper forge. A named master smith may do it after agreeing to the commission; a player doing it personally uses smithing at Extremely Hard.\n- Warded: raising a Masterwork weapon requires a rare catalyst carrying suitable power, a capable enchanter, and a staged rite. A player binding it personally uses enchanting at Extremely Hard. It cannot be improvised in combat or completed as casual spellcasting.\n- Relic: no ordinary commission, upgrade action, or single check can make one. The story must first complete a great undertaking tied to the weapon, then a binding rite; without both, the attempt is Impossible.\n\nSkipping a weapon tier during an upgrade is Impossible.",
      "license_registry": "## Impossible Without License\nThe boundaries below are Impossible without a listed license or concrete found means. An ability must be explicitly invoked in this action and available: true in Action Evaluation Context; a trait must be present; an item must be carried and used. Without a permitted route, name what is missing in impossibleReason. A license makes the attempt possible at its listed difficulty, never automatic.\n\nAnything priced by a skill description's own ladder remains attemptable there without another license. Apply each gate only when the attempt crosses its exact boundary.\n\n- Sustained flight: Empyrean through its wings, Ride the Storm (Very Hard), a concrete skyborne artifact while held, or a storm-called mount while ridden. Storm magic's Extreme line permits one immediate ascent or aerial movement, not remaining aloft.\n- Returning the established dead to life: True Resurrection (Extremely Hard), as a full rite with a named price, once for any one soul. Breath Returned applies only just after breath or heartbeat fails, before death's claim settles.\n- Maintaining raised servants past dawn: The Gray Raven (one raised carrion bird, Medium), The Gray Tether (one true servant, Medium), The Gray Muster (up to three, Hard), or The Gray Legion (up to five including greater dead, Very Hard). Raising remains priced by necromancy. Count Scene NPCs whose name or properName begins with the actor's given-name possessive. If the actor explicitly lets excess servants collapse in this action, count only those retained; otherwise exceeding the cap is Impossible.\n- Stepping between places: Threshold Step to a visible place (Hard), The Ways Between to make a new way to a known place (Very Hard), or a found Waystone Shard once. Holding open a found or existing way remains conjuration's Extreme line.\n- Crossing into the Shadelands at will and unaided: The Graywalk (Very Hard). Spirit magic's Extreme line permits one crossing through a rite, thin place, gate, or guide.\n- Permanently breaking a true curse—a lasting trait-backed affliction, a bargained item's hold, or the grey country's mark: Sunder the Binding (Very Hard), an authored rite, or a consecrated shrine. Warding's ladder still covers simple hexes, lock-spells, and temporary suppression. Willpower alone never breaks a true curse.\n- Wearing a beast's shape: the shape shifting skill's ladder for someone trained in it; Wear the Wild for one turn; or a carried concrete item's deliberately invoked named power for exactly one turn.\n- Bodily command: a character with the Monk trait may make their own living body perform one immediate feat beyond ordinary mortal limits by changing its weight, force, hardness, suppleness, senses, timing, endurance, or natural mending (Very Hard; Extremely Hard for greater scale or opposition). Use acrobatics, athletics, or unarmed combat according to the deed. The body remains material: no new anatomy, passage through matter, teleportation, sustained flight, or reach through a held object or another body without a named Form. It cannot restore missing tissue, return the dead, or remove poison, disease, or curses.\n- Divine avatar: taking a patron divinity's anatomy into the body or becoming its avatar requires the Paladin trait, an equipped Vow Focus, and explicit use of Answer for Them (Hard; the focus's established aspect for one action) or The Oath Answers (Very Hard; the patron's established battle-form for one turn). Divine channeling alone fills an unchanged mortal body and never grants divine anatomy. Without the complete route, the attempt is Impossible.\n- Declaring sanctuary: placing a person, threshold, or gathering under a god's open protection, so violence against the protected person, place, or gathering answers to the god and its house: the Cleric trait (Very Hard; Extremely Hard for a declared enemy of that faith).\n- Compelling one immediate action: Commanding Thought using psionics (Hard; Very Hard against strong resistance or a command deeply contrary to the target's interests). It grants one commanded action, not sustained control.\n- Sustained domination or possession of another mind, alteration of an established memory, or regrowing a missing limb or organ: no current license. Psionics' ladder still permits reading surface thoughts, pulling truth from a resisting mind, and breaking through trained mental defenses. Healing magic's Extreme line still restores a withered but present limb.\n\nBinding a legendary creature into service is Impossible; summoning one without binding is at best Extremely Hard. Creating a legendary enchantment or banishing a great extraplanar being is at best Extremely Hard.",
      "difficulty_tiers": "## Larion Difficulty Rules\nChoose difficulty from actual stakes, not forceful wording. A skill description's Easy, Medium, Hard, and Extreme examples are domain anchors: use the nearest example, adjust for conditions, treat Extreme as Extremely Hard, and follow boundary lines when selecting the skill.\n\nIn Larion, Easy means the outcome is already certain and only clean versus mixed success remains; its draw alone cannot fail for trained or untrained characters. Express poor circumstances as negative context modifiers. Medium is near-certain for a trained character but fallible for the untrained or heavily penalised. Somewhat Hard is the first tier where a trained character can fail and the default for a genuinely uncertain or contested action. If failure is possible in the fiction, use Somewhat Hard or higher rather than Easy.",
      "resource_conditions": "## Resource Conditions\nStamina and mana are single-combat budgets. Apply stamina modifiers only to physical combat: weapon attacks, blocks, parries, dodges, grapples, wrestling, shoves, tackles, shield-bracing, and physical combat movement. Never apply them to magic, social action, investigation, crafting, shopping, travel, ordinary movement, or noncombat physical activity. Apply mana modifiers only to casting, channelling, and magical abilities.\n\nUse current value as a percentage of maximum:\n- Above 75%: High Stamina, +4 to +6; mana has no modifier.\n- 50% to 75%: Fresh Stamina, +2 to +3; mana has no modifier.\n- 25% to 50%: neither has a modifier.\n- Below 25%: Winded stamina, -3 to -5; Strained mana, -3 to -5.\n- At 0: stamina is -8 to -10 and heavy or all-out physical actions are Impossible; mana permits only small or ritual magic.\n\nUse High Stamina, Fresh Stamina, Winded, or Strained as the modifier name when applicable.\n\n### Drinking a Potion Mid-Action\nA potion may be drunk alongside another action in the same turn, unlike a recovery ability, which costs the turn. Apply a small negative context modifier to the accompanying action.\n\n### Tarnish\nApply only when the actor's current Tarnish is above 0.\n- Any positive Tarnish: dark magic, necromancy, blood magic, and direct dealings with demons, shades, or powers of the dead country gain +2, never more.\n- 1–39: no other modifier.\n- 40–79, Tarnish-Marked: the actor's divine, healing, and warding magic receives -2 to -4; their social actions involving priests, the blessed, or the pious receive -2 to -4.\n- 80–100, Tarnish-Ridden: replace those penalties with -5 to -8; physical actions on consecrated ground also receive -3.\n\nDo not apply the pious social penalty when dealing with spirits, necromancers, demons, or the dead country."
    },
    "generateCharacterBackground": {
      "prompt": "# Character Profile Generator\nWrite a character profile in two parts: background and appearance.\n\nUse the World Foundation as the main setting context. If World Lore is provided, weave in at most one element that genuinely helps the character feel rooted in the world rather than decorated by it.\n\nUse the Character Foundation as your main guide to who this person is. Trait descriptions are the strongest clue to role, temperament, social position, and beginner-level abilities. Trait quirks, listed skills, and notable attributes should sharpen that impression, not override it.\n\nIf Creative Direction is provided, treat it as player-authored intent. Preserve its core ideas unless they directly conflict with the world.\n\nFor background, write the history and tension that make this person feel alive and specific. Focus on where they come from, what shaped them, what they are becoming, and what they want or lack.\n\nFor appearance, describe the physical and social impression they make. Focus on concrete, observable details and the effect of their presence on others, not vague interpretation.\n\nKeep the profile vivid, specific, and grounded. Avoid generic phrasing, stock archetypes, and filler. Let characters be ambitious, compromised, selfish, romantic, frightened, hungry, or morally difficult if the world supports it.\n\nDo not add headers or labels inside the field contents.\n",
      "custom": "## Shamans and Spirit Courts\nFor a Shaman, decide whether a small starting spirit court helps the concept. If so, establish at most one or two spirits with clear identities and natures, such as an ancestor, elemental spirit, place spirit, household spirit, or intangible spirit shaped by memory or purpose. Do not require an ancestor, do not default to animal companions, and do not give a level-one Shaman a crowded court. It is equally valid for a novice to know only one spirit or to seek their first lasting spiritual relationship in play. Spirit Magic is distinct from Conjuration: spirits have wills and answer through relationship, offerings, shared purpose, leverage, or a successful bargain rather than automatic control.",
      "warlock_pacts": "## Warlock Pacts\nFor a Warlock, establish the patron or a concrete form through which it answers, the pact sign and one means of contact, and what each side gave when the standing pact was made. Do not impose an unaccepted future obligation. Give the starting Pact Token a patron-specific mark and say whether the patron gave it or transformed something the Warlock already carried into it.",
      "purpose": "## Goal\nGenerate a compelling character background that makes the player eager to begin their adventure. Create patterns of engagement - recurring situations, themes, or drives - that can surface naturally throughout gameplay rather than specific quests or destinations. The background should honor the core fantasy of the character's race and class while creating hooks the AI can reference in many different contexts. The character should always contain the seeds of greatness of some kind, potential to become powerful, wealthy, important, influential, and sway the balance of affairs, even though that potential is far from being realized.",
      "tone_and_framing": "## TONE AND FRAMING: DEFAULT TO HEROIC OPTIMISM\n\nUNLESS Creative Direction explicitly requests otherwise, generate backgrounds with an optimistic, forward-looking tone. These are Level 1 characters at the START of their story. Adversity gives them something to fight against.\n\n### THE CHARACTER SHOULD FEEL:\n- Ready and eager for the world\n- Capable and confident\n- Actively seeking\n- Looking forward\n\n### ADVERSITY AS EMPOWERMENT:\nBackgrounds can include hardship, but frame it as a source of STRENGTH not BURDEN:\n\n### POSITIVE FRAMING:\nUse language that conveys capability and forward momentum\n\n### THE ENDING (FORWARD MOMENTUM):\nThe final sentence should convey EAGERNESS, CURIOSITY, or DETERMINATION",
      "context_integration": "## CONTEXT SOURCES\n\n- World Foundation: Primary setting context and themes\n- World Lore: Select relevant elements naturally (maximum 1-2 references, always explained)\n- Character Foundation: Mechanical traits, skills, and abilities - don't reference them all, just pick those that give a sense of class or role in the world\n- Creative Direction: Player's input takes priority - incorporate their vision when provided\n\nDo not invent place names unless they are provided in World Foundation or World Lore. If locations matter, describe them generically (mountain village, port city, border fort) or use provided location names.",
      "psionicist_fantasy": "Psionicist: Mental force, heightened perception, reading thoughts, and influencing minds through trained will. Public practice of psionic magic is not permitted everywhere in Larion.",
      "class_race_fantasy": "## HONORING CLASS AND RACE\n\nThe character's class and race signal what fantasy the player wants to inhabit. Generate backgrounds that make players think \"Yes, this is exactly the [CLASS] [RACE] I wanted to play.\"\n\nUse the current Larion map and factions. Prefer current places such as Silvervale, Ardan's Watch, Elfwood, Sanctuary Vale, Redrock, the Alar Coast, Jade Tarn, Highheath, the Whiteholds, the Black Fells, and the Darek Deep. Do not anchor new character backgrounds to retired region names such as Highvale, Bright Kingdoms, Highlands, Spice Isles, or Everwood.\n\n### CLASS CORE FANTASIES:\n\n- Berserker: Battle-rage as controlled momentum, pain deferred, breaking lines, fearlessness, and surviving through motion\n*Optional: Wolfpack Lodge in the Orc Wolds (wolf-bound berserkers and elite monster hunters)*\n- Cleric: Blessings, rites, healing, turning profane forces, sacred authority, and faith under pressure\n*Deities: Mercy, Justice, Passage, Hope, Hearth, or Wisdom*\n- Bard: Attention, mood, access, rumor, performance, illusion, and social tempo\n*Optional: courts, festivals, taverns, League halls, and city salons from Silvervale to the Alar Coast*\n- Blood Dancer: Bloodletting, scar channels, pulse-reading, wound control, acrobatic blades, and pain as price\n*Optional: The Red Chantry (vampire/undead hunters using blood magic)*\n- Druid: Living land, roots, weather, wild signs, blight-cleansing, shelter, and terrain magic\n*Optional: Elfwood, Sanctuary Vale, the Pact of Sanctuary, wild shrines, and old green roads*\n- Fighter: Weapon mastery, battlefield control, timing, pressure, protecting allies, and winning the exchange\n*Optional: the League of Adventurers in Ardan's Watch, city guards, mercenary companies, delving crews in the Whiteholds, or noble house champions*\n- Monk: Breath, balance, pressure points, disciplined movement, calm, and practical body-spirit control\n*Deities: Lady of Wisdom (understanding, patience), Lord of Order (discipline as virtue)*\n*Optional: temple schools, mountain retreats, fighting cloisters, or wandering orders tied to Wisdom, Order, Valor, or the elements*\n- Paladin: Oaths made active, protection, judgment, moral courage, sacred authority, and standing between danger and the vulnerable\n*Deities: Lord of Valor (courage, standing against evil), Lord of Justice (oaths, pursuing the guilty), Lady of Mercy (protecting the vulnerable), Lord of Passage (releasing undead, guiding souls), Lady of Hope (holding vigil in darkest places)*\n*Optional: The Order of the Desert Rose in Redrock, League oaths in Ardan's Watch, or local holy orders*\n- Ranger: Tracking, impossible archery, beast companions, route-finding, and weaponizing terrain\n*Optional: Elfwood wardens, Silvervale road scouts, Black Fells monster hunters, or League guides out of Ardan's Watch*\n- Rogue: Attention control, access, timing, exits, theft, infiltration, lies, and surviving locked or watched places\n*Optional: The Syndicate, Muckside, dockside smugglers, city underworlds, noble spies, or freelance thieves*\n- Sorcerer: Innate magic, raw surges, bloodline signs, instinctive casting, and control under pressure\n- Warlock: Pacts, patron pressure, pact signs, borrowed power, hexes, servants, and otherworldly claims\n*Optional: extraplanar patrons, planar bargains, fiendish debts, forbidden shrines, or hidden cults*\n- Wizard: Arcane precision, prepared spellwork, breaking hostile magic, seeing and bending hidden rules\n*Optional: Tarnholme Academy of the Arcane in Jade Tarn, the Colleges of Binders, Menders, Seekers, Sentinels, or Shapers, private tutors, or old spell libraries*\n- Tinker: Sparks, braces, fault-finding, vials, locks, traps, repairs, and one-use devices\n\n### RACE CONSIDERATIONS:\n\nLet race flavor the background without overwhelming it:\n\n- Human: Ambition, restlessness, short lives lived at high speed\n- Dwarf: Honor/clan/craft traditions, restoring or upholding legacy\n*Optional: the Whiteholds, Darek Deep, Deepgate, dwarven quarters in major cities, mining claims, and artisan houses*\n- Halfling: Good things in life, proving capability despite being underestimated, community bonds\n- Orc: Strength with purpose beyond war, confronting or transcending prejudice\n- Dragonkin: Powerful heritage as burden or gift, living up to or escaping legacy\n- Moon elf/Wood elf: Long perspective, grace, connection to magic or nature\n*Optional: Elfwood, Canopy, Sanctuary, the Pact of Sanctuary, high elf courts, moon elf exiles, or old forest roads*\n- Catkin: Independent curiosity, agile problem-solving, feline traits in approach\n- Demonborn: Dark heritage confronted or embraced, proving heritage does not define destiny\n- Giantborn: Physical power creating distance or protection, standing apart\n- Serpentkin: Exotic difference, navigating a world that sees them as other\n- Half-elf: Between worlds, dual heritage creating unique perspective or tension",
      "structure_paragraph_one": "## SECTION ONE: FOUNDATION AND MOMENTUM (6-8 sentences, divided into 2-3 paragraphs using double newlines)\n\nCreate immediate interest and forward momentum:\n\n1. OPENING: Begin with a present-tense hook - who they are NOW, what defines them. Give them a trajectory in the world but don't prescribe their role too narrowly.\n\n2. ORIGIN: Where they're from in concrete terms (use generic descriptions if no location names provided)\n\n3. FORMATIVE EXPERIENCE: A person, group, event, or circumstance that shaped them\n   - Use provided character names if available\n   - Otherwise, describe relationships without forcing invented names\n   - Make this feel consequential and real\n   - Should naturally explain their path to their class\n\n4. CAPABILITY DEVELOPMENT: How they gained their skills and abilities\n   - Use ONLY the skills, traits, and attributes from Character Foundation\n   - Be specific about training or experience\n   - Make the skill set acquired feel narratively coherent\n   - Don't try to be exhaustive, just pick a few key items\n\n5. PATTERN OF ENGAGEMENT: What draws them, what they watch for, how they respond to situations\n   - NOT a specific quest or named antagonist\n   - INSTEAD: recurring themes, types of situations, ongoing drives\n   - Should feel like a disposition or habit of mind, not a destination\n   - See PATTERNS OF ENGAGEMENT section below for detailed guidance\n\n6. FORWARD MOMENTUM: End with external pull or unresolved tension that suggests adventure\n   - External draw creating urgency or opportunity\n   - See TONE AND FRAMING section for appropriate energy and framing\n   - Create forward-looking questions: What will they do? Where will they go? What will they discover?\n\nIf Creative Direction provides specific goals or drives, incorporate them as patterns the character pursues.",
      "structure_paragraph_two": "## SECTION TWO: APPEARANCE (3 sentences)\n\nEstablish physical presence using only permanent, objective traits:\n\n- Height/build and coloration (skin, hair, eyes)\n- Distinctive permanent features (scars only if from Character Foundation)\n- Write factually: \"X is [height/build]\" or \"X has [feature]\"\n- NO interpretive language (\"intimidating,\" \"wise-looking,\" \"mysterious\")\n- NO clothing, equipment, or gear\n- NO temporary features (dirt, injuries, recent wounds)\n- NO light effects (gleaming, glowing, sparkling)",
      "patterns_of_engagement": "## PATTERNS OF ENGAGEMENT\n\nCreate DISPOSITIONS not DESTINATIONS. The background should contain recurring themes the AI can invoke in many situations.\n\n### GOOD PATTERNS (recurring, applicable in many contexts):\n\n- \"Watches for signs of [type of danger] after experiencing it firsthand\"\n- \"Helps those in situations similar to their past [displacement/oppression/loss]\"\n- \"Seeks to understand [mystery/phenomenon] that marked their past\"\n- \"Proves capability to those who underestimated or wronged them\"\n- \"Collects [stories/knowledge/evidence] about [relevant theme]\"\n- \"Intervenes when they see [specific type of injustice]\"\n- \"Protects [type of place/person] from the fate that befell them\"\n- \"Challenges [authority type] that harmed them or others\"\n- \"Searches for scattered [group members/knowledge fragments/evidence]\"\n\n### HOW TO WEAVE IN PATTERNS:\n\nDon't state the pattern directly. Show it through:\n- What they've learned to notice\n- What situations they gravitate toward\n- What experiences shaped how they see the world\n- What they're actively seeking\n\nExample: \"She's been moving between cities, seeking opportunities to use her skills where they're needed most.\"\n\n### MAKING PATTERNS CONCRETE:\n\nThe pattern should answer: \"In gameplay, what situations will feel personal to this character?\"\n\nTest your pattern: Can you imagine three different scenes where this would matter? If not, it's too specific. Can you describe what the character would actually DO or NOTICE? If not, it's too abstract.",
      "adversity_guidance": "## ADVERSITY AND STAKES\n\nBackgrounds can include hardship, but must frame it according to the TONE AND FRAMING guidelines above.\n\n**TYPES OF ADVERSITY:**\n- External circumstances: economic collapse, natural disasters, wars, displacement\n- Structural challenges: poverty, prejudice, lack of opportunity\n- Loss or failure: death of loved ones, business failures, exile, defeat\n- Worthy opponents: rivals, competitors, those who underestimated them\n\n**FORMATIVE RELATIONSHIPS (Mentors, Family, Teachers):**\nEven when demanding or imperfect, formative relationships should NOT be abusive or toxic\n\n✓ GOOD: \"His uncle was a demanding teacher who pushed him to master every form\"\n✓ GOOD: \"Her grandmother taught her blood magic with stern discipline and high expectations\"\n✓ GOOD: \"His family's traditions felt stifling, but they gave him skills few possess\"\n\n✗ BAD: \"His uncle broke his ribs during training and called it character building\"\n✗ BAD: \"His bitter old grandmother taught him that suffering was the only path to power\"\n✗ BAD: \"Her father's cruel lessons left her scarred inside and out\"\n\nCharacters can disagree with or move beyond their teachers' values without those teachers being abusive.\n\n**WHEN DESCRIBING THREATS OR DANGERS:**\n- Use concrete specifics: raiders, harsh winters, rival clans, economic collapse, bandits, wild animals\n- Avoid abstract menace: \"encroaching darkness,\" \"what prowls beyond,\" \"shadows closing in\"\n- Make dangers comprehensible: wolves not monsters, corrupt officials not ancient evils, human cruelty not cosmic horror\n- If supernatural elements exist, keep them matter-of-fact not ominous\n\n**CHARACTER RELATIONSHIP TO ADVERSITY:**\n- Characters should be tested but not broken\n- Make them admirable in how they faced challenges\n- Past troubles create capability and determination, not just trauma\n- Hardship comes more from circumstances than from people being deliberately cruel\n- Balance challenges with resilience and forward-looking attitude\n\nREMEMBER: This is the player's first narrative content. Create excitement about the adventure ahead, not overwhelming darkness about what happened before.",
      "style_principles": "## STYLE\nThe profile should feel like something you could read outloud to other players at the start of a TTRPG campaign to introduce your character. It should be easy to follow, using clear tropes and straightforward language. Remember to include double newline paragraph breaks every few sentences so that it stays readable.\n\n### DO:\n- Vary sentence rhythm to create flow and emphasis\n- Use strong, specific verbs that convey action and change\n- Favor concrete details over abstractions\n- Let stakes and relationships carry emotional weight naturally\n- Create questions in the reader's mind\n- Write with purpose and forward momentum\n- Make every sentence earn its place\n- Write using simple, straightforward language, using common words\n\n### DON'T:\n- Use purple prose, flowery language, or overwrought metaphors\n- Use fancy, big words when small, simple ones will do\n- Include clichés (gleaming eyes, dappled shadows, stark contrast, weight of world, etc.)\n- Overexplain or provide complete life histories\n- Make it feel finished - leave room for the adventure ahead\n- Use filtering phrases (\"she felt,\" \"he noticed,\" \"they saw\")\n- Rely on adverbs when strong verbs would work\n- Describe tone or timber of voice in any dialogue\n- Reference specific immediate goals or current situations",
      "anti_patterns": "## FORBIDDEN ELEMENTS\n\nNever include, UNLESS provided in Creative Direction:\n\n- The \"chosen one\" trope or prophecies about the character\n- Amnesia or mysterious parentage that must be discovered\n- Named unique villains who must be defeated\n- Specific unique magic items that must be found\n- Horror/body horror elements (corruption that physically transforms, Lovecraftian weirdness)\n- Named locations unless provided in World Foundation or World Lore\n- Romantic tragic backstories (dead spouse/lover as primary motivation)\n- Sole survivors of massacres or apocalyptic events\n- Secret royalty or noble heritage concealed from them\n- Characters who are \"the last\" or \"the only\" of their kind\n- Measurements (feet, meters, pounds, years of age)\n- Light effects in appearance (gleaming, glowing, sparkling eyes)\n- Clothing, equipment, or gear in appearance descriptions",
      "appearance_rules": "## APPEARANCE PARAGRAPH: STRICT RULES\n\nThis paragraph has ONE job: describe permanent physical traits objectively.\n\nWRITE LIKE A POLICE REPORT, NOT A CHARACTER STUDY.\n\n✓ ALLOWED:\n- Height and build (\"tall,\" \"stocky,\" \"lean\")\n- Coloration (skin, scales, fur, hair, eyes)\n- Permanent features (scars IF from Character Foundation, distinctive marks)\n- Observable facts (\"has pointed ears,\" \"walks with a limp\")\n\n✗ FORBIDDEN:\n- Interpretive language: \"clever,\" \"wise,\" \"kind,\" \"intimidating,\" \"mysterious\"\n- Comparative judgments: \"surprisingly,\" \"unusually,\" \"remarkably\"\n- Behavioral descriptions: \"eyes that miss nothing,\" \"constantly assessing,\" \"seem to know their business\"\n- Metaphoric descriptions: \"eyes the color of old bruises\"\n- Attributing internal states: \"eyes of someone who understands,\" \"hands that have seen hardship\"\n- Moves with grace, etc.\n- Any phrase that tells us what the character is LIKE rather than what they LOOK like\n\nTEST: If you can't determine it from a photograph, don't include it.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n\n✗ BAD: \"She has clever hands that seem to know their business.\"\n✓ GOOD: \"She has large hands with long fingers.\"\n\n✗ BAD: \"His eyes are dark and assessing, missing little, the color of wet stone.\"\n✓ GOOD: \"His eyes are dark brown.\"\n\n✗ BAD: \"She's surprisingly agile for her size.\"\n✓ GOOD: \"She is tall and solidly built.\"\n\nRemember: Section one does character work. Section two does ONLY physical description. Keep them separate."
    },
    "generateConversationStarters": {
