    "generateNPCIntents": {
      "core_principles": "# NPC COMBAT INTENTS\n\nGenerate specific, mechanically useful combat intents for NPCs. An intent states what an NPC attempts now; it never decides or narrates the result.\n\nGenerate intents ONLY for characters listed under Acting NPCs. NEVER generate an intent for a player, and NEVER use a player name as characterName. Players may appear only as targets.\n\nMake one decision for the complete batch: first classify the combat state, then construct the entire required output.",
      "when_to_generate": "## COMBAT STATE — CHECK FIRST\n\nClassify the entire batch as exactly one of these cases:\n\n1. NO COMBAT / NO INITIATION: Combat is not underway, and no NPC will begin combat now.\n2. ACTIVE COMBAT: Combat is already underway.\n3. NPC INITIATES COMBAT: Combat is not underway, but at least one NPC will begin it now with an immediate attack.\n\nFor Case 1, call the output tool once with an empty intents array and stop. Generate no NPC intents of any kind.\n\nFor Cases 2 and 3, apply the Final Process Check at the end of these instructions.\n\nUse Case 3 only when an NPC commits to an immediate attack now. That attack intent is what begins combat. A threat, warning, hostile posture, readied weapon, approach, or possible future attack without an immediate attack remains Case 1.",
      "what_is_not_action": "## PRACTICE AND SPARRING\n\nTraining, drills, lessons, demonstrations, challenges, invitations to spar, and consensual sparring are NO COMBAT / NO INITIATION. Return an empty intents array. Handling weapons, casting practice spells, or describing a training exchange does not make the scene combat. Only an actual attempt to injure or subdue someone, or a real fight breaking out, may begin combat.",
      "description_economy": "## COMBAT ACTION QUALITY\n\nEvery intent must be mechanically possible now and specific enough to resolve. Base it on the NPC's established abilities, equipment, condition, position, targets, and behavior.\n\nPrefer a defining ability or distinctive fighting method when useful. State the weapon, ability, item, or method; the target; relevant movement or positioning; and the immediate tactical purpose. Movement, speech, aiming, guarding, preparation, coordination, or terrain use may be included when they are part of the attempted combat action.\n\nUse only terrain, cover, objects, hazards, allies, and items established in the supplied context and physically available. Do not invent convenient battlefield features, equipment, reinforcements, or effects.\n\nMake multiple intents from one NPC distinct, complementary, and independently evaluable. Do not add filler attacks merely to meet a count.",
      "summary_established_beats": "## COMBAT CONTINUITY\n\nTreat Recent Story, current combat state, and Past Story Summary as the record of the fight. Start from the latest established exchange and account for attacks, defenses, movement, injuries, active effects, exposed positions, orders, and changed objectives.\n\nDo not repeat an earlier setup as though it has not happened. Let NPC details and hidden information shape tactics only where they remain consistent with the more recent story. Do not give an NPC knowledge it has not acquired.",
      "action_format": "## ACTION FORMAT\n\nWrite each action as 1–3 complete sentences in present tense. Describe what the NPC attempts, not what succeeds. Do not state that an attack hits, damage is dealt, a spell takes hold, a target is restrained, or any other result occurs.\n\nUse concise exterior action. Include only dialogue or sensory detail that makes the attempted combat action mechanically clearer.\n\nTargets are the characters directly receiving the intent's effect. For defend, target only the acting NPC and/or friendly characters being protected. For preventAttack, target the enemy whose attack is being stopped. For every other targeted intent, name the characters directly affected. Use exact names from Allowed targets.",
      "story_driver": "",
      "custom": "# FINAL PROCESS CHECK — APPLY LAST\n\nUse this final check to construct and verify one complete NPC intent batch.\n\nFor Cases 2 and 3, inspect EVERY name under Acting NPCs. Acting NPCs are the only characters for whom intents may be generated. Players may appear as targets, but every characterName must exactly match a name under Acting NPCs. Never generate an intent for a character listed only under Players.\n\nAn Acting NPC who is dead, unconscious, absent, or otherwise mechanically unable to take any action receives no intent. Assign every other Acting NPC exactly one of these two roles this turn:\n\n## FIGHTING\n\nGenerate this NPC's required number of intents and group them together. The FIRST intent listed for the NPC is its primary intent and MUST have type attack. Healing, defense, capture, control, positioning, support, and additional tactics may supplement that attack through secondary intents.\n\nLater intents for that NPC are secondary. A secondary intent may have type attack, mockAttack, subdue, preventAttack, evade, defend, heal, or buff.\n\nRequired number of intents for each fighting NPC:\n- trivial/weak: exactly 1\n- average: exactly 2\n- strong: 2–3\n- elite/boss/mythic: 3–4\n\nMake every intent immediately executable, distinct, complementary, and tactically connected. Include movement, approach, repositioning, guarding, aiming, speech, preparation, coordination, or terrain interaction inside the attack or secondary combat intent it supports.\n\n## FLEEING OR REFUSING TO FIGHT\n\nType other is used only for this role. An NPC who is surrendering, fleeing, withdrawing from combat, or explicitly refusing to fight now receives exactly one intent of type other describing that act, regardless of tier.\n\nIn Case 3, every initiating NPC is Fighting, and its first intent is the immediate attack that begins combat. Assign every other able Acting NPC one of the two roles above.\n\nBefore the tool call, verify the complete batch:\n- Case 1 has an empty intents array.\n- Cases 2 and 3 include every able Acting NPC.\n- Every fighting NPC has attack first and the correct number of intents for its tier.\n- Every fleeing or refusing NPC has exactly one intent.\n- Every characterName belongs to Acting NPCs; players appear only as targets.\n\nReturn the complete batch in one tool call.\n\n## FINAL `other` AUDIT — HARD REQUIREMENT\n\nImmediately before the tool call, inspect every intent whose type is `other`.\n\nAn `other` intent is valid ONLY when the NPC is explicitly doing one of these things now:\n\n- surrendering;\n- fleeing the fight;\n- withdrawing from combat;\n- refusing to fight.\n\nDelete or replace every other `other` intent before returning the batch. Speaking, moving, watching, waiting, aiming, threatening, guarding, preparing, investigating, manipulating terrain, or performing an ordinary non-combat action never qualifies.\n\nIn Case 1, return an empty intents array. Do not generate `other` intents.\n\nIf any `other` intent remains for a reason outside the four permitted actions, the entire batch is invalid and MUST be corrected before the tool call.\n\n## CLOSED OUTPUT DOMAIN\n\nDecide the batch before considering individual NPCs. The only valid outputs are an empty intents array or a combat-intent batch; an out-of-combat intent batch does not exist.\n\nJudge combat at the current moment at the end of Recent Story. If no fight remains unresolved and no NPC will attack now, return an empty intents array immediately.",
      "npc_target_contract": "NPC TARGET CONTRACT (MANDATORY):\nEvery intent object must include a targets array.\nThese intent types require a non-empty targets array of exact character names: attack, mockAttack, subdue, preventAttack, evade, defend, heal, buff.\nFor other, include targets: [].\nNever omit targets and never use targets: [] for a targeted intent."
    },
    "generateNewNPC": {
      "custom": "## New NPC Visual Description\n\nWrite 1-3 sentences of stable appearance: gender, species, age, clothing material/color/cut, and 1-2 permanent traits. Keep injuries, dirt, expressions, actions, and temporary states out.\n\n## Non-Sentient Naming\n\nDo not invent proper names for non-sentient beings: ordinary animals, instinct-driven monsters, swarms, and mindless constructs. Use a species or descriptive identity instead. Preserve an explicitly supplied name; otherwise use a proper name only when the being is sentient, intelligent, bonded, recurring, legendary, or individually important.\n\nDefault to clean, attractive, adult, socially open, and varied. Use elegant or theatrical fantasy clothing where appropriate; even less fancy clothing should be stylishly designed and aesthetic. Vary design: not all men are bulky/bearded/grim, dwarves red-haired/bearded/heavily armored, or elves pale/ethereal.\n\nMost, including authority figures, appear 18-30, attractive, and distinctive. Gray hair belongs to elderly characters.\n\n## Naming Conventions\n\nUse concept, type, gender hint, world background, heritage or birth culture, current faction/house, current location/area, recent story/summaries, and region. Determine heritage before geography: species is not culture, and current location does not erase a character's established origin. Priority: explicit culture or heritage, species family, faction/house, current community and location, then region.\n\nWhen the supplied name is descriptive, return the engine-requested number of unique proper-name candidates in most-likely-to-least-likely order. Vary length, stress, initials, syllable count, endings, and given-name/surname shape. Keep names readable and speakable. Check existing NPCs, player names, canonical names, and the current banned-name list. Use one dominant family plus at most one justified local or faction influence; do not blend several source cultures into a generic fantasy string. A faction normally changes register, title, byname, or house structure before it changes phonology.\n\nUse the concrete family rules in the naming blocks below. Do not interpret labels such as gothic, imperial, northern, maritime, or South Asian as sufficient instructions by themselves.",
      "bound_servant_naming": "### Bound servant naming\nA creature raised or summoned into a player's service receives a bound proper name beginning with its summoner's given-name possessive and a distinct epithet: \"Maera's Gravedigger\", \"Maera's Lantern-Bearer\", or \"Cyran's Veil-Servitor\". Always begin the proper name with the summoner's given name and possessive—it marks the bond—and give every servant its own epithet so no two share a name. Bound servants take the summoner's side. Ordinary servants default to weak or average tier, but a successful working that explicitly calls a dangerous creature, greater being, greater dead, or otherwise stronger servant uses the tier appropriate to that working. Never make a servant a standout merely because it was summoned. A carrion bird raised through The Gray Raven is always weak and noncombatant: it scouts and serves as an omen, but it cannot attack.",
      "NPC Type Roster": "## NPC Type Roster\nChoose one exact type key from this roster:\n- aberration: creatures wrong for reality anywhere — mimics, doppelgangers, psychic horrors, and impossible anatomy.\n- aquatic creature: water-bound beings that fight through depth, drowning, or ambush from water — drowners, merrow, sirens, sea-devils, dragon turtles, giant crocodiles, the kraken, Giant Crab, Stonescale Eel, and Weeping Hunter.\n- arcane spellcaster: mortal mages whose fighting identity and defenses come from arcane spellcraft.\n- armored fiend: fiends armored in their own hide, made to hold ground and break a line.\n- beast: animals, megafauna, and monstrous predators not better described as venomous beast, aquatic creature, or draconic kin; sapient animal-shaped peoples use mundane humanoid unless a closer family applies, while shifters are cursed.\n- celestial: skyblessed beings, angelic messengers, and servants of the Celestial Citadel.\n- construct: automatons, animated armor, clay, flesh, and iron golems, and other works of artifice still following an order.\n- corporeal undead: the dead walking in their own bodies — skeletons, zombies, ghouls, wights, revenants, mummies, drowned dead, and vampires.\n- cursed: living people carrying an affliction — were-kin, the bitten, the hag-marked, and the rot-cursed.\n- deep-thing: creatures correctly adapted to the lightless deep — grimlocks, gricks, ropers, darkmantles, stirges, purple worms, and morlocks.\n- draconic kin: wyverns, aspect-drakes, and pseudodragons below the true dragons.\n- dragon: an established named true dragon or great draconic power; never create a new one.\n- fey: the old wild and its peoples — dryads, satyrs, sprites, treants, unicorns, redcaps, will-o'-wisps, and hags.\n- fiend: demons, devils, imps, hell hounds, nightmares, tempters, ghuls, and other infernal beings that are not hide-armored walls.\n- fire elemental: living flame, magma, ash, salamanders, and burning forge-kin.\n- frost elemental: living ice, snow, winter, and supernatural cold.\n- giant-kin: giants, Trolls, Ogres, Ettins, Cyclopes, Oni, and Hundredhands; use fire elemental for Magmaborn and other magma-bodied beings.\n- goblinoid: goblins, hobgoblins, and bugbears of any allegiance.\n- merchant: an ordinary nonmagical trader, broker, appraiser, dealer, shopkeeper, or craftsperson whose trade is central to the character.\n- mundane humanoid: an ordinary mortal person with no supernatural damage anatomy; also the neutral person type for a sapient nonhuman people when no closer mechanical family applies.\n- ooze: slimes, puddings, gelatinous cubes, and other mindless creeping dissolvers.\n- plant creature: hostile fungi, choking vines, and vegetation monsters with no fey bargain or personhood.\n- priest or blessed caster: mortal priests, healers, shrine guardians, templars, champions, and other wielders of divine power.\n- shadow creature: beings sustained by darkness rather than death — shades, void-touched beings, smoke spirits, and nightmare things.\n- spectral undead: bodiless dead — ghosts, specters, wraiths, banshees, and shadows.\n- stone construct: gargoyles, stone golems, and animated statues bound to a place and an order.\n- storm elemental: living lightning, thunder, and charged wind.\n- trained warrior: exceptional martial combatants whose distinctive fighting skill is central to the character.\n- undead spellcaster: singular dead powers sustained by their own magic — liches, dracoliches, mummy lords, and necromantic archmages.\n- venomous beast: non-sapient toxin-bearers whose fighting logic is ambush, venom, and withdrawal.\n- water elemental: water itself made animate or called into service — spring-spirits, wave-shapes, and the animate flood.",
      "Functional Type Selection": "## Functional NPC Type Selection\nUse merchant when the story clearly establishes an ordinary nonmagical shopkeeper, trader, broker, appraiser, dealer, or craftsperson whose trade is central to the character. Use mundane humanoid for ordinary people whose identity lies elsewhere.\n\nA mechanically meaningful nature takes priority over a social function: retain arcane spellcaster, priest or blessed caster, undead, fiend, elemental, construct, fey, celestial, or another supernatural type when it describes how the NPC fights and takes damage. Use trained warrior when exceptional martial ability is central. The later detailing step can still portray a spellcaster, fiend, priest, or warrior as a merchant when the story establishes that role.\n\nUSE ONLY THESE EXACT TYPES: set the type field to one of the existing NPC type keys, copied exactly, and never generate newTypeDetails. Every character belongs to the closest existing family by what it is, not by its job. If several fit, choose the one that best describes how it fights and takes damage.",
      "Naming Phonology Families": "## Naming Phonology Families\n\nTreat each family as a small sound system: consonants and clusters, vowel density, syllable shape, stress, endings, and length. Use cues, not mandatory letters. Vary initials and endings across an array. Avoid apostrophe chains, unreadable consonant piles, accidental modern names, and repeated templates.\n\nWestern settled - Silvervale, Kingdom of Carendon, Westmarch: English and French sources. English names are familiar and speakable, usually 1-3 syllables; surnames may be occupational, place-based, patronymic, or ordinary family names. Aristocratic English can use longer Latinate or French-influenced given names and house surnames; rustic English uses shorter, homely, occupational, or locality names. French influence favors flowing vowels, l/r/m/n, and soft endings.\n\nNorthern and highland - Highheath, Whiteholds, Black Fells, Aurora, Whitemoor, Whitecliff Downs: Scottish, Norse, Norwegian, and Germanic sources. Favor strong initial stress, compact forms, hard clusters such as br, dr, gr, kr, sk, st, and th, and sturdy consonant endings. Clan, family, patronymic, and hold names matter. Use softer or longer forms for non-warrior, courtly, or religious characters.\n\nWoodland and high elf - Elfwood, Sanctuary Vale, and surface elven communities: Welsh, Irish, Gaelic, and Celtic sources. Favor liquid and vowel-rich patterns, l/r/m/n/w, soft fricatives, clear syllables, and lyrical 2-4 syllable forms. Use Welsh-like or Irish-like clusters only when pronounceable. High elf names may use elegant given names and inherited family or house forms.\n\nDark/moon elf baseline: refined, controlled, old, and shadowed rather than automatically evil. Favor readable 2-4 syllable forms, liquid and sibilant/fricative sounds such as l, r, m, n, v, s, z, sh, and th, measured stress, and occasional clusters balanced by open vowels. Use elegant given names with house, clan, or estate names when status warrants. Do not make every name ornate, vampiric, Victorian, or built from night, blood, raven, or shadow words; do not use apostrophes or force z/x/th into every name.\n\nRootdark dark elf: keep the broad dark-elf sound family but make it leaner and practical. Prefer clear given names and deep-road, craft, trade, hold, kin, or guild structures. A Rootdark dark elf may be a merchant, artisan, envoy, guide, agent, or exile; do not default to a sinister noble.\n\nShadow Summits moon elf: only when tied to that society, use a formal old-house and blood-court family: elegant 2-4 syllable given names, inherited house or estate names, measured aristocratic cadence, and restrained baroque ornament. Keep titles separate from the proper name. Do not apply this entire family to elves elsewhere.\n\nTarnished Reaches: use a separate dead-imperial family. Favor antique, formal, weighty names and old court, lineage, or epithet structures, but do not reuse Shadow Summits moon-elf phonology by default. The Fourth Empire and Lord Rhadan's Court supply the social register; the name still needs a speakable sound pattern.\n\nSouthern maritime - Alar Coast, Bay of Serpents, Swordlaw Isles, Great Lagoon, and Seadeep Kingdoms: use Mediterranean, Arabic, Phoenician, Greek, Italian, Spanish, or Portuguese sources only as indicated. Favor open vowels, clear syllables, and names that travel well across ports. Houses, crews, patronymics, and occupational bynames may matter. Do not blend every source into one pseudo-language.\n\nDesert - Redrock and Dunelands: use the established Arabic family where appropriate: open vowels, q/k/h/r/sh and other emphatic-feeling consonants used sparingly, plus single-name, family, lineage, or byname structures. Treat Persian-derived naming as a separate family only when the world context explicitly establishes it; do not label an arbitrary desert name Persian.\n\nRiver-jungle and South Asian - Ntar Vanna and Reedmoot Fens: use Indian or Sanskrit-derived sound and naming structures only where the story supports them. Favor readable vowel variation, voiced stops, and measured multi-syllable forms; do not flatten every river or jungle culture into one pool. Current community and faction can override.\n\nDeep-earth - Grell Hollows and Darek Deep: use compact stressed forms, sturdy clusters, and clan, hold, craft, or trade names for dwarves and ordinary deep peoples. Reserve harsher clusters for crude species or explicitly brutal cultures.\n\nSpecies overrides: dwarf - compact Norse or Scottish-influenced clan forms; halfling - warm, familiar, Medieval English and rustic family names; gnome - lighter dwarf/halfling hybrid; orc, goblin, kobold - shorter, clipped, harsher forms with simple structures; beastfolk - local culture first, animal-like sounds only when warranted; dragonkin - resonant, longer, draconic forms; demonborn and fiends - harsh or ceremonial only when type/faction warrants them; constructs, elementals, undead - single, purpose, maker-derived, or established title names.",
      "Naming Culture, Status, and Faction Registers": "## Naming Culture, Status, and Faction Registers\n\nSeparate heritage from social register. Heritage and birth culture choose the base sound family; faction, house, profession, and current location modify structure and formality. A faction does not magically create a new language. A migrant, mixed-heritage character, or faction recruit may retain an old name, adopt a local form, or use an alias only when the story supports it.\n\nName structure cues:\n- Western settled cultures: given name plus family name is common. English bynames can be occupational, place-based, patronymic, physical, or reputational. Aristocrats may use longer given names and inherited houses; rustic people may use shorter given names and practical bynames. Do not make every commoner rustic or every noble elaborate.\n- Welsh, Irish, and Gaelic cultures: use local given-name pools and patronymic or family forms where established; Welsh-like ap/ab forms and Gaelic compounds are occasional cultural structures, not decorative prefixes for every elf.\n- Scottish, Norse, and Germanic cultures: use given names with clan, family, patronymic, hold, or locality structures. A strong clan sound does not require every name to be a warrior's name.\n- Arabic and desert cultures: use the actual local pattern indicated by the story - single name, family or lineage, patronymic, occupation, place, or epithet. Do not randomly add al-, ibn, or honorifics.\n- Indian and Sanskrit-derived cultures: preserve the local given-name and family, lineage, title, or devotional structure established in context. Do not treat all South Asian names as interchangeable.\n- Maritime cultures: houses, crews, patronymics, port bynames, occupations, and nicknames can distinguish people. Keep the person's inherited family or culture visible unless the story establishes adoption.\n- Elven cultures: high and wood elves favor elegant given names and inherited families or houses; dark elves may use house, hold, guild, road, or kin structures; moon-elf court names may use estate and lineage structures. Do not make titles part of the proper name.\n- Rootdark: trade, craft, hold, guild, kin, and road relationships are useful naming structures. A deep-market envoy and a ruling house member need not share the same social form.\n- Shadow Summits: when relevant, use inherited house or estate identity, formal lineage, and old court titles as separate story descriptors. Blood-court style belongs to that society, not to every dark elf.\n- Tarnished Reaches: use old court, imperial house, succession, and epithet structures for the highborn dead. Share the sense of age and rank, not Shadow Summits' moon-elf family.\n\nFaction register rules:\n- Noble houses and courts: add house or estate identity when status matters; vary formal and familiar forms.\n- Military orders - Order of the Silver Sword, Templars Adamant, Shadow Inquisition, and similar: retain the recruit's culture; order rank or title belongs outside the proper name. An epithet may be used in story after it is earned.\n- Colleges and academies: retain heritage and region; use ordinary family names, scholarly bynames, or institution-specific epithets only when established. Do not create a universal academic language.\n- The Syndicate, mercenary companies, crews, and guilds: retain local names; add occupational, crew, debt, street, or reputation bynames only when the story makes them socially real. Do not turn criminal or mercenary affiliation into a separate ethnic sound.\n- Churches, cults, and occult orders - Church of the Pyre, New Sun, Children of Ur, Long Night, Thrallmages, and similar: preserve the person's culture and species; a devotional or ideological name may be an epithet, alias, or title, not an automatic replacement for their birth name.\n- Wildkeepers and other local fellowships: use the surrounding regional or woodland family; faction may supply a practical byname or earned epithet.\n- Goblinate, beastfolk packs, vampire houses, and other species-centered groups: species and local culture remain primary; faction modifies rank, clan, pack, house, or title structure.\n\nStatus and identity:\n- Common names tend shorter and more familiar; noble, ancient, magical, or ceremonial names may be longer, but vary this so status is not mechanically predictable.\n- Gender hints may affect endings when the selected culture supports them; never force a gender suffix across an entire species.\n- Use titles, honorifics, ranks, epithets, and group names in story text or separate fields, never inside proper-name options.\n- For aliases, nicknames, translation names, and epithets, keep the underlying ordinary name distinct unless the prompt explicitly says the alias is the only public name."
