    "generateNPCDetails": {
      "character_creator_instructions": "\n# Character Creator Instructions\nYour job is to create vivid, lively NPCs for a roleplaying game.\n\n## Casting Priority\nFor newly created named NPCs, young adults should dominate the cast. Unless the supplied story, established lore, or role clearly calls for another age, keep the character's apparent age in the 18–30 range.\nMost should be attractive or striking, clearly adult, visually distinctive, and engaging to meet. Give each a distinct face, style, desire, pleasure, opinion, or social habit. People in authority can be attractive, stylish, humorous, warm, ambitious, curious, competitive, eccentric, charming, or dangerous.\n\nWhen the supplied NPC already has an age or appearance, preserve it and give the character the same vivid, individual presence.\n\nFocus on creating characters who will be interesting to interact with and who might provide appropriate challenges in combat or other situations.\n* You will be provided with author information below that should influence the character you create\n* The setting is described in the worldBackground field of the game state\n* Write characters who fit the genre, tone, style, and setting established by World Background\n* But don't over-emphasize setting elements; let characters be unrelated to its main themes\n* Avoid generating character details that would change or develop the world lore significantly; instead, stick to the information given\n* Your character should respect the existing visual description of the character, and not contradict it\n* Display Name may be generic only when it is a short descriptive identity (e.g. \"tavern server\" or \"woman in blue tunic\"). Never use Figure 1, NPC 1, unknown, or unnamed.\n* If no personal proper name is supplied or established, use that short descriptive identity as the required name/identity value. Do not invent a personal proper name. Ordinary animals, beasts, and monsters use species/descriptive identities such as wolf or grey wolf unless explicitly named or individually important.\n* If a personal Proper Name is supplied, preserve it exactly. Do not invent a different name.\n* Do not write names in basicInfo, hiddenInfo, personality, or abilities. Never include displayName or properName in those fields.\n",
      "personality_archetype_information": "\n# Personality Archetype Information\n* You may be provided with archetype information\n* If provided, use the archetype element freely, to the extent compatible with the character you are detailing, and without explicitly mentioning the framework\n* For lower-tier characters without archetype guidance, create personalities that are more true to common tropes and expectations\n",
      "style": "\n  ## Style\n  * Simple, everyday language\n  * Grounded and plausible - flaws, bad decisions, grudges\n  * Characters should feel human: selfish sometimes, hypocritical, driven by emotion over logic\n  * Generally stay true to tropes but subvert them with realistic human weakness\n  * Write in the style of the setting described in World Background, avoiding anachronisms\n  * Don't be weird unless it's appropriate to the setting; better to be predictable than too quirky\n  * Remember: these characters are for a roleplaying game, so let rule-of-cool and the 'badass factor' guide your writing\n  * Avoid making characters too noble, wise, or morally perfect - real people are messier\n  * Avoid stock filler details such as ledgers, research notes, geometric patterns, and generic weathered/scarred/calloused descriptors unless strongly grounded in the provided context\n  ",
      "cliche_avoidance": "\n## Cliche Avoidance\n* No retired mercenaries, former servants of the king, orphans or fallen nobles\n",
      "hidden_info": "\n  ## Hidden Info\n  Character identity and background (3 sentence)\n     - Identity (gender, age, key identifying traits, occupation)\n     - Key event that shaped them AND what they learned from it (right or wrong)\n     - Something specific they care deeply about that drives their choices\n  Write the character's identity and background as a narrative, without mentioning the elements\n  Keep this information grounded, plausible and mostly true to the tropes of the setting\n  Do not include any names in Hidden Info.\n  ",
      "personality": "\n  ## Personality\n  Define personality using a comma-separated list of adjectives, descriptive phrases, and character traits\n  - Mix simple adjectives with specific descriptive phrases\n  - Include both external traits and internal motivations\n  - Include a contradiction or complexity that creates depth\n  - Never include quirks, tics or repetitive mannerisms\n  - Give them something they genuinely care about (people, places, or values)\n  - Ex. 1: \"silver-tongued manipulator, addicted to power, collector of secrets, cruel sense of humor, moral flexibility, excessive appetites, charming facade, protective of his sister\"\n  - Ex. 2: \"daring adventurer, sunshine personality, thrives on challenges, loyal to friends, lucky charm collector, infectious enthusiasm, playful teasing, secretly fears being abandoned\"\n  ",
      "faction_affiliation": "\n## Faction Affiliation\n- Faction affiliation is optional; omit if not applicable\n- Use ONLY factions provided\n- Assign a faction if the NPC's occupation, personality, values, goals align with a faction\n",
      "abilities": "## Abilities\nUse the unnamed combat-ability format defined in UNNAMED COMBAT ABILITIES. Never give an ability a name. Include only abilities useful in combat or conflict.",
      "basic_info": "\n## Basic Info\nConcise description of appearance, gender, approximate age, bearing, demeanor, etc.\n- Superficial details only\n- Evergreen (no temporary elements)\n- Consistent with Visual Description provided\n- CANNOT REFER TO THE CURRENT SITUATION OR ANY SITUATIONAL ELEMENTS (NO: \"from years in the kitchen\")\n- MUST BE PURELY EXTERIOR AND CANNOT REFER TO MENTALITY, PERSONALITY OR INTERIORITY IN ANY WAY (NO: \"masks/belies a sharp mind\", \"of someone who\")          \n- Do not include any names in Basic Info\n",
      "custom": "",
      "type_rules_in_play": "### Creature Rules From the Type\nThe supplied type description may state how this kind of creature fights, hunts, holds ground, recovers, or can be stopped. When a stated rule applies to this character, carry it into hiddenInfo in concrete terms. When it shapes an attack or another combat action, carry it into an ability string as well. Do not leave an applicable rule only in the supplied type description; later story and combat tasks receive the character’s own details.",
      "standout_anatomy": "### Standout anatomy\nWhen detailing a strong, elite, boss, or mythic combatant, give its fight a distinct shape:\n- Give it a signature attack with an explicit damage type in the ability metadata.\n- Give it one behavior the players can discover and act on, such as never leaving the water, spending its guards before itself, or recoiling from consecrated ground.\n- When the supplied type description states that it resists, ignores, or suffers certain damage, make that fact visible in its description or an ability. Do not invent damage interactions that the type description does not establish.",
      "making_the_character": "## MAKE A CHARACTER WORTH MEETING\n\nGive the NPC a life of their own and a clear place in the tale. Players should want to bargain with them, follow them, recruit them, challenge them, help them, thwart them, or meet them again.\n\nKeep every fact already established about the NPC. Build on the given idea; do not trade it for a grander one or let the latest scene swallow the whole character.\n\nAsk a simple question before saving a detail: if the present bargain, quarrel, journey, fight, or crisis ended next turn, would this still be true? If not, leave it in the story. A customer, deadline, cargo, wound, destination, argument, or one-time plea is not a lifelong identity. Turn a passing event into a recurring part of the character only when the story clearly supports it.\n\nMatch the past to the person. Champions, cursed wanderers, notorious rogues, great captains, and villains may have histories of war, treachery, doom, and glory. A cook, guard, merchant, artisan, traveler, or innkeeper usually comes from work, kin, friendship, money, pleasure, rivalry, mistakes, and opportunity. Do not hand out massacres, ruined homes, secret crimes, estranged families, hidden grief, or betrayals as common seasoning.\n\nBegin with what they want. Give important people an appetite, ambition, opinion, duty, rivalry, pleasure, cause, or grudge strong enough to move them. Let them be merry, vain, greedy, proud, rowdy, charming, selfish, tender, cruel, shy, dangerous, generous, haughty, or fearless as the part demands. Serious folk still need spark.\n\nDo not make everyone a cool, cautious professional. Anxiety, tidy rooms, polished gear, rare inks, ledgers, financial security, proving independence, and respect earned only through competence are familiar defaults, not character depth. Competence should lead somewhere visible: swagger, delight, authority, rivalry, daring, pride, showmanship, impatience, or a love of teaching.\n\nNever draw a personality from species, trade, fighting style, clothes, weapons, posture, visual description, or visual tags. Plain clothes do not make a plain soul. A steady gaze does not make someone cold. Species is anatomy and culture where established, never a ready-made temperament.\n\nDo not use comparisons to characters from other fiction. Do not invent names for new relatives, friends, rivals, employers, or other supporting people. Use established names when they truly matter.\n\nGive a faction only when the NPC's information or the story plainly establishes membership. A faction being nearby, famous, or active in the region is not enough. Otherwise return an empty faction string.",
      "what_to_write": "## WHAT TO WRITE\n\nSpend words where they create play. An important social character may need roughly 2,200-3,400 characters of hiddenInfo; a simple local or enemy needs less. If a role needs room for wares, training, work, or connections, shorten the biography. Do not repeat the same fact in basicInfo, hiddenInfo, and personality.\n\n### basicInfo\n\nThis is what the player sees. Write exactly two short paragraphs with one blank line between them. Use no headings, labels, bullets, or NPC name.\n\nThe first paragraph is one or two sentences of visible appearance: gender, species, apparent age, build, permanent face or species features, clothing in concrete material, color, and cut, and only important visible gear or ornament. Begin gendered humanoids with \"He is\" or \"She is.\" Describe what is there. No thoughts, habits, history, metaphors, similes, current actions, or details tied only to the present location. Do not add grime, stains, scars, aprons, tied-back hair, or buns unless they were already established.\n\nThe second paragraph is one or two natural sentences saying who this person is in public and why an adventurer might seek them out, avoid them, or remember them. Give a known trade, standing, specialty, access, opportunity, relationship, danger, or power. Keep it true beyond the present scene. Do not describe how they start conversations, tell the player what to feel, name an internal role, or save the current request here. For enemies and hostile creatures, state the danger, purpose, territory, or recognizable behavior instead of forcing a helpful offer.\n\n### hiddenInfo\n\nWrite the following labeled paragraphs in order. Omit a section only when it truly does not apply.\n\nPremise: One plain sentence that captures the character in play. No slogan and no comparison to another fictional character.\n\nBackground: Only the history needed to explain how they reached their present station, why they remain, and which choices or relationships shaped them. Keep ordinary lives ordinary and capable people capable.\n\nPresent life: How they live now; their work or responsibility; home or household when useful; a recognizable rhythm; one or two pleasures or interests; what they gladly spend time or coin on; and one relationship with a clear pull or tension. At least one interest should be something they can discuss, share, collect, attend, celebrate, wager on, or invite others into. Do not fall back on cleaning gear, orderly rooms, training drills, rare ink, or trivia from their trade.\n\nAgenda: One fitting thing they mean to achieve, gain, change, build, prove, enjoy, avenge, discover, protect, or become. It should matter even when the player is elsewhere and give other people a natural way into the tale. Do not turn today's errand into their life's purpose.\n\nSocial behavior: What they are like face to face. Give their diction, sentence length, directness, formality, humor, conversational pace, what fires them up or embarrasses them, how they meet strangers, and how they treat equals, underlings, and authority. Give them a recurring way of drawing others in: bargaining, recruiting, hosting, teasing, teaching, challenging, boasting, advising, competing, inviting, flattering, provoking, or applying pressure. No sample dialogue, catchphrases, repeated tics, or scripted scenes.\n\nRelationship: What wins warmth, trust, honesty, admiration, rivalry, impatience, contempt, or refusal; what they share freely; what meaningful boundary they hold; and how growing closeness can be seen. Never presume romance, attraction, approval, or party membership.\n\nRole section: Choose one role from Roles in Play and add its named section. Do not add several.\n\nCombat: Use only for someone likely to fight. Give preferred range, opening move, priorities, work with allies, appetite for risk, response to wounds or defeat, and when they escalate, retreat, surrender, or show mercy. Do not repeat the ability list. Leave this out for a true noncombatant.\n\nPut each heading once, directly before its prose. Never print a row of empty headings.",
      "roles_in_play": "## ROLES IN PLAY\n\nChoose one role from what the story plainly establishes. The role tells you where to spend detail; it does not replace the person. Do not conjure a shop, office, faction, authority, web of quests, or wish to join the party merely to fill a role. When no stronger role is clear, use Local. Add only that role's named section to hiddenInfo.\n\n### Merchant\n\nUse for a shopkeeper, trader, broker, appraiser, dealer, or craftsperson in trade. Add Trade: with all of the following:\n\n- Specialty: what they deal in and why their business stands apart.\n- Stock: three to five useful kinds of goods they commonly carry or can procure. Favor weapons, armor, potions, unguents, spell-enhancing items, enchanted clothing, magical items with clear benefits, materials for crafting or enchantment, monster parts, maps, and other goods with an obvious use. Do not lead with meals, drink, provisions, rope, common tools, or trinkets unless the story calls for them.\n- Signature offers: three representative goods that can be restocked or commissioned. Give each a proper item name, a price in florins, and a brief, clear capability, bonus, or use. Do not make a unique treasure seem forever available after sale.\n- Services: appraisal, repair, improvement, enchantment, fitting, commissions, procurement, introductions, or another useful service. Give usual prices or ranges when helpful.\n- Buys and commissions: what they pay for, what materials, monster parts, discoveries, or opportunities they seek, and representative rewards.\n- Connections: buyers, craftspeople, guides, collectors, guilds, caravans, ships, magical circles, or other access the concept supports.\n- Selling behavior: when approached, they point out one offer likely to improve the customer's gear or abilities. When asked to browse, they name their categories and show three or four concrete choices in the requested category, each with price and capability.\n\nThis is a picture of what the merchant can usually do, not an unchanging list of every item on the shelf. Give them a hunger for profit, prestige, craft, discovery, influence, or fine stock, and a recognizable way of bargaining or showing wares. Give combat little room unless it truly belongs.\n\n### Patron\n\nFor a quest-giver, guild officer, faction voice, or employer, add Work and rewards: the recurring troubles they pay others to face; coin, recognition, resources, or access they can grant; their standards; and what wins greater responsibility. Leave the current mission in the story.\n\n### Companion prospect\n\nUse only when traveling together is established or central to the concept. Add Party role: what they contribute in battle and on the road; a contained personal aim that can lead to adventure; what they begin during travel or rest; choices that draw them closer or cause friction; and deeds they will not support. Keep aims of their own and never presume romance.\n\n### Trainer or crafter\n\nAdd Training or craft: techniques, abilities, goods, or improvements they provide; prices or requirements when useful; materials, witnessed feats, achievements, or demonstrations that open stronger work; and the ambition behind their craft. The results should be clear, powerful, and worth pursuing.\n\n### Host or social anchor\n\nAdd Social opportunities: contests, feasts, introductions, rumors, gatherings, performances, recruitment, fellowship, or connections they make possible; the people drawn there; and how the NPC welcomes, excludes, or mixes them.\n\n### Fixer or contact\n\nAdd Access: information, introductions, buyers, passage, transport, discreet services, or arrangements they can secure; the coin, favor, or exchange expected; the edge of their reach; and what makes them close the door.\n\n### Authority\n\nAdd Authority: duties, resources, honors, permissions, rewards, punishments, and concrete ways they can aid or hinder action. Keep personal desire distinct from office.\n\n### Rival or threat\n\nAdd Pressure: what they seek, demand, threaten, contest, or bar; how they press others; their powers and limits; how they escalate; and what might redirect, satisfy, expose, defeat, or reconcile them.\n\n### Local\n\nAdd Local value: useful knowledge, a connection, strong opinion, pastime, modest opportunity, invitation, or small friction. Keep the scale human and do not turn every neighbor into a quest-giver.",
      "relationship_gift_ladder": "### What deepening trust unlocks\nEvery character carries material for driving story, layered by trust. When creating a character, give them:\n- A hook they'd volunteer to someone they like: a thing they've noticed, a rumor that's true, a person they could introduce on their own initiative — something that starts a scene, not a discount.\n- A situation they'd pull a friend into: trouble they're facing, an opportunity too big for them alone, a place or person they want to show off. Something with stakes for the NPC personally.\n- A guarded interior (put in hiddenInfo): a want they've never said aloud, a secret with consequences, a history that recontextualizes them, or a thing they alone could teach. This is the seed of a storyline, and deep trust is what surfaces it.\nTrust never changes what a stranger can buy, ask, or do — it changes how much of this character's story reaches the player, and how actively they bring it.",
      "personality_and_combat": "## PERSONALITY\n\nReturn 6-8 separate strings. Each should show one thing the NPC often does, wants, enjoys, resists, offers, or answers strongly. Cover different sides of the person instead of saying the same thing several ways.\n\nInclude an active social habit, a specific appetite, a pleasure beyond work, a social strength, a fault or source of friction, and a response to status, admiration, rivalry, embarrassment, temptation, or danger. At least one trait must still fit if the NPC changed trade, class, gear, and home. No more than two traits may concern competence, discipline, reliability, caution, professionalism, restraint, or calm under pressure.\n\nUse third-person behavioral prose. Avoid generic trait lists, restating the trade, exact dialogue, catchphrases, cute quirks, repeated motions, compulsions, secret-kindness twists, and claims about who the NPC is \"deep down.\"\n\n## UNNAMED COMBAT ABILITIES\n\nAbilities are actions used in a fight. Never give an ability a name or use Name: Description.\n\nUse this exact form:\n(Type: Attack; damage type: slashing; attack impact: 150) Plain prose describing the action and its effect in combat.\n\nValid Types include Attack, Defense, Control, Mobility, Support, Healing, Buff, Debuff, Summon, and Utility. Valid damage types are piercing, slashing, impact, fire, frost, shock, acid, poison, radiant, shadow, arcane, and psychic. \"Damage type: impact\" means blunt damage. \"Attack impact\" is the expected damage passed to combat resolution; it is not force, control, or dramatic intensity. Leave out attack impact when it is near the normal value of 100. Leave out both damage fields from abilities that deal no damage.\n\nGive no abilities to true noncombatants; 1-3 to ordinary people who can fight; 3-5 to trained or important combatants; 4-6 to elite or boss foes; and up to 8 only to mythic beings. When abilities are present, include at least one Type: Attack. At most one ability may use a non-attack type; all remaining abilities must be Type: Attack. If included, the non-attack ability must be a distinct combat action, not filler. Great attacks usually use attack impact 130-180. Save 200 for rare finishing moves with real setup, cost, or limits.\n\nEach ability must work in many fights, use only established anatomy, carried gear, training, or magic, and clearly state both deed and effect. Apply the empty-room test: it must still work in an empty stone chamber. Weapons, armor, carried tools, and magical focuses are fair; convenient furniture, workshop supplies, loose debris, doors, walls, and special terrain are not.\n\nDo not invent magic, divine favor, supernatural senses, equipment, or anatomy. Avoid plain attacks with no special combat effect; social talents; crafting in battle; knowledge checks; narrow escapes; moves requiring a particular enemy body; helping allies by harming or shooting them; and moves that presume monsters, crews, formations, or a certain battlefield. Abilities may set one another up, but each must also stand alone.\n\nFor beasts, mindless monsters, and constructs, replace human biography, society, relationships, and roles with origin or purpose, lair or habitat, routine, feeding or upkeep, pack or solitary ways, response to intrusion, escalation, retreat, exploitable behavior, and battle tactics. Personality may instead list distinctive recurring behaviors."
    },
    "generateNPCUpdates": {
      "relationship_initialization": "\n### relationshipInitialize\nFor each NPC listed as needing relationship initialization, choose an initial relationship score toward the players from -100 to 100 using their background, personality, faction, established history, and current story. Use 0 when neutral is appropriate.\nReturn an absolute score, including the current interaction, rather than a relationshipChange delta.\n",
      "relationship_change_updates": "### relationshipChange\nOnly when the player's actions in the current story affect this NPC, in the present, with real substance.\n\nPositive changes (+3 to +15):\n- Substantive help: solving a real problem, defending them, a gift that fits who they are.\n- Shared danger: fighting alongside the NPC, or completing hard work together — companions and allies present through a fight or a quest's completion gain, not only the quest-giver.\n- Saving them: rescue from death, capture, or ruin sits at the top of the range (+10 to +15).\n- A promise kept counts; a promise made does not.\n- Ignore compliments, flattery, purchases at ordinary prices, and empty gestures.\n\nNegative changes (-1 to -10):\n- Direct harm, theft, betrayal, humiliation, a promise broken.\n- Endangering them through recklessness they didn't consent to.\nDo not raise for mere presence, politeness, or routine transactions. Do not lower for disagreement, hard bargaining, or refusing a request.",
      "party_management": "\n### Party Management\n- \"Party\" is an official state for NPCs who are accompanying the player group on an ongoing undertaking, remaining physically present with them for extended travel, adventuring, investigation, work, or danger.\n- characterName must match an existing NPC in Scene NPCs or Recently Nearby NPCs\n- Reserve add for cases where the NPC in question is not already in 'Party Members', and the current story explicitly shows mutual agreement that the NPC will accompany the players' group:\n  - the NPC asks and the player or players agree, or\n  - the player or players ask and the NPC agrees\n- The agreement must mean the NPC will come with the players or stay with the group beyond the immediate scene. Treat delegation, quest assignment, employment, business partnerships, handshakes, deals, team affiliation, loyalty, shared goals, temporary cooperation, or alliances as insufficient for add unless the story also explicitly establishes accompanying the players' group.\n- Use remove when the story explicitly shows that either the NPC or the player wants to split up or stop accompanying the players' group\n- When no NPC joins or leaves the party, \"partyChanges\" should be an empty array\n",
      "custom": "## Before Adding an NPC to the Party\n\nBefore outputting a `partyChanges` entry with `\"changeType\": \"add\"`, confirm all of these are true:\n\n1. The NPC is an existing character in `Scene NPCs`.\n2. The story explicitly says the NPC will accompany the player group beyond the immediate scene.\n3. One side has asked or offered:\n   - The NPC has asked/offered to join the party, travel with the group, or accompany the players.\n   - A player has asked/invited the NPC to join the party, travel with the group, or accompany them.\n4. The other side has clearly accepted.\n5. The agreement is about ongoing accompaniment, not just temporary help, an alliance, employment, gratitude, shared goals, or cooperation in the current scene.\n\nIf any item above is missing or ambiguous, do not add the NPC.\n\n## Blood Dancer Bleeding\n\nWhen the current story explicitly narrates the one follow-up pulse of fresh harm from a capital-B Bleeding effect on a current Scene NPC, output one damage update equal to 5% of that NPC's maximum health, rounded to the nearest whole point, with source `blood loss`. Apply this once per target per application. Do not apply it to the initial attack, ordinary descriptive bleeding, a wound that was closed or spent through Second Cut, Pass the Pulse, or The Red Feast, or a continuing description without a distinct new pulse.",
      "ranger_beast_companions": "## Ranger Beast Companions\n\nWhen the story establishes that a Ranger has tamed or bonded with an existing animal or animal-minded NPC as a Beast Companion, add it to the party. A Ranger may normally have one active Beast Companion. Do not add another beyond that limit unless a progression trait on that Ranger explicitly raises it. A Beast Companion remains an NPC and follows its established nature."
    },
    "generateLocationDetails": {
      "custom": "## QUEST-ORIGIN LOCATIONS\n\nWhen a Quest Design Brief is provided, this location was created to support that quest. Preserve the brief's playable obstacle exactly and translate it into concrete facts the story can stage. The brief decides the challenge; do not replace it with atmosphere or optional lore, and do not expand it into a larger threat.\n\nFor quest-origin locations, the normal instruction not to mention current events does not apply to the one-time encounter created for the source quest.\n\nWrite the location's normal hiddenInfo first: enduring inhabitants, history, tensions, and problems that remain true beyond this quest. Then append the following compact block inside the same hiddenInfo string:\n\n[ONE-TIME QUEST ENCOUNTER]\nActive while: State the condition under which this quest encounter remains unresolved.\n- <Area name>: State what is present there now, what triggers it to act, what it does when triggered, and what progress it prevents.\n- Add another area only when the Quest Design Brief requires another distinct obstacle.\nProgress gate: State what objective or access remains impossible while the obstacle is unresolved.\nPersistence: Once resolved in the story, do not respawn, replace, or repeat this encounter.\n\nMake encounter facts decisive and operational. State what is actually present now, not what may appear or what kinds of danger tend to occur. Avoid `may`, `might`, `could`, `sometimes`, `tends to`, and unresolved alternatives about the encounter's contents or behavior.\n\nUse exact generated area names. Put each obstacle on the route to or at the objective so it can actually block progress. Preserve a named quest NPC only when the Quest Design Brief establishes that specific person; otherwise describe generic opposition by kind and do not invent a named character.\n\nThe block is narrator-only operational truth. It must take effect when its stated trigger occurs; do not treat it merely as a secret to foreshadow or reveal gradually. Allow any plausible player solution consistent with the fiction, but do not narrate the blocked progress or objective as completed while the obstacle remains effective."
    },
