    "generateEncounters": {
      "custom": "In departure from the standard encounter instructions, all encounters should have a strong element of opportunity and should not force the player characters to engage with them. For example, an ambush of the players is a danger, not an opportunity, and forces the players to engage with it, but chancing upon someone being ambushed, without having been seen yet, is an opportunity that the player is not forced to engage with."
    },
    "ItemGenerationAndUsage": {
      "custom": "## Item Handling Guidance\n\nOnly update inventory when an item changes possession or is transformed.\n\n### No Update For\n- Items seen, mentioned, inspected, offered, priced, displayed, nearby, or placed on a counter\n- Storage/reorganization: putting items in bags, packs, pouches, pockets, sheaths, or containers\n- NPC-only inventory changes, NPC-to-NPC trades, or unconfirmed offers/negotiations\n- Combat flavor events (dents, hits)\n\n### Add / Remove Rules\nADD when an item enters a player character's possession: taken, picked up, looted, received, crafted, purchased, or handed to them\n\nREMOVE when an item leaves a player character's possession: spent, consumed, handed over, discarded, destroyed, stolen, or sacrificed\n\nSearching or looting implies taking found items\n\nQuantities must match what changed\n\n### Transactions\nA purchase requires a stated price and player confirmation after the price has been stated\n\nIn completed purchases, remove currency AND add the purchased goods\n\nIn completed sales, remove sold goods AND add received payment\n\n### Consumables and Combat Damage\nConsumables and ammunition are removed when used: potions, scrolls, arrows, thrown weapons, etc.\n\nNormal combat does not transform or remove equipped weapons or armor. Preserve gear unless the story narrates clear loss or destruction:\n- A successful attempt to destroy the item\n- Acid, lava or similar destructive force\n- The player sacrifices or abandons it\n- The story explicitly says it is broken or lost\n\nDo not infer loss or transformation. When uncertain, do not remove or transform the item.",
      "spell_scrolls": "## Spell Scrolls\nA spell scroll is a Consumable document, slot Consumable, bonuses: []. Name it Scroll of [Spell]; state its exact spell, damage type if any, and that one attempt consumes it. Add one only when the story puts it in inventory or Action Results says Spell in Waiting succeeded; then remove one Prepared Vellum. On any release attempt remove one scroll. Notes, grimoires, and ordinary written spells are not scrolls.",
      "clerical_holy_symbols_and_reliquaries": "## Clerical Holy Symbols and Reliquaries\n\nHoly symbols and reliquaries are Accessory foci. When defining one named or transferred by the story, preserve its established faith, physical form, sacred contents, and history in its name and description. Apply the ordinary naming, quality, bonus, and Gear Balance rules. A reliquary may support healing magic, divine magic, or another function established by the story. The word \"reliquary\" does not by itself make an item Relic quality.",
      "item_creation": "## Item Creation Rules\n\nUse these rules when defining a new item, or when an item is transformed, upgraded, identified, crafted, purchased, or found as new loot\nPreserve exact names for existing known items unless the story clearly changes the item\n\n### Naming Convention\n\nItem names follow this pattern:\n\n[Quality] [Material] [Item Type] of [Damage Type]\n\nEach element is omitted when it matches the default:\n\n- Quality — omitted for mundane/common items.\n- Material — omitted when the material is what you'd expect for that item and of normal type (wooden bow)\n- of [Damage Type] — omitted when the damage type is the default for the item's shape. Swords default to slashing, spears to piercing, hammers to impact, bows to piercing. If the damage type is anything other than the shape default — whether from exotic material, enchantment, or any other source — the suffix must appear.\n\nExamples, all of which exist in this world:\n\n- Fine Steel Shortsword\n- Steel Shortsword of Flame\n- Voidglass Knife of Shadow\n- Moonstone Dagger of Radiant\n- Fine Stormsteel Mace of Shock\n- Masterwork Emberglass Saber of Fire\n- Superior Frostjade Warhammer of Frost\n- Masterwork Voidglass Greatsword of Shadow\n- Warded Sun-Gold Blade of Radiant\n\nThe rule is simple: if the damage type is not what the weapon's shape alone would deal, the suffix always appears.\n\n### Quality Tiers\n\nQuality reflects how esteemed, refined, and capable the item is. Infer from context.\n\n- (mundane, no prefix) — ordinary shop goods, basic loot, cheap materials. No bonus.\n- Fine — well-made, above-average craft, specialty shop. One small bonus: +1 attribute, +1 skill, or +3 to +5 resource.\n- Superior — named smith, minor enchantment, aspected material, dungeon loot. One meaningful bonus: +1 attribute, +2 skill, or +3 to +5 resource.\n- Masterwork — major quest reward, deep dungeon treasure, or the work of a renowned master. For weapons, this is the first tier above the ordinary shape bands. One strong bonus or two moderate bonuses, with resource bonuses at +10 to +15.\n- Legendary is not a usable quality prefix.\n- Relic (proper name, no quality prefix) — an individual item, unique within the world, with established provenance. It is earned only through a completed undertaking ending in a binding rite; never generate one as ordinary loot, shop stock, improvised payment, or a casual reward.\n\nFor weapons, use the shape-and-tier damage table in Gear Balance. Fine and Superior remain within the ordinary weapon band.\n\nWhen uncertain, choose the lower quality.\n\nUse only valid bonus types: attribute, resource, skill, stat: damage, stat: armor. Do not invent other mechanics.\n\nA skill bonus belongs on gear whose whole point is one specialisation, above all caster foci: a wand, reliquary, sealstone, or seedpod carries +1 to its own school alongside its attunement bonus. Do not put a skill bonus on a weapon. Weapon power is the damage stat, because a weapon-skill bonus of +1 to +3 moves mean damage by only a few percent and is not perceptible in play.\n\n### Damage Types\n\nA damage type is free. An \"of Flame\" suffix changes the damage type only and does not consume any part of the quality budget.\n\nUse only these damage types:\n\npiercing, slashing, impact, fire, frost, shock, acid, poison, radiant, shadow, arcane, psychic\n\n### Material Table\n\nDefault materials are not stated in the name. Exotic materials appear in the name.\n\nExotic (include in name, always paired with \"of [Damage Type]\" suffix):\n\nsilver, moonstone, sun-gold → radiant\nstormsteel, thunderglass → shock\nemberglass, red jade → fire\nfrostjade, rimebone → frost\nobsidian, voidglass, black iron → shadow\nwitchwood, aether crystal → arcane\ndream coral, thoughtstone → psychic\nvenomwood, green glass → poison\ncaustic bronze, alchemist glass → acid\n\n### Descriptions\n\nDescriptions should be 1-2 sentences. Reinforce the damage type in plain language. May name enemy families the item is especially effective against.\n\n\"A moonstone blade favored by temple duelists. Its radiant edge bites hardest into undead, demons, and shadow-bound things.\"\n\n### Spirit Court Tokens\n\nA Spirit Court token is a physical sign of an established bond between a shaman and a named spirit; it does not contain or control the spirit. Create it as a Tool with no bonuses and no equipment slot. Give each token a concrete name and physical form instead of using \"Spirit Token\" as its item name, and describe the spirit and bond as established by the story.\n\n### Paladin Vow Foci\n\nOnly after story establishes a fulfilled vow, prayer, and patron's answer, create its Vow Focus as an Accessory with slot Accessory and a form and name tied to the deed. Give it one bonus: health +15; stamina or mana +10; or sword, leadership, or divine channeling +2. State the patron, deed, divine aspect, and damage type. A later greater blessing may improve it within the quality budget.",
      "gear_balance": "## Gear Balance\n\n### Armor, Penalties and Caster Gear\n\nArmor values follow these ordinary bands. The figures are for a whole kit without a shield; shields add 60 to 100 armor according to their coverage.\n\n- Light kit, about 105 total: no attribute penalty. Leather and cloth.\n- Medium kit, about 170 to 195 total: about -2 attunement and -1 dexterity across the kit. Hardened leather, scale, and brigandine.\n- Heavy kit, about 350 to 365 total: about -5 attunement, -2 dexterity, and -5 maximum stamina across body and legs. Iron and steel plate.\n- Iron Buckler: 60 armor, no penalty.\n- Wooden Round Shield: 75 armor and -1 attunement.\n- Kite Shield: 90 armor and -1 attunement.\n- Tower Shield: 100 armor, -2 attunement, and -1 dexterity.\n\nHeavy body armor carries about -3 attunement, -1 dexterity, and -5 maximum stamina. Heavy leg armor carries about -2 attunement and -1 dexterity. Head, hands, and feet carry no attribute penalty at these armor values.\n\nPenalties come from mass and material, never from quality. A Masterwork breastplate is still plate and keeps the full penalty. Quality buys bonuses; it does not buy the penalty away. Light exotic metals are the only possible exception, and only at Masterwork or above.\n\nCaster gear carries +1 or +2 attunement: staves, wands, robes, circlets, foci, and reliquaries. Metal armor never carries an attunement bonus, so robes against plate stays a real choice. Battlemage gear is medium protection carrying no attunement penalty at all, and belongs at Superior quality or above. Superior Warded Mail and Masterwork Runed Brigandine are the two that exist.\n\n### Weapons\n\nWeapon power is the damage stat. Each point increases damage dealt with that weapon by 10%.\n\n- Light weapons — daggers, short spears, short bows, thrown weapons, and light crossbows.\n- Standard weapons — shortswords, maces, sabers, rapiers, flails, and longbows.\n- Heavy weapons — battle axes, warhammers, mauls, polearms, war bows, and ordinary greatswords.\n- Caster foci — staves and wands. They carry no damage stat and do not climb the weapon-damage tiers.\n\nWeapon damage follows both shape and tier:\n- Mundane, Fine, or Superior: light 0, standard +1, heavy +2, superheavy +3.\n- Masterwork: light +1, standard +2, heavy +3, superheavy +4.\n- Warded: light +2, standard +3, heavy +4, superheavy +5.\n- Relic: light +3, standard +4, heavy +5, superheavy +6.\n\nFine and Superior describe workmanship within the ordinary weapon band; they do not raise a weapon above the damage appropriate to its shape.\n\nMasterwork weapons require exceptional materials worked with master-level craft at a proper forge. Most are commissioned from a named master who knows the party or has been won over through play; a player smith may attempt the same work at the difficulty stated in Action Info. Warded weapons require a Masterwork weapon, a rare catalyst carrying suitable power, and an enchanter's rite played as a scene rather than a purchase. A Relic is proper-named, unique within the world, and granted only through a completed undertaking and binding rite; never improvise one as ordinary loot, stock, or payment.\n\nA cursed weapon may carry one damage point above the value for its shape and tier. A weapon cannot receive this advantage from both being cursed and being superheavy. Nothing exceeds +6 damage.\n\nQuality does not remove a weapon's ordinary handling penalties, and advancing a tier does not automatically make those penalties harsher. Penalties come from shape, material, or curse.\n\n### Gadget Weapons\nGadget weapons — spring-arms, wound wheels, sparkers, and other made weapons wielded with the gadgetry skill — are ordinary Weapons and follow the ordinary weapon bands and penalties. Each states its damage type plainly in its description; a typed gadget may take the of [Damage Type] suffix. A gadget weapon is durable and is not consumed by its attack — a device whose description says the attack consumes it is a single-use attack device, not a gadget weapon.\n\n### Superheavy Weapons\n\nSuperheavy weapons are the two-handed monsters: great mauls, executioner's axes, shoulder-swung greatswords, and siege hammers. They carry one more damage than a heavy weapon of the same tier.\n\nA superheavy weapon carries the normal heavy-weapon penalties of -1 attunement and -1 dexterity, plus -10 maximum stamina. These penalties do not increase at Warded or Relic tier.\n\nDescribe the weapon's actual handling burden with one or two concrete details suited to its shape, such as slow recovery after a committed swing, difficulty bringing it around in close quarters, or being impossible to draw quickly. Do not claim that it mechanically prevents the use of a shield.",
      "armor_vertical": "## Heavy Armor Vertical\n\nThese values govern the heavy-protection line: plate and equivalent armor intended to build toward maximum protection. They do not turn leather, robes, battlemage gear, or utility clothing into heavy armor.\n\nUse these slot values when defining or upgrading one heavy-armor piece:\n- Ordinary: body 140, legs 80, head 60, hands 30, feet 30, outerwear 25. Complete kit: 365 without a shield.\n- Masterwork: body 195, legs 105, head 75, hands 40, feet 35, outerwear 30. Complete kit: 480 without a shield.\n- Warded: body 250, legs 135, head 90, hands 50, feet 45, outerwear 55. Complete kit: 625 without a shield.\n- Relic: a completed non-shield kit may reach 750. An eventual proper-named Relic shield may bring the finished build to the armor cap of 900. Relic generation is governed separately.\n\nMasterwork armor requires exceptional materials from strong or elite opposition, a proper workshop, and a named master smith who knows the party or has been won over through play. It is commissioned or earned, never ordinary stock.\n\nWarded armor begins as a Masterwork piece. Raising it requires a rare catalyst carrying suitable power, a capable enchanter, and a staged rite. It cannot be bought ready-made or improvised through casual spellcasting.\n\nTier does not automatically increase an armor piece's penalties. Penalties come from its mass, coverage, and material. Body and legs carry the heavy kit's attribute and resource penalties. Head, hands, and feet carry no attribute penalties at these values.\n\nBattlemage gear remains a separate medium-protection line with no attunement penalty. Superior Warded Mail and Masterwork Runed Brigandine remain valid examples and do not receive heavy-armor tier values.",
      "cursed_gear": "## Cursed Gear\n\nA cursed weapon may carry one damage point above the value for its shape and tier only when it also carries at least -25 health and all ordinary weapon penalties. It cannot also receive the superheavy increase, and nothing exceeds +6 damage.\n\nGive a cursed item a three-to-four-sentence description:\n1. Establish its physical form and a concrete origin connected to the world.\n2. Describe how its equipped boon and penalty appear on the bearer.\n3. Give it one named power with an immediate, exceptional, stageable effect.\n4. State the repeatable physical manifestation of that power and the concrete appetite, loyalty, cycle, or bodily price revealed through it.\n\nThe named power should resolve in the current action or turn.",
      "blueprints_and_components": "## Blueprints and Components\n\nBlueprints use category Tool, no equipped slot, and bonuses: []. A blueprint names one exact output and states the minimum skill, toolkit, ordinary components, and any special material required. It is retained after construction.\n\nOnly when Action Results says construction succeeds and the player carries the blueprint and stated materials, remove one of each stated consumable material and add one output item. Retain the blueprint and toolkit. A failed attempt retains its materials unless Action Results explicitly says something was destroyed. Never infer missing supplies.\n\nBlueprints may be found, bought, reverse-engineered, or drafted. Drafting an original blueprint is an attempt with the relevant craft skill and must describe a plausible design within the character's established knowledge. A blueprint records a design; it does not supply materials or make an impossible device possible.\n\nMechanical Components are category Consumable, slot Consumable, and bonuses: []. They supply the ordinary physical workings of traps and modest mechanisms but never replace a special payload, rare material, or aspected material.\n\nOn a successful use of Everything Has Parts against an established broken mundane item or substantial heap of discarded manufactured scrap, add exactly one Mechanical Components. If the source was a named carried item, remove one of that item; otherwise the story must establish the scene source being expended. Without a valid source, add nothing.\n\nTyped traps and single-use attack devices use category Consumable, slot Consumable, and bonuses: []. A typed example takes the of [Damage Type] suffix and describes the concrete mechanism or material that produces that damage. A trap states what triggers it and that it strikes once before being spent; an attack device states that the attack consumes it. The authored examples are retrieval anchors, not a closed list.",
      "device_ability_inventory": "## Device Ability Inventory\nWhen Teeth Underfoot is actually performed, remove exactly one named carried trap whether its attack hits or misses.\n\nWhen Between Two Footfalls actually constructs and springs a trap, remove the complete materials named by its carried blueprint, retain the blueprint and toolkit, and do not add the finished trap to inventory because it is spent in the same action. If construction never occurs, retain the materials.\n\nWhen The Third Measure is actually performed, remove exactly one named input consumable, never one per target. If the ability is impossible or the input is never used, retain it.\n\nOne More Working uses remnants already spent earlier in the scene and causes no inventory update. The Room Was Ready likewise causes no new inventory removal when its trap was already spent from inventory during preparation.",
      "item_supply_balance": "## Item Supply Balance\n\n### Potions\n\nRestorative potions use these families and values. State the value in the item description as well as the name.\n\n- Stamina Draught: +15 stamina. Greater Stamina Draught: +30 stamina.\n- Mana Philter: +15 mana. Greater Mana Philter: +30 mana.\n- Healing Potion: +30 to +40 health.\n\nFor a restorative with no family name, estimate a lower value.\n\n### Loot Can Answer Damage Walls\n\nWhen the story establishes loot, a commission, or merchant stock meant to answer a resistance or immunity the party has encountered, define it as a concrete key to that wall—especially a weapon with an explicit of [Damage Type] suffix. Strong, elite, and boss trophies may be defined as materials suitable for crafting or trade toward such gear. Do not invent a drop merely because a wall was encountered; define only items the story actually awards, offers, or establishes.\n\n### Typed Ammunition, Weapon Oils, and Unguents\n\nTyped ammunition uses category Consumable, slot Consumable, and bonuses: []. It may use an exotic material and takes an of [Damage Type] suffix, using of Radiance for radiant ammunition. The suffix is the entire mechanic.\n\nWeapon oils, venoms, etchants, extracts, and unguents use category Consumable, slot Consumable, and bonuses: []. They can be used to coat a plain weapon and give it an exotic damage type for one attack. Never give one an of [Damage Type] suffix; name it for its substance and state its damage type plainly in the description. When one is used to coat a weapon, one unit is consumed whether the attack hits or misses, and its effect applies only to that attack.\n\n### Protective Ward Charms\n\nProtective ward charms use category Consumable, slot Consumable, and bonuses: []. Use only the four existing types: Ember Charm for fire, Rime Charm for frost, Dawn Charm for shadow, and Bitterleaf Charm for poison.\n\nEach charm raises one personal ward and is consumed when used. These charms are rite-made and scarce, never routine low-cost stock. When offered for sale, they cost 120 to 200 florins. Do not create charms for other damage types without a matching ward trait and trigger system."
    },
    "summarization": {
      "custom": "Write a concise, factual summary of the supplied story. Summarize important events, consequences, relationships, established motives, feelings, knowledge, and unresolved matters. Record these only when the story establishes them; never invent or infer them.\n\nRecord concrete social outcomes: agreed prices and terms, debts, promises and oaths, names and aliases, secrets and lies, gifts, rescues, thefts, insults, and changes in allegiance or standing. For a secret or lie, record who said what to whom; call it false only when the story establishes that. Preserve exact wording only for oaths, passwords, riddles, and terms likely to be checked later. Paraphrase everything else.\n\nRecord questions, goals, vows, doubts, warnings, and expectations only when a named character expresses them through dialogue or action. For anything not witnessed by everyone, name the audience: “alone with the smith,” “overheard by one guard,” or “unseen.” If the audience is unclear, do not guess.\n\nWhen condensing summaries:\n\n- Preserve deals, debts, oaths, names and aliases, unanswered questions, vows and goals, and events that change standing.\n- If a later input explicitly resolves an earlier question, vow, debt, or threat, replace the earlier item with the resolved outcome.\n- Keep the latest state once. Cut travel, description, repetition, and fights with no lasting consequence.",
      "wizard_spell_research": "## Wizard Spell Research\n\nWhen a Wizard explicitly records or chooses to pursue a magical principle they successfully identified, preserve the source, the specific usable principle, and what the character understood. Preserve a declared unfinished spell experiment until it succeeds, fails decisively, or is abandoned. When The Unwritten Spell successfully produces a learned spell, replace the open experiment with the completed spell's name and effect. Do not record research merely because the character saw unfamiliar magic.",
      "sorcerous_awakenings": "## Sorcerous Origins and Awakened Powers\n\nFor each Sorcerer, preserve the origin of their inborn magic when established. If it remains unknown, do not invent an answer. Preserve a recurring visible tell only when background or story establishes it as recurring. When No One Taught You This fully succeeds, retain the learned power's name and effect. Do not record ordinary spellcasting or one-time visual details.",
      "warlock_pact_clauses": "## Warlock Pacts and Clauses\n\nFor each Warlock, preserve the patron or answering form, established sign and means of contact, the standing pact's original exchange, accepted clauses and their exact prices, limits, or services, unaccepted offers clearly marked as offers, outstanding obligations, completed or broken terms and their established consequences, concrete leverage, and powers actually granted. Never turn a suggestion, threat, implication, or unanswered demand into an accepted obligation. When a clause is resolved, replace the open term with its outcome but preserve any lasting power or consequence. Do not record every ordinary use of pact power."
    },
    "generateLearnedAbilities": {
      "custom": "# Do not generate an ability when:\n\n- The character used an existing skill or ability\n- The character fought well in combat\n- The character did something they already have the skills to do\n- The character combined two things they can already do\n- The character observed, studied, read, or watched someone else do something\n- The proposed ability is a narrower or situational version of something in worldAbilityNames or existingAbilityNames - check the lists\n- The character had a conversation, made a plan, gave a speech, told a story, remembered something, or had an emotional moment\n- The character picked up, bought, found, or was given an item. Items are not abilities. Do not name abilities after items\n- The character simply used magic.  These are skill uses, not new abilities, unless the story describes a fundamentally new magical technique being invented or bestowed.\n- The event was routine, expected, or something the character has done before. Abilities come from singular breakthroughs, not repeated competence.\n\n# Only propose an ability when:\n- the story contains a clear, specific, unrepeatable moment: \n  - direct supernatural transformation\n  - a teacher completing instruction in a named technique\n  - absorption of power from an artifact or entity\n  - or a genuine first-time improvisation that produced a result the character's existing skills cannot explain.\n\n# How to Name Abilities\n- Give abilities lore- and setting appropriate fantasy names. Do NOT use technical, engineering, science, or modern terminology unless the story expressly supports it.\n\nRemember:\nWhen in doubt, return an empty list. Most turns should return an empty list. Never duplicate existing stills or abilities.",
      "psionic_techniques": "## Psionic Techniques\n\nWhen a character with the Psionicist trait explicitly uses the available Open the Mind ability and the current story shows the first fully successful performance of a new psionic technique drawn from something witnessed, studied, or practiced, propose exactly one learned ability preserving that technique. This is the narrow exception to the general prohibitions on generating an ability from deliberate study or the use of an existing skill or ability.\n\nPreserve only the technique the story shows succeeding. Give it a setting-appropriate fantasy name and describe what the character can do. Do not broaden its targets, scale, duration, or uses beyond the successful attempt; match its power to the character's current level and psionics skill. Direct mental attacks deal psychic damage; physical attacks use the damage type of the object or force. Open the Mind does not grant permission to perform feats that require a separate explicit license.\n\nDo not generate a technique for observation or study alone, a failed or mixed attempt, ordinary psionics use, or repeating a known technique. Reject duplicates of authored or already learned abilities, narrower variants, pure increases in force, combinations with no genuinely new result, and techniques whose purpose is to create or modify abilities.",
      "wizard_spell_design": "## Wizard Spell Design\n\nWhen a character with the Wizard trait explicitly uses the available The Unwritten Spell ability and the current story shows the first fully successful casting of a spell they deliberately constructed from a specific magical principle established through prior study, propose exactly one learned ability that preserves that spell. This is the narrow exception to the general prohibitions on generating an ability from deliberate study or the use of an existing ability.\n\nPreserve only the concrete spell the story shows succeeding. Give it a setting-appropriate fantasy name and describe its visible immediate effect. If it attacks, state one allowed damage type supported by the completed spell. Do not broaden its targets, scale, duration, or uses beyond the successful casting; match its power to the character's current level and magical skill.\n\nDo not generate a spell for witnessing magic, identifying a principle, finding or reading a formula, planning an experiment, a failed or mixed casting, or an ordinary use of a magic skill. Reject duplicates, narrower variants, pure increases in force, combinations with no genuinely new result, and spells whose purpose is to create or modify abilities. Never copy power that depends on another class's patron, spirit, divinity, bloodline, bodily discipline, item, or relationship; preserve only a formal magical principle the Wizard actually turned into a successful spell.",
      "sorcerous_awakening": "## Sorcerous Awakening\n\nWhen a character with the Sorcerer trait explicitly uses the available No One Taught You This ability and the current story shows the first fully successful use of a magical power they have never used before, propose exactly one learned ability that preserves that power. This is the narrow exception to the general prohibitions on generating an ability from the use of an existing skill or ability.\n\nPreserve only the concrete power the story shows succeeding. Give it a setting-appropriate fantasy name and describe its visible immediate effect. It may resemble other magic, but it must arise from the character's own inborn power rather than being learned by imitation. If it attacks, state one allowed damage type supported by the successful use. Do not broaden its targets, scale, duration, or uses beyond what succeeded; match its power to the character's current level and the existing magic skill that governs the effect.\n\nDo not generate an awakened power for ordinary spellcasting, a failed or mixed attempt, or merely witnessing or studying outside magic. Reject duplicates, narrower variants, pure increases in force, attempts to reproduce another class's patron, spirit, divinity, bodily discipline, or item-bound power, and powers whose purpose is to create or modify abilities.",
      "warlock_pact_clauses": "## Warlock Pact Clauses\n\nWhen a character with the Warlock trait explicitly accepts a new pact clause and the current story shows the patron visibly acknowledging the agreement and actually granting a distinct power, propose exactly one learned ability that preserves the grant. The power may be granted at once against an accepted obligation or after an agreed service is complete, but acceptance or completed service without the patron's actual grant is not enough.\n\nPreserve only the immediate power the story shows being granted, in the patron's established nature and style. Give it a setting-appropriate fantasy name and describe its visible effect. If it attacks, state one allowed damage type supported by the patron and the grant. Do not broaden its targets, scale, duration, or uses beyond the agreed clause; match its power to the character's current level and relevant existing magic skill. Ordinary use of the learned power carries no fresh price unless the agreed clause explicitly established one.\n\nDo not generate an ability for an offer, negotiation, omen, threat, unanswered demand, price paid without a grant, service completed without a grant, ordinary use of existing pact power, possession of a token, or power merely witnessed in a patron, servant, or enemy. Reject duplicates, narrower variants, pure increases in force, and grants whose purpose is to create or modify abilities.",
      "monastic_forms": "## Monastic Forms\n\nWhen a character with the Monk trait completes a teacher's instruction in a distinct Form, or achieves a first-time feat of bodily command during a consequential ordeal, propose exactly one learned ability that preserves the perfected feat. Prefix its name with \"Form: \". The Monk trait is a broad license to attempt the impossible; do not treat a specific perfected Form as a duplicate of that license.\n\nA Form is one immediate technique performed through the monk's own body. Give it a concrete fantasy name and describe what visibly obeys: weight leaves or gathers, flesh or bone hardens or loosens, sight yields to hearing or touch, breath or blood stills, or movements fall into exact measure. It may reach through a held object or another body only when the completed instruction or breakthrough explicitly established that reach. An attack deals impact damage unless the story established another allowed damage type.\n\nMatch the Form's reach to the character's current Will rank. With no Will rank or at Novice, it commands one part or property of the monk's own body for one immediate feat. An Adept may join two bodily commands in one sequence or carry one through a held object. An Expert may sustain a deeper command through extreme harm or opposition. A Master may combine several commands or reach another living body by touch. A specific authored ability may establish a narrower exception.\n\nDo not generate a Form for ordinary success, repetition, observation alone, practice without stakes, merely finding, buying, or reading a teaching, or merely combining actions already known.",
      "ranger_named_shots": "## Ranger Named Shots\n\nWhen a Ranger first fully succeeds at a deliberately attempted archery feat under genuine danger that changes a shot's path or timing beyond ordinary archery, propose exactly one learned ability preserving that exact Shot. Completed instruction in a named Shot may do the same. This is a narrow exception to the general prohibition on generating abilities from use of an existing skill.\n\nDo not generate one for ordinary accuracy, range, damage, multiple targets, called shots, critical hits, or use of an existing ability. Later breakthroughs may become more ambitious as the Ranger's level rises, but each Shot remains one immediate technique.",
      "berserker_fury_deeds": "## Berserker Fury Deeds\n\nWhen a character with the Berserker trait currently has Fury, Deep Fury, or Unbroken Fury and first fully succeeds under genuine danger at a deliberately attempted immediate feat of strength, endurance, or momentum beyond ordinary mortal ability, propose exactly one learned ability preserving that Fury Deed. Completed instruction in a named Fury Deed may establish the attempt, but propose the ability only after the character performs it successfully while Fury is active. This is a narrow exception to the general prohibitions on generating abilities from physical skill use or fighting well.\n\nPreserve the concrete feat the story shows succeeding. Give it a setting-appropriate fantasy name and describe one visible, immediate act of physical discipline. It may break, lift, throw, carry, withstand, or drive through one concrete opponent, obstacle, or force. It is never magic, a passive benefit, or a sustained state. If it attacks, use the held weapon's damage type, or impact when no weapon determines one. Do not broaden the feat beyond the successful deed; match its scale to the character's current level and the Fury tier that carried it.\n\nDo not generate a Fury Deed for an ordinary attack, high damage, taking damage, merely surviving a fight, entering or ending Fury, passive endurance, a feat already inside an existing skill's ordinary ladder, or success produced by an existing ability. In particular, use of Break the Line, Hurl the Unliftable, Worldbreaker, or another existing ability never generates a new Deed. Reject duplicates, narrower variants, and pure increases in force.",
      "druid_remembered_shapes": "## Druid Remembered Shapes\n\nWhen a character with the Druid trait explicitly uses the available No Shape Forgotten ability and the current story shows their first fully successful transformation into a specific living creature they previously pursued, tended, faced in danger, or closely observed, propose exactly one learned ability preserving that named shape. This is the narrow exception to the general prohibitions on generating abilities from observation, shape shifting, transformation, or the use of an existing ability.\n\nPrefix the ability name with \"Shape: \". Preserve only the whole body the story shows the Druid successfully taking. Describe its visible form and only the size, movement, senses, natural weapons, and bodily capabilities established by that transformation. The learned ability is a reliable return to that shape, not permission to separate its features into unrelated powers. If the shape attacks, state one allowed damage type supported by its body. Match its scale and capabilities to the character's current level, shape shifting skill, and the successful transformation.\n\nThe shape never grants the creature's memories, knowledge, learned skills, equipment, social identity, authority, spellcasting, or a power the transformed body did not visibly demonstrate. Preserve a supernatural bodily feature only when the successful transformation itself displayed it; never infer the creature's entire NPC or type description. The ordinary limitations in the shape shifting skill still apply while wearing the form.\n\nDo not generate a shape for observation or study alone, finding a corpse or body part, a failed or mixed transformation, ordinary shape shifting without No Shape Forgotten, taking a familiar or already learned shape, or using Greatshape, Wear the Wild, or another existing ability. Reject duplicates and narrower variants. Propose only one learned shape even when the scene contains several creatures or transformations."
    },
    "generateNPCIntent": {
      "custom": "# LARION — PLAYING A SINGLE CHARACTER\n\nAdapted from the narrator's out-of-combat NPC direction. That guidance told the narrator how to\npuppet people; this task hands the job to the person themselves.\n\n## WHAT YOU WANT RIGHT NOW\n\nBefore anything else, know what this character is after in this moment: to finish work, protect\ntheir standing, learn something, make a sale, avoid embarrassment, enjoy themselves, preserve a\nsecret, pursue an attraction, escape an obligation, or be left alone.\n\nResponding to the party is part of your situation, not the whole of your existence. Let what you\nwant shape what you say and do — including wanting them gone.\n\n## DO SOMETHING WHILE YOU TALK\n\nYou are in the middle of a life. Work, eat, drink, pack, mend, count, shop, handle stock, attend\nto someone, keep an eye on a door. Talking is rarely the only thing happening.\n\nVary it. Do not repeat the same piece of business every turn.\n\nIf this character has already delayed a promised arrival, finish it now instead of asking the player to wait again.\n\n## STRANGERS ARE NOT THREATS BY DEFAULT\n\nDo not open suspicious or hostile toward people who have done nothing. Warmth, curiosity, shyness,\nenthusiasm, attraction, awkwardness, vanity, greed, boredom, cruelty and humour are all valid\nfirst reactions. Choose the one this character would actually have.\n\nHeld hostility is different: if you already have a reason to distrust these people, keep it.\n\n## ANSWER THE MOVE THEY ACTUALLY MADE\n\nMeet humour, flirtation, kindness, rudeness, threats and disrespect the way this character would\nmeet them. A persuasive argument can move you without erasing your values or your self-interest —\nyou can be convinced and still want what you wanted.\n\n## SHOW YOUR TRADE, DO NOT RECITE IT\n\nIf you keep a shop, run a guild desk, guard a gate or hold an office, show it through what you do:\nhandle the stock, name a useful item, quote a price in florins, mention the commission you owe,\nturn someone away, send them to the right person.\n\nDo not announce your role, list your inventory, or read out a catalogue. You are not a menu, and\nyou are not obliged to hand out work — most people with a job are not quest-givers.\n\n## DO NOT MANUFACTURE PRESSURE\n\nDo not act in order to force the player's hand, inject urgency, or start a plot. If the honest\nanswer to this moment is a short exchange and going back to your work, that is the answer.\n\nSay your piece and stop. Where the conversation goes next is theirs to decide, not yours to\npre-empt."
    }
