    "Grell Hollows": {
      "name": "Grell Hollows",
      "realm": "The Underworld",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 0,
      "basicInfo": "The Grell Hollows are broad limestone caverns beneath Silvervale, close enough to the surface that daylight falls through sinkholes and broken shafts. The Flood buried the old cities of the Fourth Empire here, leaving paved streets, public squares, statues, and grand halls open to the dark. The Hollows take their name from the Grell, a mythical kobold empire that current clans claim as their birthright; the squatters below raid the surface and feud over the ruins, imperial salvage, and whose grandiose host or legion owns which dead district.",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": "The true ruins are Fourth Empire and Princes of Ur ruins buried by the cataclysmic Flood. Ardan's Watch stands above one of the old entry points; the Delvers' Lift descends from the Basement to The Scrabble. The current kobolds are squatters, not descendants proven to have built the ruins. They have claimed the name Grell for a mythical kobold empire and insist the old city is theirs by birthright. They wear and hoard imperial salvage that does not fit them — oversized armor, too-large weapons, and broken regalia strapped into place — and treat any delver as a trespasser in their empire. Kobolds from different holds, hosts, wings, and legions feud constantly; that division is why their raids remain dangerous but containable. They are hostile to Ardan's Watch and periodically raid upward, but this is not an open war. Do not place Grell kobolds as ordinary residents in the city or make them a tolerated surface faction.\n\nThe Scrabble is the League's working foothold and quest hub, not a safe settlement. The Imperial Baths are warm, overgrown, contested ruins. The Imperial Shrine to Avarice and its black water are held only around the shore. The Flooded Forum and Imperial Arsenal are deeper unknown sites. Use the region's creatures and kobold roles only when a scene calls for danger; quiet camps, negotiations, and salvage work remain possible.",
      "images": {
