    "Rathcairn": {
      "name": "Rathcairn",
      "x": -34,
      "y": -33,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whiteholds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A dwarven town in the white peaks, set above a great barrow-field where the dead of the mountain holds have been laid in honored stone for ages. The folk here are gravecrafters — the masons who carve the tomb-stones, the wrights who cut the barrow-seals, and the ward-priests who speak the rites that keep a dwarf at rest under the mountain. Their craft is failing. The dead are rising in the barrows, more than the town can put back down, and Rathcairn has begun to call for any hand willing to do the grim work.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Rathcairn is a town built around death done properly. The gravecrafters are masons and priests, not scholars — they cut the stone, seal the barrows, and keep the old rites that lay a dwarf to rest and hold them there. The dead of the white holds have been brought here for burial for as long as anyone remembers, and the barrow-field below the town is generations deep. The work is a craft and a sacred duty both, and the folk who do it are warm, plain, and grieving, because lately they are losing.\n\nSomething has gone wrong with the rest of the mountain's dead. The barrows that held quiet for ages have begun to stir, and wights are rising in the grave-field faster than the gravecrafters can seal them back down. The ward-priests will not say plainly what they fear is behind it, but they know their rites are not holding, and they know there are more unrested dead in the mountains now than there have ever been. Rathcairn is overwhelmed: the only folk with the craft to put the risen dead back to rest, and far too many doing exactly the work their craft exists to prevent.\n\nIMPORTANT: the risen dead of the barrow-field default to hostile and attack the living that disturb their ground. These are dwarven barrow-wights — the honored dead gone wrong, not the work of any necromancer — cold and strong and clad in the grave-arms they were buried with. They guard the barrows where they were laid and rise against intruders, and what is set to rest stays at rest only when the barrow is sealed again by the proper craft. Putting them down is a mercy owed as much as a danger faced; the gravecrafters reckon it the kindest work there is.\n\nThe barrows hold the grave-goods the dead were laid with — old dwarven arms and armor, ornaments, and grave-silver, finely made and ancient, though there is a weight to taking what was buried with the honored dead, and the gravecrafters mark who does. A player who learns the sealing-craft and proves themselves can rise among the gravecrafters as one trusted to lay the dead to rest.",
      "areas": {
        "The Lych Gate": {
          "description": "The town's entrance, a stone gate carved with the marks of the dead and the wards that ring the burial-ground. Rathcairn rises beyond it — low, solid stone buildings of grey mountain rock, the masons' yards stacked with half-cut tomb-stones, smoke rising from the priests' hall. Below the town the land falls away into the barrow-field, a vast slope of turfed mounds and standing stones stretching down into the snow. Some of the nearer mounds stand broken open, their seal-stones cracked and toppled.",
          "paths": [
            "The Town"
          ]
        },
        "The Town": {
          "description": "The heart of Rathcairn, a cluster of stone halls and workshops around a central yard. The masons' workshops stand open to the cold, half-carved gravestones and barrow-seals waiting on the benches, chisels and mallets racked along the walls. The ward-priests' hall fronts the yard, its doors carved with rites, and a warm inn backs onto it where the gravecrafters take their meals and their grief. A worn path leads down out of the town toward the barrow-field below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lych Gate",
            "The Barrow-Field"
          ]
        },
        "The Barrow-Field": {
          "description": "A vast slope of burial-mounds below the town, generations deep — turfed barrows and standing stones marching down into the snowbound rock, the oldest and grandest mounds set lowest and furthest out. Seal-stones stand carved at every barrow-mouth, and many of them are cracked, heaved aside, or toppled, the dark openings beneath them gaping into the mounds. Grave-arms lie scattered in the churned snow where the dead have risen and walked. The field runs down and away into the cold, the furthest barrows lost in the white.",
          "paths": [
            "The Town"
          ]
        }
      },
