    "Clanhold Stormhammer": {
      "name": "Clanhold Stormhammer",
      "x": -14,
      "y": 8,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient stronghold of vast fitted megaliths atop a storm-wracked crag, seat of Clan Stormhammer, where lightning is forever stabbing down at the peak and the dwarves have learned to forge with it. The Stormhammer dwarves are hammerdwarves — they charge, they hit first, and they hold that anything worth doing is worth doing at a dead run with a warhammer. They draw the storm down into their forges to make stormsteel, the lightning-charged metal that shatters armor and stone.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Clan Stormhammer are the hammerdwarves, and their creed fits on the head of a maul: hit it harder. They charge where others hold, swing where others guard, and trust that a hard enough blow ends a fight before a shieldwall has finished forming up. The clan runs hot and loud and proud, all swagger and big laughter and bigger boasts, and a Stormhammer would rather lose spectacularly than win by standing still.\n\nThe storm is the clan's craft and their glory. Lightning strikes the crag without cease, and the Stormhammer smiths long ago learned to draw it down into the forge and beat it into the metal — stormsteel, blue-sheened and humming, that cracks plate and stone like nothing else and answers a maul-blow with a peal of thunder. Their war-smiths call the storm down into the steel with rites of hammer and spark, and a Stormhammer thunder-hammer is a clan treasure handed down through maul-lineages, each with a name and a tally of skulls. Stormsteel is the clan's wealth and the white peaks' wonder, and they sell it dear and brag about it free.\n\nThe great rivalry is with Clan Stonewall, and most of it is glorious sport — rustled rams, ruined feasts, kill-counts tallied on every joint hunt, and a grudge-roll of insults four hundred years deep. Stormhammer thinks Stonewall are timid wall-huggers who have mistaken hiding behind a board for valor, and loses no chance to say so, especially loudly and especially at feasts. But one wound is not sport. At Varric's Span, a generation back, a joint muster against a troll-horde broke: Stormhammer charged the horde ahead of the line, and Stonewall's wall held fast and did not break to reach them, and the charge was cut off and butchered on the bridge. Stonewall says the charge doomed itself by breaking discipline. Stormhammer holds that the wall stood there and watched their kin die rather than risk the line, and they will never forgive it, and they do not joke about that day, because the dead on the bridge were theirs.\n\nThane Hilda Stormhammer rules the hold, a huge, grinning, thunder-voiced warrior-smith with a famous stormhammer and a laugh that carries over the forges, who has never once backed down from a fight or a wager.",
      "areas": {
        "The Thunder-Court": {
          "description": "The stronghold's great open court, a vast floor of fitted megalithic stone ringed by towering giant-cut walls, open to the storm-dark sky where lightning forks down at the iron rods raised along the battlements. This is where the clan gathers, drills its charges, and throws its roaring feasts, and the ground is scorched in places where the storm has struck close. The Flash and Boom tavern opens off the court, doors flung wide and common room thundering, and across the way the great forge-hall glows blue and gold, its noise rolling out across the stone.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stormforge",
            "The Flash and Boom",
            "The High Battlements"
          ]
        },
        "The Flash and Boom": {
          "description": "The clan's tavern, a riot of noise and firelight built into the old giant-stone, where off-shift smiths and warriors drink, boast, and arm-wrestle hard enough to crack the tables. The house brew is a fierce storm-ale served in tankards of blue-sheened stormsteel that prickle the lip, and the place takes its name from the game the clan plays with the storm: when the lightning flashes you drink, and when the thunder cracks you stop, and whoever's downed the most between the flash and the boom wins the round — a close strike is a mercy and a far one can drown a dwarf. The talk between rounds is all of the day's forging, the last great hunt, and exactly how soft Clan Stonewall has become, and a stranger who can hold their ale and their boasts is welcome at any table.",
          "paths": [
            "The Thunder-Court"
          ]
        },
        "The Stormforge": {
          "description": "The clan's great forge-hall, where the storm is made into steel — conductor-rods run down through the ceiling to the anvils, and when the lightning strikes the crag above, it comes down the rods in a crack of thunder and a wash of blue light, and the smiths beat the charge into the metal in the moment it lands. Stormsteel work cools blue-sheened and faintly humming on the racks — maul-heads, axe-blades, plate that crackles to the touch. War-smiths work the storm-rites at the central anvil, and the hall rings with hammer-fall and thunder.",
          "paths": [
            "The Thunder-Court"
          ]
        },
        "The High Battlements": {
          "description": "The stronghold's towering walls and wall-walks, giant-built and massive, raised along the crag's edge with great iron rods standing up from the parapets to catch the strikes. From up here the storm-wracked peaks roll away on every side, the country far below laid open, and the lightning comes down close enough to raise the hair on your arms. The stonework is colossal and old, fitted by hands far larger than a dwarf's, and the marks of the clan are carved over the giant-work that came before.",
          "paths": [
            "The Thunder-Court"
          ]
        }
      },
