    "Clanhold Icerune": {
      "name": "Clanhold Icerune",
      "x": 34,
      "y": -3,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whiteholds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress-city carved into the heart of a great glacier in the white peaks, where Clan Icerune works rune and cold into walls that the worst things in the world cannot break. They are the runeguard of the Whiteholds — the clan that seals the buried dangers shut and stands the watch at every door that must stay closed. Their rune-graved ice turns blades, holds back monsters, and binds what should never walk, and their warriors and ward-priests are the ones who go where something has gotten loose and put it back behind a wall.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Clan Icerune are the runeguard, and their creed is that some things must stay shut and someone has to be strong enough to keep them so. They hold that the deep places hold horrors meant to stay buried, that the old prohibitions were written in blood for good reason, and that cold is the discipline that holds a thing in its place — and they back that creed with rune-ice that stops an axe-blow cold and walls that a charging monster breaks its skull against. They are proud, unbending, and fearless in the way of folk who have seen what gets out when a seal fails. Their ward-craft binds the restless dead, holds the deep horrors behind stone and ice, and is sought across the mountains wherever something must be kept shut for good.\n\nThe clan's pride and their grief are the same stone: the seal that failed. Icerune ward-craft held the old prohibitions on the buried Darek workings for ages, until the night Stonehome fell into the dark — and the seals that should have held it were Icerune's. The clan holds, fiercely, that the wards were sound and the fault lay elsewhere: that Clan Deepfire's reckless delve drove a vein straight into the dark and doomed the kingdom, that no ward ever made could stand against dwarves digging toward damnation from the other side. They will not say aloud, even among themselves, that their wards failed on the one night that mattered most. The clan they blame is Deepfire, and the grudge between them is a wound that does not close.\n\nIcerune is united on the gold rush at Deepgate, and united against it. To them the delvers swarming the pit are fools tearing open the very thing the runeguard spent an age keeping shut, and every haul dragged up from the Darek dark is one more horror loose in the world. The clan wants the Deep sealed again and the sacrilege ended, and chafes that it cannot simply wall the pit and be done — not with a delver-city grown around its mouth and the clans forbidden from open war. \n\nThane Moira Icerune carries the weight of the failed seal as her own, though she was not yet thane the night it broke. She has spent her years since making certain no ward of hers will ever give, and she sends her runeguard out to seal what gets loose with a cold resolve that does not bend.",
      "areas": {
        "The Ice-Hall": {
          "description": "The clan's great hall, carved deep in the glacier where the living rock meets the ice, its walls dark stone veined with quartz and graven with protective runes that catch the firelight. Many hearths blaze along its length — warmth is sacred here, hard-won against the cold, and never let to fail. Long tables seat the clan for meals and ale strong enough to strip paint, and furs and war-banners hang from the walls between racks of rune-marked arms.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rune-Market",
            "The Warding Hall",
            "The Rimewatch Roost"
          ]
        },
        "The Rune-Market": {
          "description": "A market hall carved into the ice and stone where Icerune's craft changes hands — rune-graved arms and armor, ward-stones, sealing-work, and cold-craft sought from across the peaks. The walls are sculpted ice, clear and blue and graven with runes that hold the glacier back, and braziers burn between the stalls without ever melting them. Traders come up from the lower holds for ward-work found nowhere else, and the ward-smiths who sell here are exacting and proud of it. Passages of carved ice lead deeper toward the warding hall and the clan's chambers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ice-Hall",
            "The Warding Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Warding Hall": {
          "description": "The clan's great work-hall, a vast chamber where the rune-ice is cut and the seals are made, the walls themselves a masterwork of warding that holds back the weight and cold of the glacier overhead. Ward-priests and rune-smiths work at long benches over slabs of clear ice and graven stone, cutting the runes that turn blades and bind monsters. Finished seals and rune-marked arms stand racked along the walls, and a great test-wall of rune-ice stands at the hall's end, scarred and gouged where the clan proves its work against blade and beast.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ice-Hall",
            "The Rune-Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Rimewatch Roost": {
          "description": "A high vaulted chamber of blue glacier-ice where the runeguard keep their rime-owls — pale, silent birds perched in carved ice-hollows up the walls and along the rune-graved beams, watching the hall below with wide pale eyes. The runeguard raise the owls from the egg and bond them young, and a bonded owl rides a runeguard's shoulder out to the seals and the dark places, for a rime-owl sees clean through glacier-gloom and feels the strain on a ward before any dwarf can — it goes still and stares, or gives a low call, when a seal is pressed or something unseen draws near. Ice-perches and roosting-hollows ring the chamber, and owlkeepers move among the young birds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ice-Hall"
          ]
        }
      },
