      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A snowlander fishing village on a frozen lake shore, built around an ancient stone broch that has stood against the sea-wind since before the cold came. The folk here are ice-fishers, cutting the frozen lake for their catch, and hunters of the beithir — the great serpents of these waters — which they take together as a whole village. Dwarves of the north keep a presence here too, smithing and trading, drawn by the serpent-bone and the cold-country goods that pass through the harbor.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Dun Beithir is a safe and welcoming place, hard-used and warm-hearted — one of the snowlander footholds the cold has not yet taken, where a stranger off the ice can find a fire, a meal, and work. The villagers are ice-fishers first: they live by the holes they cut in the frozen lake and the lampfish they haul up for oil to burn through the long dark. They are also serpent-hunters, and the beithir-hunt is the whole community's work — when one is sighted, every able hand has a part, on the harpoons, the lines, the ice, or the butchery, and a successful kill feeds and supplies the village for a season.\n\nThe dwarves here are mostly of the northern holds, prospect-crews and traders who came up for the cold materials and stayed to work them. They smith beithir bone and the rarer rimebone into gear that answers the things of the cold, buy serpent-oil and hide for the southern trade, and keep their own hall by the harbor. Snowlander and dwarf get on plainly here, in the way of people who need each other's trade and each other's backs against the same sea.\n\nThe beithir is hunted offshore, out on and under the frozen lake, and is not a danger within the village. IMPORTANT: a beithir is a great cold sea-serpent, and out on the ice it defaults hostile and lethal — it strikes from below, smashing up through the frozen surface, takes prey or hunters off the ice, and submerges to circle and come again. The hunt is dangerous work and not every hand comes back from it. Beithir hide, the great fangs, and rendered serpent-oil are the village's wealth and its trade.",
      "areas": {
        "The Broch": {
          "description": "The ancient round tower at the village's heart, drystone walls thick enough to laugh at the wind, a fire always burning in its great round room. The villagers gather here — fishers, hunters, and the dwarves of the harbor-hall among them — to eat, drink, mend nets and gear, and settle the village's business. Carved housewardens watch out from the lintels, facing the cold.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ice-Harbor"
          ]
        },
        "The Ice-Harbor": {
          "description": "The working coast below the broch, where the lake lies frozen out past sight and the village's boats sit hauled up on the ice, useless until a thaw that rarely comes. Fishing-holes are cut and tended across the near ice, racks of split fish and hung nets stand along the shore, and the long frames and winches of the serpent-hunt wait beside the butchery-sheds. The dwarves' harbor-hall stands at the water's edge, smoke rising from its forge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broch",
            "The Cutting Ice"
          ]
        },
        "The Cutting Ice": {
          "description": "The frozen lake itself, out beyond the harbor, a flat white expanse cut with fishing-holes and scored by the drag-marks of past hauls. The ice groans and shifts underfoot, and dark water shows where it has been cut or broken. There is no cover here and a long way back to shore.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ice-Harbor"
          ]
        }
      },
