      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whiteholds",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "basicInfo": "Greathearth is a large hillside village where the high roads of the Whiteholds meet on neutral territory between clans. Houses, inns, and terraces climb a sheltered slope around the Greathearth, an immense communal hall where residents and travelers eat, trade, perform, and find lodging. Above the village, Mercy's Refuge is a shrine of the Lady of Mercy where priests and druids care for wounded magical creatures. Greathearth is famous for its cloudhorns, large pale mountain goats whose extraordinary footing carries riders and baggage over paths no horse could climb.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Most of Greathearth's permanent residents are human or dwarf, with humans slightly more numerous. Gnome and giantborn households also live here, along with several minotaur families whose arrival lies beyond any record the village has kept. The minotaurs have different trades and allegiances, and no one local treats their presence as remarkable.\n\nGreathearth belongs to no clan. House Merovar is a human noble family entrusted with the surrounding hillside and the safer approaches under a grant from the old dwarf kings. The family maintains Merovar House above the village, supports Mercy's Refuge, and hosts nobles, wealthy travelers, celebrated riders, and distinguished healers. It neither governs the clans nor controls the Refuge.\n\nThe Greathearth is the village's common hall and its everyday center. Residents and travelers share its tables, kitchens, entertainment, and inexpensive guest galleries. More prosperous visitors use the discreet inns and private rooms of the Guest Lanes, and Greathearth has a wide reputation as a place where people arrange meetings, begin affairs, renew old attachments, or encounter someone far from the company in which they are usually seen.\n\nMercy's Refuge shelters wounded magical creatures brought down from the peaks or carried here by travelers. Its priests provide sanctuary and healing, while druids understand unfamiliar bodies and behavior, guide rehabilitation, and determine when a creature can safely return to the wild. The patients change with the story and may include griffons, mountain drakes, owlbears, giant birds, elementals, or stranger creatures. Those well enough to tolerate company may be exercised through the village or on the high pastures under a druid's care.\n\nCloudhorns are large pale mountain goats with broad backs, sweeping horns, and extraordinary footing. They carry riders and baggage over slopes, ledges, and broken paths that horses cannot manage. They are intelligent, stubborn, and prone to challenging one another by butting horns; an unattended cloudhorn may climb stairs, walls, roofs, or outdoor tables simply because they are there.\n\nGreathearth's outfitters are known for making useful equipment for unusual bodies and creatures. They fit armor and clothing around horns, wings, tails, uncommon limbs, or great size; make saddles, harnesses, barding, and protective gear for mounts and companions; and sell restorative potions, antidotes, charms, ordinary weapons, and travel gear.\n\nWealthy patrons compete to sponsor the Refuge's most impressive patients and attend their release, sometimes caring more for association with a rare creature than for the work itself. A few residents have gone further. Recovering creatures are sometimes stolen from the Refuge and sold alive to collectors, fighting pits, or magical experimenters. A small corrupt ring identifies valuable patients, supplies sedatives, opens private routes through the hillside, and hides them in guesthouses or covered transport before they leave Greathearth.",
      "areas": {
        "The Sun Terrace": {
          "description": "A broad south-facing terrace below the Greathearth serves as the village's principal arrival and open market. Merchants and vendors sell weapons, armor, charms, potions, clothing, cloudhorn tack, prepared food, and goods brought over the high roads among outdoor tables and grassy banks. The terrace overlooks the lower valleys and ridge after ridge of white mountain. Villagers gather here for meals, music, games, dancing, and cloudhorn races on the sloping ground below.",
          "paths": [
