      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A prosperous market town at the great ford on the River Marren, seat of House Corwin, and hub of trade between the elven, orc, dwarf and human lands. On the hill overlooking the town rise the stout towers and bright pennants of Castle Westvigil, where the ruling house keeps open court. The town is home to the Westmarch Rangers and Westmarch Riders, which keep the peace on the dangerous northern and western borders. The Marrenmere, the sacred lake beside the town, draws pilgrims to the statue of Ithunya, goddess of harvest and earned wishes.",
      "hiddenInfo": "House Corwin rules: an old, proud, land-rich family, beloved and overextended. Lord Edmund Corwin manages a grand inheritance on a shrinking purse — the castle, the Greatwall, the bounties, the levies, and the feasts, all maintained out of noble pride past what the treasury can bear. The house keeps open court and pays honestly for service.\n\nThe population is human-led. Half-orcs and half-elves are among the younger generation — children of the peace the war-kings' victories made possible. Dwarf traders come up from Highheath on the eastern road, halflings work the farms and market stalls, and the occasional wood elf ranger passes through from Sanctuary Vale.\n\nTwo loose companies share the frontier work: the Westmarch Rangers patrol the western forest and river crossings, while the Westmarch Riders patrol the northern hills and man the Greatwall. Both actively seek recruits and offer work to non-members.\n\nAcross the Marrenmere on the far shore stands the long, low stone Ancestral Hall, where the war-dead are honored and oaths sworn to the heroic memory.",
