      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Gavarra is the City of Fortune, a terraced island that climbs in tiers of gambling halls and pleasure-houses toward tall towers that glow blue over the sea at night. Five great Houses run the play: games of chance, games of skill, fights and races. Coin spends the same here whatever flag it came under, so everyone's money is welcome, and the Houses compete for all of it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The towers that light Gavarra are old Empyrean storm-towers that draw the lightning off the sea-storms; their caught light glows blue over the whole island after dark and feeds the bound stormlight in its halls.\n\nGavarra lives by one rule that needs no enforcing: money is welcome here, and no one asks where it came from. A pirate's plunder, a privateer's prize-share, and an honest merchant's profit all buy the same chips and the same wine, which is why a city of gamblers stays neutral in a sea of flags — taking sides would cost custom, and custom is everything. The five Houses compete for the play and answer to no one above them; there is no lord of Gavarra, only five Masters who would each like to be.\n\nThe city's order is kept by the Surety, the Houses' shared guard. They protect Gavarra's prosperity, not its people: cheats, brawlers, and anyone who threatens a paying guest on the floor are dealt with hard and quietly, and a player at the tables is under the House's protection while his coin is good.\n\nAll debts are honored, and the Houses see to it. A player who loses past his purse signs over a marker — a ship's papers, a deed, a claim, a pledged heirloom — and the House holds it against payment. The Houses' vaults are full of such markers, and a great deal of work in Gavarra is someone trying to recover what the tables took: buy it back, win it back, or get it out of a House vault by less honest means. Things lost at Gavarra are not gone, only held, and held well.\n\nGavarra welcomes any guest with coin and turns cold the instant the coin or the conduct runs out. The Houses scheme against each other constantly — poaching champions, fixing odds, courting high rollers.\n\nThe stakes worth crossing the city for sit at the high tables of the Tower of Fortune: deeds to ships and islands, a rival's marker, charts, and relics no honest market would sell. The Gilded Cage and the Slick are the spectacle and the second economy — fortunes wagered on a fight or a race, and a fighter who survives enough bouts wins a House's sponsorship.",
      "areas": {
        "The Promenade": {
          "description": "The broad middle terrace and the city's public heart, an avenue of gambling halls, dicing-pits, and taverns climbing the island in tiers. The towers above cast a shifting blue glow. Stairs and ramps run up toward the private Houses on the heights and down toward the harbor and the racing course.",
          "paths": [
