      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "An island curved around a warm, glass-clear lagoon — the one truly gentle water in the bay, sheltered by a reef-mouth nothing big can pass. A village of whitewashed stone lines the crescent shore, and the merfolk village glows beneath the surface across the lagoon, shell-roofed, its lanterns lighting the water at night. Merfolk and surfacers have lived here as one town for generations, and people come from across the bay just to swim in water where nothing is hunting them.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The lagoon is the village's life and its playground. Merfolk teach visitors to swim, dive the shallow reefs, and hold their breath like locals — children of both peoples grow up in the water together, and some families have intermarried; their half-mer children are equally at home on land or below. The village's great games are the lagoon relays, swimmer to runner to swimmer, merfolk against surfacers, half the field laughing too hard to finish. In the evenings the merfolk sing in the water, and the sound carries through the whole lagoon.\n\nMerfolk tend pearl-gardens in the lagoon's deep middle. Pearls are the village's one export and its gift-currency — being given one means you are trusted here, and everyone knows it.\n\nThe village's fiercest dispute is whose fish stew is better, surfacers' or merfolk's. A formal contest is held every year and has never once been settled, because the judges are hopelessly partisan and proud of it.\n\nVisitors are welcomed warmly, fed, taught, and gossiped about. The village asks little except that the lagoon's gentleness be kept — it is the one water in the bay where nothing is hunted.",
