    "Confluence": {
      "name": "Confluence",
      "x": -3,
      "y": -6,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Bay of Serpents",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A floating city where the ocean currents meet, made of hundreds of boats, barges, and rafts lashed together in loose clusters with channels of open water between them. The currents bring rich fishing and calm warm seas, and the sea-peoples followed: every seafolk species in the bay moors here alongside humans and halflings. Nobody founded Confluence, nobody governs it, and nobody here answers to captain, house, or crown — the currents feed the city, and its people own nothing but their boats and like it that way.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Confluence is what the sea's peoples made when they wanted no masters. The currents feed anyone with a net, so there are no shares to owe, no house's cut, no rent on water. A boat is a whole estate: nobody is richer than their hull, nobody can be evicted, and any dispute can be settled by untying and drifting to a friendlier cluster. The people are loud, unhurried, unimpressed by anyone's name, and generous the way people are when the sea restocks them daily.\n\nEvery seafolk people is at home here and landfolk are the guests. Sealion-folk are loud and physical — they swim the channels and bark when they laugh. Shark-folk are quiet, sharp-eyed, and run the best fish stalls. Sea-turtle folk moor their great shell-boats at the rim, and the oldest of their elders has been moored here longer than anyone can prove — slow to speak and worth the wait. Frogkin crouch on every surface, and merfolk live as much in the water beneath the city as on it, surfacing alongside new boats to trade gossip before the lines are tied. Nobody cares where you're from — the currents brought you, same as everyone.\n\nAnything the sea gives can be bought or sold here, deck to deck, with no weigh-house and no house's cut. Seafolk hunters sell monster parts cheap — venom sacs, serpent oil, kraken ink, hides — and merfolk sell water-charms: small enchantments to hold your breath longer, keep your feet on a wet deck, or feel something big passing beneath you. Boats sell off gear when they need coin, so good harpoons and the odd fine blade turn up on any given day. Pilots, divers, and merfolk guides hire out from the Raft, and passage can be found to anywhere the currents run.\n\nEverything that happens on the bay is known at Confluence first. Crews and seafolk pass through daily from every water, and the talk on the Big Raft carries the whole bay's news: where the serpents are running, which crew hasn't come home, who's been asking after whom. The news is free the way weather is free — you just have to sit in it.\n\nNo one's writ runs here and no one can hold ground in a city with no ground, so people meet at Confluence who can meet nowhere else. Grudges are left at the tie-line: fighting here means fighting on somebody's boat, and every boat has an owner watching.\n\nA boat is how you belong, and hands are how you start. The city runs on volunteered work — hauling at the lashings when clusters re-tie, passing lines when weather turns — and hauling beside someone is how strangers become names. Meals happen on open decks, and being waved onto one is the invitation that counts; every seafolk people keeps the same custom of feeding a stranger.",
      "areas": {
        "The Lashings": {
