      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Tevantia is the wealthiest and proudest city of the Swordlaw sea, a place of marble halls and canals where great merchant houses feud over trade, status, and honor, and settle it with hired champions and formal duels. The Duke rules, the law holds, and the city stands as an island of order in a sea that mostly has none — and it answers that sea by hunting it, commissioning captains to run the pirates down.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Tevantia is the one place in the Swordlaw sea ruled by law, and it knows the worth of that. The city sits on the bridged isles where the Duke's writ runs; past the last bridge the open water belongs to the Swordlaw. Its answer to a sea of pirates is to license predators of its own — privateers.\n\nThe Duke is First Peer, elected for life by the Electors, the heads of the great merchant houses. Day to day the city runs on those houses, who feud constantly through spies, sabotage, hired mercenaries, and bought marriages — but never open violence, which is gauche. A quarrel between houses is settled by a champion and a formal duel on one of the canal bridge-plazas, where crowds gather the moment steel is drawn. Humans and dwarves hold most of the great houses, and the dwarven banking-clans hold the gold; but the streets and crews below them are every people of the salt sea — halflings, mersene, parrotfolk, demonborn, giantborn — and Tevantia prides itself that anyone can rise, since nobility can be bought, houses sell adoption, and a sponsor can lift a nobody who proves useful.\n\nThe Commission-Roll on the Admiralty steps carries the Duke's bounties — named pirates, their crimes, their last known whereabouts and haunts, and the price on each — for anyone bold enough to take a writ, and there is nearly always a prize being argued on the steps. The harbor's notice-posts carry the plainer work: guards wanted for a cargo run, an escort down the lanes, a berth on a hunting ship. And because the houses feud by proxy, a capable stranger is walking employment — a house whose champion is dead or hurt will hire a swordarm for the next duel, and the arena runs open bouts for anyone who wants to make a name.\n\nThe houses, the merchants, and the adventurers who pass through all need hands for things no writ covers — a rival's letter stolen, a debt collected quietly, a scheme guarded or spoiled, a thing carried where it should not go. That work is found at the Two Swords and in the back rooms off Golden Row. Steel settles things in the open; everything else settles in the dark.\n\nThe League of Adventurers keeps its regional house here, distinct from the Duke's Admiralty: where the Admiralty commissions captains to hunt pirates for Tevantia, the League registers adventurers of the wider world, ranks them up, and posts contracts flavored as escort, bounty, and recovery. Its board carries local work, but its people point members outward, to the troubles scattered across the sea — the Feathered Fleet raiding the shipping lane at North Canal; the wreckers and worse on Falselight Reefs; the cursed hoard men still die for on Garrock's Island; the fences and hired knives of Beggarsbridge; the markers the gaming Houses of Gavarra hold against their losers; the glassworkers of Earthsky and their enchanted mirrors and spirit-bottles; and Freehaven, the pirate capital across the water, where the hunted go to ground. A member rises by taking contracts and building a name, the same ladder the League offers everywhere.",
      "areas": {
        "Tevantia Harbor": {
          "description": "The great fortified harbor-plaza where the bridge from the outer isles meets the city, ships of every flag crowded at the wharves below the climbing marble city. The plaza is a permanent open market: an armorer's row of blades, an alchemist's stall of potions and spell-ingredients, a mapseller, and food-stalls thick among them. Notice-posts crowd the bridge-foot, hung with work for hire, and criers call ship-news and bounties over the din.",
