    "The Drowned City of Orichala": {
      "name": "The Drowned City of Orichala",
      "x": -5,
      "y": -25,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Seadeep Kingdoms",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Orichala was the greatest city of the Empyrean Empire, and the sea swallowed it in a single night. Not all the way: its towers still stand out of the water, and beneath them the drowned city goes down into the merfolk kingdoms' waters. Merfolk and sea creatures fiercely repel any surfacer who lands there. At the city's heart, the crown of a colossal glass dome breaks the surface, and inside it, they say, the dragon of the deep kingdoms keeps her court.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Orichala is hostile ground. The drowned-and-changed — merfolk of the deep kingdoms, once surface folk, remade for the water — garrison the standing city and attack any landing. They fight in bands of six to twelve, surfacing from the flooded streets, striking from the water side and pulling back into it when pressed.\n\nOphialtha, the dragon of the deep kingdoms, is sea-bound: she cannot leave the water, and no dry ground in Orichala is ever in her reach — only her servants'. She does not stir for fighting in the standing city. Entering the Dome of All Worlds brings her: she comes to the Queen's Lake for anything that disturbs it.\n\nThe Dome of All Worlds was the city's great wonder: a colossal sealed habitat holding living specimens from every land the empire ruled. It sank with the city and trapped its air, and its old magic still keeps the air good and the habitat alive. Centuries feral, its creatures answer to nothing: rock-apes in the high tiers, terror-birds running the grass shelf, a grown-monstrous crocodile in the channels, and bright venomous things in the green. Ophialtha enters through the sea-breach in the great lake and rules the Dome as her court — a queen of the deep presiding over a little world of the surface.",
      "areas": {
        "The Rooftop Shallows": {
          "description": "The city's edge, where the drowned streets first break the surface: a reef of rooftops, balconies, and upper colonnades standing out of the sea, with open water in the streets between them. Boats can moor against the ledges. The dry crossings run rooftop to rooftop over gaps of dark water, and the towers' upper floors give high ground the water cannot reach. The city rises tilted toward its heart, where the curve of a great glass dome stands out of the sea.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sunken Ways"
          ]
        },
        "The Sunken Ways": {
          "description": "The half-drowned mid-city between the outer rooftops and the dome. The water shallows street by street — chest, waist, knee — and the ways run over causeways of rubble, fallen columns, and flooded avenues between towers still standing to their full height, doorways gaping dark above the waterline. Open water stands deep in the sunken plazas. Ahead, the dome's crown fills the sky, split by a great breach well above the waterline, reachable by a climb up its glass-and-bronze ribs.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rooftop Shallows",
            "The Dome of All Worlds"
          ]
        },
        "The Dome of All Worlds": {
          "description": "Inside the breach, a whole living country falls away below in vast tiers — forest, grassland, reed-marsh — under a shell of glowing glass that lights it all like a pale day. Below the waterline the glass turns black where the sea presses against it from outside. Old stairs and terraces descend tier to tier through the overgrowth, and channels of open water cut between the gardens gone wild, all running down to a great lake at the heart of it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sunken Ways",
            "The Queen's Lake"
          ]
        },
        "The Queen's Lake": {
          "description": "The lake at the bottom of the Dome, ringed by a shore of white stone built in the wonder's first days. The water is broad, deep, and dark, and somewhere below it a breach opens to the ocean outside. Drowned trophies stand along the shore — ships' figureheads, statues, thrones — arranged facing the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dome of All Worlds"
          ]
        }
      },
