    "Beggarsbridge": {
      "name": "Beggarsbridge",
      "x": -16,
      "y": 4,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Swordlaw Isles",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Beggarsbridge is an anarchic shantytown built on and beneath an ancient mile-long stone bridge spanning the water between two of the larger isles. Anything can be bought or sold here — it is the one market in the Swordlaw sea with no law, no questions, and no door shut to a buyer with coin. Houses and stalls of every description cling to the span and hang under it on stilts and ropes above the canal.",
      "hiddenInfo": "No authority holds Beggarsbridge. It is the region's open market for everything the law forbids elsewhere.\n\nThe Syndicate has a heavy presence here, and the man who runs their trade here is Mardrek Venn.\n\nHumans, ratkin, and frogkin live thickest, with fishfolk and other water-folk in the lower levels that flood with the tide, swimming up through doorways the dry-folk never use. Below everything, in the canal under the bridge, the Guttermouths wait — eels grown long and bold on what the bridge drops through its gaps and spillways, which is where inconvenient things tend to go.\n\nBeggarsbridge runs on the unwritten rules: pay your debts, never look weak, and do not bring another isle's quarrel onto the span. A stranger who keeps those is left to do business; one who looks like easy money, or like trouble from somewhere else, finds himself tossed into the canal - or worse. Work here is the criminal trade itself — fencing, smuggling, hiring muscle and killers, buying what is banned, and the jobs Venn hands out to people who do not ask questions.\n\nAnything is for sale on the bridge. The Midmarket stalls carry good steel and pirate iron — cutlasses, boarding-axes, crossbows — alongside salvaged and ship-stripped armor, leathers and brigandines and the odd fine breastplate off a captain. Deeper in, the trade turns to poisons and venoms, alchemical reagents and spell ingredients, scavenged Empyrean relics and charms of doubtful provenance, scrolls and grimoires fenced from dead mages, forged papers, and stolen aspected gear nobody can sell openly — a moonstone blade, a stormsteel cutlass, a witchwood wand — turning up here with no questions about where it came from. Services are traded as readily as goods: mercenaries and killers for hire, smugglers and pilots who know the dark channels, forgers, fixers, and crews willing to take the job no honest crew will. Quality runs the full range, from cheap stall-goods to genuine masterwork plundered off the rich, and a sharp buyer can find an heirloom piece here that a lawful city would never let go.",
      "areas": {
        "The Midmarket": {
          "description": "The bridge's widest section, where the span broadens into a permanent open market roofed over with sailcloth and salvaged timber. Stalls and stacked crates crowd the stone in a maze of narrow ways, and the goods on offer run from ship's rope and tar to things kept under the counter. The stonework rises in great arches overhead, and the sea is visible far below through gaps in the deck. Stairs and ladders drop away toward the levels hanging beneath the bridge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Waterline Inn",
            "Venn's Exchange",
            "The Underhang"
          ]
        },
        "The Waterline Inn": {
          "description": "A tall rowdy tavern built into the side of the bridge on two levels. The upper floor is a sailors' common room of long tables and recruiting-corners, loud at all hours. The lower floor sits at the waterline and floods at high tide, its far doorways opening straight onto the canal, so that water-folk come and go by swimming up from below. Drink, hire, and rumor move on both floors.",
          "paths": [
            "The Midmarket"
          ]
        },
        "Venn's Exchange": {
          "description": "A sprawling fortified shopfront dug into the bridge's stonework behind reinforced doors, where the serious trade is done out of sight of the market. Counting-rooms, locked storerooms, and a forger's bench fill the deep interior, and the back ways connect to the levels below the bridge. This is the bridge's true money-floor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Midmarket"
          ]
        },
        "The Underhang": {
          "description": "The world beneath the span — a hanging warren of stilted platforms, rope walks, and shacks lashed to the bridge's underside just above the high-tide line. The stone arches close overhead and the canal runs black below, fed by spillway gaps that drop refuse straight into the water. The lowest platforms are slick and half-rotten.",
          "paths": [
            "The Midmarket"
          ]
        }
      },
