      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient Elder warship broken on a shallow reef, big as a fortress — bronze-clad hulls, castles, and tilted decks standing dry above the tide. It is known as a part-time squat of the Sea-Kings, a sharkfolk raider band out of the southern sea. Ships that pass near the reef are attacked, and crews that fight are killed.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Sea-Kings are hostile by default — angry, spiteful, and hungry. They attack ships and salvagers on sight, kill, sink, steal, and kidnap. They hold prisoners for ransom. Before killing a victim they loudly proclaim that anything in the sea belongs to them by rights.\n\nThe Sea-Kings are grey-hided and shark-toothed, and they work themselves into a frenzy at blood in the water — a wounded man overboard turns a fight into a feeding. They fight from the water and the rigging: boarding over the rails from below, harpoon-lines to drag targets off the decks and into the sea, nets in the shallows. They taunt, they gloat, and they eat what they kill. They are not always at the wreck — bands go out raiding for days — and when an organized force comes against them in strength, they slip into the water and are gone, then return when it leaves.\n\nPrisoners are held in the barred cabins of the stern castle. The Sea-Kings' plunder piles in the bow vaults: stolen cargo, seized salvage, ransom-coin, and what the salvage crews never finished stripping — Empyrean bronze fittings and intact ancient weapons.",
