    "The Red Chantry": {
      "name": "The Red Chantry",
      "x": 22,
      "y": 27,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whitemoor",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Red Chantry is a fortified manor on the moor and the home of the Bloodhunters, an order of duelists and blood-mages who hunt the dead. They live finely and fight with antique blades and old blood-magic, and they take in any who would learn the craft and take up the cause.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Bloodhunters are blood-mages and duelists who hunt the moor's dead: the war-dead, the barrow-wights, the drowned courts, and the death-knights of the Deathless. The Knights Vigilant scorn them in public for trafficking with blood-magic, and call on them in private when the clean sword is not enough.\n\nTheir magic turns blood and life against death: tracking the dead by the traces they leave in their victims, warding the mind against a dead thing's compulsion, and marking undead flesh so it cannot knit and heal. The art costs the user a little of himself each time, a slow corruption every hunter carries and bears as a point of pride.\n\nThey welcome anyone with the nerve and the skill, and the way in is the work: a guest who proves useful is taught the blade and the blood-arts, a proven hand is sworn to the order, and the sworn rise by the dead they put down. They train, arm, and send hunters out under contract. The chapter master's study above the hall holds the order's records, its richest bounties, and the Black Book that names every hunter who has fallen.\n\nIMPORTANT: the Chantry is gracious to guests and deadly to enemies, not hostile by default. They are fiercely proud of their honor and their charter, and they turn on anyone who threatens the order, exposes its dealings, or means it harm. A hunter who takes too much of the curse falls, and a fallen Bloodhunter is just what the Deathless want, so the Chantry hunts its own fallen quietly, before word can spread, and sends its best after any member who turns.",
      "areas": {
        "The Courtyard": {
          "description": "An elegant fortified manor behind tall iron gates worked with silver, a manicured garden and a fountain within the walls. The gate is warded, and the dead and the tainted cannot pass it. Beyond the garden the manor's doors stand open onto the hall, and a stone dueling-ring lies off to one side.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Hall",
            "The Duelist's Ring"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Hall": {
          "description": "A lavish vaulted hall hung with velvet and old steel, a great map of the moor and its graves spread on a central table. A long hearth and deep chairs stand at one end, wine set out on the sideboards. A stair climbs to the chapter master's study above, and doors lead off to the dueling-ring and down to the cellars.",
          "paths": [
            "The Courtyard",
            "The Duelist's Ring",
            "The Undercroft"
          ]
        },
        "The Duelist's Ring": {
          "description": "A circular stone platform for fencing and drill, ringed by benches and racks of antique blades. A workbench along one side holds tools for etching runes into steel and mixing the order's alchemy. Doors connect to the courtyard and the hall.",
          "paths": [
            "The Courtyard",
            "The Grand Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Undercroft": {
          "description": "A warded stone cellar beneath the manor, its chambers set apart for teaching the blood-arts and for cleansing corruption from those who have taken too much. Deeper in, rune-carved cells stand ready for captured dead and, now and then, for one of the order's own. The way down is the hall stair.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Hall"
          ]
        }
      },
