      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Elfwood",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "basicInfo": "The high elven capital, built among colossal silverleaf trees with the ancient First Tree at its heart. The city rises in tiers connected by winding staircases carved into the trunks. Outsiders are welcome at the lower levels, and the upper reaches are elven domain — open to those who earn the climb.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Council of Elf-Lords governs the Everwood from the high branches of the First Tree. They are divided on the dark elf question: hawks want the Sundering enforced more harshly and have been gaining ground of late, doves seek reconciliation, and a weary majority holds to containment and vigilance. The High Wardens, who enforce high elven law throughout the forest, answer directly to the Council.\n\nThe elf renegades who raid the human and orc frontiers are the hawks' radicals, gone further than the Council will openly sanction. Canopy disowns them and does not hunt them as hard as its angry neighbors would like — to the high elves they are an embarrassment to be managed slowly, and the high elves do everything slowly.\n\nCanopy is the orienting point for anyone with forest business: the High Wardens at the Warden Hall know where the trouble is and who to send. Elves at the lower levels are long accustomed to outsiders and many find them amusing — particularly their fear of heights, which elves consider endearing, since an elf moves through the high city with effortless grace and would never be so clumsy as to fall. Outsiders who show courage, usefulness, or genuine respect for elven ways are welcome. Those who cause trouble are simply escorted down and out.",
      "areas": {
        "Greenshade": {
          "description": "The ground level of Canopy, where the silverleaf trunks rise like pillars and the light filters down green-gold through the city above. A market fills the spaces between the great trees - elven craftwork, forest goods, food stalls serving honeycakes and smoked game. Lodging houses cater to visitors. The staircases begin here, winding up the trunks into the branches.",
