    "Serendipity": {
      "name": "Serendipity",
      "x": -21,
      "y": -28,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Alar Coast",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A rich trade city spread across the mouth of the Emerald Delta, where desert caravans meet sea traders in the greatest market in the southern deserts. Anything can be bought, sold, or arranged in Serendipity, and its souks never close. The city pays the dragon its tribute and lives well under the flame's peace: the Church of the Pyre's Red-cowls keep order here.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Serendipity is safe ground for ordinary business. The Red-cowls do not police theft or brawls — the Souk handles its own. They hunt one crime: enmity toward the Church. Squads of four to six sweep the streets, run informants, watch the docks, and take the marked. When a grey priest marks a person or a house, the cowls come, and the taken are not seen again. Goods leave the harbor freely; people do not. No local ships out without the Church's leave.\n\nThe Temple of the Flame Undying is the Church's southern seat. Its priests preach that the world is corrupt and the flame will burn it clean, and that the dragon's coming is the proof and the promise. It recruits Red-cowls — believers, poor men's sons, men with nowhere else. The tribute is gathered and blessed there before it goes to the Black Fells by ship. Heretics judged within are given to the flame; the city sees the smoke rise from the temple and knows. Cells beneath the temple hold the accused awaiting judgment.\n\nThe Qamarid Princes are proud, rich, and bought-in — sorcerer-princes of an old line who rule the city's law and trade, duel over slights, and keep their motto with straight faces: 'We bow only to the sun.' They never bowed to the dragon; they merely pay it. Their court runs on favor, faction, and the quiet shame nobody names.\n\nA resistance works the city's underside, seated among the delta-folk of the Mudflats. They spy on the temple, steal tribute before it ships, sabotage the Church's stores, break the marked out of the cells, and smuggle the hunted out by sea. Their door is the Blind Eye Tavern, for those they choose to find. They pass messages and arms to the Desert Tribes, and want the same from outsiders: word, weapons, and the sailing dates of tribute shipments.\n\nMarket truths: the Grand Souk sells everything, and under the counter it sells what the Church forbids — frostjade, blessed arms, and charms against fire, at ruinous prices. Cowls shake down dealers they catch; the trade thrives anyway.",
      "areas": {
        "The Grand Souk": {
          "description": "A market the size of a town: miles of covered lanes, tented squares, and shopfronts stacked two and three high, roaring from dawn until long after dark. Caravan goods, sea goods, beasts, spices, arms, curiosities, and services of every description — if it exists, someone in the Souk sells it or knows who does. The crowds are wall to wall, sightlines die at every stall, and a person can cross the whole city through the Souk without once walking a street.",
          "paths": [
            "The Qamarid Palace",
            "The Temple of the Flame Undying",
            "Lowtown",
            "The Mudflats"
          ],
          "images": {
            "establishingShot": {
