      "region": "Dunelands",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The great city of the Dunelands, built around the desert's largest oasis, where springs feed palm groves and fountains and the water never fails. White stone towers and domes rise above the markets, and House Serayaf rules the city from the highest of them. Out in the badlands stand the tombs of the old Sun Kings, and Oasis is the market where the treasure dug from those tombs is bought and sold. Its souks are crowded with traders, fortune-hunters, and the tomb-raiders who supply them.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Oasis is the most crowded crossroads in the desert, and its streets hold every people of the south: humans and desert elves, djinnborn and ifrit, jackalkin, pygmies, and traders of a dozen other kinds, all of them buying and selling. House Serayaf rules over the lot of them. The Serayaf are sorcerers, masters of wind and flame, and they ride the enchanted flying carpets that are woven nowhere else in the world but here. They rule graciously and grandly and are not loved by the city's other great houses, who say the Serayaf bloodline is demon-tainted, that no mortal line commands the air and fire as they do without a bargain behind it.\n\nThe city lives on tomb-raiding. Under the Serayaf it is open trade: raiders bring back the sun-gold of the dead Sun Kings, the Sunfire Sanctum buys it and blesses it as redeemed from the tombs, and it is sold on as holy radiant arms. Everyone takes their cut and no one calls it grave-robbing. The one line nobody crosses is the city's own dead: robbing the Sealed Crypts where Oasis buries its people is punished by burning, while robbing the god-kings is the trade the whole city is built on. There is also a quieter trade in what the Sanctum will not bless, the cursed pieces and the relics with the old rites still in them, and the Syndicate runs it for those who come asking.",
      "areas": {
        "The Great Serpent Market": {
          "description": "The vast central souk of Oasis, a roofed and awning-shaded maze of stalls and lanes around a great bronze serpent statue at its heart. Everything in the desert changes hands here: spice and silk, sunstones and charms, water and weapons, and the gold and grave-goods carried in from the tombs. Snake-charmers and fortune-tellers work the open spaces, money-changers and brokers keep their benches in the shade, and the crowd runs thick from dawn to dark. Lanes lead off to the rest of the city.",
