      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Sky Temple of the Ascension hangs above Redrock on the Unmoored Mountain, a whole red-rock peak lifted from the earth by the First Adept when the demons came. Its cliffs, spires, natural arches, bridges, towers, and halls rise through the cloud-sea, linked by the Earth Ladders, rope swings, narrow bridges, and the practiced movement of its monks. It is Larion's legendary center of monastic learning, where adepts train the mind to attune and detune the body until ordinary limits no longer hold.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Temple is a welcoming social and learning hub, not a fortified compound. There is no gate, guard line, or gatekeeper at the entrance. The Unmoored Mountain hangs above its original canyon, reached by the Earth Ladders: long ladders and suspended landings that run from the ground to the mountain and can be hauled up when the Temple must close the ascent. Visitors climb them, or take a cloudwing condor if they are untrained, injured, wealthy, lazy, or bringing supplies. Monks and serious trainees do not use the ladders for ordinary travel; they run up the surrounding cliff faces, scale the mountain's lower rock, and cross gaps by rope swing, bridge, and leap as daily training. Visitors arrive directly into the Ascension Court and the Temple's social life.\n\nThe Temple's teaching begins with the deed of the First Adept. When the demons came, the First Adept taught a mountain to let go of the ground. The peak rose, carrying the first monastery and its students above the demon armies. The present Temple preserves that feat in smaller forms. Through breath, concentration, mana, and disciplined motion, a practitioner may detune the body and become lighter, faster, and able to run along walls or across water, or attune more heavily and anchor, leap, strike, and land with immense force. Adepts can perform feats ordinary bodies cannot; the elite practitioners who master the deeper discipline are the Ascendants. The tradition still has costs and failures: distraction, exhaustion, injury, bad footing, restraints, or broken rhythm can ruin a feat. The Temple does not make the world safe; it teaches people to move through its forces.\n\nThe Temple values joy as evidence of a healthy spirit. Discipline is not dourness: meals are loud, training is full of teasing and mutual correction, hospitality and compassion are marks of progress, and monks take pleasure in music, drink, simple food, games, weather, friendship, and helping people understand one another. The Joyful Refectory brews alcohol and restorative or training potions; The Weight and Wind makes wraps, sandals, harnesses, robes, bracers, and other movement-friendly equipment. The Ascension Court is the everyday social center, while the Practice Heights, Hall of Breath, Recovery Pools, and Ascendants' Spire support training, healing, learning, and advancement.\n\nThe Unmoored Mountain is watched by the Catching Winds, wind elementals held to the First Adept's ancient covenant. They help keep the floating peak steady and intervene only in falls or other traversal accidents within the Temple grounds that would otherwise kill someone. They do not stop a bad landing, protect someone from an attack, or save anyone outside the mountain. They let the danger happen until the final moment, then catch the falling person and put them somewhere safe, often humiliating. A novice may crash through an awning, land in a meal, or swing into a bell tower before a Catching Wind takes hold. The monks treat the elementals as mischievous elder companions, not servants.\n\nVisitors may eat, shop, talk, study, and ask for instruction without already belonging to the order. The Ascendants are recognized through a series of Descents: ordeals that send an aspirant out into the world to confront a real danger or protect people beyond the Temple. A Descent may involve demon remnants, raiders, monsters, tyrants, rescue work, or defending a community. The point is not one named enemy or a private victory, but using the discipline in a battle that matters. The candidate returns with witnesses, survivors, or other proof of what they did, and the Temple names them an Ascendant only after the deed is judged worthy. Training at the Temple alone is never enough.",
      "areas": {
        "The Ascension Court": {
          "description": "The summit of the Unmoored Mountain opens into a broad red-rock court above the cloud-sea. The Earth Ladders arrive at one edge, while bridges, rope swings, and narrow wall-routes lead toward the surrounding spires. Wind bells, shaded platforms, water basins, food sellers, musicians, trainees, condor handlers, and visiting adventurers fill the court throughout the day. It is the Temple's common ground: a place to eat, mingle, watch training, trade news, and find someone willing to explain what you are looking at.",
