      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A pygmy village at the foot of a copper-rich outcrop in the canyons, its low stone houses roofed in green-aged copper tiles and built to pygmy scale, with doorways a tall outsider must stoop through. The folk here are coppersmiths and miners, known across the region for fine, precise metalwork and a fierce independence. They dig the red rock for copper, work it in open-fronted shops along the lanes, and trade the finished goods to all who pass.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Copperstone is a safe, friendly, hardworking village and a pleasant stop in hard country. The pygmies here are proud, plain-spoken, and welcoming to a guest who does not talk down to them — an easy thing to do badly, given the difference in height, and a quick way to sour them. They keep to their craft and their independence and bow to no lord.\n\nThe village lives by copper: the mines in the outcrop, and the metalwork the smiths make of what comes up. The most gifted of the young leave to do finer work elsewhere — up the heights to the brass-cities and to the Order's workshops at the Citadel — and send their earnings home, and Copperstone is proud of them and quietly poorer for their leaving.\n\nThe one worry that hangs over the village is the copper itself. The old veins are running thin, and the miners must dig deeper each year to find good ore, down to where the rock grows hot and the ground turns unsound. The deep new cuts are dangerous — the heat, the bad air, the chance of a fall — and the elders argue over how far down is too far. Copperstone's copper is the root of much of the region's fine brasswork, and there is always honest work here for a steady hand: in the shops, down the mines, or guarding the goods on the road.",
      "areas": {
        "The Village Square": {
          "description": "The open heart of Copperstone, paved in copper-flecked stone that glints in the sun, ringed by pygmy-scaled buildings with their low doors and copper roofs. Market stalls and shopfronts crowd the edges, selling finished copperwork, tools, and goods to villagers and passing traders alike. A well stands at the center, and lanes run off between the close-built houses toward the workshops and the mines.",
          "paths": [
            "The Workshops",
            "The Mines",
            "The Waystation"
          ]
        },
        "The Workshops": {
          "description": "The crafting quarter, a row of open-fronted stone shops where the coppersmiths work at forge and bench, the lanes between them bright with hammering and the smell of hot metal. Fine and exceptional pieces hang on display along the fronts, and apprentices labor beside masters at every station. The finest work in the village is made and shown here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Square"
          ]
        },
        "The Mines": {
          "description": "The tunnels driven into the copper-rich outcrop above the village, their mouths shored with timber and marked with the miners' signs. The upper workings are old and well-worn, cool and safe; the newer cuts run far deeper, and the air coming up from them is warm and close. Carts and ore-tracks run from the tunnel-mouths down to the village below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Square"
          ]
        },
        "The Waystation": {
          "description": "A building set apart at the village edge, raised with high ceilings and large furniture for the comfort of the tall folk who come to trade — a plain practicality in a town built for pygmies. It serves as inn, trading-house, and meeting-place for outsiders, with rooms to let and a common board, and the road out runs from its door into the canyons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Square"
          ]
        }
      },
