    "Gesenthame": {
      "name": "Gesenthame",
      "x": 4,
      "y": 24,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Alar Coast",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The burned ruin of a great city, razed by the dragon within living memory when its princes refused tribute. Dig-crews work the rubble for its buried wealth under the eyes of the Church of the Pyre, which oversees the digging and takes first pick. At the city's heart stands the Black Fortress, unburned and shut — no force that has tried its gates has come back out.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The fire took the city in one night, and the scorch is still black on the stones. A rich city died with its wealth still in it.\n\nRed-cowls oversee the ruin — squads of four to six footsoldiers of the Church of the Pyre posted among the diggings, a grey-robed priest directing the work. The Church is searching for something it believes lies fused beneath the city, buried by the dragonfire, and every deep find goes to the priest before anyone else sees it. Red-cowls attack outsiders on sight.\n\nThe dig-crews are rival hired bands — humans, jackalkin, and rougher folk — digging under the Church's writ, claiming ground, and robbing or press-ganging the weak where the cowls aren't looking. Captives worked in the pits can be ransomed or broken out. The crews chain dig-beasts at the pits, heavy brutes worked as haulers and set loose as guards.\n\nLoot: fine swords and mail of the city's fallen soldiery, some of it still bearing enchantments; gilt temple ornament and idols the fire didn't touch; grave-gold; and in the deep cellars, stranger things — warded strongboxes no crew has opened, and relics the priests confiscate on sight. The great prize is unfound: the crowns and burning-jewels of the Azhakid Princes, who died with their city — never pulled from the ash, and said to hold fire the way other jewels hold light.\n\nJackal packs run the ruin, following the digging for what it turns up.",
      "areas": {
        "The Breach": {
          "description": "The pulled-down stretch of wall where the coast road enters the ruin. Rubble shifted into crude ramparts, tally-posts, and hide-banners marking dig-claims among the stones. Cart-tracks run in from the road and split toward the diggings. Open ground with long sightlines along the old wall-line.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cinders"
          ]
        },
        "The Cinders": {
          "description": "The burned city proper. Streets survive as straight lines through the rubble, house-cellars stand opened like graves, and dig-pits, spoil-heaps, and hand-cranked hoists dot the ruin for miles. The spoil-heaps and standing walls break sightlines everywhere — cover in every direction, and no way to see who else is working the same street.",
          "paths": [
            "The Breach",
            "The Deep Cellars",
            "Blackstone Square"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Cellars": {
          "description": "Vaulted undercrofts beneath the rich quarters, sealed by the fire rather than eaten by it. Black as pitch past the first turnings, half-dug, tight passages and low arches — no fighting room, and light gives you away. Rope-marks and abandoned tools show how far the digging got before it stopped.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cinders"
          ]
        },
        "Blackstone Square": {
          "description": "The great court below the fortress height, its stones burned black and open to the sky — wide, flat, and exposed, with nowhere to hide from the height above. The arms and bones of every force that ever tried the gates lie in windrows going back years. Above, the Black Fortress stands whole and shut, its gates unmarked by anything brought against them.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cinders"
          ]
        }
      },
