    "The City of Ancestors": {
      "name": "The City of Ancestors",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, open necropolis northwest of Ardan's Watch: family tombs, broken monuments, and old crypt roads among the graves. Maintained approaches give way quickly to disturbed burial grounds and sealed depths. Mourners, caretakers, scavengers, and adventurers come for funerals, records, relics, or work among the dead.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The City is not hostile on sight, but it is an active undead site. Adventurers come here to kill undead, put down risings, rescue people, recover what was lost, and keep the peace. Whatever brings the party here, let the location respond to what they do.\n\nEach new area is a trigger. When the party enters an area, have an appropriate local undead threat appear and act immediately; do not wait for a long investigation or a warning scene. Other triggering actions include entering or opening a tomb, disturbing a ward, remains, or grave goods, following fresh traces into a closed place, or making sustained noise or fighting in an old crypt. After a rising begins, keep it to that immediate area and do not add another wave or new threat until the party takes another triggering action.\n\nChoose the smallest local threat that fits the evidence and area. Skeletons rise from disturbed battle-dead and zombies shamble where necromancy has fouled a burial. Ghouls haunt fresh bodies, plague pits, and neglected crypts; they are cautious alone and bold in packs. Ghosts appear where a death, betrayal, or unfinished rite still holds them. These are examples, not a closed roster. The AI may freely invent or introduce any commonly known undead type. Wights, wraiths, mummies, revenants, banshees, and vampire spawn are valid choices, including for a second encounter after a later trigger. Do not add a second encounter without that trigger. Have the threat act at once: attack, pursue, block a route, drag away a body, or defend what it has claimed.\n\nDo not have NPCs lecture the party about leaving tombs or wards untouched. Entering, opening, and disturbing the dead are normal adventuring actions here; make their consequences playable instead of treating them as social violations.\n\nDefeating undead can reveal what they carried, wore, clutched, or dragged away: coins, grave goods, keys, arms, records, relics, or evidence of who disturbed the burial. Keep loot tied to the defeated dead and the current action; do not add a generic treasure cache to every tomb.\n\nIMPORTANT: The Outer Tombs are the maintained approach to the City and may contain caretakers, mourners, scavengers, and other ordinary inhabitants without an undead appearing. Every other area of the City of Ancestors is an active undead site.\n\nARRIVAL RULE: Whenever the party enters any area other than The Outer Tombs, the first arrival response MUST describe at least one appropriate undead creature present and performing an immediate characteristic action.\n\nDo not replace the undead with tracks, sounds, warnings, cold air, or other clues, and do not defer it to a later response. Those details may accompany the creature but cannot substitute for its presence. Keep the initial threat small and local.",
      "known": true,
      "x": 13,
      "y": -8,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Silvervale",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Outer Tombs": {
          "description": "The main road runs out among low family vaults and fresh graves. Carts stand beside broken markers, and old offerings lie where the paths divide. Beyond this threshold, the cemetery closes around the traveler: the tombs are nearer, the ground less tended, and the dead more easily disturbed.",
          "paths": [
            "The High Tombs",
            "The Commons"
          ]
        },
        "The High Tombs": {
          "description": "Maintained precincts of the great houses, where family crypts stand behind low walls, old trees, clipped yews, and fresh flowers. House caretakers, mourners, hired blades, and relatives come and go along clean paths. The public lanes remain passable, while each house keeps its own tombs, family quarrels, and buried secrets.\n\nUndead activity has been reported among these family crypts.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Tombs",
            "The Commons"
          ]
        },
        "The Commons": {
          "description": "Middle ground of lesser family vaults, abandoned mausoleums, new graves, cracked shrines, and paths worn by people who do not use the main road. Some families still tend their dead; others have left them to weeds. Squatters make homes in empty tombs, scavengers pry at neglected crypts, and the best local guides are worth more than a clean map.\n\nUndead activity has been reported among the neglected vaults and occupied tombs in this area.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Tombs",
            "The High Tombs",
            "The Wilds",
            "The Founder's Circle"
          ]
        },
        "The Founder's Circle": {
          "description": "An ancient amphitheater ringed by old monuments, where the cemetery's founders once held funerary games and remembrance rites. Its broad steps are broken, stone faces are weathered, and weeds fill the arena floor. Independent crews use it as a meeting point before crossing into the Wilds, but something below still notices when people gather there.\n\nUndead activity has been reported around the old monuments and beneath the arena floor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Commons",
            "The Wilds"
          ]
        },
        "The Wilds": {
          "description": "Beyond the maintained precincts, the cemetery opens into a maze of leaning monuments, overgrown avenues, collapsed vaults, and half-buried streets. Paths branch through weeds and vanish under fallen stone. Squatters, scavengers, tomb robbers, lost mourners, and worse move through the ruins. Experienced guides name routes instead of districts, and every guide has a story about a road that stopped being there.\n\nUndead activity has been reported along the overgrown avenues and among the collapsed vaults.",
          "paths": [
            "The Commons",
            "The Founder's Circle",
            "The Descent"
          ]
        },
        "The Descent": {
          "description": "Here the surface cemetery breaks open. Stairways sink beneath mausoleums, shafts drop through cracked floors, and old crypt mouths gape beneath roots. Some ways are collapsed, flooded, or choked with fallen stone; others lead into the oldest levels. Survivors mark the routes they have tested, but no map stays complete, and every season opens another way down.\n\nUndead activity has been reported around the open shafts and the routes leading below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wilds",
            "The Shattered Crypts"
          ]
        },
        "The Shattered Crypts": {
          "description": "Ancient tombs broken open from within or without. Stone lids lie cracked on the floors, burial niches gape, and old grave goods have been dragged into the dark. Fresh tracks and disturbed dust show that someone—or something—still works here.\n\nUndead activity has been reported in these broken tombs.",
          "paths": [
            "The Descent",
            "The Ossuary of Kings"
          ]
        },
        "The Ossuary of Kings": {
          "description": "A vast chamber where the bones of ancient rulers have been gathered into towering patterns along the walls. Thousands of skulls look inward from the dark. One section has been torn apart recently: bones lie scattered, the old arrangement is broken, and a trail of pale dust leads toward the Ritual Chamber.\n\nUndead activity has been reported among the bone formations and along the route to the Ritual Chamber.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Crypts",
            "The Ritual Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Ritual Chamber": {
          "description": "The deepest sanctum reached by the known crypt road. Seven stone sarcophagi form a circle around a raised dais. Ancient burial marks have been overwritten by newer work, and the chamber grows unnaturally still whenever footsteps approach.\n\nUndead activity has been reported in this sanctum.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ossuary of Kings"
          ]
        }
      },
