      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Once the fortress of House Relland, a newly wealthy river dynasty, Demon's Head Ruins was built around an ancient black-marble statue of a Demon-Prince of Ur as a declaration of the house's motto: \"Where others kneel, we build.\" An earthquake and catastrophic flood shattered the keep and drowned its grounds; the statue's colossal head now rests on a crumbled wall, leering outward at those who approach. Recently withdrawn waters have opened fresh routes into the ruin, drawing hostile salvage crews, bandits, kobolds, and other dangers into its flooded courts and broken halls.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Demon's Head Ruins is an evergreen low-level combat-and-discovery site, dangerous from the reed-choked approach through the surviving keep. Recently withdrawn waters expose mud-slick channels, broken causeways, flooded chambers, undermined stairs, and new routes. Shifting water and repeated incursions keep the local situation active.\n\nThe ruin is occupied by active claimants, ambushers, and defenders. Hostile forces include kobold crews, armed bandits and relic-hunters, drowners, stirges, darkmantles, gray oozes, rust monsters, animated armor, gargoyles, and other old fortress defenses. Scale individual threats for a low-level party while allowing deeper sections, alarms, terrain, and coordinated groups to raise the pressure.\n\nKobold crews are the most persistent organized presence, operating rough salvage dens throughout the broken fortress. Use scouts, skirmishers, trappers, heavies, sappers, shamans, and occasional champions according to the terrain and the party's depth into the ruin. Kobolds use alarms, traps, sappers, barricades, and coordinated attacks; rival crews can contest a route, a chamber, or a valuable find.\n\nARRIVAL RULE: On first arrival at the location and on first arrival at each area, physically establish one ordinary hostile group before stopping the arrival scene. Keep the first hostile group local and manageable while creating urgency. Kobolds might set snares, pry open a sealed door, drag a carved house-stone from the mud, or signal a nearby crew. Drowners might pull themselves from flooded masonry; stirge swarms might feed in the reeds; darkmantles might cling above a route; rust monsters might worry at gate fittings; old defenses might grind awake beneath mud and rubble.\n\nLet enemies respond to the party and to one another. Drowners exploit water and unstable ground; vermin and oozes infest neglected passages; fortress defenses protect old thresholds and valuable rooms. Encounters can alter routes, trigger alarms, change water flow, expose caches, or draw another hostile group into the scene. Keep each response focused on the current local threat, with another hostile group entering when the party changes area, triggers an alarm, disturbs a defensive point, or takes another consequential action.\n\nThe colossal head is visible from the Causeway and remains a landmark from the Flooded Court and the Relland Keep. Its broken wall provides ledges, cover, climbable marble, long sightlines, and ambush positions. The main material rewards lie in the Old Armory and the Relland Vaults, while the head concentrates the ruin's visual history, tactical movement, and story revelations.\n\nTie discoveries and rewards to the immediate place and active encounter: Relland coin and seals, arms and armor, family heirlooms, house records, maps, salvageable relics, and strange valuables recovered from flooded chambers. Repeated visits can reveal newly exposed routes, changed enemy positions, rival salvage operations, and deeper sections of the keep. Treat the five core areas as stable anchors; new exposed rooms, side passages, rival camps, or flood-opened chambers can branch from them when the ongoing situation calls for it.\n\nA first-arrival scene here is complete only after it brings one ordinary hostile group physically into view and states a total equal to all names under Party member(s), counting the PC and companions. Assign every enemy role within that stated group; it is the entire on-scene enemy roster.",
      "areas": {
        "The Causeway": {
          "description": "A raised stone road runs through reed marsh toward the fortress's broken outer wall. The waters have pulled back from the road, leaving black mud between the stones and shallow channels cutting across the approach. Above the breach, the colossal black-marble head of the Demon-Prince lies wedged on the crumbled wall, its face turned outward over the causeway. The road continues through the wall toward the Flooded Court.",
