    "The Sinkwood": {
      "name": "The Sinkwood",
      "x": -38,
      "y": -26,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Sinkwood is a still, sunken wood in the south of the region where the ground is failing into the dark. Trees stand at wrong angles with their roots bared, new pits open from one week to the next, and a wood that should be loud with birds is instead wrong-quiet, the air heavy and close and the light gone flat and grey beneath the leaning canopy. Pale fungus climbs the trunks and rings the sinkholes, and the deeper a traveler goes the more the wood seems to swallow sound, until his own footsteps come back muffled and slow.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE DRAIN. Where the rest of Jade Tarn is a country of mana welling up, the Sinkwood is the one place it runs the other way. The mages of Tarnholme hold it to be a sink — a low place in the region's magic, where mana does not rise but drains away and pools, still and stagnant, in the dark below. It is why the wood is so quiet: the magic that makes the rest of the region loud lies dead and heavy here. And it is where the region's loose spellforms drift to die. A spellform that escapes elsewhere and is not caught will, sooner or later, wander to the Sinkwood and run down like a clock, until the wood is thick with their slow-fading husks — drifting, half-dead castings caught in the trees like leaves caught in a drain.\n\nWHAT THE DRAIN HOLDS. The dying spellforms are not always harmless. A combat-spell run halfway down is still a danger, lashing out feebly at whatever passes; and the oldest and strongest of them sink deepest and last longest, pooled in the lowest hollows where the drain runs strongest. The slow-fading husk of a spellform, taken before it fades, is a spell caught at the point of death — bottled, it will work once more, weakly and wildly, for one who knows how to spend it. The pale fungus that rings the sinkholes feeds on the stagnant magic and is full of it: a cap eaten raw scatters the eater's own magic for an hour, so that a spell will not come when it is called.\n\nUNDER THE WOOD. The ground is failing because something below is hollowing it out. Whatever digs there works toward the deepest pools of drained magic, the still places where the oldest spent castings have gathered for an age, and the sinkholes that open week by week are the marks of its passage..",
      "areas": {
        "The Leaning Edge": {
          "description": "The near reach of the wood, where the healthy forest begins to tilt and sag toward the sinks. The trees lean at growing angles, the ground sounds hollow underfoot, and the birdsong falls away within a few paces of the treeline. Old gathering-paths wind in among the trunks, some of them ending abruptly at the lip of a new pit, and the light goes grey and flat under the canopy ahead.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stillhollows"
          ]
        },
        "The Stillhollows": {
          "description": "The heart of the failing wood, a low country of mossy hollows and bared roots and standing pits, where the ground has dropped and the trees stand at every wrong angle. Pale fungus rings the sinkholes and climbs the trunks, the air is heavy and close, and sound dies here — footsteps, voices, the wind in the leaves all come muffled and slow. The lowest hollows lie deepest in the flat grey quiet.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaning Edge",
            "The New Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The New Pits": {
          "description": "Where the ground is freshest broken, ragged holes opening into the dark with earth still crumbling at their rims and roots dangling into nothing. The walls of the pits are too smooth and too regular for natural collapse, and rough tunnels lead away from their floors, branching down into cold close passages where the grey quiet deepens toward dark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stillhollows"
          ]
        }
      },
