      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The largest city in the Shadow Summits, built up a valley so steep and narrow that its southern, north-facing wall never sees the sun. This is the Shadowside, where black stone and silver spires climb the cliff in foggy terraces lit by caged mage-lights. Across the cold river, the northern, south-facing Sunside catches a few hours of thin light a day, and serfs work its terraced farms under the city's gaze. Vampire lords hold estates on the heights above, and their word is the only law.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Four houses hold estates over Nocturne and contend for it: House Vyrel, the oldest and most set in its ways; House Setharis, the most political; House Malren, who study the curse itself; and House Kaeris, who built their wealth on trade and keep the Crescent House. They are moon elf vampires, made by the Lady of Blood in the Elven Schism or sired by those who were. They are rivals, scheming against one another for territory and standing through marriage, murder, and proxy, and would turn on each other if the cost were ever low enough.\n\nThe lords feed on the people they rule. Each takes a fixed levy of blood from his serfs every year — a set number chosen by lottery. The lot is drawn at each dark of the moon, thirteen times a year, and those drawn go up to the estate and do not come back; a great valley gives several names a drawing, a small one gives a single name. In exchange the lord holds the valley against strigoi and worse. The Sunside families lose their own to this every year and live with it; most have known no other arrangement, and the levy is the price of being spared the older horror of being hunted at random.\n\nThe lower city is where a stranger can make a life. Moon elf and human serfs, the lords' outsider servants and soldiers, and traders passing through fill the Shadowside terraces and the riverside. Nocturne takes in the people the rest of Larion has cast out — exiles, fugitives, the cursed, the condemned — because the lords use them and the Syndicate hires them. The Guest House is where most wash up first.\n\nThe Crescent House, kept by House Kaeris, is where anything is bought that no open market would stock: blood magic, bindings, hexes, bound creatures, and the silver and moonstone weapons hunters need against the curse.\n\nThe Crimson Company is the lords' soldiery. They serve the lords collectively and are hired out valley to valley, distinct from the Syndicate's own guards. The Syndicate itself runs heavy here, with a seat in the lower city and its regional fortress at nearby Villa Parhelion.\n\nIMPORTANT: Nocturne is a dangerous, cruel place, but a stranger who is useful and has a reason to be here is tolerated - but watched. The lords hire outsiders, the Syndicate hires outsiders, the serf-towns trade with whoever passes. The League of Adventurers has no standing here and no agents to send. Outside the lit valleys, strigoi and ghouls hunt the passes.",
      "areas": {
        "Shadow's Edge Market": {
          "description": "The market spreads along the valley floor where the river runs cold and fast off the peaks, and the shadow of the summit falls across it like a drawn line — stalls on the near side in perpetual cold blue dusk, the south slope across the water still catching its thin hours of light. Traders deal in grain, cloth, livestock, and metalwork; serfs come down from the Sunside farms to sell and buy; and the Syndicate keeps a counting-house among the warehouses that handles a great deal more than grain. The mountain road comes down into the square through a fortress cut in the rock. Above it all the city climbs the sunless cliff.",
