      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "factions": [],
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A crescent valley high in the peaks, split by its own walls. The Sunside terraces catch a few hours of thin light and carry the vineyards that made Velmere famous; the wall opposite never sees the sun at all, and half the village stands under it in permanent blue shade, lamplit and awake after dark. A cold deep lake lies between them, crossed by ferry at every hour. Chateau Velmere rises on the shaded slope in black stone and silver, home to the lady who has held this valley six hundred years and never once let it fall — which is why the wine goes to distant kings and the alchemy is better than the wine. The Crimson Company keeps a retained hall on the shaded shore and hires. Above the roofline the mountain is another country, and the outlying terraces lose stock, travelers, and sometimes a family.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lady Isaveau ni Mirevaux has held Velmere six hundred years and has never lost it. More than once, when a rival's ghouls came over the ridge in numbers, she has opened the chateau to the whole valley, shut the gate herself, and stood at it. Every adult here was a child in her great hall on one of those nights, listening to something on the other side of that door. When they say the Lady provides, they are describing a night they remember.\n\nEvery other valley in these mountains has been taken at least once. Velmere has not, and six unbroken centuries is what it takes to breed a grape nobody else has and refine a tincture nobody else can make. The wealth is not why the valley is safe; it is the evidence. Other lords take a fixed levy in blood and Isaveau does not, which is why people would rather be her subject than anyone else's, and why her neighbors find her irritating.\n\nThe valley is two valleys. Sunside is worked in daylight by vintner families whose knowledge goes back generations. The shaded half never sees the sun: lamps at noon, the pressing-houses running all night, the Company's hall, and the Good Night, which never closes. A visitor sees whichever half they came for.\n\nIsaveau built the finest daylight valley in the north and cannot walk into it. Her factors walk the rows at midday and describe them to her afterward. This is the most reliable way there is to move her, and almost nobody has worked it out.\n\nThe Crimson Company serves the lords collectively and is chartered against the hungry, not against each other — the only reason a few dozen immortals tolerate an army none of them owns. Isaveau retains the Velmere hall, so in practice those blades are hers. It is also why raids take the shape they do: Val Cendrac, over the range west, sends ghouls, and vermin can be killed without anyone admitting a war. Nobody is ever answerable for the dead.\n\nPeople still go missing. Not on a schedule and not for a reason anyone can give — a boat crew, a girl on the high path, a trader who left at dusk. The valley is protected, not safe, and everyone here has made their peace with the difference.\n\nThe High Caves above the shaded slope are a Schism-era prison with ways driven in from half the mountain, and cannot be sealed. They are the hole in the valley's wall, and something is always living in them. The Company roots it out every few seasons and is paid for maintenance rather than victory. Velmere holds its floor and loses its edges: stock, then travelers, then a family on an outlying terrace.\n\nThere is no militia and no watch, deliberately. Six centuries of raids leave habits rather than institutions: shutters that bar from inside, everyone knowing where the children go, a silvered blade over a mantel that a grandfather used. Nobody here is a soldier and nearly everybody is armed.\n\nThe Good Night is where the valley finds out who came back. It keeps a book on the Company — who returns, who gets named, how many — and money changes hands over it. It is not cruelty; it is how a room carries a steady count of the dead. A good night is a night the bell did not go.\n\nThe tinctures and the poisons are worth more than the wine, and Isaveau trades them selectively; getting onto her list is worth a fortune. Bloodwine is a chateau product and is not for sale, and being poured a glass is a promotion the whole room registers.\n\nThe lake is where the chateau puts what it has finished with, and has been for six centuries. Locals fish it and never swim in it, and nobody says why out loud.\n\nCendrac is larger and richer, its forges arm most of the Summits, Velmere buys nearly all its weapons there, and its lord has asked Isaveau to marry him for six hundred years.\n\nIMPORTANT: Velmere is safe for a stranger who keeps the peace. Lady Isaveau does not attack guests or the useful, her subjects are not afraid of her, and nothing in the village below is hunting anyone. The danger is the mountain, the outlying terraces after dark, and crossing her.",
      "areas": {
        "Sunside Landing": {
          "description": "The daylight shore, where the road down from Moonrise Pass finally reaches flat ground. A market square of trestle tables and awnings runs to the water: wine factors tasting from the barrel, tincture sellers with locked cases, mule trains loading for the pass, and the whole valley's business done in the few hours of thin light it gets. The ferry works from a stone slip at the end of the square and crosses to the shaded shore at every hour of the day and night. Stone houses with slate roofs and flower boxes climb the slope behind, and above them the terraces go up in steps until the vines give out. Boats go out most mornings. The lake is very clear and very deep, the fish run fat, and nobody swims in it.",
