      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A mountain of abandoned silver mines in the high peaks, its diggings long given over to the dead. Feral ghouls den in the old workings by the hundred, and lately they range farther down the slopes and bolder than they used to. The silver veins were never worked out — the miners fled the ghouls, not a dry seam — and the metal still in the rock is the same metal that kills what guards it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Charnel Mountain was a working silver mine until the dead filled it. Now feral ghouls pack the tunnels in their hundreds, hunting in flocks through the dark, and the press has been worsening: the ghouls range lower and bolder each season, and the lords' valleys nearest the mountain feel it.\n\nNothing drives them. The deep workings run toward the thin places where the curse of the Shadow Summits pools heaviest, and the ghouls simply breed faster than the mountain can hold — the curse thickening, spilling its dead down the slopes. There is no master here to find and no bargain to strike, only more ghouls than there used to be.\n\nThe silver is the draw. Rich veins still run through the upper workings, abandoned in haste and never reclaimed, and silver is the metal that bites the ghoul and the vampire alike — so a delver fights the dead for the very thing that kills them, and what comes out of Charnel arms the hunters of the whole region. The lords tolerate prospectors and feral-cullers on the mountain; the Crimson Company will sometimes clear the lower workings when the ghouls press too near a held valley, but no one holds Charnel, and no one goes deep and comes back rich without paying for it.\n\nIMPORTANT: the ghouls of Charnel Mountain default to hostile and do not parley. They are mindless and serve no one, hunting in packs through the tunnels, drawn to warmth and movement, and bolder in the dark than in what little light reaches the upper diggings.",
      "areas": {
        "The Headworks": {
          "description": "The mine mouth on the mountain's shoulder, where the old surface buildings stand gutted — winch-houses, ore-sheds, a collapsed counting-office, all stripped and weathered. Prospectors and feral-cullers camp among the ruins, arming and resting before they go in, and trading what they bring out. The diggings open below as a black throat in the rock, and the cold air that breathes out of it carries the smell of the dead.",
          "paths": [
            "The Upper Workings"
          ]
        },
        "The Upper Workings": {
          "description": "The first levels of the mine, a maze of timber-shored galleries and ore-stopes where the richest abandoned veins still show silver in the rock. Picks and spilled ore lie where the miners dropped them when they ran. Ghouls hunt here, fewer than in the deep but bold enough, and the silver that draws delvers is the same metal that cuts the dead — so the work is done with a blade in one hand. The galleries branch and drop toward the lower dark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Headworks",
            "The Deep Diggings"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Diggings": {
          "description": "The lowest worked levels, where the air goes foul and the timber rots and the ghouls den in their hundreds. The tunnels here run toward the oldest, deepest stone, and the dead are thickest near the bottom — packs hunting in the black, the floor thick with the bones of the eaten. The richest silver of all lies in these levels, and so do the worst odds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Upper Workings"
          ]
        }
      },
