    "New Anthara": {
      "name": "New Anthara",
      "x": -21,
      "y": -13,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Ntar Vanna",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "New Anthara is a great free city of canals and quays built against the Whitemount, the pale stone ridge where the River Saranadi meets the sea. It is the richest port in the south and the gateway to the jungle interior, a loud, thriving melting-pot of a dozen peoples, demonborn most of all. The city is famous up and down the coast for its tame infernal beasts, which work the docks and the streets and answer to demonborn handlers. No crown rules New Anthara; it is a prosperous, lawless, opportunity-rich place where powerful families and crews make their own way.",
      "hiddenInfo": "New Anthara was founded by the Dravanya, the Masterless — demonborn who freed themselves of the Demon-Princes and built this city so they would never be ruled again. Their creed is the city's soul: Ur will not have them back, and it will not have this land. They keep Larion free of the Primordial Plane and stand against any who would open the door to it, the Children of Ur above all.\n\nThe Dravanya are the masters of fighting Ur with Ur's own power. Where the Children let the infernal master them, the Dravanya master it: they train and keep the demon-beasts — hellhounds for keepers and guards, imps for messengers and errands, heavier beasts to haul the cargo — and they teach a student to wield a cursed blade or an infernal art and stay himself rather than be eaten by it. They will cleanse a corrupted thing or train a bearer to carry it free. This is the work of Dravanya Hold, and a newcomer earns a place among them by standing against Ur in the field.\n\nThe river brings Ur-things down out of the ruins, and the Dravanya want every one of them brought to the Hold to be mastered and turned to good use. The danger is what slips past them: relics smuggled through Old Anthara across the river, sold to the Syndicate to move abroad, or worse, to the Children of Ur, who want the corruption left whole and working. Both trades run side by side and everyone knows it.\n\nNo one governs the city day to day. It runs like a frontier: the great families and trade-crews hold their own ground and make their own justice, the Dravanya hold the city to its creed without ruling its streets, and a newcomer who keeps out of trouble finds a land of opportunity. The peoples of the river all pass through — Vanarin and gajanari come down to trade and to feud where they meet, frogkin pilots and traders work the canals, serpentkin bring medicine and poison down from the mesa, and humans, halflings, and outsiders of every kind crowd the quays.",
      "areas": {
        "The Dock Market": {
          "description": "Where the Saranadi meets the sea: ocean ships and jungle boats crowd the quays, and traders sell straight off their decks and from stone stalls along the water — jungle fruit, spice, timber, reagents, live creatures, river fish, and relics out of the deep jungle laid out beside charm-sellers and reagent-stalls. Tame infernal beasts work the docks among the crowd. A demonborn beast-handler will sell a trained imp or hound.",
          "paths": [
            "The Burned Garden",
            "Dravanya Hold",
            "Old Anthara"
          ],
          "images": {
            "establishingShot": {
              "crop": {
                "focus": {
                  "x": 50,
                  "y": 50
                },
                "zoom": 100
              },
              "imageUrl": "https://images.aidungeon.com/generated_content/0/images/area_images/845ff51e-265e-426d-9f28-c892a28e2364.png"
            }
          }
        },
        "The Burned Garden": {
          "description": "Once a noble estate that reached for power over the free city, stormed and burned and now the finest public garden in New Anthara — terraced green, water channels, and open pavilions where the city comes to eat, drink, argue, and celebrate. Cook-fires and wine-sellers crowd the paths, musicians play under the pavilions, and a corner is given over to a fighting-ring where the crews settle scores and stake coin on bouts. Word and work pass freely across the tables here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dock Market"
          ],
          "images": {
