    "Mounagiri": {
      "name": "Mounagiri",
      "x": 16,
      "y": 32,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Ntar Vanna",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Mounagiri is a settlement of the Mounari, a chameleon-kin people, built into the sheer walls of a river gorge. Houses cling to the cliff at every angle, vine bridges span the gap, and the river runs far below. The Mounari are the finest climbers and infiltrators in the south, and the gorge is home and school both, where they raise their young to the discipline and teach it to the few outsiders they judge worthy.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Mounari are sworn against the Ur-trade — the demonic things the Children of Ur dig out of the ruins: the black Ur-ichor, the wakeful idols, the cursed arms that corrupt whoever takes them up. The Mounari hunt that trade with the one craft suited to it, moving unseen through jungle and ruin to steal the corruption back and destroy it before it can spread. They go after the cult's diggers, the shipments on the water, and the houses that fence the goods. The Mounari wage a quiet war on a cult and on rich and dangerous fences who would see it burned.\n\nThe Mounari teach climbing, stillness, and moving unseen, and to outsiders only the lesser version of it — the chameleon-craft that lets a non-Mounari blur against stone comes only after long training. A newcomer is taken in, tested, and taught by degrees; those who prove worthy and hold to the cause are trusted with the real work, the strikes against the trade itself.\n\nThe masters set the trials, and the trials are the war: reach a place no one can reach, retrieve a thing unseen, follow a shipment, get into a Children dig and out again with what they raised. The proving grounds are the region itself — the Swallowed Cities, the upriver jungle, the heights of the river citadels, the Hookmarsh.\n\nThe Mounari live their whole lives on the cliff and are quietly delighted by an outsider's fear of the drop. A guest clinging to the rock while they lounge at the lip of a lethal fall is, to them, a fine and reliable entertainment, and they will not be hurried into sympathy about it.\n\nThe order's worst enemy is its own. A Mounari who breaks the discipline and sells the craft — to a fence, to the cult, to anyone who pays — is the one threat the masters cannot simply hide from, because a fallen one knows every way they move.",
      "areas": {
        "The Gorge Rim": {
          "description": "The lip of the gorge, where the jungle gives way to open air and the whole vertical village shows below: houses fixed to the cliff faces, vine bridges crossing the gap, and the river a thin line at the bottom. A single switchback trail starts down from here, narrow and steep, the only walked way in.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cliff Village"
          ]
        },
        "The Cliff Village": {
          "description": "Houses clinging to the gorge walls at every angle — on horizontal ledges, against vertical faces, tucked beneath overhangs — joined by vine bridges and carved steps. The Mounari move across the faces in three dimensions, shifting color against the rock. Quarters for outsider students sit at the village edge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gorge Rim",
            "The Big Ledge",
            "The Training Ground"
          ]
        },
        "The Big Ledge": {
          "description": "The village common, which the Mounari call the Big Ledge: a natural shelf of rock partway down the gorge wall, cook-fires and shared mats laid across it, open at its edge to a sheer drop to the river far below. There is no rail and no wall. To the Mounari it is broad and comfortable ground, and they take their ease at the very lip of it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cliff Village"
          ]
        },
        "The Training Ground": {
          "description": "The school's working heart, set across a sheer face at the gorge's narrowest point. The rock is carved with holds and trial-routes climbing from simple to nearly impossible, and worn ledges below serve as ground for drilling stillness and breath. The drop from the routes is sheer to the river.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cliff Village"
          ]
        }
      },
