    "Gorren Crossing": {
      "name": "Gorren Crossing",
      "x": 18,
      "y": -35,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling frontier town at the ford of the River Gorren, where the orc steppe meets the settled south. No clan claims it. Every clan trades here. The old stone center was built by merchants generations ago; the rest grew up around it in timber and hide and optimism. Gorren Crossing runs on iron, horses, cattle, and the fact that everyone needs a place to do business on neutral ground. Duels in the street are legal. Theft gets you hanged. Everything in between is negotiable.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Gorren Crossing exists in a political gap. No orc clan claims sovereignty, but Clan Drokash considers the town within their sphere of influence—a position Clan Vorren increasingly contests. The town's continued independence depends on being useful to everyone and owned by no one.\n\nVorren's Hold, the stone fortress of the settled iron-builders, lies upriver in the iron hills; Camp Drokash, the great tent-city of the roving horse-lords, moves with the season out on the open steppe. Both sell their goods through Gorren—Vorren iron and Drokash horses—and both keep factors and kin in the town. The clans welcome any outsider who will fight for the Wold, and the work that draws adventurers to the steppe—hunting the beasts that prey on the herds, riding against the Oathbreakers in the south, holding the borders against raiders—is first heard of here, in the town's taverns and from the clans' agents.\n\nGovernance is layered and informal. The Watch keeps rough order—they'll break up a riot and hang a thief, but an honest fight between willing parties isn't their concern. They're funded by the mercantile houses of the Stone Quarter, which means they serve mercantile interests first. The bounty-hunters of the Crossing Court handles disputes for those rich enough to bring them.\n\nBeyond the Stone Quarter, real authority belongs to the ward bosses—local figures with no legal standing who collect protection fees and settle disputes. In Downwind Ward, the boss rules through intimidation and consistency. In Sunset Ward, the boss rules through popularity and loyalty. The two don't get along.\n\nMercantile houses hire mercenaries to protect their interests, which creates its own problems—armed enforcers loyal only to money. And in the gaps between the houses, the ward bosses, and the Watch, the Syndicate works the town's underside: smuggling goods past the customs shacks, fencing what the wards skim and what raiders sell, lending at the knife's edge, and buying the quiet cooperation of whoever can be bought.\n\nThe population is mixed: humans, halflings, orcs who've settled permanently (viewed with anything from tolerance to contempt by clan riders), Whitehold dwarves running trading posts, beastfolk up from the grass sea, and every kind of frontier drifter. Orc clan riders pass through constantly, and rivalries between Vorren and Drokash riders spill into taverns and streets regularly—brawls and contests of pride, not killing, for the clans hold their feud short of blood and settle the rest at the Gods-Anvil.\n\nDespite the disorder, enormous wealth moves through Gorren Crossing—iron from the Mogra mines, horses and livestock from the steppe, trade goods up from Westmarch and the settled south and across from the dwarf-holds of the Whiteholds. It's rough, rich, and alive.",
      "areas": {
        "The River Market": {
          "description": "The heart of Gorren Crossing: a packed strip along the south bank of the ford, where the flat-bottomed ferries land and the road becomes the town. Stalls, trestle tables, and blankets spread on mud run the length of the bank—iron tools, weapons, leather, fur, dwarf-wrought goods, southern luxuries, charms and remedies of dubious merit. Ferrymen shout for fares at the landing, customs men work the warehouse row, drovers and traders and orc riders shoulder through the crowd, and the noise carries clear across the river. No livestock—that's the Stockyards. Everything else is here, and so is everyone.",
