    "Gods-Anvil": {
      "name": "Gods-Anvil",
      "x": -16,
      "y": -16,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A flat-topped mesa rising from the open steppe, visible for miles in every direction. Orc creation myth holds that the gods forged the first orcs on its summit. No clan claims Gods-Anvil. All clans hold it sacred. When a Moot is called, the clans ride in from every corner of the Wold to camp at the mesa's base and settle their affairs on the summit above. Between Moots, a handful of keepers tend the site and the trails. No permanent settlement. No fortification. To build on Gods-Anvil would be sacrilege.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Gods-Anvil is the political and spiritual heart of the Wold, and it is safe ground. The first law here is the truce: no blood may be shed on or around the mesa, by anyone, for any cause. Breaking that truce is the gravest shame an orc can earn, and the offender's whole clan bears it. Even the bitterest rivals keep this law. The Oathbreakers—orcs cast out of the clans for breaking their oaths—are not welcome here, for the truce is the very bond they broke.\n\nA Moot can be called by any chief with a grievance that touches more than one clan, and the great Moots of spring and autumn draw every clan in the region. On the summit, chiefs sit and speak in a ring of ancient standing stones; any orc may climb up and be heard, though interrupting a chief carries consequences. Non-orcs may attend only as the guest of a chief, which is a high honor—and Gods-Anvil is where an outsider who has fought for the Wold can be granted real standing among the clans, sworn and witnessed on sacred ground.\n\nThe rivalry between Clan Vorren and Clan Drokash runs through every Moot: Drokash expects to speak last and loudest as old tradition allows, and Vorren increasingly refuses to defer. In the camps below, riders of the two clans clash in contests that are friendly only on the surface. But here the feud stays in words and trials and wagers—never in blood, because blood on this ground is unthinkable. This is the pressure-valve that keeps two proud clans from open war.\n\nThe keepers are a small order of all clans who tend the mesa, guard the trails, and oversee the trials and oaths. They answer to no chief and are respected by all.",
      "areas": {
        "The Base Camps": {
          "description": "Open ground around the mesa's foot, worn flat by generations of use. Each clan has its traditional camping ground, marked by firepits and picket-lines that never fully disappear between Moots. During a Moot, this becomes a temporary city—thousands of orcs, their herds, their banners. Contest rings for wrestling, riding, and archery. Cook-fires and song circles and arguments that stop just short of violence. Between Moots, a few keepers maintain the trails and tend the site.",
          "paths": [
            "The Summit"
          ]
        },
        "The Summit": {
          "description": "The flat top of the mesa, reached by a single trail carved into the rock face. A ring of standing stones marks the Moot Circle where chiefs sit and speak. At the center stands the Anvil—a great slab of dark stone, flat-topped and roughly rectangular, split down the middle by a crack that legend says was struck by a god's hammer. Oaths sworn on the Anvil are the most binding words in orc culture. On clear days you can see the steppe in every direction to the horizon.",
          "paths": [
            "The Base Camps"
          ]
        }
      },
