      "radius": 3,
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": false,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The one fixed place in the Shadelands — a vast ruined palace that Lord Rhadan holds against the grey in service to the Black Tower. At its heart, a round courtyard catches the Beacon: a single shaft of cold light falling from the black clouds, the only safe ground in the realm and the only way back to the living world. The Hound guards the court against the dead, and Rhadan holds the rest. Those who cross the Pit arrive here, and set out from here when their work takes them deeper into the grey.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lord Rhadan's Court is the Black Tower's foothold in the land of the dead — an embassy, a waystation, and the only stable point in a realm with no fixed geography.\n\nThe Beacon is the way in and the way home: a crosser from the Pit of Shadow lands in the courtyard, and to return stands in the light and wills themselves back. If the Beacon ever failed, anyone in the Shadelands would be stranded. The Hound — a great dark beast bound to the light — devours any shade that crosses into the courtyard uninvited, and so keeps the transit point clear. It lets the living pass, and watches them every step.\n\nRhadan is not a king of the dead and does not rule the Shadelands. He was a king in life, in an age so old its name is lost even here, and one of the sworn fellowship of the hero who threw down the Fourth Empire; his charge was the border of the dead, and he has held it ever since. His hall is not a dreamed memory like the rotting palaces around it — it is a watchtower he holds by will against the grey, and the one place in the Reaches that is not going to ruin. He is bound to this post and cannot leave it; were he to fall, the Court and the Beacon would fall with him. He knows the realm as no living thing can — which dreamed worlds have soured, which shades have turned dangerous, where a disturbance among the dead is bleeding up into the living world — and those who come to end such things start with him.\n\nThe dreamed worlds of individual shades are not reached by walking. A crosser learns from Rhadan, or from the Tower, which shade is the root of their trouble, and crosses toward it from here — the realm folding them toward the place that shade dreams. Each such projection is its own delve, stable only while its shade holds together.",
