    "The Imperial Baths": {
      "name": "The Imperial Baths",
      "region": "Grell Hollows",
      "x": -34,
      "y": 31,
      "radius": 3,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "basicInfo": "A vast pleasure district of the old Fourth Empire, buried beneath Silvervale by the Flood and reopened beneath great sinkholes. Green daylight falls through the shafts onto moss-soft marble, wild pale gardens, steaming bath-halls, and a ruined arena. The warm water and shelter make the Baths the most fought-over place in Grell Hollows: kobold hosts, goblin crews, and other underfolk all claim pieces of the old resort.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Imperial Baths are not a safe settlement, but they are not a static kill room or automatically hostile on arrival. This is an active contested ruin: Grell kobold hosts, goblin salvage crews, splinter raiders, cave creatures, and delvers all use parts of it. Let the site respond to what the party does.\n\nWhen the party first enters an area for the first time, always stage one immediate local challenge in that area. The threat must act now: attack, pursue, seize salvage, cut off a route, drive the party away from the warm water, or trigger a piece of old machinery. Choose the smallest threat that fits. At The Broken Gate use kobold scouts, skirmishers, or trappers; in The Garden Halls use kobold scouts, shriekers, violet fungus, or a creature in the growth; in The Bath of Princes use kobold heavies or raiders defending the pools, or a danger caused by steam, water, or bad air; in The Ur Arena use a kobold champion or heavy, a chained or escaped beast, or a half-working arena mechanism. Do not wait for a long investigation or warning scene before the first challenge.\n\nAfter that first challenge, do not add another encounter merely because the party won. A later encounter requires a new triggering action: entering another area; opening a sealed door or beast-pen; following fresh tracks into a closed space; disturbing, stripping, or carrying off imperial fittings or salvage; making sustained noise, bright light, fire, or combat; resting conspicuously in the baths or gardens; or pursuing a fleeing group. Each trigger produces at most one immediate local encounter. Keep it in the area where the trigger occurred, and do not stack waves without another trigger.\n\nUse escalation only when the party's actions justify it. A quiet intrusion may draw scouts, a warning, a retreat, or a bargain. Repeated noise, theft, injury, or attacks can bring a larger raiding party, a kobold heavy or champion, a rival host seeking the same claim, or a deeper creature displaced from the growth or pools. The Baths are contested, so different groups may react differently; do not turn every threat into one unified faction. If the party negotiates, hides, retreats, or leaves a threat unresolved, honor that choice and let later consequences follow from what was actually disturbed.\n\nDo not have local NPCs lecture the party about avoiding the ruins. Entering, opening, salvaging, and taking risks are normal adventuring actions here; make the consequences playable. Loot should be tied to the defeated threat, abandoned camp, disturbed fitting, or current action, not a generic treasure cache. The warm baths can provide shelter only while the party can hold the approaches.",
