    "The Imperial Arsenal": {
      "name": "The Imperial Arsenal",
      "region": "Grell Hollows",
      "x": 22,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 1,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "basicInfo": "A buried military complex of the old Fourth Empire, hidden behind massive gatehouses and a maze of stone courtyards. Racks of rusted weapons, toppled soldier-statues, sealed storehouses, and a command hall lie beyond the gates. The halls still bear the order of an army that vanished, though recent movement has disturbed the dust.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Imperial Arsenal is the provisional command center of the unified kobold host. It is an active military ruin, not a safe settlement and not a static kill room. The old complex, the new host, and the hostile stone-boring beetles drawn by Big Buzzer all respond to intrusion. Let the Arsenal's defenses reflect what the party touches, breaks, steals, or announces.\n\nWhen the party first enters an area for the first time, always stage one immediate local challenge in that area. The threat must act now: attack, pursue, raise an alarm, cut off a route, activate a signal, or force the party out of a defensible position. At The Arsenal Gate use kobold scouts, skirmishers, or trappers; in The Muster Yard use kobold heavies, raiders, or a champion drilling a small force; in The War Stores use kobold sappers, a rust monster, or a dangerous failure among the old arms; in The Boring Hall use a hostile stone-boring beetle attacking on sight, a Buzzer disturbance, or kobold sappers; in The Command Hall use a kobold champion, heavy, or organized guard defending the court. Do not wait for a long investigation or warning scene before the first challenge.\n\nAfter that first challenge, do not add another encounter merely because the party won. A later encounter requires a new triggering action: entering another area; taking or damaging imperial weapons, armor, standards, maps, or command fittings; crossing a marked defensive line; disturbing Big Buzzer or a fresh bore; breaking a sealed signal fitting; making sustained noise, bright light, fire, or combat; resting inside the complex; issuing a public challenge; or pursuing a fleeing kobold. Each trigger produces at most one immediate local encounter. Keep it in the area where the trigger occurred, and do not stack waves without another trigger.\n\nEscalate only when the party's actions justify it. A quiet intrusion may draw a sentry, a warning, a retreat, a bargain, or a rival host seeking leverage. Repeated attacks, theft, injury, or interference with the Boring Hall can bring coordinated skirmishers, heavies, sappers, a champion, a collapse, or a larger organized force. Do not treat every kobold as equally loyal to the warlord: fear, equipment, rivalry, and grandiose belief all matter. A stone-boring beetle can create a new danger, but it is a hostile animal attracted and irritated by Big Buzzer, not a machine the kobolds control.\n\nAfter the unified host is broken, let the Arsenal return to contested, factional Grell play unless a later event establishes another occupant. Loot should be tied to the defeated defenders, opened stores, broken command, or current action, not a generic treasure cache. Do not have local NPCs lecture the party about leaving the military ruin alone; make the consequences playable.",
      "areas": {
        "The Arsenal Gate": {
          "description": "A massive gatehouse of fitted imperial stone, its doors tall enough for a human army and scarred by generations of forced entry. Toppled soldier-statues flank the approach, while crude kobold perches and watch-slits have been built into the old walls. The halls beyond carry the smell of oil, rust, and disturbed dust.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Boring Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Muster Yard": {
          "description": "A broad stone courtyard where old formations once gathered. Ranks of oversized armor, shields, and weapons stand propped against the walls, mixed with patched kobold standards and rope-secured salvage. The yard is large enough for drilling, mustering, and a loud argument between rival hosts.",
          "paths": [
            "The Arsenal Gate",
            "The War Stores",
            "The Command Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The War Stores": {
          "description": "Long storehouses packed with imperial arms, empty racks, bent shields, and piles of metal sorted according to whoever last claimed the room. Rust blooms across neglected weapons while kobold sappers strip useful parts from anything they cannot carry. Some sealed chests still bear the marks of the old army.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Boring Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Boring Hall": {
          "description": "A deep Fourth Empire hall where surveyors and stonecutters bored passages into the buried city. Dozens of old tunnel mouths converge here, their rims pale with stone dust and their floors worn by traffic from below. At the center, on a squat bronze-and-stone plinth, rests Big Buzzer: a directional ground-vibration artifact the kobolds recovered from the Arsenal and carry to chosen surface sites. Its pulses attract and irritate stone-boring beetles but do not command them. Kobold sappers use hand tools, rope, braces, and scavenged timber to exploit the beetles' fresh tunnels; the floor trembles whenever the Buzzer is active.",
          "paths": [
            "The Arsenal Gate",
            "The War Stores",
            "The Command Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Command Hall": {
          "description": "A high hall with a raised imperial dais, walls built for maps and standards, and a throne-sized chair dragged from some larger court. New banners and salvaged armor crowd the old military reliefs. The room can be a war council, a court, or a battlefield depending on who has reached it first.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Boring Hall"
          ]
        }
      },
