    "Mirgreth Mire": {
      "name": "Mirgreth Mire",
      "x": 15,
      "y": -42,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Duchy of Drusavia",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Mirgreth Mire is poor, wet farmland in southeastern Drusavia: reed beds, black ditches, eel-traps, and hamlets on pilings, joined by raised causeways across the fen. The fenfolk keep old customs with the fen's dead — offerings left at ancient tribute stones, doors marked, the causeways not walked after dark — and they keep them for good reason.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mirgreth is poor, wet farmland the duchy has never much protected, and the fenfolk long ago made their own peace with what shares the fen. The wet ground holds old dead — drowned dead from before the duchy, and the unaccounted dead the purges pushed into the water — and the fenfolk keep them quiet with courtesies: offerings of bread, milk, and small silver left at the tribute stones, doors marked, the causeways not walked after dark. Nobody explains the customs and nobody argues with them. The Temple calls it superstition; the Shadow Inquisition has never once told the fenfolk to stop.\n\nThe fenfolk are human, closemouthed with strangers, and unimpressed by the duchy — help has never come from Drusavia and none is expected. They are not unfriendly, only practical: a stranger who lends a hand, keeps the customs, and doesn't ask stupid questions will find a dry bed and a hot meal. One who mocks the stones will find every door shut.\n\nIMPORTANT: The fen's dead rise at night from the black water — drowned dead, heavy and silent, that drag the living into the ditches and meres. Will-o'-wisps drift over the deep fen, luring travelers off the causeways into drowning ground. Both keep to the wet and the dark: the causeways are safe by day, and a marked door with a kept threshold is not entered. Where the courtesies are kept, the dead mostly let the living be; where offerings have stopped or been defiled, they hunt.\n\nThe old peace is fraying. Offerings that held for generations are lately refused — stones found overturned, gifts untouched, dead walking where they never walked — and the fenfolk don't know why. Cultists of the Gravebound are working the deep fen: defiling tribute stones to break the old courtesies, raising the drowned dead where they can, and coming to frightened households as friends, saying the old ways are failing and offering stronger ones. Some households have started listening.",
      "areas": {
        "The Reed Causeways": {
          "description": "Raised paths running between wet fields and black drainage cuts, the reed beds standing higher than a rider. Marker posts stand at the branchings, and open meres show dark through the reeds beyond.",
          "paths": [
            "Mirgreth Stead",
            "The Tribute Stones"
          ]
        },
        "Mirgreth Stead": {
          "description": "A hamlet of low houses on pilings above the wet, peat smoke over the roofs, marked lintels on every door, nets and eel-traps drying on racks. A plank commons runs between the houses, with a shared well and a bell-post.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reed Causeways"
          ]
        },
        "The Tribute Stones": {
          "description": "Ancient leaning stones out in the fen, moss-grown and older than the duchy, offerings fresh and old at their feet — bread, ribbons, small silver in the mud. Here and there a stone lies overturned, its gifts scattered.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reed Causeways"
          ]
        }
      },
