    "Rovena": {
      "name": "Rovena",
      "x": -27,
      "y": 26,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Duchy of Drusavia",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Rovena is a rich brewing town in the vineyard dales of Drusavia, famous for its dark hill-wine and for brews that are proof against ghosts — for these beautiful dales are haunted, and the dead of a bad old time still rise at night among their tombs. Rovena is prosperous, handsome, and loud despite it: at sunset the hearth-bell rings from the Shrine of Galard, everyone gets behind a lit hearth, and the town goes on with its evening.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The burning of Rovena in the Liberation left the dales full of restless ghosts — the dead of those days, never honored, haunting the tombs, battle-graves, and burned places. The people of Rovena know all this and don't care to dwell on it. The ghosts are dangerous, they are a fact of life, and talk of them is practical or not at all: which one, where, who was taken, what it will take. The history behind them bores and tires the locals in equal measure.\n\nIMPORTANT: The ghosts rise only at night and cannot abide sunlight. They cannot cross a threshold with a living hearth burning behind it — homes and halls with lit hearths are safe. Outside after dark, they attack the living on sight: beautiful, terrible figures in the dress of the old burning, mad with rage and grief, beyond speech or bargain. Their touch is chillfear, the killing cold terror; some victims die where they stand, and others are carried off to hidden tombs in the dales, imprisoned, and slowly drained. The taken can still be rescued while they last. Radiant weapons and balefire destroy the ghosts.\n\nThe town itself is young, loud, and alive: vintner and distiller families rich on the trade, harvest festivals, full halls every night, and an unspoken rule that nobody walks alone after the bell. The distillers sell hearth-cordial, which steadies the heart against chillfear, and balefire brandy, drunk by the shot or thrown by the flask to bloom in white flame. When ghosts take someone or a haunting turns bad, the town calls the Shadow Inquisition by the crow-perch at the shrine — and hunters who happen to be closer find eager customers.",
      "areas": {
        "Demijohn Square": {
          "description": "The market square, crowded with stalls of bright glass demijohns in wicker: hill-wine, cordials, and balefire brandy, with carts rolling in from the estate breweries and vineyards on the slopes. Stone houses with deep cellars ring the square, and the terraced dales rise beyond the rooftops.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dead be Damned Tavern",
            "The Shrine of Galard"
          ]
        },
        "The Dead be Damned Tavern": {
          "description": "Rovena's great wine-hall, built around the biggest hearth in the dales, candles on every table and barrels racked to the ceiling. Long tables, music, and room for every crew, wedding, and harvest gang in the valley.",
          "paths": [
            "Demijohn Square",
            "The Shrine of Galard"
          ]
        },
        "The Shrine of Galard": {
          "description": "A stone shrine with a tall belfry that rings the hearth-bell at sunset, heard across the whole valley. Its priests uphold Galard's old Proving-Year custom independently of either estranged order: they may give an aspirant the plain iron Road-Shield and recognize worthy deeds upon it. The shrine's stone is scarred where defacements have been chiseled and scrubbed away, more than once. In the yard stands an old iron crow-perch.",
          "paths": [
            "Demijohn Square",
            "The Dead be Damned Tavern"
          ]
        }
      },
