      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Wolvesholt Heights are the high country above the Wolvesholt Range, crowned by the ruin of a great hunting estate abandoned long ago. No one lives here now but the Ferals — werewolves who took the curse willingly, and hold the heights as their own.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Heights are the black heart of Carendon's wolf-country: the high reaches above the Wolvesholt Range, where a lordly hunting estate once stood and now lies in ruin, taken over by the Ferals. The Ferals chose to become what they are — they are men and women who went up to the heights to be made wolves, trading their humanity for the strength, the freedom, and the pleasure of the predator. They are cunning, organized, and proud.\n\nThe estate was a great house built for the hunt, a lord's seat for running game across the high country with hound and horn. The Ferals keep it as their den and their mockery: hunters who became the hunt, lairing in the bones of a place built to master the wild. They take the bitten and the willing, the desperate and the cruel, and turn them — so the pack refills no matter how often it is thinned. Their alpha holds the deep lodge and decides who is made and who is meat.\n\nThe Order of the Silver Sword and the crown of Carendon both send hunters up to the Heights to cull the Ferals, by writ and never by garrison. It is the worst work the Order takes in the kingdom and the surest place to die. The Ferals revere the Grey Mother of the deep range and speak her name in their rites, though whether she knows or cares for them is not a thing anyone has lived to learn.\n\nCRITICAL: the Ferals of the Heights default to hostile and do not parley. They are willing predators who hunt and kill by choice, and they treat any intruder as prey or as a recruit to be broken. There is no safe arrival here and no ordinary life.",
      "areas": {
        "The Overgrown Approach": {
