      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Crownhill is the noble quarter of Carendon, the gracious height above the river-town where the king's Palace stands and the great houses of the kingdom keep their seats. It is a quiet, wealthy district of fine townhouses, walled gardens, and liveried servants, set apart from the trade and bustle below.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Crownhill is where the kingdom is governed and where its old families live when they are not on their country estates: the Palace at its crown, the great houses ranged below it, and the Coronet Club where they take their leisure. The watch here is attentive and the servants well-trained, and both can tell at a glance who belongs and who does not. No one is turned away at the gate — Crownhill is not hostile to outsiders — but a stranger receives courtesy and nothing more until they have given the quarter a reason to receive them.\n\nThe houses deal with people who have made themselves worth dealing with. The surest way into their regard is to solve the kind of problem a great house cannot solve for itself and would rather not make public: the beast in the woods of a country manor, the rival to be quietly embarrassed, the heir who rode out and did not come home, the matter that wants a discreet and capable outsider rather than the king's soldiers. A hunter with a name and a kill behind them is exactly such an outsider, and a service well done is worth more on Crownhill than any letter of introduction.\n\nHouse Harstone keeps its townhouse here, the old family that gave Castle Harstone to the Order of the Silver Sword an age ago and has lived on its name and its court connections since. They are proud, reduced, and well-informed, and they hold opinions about the brotherhood squatting in their ancestral seat. The crown's own relationship with the Order runs through this quarter: the king needs the Silverblades and cannot command them, consults their master like a peer, and keeps a court that includes both those who would see the Order brought to heel and those who know better than to try.",
