    "Houndsditch": {
      "name": "Houndsditch",
      "x": 10,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Kingdom of Carendon",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Houndsditch is the old carcass-quarter outside Carendon's eastern wall, where the renderers, tanners, and knackers handle the messy end of the hunting trade that the gracious city won't have within its gates. It is a poor, hard-working district built along the offal-ditch that gives it its name, loud with the half-wild dogs that scavenge it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Houndsditch is the unlovely back-end of the kingdom's monster-economy: the place the carcasses go. Carendon will not have hide-yards and rendering-pots and gut-wagons inside its walls, so by old custom the whole filthy, necessary trade collected outside the east gate, along the drainage-ditch, and the people who work it have lived there for generations. It does work the kingdom needs — and it is exactly why the Syndicate is here.\n\nThe black trade hides in the honest one. In a quarter where monster-carcasses come and go all day and a hundred hands handle hide and horn and bone, no one can tell an honest kill from a poached one — a pelt is a pelt once it is off the beast, and the wood is wide and no one watches who takes what in it. The Syndicate launders its goods through that flow: poached kills, beasts the Order of the Silver Sword forbids the killing of, the parts and powders it keeps out of common hands, all moving out alongside the honest trade to buyers who don't ask. The watch knows the Syndicate works Houndsditch the way it knows crime sits in any poor and crowded place, but the trade is legal, the crime is real but unpinnable to any one carcass, and most in the city would rather take its cut and look away.\n\nThe population is the city's poorer and rougher sort — the renderers and tanners and their families, the desperate who take the work no one else will, and threaded among them the Syndicate's own: fences, fixers, and bravos. There are a fair number of failed and struck-off hunters here too, men and women who know the wood and the work and found the Syndicate pays better than the bounty. Houndsditch is dangerous in the ordinary way of a hard, lawless quarter — thieves, bravos, and old scores, not beasts. A visitor with coin, a name, or honest-enough business is dealt with civilly; a soft mark is robbed.\n\nThe Syndicate's operation in Houndsditch runs out of the Black Boar, and it answers to a single figure — the boss who holds the quarter's trade and takes a cut of every poached carcass that passes through it. A newcomer does not meet him. The fixer at the Black Boar hands out the work, the fence buys the goods, and a hunter who proves useful and discreet rises by degrees — tolerated, then trusted, then brought through to the back where the real business and the real money are.",
