    "Argenval": {
      "name": "Argenval",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -42,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Kingdom of Carendon",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Argenval is Carendon's silver-mining town in a high mountain valley. Miners, traders, hunters, families, dwarf delvers, and young makers fill its streets. The town supplies the silver used by Carendon's monster-hunters and keeps its own watch beneath the troll-haunted Crags.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Argenval is the source of Carendon's radiant silver and a close-knit, mixed working town of miners, smelters, smiths, haulers, shopkeepers, inn families, caravan hands, local hunters, visiting Silverblades, and dwarf delvers who have made the deep trade their own. Children race ore-cart ribbons down the safe slopes, families share courtyard ovens, and the town's favorite drink is blackberry-and-ginger miner's tea cut with strong spirits. Its favorite music is hammer rhythm, fiddle, and whatever a tired crew can sing badly after midnight.\n\nThe Deep Cup hosts the weekly Shift-Off: miners' crews challenge one another at stone-skipping, arm-wrestling, knife-dart, and improvised songs, while matchmakers, recruiters, traders, and information brokers work the room. The Lantern Steps below the miners' guild become a market and dance floor on feast nights, hung with blue-white lamps made from thin silver glass. The biggest celebration is the First Vein festival, when the first rich ore of the season is paraded through town, children receive sugared nuts, makers unveil stylish new tools and jewelry, and every crew claims its foreman is the reason Argenval prospers.\n\nLocal pride is warm but not gentle. Old mining families resent crown officials and outsiders who take the silver's value downhill while calling Argenval backward; younger workers want fame, better pay, romances, and a chance to form crews of their own. Gossip travels through the Cup and the bathhouse: who is courting whom, whose mine claim is disputed, whether a missing lamp or tool was stolen by a trow, and whether the crown will send someone to ban the town's next custom.\n\nIMPORTANT: Argenval proper is safe and ordinary by day and lively by night. The danger is up in the Crags and down in the Mine; the watch, walls, and terrain keep the trows and trolls out of the streets. The town still fears a cave-in, a bad shift, a lost child near the mine entrance, and the day the Crags stop staying above them.\n\nThe old troll tribute is now forbidden by the crown. Some families are furious that the king outlawed the bargain without clearing the Crags; others are ashamed that their grandparents paid monsters at all; a few quietly believe an offering would save lives. Silver kills the unnatural beasts of the wood, while the Crags' trolls and trows are resistant to it, making Argenval's proudest export useless against its oldest enemy.\n\nThe mine remains the town's wealth and wound. The richest seams meet the trow-warren, so hunters can earn recurring cull contracts, miners can become heroes or disappear, smugglers can move high-grade silver through friendly crews, and makers can sell clever lamps, climbing gear, charms, weapons, jewelry, or fashionable protective workwear. Natural scenes include a crew recruitment duel, a festival theft, a missing shift-worker, a disputed mine claim, a crown-versus-town argument, or a rescue in the mine.\n\nMemorable locals include a glamorous young silver-smith who wants to design the next hunter's blade, a beloved Deep Cup singer who hears every secret, a mine-rescue captain building an independent crew, a crown courier trying to look brave, and a sharp-tongued child who knows every safe shortcut.",
      "areas": {
        "The Town": {
          "description": "The mountain road opens into Argenval's high valley, with slate roofs, ore-carts, family gardens, covered stalls, and a central fountain. Miners come off shift, traders call from the market, and children play in the square. The miners' guild and buildings around the mine entrance are visible from town.",
          "paths": [
            "The Deep Cup",
            "The Lantern Steps"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Cup": {
          "description": "A low tavern built around a black iron stove and a small stage. Shift crews eat pepper stew and honey bread here, hunters compare scars, traders bargain over ginger liquor, and the keeper's family serves gossip with every bowl. On Shift-Off nights the room fills with songs, dares, courtship, crew recruiting, and arguments between rival crews.",
