      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Silver Mine supports Argenval, its tunnels cut deep into the mountain at the head of the valley. Miners dig silver from the upper tunnels; the deepest veins reach caves where trows break through, making the richest silver the most dangerous to extract.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Silver Mine is the source of Carendon's silver and the place where trows from the mountain caves threaten Argenval’s miners. The upper and middle tunnels are busy mining passages, full of miners cutting the radiant ore the kingdom's hunters depend on. The deepest tunnels, where the richest veins run, have been dug far enough to meet the natural caves that riddle the mountain — and through the broken seams the trows come: the small, vicious, swarming cave-kin of the trolls, lesser things of the same ugly house, that den and breed in the dark without number. Clearing them is endless work, because the warren up in the crags always fills what is emptied below, and it is the standing hunting-contract of the region — the everyday cull a working hunter takes again and again.\n\nThe trows are dangerous in the way of a pack of malicious, half-clever animals: cowardly alone, deadly in the swarm, fond of ambush in the dark and of dragging a lone miner off into the cracks where no rescue follows. They break into the deep seams through fissures too tight for a person to follow, so the mine cannot be cleared at the source from below; the warren proper is reached only by the crag-trails above the town, not through the mining tunnels.\n\nThe trows here are resistant to silver — the town's own metal does not bite them as it bites the beasts of the wood, whether the warren bred the resistance over the long years or whether something made them so.\n\nIMPORTANT: the trows of the deep seams default to hostile and hunt by ambush in the dark.",
      "areas": {
        "The Mine Mouth": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Silver Mine at the top of the valley. Sheds house the machinery that raises and lowers loads, ore-carts stand on rails, and miners come off shift beside the main shaft descending into the mountain.",
