    "Drowner's Deep": {
      "name": "Drowner's Deep",
      "x": -38,
      "y": -22,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Kingdom of Carendon",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Drowner's Deep is the great marsh in the low west of Carendon, where the river spreads into a maze of black water, reed, and sunken wood. It is named for the hostile aquatic species that lives in it. The fenfolk who cut peat and snare eels at its edges do not go far into the water, and the bad paths through it swallow travelers and livestock alike.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Drowner's Deep is Carendon's drowned country — a wide, slow marsh of black channels, reed-islands, and the rotted stumps of a wood the water took, where the kingdom's western river loses itself. It is a different hunting-ground from the forest and the crags, and a different bestiary. The drowners are the lords of it: pale, clammy, drowned-looking things, man-shaped and worse, that lurk under the water and drag down whatever crosses the bad fords — they hunt in numbers and they are always here, breeding in the deep meres. With them in the water are the things a marsh holds: leeches and serpents grown large, a fen-hag in the deepest reeds for the worst of it, and the will-o'-wisps that lead the unwary off the path and into the meres where the drowners wait.\n\nThe fenfolk live at the dry edges — peat-cutters, eel-snarers, reed-folk — hard and insular and unsurprised by death, and they will guide a hunter who pays and warn one who doesn't. The marsh is worth the danger: it yields what a marsh yields, the reagents and rare growths of the deep fen, the things drowned travelers carried down, and the parts of the drowners themselves that the alchemists of Silver Row will buy. A hunter takes contracts here to clear a ford, recover a body or a cargo the marsh took, or put down whatever has been pulling people under that season.\n\nIMPORTANT: the drowners and the other beasts of the deep marsh default to hostile and hunt by ambush from the water. The dry margins where the fenfolk work hold ordinary life; the danger is in the channels and the meres, and worsens the deeper into the water a hunter goes.",
      "areas": {
        "The Fen-Edge": {
          "description": "The dry margin of the marsh, arrived at where the lowland road runs out into the reeds — peat-stacks and eel-traps, the fenfolk's stilt-huts on the firm ground, flat-bottomed boats drawn up, and the black water and reed-maze running away beyond. The fenfolk work here and watch the water.",
          "paths": [
