    "The Scarps": {
      "name": "The Scarps",
      "x": -13,
      "y": 44,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Westmarch",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Broken crag-country in the northern foothills above Branton, where limestone ridges shatter into ravines, scree slopes, and shallow caves. The Scarps are orc reaver territory — raider bands stage here before striking south into Westmarch. The Westmarch Riders and Branton's militia patrol the approaches but do not hold the ground.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Scarps are hostile territory. Orc reavers use the crags as staging ground before raids on Branton, the hill-pastures and the valley settlements. They are not a permanent garrison — bands move through, camp for days or weeks, and leave. Every visit to the Scarps is different.\n\nOrc reavers operate in small bands. They are experienced ambushers who know the terrain. They post lookouts on the high points, use ravines to channel pursuers into kill-zones, and withdraw deeper into the crags rather than fight losing battles in the open. They are raiders, not berserkers — they want to survive to spend the plunder. A cornered band fights hard; a band that can retreat will.\n\nOgres den in the deeper caves of the high crags, alone or in pairs. The reavers tolerate them as useful muscle and sometimes bribe them with meat and stolen ale to join a raid or guard a camp approach. An ogre den stinks before you see it.\n\nEncounter variety for the narrator to draw from: a raider band returning from a raid, loaded with plunder. A scouting party watching Branton from the ridgeline, counting the militia. A cold camp, abandoned — they have already moved south, and the ashes tell you how recently. A wounded reaver left behind by his band, who will trade what he knows for his life or a clean death. An ogre asleep in its den with a reaver camp just below, using the ogre as an unwitting sentry. Two rival reaver bands in the same stretch of crags, avoiding each other badly — the player walks into the middle of it.\n\nLoot: orc-forged weapons — heavy cleavers, brutal hand-axes, iron-shod spears, all rough-made but hard-hitting. War-gear stripped from the reavers' kills — Westmarch-made swords, shields with house-marks, a Rider's helmet. Coin and valuables from caravans. The crags themselves turn up old war-king era equipment — centuries-old steel and bronze weathering out of the scree where frontier battles were fought in the old wars. Occasionally something that does not belong in a reaver camp: a restored war-king era blade with blood-channels scored into the steel, or a ritual cutting-knife with fresh blood on the edge. Do not explain the connection — present the evidence and let the player draw the conclusion or ignore it.\n\nWolfgar Keep is visible from the highest points of the high crags on clear days, a dark ruin on a crag to the east. Reavers sometimes mention it as a place they avoid — someone is there sometimes, and those ones are not orcs, and the reavers were told to stay clear.",
      "areas": {
        "The Lower Scarps": {
          "description": "Broken foothills where the pastureland gives way to limestone ridges and scree. The trails narrow between ravines and the ground is loose underfoot. Reaver tracks, cold fire-pits, and stripped bones mark campsites used and abandoned. The approaches to Branton and the hill-pastures are visible below.",
          "paths": [
            "The High Crags"
          ]
        },
        "The High Crags": {
          "description": "Deep crag-country: shattered ridgelines, overhangs, and cave mouths in the limestone. Orc reaver bands camp in the sheltered ravines and use the caves for stores and shelter. Ogres den in the deeper caves, their leavings piled at the mouths. The ground is bad for horses and good for ambush. On clear days Wolfgar Keep is visible to the east, dark against the skyline.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Scarps"
          ]
        }
      },
