    "Morcroft Hall": {
      "name": "Morcroft Hall",
      "x": 24,
      "y": -11,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Westmarch",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A stone manor-castle and small village in the southeastern farmlands of Westmarch, seat of House Morcroft. The Morcrofts are an old military family with the finest warhorses and heaviest cavalry in the region. Their household warriors drill in the yard daily and ride the southern roads in good order. House Morcroft has served the Corwins loyally for generations, though the two families agree on very little.",
      "hiddenInfo": "House Morcroft holds that the Corwins are sentimental stewards spending the region's wealth on an unfinished wall and festival grounds while the border bleeds. Their own estate is ready for a war nobody has declared.\n\nLord and Lady Morcroft receive visitors with formal hospitality and offer work — patrol escort, hunting, horse-breaking, and courier rides. Pay is good and often comes in gear rather than coin: a Morcroft-forged blade, a trained warhorse, riding armor from the estate's own smiths. The household warriors are a belonging ladder — ride with them, prove yourself, and earn a place at the lord's table and arms from the family's stocks.\n\nThe Morcrofts believe the peace with the orcs and elves is a temporary reprieve the Corwins are wasting. They advocate for finishing the Greatwall, garrisoning Wolfgar Keep, and pushing the border north to the old war-king lines. This position is popular among the southeastern gentry.\n\nThe Morcrofts deny the family funds or shelters Bloodsworn crusaders and point to their unblemished military record.",
      "areas": {
        "The Training Yard": {
          "description": "Warhorses in the paddocks, men-at-arms drilling with real steel, and a master smith forging cavalry arms and heavy armor. The stables breed the finest horses in Westmarch — big, sure-footed, trained for battle. The smith sells quality weapons, riding armor, lances, and warbows. A horse-master teaches horsemanship and mounted combat. The household warriors accept volunteers for patrol-rides.",
          "paths": [
