    "Ancestral Hall": {
      "name": "Ancestral Hall",
      "x": 2,
      "y": 11,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Westmarch",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A long, low stone hall on the far shore of the Marrenmere, visible from Marrenford across the water. Ancestral Hall holds the arms and armor of Westmarch's war-dead, tended by a keeper of noble family. Warriors come to swear oaths before the dead, ask to carry a hero's weapon into battle, or have a blade blessed.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The hall is older than Castle Westvigil — some of the stonework is war-king era. The door faces the lake; you arrive by boat or by the shore-path and enter facing the northern hills where the war-kings fought. A stone hearth at the center is kept burning as a duty. The fire represents the living memory of the dead.\n\nThe walls carry real weapons of the honored dead — each set with a name-plate and a carved stone marker. The tradition is that a warrior may ask to hold a dead hero's weapon before battle. The keeper grants or refuses based on whether the asking is worthy. The weapons are old, named, and good — some are war-king era steel, Superior or Masterwork grade, with history attached.\n\nWeapons held in the hearthfire and spoken over by the keeper carry a blessing against the undead for a time.\n\nA stone dais at the far end of the hall is where vows are sworn to the fallen. A warrior who swears before the dead and keeps the oath earns standing in Westmarch.\n\nThe keeper is traditionally of a noble family, often a Morcroft. The role draws those with genuine devotion to the war-dead — and opinions about whether the peace honors their sacrifice.",
      "areas": {
        "Ancestral Hall": {
          "description": "A long stone chamber lit by the hearthfire at its center. The walls carry the arms and armor of Westmarch's honored dead — swords, shields, spears, helmets, each hung with a name-plate and a stone marker beneath. Some of the weapons are centuries old. The keeper tends the fire and knows every name on the wall. He blesses weapons in the hearthfire for those riding against the dead, lends the heroes' arms to those he judges worthy, and swears oaths at the stone dais at the far end of the hall. He posts work on a board by the door: arms-recovery, body-escort, and grave-tending in the border country.",
          "paths": []
