    "The Eastwood": {
      "name": "The Eastwood",
      "basicInfo": "A broad eastern stretch of the Everwood, where the deep forest thins toward the open country of Westmarch - a wide frontier of old timber broken by clearings, brush-choked ridges, and stream-cut hollows, going on for miles.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Eastwood is the front of a fight the high elves will not dirty their hands with. Human Bloodsworn crusaders cross from their bases in Westmarch to hunt the elf renegades who hide in this wood, and they do not much care what they burn to flush them out - groves, holdings, whatever stands between them and their quarry. The high elf Wardens avoid open war with the crusaders and leave these outsiders to Sanctuary and the Wildkeepers, who hold the Eastwood as best they can.\n\nThe crusaders are an active, moving presence, not a fixed force. They range the wood in war-parties, throw up camps and abandon them, and burn as they go, so the Eastwood is never the same wood twice: a fresh burn-scar, a war-band on the move, a camp pitched in a clearing for a few days and gone. They are not found in one fixed place - dealing with them means hunting for them, patrolling the wood, cutting their trail, scouting a camp, lying in wait.\n\nThe elf renegades the crusaders hunt are real, and shelter deeper in the Everwood; they raid Westmarch to provoke war, and every grove the Bloodsworn burn is proof to them that the outsiders must be driven out.\n\nIMPORTANT: Human Bloodsworn crusaders default to hostile and attack others they find in the wood. They are blood-cult zealots who hold their crusade righteous, and they take any armed stranger for an enemy or an obstacle. The wood holds ordinary dangers besides - it is deep, broken country, and dire wolves run the hollows in packs while giant spiders web the old timber where the canopy grows thick. A careless traveler can come to grief here without any help from the crusaders.",
