    "The Sundwen Mire": {
      "name": "The Sundwen Mire",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -29,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Sanctuary Vale",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A drowned stretch of the Everwood in the region's low country - black water, reed-islands, and trees standing to their knees in the murk. The Sisterhood of the Hunt hold it: a band of women warriors most reckon plain bandits, who rob, take, and kill travelers in the mire - men worst of all - and are known to recruit women into their number.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Sisterhood of the Hunt believe they serve the cause of the dryad-queen Yslaine. Yet her dryads have never accepted that service - they refuse to speak with the Sisters and attack them when approached, the same as any other outsider. So the Sisters further the queen's cause as they can from the mire, hunting those who despoil the wood.\n\nOne offering has proved itself. Young girls left at the edge of Yslaine's demesne are taken in and made into dryads; the Sisters have seen it. Grown women are not taken - they are killed at the threshold like any other intruder, which is why the Sisters cannot offer themselves and must take girls instead. That the girls, once turned, attack them on sight like all the rest has not stopped them; they hold it an honor done the girls, and the surest service they can render the queen. So they take girls, both those who come willingly and those seized from the wood's edge and the roads, and carry them to the threshold.\n\nWomen go missing into the Sundwen two ways: some girls are seized for the offering, and some women run to the Sisters and are not given back.\n\nIMPORTANT: The Sisterhood default to hostile toward men and toward any outsider they judge a despoiler of the wood, and toward most travelers as marks to rob or kill. They strike from the water and reeds with bow and spear and mean to kill or drive off. The mire itself is bad ground: deep water, sucking mud, and the things that live in a drowned wood.",
      "areas": {
        "The Black Water": {
          "description": "The open mire, where the Everwood stands drowned - trees rooted in slow black water, reed-banks and islands of higher ground between them, and channels winding off in every direction under a low canopy. The footing is water, mud, and the odd causeway of fallen timber and trodden reed. Old sign marks the through-ways: a swamped cart sunk to its bed, a campsite picked clean.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sisters' Hold"
          ]
        },
        "The Sisters' Hold": {
          "description": "The Sisterhood's seat, raised out of the water on stilts and on the larger islands - longhouses of reed-thatch and bound timber, drying-racks and drawn-up boats, walkways of planking strung between the trees above the murk. Smoke stands over it, and the spoils of the mire are kept here: penned and stored goods, and what the Sisters take off the people they catch in the Sundwen.",
          "paths": [
            "The Black Water"
          ]
        }
      },
