      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "basicInfo": "An old badger-warren and tumble of rocks at the wood's edge, dotted with statues - birds, foxes, a goat, and here and there a person, all turned to stone mid-step. Cockatrices nest in the burrows. The petrified still hold what they carried when they froze, good steel and gear under the grey, and the cockatrices themselves are worth taking: the gland behind the beak, ground right, makes the salve that turns stone back to flesh, and alchemists pay well for it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The cockatrices of the warren are hostile and rush anything that comes near the burrows.\n\nThey are not clever and not large, but they come in ones and twos from the holes and go straight for exposed skin - their bite turns flesh slowly to stone, and one solid bite can cripple a fight. They scatter when struck and dart back in, more nuisance than terror singly, dangerous in numbers and in a cramped space where they can swarm. Kill a few and the rest grow wary but do not flee their nests.\n\nThe loot is on the petrified dead - a stone hand closed on a stone sword that is real steel under the grey, a frozen pack, a ring - prised loose from victims nobody came back for; the older statues deeper in the burrows hold the better leavings. Cockatrice glands and feathers are worth taking off the kills.",
