    "The Churn": {
      "name": "The Churn",
      "x": -34,
      "y": -15,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A stretch of the iron hills where the ground itself looks wrong — turned over, heaved into ridges and sinkholes, the grass growing back in patches over raw earth. Land-sharks den here in numbers, and the broken ground is their wake. They take cattle off the hill pastures and horses off the picket-line, and a careless rider on open turf with them. Both clans pay well to thin them, and the hides are worth the ride: a land-shark's back-plates turn a blade like good steel.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The Churn is hostile. The land-sharks here are wild predators that hunt anything that moves across their ground, and they are always hungry. They do not pause, do not threaten, and cannot be reasoned with. A hunter comes here to kill them or to be killed.\n\nThe broken country is the work of generations of land-sharks denning and breeding in the soft iron-veined earth. There are always several, and there are always more coming — the clans cull them and the ground fills again.\n\nA land-shark hunts by what it feels through the ground. It lies buried with only the ridge of its back breaking the surface, senses the footfalls of anything heavy enough to be food, and bursts up beneath it — through turf, through a picket-line, through a standing rider. On open ground a man on foot cannot outrun the strike. The orcs who hunt them do it from horseback, in pairs, keeping moving and keeping to the bare stone shelves where the beast cannot dig, baiting it up and taking it as it surfaces. A land-shark that misses its lunge is slow to turn and slower to dig back down; that is the window.\n\nThe back-plates and ridge-hide are the prize, sold to the armorers of both clans and worth a season's coin for a clean kill.\n\nCombat profile: ambush predator, armored, fast off the line. Opens from below with no warning — a single explosive lunge that can swallow a man or break a horse. Bites and thrashes, throwing riders and scattering a hunting party. Heavily plated along the back and flanks; the soft places are the underside and the joints of the jaw, reachable only when it rears up out of the ground. Wounded, it does not flee — it digs, vanishing into the earth to surface again somewhere else moments later. Hunts alone but several may rise if a party blunders across more than one den.",
      "areas": {
        "The Broken Ground": {
          "description": "The edge of the Churn, where the hill pasture gives way to turned earth. Ridges of raw soil run in long lines where something huge has moved beneath the surface; the grass grows in islands between them. Old bones surface here — cattle, horses, the occasional picked-clean rider — and the bare stone shelves stand up out of the dirt where the digging cannot reach. Hunters stage on the stone and watch the ground for the tell-tale ridge of a back breaking the surface.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dens"
          ]
        },
        "The Dens": {
          "description": "Deeper in, the ground is all sinkhole and tunnel-mouth, collapsed in places where the warrens beneath have caved. This is where the land-sharks lie up between hunts, buried in the soft iron-veined earth with only their backs breaking the surface. The biggest of them den here, and the ground is thickest with bone. Bare stone is scarce and the footing is treacherous; a hunter who comes this far has nowhere safe to stand.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broken Ground"
          ]
        }
      },
