    "Roc Bluffs": {
      "name": "Roc Bluffs",
      "x": 17,
      "y": 36,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Wind-scoured tors rising sheer from the open grass, white with bird-lime and crowned with the great stick-nests of rocs. The birds are enormous — wide enough across the wing to throw a shadow like a passing cloud — and they hunt the steppe for anything they can carry: cattle, horses off the picket-line, a rider and his mount together, now and then a whole wagon. What they carry up, they keep. Generations of nests sit on those crags stuffed with the leavings of everything a roc ever took, and a climber bold enough to reach one can come down rich. Most who try come down in pieces, or not at all. Both clans prize the flight-feathers, and the maddest riders dream of carrying down an egg.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Roc Bluffs is hostile. The rocs are animals and defend their nests without hesitation — a roc that sees a climber on its crag will stoop on him, and there is no warning and no parley. They are most dangerous near the nests and most absent at midday, when they range far out over the grass to hunt.\n\nRocs have always nested on these tors and always will; the clans cannot drive them off and have stopped trying. There are several nests and the birds come and go with the seasons.\n\nA roc carries its kill back to the crag to feed, and over generations the eyries have filled with everything its prey was carrying when it died: coin and jewelry, arms and armor stripped from the bones of taken riders, saddle-tack and harness, a snatched merchant's strongbox, trade-goods, and now and then the enchanted blade of a knight who went up screaming in a roc's grip.\n\nThe birds themselves are worth nearly as much. The great flight-feathers are sought by the fletchers of both clans, who fletch their finest war-arrows with them — arrows that fly farther and truer than any other in the region, and command a high price. The talons and the hooked beak are worked into spearheads, knives, and tools; the down lines winter gear and padded armor.\n\nAnd in the nests, sometimes, an egg — pale and vast and worth a fortune whole, the one prize that draws the truly reckless. A roc egg sells for a king's ransom, and a roc raised from the shell can, the boldest claim, be broken to the saddle and flown. Most who reach for an egg find the parents come back early.\n\nCombat profile: aerial apex hunter. Opens with a diving stoop out of the sun, talons first, fast enough to pluck a rider from the saddle or knock a climber off the rock. Climbs, wheels, and stoops again rather than landing to fight; on the ground it is less sure but its beak and talons still kill in a stroke, and a buffet of its wings scatters a party and fouls bowshots. Defends the nest to the death and will not be driven from it. Two may stoop together if a nest holds a mated pair.",
      "areas": {
        "The Windward Tors": {
          "description": "The foot of the crags, where the wind comes off the grass hard enough to lean on and the white-limed rock rises sheer overhead. Looking up, the great nests are dark masses against the sky on the highest pinnacles; looking down, the ground is littered with what the rocs have dropped on the way up — cracked bones, a buckled shield, the splintered boards of a wagon, the occasional coin trodden into the dirt.",
          "paths": [
            "The Eyries"
          ]
        },
        "The Eyries": {
          "description": "The nest-shelves near the summits, reached by a brutal climb up wind-blasted rock. The nests are huge tangled platforms of branch and bone and carried-off wreckage, fouled with lime and thick with the leavings of generations of kills. This high there is nothing to hide behind and nowhere to run.",
          "paths": [
            "The Windward Tors"
          ]
        }
      },
