    "Saber-Grass": {
      "name": "Saber-Grass",
      "x": -5,
      "y": -44,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A swathe of the deep steppe where the grass grows higher than a mounted rider, a green sea that swallows sound and sight together. Saber-tooths hunt here, and the tall grass is theirs: a rider sees nothing until the grass moves, and by then the cat is already in the air. They are huge, patient, and unafraid of horses. The clans hunt them for the great fangs and the striped pelts, and a saber-tooth taken clean is a deed worth telling.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Saber-Grass is hostile. The saber-tooths are animals and hunt anything that enters their range — they stalk unseen through the high grass and strike from ambush, and they do not threaten or parley before they do. They are boldest where the grass is tallest and thickest.\n\nSaber-tooths have always ranged this stretch of steppe, drawn by the herds that graze the deep grass, and the tall cover is what makes them so deadly: it hides a beast the size of a horse until the moment it leaps. There are always several working the grass, and the clans cull them without ever clearing them.\n\nThe great curved fangs are the prize, carved into knife-hilts and spear-points and the honor-trophies a hunter wears to show what he has taken, and bought by both clans for good coin and better standing. The striped pelts are worked into cloaks, bedrolls, and the war-harness of chiefs, who prize a saber-tooth hide above any other; a whole unmarked pelt fetches a small fortune. The claws are set into gauntlets and charms, the heavy bones worked into tools.\n\nCombat profile: ambush predator, huge and silent. Stalks unseen through grass that hides it completely, then breaks cover in a single long leap onto the back of horse or rider. Bears its kill to the ground and works the throat; if the first leap misses, it circles back into the grass and stalks again rather than fighting in the open. Fast over short distances, tireless at the stalk, and unafraid of numbers — several may be working the same stretch of grass, and a party that kills one may find it was never the only one watching.",
      "areas": {
        "The Grass Edge": {
          "description": "The edge of the deep grass, where the steppe rises from horse-high to higher than a rider's head and the wind moves it in long slow waves. Trodden runs wind away into the green where heavy bodies have pushed through — herd-paths and hunting-paths both, impossible to tell apart. Bones lie in the flattened hollows where kills were made and eaten, half-grown over. Sightlines end at arm's length.",
          "paths": [
            "The Killing Grounds"
          ]
        },
        "The Killing Grounds": {
          "description": "Deeper in, where the grass is tallest and the runs converge on the trampled hollows the saber-tooths use to lie up and feed. The ground here is littered with the bones of horses and oxen and the occasional rider, and the grass stands like walls on every side. There is no high ground, no clear line, and no way to see what is moving until it is close.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grass Edge"
          ]
        }
