    "The Western Border Woods": {
      "name": "The Western Border Woods",
      "x": -44,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Where the open Wold runs up against the eastern edge of the great Everwood, the grass gives way to old trees and tangled understory. Elf raiders out of the deep wood have long camped in this vast region to strike at the steppe — burning out herders, cutting down travelers, vanishing back under the canopy before riders can answer.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The elf renegades who are often found in this location attack anyone they find — orc, outsider, traveler, it makes no difference. They will not parley, because they are here to kill and to make trouble, and anyone who sees them is a witness they cannot allow. They strike first, from cover, and ask nothing.\n\nThese renegades are the radical edge of the high elves of the forest: the faction that holds the wild peoples have no place at the forest's borders and that peace between elf and orc is a betrayal. The renegades raid the Wold not for plunder but to provoke: every burned steading and murdered traveler is meant to push the orcs and the elves toward the war the renegades want. They take orc goods and travelers' coin when it is to hand, but ruin is the purpose, not profit.\n\nThey fight as raiders, not soldiers. Small bands move through the woods and cannot be pinned to one place — they range the whole location, striking and slipping away. They favor the bow and the ambush above all: arrows from cover, then a rush with curved blades. They know the ground and use it, breaking line of sight and circling to strike from behind. Cornered, they fight to the death rather than be taken, because a captured renegade compromises the rest.\n\nThe woods carry their own dangers besides. Dire wolves run the deep timber in packs, and they do not distinguish raider from hunter. An owlbear dens somewhere in the heavy growth and kills anything that crosses its range.\n\nWhat the renegades carry is worth the taking: elven war-bows and the long curved blades of the forest, and elven mail, light and strong. Their arrows are elf-fletched and fly true. Among a band's leaders there is sometimes elven work with old enchantments on it — a blade that bites cold, a bow that never misfires, a cloak that turns the eye.\n\nCombat profile, elf renegade: light, fast, deadly at range. Opens with arrows from cover before the target knows they are there. Closes only with advantage — a rush of curved blades, several at once on a single foe — and breaks off the moment the advantage is lost, fading into the trees to loop around and strike again. Never holds ground it does not have to. Fights as a band, never alone if it can help it, and covers a withdrawal with massed bowfire. Will die before surrendering.",
      "areas": {
        "The Wood's Edge": {
          "description": "The march between grass and forest, where scattered old trees stand in the long grass and thicken as the land rises westward. Sightlines are long here at the steppe's edge and shorten with every pace into the trees. The ground is broken by deadfall and bramble, with open lanes of grass running between stands of timber — fast going in the open, slow and blind in the thickets.",
          "paths": [
            "The Deep Timber",
            "The Hollow Ground"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Timber": {
          "description": "Heavy old forest where the canopy closes overhead and the light goes green and dim. The trunks stand close, cover is everywhere. The understory is thick enough to hide a crouching figure within arm's reach. Game-trails and older paths wind through it, the only fast ways through a wood that otherwise swallows movement.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wood's Edge",
            "The Hollow Ground"
          ]
        },
        "The Hollow Ground": {
          "description": "A stretch of broken forest floor where the land folds into gullies and dry stream-cuts under the trees, choked with deadfall and root-tangle. The cuts run deep enough to hide a moving party below the lip of the ground, and the high banks command everything below them. Footing is bad and loud — dry sticks and loose stone — and the gullies branch and rejoin so that a straight line through is impossible.",
          "paths": [
