    "The Southern Badlands": {
      "name": "The Southern Badlands",
      "x": -27,
      "y": -29,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Orc Wolds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Broken, dry country at the Wold's southern edge, where the grass fails into cracked earth, dust-gullies, and bare stone ridges. The Oathbreakers hold this waste — orc raiders cast out of the clans, who ride from here to burn and plunder south across the border and prey on anything that crosses the badlands. The clans name them kin no longer.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Oathbreakers are hostile and will not parley. They are orcs who broke the clan-oath — the loyalty sworn at the Gods-Anvil that binds rider to clan and clan to the Wold. They hold that the clans have gone soft: that tolerating Gorren Crossing, taking in outsiders, and keeping the truce between Vorren and Drokash is weakness, and that the Wold's strength should be spent burning Westmarch and the Everwood to the ground. The clans disown them utterly, for to an orc there is no fouler thing than an oathbreaker.\n\nThey ride to Blood-Altar Mountain to take the Wrath-Speakers' rites, and come back worse.\n\nThey are raiders, not soldiers, and have no single chief — bands gather under whoever is strongest that season and scatter when beaten. They cannot be pinned to one camp; they range the whole waste, striking from the gullies and the broken ground and riding down what flees. Worgs run with them — wolves the size of horses, cunning and cruel and able to speak, that scout, flank, and hunt alongside the riders and are no dumb beasts to be herded but allies in the killing. Carrion-birds trail the bands across the badlands, and where they circle, the Oathbreakers have lately been.\n\nWhat the Oathbreakers carry is reaved plunder — coin, arms and armor, and the goods stripped from burned steadings and broken caravans on both sides of the border — along with their own war-gear, hard-used and well-kept. The worgs wear collars and harness taken from their kills, sometimes with worked metal and coin braided into them as trophies.\n\nCombat profile, Oathbreaker raider: mounted, fast, and vicious. Strikes from ambush out of the broken ground, looses arrows from horseback, then closes with axe and blade in a screaming rush meant to break nerve as much as line. Fights alongside worgs that flank and drag down the isolated. Gives no quarter and expects none; a band that loses badly scatters into the gullies to regroup rather than die in place.\n\nCombat profile, worg: cunning pack-hunter. Runs with the raiders or hunts in its own pack, flanking to drive prey onto the riders' blades. Fast, tireless, and clever enough to lay an ambush; speaks to coordinate the hunt. Bold in numbers and cowardly alone, breaking off when the odds turn.",
      "areas": {
        "The Dust Gullies": {
          "description": "A maze of dry, steep-sided ravines cut into the badland floor, deep enough to hide riders below the level of the plain. The gullies branch and rejoin without pattern, the walls are crumbling earth and loose stone, and the floors are soft sand that muffles hooves. A party in the gullies cannot see far and cannot be seen, and command belongs to whoever holds the high lip above.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cracked Flats",
            "The Ridges"
          ]
        },
        "The Cracked Flats": {
          "description": "Open hardpan between the gullies, the earth split into a dry crazed pattern and scattered with bleached bone and wind-worn stone. The flats run wide and level, with nothing to break a sightline and nothing to hide behind — fast riding for anyone crossing, and no cover at all when something comes out of the gullies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dust Gullies",
            "The Ridges"
          ]
        },
        "The Ridges": {
          "description": "Bare stone spines rising above the waste, their flanks broken into ledges and notches and their tops commanding the flats and gullies below. The rock is split and weathered, with caves and overhangs worn into the higher faces. From the ridgelines the whole badland lies open to view; the climbs to reach them are loose and exposed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dust Gullies",
            "The Cracked Flats"
          ]
        }
      },
