      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A dead snowlander town on the ice, a day from Warmwater, that the cold took. Its hot spring kept it open and alive the way Warmwater's still does — until the spring went dead one winter and the town froze solid, people and all. They named it Sweetwater when the water was warm. They call it Stillwater now.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Stillwater is bitterly, unnaturally cold — colder than the open ice around it, the way a place is cold where the Hearth has died and the Widow has come all the way in. The cold itself is the chief danger to the unprepared. Ice mephits gather thick in the dead streets, drawn to where the cold runs deepest, and rime-wights drift among the buildings — the town's own people, some of them, walking the streets they died in. The wights do not hunt; they wander, and turn on what disturbs them.\n\nThere are no bodies in the houses. The lamps were lit and the tables were laid and the people are simply gone.",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Streets": {
          "description": "The lanes of a snowlander town, carved-timber houses rimed white and roofed in ice, lamps frozen in their brackets at the doors and windows. Snow has drifted against the walls and filled the open spaces. Doors stand shut, frozen in their frames; some windows are clear enough to see the laid tables and cold hearths within. The streets run inward toward the town's heart.",
          "paths": [
