      "region": "Aurora",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The chief town of the snowlanders. It sits on the shore of Warmwater Lake, where hot springs rise from the earth and keep the near water open and steaming all year, in a country where everything else is locked in ice. The snowlanders live by the lake — fishing, trading, and holding to their hearths against the cold — and an old stone castle of the vanished Northrealm stands on the height above the town, reoccupied now as the watch that guards the whole region against the white.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The snowlanders are a hardy, plainspoken, stubborn folk, generous to anyone who comes in off the ice and quick to take in a useful stranger. They are the last of the people of the old Northrealm — the kingdom the cold killed — and they know it; they live where they live because the springs let them, having given up the cities and the sea their forebears held for the one warm shore the Widow cannot reach. \"Come Hrimnara or hot water,\" they say of anything they have set their minds to.\n\nThe town has two layers, and they sit one above the other. Below is the warm life of the lakeshore — the springs, the market, the fishing, the bathhouse, the kennels, the hearths — where most folk live. Above, up the climb, is the old Northrealm castle, Whitewatch, and the Long Watch it houses: the snowlanders' standing vigil against the cold and the things that come with it, and the League of Adventurers' foothold in the north.\n\nThe springs are why the town lives. They are volcanic — heat from deep in the earth — but the snowlanders hold them the gift of the Lady of the Hearth, the warmth she sends up through the ground to keep her people alive. The Hearth faith is kept here: a fire kept always burning, a lamp set in every window against the dark, the words \"your hearth, my life\" said before food or drink passes the lips indoors, and carved housewardens — wolves, serpents, guardian-beasts — watching outward from every gable.",
      "areas": {
        "The Market": {
          "description": "The town's trading-square and the heart of its daily life. It sits along the warm shore, the near water of the lake steaming and open at its edge while the far reaches stand frozen white, carved-timber houses with steep snow-shedding roofs crowding behind. Stalls and trade-halls roofed against the snow deal in fish and lamp-oil, furs and pelts, frost-gear and sled-tack, and the cold-country goods that come down off the ice — drake-bone, serpent-hide, and rarer things. Snowlander traders work alongside dwarves up from the northern holds and the odd hard-bitten prospector in from the white. Boats sit at the jetties, and a walled road climbs from the square to the old castle on the height above.",
