    "Loot Landing": {
      "name": "Loot Landing",
      "known": true,
      "x": 37,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Black Fells",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Goblinate's war-harbor on the eastern shore, where raider fleets put in heavy with plunder and the docks turn into a marketplace the moment a prize-ship ties up. Cranes haul spoils onto the wharves, auctioneers bawl over the crowds, and crews flush with shares spend them on the worst the harbor offers. Cargo, captives, and raw materials all come ashore here.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Loot Landing is Goblinate military ground, run by the harbor-master's goblins and the marines who crew the fleet. It is not openly hostile to outsiders the way a monster lair is—a useful stranger with cargo to sell or coin to spend is tolerated and watched, the same as Smoketown. But it is a war-harbor under guard: spies are taken, and anyone caught freeing captives or moving against the fleet is killed or thrown in the pens.\n\nThe harbor runs on plunder. Prize-ships arrive and their hauls are auctioned dockside—luxuries, arms, ore, captured steel, anything the fleet has taken. Coin and treasure flow up to the Goblinate; raw materials, sulfur, timber, and ore flow on to the works. The marines are dangerous, drunk as often as not, and quick to violence, but they answer to the harbor-master and the harbor-master answers to the Governor.\n\nThe prisoner pens hold what the raiders take alive: pressed rowers for the galleys, captives held for ransom, and the unlucky bound for sale or the pyre.\n\nThe Syndicate works the harbor's underside, fencing goods the marines skim from their own hauls and selling passage and forged papers to those who can pay.",
      "areas": {
        "The Wharves": {
          "description": "A long stone waterfront of berths and jetties, crane-gantries leaning over the water to haul cargo from the holds. Ships of the raider fleet ride at anchor and tie up at the quays, low and lean and built for speed. Ramps and gangplanks run down to the dock, and stacked crates and barrels crowd the open ground between cranes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Auction Yard",
            "The Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The Auction Yard": {
          "description": "A broad cobbled square set back from the water, ringed with auction-blocks and trestle-stalls. Raised platforms stand where goods are shown and cried. Strongrooms and counting-houses line the landward edge, their doors iron-bound and guarded. The square is built to hold crowds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wharves",
            "The Pens",
            "The Cranehouse Rows"
          ]
        },
        "The Cranehouse Rows": {
          "description": "The harbor's working district behind the auction square: warehouses, ship-chandlers, rope-walks, and the drinking-dens and gambling-halls where crews spend their shares. Narrow lanes run between the storehouses, easy to lose a tail in. The buildings are timber and salvaged ship-timber, packed close.",
          "paths": [
            "The Auction Yard"
          ]
        },
        "The Pens": {
          "description": "A walled compound of holding-cages and barred sheds at the harbor's edge, set apart from the trade. Heavy gates and a guarded yard control the way in. Stocks and chaining-posts stand in the open ground. The compound backs onto the water, where galleys take on rowers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wharves",
            "The Auction Yard"
          ]
        }
      },
