    "The Rumblehorn Run": {
      "name": "The Rumblehorn Run",
      "x": 13,
      "y": 31,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A long upland glen contested by Stonewall and Stormhammer, threaded by old droveways and dotted with cairns. A half-wild herd of rumblehorns runs here — huge, shaggy, foul-tempered highland cattle that both clans claim by founding-right and neither will cede. No one holds the glen. Both clans send crews in to cut beasts from the herd, brand them, and drive them home, and the crews cross each other doing it. Wargs den in the high crags and hunt the herd's stragglers.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The glen is contested ground. Stonewall says their forebears first penned the rumblehorn line; Stormhammer says possession settles it and rides in to take beasts whenever they please. Both send rustling crews each season to brand prize beasts with their clan's mark and drive them home, and to cut loose any beast the rival branded first. The rules are unwritten and both sides pretend not to know them: no killing over a beast, no burning a rival's cairn, everything else fair. An outsider crew can hire on to either clan, run both, or cut beasts for themselves and sell to whoever pays.\n\nThe rumblehorns are the first danger. They are not malicious, but a bull weighs as much as a loaded cart and a spooked one will flatten a crew without noticing. They move where they like and gore anything that crowds them wrong. Cutting one prize beast clear of the herd and getting it home is the whole game and most of the risk.\n\nIMPORTANT: the wargs of the high crags are hostile and hunt the glen by their own appetite. They den in the broken rock, run in packs, and work the herd's edges — cutting off the straggler, the wounded, the lone. They open by circling and harrying from cover, drive prey toward the rocks where the rest of the pack waits, and break off only when badly outmatched, returning after dark. A crew busy wrestling a bull is exactly the straggler they want.\n\nThe old cairns are droving-burials, generations of herders and clan-dead laid under stone along the paths. Most are quiet. A few of the larger barrows hold more than bones, and what was set there to guard the dead does not always stay still.\n\nA branded rumblehorn is a prize beast worth a season's coin, and a champion bull is clan-wealth on the hoof. Both clans pay bounties for beasts brought to their own mark, and pay better for beasts cut loose from the rival's. Warg pelts and fangs are hunt-trophies and crafting stock. The barrows hold old arms, bronze ornaments, and grave-silver, and the deepest hold grander grave-goods under a guard.",
      "areas": {
        "The Droveway": {
          "description": "A worn cattle-track climbs between low shoulders of heather and grey stone, then crests and opens onto the glen below. The ground falls away into a long green hollow ringed by crags, droveways crossing it like old scars, cairns standing here and there along the paths. Far across, the rock breaks into a tumble of scree and shadowed clefts under the high ridge. A standing waystone at the crest is carved on two faces — a Stonewall mark cut deep on one, a Stormhammer mark cut deeper over it, and both re-cut again above.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glen Floor"
          ]
        },
        "The Glen Floor": {
          "description": "The hollow's grassy bottom, wide and open, cropped short by grazing and churned to mud along the droveways. Small cairns of piled stone stand at the path-forks, some no higher than a knee, weathered smooth. The grass hides dips and runnels deep enough to turn an ankle or screen a crouching shape. Broad trampled lanes mark where the big beasts move. Toward the ridge the ground rises and the stones grow thicker; toward the lower end a stream cuts a muddy ford.",
          "paths": [
