    "Tuskhollow": {
      "name": "Tuskhollow",
      "x": -32,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A giantborn stead in a high cattle-vale above the dwarf country, where the Tuskhollow folk raise enormous shaggy bulls and settle every dispute by feats of strength. They claim descent from the ur-giants who shaped the mountains, and they hold a stretch of Stonewall grazing-ground and a Stormhammer drove-road as theirs by that birthright. The dwarves say otherwise. The boundary gets argued every season, sometimes with fists. The vale's high crags hold predators that come down for the calves.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Tuskhollow giantborn are impossible neighbors. In peace they are open-handed, loud, and generous; they feast hugely and laugh louder. But they take honor with absolute seriousness, settle everything — debts, disputes, insults, who was right about a bull's bloodline — by wrestling and feats of strength, and they remember a slight for generations. Insult one and you face a challenge that ends in broken bones; back down from a fair challenge and you are nothing to them ever after. They are half again a human's height and strong past any dwarf, and they know it.\n\nThe trouble with the dwarves is a boundary. The Tuskhollow claim a high meadow Stonewall has grazed for ages and a drove-road Stormhammer uses to move beasts, both theirs, they say, since the ur-giants walked. They drive off dwarf herds, block the road, and answer complaints with a grin and an offer to settle it in the ring. Neither clan can shift them by force without a war nobody wants, and neither will concede the ground.\n\nThe Tuskhollow hire out the work their own hands are too big or too few for: a prize bull gone down a crevice too narrow for them, calves taken by crag predators, a beast strayed onto ground they would rather not be seen crossing. They pay well and they pay in their own coin — giantborn-forged gear, fine cattle, and favor, which from a people this strong is worth more than silver. They honor a debt as long as they remember a slight, which is to say forever.\n\nIMPORTANT: the predators of the high crags are hostile and hunt by their own hunger, not on anyone's command. Hill-cats — great tawny mountain hunters the size of ponies — den in the rocks above the vale and come down for stragglers and calves. They open from cover with a rushing charge, drag down the isolated, and break off when met with a real fight, slinking back to try again later. The Tuskhollow treat losing a calf as an insult to answer rather than a danger to flee, which is how a fence-mending job becomes a hunt.\n\nGiantborn-forged gear is oversized, plain, and brutally well-made — work-tools and arms built to a giantborn's hand and reach. Tuskhollow prize cattle are clan-grade beasts worth real coin at market. Hill-cat pelts are luxury hunting-trophies and their fangs and claws are crafting stock.",
      "areas": {
        "The High Track": {
          "description": "A steep path switchbacks up out of the dwarf valleys into thinner, colder air, the grass turning to high meadow. Ahead the vale opens: a broad green bowl under bare peaks, longhouses sized for folk half again a human's height, stone-walled bull-pens stretching toward a stream, and a great standing stone at the boundary carved with a horned, broad-shouldered figure.",
          "paths": [
            "The Vale Common"
          ]
        },
        "The Vale Common": {
          "description": "The heart of the stead — an open common of cropped turf ringed by oversized longhouses, their doors built for giantborn with smaller doors cut alongside for ordinary visitors. A wrestling-ring of standing stones marks the center, the grass inside worn to bare earth. Graduated lifting-stones line one edge, the largest a boulder taller than a dwarf. The chief's longhouse stands at the head of the common; the largest hall, doors flung wide in daylight, serves food and drink at tables a dwarf must climb onto. The bull-pens lie just beyond, and the high fences mark where the meadow runs up toward the crags.",
          "paths": [
            "The High Track",
            "The Bull-Pens",
            "The High Meadow"
          ]
        },
        "The Bull-Pens": {
          "description": "Massive dry-stone enclosures holding the stead's prize bulls apart from the main herds — animals shaggy, horned, and ill-tempered, each pen built to a scale that dwarfs a human. Beyond the pens the vale opens into grazing land, the main herds scattered across the slopes. A deep, narrow crevice splits the ground past the furthest fence, the rock dropping away into shadow too tight for a broad-shouldered body to follow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Vale Common"
          ]
        },
        "The High Meadow": {
          "description": "The disputed high ground — a wide upland meadow of good grass, dwarf grazing by long custom and giantborn ground by claim. A drove-road crosses it, rutted by generations of cattle. Boundary-stones stand along the edges, and someone has been at them: a dwarf clan-mark cut on one face, a horned ur-giant figure carved over it, the dwarf mark re-cut deeper above that. Above the meadow the slope steepens into grey scree and split rock, riddled with dens and shadowed clefts, the whole vale laid out below it from the heights.",
          "paths": [
            "The Vale Common"
          ]
        }
      },
