    "The Crookback": {
      "name": "The Crookback",
      "x": 6,
      "y": -7,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A wind-bent tor on the high trade road, its summit a crooked finger of rock, the gullies beneath it a warren of camps. Clan Bighorn holds it — a clan of goatfolk who style themselves the Cloven People, ruled by a thane, flying a war-banner, and utterly convinced they are a highland clan the equal of any dwarven house. They keep the road as their clan's own, demand tribute from all who cross, and take by horn and blade from anyone who refuses.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Clan Bighorn are bandits and default to hostile. The 'tribute' they demand is robbery dressed as a clan's lawful due — there is no safe passage truly bought, only what a frightened traveler can hand over to buy a few more steps. They favor the shakedown over the kill because a robbed traveler can be robbed again, so a fat purse and a respectful manner may buy a way through; a thin purse, a sneer at their clanhood, or a party worth stripping brings the charge instead. They are goatfolk — horned, hoofed, and shaggy — and they fight with a headlong butting rush down the gullies and good salvaged steel.\n\nThe Cloven People believe in themselves with their whole hearts. They have watched the dwarves keep clans and holds and thanes and honor for ages and decided they will have all of it and do it better, having been born with the horns for it. They have a thane, a war-banner of their own design, a roll of grudges, and a fierce touchy pride in the dignity of the clan — and they are deadly serious about every scrap of it. Insult Clan Bighorn's standing and you have done worse in their eyes than refuse the tribute. What the dwarves find an outrage and a mockery, the Cloven find simple truth: they are a clan, the road is their clan's ground, and the dwarves are merely sore about the competition.\n\nThe thane is Old Battlehorn, a massive grey-pelted goatfolk with horns scarred from a lifetime of settling matters skull-first, who rules the clan from the hall atop the tor and takes any slight to its honor as a thing to answer in person. He wants a seat at the Folkmoot and a clan's respect across the highlands, and the dwarves will see the Crookback burned before they grant a goat-clan either.\n\nClan Bighorn lives by what the road brings and by trades no honest crew will touch — fencing stolen goods, running rustled cattle through the gullies, holding the occasional captive for ransom. Both Stonewall and Stormhammer want Battlehorn's head, and neither has spared the warriors to climb the tor and take it.\n\nThe thane's hoard holds the pick of years of robbery — coin, plate, trade-cargo, fenced goods. Cattle penned in the gullies carry the high glens' brands and drive back for clan-bounty. Battlehorn is wanted by both dwarven clans, and his head pays from either hand. Bighorn arms and armor are a patchwork of good salvaged gear, often dwarven-made, worn now by the Cloven who took it.",
      "areas": {
        "The Narrows": {
          "description": "The trade road bends into the tor's shadow and the walls close in — a gully barely two carts wide, the rock rising sheer on both sides, the crooked summit looming over the rim. Boulders and the dragged-out wrecks of old wagons choke the verges, stripped to the axles. Ledges and clefts notch the walls above, well-placed for watching the road or raining stones down on it. The way ahead twists out of sight; the way back is just as narrow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gullies"
          ]
        },
        "The Gullies": {
          "description": "A maze of steep-sided ravines beneath the tor, crammed with Clan Bighorn's warren — lean-to camps and shacks of salvaged timber wedged into the clefts, rope-bridges slung between the rims, cookfire smoke hanging in the still air. Stolen goods sit stacked under tarps in the dry hollows, and a clan-banner of stitched hides and painted horns hangs from a high pole. A wider cleft holds dry-stone pens churned to mud, thick with the smell of penned cattle. The paths branch and double back, the walls cutting every sightline short and climbing too sheer to scale. A switchback trail climbs toward the summit.",
          "paths": [
            "The Narrows",
            "The Crook"
          ]
        },
        "The Crook": {
          "description": "The tor's crooked summit, a bent spire of wind-scoured rock with a broad shelf beneath the finger where Clan Bighorn has raised its hall — a long structure of fitted salvage and hide, grander than the camps below, the great clan-banner snapping above it. Trophies of the clan's making line the approach: dwarven shields with the marks defaced and re-painted in Bighorn's colors. The whole sweep of the road lies open from here, gullies and narrows small below, the high country rolling away on every side.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gullies"
          ]
        }
      },
