    "Varric's Span": {
      "name": "Varric's Span",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 8,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A stretch of the great trade road where it crosses a deep gorge on a series of high dwarven bridges, ancient stonework spanning a drop that swallows the river-roar below. Trolls have taken the bridges — denning in the piers and the dark beneath the arches, hunting the road by night. It is the fastest way through the high country and the most dangerous, and it is the ground where a clan muster once broke in blood, a place every dwarf in Highheath knows by name.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: the trolls of the span default to hostile and hunt anything that crosses after dark. They den in the gorge piers and under the arches, hauling prey down off the road and into the dark to feed. They are slow to rouse but relentless once roused, and their wounds knit shut as you watch — cuts close, bone resets, a felled troll rises again unless the hurt is burned. Fire stops them; nothing else stops them for long. By day they hole up in the cold dark beneath the spans, and a fast crew can cross unmet; by night the bridges belong to them.\n\nThe Day at Varric's Span is the region's one true grudge, and it is not joked about. A generation back, Stonewall and Stormhammer mustered together against a troll-horde that had closed the road. Stonewall formed the wall and held it; Stormhammer charged ahead of the line and died on the bridge, cut off and overwhelmed while the wall stood fast and did not break to reach them. Stormhammer says the wall stood by and watched their kin butchered. Stonewall says the charge broke discipline and doomed itself. Every dwarf in Highheath knows someone who was there.\n\nThe troll-dead and the war-dead lie tangled in the gorge below, where bodies fell from the bridges and were never recovered — the drop is sheer and the trolls den at the bottom.\n\nTroll-hide and troll parts are hunt-trophies and crafting stock, prized by those who know that what regenerates is worth the taking. The arms and armor of the fallen muster still lie on the spans and in the gorge where dwarves died — a broken Stormhammer warmaul, Stonewall shields rusting where the wall held the line, clan-marked blades — old work, finely made, and heavy with meaning to anyone who carries it home. The gorge floor holds what fell and was never climbed down for.",
      "areas": {
        "The Approach": {
          "description": "The trade road climbs to the lip of a vast gorge, where the first of the high bridges leaps out across the drop. The stonework is old dwarven make — fitted blocks, carved piers, arches thrown across a chasm so deep the river is only a white thread and a distant roar far below. Wind funnels up the gorge and over the road. Cairns stand along the verge before the first span, weathered grey, some carved with clan-marks. The bridge runs out ahead into open air, the next pier rising from the dark below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Spans"
          ]
        },
        "The Spans": {
          "description": "The crossing proper — a chain of high arched bridges leaping pier to pier across the gorge, the road running narrow along their backs with low parapets and long drops to either side. The piers plunge into shadow below, their bases lost in the dark where the river runs. Rusted gear lies scattered along the stonework and caught in the parapet-joints — blades, shield-rims, a helm wedged in a crack. Dark openings gape in the pier-tops and under the arch-springs, leading down into the bridgework. The wind never stops.",
          "paths": [
            "The Approach",
            "The Underspan"
          ]
        },
        "The Underspan": {
          "description": "Below the bridge-decks, a world of cold stone and shadow — the great piers descending into the gorge, ledges and galleries cut into the rock between them, the arches springing overhead and the drop falling away beneath. Bones lie heaped in the sheltered hollows, cracked and gnawed, tangled here and there with rusted iron and rotted leather. The river roars somewhere below in the dark. The deepest ledges run down toward the gorge floor, where the light does not reach.",
          "paths": [
            "The Spans"
          ]
        }
      },
