    "Storm Crag": {
      "name": "Storm Crag",
      "x": -18,
      "y": 26,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A high peak wrapped in a storm that never breaks — a black thunderhead clamped over the summit year-round, lightning walking the crags, thunder rolling down the passes below. The Laughing Jarl holds it: a rogue storm giant who broke from the lawful giants and made the mountain his seat, his tribute-beasts ranging the slopes and his plunder heaped in the hall at the top.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Storm Crag is the Laughing Jarl's ground and everything on it is hostile. The beasts he has gathered — warg packs, hill-trolls, and worse — range the slopes as his hunters and tribute-takers, and they attack anything climbing toward the summit. The Jarl himself does not parley; he toys. He is a mountain of a giant, vast even by giant measure, and he finds the whole world funny, most of all the small things that come to kill him. He will talk, taunt, bargain in bad faith, and laugh the while — and the laughing is a trap, because he is never more dangerous than when he is amused.\n\nThe Jarl broke the old peace of the high giants for sport and appetite and made the storm-peak his own. He gathers beasts and broken things to him as a warlord gathers a warband, breaks bridges and crushes caravans for the joy of it, ransoms what he takes, and sets the dwarf clans at each other with cruel jokes that cost lives. Half the songs sung in Dun Solas are about him, and it is said he enjoys hearing them. The lawful crag giants of the high peaks disown him utterly and would pay richly to see him fall.\n\nIn a fight the Jarl throws stone and calls the storm down — lightning answers his hand, and a struck blow can fling a dwarf off the mountain. He fights like the contest is beneath him, careless and grinning, until the hurt is real, and a giant who stops laughing is a giant fighting for his life. He keeps his beasts between himself and danger and laughs when they die for him.\n\nThe Jarl's hoard is generations of ransom and plunder heaped in the summit hall — coin, plate, fine arms and armor, the wealth of a hundred crushed caravans. His tribute-beasts yield warg pelts and fangs, hide, and trophy parts to those who can take them. The captives he holds for ransom are worth more returned alive than anything else on the mountain.",
      "areas": {
        "The Thunder Road": {
          "description": "The pass climbs toward the storm-peak, the air growing heavy and charged, thunder rolling down to meet the climber. The trail switchbacks up bare grey rock toward a black wall of cloud clamped over the summit above, lightning flickering inside it. Scorch-marks blacken the stone where strikes have fallen. Wind drives down the slope hard enough to stagger.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crag Slopes"
          ]
        },
        "The Crag Slopes": {
          "description": "The mountain's broken shoulders, a wilderness of scree, boulder-fields, and shadowed clefts climbing into the storm. Dens and lairs notch the rock — bone-littered hollows, beast-trampled ground. The cloud closes lower here, rain and lightning lashing the heights, visibility cut to grey murk between the strikes. Switchback trails and goat-paths climb toward the summit, and the footing is loose and steep and treacherous in the wet.",
          "paths": [
            "The Thunder Road",
            "The Storm-Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Storm-Hall": {
          "description": "The summit, inside the storm — a vast open shelf of black stone beneath the heart of the thunderhead, lightning falling close. A great hall stands here, giant-built from raised slabs and the bones and timbers of what the Jarl has broken, its roof open to the raging sky. Plunder is heaped within and spilling out across the shelf — coin, arms, the splintered wealth of crushed caravans. The whole of Highheath lies spread out far below through the gaps in the cloud, tiny and distant.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crag Slopes"
          ]
        }
      },
