    "The Folkmoot": {
      "name": "The Folkmoot",
      "x": 4,
      "y": 26,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Highheath",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A contest-ground at the crossing of the high roads, built around the Stone of Bonds — an ancient black monolith that binds any oath sworn before it. This is where the clans settle what they cannot let lie: not with axes, but by out-doing each other.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Folkmoot is how Highheath settles a grudge without going to war. A debt, a boundary, an insult three generations stale — you do not raid over it and you do not sue, you bring it to the Moot and settle it in front of the whole highlands. An oath sworn at the Stone of Bonds binds; a contest fought and lost in fair sight is over, and the Stone makes it stick. This is the only reason Stonewall and Stormhammer have not burned each other down: the Moot lets a dwarf lose without losing face.\n\nThe contests are deadly serious and gloriously stupid in equal measure, and no one here sees any contradiction in that. The drinking matches are real tests of a dwarf's standing and not entered lightly. The stone-lifts run up to a boulder only a handful of giantborn have ever raised. And the Aurochs Stand is exactly what it sounds like: a dwarf plants their feet, a penned rumblehorn is loosed, and brow meets horn, and the one who holds the line wins. The wall of names runs back generations, and some of the names are crossed out.\n\nThe Moot-keeper is Vergi Hammerdram, a barrel-shaped old dwarf with a voice that carries across the whole ground and a record at the Stand no one living has matched. She runs the contests, witnesses the oaths, and bellows insults at champions twice her size to get a rise out of the crowd. Her neutrality is iron, because she has beaten everyone and owes nothing to anyone, and she is the only soul both clans trust to call a match fair. She is loud, rude, gleeful, and entirely incorruptible.\n\nAn outsider can earn standing here with no clan's leave — enter the contests, take the Stand, swear a Moot-oath that means something. A thane will sponsor a promising stranger against a rival's champion, and the side-bets on a good match run heavy. A player can settle their own business under a binding oath, or win another's grudge for them in the ring.\n\nIMPORTANT: the Stone of Bonds is real and an oath sworn before it binds in earnest — the narrator should treat a Moot-oath as a genuine, weighty commitment, not empty ceremony. A crew called the Stake-men work the contests on the quiet, fixing matches, shaving the odds, and leaning on fighters to throw a bout. They are the Syndicate's hand at the Moot and they keep it well hidden, because rigging the Stone's own ground is the one thing that would turn the whole highlands against them.\n\nA Moot-oath is a binding commitment witnessed by the Stone and the crowd both. Standing won at the Moot is a real reputation in dwarf country that opens doors a stranger's coin cannot. Thanes pay well to see a rival's champion humbled, and the side-betting makes a good fighter coin.",
      "areas": {
        "The Crossing": {
          "description": "Where the high roads meet, marked by a carved waypost worn smooth by hands. Low stone buildings with bright-painted doors line both roads, and the Stone of Bonds rises dark from the center of it all — a black monolith twice a dwarf's height, its surface carved over every inch with the marks of oaths sworn across the ages. Stalls and traders crowd the road-edges. From here the ground falls away toward a natural hollow ringed with stone seats, and a roar of voices rises from it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Contest Ground"
          ]
        },
        "The Contest Ground": {
          "description": "A natural hillside amphitheater, stone seats rising in worn tiers around a beaten-earth floor. A wrestling-ring of standing stones marks one end, the grass long since stomped to bare dirt. Graduated lifting-stones line the floor's edge, the heaviest a boulder taller than the dwarves who fail to shift it. A stout-fenced pen stands at the far end where a great shaggy rumblehorn waits, and before it a worn patch of ground where the Stand is taken. A board fixed to the amphitheater wall is crowded with carved names, a good many struck through.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crossing",
            "The Steaming Pools"
          ]
        },
        "The Steaming Pools": {
          "description": "Open-air hot springs at the ground's edge, stone-lined pools steaming in the cold air, ringed by benches and low tables. This is where the contests cool off and the real talk happens — challenges thrown, bets laid, oaths agreed before they go to the Stone.",
          "paths": [
            "The Contest Ground"
          ]
        }
      },
