    "Twicehold": {
      "name": "Twicehold",
      "x": -21,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whiteholds",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "An old dwarven holdfast on a high crag, broken open and squatted by ogres who call themselves the Twicelings. They reason that if halflings are the small folk, they are the large folk — twice the size and so, plainly, twice as great — and they hold the ruined hold as the seat of the finest folk in the mountains. They have knocked it about to fit their bulk, piled it with what they have taken, and they demand toll from the roads below in the firm belief that this is their due.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: the Twicelings default to hostile and take what they want from anyone smaller and weaker, which is nearly everyone. They are huge, slab-muscled, and slow-witted, and they fight by wading in and swinging — clubs, torn stonework, whatever is to hand — trusting their size and thick hide to carry them through. A traveler who flatters them, hands over tribute, or amuses them may be let pass or even welcomed as an honored small guest; a traveler who insults the Twicelings' obvious greatness, or simply looks like easy pickings, gets grabbed. They are not clever, but they are not always wrong about who to grab.\n\nThe Twicelings believe in their own magnificence completely and without strain. They took the hold because it was the grandest thing they had ever seen and grand things belong to great folk; they wear dwarven armor beaten out flat to fit, sit on dwarven thrones built for far smaller bodies, and have given themselves enormous titles they cannot quite keep straight. They are genuinely hospitable to anyone who treats them as the lords they know themselves to be, and genuinely baffled and hurt when the dwarves come to throw them out of a home they are so obviously improving. The dwarves want the hold back and want the indignity of ogres in it ended, and have not yet mustered the strength to do either.\n\nThe Twicelings hoard everything bright or interesting they take off the roads, with no idea what most of it is worth — they will trade a masterwork blade for a shiny bauble and feel they got the better of you. Their hall is heaped with plunder: coin, plate, arms and armor, fine goods carried up from the valleys. Dwarven gear taken from the hold and from travelers lies among it, much of it ruined by ogre handling, some of it still good.\n\nVoice note: when Twicelings boast, charge, celebrate, or work themselves up, give them blunt doubled slogans and chants in this register: 'KILL TWICE! EAT TWICE! TWICELINGS!' They should sound huge, delighted with themselves, and proudly stupid rather than clever.",
      "areas": {
        "The Broken Gate": {
          "description": "The holdfast's gate stands torn half off its hinges, the stone archway above cracked and the dwarven carvings on it scarred and chipped. The road up to it is littered with the wreckage of carts and the leavings of crude meals. Past the gate a courtyard opens, its fine flagstones heaved up and broken under heavy tread, dwarven statues toppled and a few stood back up wrong. Crude lean-tos of timber and hide huddle against the old stone walls.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Court"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruined Court": {
          "description": "The holdfast's main yard, once handsome, now a Twiceling camp. Dwarven masonry stands cracked and soot-blackened, doorways knocked wider to admit ogre bulk, and oversized firepits scar the flagstones. Piles of taken goods sit heaped against the walls under no order at all — timber, sacks, bright wreckage. Broken dwarven furniture has been dragged out and crudely re-purposed into seats and tables built up to ogre scale. A wide stair, its steps cracked under heavy use, climbs toward the great hall above.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broken Gate",
            "The Great Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Great Hall": {
          "description": "The holdfast's feast-hall, its high carved ceiling now hung with crude trophies and stripped banners. The dwarven lord's seat still stands on the dais, far too small for the bulk that has tried to claim it, with a larger crude throne of stacked stone and timber built up beside it. Plunder lies heaped along the walls and spilled across the floor — coin, plate, arms, the bright leavings of the roads. The dwarven hearth at the hall's end has been widened with smashed masonry into a great rough firepit.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Court"
          ]
        }
      },
