    "The Witchwood": {
      "name": "The Witchwood",
      "x": 28,
      "y": 38,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Witchwood is a dark swamp of black standing water and close, leaning trees, wrapped around the village of Eidelmar at its heart. It is easy to get lost in: the ground is a maze of water, root, and reed, trails are hard to find again once left, and a traveler often has the feeling the trees are not the same as when he passed them before. The wood is rich in the materials of charm-craft — rowan and witch-hazel, silver-bark from the deep mire, river-reed, bog-iron, and the pale moss that only grows in foul air. The weather turns without warning, here as across the region: a clear morning can fog over in minutes, or snow, or close into dark. In fog, in storms, and after nightfall the wood grows dangerous, and the beasts of its depths come to take the unwary.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE PLANAR BLEED. The mages of Tarnholme hold the Witchwood to be a bleed of change-mana — a place where the Plane of Change wells up through the ground, the dark twin of Eidelmar's bleed from the Plane of Stasis — and they say it is why living things that linger here too long start to alter, and why the village's charms work so well so close by. The locals do not put it that way. They say the wood is witched, that it has always been witched, and that the charms and the scarewitches are simply what keeps a person themselves while they are in it.\n\nTHREATS. Werewolves run in the wood's depths. Great tusked boars, far too large and intelligent to be mere beasts, hunt in packs. Wild spellforms drift the mire and change what they touch; warped beasts that have lost their right shape den in the deep water; and on the worst nights something comes to the village edge wearing a face that is almost right. After dark and in foul weather these press toward the charmed margins; by day they keep to the deep wood. The things taken from them carry the wood's own change: a werewolf pelt is a shape-ward — worn, it turns aside magic that would alter the wearer's form. A great boar's tusk holds a carved rune longer and deeper than any common bone, and a rune cut into one does not wear away. The slow-fading residue left where a spellform comes apart can be caught in glass while it still glows; held, it warps the next casting near it, for good or ill.\n\nTHE SISTERS. Three witches keep the deepest wood, sisters who were an Eidelmarn family generations back and walked out to take the wood's power. They live in huts that stride about on great three-toed legs and roost where they please. Their errands are run by made-things: hounds and watching birds and worse that were once people who crossed them.\n\nWHEN IT RUNS HOT. A change-manastorm comes apart in half-finished shapes, the ground going strange, a careless traveler's own hand altering on his wrist if he stands in it too long. A manasurge wakes the whole wood at once: the warped things run thick, and the sisters' huts walk far past their usual ground, out to where the scarewitches stand and the black water laps at the last firm trail to the village.",
      "areas": {
        "The Gatherers' Verge": {
          "description": "The near wood, within the scarewitches, where the timber still stands in daylight and the ground is firm enough to walk. Cut stumps and stripped rowan mark where the village works; charm-cords hang from low branches, and a worn path winds in from the village of Eidelmar before it forks and fades among the trees. Past the last charm-cord the canopy closes and the light goes green.",
          "paths": [
            "The Black Mire"
          ]
        },
        "The Black Mire": {
          "description": "Standing black water under a low roof of leaning trees, broken by hummocks of root and reed and the odd ridge of firmer ground. The water gives no reflection and no bottom a pole can find. Footing that looked solid sinks underfoot, and the wood looks the same in every direction, so that a traveler who turns around twice cannot say which way the village lies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gatherers' Verge",
            "The Long Crossing",
            "The Striding Ground"
          ]
        },
        "The Long Crossing": {
          "description": "A causeway of old fallen trunks and piled stone laid across the deepest stretch of the mire, the only dry footing for a long way in any direction. Moss-slick and narrow, it runs between two banks lost in fog, with deep black water to either side and the bones of less careful crossings tangled in the reeds below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Black Mire"
          ]
        },
        "The Striding Ground": {
          "description": "The oldest and deepest part of the wood, where the trees grow vast and wrong-angled and great three-toed prints are pressed into the soft ground, leading nowhere and everywhere. Clearings open where something heavy has lately stood and moved on, the grass flattened in a wide ring.",
          "paths": [
            "The Black Mire"
          ]
        }
      },
