      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Eidelmar is a quaint village of colorfully painted timber homes that stand along sparkling canals. Travelers from all Larion come here to purchase the famous charms, wards and runes that purify its air and water from the fetid Witchwood it borders and protect its inhabitants from the fierce beasts who den in its swampy depths. Clean water flows under its little hump-backed bridges where the wood outside runs black and pestilent, and the weather within the village is sunny and mild while the swamp storms and festers a stone's throw past the last house. The Eidelmarn are proud of the bangles and charms that hang from every eave and post, the witch-balls that dangle in every window, and especially of the doll-like guardians called scarewitches posted around the village's edge, marking the border between fair and foul.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Eidelmarn have perfected their charm-making craft over generations and are glad to teach it to outsiders, not only to sell it. Though they welcome the Academy's help in the worst of the magic storms and fogs, they have little patience for the learned wizards of Tarnholme and their talk of mana welling up from the Plane of Stasis. To an Eidelmarn it is really quite simple: a good charm holds a thing as it is, fair and whole and itself, no matter what the wood would make of it. Rowan and witch-hazel and silver-bark, all gathered from the depths of the Witchwood despite the dangers, are carved and inscribed and painted into charms, wards, and runecraft.\n\nThe scarewitches are the village's most powerful charms: man-sized dolls of painted Witchwood, lovingly dressed in fine clothes and kept in repair by the families whose stretch of edge they watch. It is said no beast of the Witchwood can bear their gaze, so any field or verge a scarewitch keeps is safe from whatever creeps or crawls. Some say the oldest of them have taken on a secret life: when no one is looking, the dolls will walk their rounds, murmur a warning, or turn to watch a stranger — though few villagers will swear they have seen one move.\n\nThe weather is fine most days, but when the witchfog rolls in off the swamp and will not blow out again, or a purple lightning-storm hangs over the rooftops for days on end, the Eidelmarn know it is time to swallow their pride and ring the great bell that calls Tarnholme. The Academy sends its finest to quiet the weather, set the wood back in its place, and drive home whatever the fog let in.",
      "areas": {
        "The Canal Quay": {
          "description": "Clear water enters the village here through a stone arch, running bright between mossy quay-walls where the swamp's black water is held back beyond the grating. Flat-bottomed boats tie up along the steps; painted houses lean over the channel on both banks. A little humpbacked bridge crosses to the lanes beyond.",
          "paths": [
            "The Charm Market",
            "The Clearwater"
          ]
        },
        "The Charm Market": {
          "description": "A cobbled square strung overhead with charms on cords — bangles, bells, knots of red thread, and witch-balls that catch the light. Stalls and shopfronts crowd the edges under flowered awnings, their tables laid with trinkets and wards. The painted facades here are the brightest in the village, every shutter and sign freshly kept.",
          "paths": [
            "The Canal Quay",
            "The Clearwater",
            "Better Half Charms",
            "The Scarewitch Walk"
          ]
        },
        "The Clearwater": {
          "description": "A timber tavern built out over the canal on stilted piers, its windows of thick leaded glass glowing amber after dark. Inside, low beams, a wide hearth, and long scrubbed tables; a balcony at the back hangs over the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Canal Quay",
            "The Charm Market"
          ]
        },
        "Better Half Charms": {
          "description": "A tall crooked workshop-house on the finest stretch of canal, its ground floor a cluttered shop of finished charms and its upper floors loud with the tapping and filing of charm-work. Witchwood stock — pale silver branches, bundles of rowan and reed — is stacked along the walls and drying from the rafters. Two married couples keep the place, and their good-natured quarreling over whose work is finer carries into the street.",
          "paths": [
            "The Charm Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Scarewitch Walk": {
          "description": "The village's outermost path, where the last houses give way to the wood and the scarewitches stand at their posts — painted figures in village colors, dressed and crowned with charms, spaced along the tree-line as far as the eye follows. Beyond the last of them the ground turns wet and the timber goes dark. Old Pleasance keeps the mill-bridge, where the clear water flows out toward the swamp.",
          "paths": [
            "The Charm Market"
          ]
        }
      },
