    "Planes-haven Isle": {
      "name": "Planes-haven Isle",
      "x": -10,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Planes-haven Isle is a crowded little island in the Jade Tarn whose shores offer safe haven to creatures from other planes. Narrow lanes wend up and down steep hills between crooked, mismatched buildings in styles as varied as their residents. Most of the island's inhabitants are freedfolk: exotic beings who escaped their bonds in other worlds and made their way to the one place where they can live in peace. Known as the City of Fortuitous Meetings, Planes-haven Isle offers visitors the chance to meet people, learn things, and lay hands on goods to be found nowhere else in Larion.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE PLANAR BLEED. The mages of Tarnholme hold Planes-haven Isle to be a bleed of conjuration-mana — a place where the Plane of Summoning wells up. To the freedfolk, the island is simply home, and the only safe ground in Larion.\n\nWHY THEY STAY. A freedman cannot go home: the masters and powers he escaped want him back. Now and then a hunter from another plane crosses over and stalks the island in disguise, searching for a freedman who got away, meaning to drag him back in chains. And a freedman cannot leave Planes-haven Isle either. Away from the island, Larion's wild mana works on a freed planar creature like strong drink — it makes him giddy, then wild, then dangerous, until he is a menace running loose with no sense left in him. On the island he is steady and himself. Off it, sooner or later, he goes rogue.\n\nWHO KEEPS THE ISLAND. The freedfolk govern themselves through a council of their eldest — those who came through longest ago and have kept the peace longest. They settle their own quarrels, stop rogue outsiders, and ring the bell that calls Tarnholme only when something has gone badly past their handling.\n\nWHAT A VISITOR FINDS. Across Larion the Pact of All Planes forbids planar outsiders to walk free; on Planes-haven Isle that rule is set aside, and so the island offers what nowhere else dares. A conjurer can learn rare callings and bindings. A visitor can sit and bargain face to face with a strange planar being. Goods from other worlds change hands here, and secrets travel with them.\n\nWHEN IT RUNS HOT. A manastorm or manasurge may throw the gates between worlds wide, allowing things to flood through uncalled, or powerful hunters to steal into Larion to claim back those who have slipped their bonds.",
      "areas": {
        "The Quay": {
          "description": "The island's one landing, a crescent of worn stone steps and tie-posts where the ferries from Tarnholme and the lakeshore come in. The Jade Tarn laps green on every side, and from the quay the town climbs away up its hill in a tumble of roofs and lantern-strung lanes. Boats crowd the steps; beyond the last mooring there is only open water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Flotsam and Jetsam Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Flotsam and Jetsam Market": {
          "description": "A steep market street switchbacking up the hillside, crammed on both sides with stalls and stacked shopfronts under a clutter of awnings and hanging lamps. Here a visitor finds what no Larion market sells: bottled reagents for callings and bindings, chalk and cord and graven foci for the summoner's circle, charms cut to bind or banish a planar thing, and oddments carried through from other worlds — strange metals, glowing salts, and fruit and cloth and trinkets of no earthly make. The lane is narrow, the crowd close, and the steps worn smooth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Quay",
            "Strangefellows",
            "The Heights"
          ]
        },
        "Strangefellows": {
          "description": "The island's great tavern, a leaning timber house of many floors built where the climbing lanes meet, its windows lit at all hours and its doors never quite shut. Inside, a warren of low rooms, snugs, and galleries on different levels, joined by crooked stairs — tables tucked into corners made for quiet talk, and a long common room below where the noise and the music gather.",
          "paths": [
            "The Flotsam and Jetsam Market",
            "The Old Moot"
          ]
        },
        "The Old Moot": {
          "description": "An old stone house at the crown of the hill where the lanes all run together, where the island's eldest sit to hear whatever the day brings them. Folk come up to the long room within to bring their troubles to the elders — a neighbor who has not been seen in days, a stranger asking too many questions, one of their own grown wild and wandered off the island. A worn bell hangs by the door, the one that calls Tarnholme when a trouble has outrun the island's own hands.",
          "paths": [
            "Strangefellows"
          ]
        },
        "The Heights": {
          "description": "The upper slopes of the island, where the freedfolk make their homes in a tumble of small, beautiful, mismatched houses. No two are alike: a snug round tower with a pointed cap and one warm window; a cottage built into the hillside with a turf roof and a crooked chimney; a slender house of many balconies stacked like a stair; a little bridge-house spanning the gap between two rocks. Lanterns hang at every door, flowers spill from every sill, and the lanes between are steep, narrow, and swept clean. From the height the whole green Tarn lies below, and the white towers of Tarnholme rise across the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Flotsam and Jetsam Market"
          ]
        }
      },
