    "The Spell-Welter": {
      "name": "The Spell-Welter",
      "x": 24,
      "y": -28,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A country of cave-riddled wind-carved red stone and dry gulleys in the southeast of the Jade Tarn, under a sky that never settles. A rich manawell wells up beneath it, and the magic stirs an endless wheel of small magical storms that drift across the badlands and never blow out. The mana draws magical creatures from across Larion, and the wild magic has left them changed: beasts that take to the air on new-grown wings, that flit from place to place in a blink, that fade from sight but for their eyes.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE BLEED. The Academy has tried to quell the storms and failed every time.\n\nCONTAIN OR HARVEST. Most at the Academy would guard it and hunt what emerges: it breeds rogues and spills dangerous creatures, but is too dangerous to master. Others say the Academy should harvest its spellforms, creatures, and powers found nowhere else.\n\nTHE CREATURES. The wild mana draws magical creatures and then grants them extraordinary powers. A hound learns to warp from place to place; a great cat fades from sight but for its eyes; a stag or a horse grows wings; a serpent's gaze turns the looker to stone. Each carries its power in its body. Such a beast's sinew worked into a charm carries the same blink, a vanishing cat's pelt hides the wearer from sight, a winged thing's feathers lift a working into the air, and a stone-gaze gland is the one sure cure for being turned to stone, and poison to anyone who tries to use it raw. Among them move drakelings, small dragons that nest in the high stone and breathe true fire; manticores, the deadliest hunters of the badlands; and will-o'-wisps that drift the gulleys, luring the lost and drinking the life out of them.\n\nTHE SPELLFORMS. Spellforms that escape elsewhere in the region drift here and do not fade as they would on tamer ground; in the rich mana they linger, and the oldest of them grow. The common ones are simple combat-spells given shape — darting fire, creeping frost, a wandering ward — that fling their magic at whatever comes near, dangerous as wild animals. The rare ones are stranger, stronger spells drifting loose, and a spellform taken alive gives up the spell it is, so that a careful hunter learns from it a casting no master at Tarnholme would teach.\n\nTHE ROGUES. Rogue mages den in the Spell-Welter. They come to catch its spellforms, take its creatures, and work magic the Academy would never allow — and being beyond all law here, they guard their finds jealously and kill rivals who wander too close. Some go further, and try to feed the bleed rather than fight it: stirring the storms wider, driving them out toward the green country, in the belief that a Larion gone wild with loose magic is theirs to inherit. The deepest-dwelling of them have worked the raw mana so long they are halfway to losing their own minds and shapes to it.\n\nWHEN IT RUNS HOT. The badlands is never still, but now and then the storms gather all at once into a greater surge, and the whole country turns deadly: the manastorms rage together, the creatures run frenzied and bold, and spellforms boil up out of the ground thick as gnats.",
      "areas": {
        "The Redbreak": {
          "description": "The edge of the badlands, where the green farmland of the Tarn runs out against a wall of broken red stone and goes no further. The ground rises in shelves of cracked rock, dry washes cut down between them, and the grass thins to nothing within a few paces of the line. Beyond, the land opens into wind-carved country, and the small storms can be seen wheeling over it even from here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stoneflats"
          ]
        },
        "The Stoneflats": {
          "description": "A wide reach of bare red rock and rolling broken ground, weathered into ridges, knobs, and shallow basins as far as the eye carries. There is little cover and a long line of sight, so the wandering storms are visible from a great way off, drifting slow across the open and trailing strange light. Dry washes thread away between the rises, and the higher rock stands jagged on the skyline.",
          "paths": [
            "The Redbreak",
            "The Gulleys",
            "The Spires"
          ]
        },
        "The Gulleys": {
          "description": "A maze of dry channels cut deep into the stone, narrow and winding, their walls rising close overhead so that the sky is only a bright crooked seam above. The light pools strange down here, green and violet washing along the rock when a storm passes over, and sound carries oddly. The channels branch and rejoin without pattern, and it is easy to lose all sense of which way leads back out.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stoneflats"
          ]
        },
        "The Spires": {
          "description": "The high country of the badlands, where wind and weather have carved the red stone into tall pinnacles, arches, and sheer-sided towers. Ledges and clefts pock the heights, and the climbing is hard and exposed. From the tops the whole storming country lies open below, the slow wheel of the small storms turning across the flats, and the green edge of the Tarn a thin bright line in the far distance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stoneflats"
          ]
        }
      },
