    "The Bastion": {
      "name": "The Bastion",
      "x": -9,
      "y": 14,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Bastion is the island of the College of Sentinels, Tarnholme's wardens and battle-mages. Its halls, dueling rings, and sky-skate moorings sit on stone rises linked by stepping-stones, swaying spans, and leaping-stones over the green shallows. Crossing the island is part obstacle course and part running joke: a shove of force, a tripping-hex, a gust off the water, and the careless go straight into the Tarn.",
      "hiddenInfo": "ACADEMY TONE. Narrate Tarnholme as a lively fantasy school of wizardry, not a modern scientific or technical institution. Use concrete, visual, character-centered magical language: wards flare, charms misbehave, old books answer, summoned beings bargain, and spells take strange shapes. Avoid modern scientific jargon, clinical diagnosis, measurements, technical-system explanations, and pseudo-scientific accounts of how magic works. Do not make Academy life uniformly solemn, dry, or over-serious; preserve wonder, rivalry, humor, danger, craft, and adventure even when the work matters.\n\nSentinels train in shielding, warding, and combat-magic, and they like being the College everyone calls when something has gone wrong. They are loud, physical, competitive, and proud of being hard to rattle.\n\nThe island's dunking game is training in disguise. Warded shoves, tripping-hexes, and sudden gusts are tolerated because a Sentinel who wards on instinct and comes up laughing will hold steadier when the work is real. The masters let the game run, then make serious drill absolutely serious.\n\nThe Dueling Rings hold the Three-Touch standings: clean hits, quick wards, no maiming, and endless argument over who really won. A newcomer with a good arm rises fast and gets challenged faster.\n\nSky-skate riding is status as much as transport. Beginners ride harnessed; good riders stand loose; show-offs carve turns in bad weather where everyone can see. The moorings are a lively hangout as much as a stable: students crowd the rail to flirt, boast, shout dares, and call challenges before an audience.\n\nWhen the muster bell rings, play stops. Sentinels tack up and ride out to manastorms, haunts, escaped summons, dangerous Practica, and anything else that needs wards raised fast.\n\nThe island's standing posts are held by men, and they are who a newcomer has to get past first: the yard-masters who run drill on Shock-Rock and shout corrections across the water, the bout-callers who keep the Three-Touch standings and argue every disputed hit, the ward-serjeants who drill first-years until a shield comes up without thinking, and the skate-masters at the Moorings who say who rides harnessed and who rides loose. The student ranks themselves are young men and women in roughly equal number.",
      "areas": {
        "The Ringstead": {
          "description": "The island's portal landing, a round white court just above the water where an iron-bound ring-arch opens to the other islands for those it knows. The court has no wall; the first leaping-stones run from its edge across the green shallows.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaping-Stones"
          ]
        },
        "The Leaping-Stones": {
          "description": "The way across the island: stepping-stones, swaying plank-spans, and tall leaping-stones strung between rises above cold green water. Students cut across at speed, dodge each other, and sometimes help a friend into the Tarn for educational reasons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ringstead",
            "The Muster Hall",
            "The Dueling Rings",
            "The Moorings",
            "Shock-Rock"
          ]
        },
        "The Muster Hall": {
          "description": "The Sentinels' hall and lodgings, a long white-stone house with scrubbed tables, bunk-galleries, weapon-racks, shield-stands, riding tack, and a heavy bell tower heard across the archipelago. The windows look out over wheeling skates.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaping-Stones"
          ]
        },
        "The Dueling Rings": {
          "description": "Raised stone circles along the southern rise, ward-hazed and worn smooth at the center, with benches beside the largest ring. A posting-board at the row end is thick with chalked names, challenges, and Three-Touch standings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaping-Stones"
          ]
        },
        "The Moorings": {
          "description": "The skate-cote at the island edge: timber perches, padded roost-ledges, rigging-posts, saddle-rigs, bridles, and straps under a long lean-to roof. The shallows are kept clear so sky-skates can rise and settle from the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaping-Stones"
          ]
        },
        "Shock-Rock": {
          "description": "A bare rocky islet off the Bastion edge, used for hard training. Its flats are scorched, frost-cracked, cratered, and split by generations of ward-breaking spells; target-walls and shattered practice-stones stand around the scars.",
          "paths": [
            "The Leaping-Stones"
          ]
        }
      },
