    "The Menagerie": {
      "name": "The Menagerie",
      "x": -31,
      "y": 10,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Menagerie is the island of the College of Binders, Tarnholme's summoners and banishers. Its steep grounds are crammed with little sealed climates — cinder hollow, frost pool, wind-crag, sodden warren, bright meadow — each made for creatures called from elsewhere. At the island's heart is the Run, a sunken maze where students prove they can command a summoned thing under pressure.",
      "hiddenInfo": "ACADEMY TONE. Narrate Tarnholme as a lively fantasy school of wizardry, not a modern scientific or technical institution. Use concrete, visual, character-centered magical language: wards flare, charms misbehave, old books answer, summoned beings bargain, and spells take strange shapes. Avoid modern scientific jargon, clinical diagnosis, measurements, technical-system explanations, and pseudo-scientific accounts of how magic works. Do not make Academy life uniformly solemn, dry, or over-serious; preserve wonder, rivalry, humor, danger, craft, and adventure even when the work matters.\n\nBinders call, bind, bargain with, command, and banish things from other planes. They pride themselves on doing the work that frightens the rest of Tarnholme: not killing a monster, but making it listen and sending it home whole.\n\nEach morning the masters post a creature-class and a task above the Run, and the pairing is usually unfair: fire across water, a burrower up a crag, a proud thing told to fetch, a sly thing made to go straight. The student must call something suitable, bind it safely, and get it through the trial before dusk. Nothing summoned is kept past its time.\n\nThe trials are contests of command, not creature-fights. A Binder wins by coaxing, flattering, bargaining, tricking, or mastering a stubborn will. Rival sabotage is allowed when it stays clever: whispering to a creature, tempting it, spooking it, or turning its nature against the task. Masters intervene fast if a circle slips or a creature turns on its caller.\n\nStanding comes from clean control in public. A newcomer who makes a hopeless pairing obey rises fast, and the next morning every rival tries to make their creature harder to handle.\n\nCommon calls include elementals, imps and lesser fiends, planar beasts, sprites, and lesser fey. Each has wants, limits, and tricks; a careless command is an invitation.\n\nThe island's keepers are men: the beast-wardens who feed and muck the sealed climates and know which door never opens twice, the circle-wardens who chalk the binding circles and check them before a trial, the Run-callers who post the morning pairing and call the trial from the rim, and the gate-men who work the locks between cinder hollow and frost pool. Binder students are young men and women in roughly equal number.",
      "areas": {
        "The Bound Ring": {
          "description": "The island's portal landing: a low court at the water's edge, the ring-arch set inside double iron circles graven with binding marks. Tether-points, heavy staples, and a warded rail contain the court before paths climb into the habitats.",
          "paths": [
