    "The Stacks": {
      "name": "The Stacks",
      "x": -33,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Stacks is the island of the College of Seekers, Tarnholme's loremasters: a white marble tower-library grown over generations into a vertical maze of shelves, bridges, reading-courts, and spiral stairs. Students come here to identify strange magic, name monsters, translate dead scripts, trace old maps, and turn mysteries into directions. There is no map of the Stacks itself. Visitors find their way by asking the librarian statues, who know every shelf and wake gladly for a real question.",
      "hiddenInfo": "ACADEMY TONE. Narrate Tarnholme as a lively fantasy school of wizardry, not a modern scientific or technical institution. Use concrete, visual, character-centered magical language: wards flare, charms misbehave, old books answer, summoned beings bargain, and spells take strange shapes. Avoid modern scientific jargon, clinical diagnosis, measurements, technical-system explanations, and pseudo-scientific accounts of how magic works. Do not make Academy life uniformly solemn, dry, or over-serious; preserve wonder, rivalry, humor, danger, craft, and adventure even when the work matters.\n\nSeekers find things out: what a relic is, what a haunt wants, what language a curse is written in, what beast left the tooth, what spell went wrong, and what old name opens a locked door. They are sharp, curious, competitive, and happiest when a question points somewhere dangerous.\n\nThe Stacks turns research into outward motion. A torn map becomes a route; a monster sign becomes a hunt; a name in a margin becomes a ruin, faction, bloodline, or warning. The College does not treat knowledge as an end in itself — the useful answer is the one that tells someone where to go next and what to be afraid of when they get there.\n\nThe librarian statues are part of the building: marble index-keepers who wake when addressed, remember every shelf, and guide honest seekers through the maze. Each has a manner — dry, grand, sleepy, theatrical, or ancient enough to answer a breath late — but they are helpful before they are amusing. They do not lie, hoard answers, or make a performance out of blocking the way.\n\nThe harder answers are higher up, deeper in, or held in books that require a master-key, a College sponsor, or a good reason. Students race each other up stairwells after disputed citations, argue across balconies, and trade leads over cold tea before running off to prove a theory in the field.\n\nBeneath the statues the Stacks is run by men: the shelf-wardens who walk the galleries and quiet the reading-courts, the ladder-men who fetch down from the high terraces on request, the key-masters who hold the master-keys and decide whether a reason is good enough, and the old citation-callers who settle disputed sources aloud from the balconies. Seeker students are young men and women in roughly equal number.",
      "areas": {
        "The Ringstead": {
          "description": "The island's portal landing, a round marble court at the waterline where the ring-arch opens to the other islands for those it knows. Weathered librarian statues flank the arch, and behind them the library rises straight up in galleries, bridges, and stairs.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Galleries"
