    "The Mend": {
      "name": "The Mend",
      "x": -19,
      "y": 5,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Mend is the island of the College of Menders, Tarnholme's healers and curse-breakers: warm pools, shaded gardens, open lawns, and long tables always laid with food. Students from every College come here to recover, flirt, gossip, and soak off spell-burns and bruises, while patients from across Larion arrive hoping the Menders can set right what no one nearer home could mend.",
      "hiddenInfo": "ACADEMY TONE. Narrate Tarnholme as a lively fantasy school of wizardry, not a modern scientific or technical institution. Use concrete, visual, character-centered magical language: wards flare, charms misbehave, old books answer, summoned beings bargain, and spells take strange shapes. Avoid modern scientific jargon, clinical diagnosis, measurements, technical-system explanations, and pseudo-scientific accounts of how magic works. Do not make Academy life uniformly solemn, dry, or over-serious; preserve wonder, rivalry, humor, danger, craft, and adventure even when the work matters.\n\nMenders close wounds, draw out poison and blight, break curses, and treat the strange harms magic leaves behind. Their creed is simple: anyone who comes hurt is taken in and tended, friend or stranger, rival or enemy. A student learns by tending real people with real trouble.\n\nThe Mend belongs to everyone. Sentinels come in bruised from the rings, Binders come in singed from circles, Shapers come in with hands stuck briefly as claws, and Seekers arrive carrying something that should not be humming. The pools and tables turn into the Academy's softest social battleground: gossip, comfort, apologies, romances, and arguments conducted waist-deep in warm water.\n\nThere is always someone at the pool's edge with a story: a hunter whose wound opens again each dawn, a child who will not wake, a carter shaking after strange fog, a woman whose reflection moves a half-step late. Most are mended in a day. Some become Practica.\n\nA Mender's field work starts wherever the trouble is: cursed wells, sick herds, blighted gardens, haunted houses, bad charms, mana-sickness, and villages that have waited too long to ask for help.\n\nThe Mend's working posts are held by men, and the hurt who arrive from across Larion are as often men as women: the pool-keepers who heat, drain, and salt the warm pools, the litter-bearers who carry the badly wounded up from the water, the table-masters who keep the long tables laid at every hour, and the ward-porters who sit the Quiet Ward through the night. Hunters, carters, drovers, boatmen, and soldiers come up the Garland Arch with their trouble as readily as anyone else. Mender students are young men and women in roughly equal number.",
