    "Lanternby": {
      "name": "Lanternby",
      "x": -1,
      "y": -11,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Lanternby is the ferry town on the near shore of the Jade Tarn, the last solid ground before the islands and the place the whole Academy comes ashore. Painted houses and crowded shopfronts climb the slope above a long stone pier, where the skiffs put in and students step off to spend a free evening away from the towers. It is most famous for its lamps. Generations of Tarnholme students have made enchanted lights and never quite gotten them right — lamps that drift, that gutter when someone lies, that only kindle for a face they like, that hum to themselves — and rather than waste them the town took them in, until now they hang from every eave and gable and pole, and the lamp-sellers of the market will press a dozen stranger ones on any visitor who slows down to look. By day the Tarn is green and the lamps hang dark and for sale; by night the whole town comes alight in mismatched colors, and its reflection on the dark water is the first thing a student sees rowing home.",
      "hiddenInfo": "TOWN AND GOWN. Lanternby lives off the Academy and is thoroughly unimpressed by it. The townsfolk — mostly human and halfling, ferry-families and innkeepers and tailors and brewers whose trades have served the school for generations — see the students as a pleasant mix of custom, nuisance, and spectacle, and a young mage who puts on airs here will be charged double and humored not at all. For the students it is the one place that is not the campus: no Circles, no Colleges, no masters, just an ordinary town where a person can drink and eat and be no one in particular for a night. Every kind of trade the school needs has a shopfront here — robe-tailors, foci and reagent shops, booksellers, and of course the lamps — and the inns and taverns along the front do a roaring evening trade in homesick, footloose, and celebrating students.\n\nWHAT PASSES THROUGH. Being the crossing-point, Lanternby sees more of the wider world than the rest of the region: traders up from the lakeshore and the coast, the odd retired mage who chose a house in town over a cell on the islands, a Witchwood charm-seller with a stall of scarecrow-trinkets, now and then a freedman down from Planes-Haven to walk on solid ground for a day. The locals are ordinary; it is the traffic through them that is strange.\n\nTHE BACK ROOMS. Not all the trade is in the front of the shop. A magic school produces things worth a great deal to the wrong people — relics pulled from the deep without the Academy's leave, copies of texts from the locked stacks, confiscated charms that walk out of the vaults, bound creatures sold quietly to collectors — and most of it moves through Lanternby, because this is where the islands touch the world. The pawnshops along the back lanes ask no questions, the fences keep rooms above the taverns, and the men who run it are the Syndicate, who have made this shabby shore-town the seat of their business in the region. They buy from greedy students and bent servants, they sell to buyers who should know better, and they prefer the goods to leave the region by night and by water rather than under anyone's seal.",
      "areas": {
        "The Pier": {
          "description": "The long stone landing where the lake-skiffs put in, running out into the green shallows on worn pilings, with tie-posts, mooring-rings, and a weathered ferry-house at its root. This is where the islands meet the shore: students step off the skiffs here and the town climbs away above, while out across the water the white towers of Tarnholme rise from their islands. Lamps hang dark along the pilings by day and burn at night to guide the last boats in.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lamp Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Lamp Market": {
          "description": "The town's market square and the heart of its trade, a sloping cobbled space crammed with stalls and shopfronts under a low sky of hanging lamps — rank on rank of them strung overhead on cords and poles, no two alike. The lamp-sellers' stalls crowd thickest here, their wares lit even by day to show off a drift, a color, a glow, and a buyer who pauses is set upon at once with a dozen stranger ones. Robe-tailors, booksellers, and reagent shops line the square's edges, and lanes run off it down toward the water and back into the older town.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pier",
            "The Skiff and Star",
            "The Back Lanes"
          ]
        },
        "The Skiff and Star": {
          "description": "The great inn of the front, a broad timber house of many rooms facing the water, its common room low and warm and loud of an evening, its windows hung with the finest of the town's lamps. Long tables, a deep hearth, and a gallery above where rooms are let; the smell of supper and spilled cider, and a din of students and townsfolk and travelers that carries out onto the front. A lamp over the door burns a steady gold — one of the few that has never once misbehaved, and the innkeeper's great pride.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lamp Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Back Lanes": {
          "description": "The older, narrower part of town behind the market, where the lanes run crooked and close between leaning houses and the lamps hang fewer and dimmer. Pawnshops and dark little shopfronts keep odd hours here, their windows shuttered or crowded with unsorted oddments, and the taverns are smaller, quieter, and choosier about who comes up the stairs. It is not dangerous by day, only shabby; but a stranger asking the right questions in the right rooms can find a buyer or a seller for almost anything.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lamp Market"
          ]
        }
      },
