    "Wortwell": {
      "name": "Wortwell",
      "x": -37,
      "y": -20,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Wortwell is a brewing village in the green country south of the Tarn, a cheerful huddle of malt-houses, drying-sheds, and cellars about a broad green where the brewers' carts come and go. It brews the table-beer the Academy drinks and bottles the draught-trade's stock. At its heart is the old well the village is named for, which has not given plain water in living memory: what comes up the bucket is the village's making, the strange good brew that no one has been able to copy anywhere else, and that the Wortwell folk have long since stopped wondering at.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE MINOR BLEED. The mages of Tarnholme will tell anyone who asks that Wortwell sits on a small, mild bleed, and that the well draws up water touched by it — which is why the brewing here comes out as it does and cannot be matched elsewhere. The villagers do not much care for the explanation. The well has always brewed strange and good, the beer has always sold, and that is enough for them.\n\nWHEN IT MISBEHAVES. A bleed is a bleed, mild or not, and now and then Wortwell's runs odd. The well-water comes up wrong — too strong, or bitter, or apt to set a whole cellar's casks working at once and blow their bungs across the room; the brew takes strange turns, a batch that will not stop fermenting or makes the drinker's nose glow for an hour. Most of it the village takes in stride and laughs off. But when the well turns truly sour and will not come right, or the cellars go wild together, the brewers send word to the Academy\n\nTHE VILLAGE. The Wortwell folk are brewers to the bone — broad, unhurried, generous with a sample and stubborn about their craft — human and halfling families who have worked the same cellars and guarded the same recipes for generations. They are used to students coming out from the Academy to look at the well, and they feed them well and tell them very little they do not already know.",
      "areas": {
        "The Green": {
          "description": "The village common, a broad open green ringed by malt-houses and brewers' cottages, with the old well at its center under a mossy stone canopy and a worn track running in from the Tarn road. Carts stand about loaded with casks and sacks of grain, and the ground is trodden bare around the well-head where the buckets come up.",
          "paths": [
