    "Undergate Isle": {
      "name": "Undergate Isle",
      "x": -9,
      "y": 6,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Jade Tarn",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A small walled island a short row from the campus, set apart from the others and ringed by a low towered wall, with a single warded gatehouse and little else above ground. It is the quietest island of the Tarn and the least visited: there is nothing here to come for but the shaft, and the shaft is not open to just anyone. Beneath the gatehouse the warded stair goes down — the one way from the surface of Jade Tarn toward the Manawell itself, far below.",
      "hiddenInfo": "THE DOOR DOWN. All the region's mana wells up from the Manawell, a rift into magic-rich reality deep beneath Jade Tarn, opened in an age no one remembers by an art no one living understands. Undergate Isle is where the Academy holds the only known way down toward it. The school does not pretend to control the Well, and it does not let students wander toward it: descent is by a Master's writ, logged at the gatehouse, watched, and granted only to upper-Circle students with cause to go and the craft to survive it. The stair is the gate of the mage's deepest road, and the willingness of a master to write the leave is itself a kind of rank.\n\nHOW FAR THE WRIT RUNS. The Academy's hold reaches only a little way down — a few warded landings, kept and lit and patrolled. Below that the writ means nothing and the deep belongs to itself: the tunnel-and-cavern country of bare living stone the mages call the Manawell Deeps, where the magic does not shape the rock but shapes its inhabitants, and the deep world's magical creatures gather toward the Well as the region's gather toward the Tarn. The deep gnomes of Gloamdelph keep a foothold in the upper galleries and will guide the sighted for a hard bargain; below them the warrens and the wormfolk's country run down stratum by stratum toward the rift, and almost nothing that goes all the way down has come back to say what is there.\n\nTHE BOTTOM OF THE WELL. What the elder makers were, and why they opened the Well, the Academy does not know and does not teach. The wormfolk who tend the rift's edge hold it as holy ground and defer to something at the bottom of it — and the oldest spellforms, the strongest things the drained and welling magic has ever grown, are pooled deepest of all, down where the flow runs strongest. The mage who goes to the raw flow and takes it into himself does not master it; he becomes overdrawn, more current than vessel, less a person and more a thing of loose magic, until he is one more of the deep's own monsters. It is the cautionary end of the mage's road, and the reason the Academy holds the door.",
      "areas": {
        "The Gatehouse": {
          "description": "The warded stone gatehouse that is nearly the whole of the island above ground, a squat strong building astride the head of the shaft, its heavy door bound in iron and graven with binding-marks. A duty-master's post and a logging-desk stand within, and beyond them the mouth of the stair goes down into the dark. The walls of the small court without are low and towered, and the green Tarn laps close on every side.",
          "paths": [
            "The Warded Stair"
          ]
        },
        "The Warded Stair": {
          "description": "The head of the long descent, a broad spiral of worn stone steps winding down into the rock, lit by lamps set in the wall at each turn. The first landings are kept and clean and the air is still, but it grows colder and closer with each turn down, and the lamplight ends somewhere below, where the Academy's keeping ends and the dark of the deep begins.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gatehouse"
          ]
        }
      },
