    "The Bonecrags": {
      "name": "The Bonecrags",
      "x": 36,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Redrock",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A run of high red cliffs above the canyons, white-streaked and littered at their feet with broken bone. Dire vultures nest on the ledges — huge, patient, ugly birds that circle the badlands for anything traveling alone, carry it up in their talons, and dash it on the rocks below to crack it open. They are drawn to anything that shines, and an age of it has packed their cliff-nests with the bright leavings of the dead: coin, buckles, rings, blades, and gold stripped from everyone they ever took.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: the Bonecrags are hostile. The dire vultures are animals and there is no parley with them. Out over the open ground they hunt by snatch-and-drop: a vulture stoops on a target, seizes it in its talons, hauls it up, and lets it fall to the rocks to break it open. They pick the lone, the weak, and the already-wounded first, and leave a strong, close-packed group mostly alone — the danger out here is to stragglers and the hurt. At the nests it is different: the birds defend their hoard and their young without limit, stooping again and again, fouling a climber's footing and bowshots with their wings, and fighting to the death on the ledges.\n\nThe nests are the prize. Each is a midden of shattered bone and the bright metal the vultures magpie out of everything they kill — loose coin, gold and silver ornaments, buckles and rings, and the odd whole blade or piece of armor stripped off the dead and worth carrying out. A slain dire vulture yields a hooked beak and talons that make a fine piercing weapon, and broad stiff flight-feathers. The richest nests sit highest, on the worst ledges.",
      "areas": {
        "The Bone Ground": {
          "description": "The open rock at the base of the cliffs, pale with bird-lime and strewn with broken bone where the vultures drop what they catch. The ground is exposed on every side, with no cover and a clear view up the red rock face to the ledges and the dark clutter of nests far above.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Ledges"
          ]
        },
        "The Lower Ledges": {
          "description": "The first reachable shelves of the cliff, narrow and wind-scoured, the rock fouled white and crusted with old nest-litter. Bone and bright scraps lie wedged in the cracks. The face steepens sharply above, the larger nests visible higher up on ledges that thin to nothing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bone Ground",
            "The High Nests"
          ]
        },
        "The High Nests": {
          "description": "The top of the cliffs, where the biggest stick-and-bone nests sit on the highest and narrowest ledges, open to the sky and the long drop. The footing is bad and the wind is strong. From here the whole run of canyons spreads out below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Ledges"
          ]
        }
      },
