    "The Ruins of Naran-Kadish": {
      "name": "The Ruins of Naran-Kadish",
      "x": -29,
      "y": 3,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Redrock",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The broken city of the Flame Princes, sprawled across a wide red canyon floor — a once-great place of domed palaces and pillared courts, burned and fallen an age ago and half-swallowed by sand. Gnoll clans hold it now, each prince ruling a quarter of the ruin from a palace he has crowned himself in, draped in stolen finery and waited on hand and foot. The greatest of them styles himself the Sultan. Strangers can come and go and even trade here, for a price and a careful tongue, but the peace is thin: the gnolls are tyrants under their silks, and a guest who gives offense can become a prisoner.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Naran-Kadish is dangerous but not hostile on sight. The gnoll princes suffer outsiders who come to trade, pay their tolls, and show respect, and a visitor can walk the ruin, deal, and ask questions if he minds his manners. This holds only so long as he is useful and deferent. Give offense, cheat a prince, free a captive, or be caught somewhere forbidden, and the welcome ends at once — the gnolls turn on a guest in a breath, and the captured are killed or chained and sold. The princes are vain, greedy, and quick to take insult, and they scheme against one another constantly; a clever outsider can play one quarter against another.\n\nNaran-Kadish burned by its own hand. When the Demon-Princes of Ur came in the Fourth Age, the djinnborn nobles who ruled here burned their own city in a great working to drive them out. It worked, and the demons were thrown back, but the fire never fully died — it still smolders far below, and the ground stays warm and the demons still leak up from the deepest ruins. The gnolls neither know nor care what their city sits upon.\n\nThe princes set captives and the desperate to digging the ruins for buried treasure, and they sell what comes up to anyone who pays. Most is old gold, glass, and djinnborn relics. But the diggers also turn up shards of the old sealing-work and stranger things touched by what burned here, and a quiet sort of buyer comes for exactly those — robed strangers who pay well, ask for the marked pieces by description, and are let in where ordinary traders are not. The princes think them harmless cranks with deep purses.\n\nThe palaces hold a sultan's plundered wealth — gold, jewels, fine arms, and the relics dug from the ruin. Captives are held throughout for labor and sale. A prince's favor, his treasury, or his hoard of dug relics are all there for an outsider bold or cunning enough to take them.",
      "areas": {
        "The Shattered Gate": {
          "description": "The old ceremonial entrance to the city, a colossal broken arch of red stone leaning over the canyon road, carved with the worn figures of winged, crowned beings. Crude banners and gnoll trophies hang from it now. Beyond, the ruined avenues open out, and sentries watch the approach from perches in the broken stonework. The wide street runs in toward the heart of the ruin.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market Ruins",
            "The Palace Quarters"
          ]
        },
        "The Market Ruins": {
          "description": "A great fallen plaza where the gnolls trade, the toppled colonnades and dry fountains strung with awnings and stalls. Goods of every kind change hands here in the open, watched over by armed gnolls. Lanes thread off between the broken buildings, and a guarded way leads down toward the digging-grounds at the city's lowest edge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Gate",
            "The Palace Quarters",
            "The Diggings"
          ]
        },
        "The Palace Quarters": {
          "description": "The grand ruined palaces where the gnoll princes hold court, each a domed and pillared hall crowned with stolen splendor and guarded by its prince's own crew. Sand-choked gardens and dry pools lie between them, and the quarters of rival princes stand walled off from one another in wary distance. The Sultan's palace, the largest, rises at the center.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Gate",
            "The Market Ruins"
          ]
        },
        "The Diggings": {
          "description": "The lowest part of the ruin, where the city sank deepest into the canyon floor and the rock is broken open into pits and tunnels. The ground here is hot to the touch and threaded with smoke rising from far below. Diggers work the rubble under guard, and the oldest, most buried halls run down into the dark beyond where the digging has reached.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market Ruins"
          ]
        }
      },
