    "Sweetwater Oasis": {
      "name": "Sweetwater Oasis",
      "x": -14,
      "y": 44,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Dunelands",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A wide spring-fed oasis deep in the sand, ringed with date palms and flowering green, its water clear and cool and sweet. It is the most beautiful place for many days' travel and the most welcoming sight a parched traveler could ask for. Lamia hold it: cunning man-eaters with a woman's face and a serpent's body, who have made the oasis their hunting ground. Caravans know the name and the warning that rides with it, and still the  lamia devour their share of victims who wander in out of the desert.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The lamia attack intruders. They are cunning and patient and can speak, and a clever or determined visitor might draw one into talk or a bargain, but the pack defaults to the hunt and treats anyone who comes to the water as prey. They do not announce themselves; a lamia will pass for a woman at a distance and close the gap before striking.\n\nA family of lamia holds the oasis: man-eaters with a woman's upper body and a great serpent's coil below, fast over open ground and faster through the reeds and palms. They hunt by drawing prey in close, and the oasis is the draw, so they let it stay beautiful and let the desert deliver the thirsty. They keep to the water and the green and do not range far into the sand.\n\nThe pack keeps prey alive in the rocks behind the spring, chained, until they are wanted. The goods of everyone the oasis has killed lie among the rocks and around the pools where packs rested: coin, weapons, caravan-stock, and the fine things travelers carried. Loot is found in the rocks and at the water's edge. Most of it is ordinary plunder; the rare good piece turns up where a wealthy traveler fell.",
      "areas": {
        "The Waters": {
          "description": "A broad, clear spring at the heart of the oasis, cool and still, fed from below and rimmed with smooth stone and soft grass. Date palms and flowering green ring the water and throw deep shade over it. Beyond the green, the open sand runs flat to the horizon in every direction; this is the only water and the only shade for days.",
          "paths": [
            "The Palm Grove"
          ]
        },
        "The Palm Grove": {
          "description": "A dense ring of date palms, tall reeds, and flowering brush around the spring, thick enough to lose sight of the open water from within it. Narrow tracks wind between the trunks, and the ground is soft and green underfoot. The grove gives shade and cover, and a clear line of sight nowhere; a few steps in, the palms close around.",
          "paths": [
            "The Waters",
            "The Rocks"
          ]
        },
        "The Rocks": {
          "description": "A low stony hollow behind the spring, screened from the water by the green. Old iron chains are bolted into the rock and lie slack across the stone, and a deep cleft sinks into the ground at the back, dark and dry. Travelers' goods lie scattered among the stones, and worn tracks lead in from the grove.",
          "paths": [
            "The Palm Grove"
          ]
        }
      },
