    "Tomb of the Sun's Eye": {
      "name": "Tomb of the Sun's Eye",
      "x": 21,
      "y": -9,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Dunelands",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A pyramid-tomb of the Sun Kings, crowned with a great aperture open to the sky that the raiders call the Eye. Within, the halls are bright and full of mirrors and lensed brass, and the burial chambers run deep with the king's sun-gold and the bright-craft of his tomb. Metal guardians hunt the passages, and the light itself is turned against intruders. Raiders cannot wait for darkness to slip in; the tomb stays lit day and night. Few who try the Eye come back.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The guardians attack anyone who enters the tomb. They are constructs with no minds to reason with; they default to destroying intruders and do not stop, parley, or tire. The light is a standing hazard apart from them: the focused beams burn what stands in their path and the glare blinds.\n\nThe tomb was built to judge intruders with light. Mirror-bright flying constructs, brass drones shaped as scarabs and hawks, rest in the lower tomb and boil up into the lit halls when anyone enters, darting through the beams and turning the focused light to sear. They are fast and small and hard to strike, and they come in numbers. They hold thickest in the depths around the king's chamber.\n\nThe halls themselves are the weapon. The Eye at the peak takes sunlight by day, and a vast crystal at the tomb's heart holds the light and pours it back through the halls by night, so the tomb is never dark and intruders cannot wait out the light. Mirrors and lenses fed by the Eye and the crystal throw beams of focused light across the chambers, the same craft the empyreans set in their sky-weapons. The mirrors can be turned by a careful hand to bend a beam aside, onto a guardian, or onto a sealed way. Standing in a beam burns; the glare off the mirrors blinds at the wrong angle.\n\nThe loot is the king's sun-gold and the bright-craft of the tomb, richest in the deepest chambers. Sun-gold blade: radiant, bites undead, demons, and shadow-things hardest. Empyrean focusing-glass: a lens of the sun-craft, worked into a weapon or a tool. Lenses, mirrors, and sky-glass fittings throughout. The constructs are unmade by shock, and their brass eaten by acid.",
      "areas": {
        "The Aperture": {
          "description": "The pyramid's high entrance hall, lit from above through the great open Eye at the peak, a shaft of sky-light falling straight down the center. The walls are set with polished mirrors and lensed brass that catch the light and throw it inward, and the chamber stays bright whether the sky beyond is day or dark. Passages lead down and inward, each one lit, none of them dark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Mirror Halls": {
          "description": "A maze of chambers and corridors strung with angled mirrors and lenses, beams of focused light crossing the rooms at fixed lines and pooling bright on the carved walls. The mirrors stand on pivots and rings, some hanging, some set in the floor, and the light bends wherever they turn. There is glare in every direction and shadow almost nowhere; the lit lanes weave deeper into the tomb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Aperture",
            "The Crystal Vault"
          ]
        },
        "The Crystal Vault": {
          "description": "The burial chamber at the heart of the tomb, where a vast cut crystal stands above the king's sarcophagus, blazing with light gathered from the Eye and pouring it across the whole hall. The chamber is the brightest in the tomb, lit white and gold from the crystal day and night, and the richest of the grave-goods and sun-gold lie ringed around the dais in the glare.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Halls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [],
      "images": {
        "establishingShot": {
          "crop": {
