    "Tomb of the Bound Host": {
      "name": "Tomb of the Bound Host",
      "x": 32,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Dunelands",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A pyramid-tomb of the Sun Kings where the dead were made: the great embalming-works that wrapped a king's whole household to lie with him. Its halls are lined with rows of the bound dead, wrapping-vats, and jars by the thousand, and the dead here do not lie still. They come in numbers, more than any raider can cut down, drawn up out of the lower halls by anything that enters. The embalming-gold and the household's grave-goods lie deep among them, and the tomb is named for the bound host that fills it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: The dead here default to hostile and attack anything that enters. They do not parley, tire, or stop, and they come in numbers; the danger is being overwhelmed, not overpowered by any single thing. There is no master to find and no head to cut off, only the host.\n\nThe tomb embalmed and wrapped a Sun-King's entire household to be buried with him, and the work was never finished when the empire fell. The halls are full of the bound dead: lesser mummies in their hundreds, wrapped and half-wrapped, standing in rows and stacked in niches, that rouse and shamble toward intruders and keep coming. Scarab swarms nest in the corpses and the wrapping-jars and pour out in clouds that strip flesh. Among the host are the embalmer-dead, the wrapped priests who did the work, tougher and quicker than the rest. The host is thickest in the lower halls and presses up from them.\n\nThe loot is the embalming-gold and the household's grave-goods, scattered through the workrooms and the burial niches and richest in the lower halls. Sun-gold ornament: radiant, bites undead, demons, and shadow-things hardest, worked into the wrappings of a household lord. Embalmer's tools and canopic gold lie among the vats. The bound dead are dry and take fire badly; flame runs through the wrappings and the host.",
      "areas": {
        "The Outer Workrooms": {
          "description": "The tomb's upper halls, wide stone workrooms strung with the gear of embalming: stone slabs and drained vats, racks of tools, and shelves of sealed jars stacked floor to ceiling. Wrapped shapes lie on the slabs and lean in the wall-niches, still and bound. Passages run inward and down, and the rooms grow more crowded with the dead the deeper they go.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wrapping-Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Wrapping-Halls": {
          "description": "Long galleries lined floor to ceiling with the bound dead, wrapped and half-wrapped, standing in rows and packed into niches down the length of the walls. Broken jars and split wrappings litter the floor, and the air is thick with dust. The galleries run on into the dark, the rows unbroken, sloping down toward the lower tomb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Workrooms",
            "The Lower Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Lower Halls": {
          "description": "The deep burial halls at the bottom of the tomb, where the household was laid and the wrapping-work was densest. The dead are stacked thickest here, niche on niche and row on row, and the king's household goods lie heaped among them in gold and bound cloth. The halls open wide and low, and the rows run back further than the eye can follow into the dark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wrapping-Halls"
          ]
        }
      },
