    "Drover's Rock": {
      "name": "Drover's Rock",
      "x": 14,
      "y": 37,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Wild Grass Sea",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Drover's Rock was once a watering-stop on the south road, until the warlord Vaskarn took it for his seat. His band holds it now, takes what crosses the grass nearby, and calls it tribute. Travelers who cannot pay or fight go around.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Vaskarn's band, the Vaskarni, hold Drover's Rock and default to predation. A lone or weak traveler is robbed, taken, or killed; a strong or useful one may be sized up, taxed, or invited to join rather than fought. They are not a mindless lair, since Vaskarn is recruiting and a hard fighter is worth more to him standing than dead, but refusing him, crossing him, or mocking him to his face turns the whole Rock hostile at once.\n\nVaskarn is a lionkin warlord who styles himself the Pride of Prides and holds court here as if Drover's Rock were a true pride-hold and he a great pride-lord. He claims no crown, only what any pride-lord claims, he says: one pride set above all the others, and then above all the peoples of the grass. He gathers to himself the clanless, the exiled, and the repudiated of every species, who find in the Vaskarni the pride that cast them out. The tribes refuse him his title and call him the Straw King, fit only for the Burning Ground; he has killed for the name, and it has not stopped spreading.\n\nThe Vaskarni carry better steel than a road-band should, sold to them quietly by people who profit from the grass staying dangerous, and the tribute squeezed from the road piles up at Vaskarn's seat: coin, gear, and those good bought weapons.",
      "areas": {
        "The Roadgate": {
          "description": "The south road runs straight up to the foot of the rock, where a wall of stacked stone, wagon-wrecks, and sharpened stakes has been thrown across it, with a single gap wide enough for one cart. The old wagon-ground around the foot is a war-camp now, hide tents and picket-lines churned to mud and ringed with the burnt-out frames of wagons that did not pay. Trophies and skulls are lashed to poles along the wall-top, facing the road.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lion's Seat"
          ]
        },
        "The Lion's Seat": {
          "description": "The top of the rock holds the old waystation's hall, a long stone building hung now with banners, hide trophies, and the painted boards of caravan-families taken off the road. A high seat of horn and hide stands at the far end, raised on a dais of stacked paving-stones. Around it the rock falls away on all sides to the grass, with the road and the war-camp laid out far below.",
          "paths": [
            "The Roadgate"
          ]
        }
      },
