    "Brackenhill Village": {
      "name": "Brackenhill Village",
      "x": -30,
      "y": 24,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whitemoor",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Brackenhill is a working village out on the dales, peat-cutters and eel-fishers and herders who pay a tithe to the death-knights of the dales for protection. The reavers and the dead that plague the rest of the moor mostly leave it be, and to a traveler crossing Whitemoor it is a rare safe place to stop.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The tithe is paid in living people. Once a year the village draws lots at the moot, and the chosen are given over when the death-knights' riders come; in return the Deathless keep the reavers and the dead off the dale. It is no secret, and the village does not pretend otherwise. The bargain has held longer than the old folk can remember, and Brackenhill is safer for it than any walled town on the moor.\n\nBrackenhill is moorfolk and marshfolk: humans and halflings cutting peat and herding sheep on the dales, frogkin netting eels in the fen-bottoms below. They keep the old moor-customs and the moot, the moor-wives keep cures and charms, and they have no wall and no guard, having never needed either.\n\nThe bargain makes the village's life. An elder keeps it and draws the lots; some households have given up their own and others have not; there are always a few whose turn is feared to be coming, and now and then one who runs rather than be given over. Not everyone holds the cost worth paying, and they do not all agree on what else there is to do. The death-knights' riders are the only outsiders the village both fears and depends on.\n\nFor a traveler the village is a rare safe rest on the deep moor, and it trades peat, wool, eels, and the moor-wives' cures, and sells guidance across the fen to those who can pay. The folk speak plainly of what they pay and why, but they have no love for an outsider who would meddle with it, having a great deal more to lose by it than he does.",
      "areas": {
        "The Green": {
          "description": "An open green at the heart of the village, a market-cross and well at its middle and the long moot-hall standing along one side. Stone-and-turf houses ring the green, and lanes run out into the dales and down toward the fen.",
