    "Aldwall": {
      "name": "Aldwall",
      "x": -9,
      "y": 32,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Whitemoor",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Aldwall is the last walled town of Fethloren, a star-fort of pale dwarf-cut stone standing over the moor, with the king's town within the walls and the cliff-fortress of the Knights Vigilant above. The stonework is a wonder no one can match anymore, carved over every surface and cut through with deep lines the dwarves say are runes that even they can no longer read.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Aldwall shelters inside fort-craft the dwarves raised in Fethloren's great age and cannot reproduce now: the walls turn the dead, the carvings hold, and the knack of cutting them is lost. The town lives within the walls; the Knights Vigilant hold the cliff above and ride out against the moor's dead; the king holds his hall below. The king carries the older honor and the order the harder power, and both keep their pride about it.\n\nKnights come swaggering down the cliff-stair to drink and trade boasts over whose kill was greater. Old soldiers who have fought the dead all their lives speak of barrow-wights the way other men speak of bad weather. Dwarf carvers corner strangers to argue what the wall-runes mean and swear blind they once knew. The watch knows every face in town, and the moor-guides name their fee in drink before coin.\n\nUnder the carved walls the market trades the goods of a hard country: armorers and the Temple's overflow of blessed arms, silver and moonstone work, moor-cures and draughts from the marsh-wives, horses and moor-ponies, salted provisions, and guides who will take a stranger across the fen for the right price. A fence works the back lanes for those who know to ask.\n\nThe moor's troubles are common talk in the market and the tavern, and they point outward. The drowned cities of Bryngard and Avelmere lie out in the fen, full of the dead and their grave-gold. Reavers raid from a bog stronghold called Carrowgate. The barrow-fields are worked by grave-robbers and guarded by wights. Below the talk runs the real fear: the death-knights of the Deathless, gentry of Fethloren's own dales who are said to have turned to necromancy, take the living from villages like Brackenhill and answer to no name a man can prove. And the Syndicate moves stolen radiant goods through the town, having bought a gate-captain, a quartermaster, and a chaplain's silence.\n\nAldwall is human-led and old-chivalric, with dwarves at the forges and the carvings, halflings and frogkin in from the heath and fen, and the odd beastfolk or giantborn up from the grass sea. It is a refuge and a place to belong: the Vigilant take recruits, the market gives work, and the Stonesaga is where strangers become regulars.",
