    "The Stone Shallows": {
      "name": "The Stone Shallows",
      "x": -16,
      "y": -7,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Great Lagoon",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "The Stone Shallows are the old city that sank into the lagoon ages ago, lying half-drowned beside Caranthoria, which the Solarians built on the dry ground to the east. The streets are mostly walkable — the pavement holds at the height of the upper storeys, while the lower city lies under water and the buildings stand sunk to their windows on either side. Broken colonnades, flooded squares, and the stumps of old statues mark out the old districts. The Solarians work it for salvage and pay well: old imperial gold, regalia, and cut stone come out of it by the cartload, and their legion hires outsiders for the flooded structures its own soldiers won't enter. The water is open to the fens, and what comes in from the swamp dens in the drowned buildings, so the salvage crews go armed, and rarely work the flooded buildings alone.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The salvage crews are drawn from every people the lagoon holds: humans, lizardfolk, frogkin, ratfolk, turtlefolk, and outsiders who came for the work. The legion runs the operation from winch-stations along the Boundary Wall and holds the dry edge, but leaves the flooded streets to the crews and their flat-boats.\n\nIMPORTANT: giant eels den in the flooded lower city and the sunken side-streets and default hostile to anything in the water. They lie still in submerged doorways and below the surface of the squares, strike without warning, and drag prey under; they do not follow onto dry stone. The open water is theirs.\n\nThe Children of the Wyrm raid the salvage. Lizardfolk war-parties come up from the fens to take hauls, kill crews, and cut the winch-lines for the swamp kingdom that sends them, sometimes driving a mire drake ahead of them, and they do not parley with crews working for the empire. Rival scavenger crews are the nearer danger — armed, set on a building they have claimed, and ready to settle a disputed haul with steel where the legion cannot see.\n\nThe old city's wealth went down with it. Cut stone, bronze fittings, and drowned household goods lie scattered through the upper storeys and side-streets, but the prize is under the Forum of the Sun, where the imperial treasure-vaults stand flooded with their doors still on them — old coin, regalia, and worked goods behind them. The deeper a building has sunk, the more is left in it and the harder it is to reach.",
      "areas": {
        "Dryfoot": {
          "description": "The strip of dry ground where the city's pavement gives out at the foot of the Boundary Wall. Winch-frames and crane-arms stand along the water's edge over the first of the sunken streets, with recovered stone and timber stacked between them and lean-to shelters built up against the wall. Plank causeways and tethered flat-boats run out from here into the ruin, where the pavement drops below the water and the drowned buildings begin.",
          "paths": [
            "The Colonnade Walk"
          ]
        },
        "The Colonnade Walk": {
          "description": "The old city's great avenue, and the one road through the ruin that still stands above the water. A raised stone causeway runs its length between two rows of standing columns, the pavement cracked and tilted where it has settled. Sunken shopfronts and house-doors sit at the waterline on either side, and flooded side-streets open off the avenue between the buildings, running back into open water.",
          "paths": [
            "Dryfoot",
            "The Forum of the Sun"
          ]
        },
        "The Forum of the Sun": {
          "description": "The old imperial heart, a wide flooded square with the broken Sun-Temple standing at its head. The square lies wadeable, the water waist-high over cracked marble, and the toppled statues of old emperors stand out of it. The temple's outer hall holds dry above its columns; at the back, a stair runs down into the flooded sanctum.",
          "paths": [
            "The Colonnade Walk"
          ]
        }
