    "East Edge Ruins": {
      "name": "East Edge Ruins",
      "x": 17,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Great Lagoon",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Ruins on the dry eastern edge of the lagoon — old quarters of the ancient city that stood here before the Solarians raised their new capital. Solarian deserters often hole up here in numbers, living off the road and the salvage — robbing travelers, raiding the work-crews, and selling what they take.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Every sworn legionnaire is branded at the neck. Inside the empire it is an honor; outside it is a verdict, and a sun-branded neck is known in every inn in Larion. A deserter cannot lose himself in an ordinary town, so the deserters gather here, in the one place a scarred neck draws no second look, and there are far more of them than the legion likes to admit. They are humans, lizardfolk, frogkin, and every other people the legion takes, run from the line for every reason men run.\n\nIMPORTANT: the deserters default hostile to anyone who comes into the ruins. They are criminals by trade now, they assume a stranger is either the legion come for them or prey come to be robbed, and they will rob or kill on that assumption. The one thing that buys a hearing instead of a knife is being plainly no friend of the empire and carrying something they want — coin, a fence, news, or a job worth more than what is in your pockets. Even then it is a wary hearing among armed and frightened men, not a welcome.\n\nThere is no fixed camp to storm. When a legion cohort comes in force the deserters melt back into the Hollow Quarter and the vaults under it, leaving the approaches trapped behind them, and trickle back when the soldiers give up. When they are dug in, the Throat is manned and overlooked; when they are scattered, it is only the traps and tripwires left behind. A band this size has leaders — the hardest and longest-surviving men hold the rest together — and they run the trade in stolen goods, ransom prisoners worth ransoming, and sell what the legion would confiscate to buyers who come quietly by the eastern road.\n\nThe cellars of the Hollow Quarter are not held by the deserters alone. Things have come up into the flooded cellars and the dark under the broken foundations and den there, and the deserters give the worst of it a wide berth.",
      "areas": {
        "The Broken Streets": {
          "description": "The edge of the old district where it meets the dry shore: collapsed tenements and roofless halls along streets choked with fallen stone, the lagoon on one side and the gutted city running inland on the other. Sightlines break at every rubble-pile and standing wall.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throat"
          ]
        },
        "The Throat": {
          "description": "The one sound way deeper into the district, where the fallen blocks pinch the route down to a single overlooked passage between standing walls and high broken floors. Murder-holes and firing-points look down on it from above, and the narrows are strung and rigged where the way bends.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broken Streets",
            "The Hollow Quarter"
          ]
        },
        "The Hollow Quarter": {
          "description": "Where the district collapsed worst, blocks fallen into their own cellars and the streets dropping away into rubble-choked vaults below. The footing is bad and the ways twist underground between the broken foundations, on and on into the dark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throat"
          ]
        }
