      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Hookmarsh is a backwater of reed-channels and mud islands where a tributary feeds into the Saranadi. A lizardfolk warband holds it under a warlord named Ghessek, and they make their living off the river: they block the main channel and force passing boats to detour through the marsh, then take them in the reedways and ransom back the people aboard. The channels are staked and snared, and Ghessek's men watch the water from blinds in the reeds. An old stone fort on the marsh's one dry island is where the warband keeps its captives and its plunder.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Hookmarsh runs on ransom. Ghessek's warband blocks the Saranadi with sunk timber and chain, so boats must turn off into the marsh, where the channels are staked, snared, and watched from reed blinds. Boats that detour are taken in the reedways and their people held for ransom.\n\nGhessek honors ransoms that are paid. This is known on the river, and it is why the trade works: pay, and your people come back. A captive can also buy their own freedom by handing over everything they carry.\n\nThe still water hides giant leeches that fasten on in the shallows. Giant centipedes nest in the reed-mats and the rotten timber of the fort.\n\nIMPORTANT: the warband defaults hostile to anyone in the marsh. The single exception is ransom business: money on the table or a captive to redeem buys a conversation. They do not parley over anything else.\n\nLoot is the warband's plunder, held in the fort: coin, ransom-chests, and cargo stripped from taken boats. Captives worth redeeming are held in the dry cells. Leech glands yield a blood-thinning draught; centipede venom is alchemical poison stock.",
