    "Falselight Reefs": {
      "name": "Falselight Reefs",
      "x": -7,
      "y": -40,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Swordlaw Isles",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Falselight Reefs are a low island walled in black rock and broken water, the worst wrecking-ground in the Swordlaw sea. Wreckers live here. On bad nights they move the shore-fires to false places, and ships steering by them come apart on the reef; on any night, the rocks and the storms do the work for free. The wreckers strip what washes up and kill what walks out of the surf, and they have grown rich on it. Every sailor knows to give Falselight a wide berth, and every sailor blown there anyway learns why.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The wreckers are a clan of perhaps forty hard coast-folk who live by the reef and nothing else. They show false fires on storm-nights to draw ships onto the rocks, then put out in skiffs to gaff survivors and strip the wrecks before the tide takes them. They are humans and a few frogkin, raised to it from birth, and they do not see themselves as monsters — the sea sends them ships, and they take what the sea sends. Outsiders are either prey or a problem, never guests.\n\nThe reason the false light never fails sits at the island's far point, in the old lighthouse the clan calls the Lure. An honest tower once, until the wreckers and the thing that came to live in it struck their bargain. A coven of three sea-hags holds the tower now. The hags raise the false light, foul the water, and sing the fog down off the headland; the wreckers strip the wrecks and leave the hags the drowned. Both sides feed. The clan knows exactly what lives in the Lure and would no sooner climb to the lamp-room than walk into the sea, and they will warn an outsider off it not out of mercy but because a meddler who stirs the coven ruins the catch for everyone.\n\nIMPORTANT: This is a hostile location. The wreckers attack outsiders who come ashore — not in endless waves, but hard and in numbers, with gaffs, fish-spears, and cutlasses, fighting on ground they know in water they don't fear. They will parley only with someone who plainly has the upper hand or something they want more than a fresh wreck, and even then they bargain like people who eat what washes up. The sea-hags in the Lure are hostile without exception and beyond reason; they do not negotiate, they lure and they drown. A grown sea-hag is far past a single fighter — three together are a site-boss.\n\nThe wreckers fight as a pack and a home crew: they let a stranger come in off the shingle, then close from the rocks and the huts on three sides, drive him toward the water, and gaff him in the surf where his footing fails and theirs holds. Wounded or outmatched, they break for the Holt and the skiffs rather than die for pride, and a clan-head who falls is mourned by nobody scrambling for his share. The hags fight from the tower: glamour and false voices to split a party and walk men off the gallery, fouled water and grasping weed at the tower's foot, claw and drowning-grip up close. Losing, a hag flees into the deep water under the Lure and is gone.\n\nLoot is the harvest of a hundred lost ships. The Holt's salvage-shed holds stripped cargo — bolts of ruined silk, casks, ship-fittings, a captain's strongbox no one's forced yet, charts worth more than the gold — and the wreckers wear and carry the good steel and oddments off the dead. The Lure's lamp-room and the rock beneath it hold what the hags keep: drowned-men's rings and teeth, a sea-hag's brine-charm that turns a calm sea foul, and the great lamp itself, an Empyrean bridge-lantern the wreckers hauled up from a sunken span, which throws a light that carries for miles and can be made to lie.",
      "areas": {
        "The Cove": {
          "description": "The one break in the island's black reef-wall, a steep shingle cove of grey stone and tide-wrack. The bones of broken ships lie everywhere the water reaches — split hulls, snapped masts, rigging fouled in the rocks — and more of it grinds in the surf with every swell. Black headlands climb on both sides, and a narrow path of cut steps and driftwood ladders works up the southern point toward the huts above. The far point of the island, where a stone tower stands over the worst of the reef, is visible across the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wrackers' Holt"
          ]
        },
        "The Wrackers' Holt": {
          "description": "A clutch of low stone huts roofed and walled with ship-timber, dug into the lee of the southern headland above the cove. Drying-racks, skiffs hauled up on rollers, and stacks of salvaged spars stand between the houses. The largest building is a long salvage-shed, its doors barred, packed with stripped cargo. Paths run down to the cove behind and out along the shore toward the tower at the island's far point.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cove",
