    "Garrock's Island": {
      "name": "Garrock's Island",
      "x": -23,
      "y": -25,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Swordlaw Isles",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Garrock's Island is a lush green peak alone in the Swordlaw sea, where the pirate captain Garrock is said to have buried a fortune before he died. The island is cursed now, and the dead walk it. None of that has ever stopped the crews who land in every season to try their luck.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Garrock was a pirate captain who took a fortune and meant to keep it. He put his crew to cutting a hole in the mountain to hide it, then killed every man who dug, so that none could lead another back to it. He died before he could return, and the gold is still in the rock where he left it.\n\nThe island is cursed, and the curse feeds on greed. Anyone who dies here with greed in their heart rises and walks — which is why the murdered crew were the first of the dead, and why every grasping crew that has landed since and not left has swelled their number. The dead are thickest at the diggings, where the most men have died clawing at the rock, and thinnest at the shore.\n\nThe danger from the living is rival treasure-crews — there is usually one or another somewhere on the slope, digging, lost, or already turning on each other, and they meet a newcomer as competition: warily, ready to deal, rob, or fight depending on who looks stronger and who got there first. Past their cold camps and hauled-up boats, the island is overgrown and trackless, and giant reptiles den in the green: constrictors thick as a mast in the gulleys, monitor lizards big as ponies, and snapping things in the standing water.\n\nIMPORTANT: The dead of Garrock's Island come in kinds, by where men died: drowned dead in the surf and the wet lower ground, waterlogged and slow, rising out of the tide-line and the bog; gallowsmen in the Greenwall, the mutineers and thieves Garrock hanged, dropping from the branches with the rope still on; bonecrews in the diggings, the old picked-clean pirate dead, fast and many, fighting with rusted cutlass and boarding-axe in the way they fought living; gravechewers among them, the greedy dead who never stopped digging, clawing at the rock and at the living, drawn to gold above all.\n\nLoot is what a hundred buried and drowned crews carried: coin and plate in the wet ground and the workings, good steel and pistols on the bonecrews, rings and grave-gold on the gravechewers, and the charts and oddments in the cold camps of crews that did not leave. Garrock's own hoard sits somewhere in the deepest workings — gold, gems, and the famous pieces a great captain dies owning. The giant reptiles give hides, monitor venom, and constrictor skin worth carrying out.",
      "areas": {
        "The Beach": {
          "description": "A single reef-fringed strand of pale sand below the island's green slope, the one easy landing on a coast otherwise walled in rock and surf. The mountain rises straight up from the treeline behind it, and high on its flank the bare scarred rock of the diggings shows through the green, dark with openings. Rotting boats lie hauled up past the tide-line, a fire-pit long gone cold sits in the dune-grass.",
          "paths": [
            "The Greenwall"
          ]
        },
        "The Greenwall": {
          "description": "The overgrown slope between the shore and the peak, dense drowned jungle over old ground. Cut trails run up it half-swallowed by growth, branching and doubling and dead-ending in gulleys, standing pools, and curtains of vine. Stretches of bog and slow green water lie in the low places, and the canopy closes overhead until the light goes dim and the way is easy to lose.",
          "paths": [
            "The Beach",
            "The Diggings"
          ]
        },
        "The Diggings": {
          "description": "The mountain's height, a slope of broken rock where generations of crews have hacked the old cave-mouths open and torn the ground apart chasing the hoard. Dark workings run back into the peak — cut tunnels, half-collapsed shafts, and chambers Garrock's crew carved and the diggers since have widened — opening one into another in no order anyone planned. Abandoned tools, and shored timber lie everywhere the digging reached. The deepest cuts run far enough into the rock that daylight does not follow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Greenwall"
          ]
        }
      },
