      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Swordlaw Isles",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Freehaven is the pirate capital of the Swordlaw sea, a city built across a scatter of rocky islets joined by bridges and skiffs, off the bridge-network and reachable only by sail. No crown rules it and no flag flies over it — it is the freest port in the world, run by the crews who harbor here and held together by one law, the Swordlaw: settle your quarrel with steel, and the winner's word stands. Pirates come to fence their hauls, refit their ships, find a crew or a captain, and spend their plunder loud. Anyone can make a new life here, and a careless one can end it just as fast.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Freehaven has no governor, no watch, and no court; the crews hold their own ground and their own ships, and the captains everyone has heard of are the nearest thing to power. Duels and other challenges are frequent; a challenge given and accepted binds both parties to abide the outcome, and the winner's word stands after.\n\nReputation matters greatly here. Nobody asks where a newcomer came from or what he was before — Freehaven is where a man starts over — but everybody watches what he does here, and he rises or falls by it. A stranger who carries himself well, keeps business flowing, and does not flinch is left to his business and soon known; one who reads as easy money is tested, and the sea is close on every side.\n\nCrews are found and built in the taverns, above all at the Above Board, where captains hold court and recruit and the day's work is spoken: a berth on a raid, a fence who needs a thing recovered, a treasure-rumor for sale, a run no honest crew will make.\n\nThe population is every people the salt sea throws together: humans and halflings throughout, parrotfolk carrying messages dock to dock, dwarf and giantborn shipwrights at the yards, and dark orcs, half-sea-elves, demonborn, and birdfolk crewing alongside everyone else.\n\nGoods and loot move through the Careenage: the cargo and fittings stripped from captured ships, weapons and gear off the dead, charts, and the odd plundered relic, all sold cheap and fast by dealers who do not ask where it came from. A crew flush off a prize will spend the whole share here in a week and sail out for more.",
