      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Open water where strong currents meet, so thick with fish that the surface boils — and the sea serpents follow the fish. Serpents run these waters in numbers found nowhere else, and hunting them is how the bay's crews earn their shares. Hunting boats work the runs in every season. So does everything that eats.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Three kinds of serpent are most common in these waters, each hunted differently. Reef serpents are man-length pack-hunters that swarm over the shallow banks — they coil and constrict swimmers and tip small boats by weight of numbers. Silverback serpents are the staple catch: boat-length, silver-crested, and fast, they breach clear of the water and crash down on hulls to break them. Bull serpents are old silverbacks grown huge, scarred with the broken harpoons of every crew that failed — a bull kill makes a hunter's name for life. Serpents are cunning, aggressive when hungry or struck, but they can be baited, driven, tired out, and outsmarted, and a fed serpent ignores a quiet boat.\n\nHunting crews are on the water in all weather: harpooners standing in the bows, boats running lines to a struck serpent, a mauled crew bailing and limping for home. Crews hail each other on sight, and a boat in trouble gets help — that is a courtesy of the bay, and everyone keeps it.\n\nLoot: serpent scale sells by the sack as light armor stock. Serpent oil, rendered from the fat, is lamp-oil and an alchemy base. Reef serpent venom sacs sell whole to alchemists. A bull's harpoon-scarred hide is armor-grade and worth a share on its own. Serpents taken alive and unmarked fetch triple from the live-trade — and live capture is three times the danger.",
