      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A great river meets the bay here in a mangrove maze miles wide — stilt-rooted trees standing in the tide, threaded with channels that shift with every flood. The brackish water breeds prey found nowhere else in the bay, and hunting crews pole flat skiffs into the tangle after hides, glands, and ink that alchemists and armorers pay dearly for. The deeper you go, the richer the hunting — and the more that hunts you back.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The estuary's prey, each hunted its own way. Snapdragons are crocodile-sized drakes that lie in the channels like drifting logs, spit a gob of caustic spit, then lunge — their acid-glands and drake-hide both sell high. Great morays, thick as a man, den in the stilt-root hollows and strike from their holes; hunters bait the den-mouths. Eelskin is supple armor-grade leather, and the jaw-glands go to alchemists whole. Bog krakens are barrel-bodied lurkers that anchor themselves among the roots of the deep pools and take waders and skiffs from below — the estuary's biggest prize and worst way to die. Kraken ink and arm-hide fetch a season's shares.\n\nIMPORTANT: Grindylows stalk the deep channels in packs and are hostile by default — they do not parley. Goblin-sized, weed-haired, long-fingered water-lurkers, they let hunting crews do the dangerous work, then swarm a loaded skiff at dusk to rob the catch, cut throats, and drag the dead under. The richer a skiff rides in the water, the more eyes follow it from the weeds. They fear fire on the water and break off from a burning brand, but they come back.\n\nHunting crews work the margins and channels by day and are gone by dark. A skiff poling out heavy is at its most dangerous hour on the way home.",
