    "Drakerider Isle": {
      "name": "Drakerider Isle",
      "x": -3,
      "y": 29,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Bay of Serpents",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A small island with a dormant volcanic peak, home of the bay's sea-drakes and the families who ride them. The isle's black-sand beaches are warm from the rock beneath, and the drake herds haul out on them to bask and breed as they have for generations. Saddled sea-drakes carry riders across open water faster than any sail, and people come from across the bay to earn one — the drakes cannot simply be bought.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Sea-drakes are flippered, long-bodied, and fish-eating — placid toward people, which is why a village of families lives on a beach full of them. They swim at the surface and plane when they sprint, and a rider grips the finned crest. The danger in them is mass, not malice: a drake that does not want the saddle throws its rider, rolls, and drags them through the surf. Wild-born pods run the isle's home shallows, claimed by family line and known by name; each season's young are driven into the tide-pens and gentled.\n\nA drake bonds to the person who breaks it — this is nature, not custom. A drake broken by someone else obeys grudgingly and swims home given the chance. So the isle sells the breaking, not the beast: anyone who wants a drake works the season in the pens, is there for the gentling, and lets the drake take to their hands. The breaker-families judge who is worth teaching, and they are not quick about it.\n\nRustling and drake-poaching are the isle's unforgivable crimes. A stolen drake is a sullen drake, but there are buyers who do not care, and foals taken before bonding can be broken elsewhere. The families keep watch on their herds and pay well for word of poachers.\n\nThe Drake Crown is the isle's great annual race — riders and their drakes around the whole island through open water and surf. Winning it makes a rider's name in every port in the bay.\n\nTrade: drake-tack and saddlery found nowhere else, the breaking-season itself, and stud and brood arrangements between the family lines.",
      "areas": {
        "Warmsands Beach": {
          "description": "Black sand, warm underfoot from the rock below. Drake herds haul out along the whole strand, foals among them in the shallows, and riders run the surf beyond the breakers.",
