    "The Stiltwood": {
      "name": "The Stiltwood",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -20,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Reedmoot Fens",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A drowned mangrove forest on the fen coast, where the trees stand on stilted roots taller than a man and the ground is water, mud, and root-tangle for miles. It is heronfolk country — tall wading birdfolk who live high in the mangroves and want no visitors. Trespassers are shot from the canopy by archers they never see, and the fen-strip folk give the place a wide berth.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The heronfolk are tall, lean birdfolk with long legs, grey-white plumage, and spear-straight bearing. They live in roost-villages high in the mangrove crowns, fish the channels, and hunt with longbows from the canopy. They are not raiders — they never leave the Stiltwood to attack anyone — but they kill trespassers who push into their country, and they are very good at it.\n\nIMPORTANT: heronfolk default to hostile toward anyone who enters the Tangle, but their hostility follows a strict custom. The first arrow is a warning shot, placed close and deliberate. An intruder who stops, lays down arms, and turns back is allowed to leave. One who keeps coming is shot to kill, from cover, from above, by archers who reposition after every volley and never close to melee while they hold the high ground.\n\nThe boundary stakes at the Root Margin are a trading place. Goods left at the stakes — steel arrowheads, salt, good rope, cloth — are taken overnight and paid for in kind: smoked fish, feather-fletched arrows, pearls, and salvage the heronfolk pull from the drowned wood. Fair traders are remembered by face. A proven friend — one who has traded fairly, kept the custom, or done the roosts a service — is invited up: a climbing-rope lowered from the canopy, a place at the cook-fires, and heronfolk guides who know every channel in the Fens. The heronfolk trade in what they know as much as what they make; nothing moves in the wetland that the roost-sentries have not seen.\n\nHeronfolk fight from height and distance. Opening volley from three directions at once, then silence and repositioning. Wounded intruders are left to retreat; dead ones are stripped and their gear hung on the boundary stakes as warning. If the roosts themselves are attacked, every adult fights, and the staked water below the platforms does the rest.\n\nLoot: heronfolk longbows are prized — man-tall bows of laminated mangrove that outrange anything made on the strip. Their feather-fletched arrows fly true, and roost-pearls pulled from the drowned beds sell well anywhere.",
      "areas": {
        "The Root Margin": {
          "description": "The outer edge of the mangroves, where the open fen meets the first stilt-roots and the going is still passable by punt or wading. Feathered stakes stand in a ragged line through the shallows, hung with bones, shell, and weathered gear. Beyond them the trees thicken and the light goes green.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tangle"
          ]
        },
        "The Tangle": {
          "description": "The maze itself. Root-arch over root-arch, channels that fork and dead-end, mud that swallows boots to the knee. No sightline runs longer than a spear-throw, and the canopy is closed overhead. Old arrow-shafts stand in the snags, and small platforms and rope-ways show high in the trees, always empty when looked at.",
          "paths": [
            "The Root Margin",
            "The Roosts"
          ]
        },
        "The Roosts": {
          "description": "A village of woven platforms, walkways, and roundhouses built high in the crowns of the biggest mangroves, joined by rope-bridges and reached only by climbing-ropes lowered from above. Cook-smoke drifts through the canopy, and drying-racks of fish and hides hang between the houses. The water below the platforms is staked.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tangle"
          ]
        }
