      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "An old imperial road running west into the deep Fens — broken, half-sunk, and still the only firm footing through miles of bog, mire, and quicksand. Its stone bed stands a stride above the water in places and vanishes beneath it in others. Everyone who moves through the Fens moves on the Drowned Road, and not everyone on it wants to be seen.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The road belongs to the dragon-kingdom of the Fens — the lizardfolk realm that closed the wetland to outsiders a generation ago — but the kingdom does not garrison it. Lizardfolk war-parties travel it moving east and west, some with mire drakes on the leash, and they attack outsiders on sight. Between their passages the road carries quieter traffic: smugglers moving goods west, salvagers and egg-hunters slipping toward the drowned ruins, and captives being marched west under guard to be given to the dragon's brood.\n\nIMPORTANT: lizardfolk war-parties on the road are hostile by default and do not parley. They take the strong captive and kill the rest; captives are bound and marched west. They do not pursue far off the road — the bog is their ally, and they let it do the killing. Anyone hiding off the roadbed in the mire risks quicksand, snakes, and giant leeches instead.\n\nWar-parties, captive columns, and smuggler crews are traffic, not fixtures — the road is empty as often as not, and what a traveler meets on it is a matter of timing and luck. A captive column is guarded but slow; its guards will not abandon their charges to chase anyone.\n\nGiant crocodiles hold the sunken stretches where the road dips underwater, and constrictor snakes hunt the reed-choked verges. In the far west, where the road runs between drowned imperial ruins, mire drakes den in the broken stonework.\n\nLoot: travelers who died on the road lie where the bog took them, with their packs, coin, and steel. The drowned ruins along the western stretch hold old imperial stonework, gilded fragments, and sealed cellars the water never reached.",
      "areas": {
        "The High Causeway": {
          "description": "The eastern stretch, where the road runs best: a raised stone bed a stride above the water, wide enough for a cart, its paving heaved and cracked but sound. Reed beds stretch flat to the horizon on both sides. Old wheel-ruts, cold campfires, and bootprints in the mud at the road's edge mark recent passage.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sunken Miles"
          ]
        },
        "The Sunken Miles": {
          "description": "The middle stretch, where the road fails. The stone bed dips beneath dark water for spans at a time, forcing travelers to wade the flooded roadbed or detour through mire and quicksand on rotten plank-ways laid by those who came before. Drag-marks scar the mud banks, and the reeds stand torn where something large has passed.",
          "paths": [
            "The High Causeway",
            "The Ruined Miles"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruined Miles": {
          "description": "The western stretch, where the road runs between the remains of drowned imperial buildings — columns leaning out of the water, roofless walls, sunken courtyards green with weed. The paving here is scattered and the way weaves between the ruins. Broken stonework rises on every side, and the water between the walls is still and deep.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sunken Miles"
          ]
        }
      },
