    "Port Lucre": {
      "name": "Port Lucre",
      "x": -17,
      "y": 41,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Reedmoot Fens",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A ramshackle port on the fen's north coast — docks, warehouses, taverns, and shanties piled inside a timber stockade, the only place on the closed fen coast where a ship can land and a stranger can walk around. It is a smugglers' town, run by no one and owned by the Syndicate, where anything can be bought, sold, or hired and nobody asks where it came from. Rough, loud, and lawless — but the walls hold, and trade is trade.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Port Lucre exists by an open arrangement: the Syndicate — the criminal brotherhood whose port this is — lands steel, tools, and forge-goods, ships out salvage and plunder, and keeps its own stockade against the fen. The lizardfolk dragon-kingdom that rules the Fens tolerates the port by its dragon's word and never comes inside the walls. The trade goes on; nobody pretends it's anything but what it is.\n\nThere is no law in Port Lucre, only the Syndicate's one rule: nothing touches the take. Syndicate bravos — armed, tattooed, unhurried — guard the warehouses, the docks, and the Roost, and they do not police anything else. Brawls, card-cheats, alley robbery, and shanghaiing the dead-drunk are entertainment; a fight that damages cargo or a hand raised against Syndicate business ends with the offender face-down in the harbor by morning. Strangers are welcome exactly as long as they are paying or useful.\n\nThe town is every flag and species the sea washes in: human smugglers, halfling fences, frogkin dockhands, giantborn stevedores, moon elf deserters, and crews that don't name their home port. Everything is for sale in the Glut: salvaged imperial arms and sun-gold, fire-stores at gouge prices, weapons, kit, passage out, fen-guides, and information priced by the question. The Roost hires: salvage crews for the burned fleet's wrecks, guards for smuggling runs west, and hard people for jobs nobody explains twice. The Syndicate's ladder here has one rung — prove useful and get paid, or become cargo — but a name that proves useful more than once starts getting the better jobs and the quiet nod at the door.\n\nPort Lucre robs the careless as cheerfully as it serves the paying. Purses are cut on the Landing, drinks are drugged in the shanty-taverns, and a rich-looking stranger who flashes gold walks out of the Glut with an escort he didn't hire. The wise deal sharp, drink light, and sleep behind a bolted door.",
      "areas": {
        "The Landing": {
          "description": "The crowded harborfront: three long timber docks, ships working cargo at all hours, and a waterfront strip of taverns, flophouses, and shanties leaning against each other. Cranes swing bales and crates over the heads of crews, hawkers, gamblers, and drunks. Gulls fight over the fish-gut barrels, and the stockade's spiked timber wall closes off the landward end.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glut",
            "The Roost"
          ]
        },
        "The Glut": {
          "description": "The market — a roofed sprawl of stalls, auction blocks, and locked strongbox-booths packed between the warehouses, named by its own traders and proud of it. Salvage lots go under the hammer, fences weigh goods on doctored scales, outfitters rack weapons and fen-gear, and chalkboards post charters, bounties, and passage for sale. Everything has a price and half the prices are lies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Landing",
            "The Roost"
          ]
        },
        "The Roost": {
          "description": "The Syndicate's house: a fortified stone warehouse at the town's heart, iron-shuttered, with a tavern floor at ground level and business above. Bravos flank the doors, the good liquor is behind the bar, and a balcony overlooks the floor where charters are signed and pay is counted. The vault below is the best-guarded room on the fen coast.",
          "paths": [
            "The Landing",
            "The Glut"
          ]
        }
      },
