      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A palisaded village on the eastern edge of the Fens that refuses to submit to the lizardfolk clans and their dragon ruler. It stands behind a timber wall, governed by an honest moot and beholden to nobody.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Reedmoot is fenfolk humans, frogkin, and halfling eel-netter families in roughly equal measure — young, loud, and stubborn. They fish, net eels, cut reed, and salvage. Lizardfolk war-parties probe the strip and people are taken, carried west to be sacrificed to the kingdom's brood; the moot mounts rescues, and rescue parties never want for volunteers.\n\nThe moot governs through speakers, not chiefs. Belonging runs in visible rungs: strangers eat at the guest-planks; taking wall-watch gets a name chalked on the roster and a greeting by name after; the militia oath is sworn with a hand on the raid-bell; the moot's full trust means a named peg in the Moot Hall and a voice at moot. Villagers treat each rung as real and defer accordingly.\n\nThe fire-stores — clay vessels of salvaged alchemical fire, locked in the stone store-house — are doled out by the moot for the wall's defense and never sold. Offering to buy them causes offense.\n\nBehavior: halfling netting families feud over water-rights and race eels on feast days; everyone mocks the dragon out loud because everyone fears her. Frogkin watermen hire out as fen-guides. Work on offer: wall-watch, salvage crews, guide-work, reed-cutter escort, and rescues. When the raid-bell rings, everyone armed goes to the wall, and a stranger who goes with them is family after.\n\nIMPORTANT: Reedmoot is safe ground and welcoming to outsiders. The wall and militia exist for what comes out of the fen, not for guests.",
