    "Manywrecks": {
      "name": "Manywrecks",
      "x": 2,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Seadeep Kingdoms",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "Manywrecks is a maze of low shoals where a hard current drives ships onto stone crags, and has done for centuries. The hulks lie piled on the reefs in their hundreds, masts standing out of the water like a dead forest. Their holds were never emptied — everyone knows the wrecks are full, and everyone knows why they stay full. The merfolk of the deep kingdoms hold that what the sea takes, the sea keeps, and they come up for anyone who touches it. Ships steer wide of Manywrecks; the ones that don't join it.",
      "hiddenInfo": "IMPORTANT: Manywrecks is hostile ground. The drowned-and-changed — merfolk of the deep kingdoms, once surface folk, remade for the water — enforce the kingdoms' law here: what the sea takes, the sea keeps. A vessel passing wide is ignored. Anyone who anchors, boards a wreck, or takes from one is answered. They surface in parties of four to eight, quiet, from the water side — cutting lines and holing boats before showing themselves — and they prefer taking the living down alive to killing them.\n\nLow tide opens wading paths between the outer hulks and bares the teeth; high tide closes the paths, floods the lower decks, and gives the field to the changed. Fresh wrecks happen with every storm, and survivors sometimes fort up in a sterncastle for days, fighting at the rails at high water and resting at low, until the kingdoms' patience or their water runs out. Reaching them first is the one errand that brings anyone here on purpose.\n\nGiant reef crabs den in the flooded holds, and great morays hunt the dark water between hulls.\n\nThe wrecks hold what they sank with: sealed cargo chests, navy paychests, ships' bells and bronze cannon, officers' plate and swords, charted rutters, enchanted compasses, and wine and spice untouched in sealed holds.",
      "areas": {
        "The Dry Bar": {
          "description": "A long sandbar standing above the high-tide line, bare except for driftwood and old timbers half-buried in the sand. Open ground, firm footing, and clear sight in every direction over the shoal water. The nearest hulks lie a short wade off its western end at low tide.",
          "paths": [
            "The Old Field"
          ]
        },
        "The Old Field": {
          "description": "The outer wrecks, oldest and most broken, heeled over on the shallowest teeth. At low water, sand paths and exposed reef run hulk to hulk; at high water the paths drown and the hulls stand isolated. Decks are rotten and tilted, holds are flooded to the waist, and broken spars bridge some of the gaps. The standing water between the hulks is dark and deeper than it looks.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dry Bar",
            "The Masts"
          ]
        },
        "The Masts": {
          "description": "The heart of the field, where the current rafts the newer wrecks against each other in a tangle of hulls. Ships lie boardable deck to deck, and the standing masts and rigging offer high lines of sight and crossings that never touch the water. Sterncastles and sealed holds sit intact above the waterline. Between the rafted hulls, gaps of open water drop away black.",
          "paths": [
            "The Old Field"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [],
      "images": {
        "establishingShot": {
          "crop": {
