    "The High Caves": {
      "name": "The High Caves",
      "x": 19,
      "y": 38,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Shadow Summits",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A prison complex cut into the mountain above Velmere, built during the Elven Schism to hold what the first vampire lords made by mistake, and disused for longer than anyone has kept records. Nobody can seal it. The moon elves who built it drove ways in from half the mountain so their keepers could move, and those ways are still open. So whatever wants a defensible hold within a night's walk of a rich valley takes it — strigoi, worse things down from the peaks, and sometimes people. Velmere loses stock, then travelers, then a family on an outlying terrace, and the Crimson Company goes up and roots out whatever is living in there. It has been rooted out many times.",
      "hiddenInfo": "WHY THIS PLACE NEVER STAYS EMPTY: it is a purpose-built stronghold, hidden, defensible, and an hour above a wealthy valley that cannot defend its outlying terraces. Clearing it does not solve it — clearing it creates a good empty lair next to Velmere, and something always takes the vacancy. The Company knows this. The work is maintenance, not victory, and it is paid as maintenance.\n\nTHE BUILDERS: moon elves, during or just after the Schism, when the Lady of Blood's gift was new and going wrong constantly and the first lords needed somewhere to put their failures. The architecture is institutional and competent: cell corridors, keeper stations, silvered grilles, chain-and-counterweight gates, murder-slots, and drops cut deliberately across the floors. Much of it still works. It was abandoned, not destroyed, and nobody now living knows the whole of it.\n\nWHO IS IN THERE varies by visit and is established on arrival. Use exactly the occupiers the arrival brief names, and do not mix tenants:\n- STRIGOI: mindless, no parley, sluggish by day and fierce after dark, wall-crawlers, denning in the cells the place was built to hold them in.\n- A FERALS PACK: werewolves and beast-shifters who took the curse willingly. Organized, they post watches, they hold ground, and they talk — which nothing else up here does. Silver and moonstone bite them.\n- RAIDERS: people. Outlaws, deserters, a Company crew that went bad. They parley, ransom, bluff, and betray, and they may be holding somebody from the valley.\n- GHOULS: down from Charnel Mountain, in numbers, with no tactics, dug into the lower cells.\n\nWHAT IS WORTH TAKING, in two kinds. First, the keepers' own arsenal, cached and mostly untouched because most tenants cannot use it: exact catalogue names Moonstone Dagger of Radiant, Silver Binding Chain, Silver-Tipped Bolts of Radiance, Vampire Hunter's Crossbow, Iron Shortsword, Leather Jerkin. Second, and this is what makes the site worth returning to, whatever the current tenant has dragged in: coin, stock, road-plunder, trophies, a strongbox, the effects of people who did not come home. That refills every time the tenant changes.\n\nARRIVAL RULE: name who holds the place this visit and how dug in they are before anything else, then make contact immediate and physical at or just inside the outworks.\n\nIMPORTANT: the Unmapped is the reason this site cannot be closed. The plan runs out down there, the builders cut into natural cave, and there are ways in from the mountain that nobody has ever found. Never let the party permanently seal, collapse, or end the site. They can clear it, and clearing it is worth doing and worth paying for.",
      "areas": {
        "The Outworks": {
          "description": "The built face of the complex, cut square into the shaded wall a hard climb above the vineyard terraces: a gate arch wide enough for a cart, guardrooms either side of it, and slots in the stone above that were made for shooting down through. The gates themselves are long gone. On the shelf outside, the Crimson Company keeps a cull-camp — fire ring, spear rack, a bench, and a post with a tally cut into it that has run out of room twice. Daylight reaches through the arch and a good way down the first corridor, and nothing that lives here comes past that line while it lasts.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The Cells": {
          "description": "The ordered body of the complex, and the part somebody drew a plan of once. Corridors of cells with silvered grilles gone dull and grey, keeper stations at the junctions with their slate boards still bolted up, chain-and-counterweight gates that can still be worked by anyone who reads how, and drops cut across the floors in places that make sense only if you were herding something. Whatever holds the complex dens along these corridors and stores what it takes in the cells.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outworks",
            "The Unmapped"
          ]
        },
        "The Unmapped": {
          "description": "Where the plan runs out. The builders cut into natural cave and kept going, and the levels below that are collapsed, flooded, or simply unrecorded. Cold air moves through here from somewhere, steadily, which means the mountain is open to it in places nobody has ever found. This is where whatever leads the current tenants sleeps, and it is why the front gate was never the problem.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cells"
          ]
        }
      },
