    "Val Cendrac": {
      "name": "Val Cendrac",
      "x": 12,
      "y": 46,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Shadow Summits",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "factions": [],
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "basicInfo": "A wide valley beyond the range west of Velmere, and the largest fief in the western Summits. Cendrac's forges have burned day and night for longer than anyone has counted, and what falls on the town is as often ash as snow — grey on the roofs, grey on the statues, grey underfoot by morning. The town beneath is beautiful even so: elegant stone townhouses in tiers up the valley side, wide walks lined with the statues of its ancient Peers, and a bathhouse the size of a temple, heated off the furnaces and free to anyone who walks in. Silver and moonstone out of Cendrac's workshops arm most of the Shadow Summits, the Crimson Company included. Lord Bertran ni Verrone holds the valley from Keep Verrone above the town, and one road joins it to Velmere: Ashfall Pass.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lord Bertran ni Verrone has held Val Cendrac a long time and has asked Lady Isaveau ni Mirevaux of Velmere to marry him, at intervals, for something close to six hundred years. She has never accepted and has never quite closed the door either, and the correspondence is famous among the Peers in the way a long joke is famous.\n\nWhat he says he wants is water. Cendrac is furnaces on dry black rock, and forges at this scale drink more than the valley can give; Velmere holds the largest body of water in the Summits, cold and deep and fed off the peaks. He also says, and it is harder to answer, that a lord with no heir is a valley that will one day be masterless — and a masterless valley on his border becomes another Vale Sable, and his problem forever. He does not say what else he wants, and everybody knows.\n\nCendrac keeps the levy and keeps it strictly. The chosen go up to Keep Verrone and do not come back, thirteen times a year, and the fief counts it the highest honour it can give: the family is compensated generously and permanently, and households put themselves forward. This is not resented here. It is competed for. The valley is large, rich and proud, and considers Velmere's refusal to take a levy at all to be sentimentality that its neighbours will eventually pay for.\n\nGhouls out of Cendrac come over the ridge onto Velmere's outlying terraces. Nobody says so. Ghouls are vermin, vermin can be killed by the Crimson Company without anyone admitting a war, and both houses get what they want out of the arrangement. Every few years it worsens, and it worsens after a refusal. The connection has never once been stated aloud by anyone who matters.\n\nAshfall Pass is watched from both ends and garrisoned at neither, because neither lord needs a gate. The tunnels are the real traffic: old workings and cut ways through the range, used by smugglers, by Cendrac's people, and by whatever wants to cross unseen. Isaveau collapses them as she finds them. She has been finding them for six centuries and has never found them all.\n\nThe two valleys cannot afford to finish this. Cendrac's silver and moonstone are the only things that reliably kill what comes down out of the peaks, and Velmere buys nearly all of its weapons here; Velmere's water is the only water at that volume, and its tinctures and poisons are traded nowhere else in Larion. Each holds something the other cannot manufacture, which is why six hundred years of this has never once become a war.\n\nBertran has married, more than once, and has get: a real house of made vampires, properly sired by the rite of two, several of them centuries old and holding offices in the valley. He is aware that Isaveau has none, and mentions it only by being seen with his.\n\nIMPORTANT: Val Cendrac is safe for a visitor who keeps the peace. It is a wealthy, orderly, well-policed fief, its people are neither cowed nor secretive, and they will discuss the levy with a stranger quite happily. Nothing in the town is hunting anyone.",
      "areas": {
        "The Statuary": {
          "description": "The town's long central walk and the place everyone crosses to get anywhere else, laid in pale stone that never looks pale for long. Statues of Cendrac's Peers stand down both sides in two unbroken ranks, carved in the fashion of whichever century put them up, each with the years of their holding cut at the foot. Elegant townhouses rise in tiers behind them with iron balconies and shuttered ground floors. Ash comes down out of the sky most days, mixed with snow or instead of it, and there are people whose whole trade is sweeping it, working the walk from end to end and starting again.",
          "paths": [
            "The Long Evening",
            "The Peers' Gift",
            "Keep Verrone",
            "Ashfall Pass North"
          ],
          "images": {
            "establishingShot": {
              "crop": {
                "focus": {
                  "x": 50,
                  "y": 50
                },
                "zoom": 100
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "The Long Evening": {
          "description": "The house where Cendrac receives anyone worth receiving: a tall stone building off the Statuary with a lit portico, a doorman who knows faces, and rooms that run back further than the frontage suggests. Inside is panelling, low light, deep chairs and very good glass. Envoys of the houses stay here, Syndicate factors take rooms by the season, and people waiting on an audience at the Keep wait here, sometimes for weeks. The kitchen is superb. The lord's own get come down of an evening and are given the best table without anyone asking. Business is done at the tables in plain sight, quietly, and the staff have been trained for generations not to hear it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Statuary"
          ],
          "images": {
            "establishingShot": {
              "crop": {
                "focus": {
                  "x": 50,
                  "y": 50
                },
                "zoom": 100
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "The Peers' Gift": {
          "description": "A bathhouse the size of a temple, built over the vents that carry the forges' waste heat, and free to anyone who walks through the door — miner, factor, envoy, or beggar, with no attendant at the entrance to ask which. Vaulted halls in grey and green stone, pools graded from scalding to snowmelt, steam standing in the roof, and mosaics of the valley's Peers set into the floors where bathers walk over them without a thought. It is the warmest place in the Shadow Summits and the loudest room in Cendrac: half the valley's business, gossip, courtship and quarrelling happens here, undressed and unarmed. An inscription over the entrance names which Peer gave it and in what year, and nothing else.",
          "paths": [
            "The Statuary"
          ],
          "images": {
            "establishingShot": {
              "crop": {
                "focus": {
                  "x": 50,
