            }
          }
        },
        "Keep Verrone": {
          "description": "Lord Bertran's seat, standing on the shoulder of the valley above the town and lit along its whole face at night so the town can see it. Black stone, no rougher than it needs to be, with tall glazed windows and a long stair up from the Statuary that is swept before dawn every day of the year. Inside is warm — the same vents that feed the bathhouse run under these floors — and hung with six centuries of acquisitions arranged by somebody with taste and time. His get hold offices here and are visible about the place, several of them older than most of the visitors who come to see their sire. The correspondence room is kept as a room in its own right, and the letters in it go back further than the town does.",
