  "factions": {
    "The League of Adventurers": {
      "name": "The League of Adventurers",
      "basicInfo": "The League of Adventurers is a fellowship of heroes headquartered in the Watchtower at Ardan's Watch. It gives out adventuring work, ranks members, and sends capable people where crowns and armies cannot solve the problem. The League answers to no crown and welcomes anyone willing and able. That neutrality is its purpose: rival realms that would never trust each other trust the League, and so its members move freely across borders closed to everyone else. The League also keeps a longer memory than most. Larion was ruled in ages past by demons and tyrants, and its oldest charge is to see that such tyranny never rises again.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Greater Goblinate": {
      "name": "The Greater Goblinate",
      "basicInfo": "The Greater Goblinate is the goblin war-state based in Smoketown in the Black Fells, risen from scattered clans into one nation under a single banner: the Dragon Queen, in whose name they mean to burn the old world down and raise a goblin age in its place. Their armies carry fire-weapons that throw dragonfire, and they raid by sea and by land — taking plunder, prisoners, and land.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Church of the Pyre": {
      "name": "The Church of the Pyre",
      "basicInfo": "The Church of the Pyre is a fire-cult that preaches the old world is rotten and must burn, so that a new and pure one can rise from the ash. They worship Ignath, Lord of Fire, who they say sleeps beneath the world and will wake to cleanse it — and they hail the Dragon Queen as his fire come at last into the world. Its priests channel dragonfire through their own scarred bodies, carry the faith to the desperate and the ruined, and forge the dragonfire weapons the goblin armies carry. They conduct the great fire-rites and mark those who will not convert as fuel for the world they mean to raise. The deeper a priest goes into the fire, the more it shows on him — and the more of him it has taken.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Syndicate": {
      "name": "The Syndicate",
      "basicInfo": "The Syndicate is the web of crime-lords that holds the underside of Larion. In every city of any size there is a boss — a rich and dangerous man grown fat on smuggling, extortion, theft, and the selling of people — and everyone knows who he is. He doesn't hide it. He owns warehouses on the docks and a tavern where his men drink, he keeps the magistrate in his purse or in fear, and the city lives with him because crossing him means a knife in the dark or the trade choked off. His toughs walk the market for their cut, and the stall that won't pay burns. Above the local bosses are greater bosses, in greater cities, and above them the High Men — the richest and most feared crime-lords alive, who command the rest through tribute, debt, and murder. Syndicate men wear their allegiance in ink — an open black hand tattooed on the neck or forearm, the hand that takes the cut — so that anyone who crosses them knows exactly whose man they are hurting. The mark is a threat that walks.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Thrallmages of Falmastra": {
      "name": "The Thrallmages of Falmastra",
      "basicInfo": "The Thrallmages of Falmastra are the mind-mages of a small island city-state off the southern coast. They sell their arts to any power that can meet the price: reading thoughts, speaking mind to mind, bending the will of others, and binding many minds into one. Thrallmagic breaks most who attempt it; those who master it are the strongest-willed people alive, and without exception they are people who wanted power over others. Few dare move against Falmastra, for the thrallmages answer any harm done to one of them a thousandfold, and they do not forget.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Children of Ur": {
      "name": "The Children of Ur",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Children of Ur are a cult that worships the Demon-Princes of Ur, the demon-lords who ruled Larion in the age of the Fourth Empire before they were cast down. The Children hold that fall to be a wound, and they work in scattered cells to undo it. They are thickest in the old places — buried ruins, drowned cities, the deep south where the Princes' marks still lie in the stone — digging for what their masters left behind and gathering what their rites require. A cell hides inside ordinary life, in a household or a guild or a temple gone quietly wrong, and is known by what it leaves: the disappeared, the defiled grave, the old seal broken open.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Ferals": {
      "name": "The Ferals",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Ferals are a cult of werewolves and other beast-shifters who have given themselves over to the beast. They take the curse willingly — through stolen rites and draughts of tainted blood — for the strength of the predator and the thrill of the hunt, and the deeper a Feral goes, the less remains of whoever they were. Wolves are the most common, but the boldest take greater and worse shapes. They recruit with the promise of power to anyone weak enough to want it badly, they run in packs in the wild places, and a young Feral may still pass for an ordinary person in a town until the hunger takes them.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Order of the Silver Sword": {
      "name": "The Order of the Silver Sword",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Order of the Silver Sword is a brotherhood of monster-hunters seated at Castle Harstone in the Kingdom of Carendon. Candidates enter as Initiates and complete one supervised trial hunt before they may be admitted as full Silverblades and receive the Order's silver blade pin. The name is traditional: Silverblades may carry any kind of silvered weapon, a silver-faced shield, or a silver-bound magical focus or implement. They take contracts wherever a monster has outmatched the people meant to stop it, and they go where those people will not: into the lair, the barrow, and the deep dark beneath the world.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Order of the Desert Rose": {
      "name": "The Order of the Desert Rose",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Order of the Desert Rose is an order of demon-hunters seated at the Citadel of the Pact in the Redrock. A demon slain in the world is not destroyed — only sent home to the plane that bore it, to heal and be summoned again — so the Rose does not merely slay the fiends it hunts. It binds them: caught and caged in warded brass and rose-stone, sealed in its vaults, taken out of the world for good. A hunter of the Rose carries the means to bind as well as kill, knows the names and natures of fiends, and keeps the Pact of All Planes, by which the planes are sovereign and nothing from beyond may walk the world unbound.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Templars Adamant": {
      "name": "The Templars Adamant",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Templars Adamant are Drusavia's elite Shieldbearer order, seated at the Temple of Galard. They wear beautiful blessed armor and wield radiant arms against the dead, refusing all traffic with shades: they do not bind them, question them, or bargain with them. Their members rise from Templar Initiate to Templar Adamant, Templar Captain, and Templar Champion through recognized works of protection, purging, warding, and sealing. They seek to close the Pit of Shadow and seal the Shadelands away from the living world, even though the Shadow Inquisition warns that doing so would leave the duchy blind to the dead's return.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Wildkeepers": {
      "name": "The Wildkeepers",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Wildkeepers are a fellowship of druids, rangers, and priests seated at the great oak of Velantami in Sanctuary Vale. They are bound to the wild places and hold that they must never be owned or tamed, but left as they should be: free and alive in the natural balance. A Wildkeeper protects that balance and is the enemy of all that breaks it: the poacher and grove-feller who strip the wood for gain, the blight and spell that corrupt its cycle, the crown and settler who would cut a road through it and call it theirs. The Wildkeepers answer to no king, no judge, no written law, only to what each knows in his heart he owes the wild. Though they may choose to guide the lost and aid the honest who pass through their domain, their vow is to keep the wild against the greedy who would strip it and the well-meaning who would tame it alike.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The New Sun": {
      "name": "The New Sun",
      "basicInfo": "The New Sun is the remnant of the Sun Kings' burial priesthood, still working in secret to wake the entombed god-kings of the Dunelands four ages after their fall. They take the empyrean Empress's rise over Caranthoria as proof that the old dawn is returning, and their rites are why the dead stir in the Tombs of the Sun Kings.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "Khenret's Company": {
      "name": "Khenret's Company",
      "basicInfo": "Khenret's Company is a mercenary tomb-raiding company seated at the First Pyramid in the Dunelands. Khenret's crews strip tombs for hire and for shares — hard men and women with picks, ropes, and blades, who will race rival diggers to a find, strong-arm them off a claim, and fight anyone who contests a haul. They honor their contracts, guard their own, and owe nothing to anyone they haven't been paid to care about.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "Greloq's Company": {
      "name": "Greloq's Company",
      "basicInfo": "Greloq's Company is the richest and most hated delving outfit at Deepgate in the Whiteholds, holding the Old Keep above the town and reaching the best finds in the Darek Deep first by every dirty means there is: bought maps, jumped claims, hired muscle, and bribed lift-crews. The Company is the Syndicate's hand in the rush — its backers fund crews, fence Darek salvage, and corner the soulstones coming up the pit — and Greloq keeps the arrangement profitable and quiet.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "Blackfire": {
      "name": "Blackfire",
      "basicInfo": "Blackfire is a Sanctuary mercenary crew that takes contracts for protection, salvage, creature hunts, and work in the forest. Its signature weapons are alchemical dragonfire projectors that spew black alchemical fire. The crew is useful, feared, and notorious for treating living forest and creature territory as something to burn through.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": "Blackfire is greedy, unscrupulous, and brutal. Its captains take work from anyone who pays and accept quiet Syndicate backing when it supplies better contracts, information, or fuel. Their signature projectors are illicit adaptations of Goblinate firelance craft, obtained through black-market trade and modified for short-range work by a mercenary crew. They spew thick black alchemical fire that clings to wood, canvas, brush, and exposed targets, driving people and creatures out of cover and leaving blackened scars after it dies. The weapons are costly and dangerous: a damaged reservoir can rupture, a misfire can burn the crew, and Blackfire crews sometimes burn the ground or people they were paid to protect. They treat Wildkeeper objections as interference and may call living forest, creature territory, or captured beasts contract property when that serves the job."
    },
    "The Long Night": {
      "name": "The Long Night",
      "basicInfo": "The Long Night are cultists of Hrimnara, the Widow of Winter, who hold that the cold should be fed rather than appeased, until it covers the world. They work by choking the hot springs the north's settlements live by: the frozen town of Stillwater is their proof, and their seat is a desecrated shrine of the Lady of the Hearth above a spring they killed. Snowlanders hate them above anything else on the ice.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Shadow Inquisition": {
      "name": "The Shadow Inquisition",
      "known": false,
      "basicInfo": "The Shadow Inquisition is an order of ghost-hunters and occult investigators seated at the Black Tower in Drusavia. Accepted candidates begin as Initiates bearing the Mark of Rhadan. After one supervised qualifying case, an Initiate may undergo the Rite of Bonding at the Pit of Shadow and rise as a full Inquisitor with a Shade. Inquisitors answer hauntings, necromancers, restless dead, and threats rooted in the Shadelands; Adepts lead deeper work, and Masters govern the order's gravest cases. Folk are glad of them and uneasy in their presence, for the Inquisition keeps dark company to protect the living.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The College of Sentinels": {
      "name": "The College of Sentinels",
      "basicInfo": "The College of Sentinels is one of the five Colleges of Tarnholme Academy, seated on the Bastion. Its students train as wardens, battle-mages, shield-workers, and fast-response field mages: the ones called when a manastorm breaks, a summon slips, a haunt needs containing, or another Practicum has gone sideways. Sentinel culture is physical, loud, competitive, and practical. Students prove themselves in leaping-stone crossings, ward drills, sky-skate riding, and the Three-Touch dueling rings, but the play stops when the muster bell rings. A good Sentinel wards on instinct, stands steady under pressure, and can laugh after being knocked into the Tarn.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The College of Shapers": {
      "name": "The College of Shapers",
      "basicInfo": "The College of Shapers is one of the five Colleges of Tarnholme Academy, seated on the Wonderworks. Its students are artificers, enchanters, device-builders, and practical magical makers who build the portal-rings, lamps, lifts, wards, tools, constructs, and odd machinery the Academy depends on. Shaper culture is fast, cocky, inventive, and competitive. The daily Piece Pit challenge gives students bad parts and an impossible task, and the winner is the one who makes a strange hand of movers, grippers, eyes, cores, and oddments solve the problem anyway. A good Shaper makes the broken useful before anyone else has finished explaining why it cannot work.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The College of Binders": {
      "name": "The College of Binders",
      "basicInfo": "The College of Binders is one of the five Colleges of Tarnholme Academy, seated on the Menagerie. Its students call, bind, bargain with, command, and banish things from other planes. The College prides itself on doing the work that frightens the rest of Tarnholme: not killing the dangerous thing, but understanding its nature, making it listen, and sending it home whole. Binder training centers on circles, contracts, command, containment, and the Run, where students must guide a summoned creature through an unfair task without losing control. A good Binder knows that every called thing has wants, limits, tricks, and pride, and that a careless command is an invitation.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The College of Seekers": {
      "name": "The College of Seekers",
      "basicInfo": "The College of Seekers is one of the five Colleges of Tarnholme Academy, seated in the Stacks. Its students are loremasters, investigators, translators, relic-readers, monster-namers, and field scholars who turn mysteries into directions. Seeker culture is sharp, curious, competitive, and outward-facing: the useful answer is the one that tells someone where to go next and what to fear when they get there. The librarian statues know the shelves and help honest questions find their books; the harder answers sit higher up, deeper in, or behind master-keys and sponsor writs. A good Seeker can make a torn map, old name, monster sign, curse-script, or marginal note point to a real place in the world.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The College of Menders": {
      "name": "The College of Menders",
      "basicInfo": "The College of Menders is one of the five Colleges of Tarnholme Academy, seated on the Mend. Its students are healers, curse-breakers, poison-drawers, blight-workers, and treaters of the strange harms magic leaves behind. Mender culture is warm, watchful, socially tangled, and uncompromising about care: anyone who comes hurt is taken in and tended, whether friend, rival, stranger, or enemy. Students learn around warm pools, gardens, recovery lawns, the Long Table, and the Quiet Ward, where dangerous cases are contained. A good Mender notices the hurt person before the impressive spell, and knows when a wound, curse, sickness, or reflection that moves late has become a Practicum.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Crimson Company": {
      "name": "The Crimson Company",
      "basicInfo": "The Crimson Company is the standing soldiery of the vampire lords of the Shadow Summits, serving the lords collectively rather than any single house and hired out valley to valley under a red sword on black. The Company holds cordons, culls strigoi and ghouls, escorts what the lords trade, and keeps order in the lit valleys. It takes anyone the rest of Larion has cast out — exiles, fugitives, the condemned — so long as they can fight and keep a bargain, and it feeds, clothes, and pays them far better than an honest army would. Its officers came north from Silvervale after a failed coup and have made a creed of it: that the rest of Larion is soft and deluded, and that the Summits face the real problem. Its elite are occasionally Embraced, and every blade in the ranks can name them.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    },
    "The Lords of the Blood": {
      "name": "The Lords of the Blood",
      "basicInfo": "The Lords of the Blood are the vampire lords of the Shadow Summits: a few dozen moon elf vampires, each holding a valley from a fortified estate and answering to nothing above them. Among themselves they are the Peers of the Blood, and the style is used to their faces and in their letters. They are not a government. What binds them is a shared ancestor in the Lady of Blood, a shared rite at the Black Gate where alone a new lord can be made, and a truce they keep there and nowhere else. They feed on the people they rule by fixed levy and defend those valleys in return, and they hold that this is the price of the north: that the Hunger wells up wherever the barrier runs thin, and that they are the only ones who can stand on it and keep their minds. They scheme against one another constantly through marriage, murder, and proxy, and they take in the world’s outcasts to do the work they will not.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "known": false,
      "hiddenInfo": ""
    }
  },
