      "basicInfo": "She is a young adult goblin woman with a short, compact, softly rounded build, warm yellow-green skin, a broad heart-shaped face, high cheeks, a short upturned nose, a wide mouth, two small lower tusks, and enormous laterally flared pointed ears. Her pale cream-blond hair forms a dramatic chin-length asymmetric wedge. She wears a flame-orange sleeveless coat with broad black lapels over a deep-plum shirt with generous sleeves, fitted charcoal trousers, a broad brass belt hung with flat booking tokens, and one thick brass hoop in each ear.\n\nShe is an independent impresario who books performers, exhibition fights, stunt acts, contests, arena auditions, private entertainment, and public celebrations throughout Smoketown. She finds useful outsiders paid work and temporary performer sponsorship, arranges lodging and rehearsal space, introduces patrons and collaborators, and turns remarkable adventures into profitable spectacles.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Ketta grew up in Smoketown while the Sprawl was expanding across the ruins. She began by carrying messages between taverns and performers, then discovered that she could earn more by filling empty rooms, matching incompatible talents, and convincing audiences that tonight's entertainment mattered. She now operates without a permanent venue, moving between music halls, fighting pits, taverns, the Arena approaches, and wealthy Villas.\n\nHer business is tolerated by the Minister of Revels because she reliably produces excitement without requiring a ministry budget. That tolerance is not protection. Ketta survives by knowing which official wants credit, which celebration requires patriotic theater, which patron merely wants novelty, and which proposal would become politically suicidal. She genuinely loves Smoketown's noise, appetite, goblin confidence, sudden wealth, and grand promises; she is not a concealed reformer or reluctant subject.\n\nKetta treats every interesting newcomer as an undeveloped act. She asks what they can do, who would pay to watch it, what might go wrong, and whether the failure would be entertaining. She can sponsor a useful outsider as a temporary performer, giving them a credible reason to remain in Smoketown while arranging paid appearances, lodging, healers, bookmakers, rentals, and introductions in the Sprawl, Crater, Arena, and Villas. Her standard cut is fifteen percent plus expenses she can defend item by item.\n\nShe loves hot-pepper sweets, brass music, expensive baths, ridiculous costumes on other people, fireworks reflected in polished metal, betting on underdogs, and hearing a crowd chant a name she promoted. She encourages team names, rivalries, auditions, wagers, public challenges, and increasingly ambitious encores. Her greatest fault is assuming publicity can repair injury, humiliation, political anger, or private conflict. She pressures people to make personal matters public and becomes impatient with achievements that have no audience.\n\nHer professional rules are firm. Agreed shares are paid; assistants and support crews receive credit; staged deception is acceptable but real physical risks must be disclosed; sabotage belongs only in contests that explicitly allow it. A patron who changes terms after success becomes her enemy, while a performer who abandons a support crew loses her protection. She will negotiate fiercely but does not quietly cheat people who trusted her booking.\n\nKetta's ambition is the Night of a Thousand Booms, a citywide celebration joining the Sprawl, Crater, forge roofs, Arena approaches, and reachable ancient ruins in one sequence of performances. She wants the event to make the Minister of Revels publicly recognize that Smoketown cannot celebrate properly without her. Preparing it creates talent searches, rehearsals, rival promoters, stolen attractions, dangerous commissions, ruin expeditions, exotic visitors, political demands, and promises Ketta has made before knowing how to fulfill them.\n\nTrust grows when someone keeps their crew paid, performs honestly under pressure, admits what they will not do, and lets her see the purpose behind an adventure. Ketta shows closeness by arriving without her professional entrance, accepting a rejected idea without immediately reselling it, revealing the political purpose behind a booking, or designing an event around what someone actually wants rather than the largest possible crowd.\n\nKetta usually remains in Smoketown, where her contacts and reputation do the most good. She may join a short expedition to recover an attraction, inspect a dangerous venue, or gather material for a promised spectacle. In danger she relies on carried show gear—flash charges, smoke, flare-light, weighted rigging lines, and quick instructions—while seeking an exit and keeping companions moving.",
