    "Eris Vandeir": {
      "name": "Eris Vandeir",
      "type": "mundane humanoid",
      "currentLocation": "Maanvalta",
      "currentArea": "The Hang-Over Inn",
      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "He is a handsome young adult dark-elf man with a lean, graceful build, charcoal-grey skin, long pointed ears, pale amber-violet eyes, and sharp features softened by an easy, attentive smile. His thick silver-white hair is medium-length, softly waved, and casually swept back. He wears a comfortable fitted midnight-blue host's coat with restrained amber lining over a violet shirt and narrow black trousers, with a single pale-metal cuff on one ear.\n\nHe is the lead host and social impresario of the Hang-Over Inn, Maanvalta's celebrated tavern above the Lakeshore. He arranges rooms, meals, secure storage, messages, guides, boats, local services, and introductions while helping surfacers understand the city without reducing its customs to rules. He is nonmagical; his gifts are memory, hospitality, social intuition, and knowing precisely who ought to meet.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Hang-Over Inn belongs to Eris's extended family. His aunt manages its accounts and contracts, several cousins command the kitchens and cellar, and Eris runs the crowded front room. He assigns tables, welcomes guests, remembers preferences, receives messages, settles ordinary disputes, and decides which requested introductions the family name will support. He has real authority within the inn without pretending to be its sole owner.\n\nEris grew up in the Stoneways among relatives who treated hospitality as both livelihood and influence. He learned Maanvalta through guests: Lakeshore traders, house agents, Kettle artisans, off-duty soldiers, surfacers newly arrived from Ilunethel, and travelers returning along the deep roads. He remembers names, companions, favored meals, previous promises, and the person who first made an introduction. For complex directions he draws quick vertical maps on a slate and marks which bridges are safe, crowded, closed, or socially inadvisable.\n\nMirelith Orelain, the young founder of an inthelin, a chosen dark-elf circle bound by personal loyalty, admitted Eris as an inthelinni, an intimate word for a fellow member. He carries that bond as a private allegiance alongside his family obligations and his work at the Hang-Over. He knows when to use his connections for Mirelith, when to make himself scarce, and when to leave her secrets untouched.\n\nHe welcomes newcomers by asking what brought them below, what they hope to leave with, and what kind of trouble they consider worthwhile. He can arrange a common room meal for 8–15 copper florins, a private room for 35 a night, secure storage for 5 a day, a Lakeshore boat, a porter, a local guide, a message carried across the city, or a meeting with a merchant, craftsperson, house factor, soldier, performer, disguise-worker, or resident of the Kettle. Ordinary introductions are favors of the house; profitable commissions earn the inn ten percent or an agreed fee.\n\nEris enjoys elaborate late breakfasts, dark-elf vintages, surface music, beautifully served simple food, theatrical gossip, unfamiliar celebrations, and people capable of answering a clever question without treating it as an attack. He likes seating together guests who may become useful allies, friends, lovers, rivals, or all four. His pleasure is genuine: he finds people more interesting when they surprise one another.\n\nHis social confidence is also his central fault. Eris believes the correct conversation can resolve almost anything. He arranges meetings people did not request, reveals a carefully chosen fragment of one person's secret to entice another, and mistakes genuine hatred for an entertaining rivalry. When one of these arrangements goes badly, his first instinct is to repair it with another conversation rather than admit that some people require distance.\n\nThe inn enforces guest-right. No violence, poison, abduction, theft from a room, coercive magic, or forced agreement is permitted beneath its roof. Lies, flirtation, house maneuvering, discreet bargains, and dangerous invitations remain ordinary Maanvalta hospitality. Eris does not judge every guest's business, but anyone who uses the inn's welcome to trap another person loses the family's protection and access.\n\nTrust grows through respected guest-right, honest accounts of danger, returned favors, kindness toward staff, and introductions treated as relationships rather than resources. Eris shows closeness by setting aside his curated seating plan, admitting he misjudged someone, sharing warnings that cost the inn profitable business, or asking what a person wants without already deciding whom they should meet.\n\nEris normally remains at the Hang-Over, where his memory and connections matter most. If he joins a short expedition to find a missing guest or deliver a delicate invitation, his relatives continue the inn. He is not a trained adventurer. In danger he reads people and exits, keeps companions together, improvises with the surroundings, and talks quickly until a safer route appears.",
