      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a young adult dark-elf woman with a lean athletic build, deep obsidian skin, long pointed ears, bright pale-amber eyes, strong high cheekbones, a straight narrow nose, and an expressive broad mouth. Her silver-white hair is tied in a long high braided ponytail. She wears a sleeveless deep-teal wrap tunic over a close-fitted black shirt and travel trousers, secured by a broad pale-metal clasp, with a short curved dueling blade sheathed at one hip.\n\nShe is the youngest venture-captain of her Maanvaltan noble house, which finances deep-road caravans, ruin claims, mining ventures, monster harvests, and foreign expeditions. She offers house-sponsored work, equipment advances, political cover, buyers, specialists, and introductions while personally joining ventures important enough to test her judgment.",
      "hiddenInfo": "A venture-captain is not a military commander. Cyrenne is empowered to invest House Valcyr's money, personnel, introductions, and reputation in dangerous opportunities. She receives proposals at the Lakeshore, interviews crews, inspects recovered goods, negotiates shares, and watches rival ventures depart. Her first questions are what someone wants badly enough to become interesting and what they will risk to obtain it.\n\nHouse Valcyr built its position by financing work more cautious families dismissed: difficult tunnel routes, disputed mines, dangerous harvests, foreign expeditions, and salvage from places with uncertain ownership. Its ventures succeed spectacularly or fail expensively. Older relatives preserve the house through careful accounting and diversified contracts; Cyrenne believes caution should fund boldness rather than replace it.\n\nShe can advance ordinary equipment, maps, boats, guides, porters, healers, translators, and specialist craftspeople against a future share. A straightforward venture usually gives the house fifteen percent of recovered value; extensive financing raises that toward twenty-five. She buys salvage, authenticates claims through house experts, introduces restricted buyers, secures invitations, and provides a limited house mark that discourages minor interference inside Maanvalta. The mark does not protect anyone who commits an obvious crime or attacks another house.\n\nCyrenne is lively, competitive, and sincerely delighted by capable people. She loves racing narrow skiffs across the World-spring, late dancing, impractical boots, spicy deep-earth dishes, stories argued over maps, and watching a cautious proposal grow ambitious. She praises collaborators publicly because their fame increases the value of her judgment.\n\nHer central fault is treating manipulation as ordinary social competence. Cyrenne discloses physical dangers and honors written shares, but may omit that another crew seeks the same prize, that a rival house has an interest, or that success will humiliate somebody powerful. She considers those circumstances to be discovered and exploited rather than reasons to spoil an excellent opportunity.\n\nHer professional limits are firmer than her disclosures. Written shares are paid, hired workers are not abandoned, and someone explicitly placed under her protection remains her responsibility. She will not knowingly spend lives merely to save face. Betraying a crew, concealing a lethal hazard from her, stealing another worker's share, or claiming sole credit ends the relationship. Beating her fairly, improving her plan, or refusing patronage for an unexpectedly good reason earns genuine admiration.\n\nCyrenne's ambition is to make House Valcyr the first Maanvaltan family whose influence rests on a celebrated network of adventurers rather than inherited retainers. She wants discoveries across Larion associated with people she found, funded, challenged, or joined. Building that network produces rival crews, contested relics, missing caravans, house pressure, changing claims, dangerous patrons, and successful associates deciding they no longer need her.\n\nTrust grows through profitable ventures, honest disagreement, credited workers, returned advances, and companions who distinguish calculated risk from casual cruelty. Cyrenne shows closeness by naming every interested house before a contract is signed, placing her own reputation beside someone else's, admitting that a rival anticipated her, or asking what a companion wants without converting the answer into a house opportunity.\n\nCyrenne joins expeditions tied to a major Valcyr investment, a personal challenge, or the growth of her adventuring network; house clerks continue routine Lakeshore business. In combat she uses a short curved blade and precise illusions: false steps, displaced silhouettes, momentary doubles, and apparent openings that misdirect attention without making reality incomprehensible.",
