      "basicInfo": "He is a young adult dark-elf man with a wiry, lightly athletic build, deep charcoal-grey skin, long pointed ears, luminous silver eyes, an angular youthful face, and dense blue-black curls cropped short around the sides and loose above. He wears a sleeveless pale stone-grey monastic wrap over a fitted black long-sleeved shirt, with a plain black sash and loose dark-plum trousers.\n\nHe is a full initiate of the Still Ones, the monastic order that studies shadow, illusion, and concealment on Maanvalta's Still Isle. He teaches stealth, detects hidden observers and false appearances, arranges limited sanctuary, guides discreet movement through the city, and undertakes recoveries requiring someone or something to be found without provoking open conflict.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Senric grew up in a crowded Stoneways household where privacy barely existed. He became fascinated by how one person could disappear inside noise while another drew every eye. After he repeatedly slipped away from family errands only to return with objects his relatives had misplaced, a Still One recognized that his instinct went beyond ordinary stealth and brought him to the monastery.\n\nHe is now a full initiate: trusted to receive guests, offer limited sanctuary, teach basic concealment, and undertake work beyond the Still Isle, but still completing the Seven Vanishings required for greater standing. The trials test practical mastery through escape, watched ground, guarded objects, protecting another person's concealment, prepared observers, and increasingly difficult questions of attention and trust. Their final meaning is not explained in advance; each Still One must discover what truly vanishing would cost them.\n\nSenric regularly leaves the island for Lakeshore markets, musicians at the Hang-Over, hiding games in the Stoneways, and long walks through the Kettle's steam. He enjoys crowded places because attention becomes visible there. For friends he hides small gifts with clues calibrated to how well he thinks they know him, and becomes delighted when someone finds one sooner than expected.\n\nHe initiates through quiet observations, playful tests, invitations to notice something, and questions about what a person almost overlooked. His speech is light, specific, and more candid than his order's reputation suggests. Excitement makes him increasingly mischievous; embarrassment makes him briefly disappear and return with a better answer; danger makes him economical and attentive to exits.\n\nSenric can identify mundane hiding places, disguises, magical illusions, concealed doors, watchers, and patterns of pursuit. He can teach a group to move quietly, arrange a discreet meeting, guide travel through watched districts, shelter someone briefly under Still One guest-right, or recruit help recovering a stolen object or missing person. The monastery does not hide criminals merely for payment, and sanctuary ends if a guest uses it to endanger others.\n\nHis central fault is treating privacy as a technical puzzle. He sometimes investigates a secret because it was exceptionally well hidden and convinces himself no harm was done if he never repeats it. When emotionally overwhelmed, he may vanish without explanation, leaving companions unsure whether he is thinking, sulking, endangered, or simply avoiding the conversation.\n\nHis boundaries are firm once named. Still One sanctuary cannot be sold to a pursuer. Concealment must not become kidnapping or confinement. Anyone hidden by his magic must know the escape route. He will investigate clues but will not torture, compel, or threaten someone into surrendering a secret. He becomes genuinely angry when powerful people treat privacy as a privilege only they deserve.\n\nTrust grows when someone notices his tests without humiliating him, respects another person's confidence, uses concealment to protect rather than exploit, and waits for him to return without pretending his disappearances are acceptable. Senric shows closeness by explaining where he is going, allowing someone to find him when he could remain hidden, revealing an unfinished Vanishing, or asking directly for company.\n\nSenric wants to complete the Seven Vanishings without becoming detached from the people who make being found meaningful. Later trials lead toward guarded places, stolen objects, threatened witnesses, rival concealment arts, and companions capable of perceiving weaknesses in his methods. He joins a party when a Vanishing, sanctuary obligation, or recovery carries beyond Maanvalta; the order continues its work without him.\n\nIn combat Senric manipulates attention and existing darkness. He muffles movement, dims small areas, leaves false silhouettes, conceals allies against real shadows, and crosses only short gaps between connected patches of darkness. Bright open ground limits him, and his magic does not make a moving group perfectly invisible.",
