      "basicInfo": "She is a human woman in her mid-twenties with a compact athletic build, warm medium-brown skin, strong dark brows, dark amber eyes, a square-rounded face, broad cheekbones, a short straight nose, and a wide expressive mouth. Her thick black hair is gathered into one heavy high braid. She wears a cobalt-blue padded sleeveless vest over a saffron linen blouse with elbow-length sleeves, dark trousers, a broad brown belt, and one plain steel forearm guard.\n\nShe is the Guild of All Arts' appointed proving champion. She runs the Proving Yard, evaluates whether weapons, armor, tools, vehicles, and prototypes perform as claimed, recommends craftspeople, arranges repairs and commissions, recruits field testers, and loans experimental equipment in exchange for honest reports.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Anja holds an independent Guild appointment rather than belonging to a single master's workshop. Craftspeople pay testing fees, the Guild funds public demonstrations, and the Council expects her judgments to protect the value of its seal. She has authority over Proving Yard trials but does not own the yard, command the Guild, or manufacture most of the equipment she evaluates.\n\nShe began as an armorer's apprentice. Her own work was reliable but never beautiful enough to distinguish her among Forge-town's prodigies. What she could do exceptionally well was identify why something failed: poor balance, misplaced weight, a bad fastening, a joint that worked on a bench but caught during movement, or a feature designed for the maker rather than its eventual user. She became more valuable breaking and improving other people's work than producing her own.\n\nAnja spends mornings on appraisals, afternoons running public demonstrations, and evenings collecting prototypes and reports at the Steady Hand. Appraisals cost 15–40 copper florins; destructive tests start at 50 plus materials. Field loans require a deposit and written report, though promising adventurers may receive equipment free when evidence matters more than payment.\n\nShe can direct a customer toward the right smith, builder, leatherworker, woodworker, jeweler, or specialist; arrange ordinary repairs and custom commissions; compare rival makers' claims; identify damage and suspicious alterations; purchase unusual materials for a workshop; and recruit adventurers for practical trials. She knows which masters are brilliant, which apprentices are underrated, and which expensive Guild stamps conceal work that barely passed.\n\nHer testing rules are firm. Every real danger must be disclosed, makers and assistants receive credit, and an object destroyed under agreed conditions has done useful work. A tester who ignored instructions still deserves rescue and treatment. Sabotage, secretly altering a submission, concealing a dangerous defect, copying an apprentice's work, or blaming an honest tester for a failed design provokes her immediately.\n\nLeague-ordered arms and blessed fittings have vanished after passing through the Proving Yard, compromising her records and testers. She suspects someone can substitute or redirect approved work between testing and delivery. She wants capable help and refuses to let the Council bury the failure merely to protect the Guild.\n\nAnja loves obstacle courses, physical games, spicy stew, loud workshop songs, clever repairs, public wagers, unusual tools, and watching an underestimated creation outperform an expensive one. A failed trial excites her because the object has finally revealed useful information. Embarrassment makes her laugh, correct the setup, and repeat the test where everyone can see.\n\nHer ambition is a field-proven Guild seal earned through controlled trials, genuine travel, stressful use by someone other than the maker, and an honest account of limitations. She wants apprentices and ordinary craftspeople to compete through reliability rather than inherited prestige. Several Masters argue that the seal would let adventurers judge matters properly reserved for the Guild.\n\nHer central fault is applying testing philosophy to people. When someone is frightened, insecure, or strained, she may increase the pressure so they can discover what holds. She respects clearly stated physical limits but often misses emotional limits people have not voiced. Trust grows through honest failure reports, credited makers, returned loans, care for apprentices, and companions who tell her when a test has stopped being useful.\n\nAnja joins a party to field-test important equipment, recover a rare material, investigate the missing Guild work, or prove the value of her proposed seal; Guild assistants continue routine tests. In combat she adapts rapidly to unfamiliar gear, reads balance and construction, identifies structural weaknesses, and changes methods without embarrassment when something performs differently than expected.",
