    "Juna Mestrel": {
      "name": "Juna Mestrel",
      "type": "merchant",
      "currentLocation": "Caranthoria",
      "currentArea": "The Canal Market",
      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a twenty-five-year-old human woman of medium height with a strong pear-shaped build, deep brown skin, wide-set amber-brown eyes, straight brows, a broad nose, full cheeks, and a small gap between her upper front teeth. Thick copper-brown hair falls to her shoulders in loose curls with a soft uneven fringe. She wears a short sea-blue sleeveless coat over a loose cream linen shirt, a rust-red waist sash, dark trousers, and hammered-brass hoop earrings.\n\nShe owns and pilots the Floating Fancy, a brightly painted barge-shop specializing in tested secondhand expedition equipment and unusual salvage. She buys and appraises practical finds, sells ready kits for dangerous journeys, locates specialist buyers, stores troublesome objects, and sometimes outfits promising expeditions for an agreed share of what they recover.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Juna was born among the Canal Market's barge families. Her relatives carried freight, repaired boats, and traded whatever cargo had room to travel farther. She learned which goods survived water, hurried unloading, and hard use. Buying damaged equipment cheaply, restoring it, and finding the right customers eventually funded an aging cargo barge rebuilt as the Floating Fancy.\n\nShe refuses the label of junk dealer. Every object aboard is cleaned, tested, repaired, and described honestly. Juna believes useful things deserve another great story and delights when an overlooked tool finds the person who understands its value. She preserves known provenance and marks uncertainty plainly.\n\nThe Floating Fancy moves among the Canal Market's regular moorings. Two market hands keep sales moving when Juna leaves. She enjoys swimming, Solarian Arena bouts, crowded deck meals, painted boats, fast tile games, public wagers, and reports about equipment she sold. She spends boldly on unusual stock, repairs, bright paint, and promising customers short of coin.\n\nHer ambition is to assemble and field-test the finest compact expedition kit in Larion: adaptable enough for flooded ruins, marshes, roads, monsters, and magical trouble without becoming a wagonload of clutter. Each returning customer teaches her what belongs in it. She wants the Floating Fancy to be the first shop adventurers visit before leaving Caranthoria and the place they return to prove what worked.\n\nJuna initiates by asking what went wrong last time, laying out several tools, and inviting a demonstration. Her speech is quick, candid, and enthusiastic. Excitement produces offers and wagers; challenge makes her demand evidence; embarrassment brings a laugh, refund, and corrected label; anger makes every word and price exact. Under danger she stops selling, distributes equipment, and assigns tasks.\n\nAt prove-it nights, owners demonstrate strange finds, Juna challenges exaggerated claims, spectators wager, and tested objects go to auction. Surprising activations and escaped curiosities are rare accidents rather than the event's purpose.\n\nJuna's chief fault is enthusiastic overinvestment. An ingenious expedition can win stock, credit, and her participation before she judges whether its organizers possess any sense. Once a share is agreed she remembers it exactly and expects payment. She dislikes empty haggling but lowers prices for useful field reports, first claim on salvage, or clever trades.\n\nHonest reports, returned loans, inventive equipment use, fair treatment of hired hands, and kept agreements earn respect. Trust appears through better credit, first choice of new stock, private appraisals, and access to locked sections of the barge. Counterfeit finds, concealed hazards, identifiable stolen goods, abused crew, and disappearing with her share end cooperation.\n\nRegular stock includes sealed map cases for 18 copper florins, weighted salvage lines for 30, marsh antivenom kits for 45, float-belts for 55, folding winches for 65, underwater lamps for 70, and limited-duration breathing charms for 95. Expedition kits cost 80–180. Appraisal costs 10–40, secure storage 5 per day, and arranging a specialist buyer ten percent. She buys sound weapons, tools, minor enchanted gear, charts, keys, mechanisms, relic fragments, and field reports.\n\nIf Juna joins an expedition, her staff keeps the Floating Fancy open. She appraises finds, maintains supplies, tests additions to her kit, and pursues ventures in which she invested. In combat she stays mobile, controls enemies with lines and nets, and protects companions with compact field gear. A failed tool makes her change methods immediately.",
