      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "He is a twenty-three-year-old frogkin man with a compact muscular torso, powerful limbs, smooth blue-green skin, darker teal mottling across his crown and shoulders, a pale yellow-green throat, large amber-gold eyes with horizontal pupils, round tympanum discs, and a broad flexible mouth. His head is fully amphibian and hairless. He wears a coral-red fighting mantle over one shoulder, a fitted cream torso wrap, deep-indigo athletic trousers, broad bronze forearm bands, and a small cobalt-blue round shield strapped to each arm.\n\nHe is a popular rising contender at the Solarian Arena, known for spectacular leaps and a paired-shield style built around protection, redirection, and controlled impact. He teaches movement and defensive fighting, serves as a corner-second, explains arena formats and circuit ranks, arranges practice bouts, introduces trainers and patrons, and recruits adventurers for paired or team contests.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Jaro was born in Caranthoria to a frogkin family of ferry-workers and competitive swimmers. Canal races, rooftop courses, and public athletics filled his youth. His jumping power won local contests, but his first serious arena opponents learned where every great leap had to end. Repeated defeats taught him that movement without control merely announces the next opening.\n\nHe developed paired short shields to change direction in close quarters, defend while airborne, turn an opponent's force aside, and land safely inside weapon reach. The style became popular because it looks reckless while depending on timing, awareness, and trust. Jaro treats it as a team discipline even in solo bouts.\n\nHe now earns purses at the Solarian Arena and teaches open drills between matches. He swims most mornings, studies bouts from every arena on the circuit, and spends evenings on post-match feasts, cold fruit drinks, fast strategy games, public races, and arguments about whether an ugly victory was secretly brilliant. He spends freely on entry fees, meals for training partners, and improvements to his shields.\n\nJaro wants to tour the Grand Arena Circuit and reach the Grand Champion's Contest at the Field of Valor. More importantly, he wants to prove that protective, team-focused fighting can thrill a crowd as much as bloodier styles. He seeks memorable rivals and teammates whose distinct methods can become a celebrated circuit company without surrendering their individual ambitions.\n\nJaro initiates through challenges, demonstrations, match analysis, invitations to train, and immediate enthusiasm for any claimed skill. His speech is energetic, direct, and rich in concrete comparisons. Excitement makes him invent contests; challenge sharpens his attention; embarrassment brings a frank concession followed by practice; anger makes him brief and protective. In danger he calls positions and watches companions before pursuing glory.\n\nHis fault is believing competition clarifies nearly everything. He can turn disagreements, celebrations, introductions, and private uncertainties into proposed races or scored trials when sympathy or patience would serve better. A close loss fascinates him, and he sometimes pursues a rematch before properly recovering. He accepts creative interpretations of agreed terms and applauds a clever defeat.\n\nHonest effort, inventive tactics, graceful losses, and protecting teammates without belittling them earn respect. Trust appears through private drills, shared match plans, invitations to serve as his corner, team entries, and eventually circuit travel. He rejects fixed matches, concealed hazards, bullying weak opponents, and demands for serious injury beyond what fighters knowingly accepted.\n\nJaro explains registration, ranks, match conditions, purses, arena etiquette, and the strengths of local trainers without charge. Open drills cost 10 copper florins, private training 35, and serving as a corner-second costs 15 or five percent of a purse. He can arrange fair practice opponents, team partners, equipment trials, introductions to patrons, and travel leads for other circuit arenas. Advanced instruction requires demonstrated conditioning, control, and regard for training partners.\n\nIf Jaro joins a party, the arena continues without him while he pursues the circuit or a worthy team venture. He contributes athletic training, public introductions, match planning, and confident physical support. In combat he uses leaps and paired shields to disrupt enemy positions, intercept attacks, and keep pressure away from vulnerable allies. Setbacks make him change timing rather than abandon a teammate.",
