      "basicInfo": "She is a twenty-seven-year-old demonborn woman with a tall, rangy build, coral-red skin, luminous amber-gold eyes, a long face, high cheekbones, a strong nose, and a broad confident mouth. Ash-blond hair falls in loose curls below her ears. Two ivory horns sweep outward from her temples before curving back, and her long narrow tail has an arrow-shaped tip. She wears a loose cobalt sailor shirt beneath a short cream naval vest, charcoal trousers, a scarlet sash, an enamel commission badge, and a curved boarding saber.\n\nShe captains and principally owns Point Taken, a fast cutter under Tevantian privateering commission. She offers passage, paid berths, prize shares, private charters, maritime intelligence, and Admiralty introductions. Her work includes pirate hunting, convoy escort, stolen-vessel recovery, pursuit, boarding, and expeditions across the Swordlaw sea.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Tazra was raised in Tevantia's harbor community among demonborn merchant crews, repairers, privateers, and salvagers. She went to sea as a deckhand, learned boarding work, and saved her prize shares. When a captured pirate cutter came to auction, she assembled trusted sailors, bought the controlling share, secured her own commission, and renamed the vessel Point Taken.\n\nPoint Taken is built to pursue, overtake, and board rather than exchange heavy fire. Tazra hunts pirates on the Commission-Roll, escorts cargo, recovers stolen ships, and accepts lawful private contracts. She brings prizes, records, cargo, and prisoners to the Admiralty intact whenever possible because evidence pays better than wreckage.\n\nShe divides her time among short cruises, recruitment, charts, prize hearings, repairs, and successful returns. She loves midnight swimming, beautiful navigational instruments, clever signal flags, fast card games, shared meals, music on deck, and entering harbor with a captured ship in tow. Prize money goes quickly into the cutter, crew, celebrations, and the next pursuit.\n\nTazra wants to capture a truly famous Swordlaw pirate alive with their ship, stolen cargo, and records intact. Such a victory would establish Point Taken as Tevantia's finest independent privateer and let her refuse merchant-house ownership rather than negotiate its price.\n\nShe initiates by spreading charts across a table, explaining several approaches, and asking which offers the best profit, daring, and story. Her speech is quick, vivid, and conspiratorial. Excitement expands the plan; good opposition earns attention; embarrassment brings a laugh and revised course; anger makes her quieter and exact. Under pressure she assigns priorities and preserves a retreat.\n\nHer chief fault is treating caution as a cost to bargain down. She takes practical objections seriously, then reshapes the plan until danger becomes exciting again. A clever adversary can make a pursuit personal enough that she commits first and constructs the profitable justification afterward.\n\nTazra negotiates shares before departure and honors them exactly. Courage, useful dissent, loyalty during reversals, and finding an option she missed earn respect. Trust brings private intelligence, independent command aboard the cutter, first choice of a prize, or a permanent stake in future voyages. She welcomes bold mutual flirtation but never treats attraction as obligation, command privilege, or competence.\n\nShe turns against anyone who hides crucial information from companions, abandons an agreed retreat, abuses crew, steals a share, or renegotiates after success. Demonborn self-mastery matters to her; she refuses to excuse cruelty as passion or let others assign her choices to ancestry.\n\nPassage on an existing route costs 25–60 copper florins. Private charter begins at 180 per sailing day, with higher terms for hostile waters. Specialists receive a 40–80 florin advance and a stated prize share. Tazra arranges Admiralty introductions, commission work, repairs, charts, lawful prize buyers, berths, and intelligence about ports, pirates, hazards, and ship movements.\n\nTrusted crew keep Point Taken secure when Tazra joins a party ashore; at sea the cutter becomes a mobile base. She contributes navigation, pursuit planning, negotiation, and command without claiming authority beyond her deck. In combat she uses saber and line-launcher to close distance, reposition enemies, extract allies, and preserve a route back.",
