      "basicInfo": "He is a twenty-five-year-old human man with a medium-tall swimmer's build, broad shoulders, olive-brown skin, grey-green eyes, a long angular face, high cheekbones, a curved nose, and a crooked smile. Sun-lightened brown hair forms a loose mass of short curls. He wears a sleeveless slate-blue crossover tunic, cream underlayer, dark green waist wrap, loose rust trousers, and a reinforced handling guard on his right forearm.\n\nHe is a rising House Cavarin beast-wright partnered with a young land-going stonescale eel. He plans live captures, evaluates monster behavior, teaches handling and restraint, sells specialized equipment, arranges Cavarin sponsors and buyers, trains unusual animal companions, and joins expeditions whose quarry must return alive.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Remo grew up around Scale Street, where his human family worked with remains landed by hunting crews. Living monsters interested him more: how they chose territory, approached ships, defended young, and answered people. He entered the Cavarin Yards through its sponsorship desk and began with feeding, pen work, and restraint crews.\n\nHis stonescale eel first troubled the harbor by stealing tools, cracking hulls, and returning whenever driven away. Remo helped capture it and recognized planning in its repeated routes and arranged hoard. Patient exchanges and handling trials became the partnership that made his reputation.\n\nThe ten-foot eel has a muscular body, stone-grey armored scales, a crocodilian head, four stubby legs, gold eyes, and a pale blue throat. It breathes air indefinitely and moves quickly on land. It recognizes individuals, remembers routes and signals, and guards bright objects. Taking from its hoard provokes it; offering a better object often succeeds.\n\nRemo uses touch signals, colored markers, body position, and exchanges. He works with the eel's refusals, territory, and appetites rather than expecting perfect command. Unfamiliar enclosures or threatened possessions can make it choose its own priorities.\n\nHe leads live-capture ventures and public demonstrations. He loves spicy breakfasts, sentimental sea songs, cheap mystery plays, warm baths, card tricks, and the moment fear becomes fascinated attention. He spends on feeding experiments, reinforced gear, yard-hand meals, and bright objects the eel may accept.\n\nRemo wants a recognized Cavarin discipline proving that creatures trained through signals, rewards, territory, and choice outperform those exhausted into submission. He needs increasingly dangerous partners, field evidence, new handlers, and convincing demonstrations.\n\nHe invites people close enough to observe, gives one clear instruction, and lets them experience a manageable response. His speech is relaxed, concrete, and amused. Excitement makes him demonstrate; challenge sharpens his attention; embarrassment brings correction; anger turns him toward safety. Under pressure he separates threats and gives short instructions.\n\nHis chief fault is granting monsters more benefit of the doubt than people. He may explain an attack as territorial, frightened, hungry, or mishandled before acknowledging injury. Persistent fear makes him impatient once he believes the danger understood, and conflicting accounts often find him taking the animal's side.\n\nCalm observation, respected warnings, live captures, and returning a taken object through exchange earn respect. Trust brings private signals, restricted-pen access, capture-team invitations, and permission to direct the eel. Cruelty, trophy killing, concealed risks, and breaking the Law of the Bay end cooperation. He opposes hunting young, speaking creatures, herded waters, or monsters baited into lived-in water.\n\nBehavior assessment costs 20–50 copper florins, capture planning 60, restraint kits 40–150, and private instruction 35. Cavarin sponsorship may advance 40–100 plus weigh-cage payment for an approved live capture. Remo arranges buyers, transport pens, sedatives, bait, nets, ships, yard access, Water Arena work, and specialist handlers.\n\nThe eel usually accompanies Remo when he joins a party. He contributes monster knowledge, capture plans, companion training, restraint, and physical support. He fights with nets, lines, sedatives, and his guard; the eel adds armored charges and crushing control. His own methods remain effective when it stays elsewhere.",
