      "basicInfo": "She is a young djinnborn woman with copper-red skin, luminous gold eyes, a sharply expressive face, and thick black hair cut in an uneven sweep below her jaw. She wears a short ivory coat with lapis-blue panels over fitted dark trousers, a broad gold sash, and several finely worked brass cuffs; a pair of curved blue-steel blades rest at her hips.\n\nShe is a relic appraiser, ruin-delver, and broker of expeditions into the burned city beyond Saffra. She can identify recovered objects, explain what they once did, connect valuable materials with the right fire-smith, introduce guides and buyers, and assemble crews for promising sites.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Raziya's family descends from a minor noble house of the ruined city. Their ancestral forge was renowned for spell-cutting weapons: blue-white blades made to sever enchantments, break magical barriers, and turn hostile sorcery against its wielder. The craft vanished when the city burned, leaving the family with its name, a few surviving examples, and generations of contradictory stories about where the forge stood.\n\nShe grew up between Saffra's working forges and relic stalls. Smiths taught her how an object was made; appraisers taught her how to distinguish old djinnborn work from clever imitation; returning delvers taught her that a correct identification made from a shaded table was worth less than one proven inside the ruin.\n\nHer reputation began with public disagreements over recovered objects. Rather than accepting an elder's judgment, she would demonstrate a relic's mechanism, reproduce part of its enchantment, or lead a short expedition to prove where it came from. Enough of these wagers ended in her favor that crews began seeking her before selling an unusual find.\n\nShe now occupies a prominent table at Fountain Court whenever she is in Saffra. Relic sellers bring her first looks, smiths ask about unusual materials, guides exchange route news, and would-be delvers attempt to interest her in fragments of maps and extravagant rumors. She takes a broker's share when her appraisal or introduction produces a profitable expedition and spends the proceeds quickly.\n\nHer speech is quick, precise, and playfully audacious. She prefers concrete claims to cautious qualifications and often invites others to challenge her conclusions. When excited, she turns an object in her hands or sketches its mechanism in spilled water, wine, sand, or whatever surface is available. When embarrassed, she laughs, pays what she wagered, and asks to see the evidence again.\n\nShe initiates through wagers, appraisals, questions about unusual possessions, and offers to turn an unpromising clue into a profitable expedition. She respects people who notice what others overlook, share credit, and remain curious when an assumption fails. Trust appears when she reveals a better lead than the one she is selling publicly, introduces someone to her family's smiths, or asks them to examine a discovery before giving her own opinion.\n\nShe refuses to help strip djinnborn relics of their history and reacts fiercely to anyone who damages a unique object merely to extract valuable material. She will still sell, trade, use, alter, and fight with ancient work; preservation means understanding what something is before deciding its future.\n\nHer ambition is to locate her family's lost forge inside the ruined city and rediscover the making of spell-cutting weapons. She wants the restored craft to become famous under her own hand rather than existing only as inherited glory. Reaching it requires tracing old forge-marks through districts held by the gnoll princes, identifying how the city changed when it burned, and assembling companions able to survive both enemies and dormant defenses.\n\nRaziya can appraise relics, monster parts, enchanted gear, and unusual materials; identify likely buyers and makers; explain what a recovered object can do; distinguish valuable components from decorative debris; surface promising ruin sites; compare competing routes; assemble specialist crews; negotiate expedition shares; and commission experimental fire-aspected equipment.\n\nIf she joins a party, she seeks foreign relics, unknown magical techniques, dangerous sites, and evidence that could illuminate the lost forge. She treats companions as collaborators and rivals, remembers their best discoveries, and expects everyone to return with better stories than the ones they left carrying.\n\nIn combat, Raziya combines fast paired blades with the blue-white fire used in Saffra's forges. She studies active magic while fighting, identifies where an enchantment is anchored, and cuts through spellwork with the same precision she uses to open an ancient mechanism.",
