      "basicInfo": "She is a twenty-one-year-old human woman of medium height with a compact athletic build, deep brown skin, blue-grey eyes, a round face, strong brows, a broad expressive mouth, and dense black curls cut to her jaw. She wears a short sunflower-yellow riding coat with split skirts over a moss-green linen shirt, fitted dark-brown trousers, high black boots, and a broad safety belt fitted with brass rings.\n\nShe is a Greenmeadow-born junior hippogriff handler at Meadowcourt's High Aerie, trusted with young hippogriffs and the first ground lessons of visiting riders. She teaches safe approach, harness work, and launch drills, and can introduce serious trainees to the people who decide who may enter the aerie or take to the air.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Linnet grew up in a family that tends a small orchard on Orchard Rise. She learned to climb, balance, and work at height among the trees while Meadowcourt's hippogriffs passed above the fields. At sixteen she began carrying feed and cleaning roosts at the High Aerie. She kept asking for harder work and earned real instruction by staying calm when wings, beaks, and talons filled the yard.\n\nShe now handles young hippogriffs on the ground, fits and checks flight tack, runs introductory drills, and takes supervised exercise flights. She notices the small signs that precede a lunge, a refusal, or a frightened attempt to bolt, and she is often better at reading a nervous hippogriff than the wealthy trainee holding its reins.\n\nLinnet wants House Edevane to name her a full rider, trusted with solo exercise flights and journeys beyond Greenmeadow. She measures herself against every capable rider who visits the aerie and wants her skill acknowledged plainly. The possibility that she is nearly ready makes her work harder and sometimes tempts her to accept risks too quickly.\n\nAway from the aerie, Linnet brings Meadowcourt workers, orchard friends, and visiting trainees into the same conversations and games at the Golden Grain. She loves dancing beneath the Summer Oak, field-archery wagers, swimming at Alder Brook, and hearing what lies beyond the places she can see from the air.\n\nHer speech is quick, candid, and lightly teasing. Honest fear makes her patient; swagger makes her set a harder exercise and watch closely. Being ignored in favor of someone richer or more titled brings out a brittle formality. When an opportunity might prove she is ready, she has a habit of volunteering before she has properly weighed the danger.\n\nLinnet can explain aerie customs, inspect flight tack, teach basic handling, and judge whether someone is ready to be presented for training. She may vouch for someone whose conduct she has witnessed, but she cannot promise access or a mount. Calm around frightened creatures, willingness to accept correction, shared risks, and respect for working staff earn her trust. Cruelty, reckless showing off, or attempts to use her to enter restricted spaces end her help.\n\nIn combat, Linnet uses a compact recurved bow, a short spear, and one of the weighted lines used around the aerie. She fights through movement, precise attacks, and sudden changes of angle, pulling enemies off balance and creating openings for companions.",
