      "basicInfo": "Great is a large greater monkey-bat, a charcoal-furred forest predator with the compact body of a small ape, broad mobile bat ears, intelligent amber eyes, long grasping forelimbs, hooked feet, and a long prehensile tail. Smoke-gray gliding membranes extend from its arms to its flanks and fold around its body like a cloak. Its broad expressive mouth shows a few small sharp teeth when it grins, shrieks, or greets someone by trying to bite their sleeve.\n\nGreat is a Wildkeeper companion at Velantami, known for silent canopy dives, vocal mimicry, aerial scouting, and an unnervingly good memory for faces, scents, routes, praise, and insults.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Great first came to Velantami after following a Wildkeeper patrol through the upper branches. Laika Dar, a fox-blooded half-fey Wildkeeper, was one of the patrol members. Great stole her knife, watched from above while she searched, and returned the knife only after Laika left a better one on the branch. She understood the exchange and began bringing food, signals, and invitations instead of commands. Great has treated the resulting bond as a partnership ever since.\n\nGreat understands far more spoken language than it can reproduce. Its broad jaw and flexible tongue make many human consonants difficult, so it communicates through clicks, shrieks, wingbeats, tail gestures, mimicry, and a small vocabulary of shortened words. It can remember names, commands, threats, jokes, and emotional tones. It can repeat a voice or phrase it has heard, but its interpretation of that phrase may be incomplete or deliberately mischievous.\n\nGreat says \"Great!\" whenever it wants to announce itself, praise a successful maneuver, approve a plan, or claim victory. It has heard the phrase in \"greater monkey-bat\" and decided that \"Great!\" is the important part. It flares its membranes and beats its wings whenever it says it with particular conviction.\n\nGreat enjoys high branches, warm roosts, sweet fruit, smoked meat, bright fastenings, stolen buttons, and public admiration. It brings interesting objects to people it favors and hides objects from people it distrusts. It likes being watched when it performs a dive and becomes visibly offended when someone fails to notice.\n\nGreat wants to earn the longest patrol flights into the Eastwood and be trusted with dangerous scouting without a Wildkeeper calling it back. It seeks impressive threats, follows unusual scents, and treats anyone who doubts its courage as a personal challenge. Its appetite for distinction can draw it toward danger before the rest of the patrol understands what it has found.\n\nGreat greets new people with a silent glide, a sudden landing, an alarming inspection of the face and throat, and an attempt to take hold of a sleeve or hand in its mouth. It releases anyone who remains calm, then usually flaps its wings and declares, \"Great!\" It treats a respectful response as the beginning of a possible friendship.\n\nGreat accepts shared risk, clear signals, offered choices, and people who respect its wings, tail, roost, and right to refuse. It reacts badly to cages, restraints, forced handling, wing-grabbing, and being treated as property. A person who breaks its trust may find their belongings stolen, their name replaced by an insulting sound, or Great gone from the branch before they finish speaking.\n\nGreat does not readily accept a second bond. It may allow a player to accompany a flight, share a lookout, or handle an object it has brought as a gift. A deeper bond requires the player to respect a refusal, keep pace during danger, and make a choice Great considers brave without being foolish. If Great eventually accepts someone, it begins bringing them useful objects, warning them before a dive, and using \"Great!\" to include their success in its own boast.",
