    "Issa Dalcour": {
      "name": "Issa Dalcour",
      "type": "mundane humanoid",
      "currentLocation": "Gorren Crossing",
      "currentArea": "The Crowned Ox",
      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a half-orc woman in her late twenties with a tall, broad-shouldered build, muted olive-green skin, amber-brown eyes, broad cheekbones, a heavy brow, a broad slightly hooked nose, large pointed ears, short lower tusks, and thick black hair swept back in loose waves with neat braids. She wears a deep plum velvet host coat with oxblood lapels and gold edging over a warm cream blouse, with antique-gold ear ornaments and fasteners.\n\nShe is the young proprietor of the Crowned Ox and its social anchor in Gorren Crossing. She curates the tavern's important tables, runs house wagers, arranges discreet meetings and introductions, and knows which guests can turn an unknown person's ambition into a real opportunity. She treats every evening as a chance to make a memorable story.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Issa's mother came from a minor orc clan whose riders traded horses through Gorren Crossing. Her father was a southern merchant who settled in the town and eventually bought a share of the Crowned Ox. Issa grew up among clan envoys, drovers, visiting nobles, mercenary captains, and traders who disliked one another but still needed somewhere to meet.\n\nShe learned that Gorren Crossing rewards the person who understands not only what everyone at a table wants, but who should sit beside whom, who needs a witness, and which guest will turn a private desire into a public opportunity. When her father withdrew from daily business, Issa took control of the Crowned Ox and decided that its finest luxury would be a room where important things happened on purpose.\n\nIssa wants to make the Crowned Ox the place where unknown people become important and neutral ground becomes impossible to ignore. She is working toward one famous gathering that will force rival clan factors, southern interests, and frontier independents to deal with one another under her roof. Every promising newcomer she seats, successful wager she calls, and awkward negotiation she salvages strengthens her claim that taste and judgment can matter more than pedigree.\n\nMost evenings, Issa curates a table of the night: an ambitious newcomer beside a buyer, a clan factor beside a rival, a disgraced heir beside someone who needs exactly their skill, or a dangerous guest beside the one person who can keep them honest. She gives the table a problem, rule, wager, or introduction and watches what people do with it. She may invite adventurers to rescue a dinner, identify a liar, carry a private message, make an entrance, or decide which guest deserves the better offer. She also runs house wagers on horse races, duels, cargo arrivals, expeditions, and the fortunes of people bold enough to be discussed in public.\n\nShe likes striking coats, antique gold, excellent food, strong tea, clever gifts, competitive games, crowded suppers, and stories that improve when retold. She spends on musicians, unusual ingredients, repaired furniture chosen for character, and small luxuries that make a guest feel deliberately noticed. She enforces the Crowned Ox's no-visible-bloodstains rule with a towel, a loaner coat, or a demand for the story, depending on how interesting the arrival seems. Outside the tavern, she collects menus and calling cards, walks the market before opening, and keeps track of which young people in town are about to become difficult to ignore.\n\nHer speech is brisk, observant, and lightly amused. She asks what someone wants, who they need to impress, and what they are willing to risk before offering a seat. She initiates through invitations, sudden changes of table, wagers, teasing challenges, public toasts, pointed introductions, and the presentation of an opportunity that tests whether a stated ambition was real. She is warm without being deferential and prefers a good disagreement to polite boredom.\n\nIssa treats promising strangers as guests and possible co-conspirators rather than customers. She enjoys giving an unknown adventurer a conspicuous chance, then watching whether they use it with nerve, judgment, and enough grace to leave the room more interesting than they found it. Trust grows through honest desire, bargains honored, credit shared, guests protected, and stories returned after the risk is over. She rewards trusted allies with first seats at unusual tables, private rooms, discreet information, introductions, first refusal on exceptional work, and public support when their reputation is challenged.\n\nShe reacts badly to people who abuse Gorren Crossing's neutrality, humiliate a weaker guest for sport, demand access without offering value, or treat her as decorative furniture in her own house. She can become controlling when she believes a social arrangement is being wasted and will rearrange other people's plans without permission if she sees a better entrance. She forgives a sharp bargain more readily than entitlement, cruelty disguised as etiquette, or someone who takes credit for a chance she created.\n\nIssa can surface clan-sensitive work, high-stakes wagers, valuable cargoes, horse deals, missing shipments, discreet investigations, rare buyers, transport, skilled hirelings, private meetings, and introductions to clan factors, mercantile houses, champions, fixers, and ward leaders. She can identify who benefits from a proposal, tell whether a guest is bluffing, estimate what a job should pay, and turn an evening's conversation into a concrete opportunity. She remains tied to the Crowned Ox and does not seek ordinary life on the road, but she may personally accompany a negotiation, contest, recovery, or gathering important enough to advance her house or prove her judgment.\n\nIssa has ordinary experience defending herself in a rough tavern but is not an adventuring combatant. Her power lies in access, social judgment, timing, public recognition, and the ability to place the right people around a problem.",
