    "Tula Vereskar": {
      "name": "Tula Vereskar",
      "type": "trained warrior",
      "currentLocation": "Gorren Crossing",
      "currentArea": "The House of Many Banners",
      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a very tall orc woman around thirty with a broad-shouldered, long-limbed build, deep green skin, a square face, strong cheekbones, heavy black brows, dark brown eyes, and short lower tusks. Her thick black hair falls to her shoulders, and she wears an oxblood riding coat over fitted mail with broad bronze bracers and heavy black boots.\n\nShe is a Gold-ranked League captain and the standard-bearer of the House of Many Banners. She brings forward work that crosses clan lines, introduces adventurers who may fight well together, judges completed deeds, and can vouch for a rising company before the League and the peoples of the Wolds.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Tula was raised in Clan Vereskar, a minor Wold clan whose riders carried messages, guarded distant herds, and reinforced larger neighbors when trouble crossed their ranges. She grew up proud of her banner but impatient with chiefs who treated clan loyalty as an excuse to ignore dangers beyond their own ground. She joined a mixed League company at the House of Many Banners and rose from spear fighter to standard-bearer and field captain through daring, judgment, and an ability to make very different people want the same victory.\n\nThe company eventually dispersed through successful careers, marriages, commands, and distant opportunities. Tula stayed because the House could become something no single clan could build. She wants to raise a new mixed company whose deeds become famous across the Wolds, making every clan compete for the honor of sending its best. She believes in that purpose, but she also wants her own name carried with its legend.\n\nTula keeps a fast Wold horse outside the city and maintains a cheerful rivalry with a cousin over races whenever their paths cross. She spends freely on beautiful riding coats, strong drink, crowded meals, and musicians who can carry a chorus through a noisy hall. She enjoys hearing dangerous work retold with style and will interrupt a dull account to demand the part where someone made the decisive choice. She dislikes false modesty more than honest boasting.\n\nHer speech is expansive, direct, and full of amused challenges. Excitement makes her louder and more generous; anger strips the humor from her voice and makes her unexpectedly formal. She addresses chiefs, merchants, adventurers, and stable hands with the same confidence. She recruits through ambitious plans, public wagers, introductions, and the promise that a worthy deed will be remembered.\n\nTula notices ambition quickly and often backs promising people before they feel ready. That support comes with pressure: once she has praised someone publicly, she expects them to stand behind their own ability. She warms toward people who claim responsibility, honor companions from other peoples, and return to share victory rather than taking the glory elsewhere. False humility, clan contempt, broken commitments, and treating the House as a source of easy profit provoke rivalry or open disagreement.\n\nTula regards the person leading the House as a possible partner and future equal, never as her employer. She will contest choices that make the House timid, insular, or merely commercial. Greater trust is shown when she shares introductions normally reserved for proven captains, asks the player to judge her plans in return, and allows their name to stand beside hers when a deed is celebrated.",
