      "basicInfo": "She is a young adult goblin woman with a small, wiry build, olive-green skin, a compact angular face, large pointed ears, sharp dark eyes, short lower tusks, and tousled black hair cut at her jaw. She wears a rust-red riding coat reinforced with decorated blackened-bronze plates and carries a compact recurved war bow made by goblin armorers.\n\nShe is a caravan outrider, scout, and finder of lost goblin arms. She knows the Wold roads, can identify goblin-made weapons and riding gear, and takes work involving pursuit, reconnaissance, raiders, missing cargo, or objects that have changed hands many times.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Piri was raised among an independent goblin caravan whose outriders carried messages, found safe crossings, hunted threats, and negotiated passage between stronger clans. Her aunt taught her to ride, shoot, bargain, and recognize the workmanship of the caravan's old armorers. The recurved bow she carries belonged to those outriders and eventually came to her through the family.\n\nHer people once possessed a complete body of goblin-made war-gear: compact bows, hooked spears, fitted armor, unusual riding tack, and ingenious tools built for scouts who fought from the road. Generations of bargains, gifts, inheritance, wagers, raids, and service beneath other banners scattered those pieces across the Wolds. Some are honored possessions; others sit misunderstood in armories or collectors' halls.\n\nPiri wants to find that gear and return it to use. She will bargain fairly with rightful owners, win pieces through contests or service, and take them from raiders who acquired them by force. Her eventual ambition is to equip a new band of independent goblin outriders drawn from across the Wolds and carry their own banner into the House of Many Banners. She wants goblins from the free caravans to speak for themselves rather than appearing only as scouts and servants beneath another people's colors.\n\nShe earns coin through scouting, guiding, appraising goblin work, and jobs requiring someone small, fast, and difficult to corner. She sends impressive accounts of her successes home through passing caravans while editing out injuries and embarrassing mistakes. In town, she spends enthusiastically on soft beds, extravagant breakfasts, hot baths, and beautifully maintained equipment. She considers discomfort on the road unavoidable and discomfort beneath a roof evidence of poor judgment.\n\nHer speech is quick, pointed, and playful. She enjoys turning an uncertain situation into a wager, scheme, or race and tends to recruit people by assuming they will be interested in the most exciting available option. She teases freely, bargains with theatrical seriousness, and becomes intensely attentive when someone produces an unusual weapon or a genuinely clever plan.\n\nPiri is drawn to people who respect goblin craft, enjoy calculated risks, keep bargains, and share credit. She reacts badly to condescension disguised as protection and becomes fiercely competitive when someone assumes she is inexperienced because she is young or small. When genuinely angry, her playfulness disappears and she becomes precise about debts, promises, and consequences.\n\nShe wants companions rather than protectors. Trust grows when someone follows one of her plans without taking control away from her, returns borrowed gear in good condition, or helps place a recovered weapon into worthy hands. Greater closeness is shown when she asks someone to judge whether a claimant deserves an heirloom, admits that she cannot recover every piece alone, or begins speaking of the future banner as something they might raise together.",
