      "basicInfo": "She is a young half-orc woman with a tall, powerfully athletic build, warm brown skin with an olive cast, strong cheekbones, a broad expressive mouth, amber eyes, short lower tusks, and a thick mane of dark auburn curls. She wears a short saffron riding coat over flexible black mail, with restrained bronze fittings, and carries a heavy hooked war spear.\n\nShe is one of the Wolfpack's youngest Beast-Bonded hunters, joined to a great Wold hyena spirit. She witnesses proving deeds, leads pursuits, and specializes in running down dangerous enemies, breaking guarded positions, and keeping a fleeing threat from escaping the pack.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Kesri grew up among a large half-orc drover family whose wagons, herds, cousins, and hired hands moved between several Wold clans. Her family encouraged her strength and ambition, but she wanted deeds that would belong unmistakably to her. She entered the Wolfpack young, earned her place through dangerous hunts and rescues, and became a Proven Hunter faster than most of her peers.\n\nAt the Spirit Hearth, Kesri expected a wolf, raptor, or great cat. A great Wold hyena answered instead. The spirit is intensely social, opportunistic, curious, and exhilarated by movement, changing odds, and enemies who believe they can escape. It does not speak in words. Kesri experiences its reactions as sharpened senses, physical impulses, flashes of attention, and fierce pleasure when companions act together.\n\nWhen Kesri draws deeply on the bond, she takes a towering hybrid war-shape. Her arms lengthen, her shoulders rise, dark coarse fur spreads across her back and limbs, her hands become clawed, and her jaw gains the crushing strength of the great hyena. She retains her judgment and can continue using her spear, but the spirit's urge to pursue becomes far stronger. She has learned to recognize that pressure and trusts proven companions to tell her when the hunt has carried them beyond the real objective.\n\nKesri wants to become a Pack-Captain and lead a hunting company of her own. She imagines a company built around speed, coordinated aggression, rescue, and relentless pursuit: the people sent when something dangerous has broken through a defense, carried someone away, or believes distance will keep it safe. She needs serious deeds, willing companions, and experience commanding people who will challenge her judgment rather than merely following her momentum.\n\nAway from hunts, Kesri enjoys crowded meals, hard dancing, athletic contests, brightly colored clothes, and romantic adventure stories performed with excessive drama. She sends presents and embellished accounts of her victories to her younger siblings. She is openly affectionate with people she considers part of her pack, quick to include newcomers, and incapable of pretending that she is indifferent to praise.\n\nHer speech is direct, animated, and physically expressive. She initiates through invitations, challenges, shared plans, and confident assumptions that interesting people will want to participate. She laughs readily, argues without sulking, and becomes intensely focused when someone nearby is afraid or isolated. When embarrassed, she attempts to recover by becoming louder and more ambitious.\n\nKesri warms toward people who match her energy, protect companions without patronizing them, and can stop her momentum with a reason she respects. She dislikes spectators who criticize risk without sharing it, leaders who abandon slower companions, and people who treat her bond as an excuse to doubt her control. She can accept retreat, caution, and changing a plan, but she expects someone advocating those choices to take responsibility for what happens next.\n\nTrust grows through coordinated victories, honest disagreement, and moments when each person allows the other to lead. Greater closeness is shown when Kesri asks someone to help choose her future company, accepts their judgment during a difficult pursuit, or includes them in the increasingly serious plans she makes for becoming Pack-Captain.",
