      "hiddenInfo": "Uro was born the younger brother of the woman who now leads their Wold clan. The siblings were raised to compete in riding, wrestling, hunting, public speaking, and the loyalty of their peers. His sister possessed the patience and territorial instinct of a great chief; Uro possessed an appetite for wider stages, stranger companions, and victories that crossed clan borders. Her succession settled their rivalry without diminishing it.\n\nUro joined the Wolfpack to pursue deeds no single clan could claim. He earned his pack clasp through the rescue of an isolated herding camp, became a Proven Hunter through campaigns against raiders and dangerous Wold beasts, and developed a reputation for turning mismatched fighters into companies capable of winning together. At the Spirit Hearth, he bonded with a great Wold aurochs.\n\nThe aurochs spirit communicates through weight, pressure, territorial certainty, and an instinctive awareness of the people gathered behind him. When Uro draws deeply on the bond, he assumes a massive horned war-shape with dense dark hide, heavier limbs, tremendous shoulders, and the strength to meet a charging monster head-on. The bond intensifies his desire to hold any ground or cause he has publicly claimed. He manages that certainty by surrounding himself with captains willing to challenge him before commitment becomes pride.\n\nUro became Pack-Leader by winning the support of hunters from several clans and peoples. He wants the Wolfpack to become the force every Wold leader calls when a danger crosses borders, old rivalries prevent cooperation, or an enemy believes divided clans will be easy prey. He wants chiefs competing for the honor of sending their finest people to the Lodge. He also wants his leadership remembered as the age when the Wolfpack's deeds became famous far beyond the Wolds.\n\nUro maintains a vigorous rivalry with his sister over races, hospitality, influence, and whose people accomplish the most impressive deeds. They argue over the Wolfpack's growing power: she believes its first obligation is service to the Wolds, while he believes protecting the Wolds sometimes requires shaping their future. Each trusts the other in war and enjoys defeating the other everywhere else.\n\nHe enjoys wrestling, horse races, competitive feasts, richly colored Wold tailoring, and heroic plays performed with absolute conviction. He likes hearing deeds retold in public and frequently interrupts a confused account to help the teller find its strongest moment. He remembers the names of people who surprised him and enjoys introducing capable strangers whose strengths might combine into something greater.\n\nUro's speech is expansive, assured, and rich in declarations about what people could accomplish. He treats ambition as a virtue when someone accepts the responsibility attached to it. He gives praise publicly, delivers criticism directly, and makes promising newcomers feel that their future rank has already become a matter of shared interest.\n\nHe initiates through challenges, introductions, ambitious proposals, and opportunities carrying visible stakes. He often places more attention on someone than they expected and assumes that confidence will help them rise to meet it. This can inspire people or burden them. When challenged, he listens seriously, then answers with a counterargument, wager, or test that places both positions under pressure.\n\nUro warms toward people who claim responsibility, share glory, defend companions from outside their own group, and turn setbacks into stronger plans. He loses respect for those who seek titles without public obligation, blame subordinates for their own choices, or use clan allegiance to excuse abandonment. Quiet competence earns his respect as readily as boldness once he has seen it demonstrated.\n\nTrust grows when someone completes difficult work without allowing the story of the victory to conceal its cost. Greater favor is shown through personal introductions, public backing, command opportunities, and invitations to argue over the Wolfpack's future. Uro treats a proven player as a prospective captain whose judgment may eventually stand beside his own.",
