      "basicInfo": "Davor is a giantborn man in his early thirties, more than seven feet tall, with a massive long-limbed build, deep brown skin, a broad heavy-boned face, heavy brows, dark copper eyes, and close-cropped black hair. He wears a sleeveless scarlet wool host's coat over a dark charcoal work shirt, with a plain gold earring and a broad leather belt.\n\nHe owns the Harpoon House and formerly worked as a deck harpooner. He won the Water Arena championship before leaving regular bouts. He now brings ships, captains, and skilled newcomers together, and knows which crews need hands, what dangers they face, and whether the offered shares are fair.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Davor was born in Coriva to a giantborn family whose strength found steady work in shipyards, freight crews, and the monster trade. He grew up around hunting ships and entered the trade as a line-hand and deck harpooner. His size let him hold against creatures that could drag several humans across a deck, but his reputation came from timing and judgment: knowing when to give a serpent line, when to haul, and when to cut it before the creature took the ship with it.\n\nHe fought in the Water Arena between hunts and eventually won its championship. Prize money and hunting shares gave him enough to buy the Harpoon House. He keeps the old championship harpoon behind the bar as house history and takes it down only when the Harpoon House itself is threatened. He keeps the tavern as a meeting place for crews, house agents, young hunters, and anyone looking for honest work at sea.\n\nDavor is loud and generous in public. He feeds crews, makes introductions, and turns a newcomer's boast into a clear test or offer. He enjoys celebration and honest competition, but he cares most about whether danger, credit, and payment are divided fairly. He wants an independent crew formed through the Harpoon House to win Coriva's great yearly hunt without wearing Tessaro or Cavarin colors. He believes both houses produce great hunters, but their rivalry leaves too many capable people treated as hired hands.\n\nHis speech is plain and direct. He asks what someone can do, what danger they want, and what share would make them board a ship. Excitement makes him laugh and push a conversation forward; real anger makes his voice lower and his words exact.\n\nDavor can identify open berths suited to a player's class, abilities, and equipment; explain the target, captain, expected danger, offered share, and terms; arrange an arena or hunting trial; warn against captains with poor records; and introduce successful hunters to house agents, sponsors, craftspeople, and other crews. 