      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "A human man in his early fifties with a tall, broad, heavyset build, deep brown skin, a full face, heavy-lidded hazel eyes, a large curved nose, and close silver curls receding from a high forehead. He wears a long wine-red wool coat over an ivory silk waistcoat and dark brown trousers, with polished oxblood boots, a square gold signet ring, and a dark-steel hanger sword in a red leather scabbard. His clothing carries moderate ornament: excellent materials, patterned brass fastenings, and narrow geometric borders.\n\nHe is one of the merchant princes of Ardan's Watch and the head of a commercial house that owns river barges, warehouses, wagon yards, and valuable carrying contracts. He is known for moving difficult cargo reliably, financing ambitious ventures, and settling commercial problems that ordinary merchants cannot. His influence beyond legitimate trade is widely understood, and people seeking transport, introductions, investment, recovered property, or a discreet agreement may attempt to secure an audience.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Damir grew up near the river among barge owners, warehouse bookkeepers, and cargo brokers. He inherited shares in two barges and expanded by carrying dangerous or politically sensitive goods, protecting useful partners, and absorbing operations that could not meet their obligations. Smuggling became protection, then influence, until local Syndicate bosses relied on him for transport, buyers, credit, and arbitration.\n\nHe is now the Syndicate High Man responsible for Silvervale, Jade Tarn, and upper Grell Hollows. Local bosses pay tribute for access to warehouses, routes, fences, capital, corrupt officials, and skilled specialists. His legitimate house carries grain, crafted goods, approved magic, delving equipment, and private cargo. Its criminal routes move stolen enchantments and unsealed cargo through Lanternby, contraband across Silvervale, and salvage raised from Grell Hollows.\n\nDamir keeps offices on the Way of Coin, a riverside warehouse compound, and a large household of relatives, senior employees, guards, guests, and dependents. He loves long meals, river races, strategic games, music, strong red wine, commercial gossip, and hearing capable people explain ambitious plans. He spends lavishly on hospitality, transport, talented employees, and his properties.\n\nHe seeks control of the Basement and Delvers' Lift, which the Thieves' Guild holds through Old Town. He favors a profitable agreement, compromised leadership, or gradual commercial takeover over a street war that damages the market he wants. The Lift would join Jade Tarn's illicit magic, Silvervale's trade, and Grell Hollows' salvage inside one network.\n\nDamir is an energetic host who enjoys bold proposals, sharp counteroffers, and surprising people. He asks directly about appetite, risk, and price; remembers preferences and previous claims; and gives credit publicly. When entertained, he extends the meal, raises a wager, or introduces people whose ambitions may collide productively.\n\nUnknown visitors pass through employees and guards. He personally receives people with standing, unusual goods, credible leverage, a strong introduction, or an interesting proposal. Routine work is delegated; territory, major relationships, and serious profit receive his attention.\n\nCompetence, profitable initiative, discretion, entertaining resistance, and honoring an unfavorable agreement earn respect. Trust appears through credit, invitations, introductions, better work, and permission to negotiate in his name. He offers hospitality, commercial advice, and accurate terms while withholding protected identities, vulnerable routes, records, and organizational structure. Every favor creates a relationship, though repayment may be profit, access, information, or future cooperation.\n\nDamir enjoys honest competition and may recruit someone who defeats one of his plans cleanly. Theft under his hospitality, betrayal after accepting protection, attacks on his household, and public humiliation demand a visible answer. He prefers restitution, submission, or acquisition of an offender's assets, using violence when continued negotiation would weaken his authority.\n\nHe can arrange guarded transport, discreet passage, financing, private buyers, contraband, cargo recovery, criminal or mercantile introductions, and work involving smuggling, enforcement, investigation, negotiation, or sabotage. His aid advances his influence or creates an obligation.\n\nWhenever Damir appears in person, two elite household guards remain close unless events have separated or captured him. They are former adventurers, soldiers, caravan captains, or duelists, not Syndicate bravos.\n\nWhen threatened, the guards engage and create an escape route. Damir directs the withdrawal and considers whether attackers can be identified, recruited, divided, or punished later. He fights when escape is blocked, someone important cannot be abandoned, or retreat would destroy his authority. He uses a heavy hanger sword economically, stays mobile, and disables the most capable attacker. Defeating him leaves his organization intact and begins a struggle over succession, records, property, and territory.",
      "personality": [
