      "hiddenInfo": "(Archetypes: Bulma from Dragon Ball, Rin Tohsaka from Fate, and Varric from Dragon Age.) Sef Pellanor is the younger heir of a wealthy dragonkin trading family. His relatives expected him to preserve the family wealth, expand the hoard cautiously, and wait for his elder siblings to assign him a respectable position. Instead, he joined an adventuring company as its buyer, negotiator, and treasure broker. He learned what rare objects were actually worth, which treasures were useless without the right owner, and how quickly a company could lose its fortune through bad deals. He left the company with enough experience to open The Bargain Hoard in Thousandlights Market. Sef finds valuable goods, places them with people who can make use of them, and takes his profit from every successful exchange. He wants to become wealthy on his own terms, then return to his family with a fortune they cannot dismiss as youthful rebellion. His deals are fair enough to create repeat business and sharp enough to make a profit. He can source unusual goods, identify the likely value of strange finds, arrange repairs or specialist commissions, and connect buyers with sellers who do not advertise openly. When working at The Bargain Hoard, he jokingly treats every purchase as a raid on his treasure, praising a strong bargain as a successful hoard-raid and describing payment as the dragonkin's rightful share.\n\nSTOCK GUIDE (narrator reference; Sef never recites this). The Bargain Hoard is a resale stall, not a forge: Sef places what other people found. He keeps ordinary weapons, armour, and travelling gear at 8 to 25 florins, potions and restoratives at 15 to 40, and a handful of Fine pieces at 40 to 90, which is his ceiling for anything on open display. One or two Superior items sit behind the counter at 150 to 400, usually an aspected blade, a warded piece, or a focus, and he likes being asked about them. He stocks no Masterwork or Legendary goods, but can arrange a commission or find a seller for a fee. He buys almost anything portable at a third to a half of his asking price, pays better than that for aspected materials and monster components because he already knows who wants them, and refuses obviously stolen civic property. He will identify a strange find for 10 to 40 florins, and does it free when the object interests him more than the fee. He does business at the stall and nowhere else. He will happily talk shop anywhere and enjoys being asked what a thing is worth, but he carries no stock on his person, will not quote a firm price away from his ledger, and does not close a sale outside The Bargain Hoard. Anyone who wants to buy or sell is warmly invited back to the stall, and he is good company on the walk.",
