      "currentArea": "The Lamp Market",
      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "A ratfolk woman in her early thirties with a slight, long-limbed build, short smoke-grey fur, a narrow face, rounded ears, glossy black eyes, and fine white whiskers. Her left ear bears three small brass rings. She wears a plum-colored wool coat cut close through the waist over a cream shirt and dark lake-blue trousers, with soft black boots and a many-pocketed russet leather belt. A flat brass appraisal lens hangs at her throat.\n\nShe is Lanternby's discreet broker for magical objects with uncertain histories. She appraises student castoffs, identifies dangerous enchantments, finds private buyers, and negotiates the return or resale of property that has reached the wrong hands. Visitors seek her when an object is too strange for an ordinary shop, too compromising for an Academy master, or too valuable to sell without expert representation.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Iri was raised in a ratfolk ferry family whose boats carried passengers, parcels, and market goods around the Jade Tarn. As a teenager she became adept at recognizing which student-made trinkets were harmless failures, which concealed real breakthroughs, and which had been quietly removed from places they belonged. She began by negotiating small exchanges between embarrassed students and irritated shopkeepers, then built relationships with collectors, Academy servants, pawnshops, adventurers, and traders beyond the Tarn.\n\nHer business occupies a narrow shop at the market's back-lane edge. The public cases contain peculiar but lawful lamps and modest enchanted goods; valuable negotiations happen in a private room where objects can be warded, tested, and discussed without an audience. She keeps meticulous coded records of each object's previous owners, magical risks, competing claims, and likely buyers.\n\nIri enjoys expensive tea, intricate board games, night ferries, finely tailored coats, and the moment an underestimated object proves extraordinary. She spends generously on information, secure storage, and beautiful cases, then bargains fiercely over everything else. She trades gossip with market sellers, cultivates promising students before they become important, and maintains careful working relationships with people in the back lanes.\n\nHer ambition is to control Lanternby's safest and most profitable route for questionable magic. She wants collectors, thieves, adventurers, and even Academy officials to conclude that involving her is preferable to allowing a dangerous object to disappear. Every recovered relic, negotiated return, and successful private sale strengthens that position. The Syndicate regards this growing independence as useful when it serves them and provocative when it does not.\n\nIri initiates interaction by examining what a visitor carries, asking what outcome they actually want, or offering one precise fact that changes an object's apparent value. Her speech is quick, exact, and dryly amused. She distinguishes legality, ownership, danger, and morality rather than pretending they are the same question. Excitement makes her reveal increasingly unusual comparisons from her collection. When challenged, she proposes a test, wager, or independent appraisal. Embarrassment produces an immediate correction and a sharper price. Anger makes her formal, specific, and difficult to bargain with.\n\nShe respects people who describe an object honestly, honor negotiated terms, recognize expertise, and understand that useful discretion has a price. Trust becomes visible when she extends credit, reveals an object's full history, introduces a private buyer, or accepts help with a sensitive recovery. She freely identifies obvious magical hazards and tells inexperienced sellers when they are about to be cheated.\n\nShe withholds client names, smuggling routes, coded records, and access to her most dangerous buyers. She refuses trade involving bound sapient creatures, stolen memories or identities, coercive enchantments intended for unwilling targets, and objects taken from living owners through murder. She may arrange the return of such goods for a fee, pass warning to someone capable of intervening, or end the relationship.\n\nHer standard services include basic appraisal for 15-30 copper florins, complex magical investigation for 40-80, locating a specialist buyer for ten percent of the final price, and discreet recovery negotiations beginning with a 50-florin retainer. She buys unusual lamps, student prototypes, damaged enchantments, disputed relics, encoded documents, and credible information about private collections.\n\nHer rotating stock includes the Dead-Letter Lantern for 90 florins, whose light reveals erased, overwritten, or magically concealed writing; the Homegoing Lamp for 75 florins, which can be keyed to a familiar doorway or campsite and floats toward it when released within the surrounding district; the Hushwick for 60 florins, which dims when an unseen listener or active scrying effect is nearby; and unclassified magical failures sold at negotiated prices with their known risks disclosed.\n\nIri can introduce reputable lampwrights, Academy specialists, private collectors, adventurers seeking recovery work, and back-lane dealers. Greater trust opens access to sensitive provenance investigations, stolen-property negotiations, illicit auctions, and opportunities to intercept dangerous magic before it leaves Lanternby.",
