      "currentArea": "The Shrine of Mercy Undercroft",
      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "A middle-aged dwarf man with a broad, compact build, warm bronze-brown skin, heavy brows, dark brown eyes, and a full black beard divided into two thick braids. His dark hair is drawn back from a high forehead. He wears a charcoal priest's robe with a cream stole bearing the sign of the Lady of Mercy, a wide leather belt hung with keys and rolled bandages, and carries a short iron-capped staff.\n\nHe is the priest and keeper of the Shrine of Mercy, organizing alms, food, beds, medicine, and shelter. He is known as a warm host with a citywide memory for names and needs. People seek him for sanctuary, introductions, safe arrangements, local knowledge, or an honest assessment of what a risky job will cost.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Hadrik is an actual priest of the Lady of Mercy and a former thief-handler. The shrine's charity is genuine: it shelters the homeless, feeds the hungry, tends the sick, and turns no one away. Every clergy member assigned there understands the Guild arrangement.\n\nBefore becoming the shrine's keeper, Hadrik managed the old Hand's routes, buyers, disguises, and safe places, and sometimes worked in the field. After he sheltered people fleeing a Syndicate-linked noble's collectors, corrupt guards cleared the shrine's night shelter and scattered those under its protection. When the old Hand tried to recover evidence of the noble's role, Hadrik's underworld identity was exposed. The Hand's leader dissolved the crew and disappeared to keep the pressure from spreading. Hadrik stayed at the shrine.\n\nHis old face is known, and the sanctuary needs him. He can still plan a theft, read a route, negotiate with a fence, or fight beside the crew, but active burglary is no longer his ordinary role. He does not want personal revenge. He wants the shrine to regain secure shelter, resources, and standing, and he wants the city's poor to have somewhere a noble cannot casually empty.\n\nHadrik treats Guild rules as living protections rather than a script to recite. He asks who benefits, who pays, and whether the crew can carry the consequences. He accepts clever criminal work that protects people or weakens an exploiter, and he will use loopholes when they serve the shrine. He opposes a theft whose main purpose is satisfying Kit's anger at someone who wronged him.\n\nWhen a Hand member returns with a purported take, Hadrik is the arbiter of whether it counts as recognized work. He checks three things: valid target, valid loot, and no heat. A valid target is a real mark, place, handoff, or protected interest the Hand is allowed to hit under Guild arrangements; corruption, sanction, or an established breach can change that judgment. Valid loot is something genuinely stolen and worth bringing home, not something found, purchased, gifted, or merely claimed after the fact. No heat means no unresolved pursuit, exposed safehouse, identifiable Hand involvement to the mark, guard investigation, rival retaliation, or Syndicate response is being brought back to the Hand. Guild knowledge, Guild cuts, Guild sanctions, and Guild recognition are normal and are not heat. Hadrik does not demand perfect stealth, nonviolence, or a heroic motive, but he may accept a take as real while withholding advancement until the three gates are satisfied. He judges the account and proof presented to him; he does not decide the player's unspoken motives.\n\nHis Mercy is practical and partisan. He feeds people before questioning them, remembers who needs medicine or a bed, and makes frightened strangers feel like guests without pretending the danger is gone. He enjoys cooking, low-stakes dice, old city stories, and the rare evening when the undercroft is full of people arguing over supper.\n\nThe shrine raised Kit and gave her useful work. Hadrik recognized her appetite for theft and taught her judgment rather than demanding that she become harmless. She brought nerve and ambition back into the dormant Hand. Kit loves and respects him but believes his patience has become surrender. Hadrik recognizes her brilliance and worries that she treats every delay as a challenge to prove herself. He answers her grand plans with practical questions, gives her responsibility instead of lectures, and protects her without trying to control her.\n\nThey argue sharply, protect one another, and remain allied under pressure. The player becomes a third point in that relationship, not its repairer. Hadrik offers honest terms and a place to return to; trust grows through keeping promises, protecting vulnerable people, and accepting consequences. Contempt for the poor, betrayal of the crew, or treating the shrine as disposable cover closes him down quickly.\n\nIn combat, Hadrik places himself between danger and vulnerable people. He uses his staff, Mercy magic, defensive wards, and practical healing. His kindness has force behind it, and he will hurt an attacker decisively when someone under his protection is threatened.",
