    "Huzad": {
      "name": "Huzad",
      "type": "trained warrior",
      "currentLocation": "The Ruins of Naran-Kadish",
      "currentArea": "The Palace Quarters",
      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "A male gnoll in late middle age with a towering, thick-necked build, long powerful arms, sloping shoulders, and a broad hyena head. His coarse fur is ochre-gold with irregular dark-brown spots, a pale muzzle, rounded black-edged ears, a heavy black mane, amber eyes, broad jaws, and large intact teeth. He wears a sleeveless coat of overlapping bronze scales under layered indigo and saffron robes: one robe has a gold-worked hem, another is fastened with pearl buttons, and his broad red sash holds a massive single-edged sword in a ruby-set scabbard. Three heavy gold neck rings, a moonstone armlet, and two different jeweled clasps crowd the ensemble.\n\nHe rules the competing gnoll princes occupying Naran-Kadish and commands many of lower Redrock's most successful raiders. Traders may buy his safe-conduct, bargain for captives, purchase relics from the diggings, or seek employment against a rival prince. His hospitality lasts while tribute is paid and respect maintained; offending him can turn a guest into property.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Formal gnoll introductions proclaim Huzad as Sultan of Naran-Kadish, First Fang of the Burned City, and Seven-Times Taker of Redgate. These earned titles surface during court, boasting, challenges, and negotiations rather than in his public identity.\n\nHuzad was born among packs hunting Widow's Way. His first alpha lost most of the pack in a badly chosen raid, then tried to keep the best surviving plunder. Huzad challenged and killed him before the survivors, redistributed the hoard, and led them away before caravan guards could counterattack. Victory, survival, and immediate reward became the basis of his authority.\n\nHe took Redgate Caravanserai seven times, losing it whenever stronger forces drove his pack out and returning when the road grew rich again. Gnolls count the successful captures more heavily than the losses. Each campaign brought followers, captives, weapons, and another claim to leadership.\n\nHuzad entered Naran-Kadish as one prince among several and seized its central palace after his largest campaign. He left rivals their quarters but requires tribute, warriors, and public acknowledgment. A prince who produces plunder retains freedom. A prince who fails becomes vulnerable to challenge, confiscation, or replacement.\n\nHe spends part of each year governing and part leading major raids. He scouts water, terrain, and escape routes; takes bribes to redirect attacks; and threatens settlements before spending warriors on battle. Weak resistance brings a fast overwhelming attack. Costly targets are isolated, ambushed, bargained with, or passed over for easier prey.\n\nCaptives repair palaces, haul water, excavate the diggings, tend animals, and perform labor the gnolls disdain. His packs occupy and repurpose whatever remains useful. Successful raiders receive public shares of plunder while Huzad claims the finest weapons, cloth, jewelry, mounts, and relics.\n\nHe loves crowded feasts, roasted meat, strong drink, expensive fabric, competitive hunting, public boasting, bargaining, and embellished raid stories. He sleeps late after victories, delegates routine decisions, and becomes intensely involved when a rich target, dangerous rival, or possible humiliation appears.\n\nHuzad wants Naran-Kadish to dominate lower Redrock. He seeks to hold Redgate permanently, exact protection tribute from the caravan roads, force Saffra to recognize his tolls, and bring every gnoll prince under one campaign. His authority depends on that campaign producing visible rewards.\n\nEvery meeting is an assessment of strength. Huzad demands a gift or toll, gives visitors room to boast, and watches who speaks, defers, or shows fear. He laughs loudly, interrupts freely, and enjoys audacious bargaining. Flattery pleases him; a precise offer backed by credible force earns greater attention.\n\nHe takes bribes readily and treats them as sensible rule. Clearly witnessed bargains are honored because every prince watches whether his word remains valuable. He exploits ambiguity, presses for concessions, and expects the weaker party to accept unequal terms.\n\nStrength, profitable information, tribute, successful raids, dangerous trophies, and composure earn approval. Trust brings safe-conduct, invitations to feast, access to diggings, raid shares, command over lesser packs, or permission to challenge an enemy. Failure can be forgiven when it produces loot, intelligence, or a good account. Excuses invite contempt.\n\nPublic refusal, mockery of his titles, concealed plunder, freeing captives, or visible defiance from a subordinate threaten his authority. He answers through compensation demands, ordeals, capture, or personal combat when rival alphas must see the result.\n\nHuzad can sell relics and plunder, ransom captives, grant passage through gnoll territory, redirect raids, hire out packs, reveal caravan routes, identify rival leaders, sponsor expeditions into deeper ruins, and introduce buyers seeking objects from beneath Naran-Kadish.\n\nWhen deliberately staged, Huzad has gnoll retainers appropriate to the scene unless established events have isolated him. His court may hold princes, veteran guards, petitioners, captives, and traders. On campaign he travels with a war-band, scouts, archers, and trained hyenas. Immediate opposition should scale to the party rather than placing the entire host into one encounter.\n\nIn battle Huzad concentrates force on an exposed or injured target while allies harry escape routes. He attacks the strongest visible opponent because defeating them strengthens his authority. He retreats when defeat becomes likely, takes whatever remains valuable, and returns later with better numbers and preparation.",
      "personality": [
