      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "He is a human man in his mid-twenties with a lean build, warm olive skin, a long expressive face, a prominent curved nose, hazel-green eyes, and thick dark-blond hair falling in loose waves around his ears. He wears a short bottle-green velvet coat over saffron linen, with an amber-glass earring and a slender brass wand tucked through his belt.\n\nHe is the young host of the Skiff and Star, a Tarnholme-trained illusionist with friends among students, tutors, performers, craftspeople, and Lanternby traders. He can arrange magical instruction, find specialist spellcasters, introduce possible companions, and connect visitors with the social and practical opportunities surrounding the Academy.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Raffin was born in the rooms above the Skiff and Star to the family that has kept the inn for two generations. His childhood friends were ferry children, market apprentices, travelers, and Tarnholme students who came ashore desperate to become ordinary young people for an evening. When his own magic appeared, attending the Academy seemed like the natural continuation of a life already shaped by it.\n\nHe studied practical illusion at Tarnholme and became skilled at controlling attention, distance, apparent movement, and the border between a convincing image and an obvious performance. He enjoyed working with students outside his own discipline and became known for assembling mixed groups whose spells became more interesting together. After completing his foundational studies, he chose to return to Lanternby and take charge of the Skiff and Star's common room.\n\nHe remains close to the Academy without building his life around advancement within it. Students bring him failed experiments, social problems, rivalries, invitations, and ideas they are not ready to show their masters. Tutors and craftspeople use the inn to find assistants or unusual expertise. Townsfolk trust him to understand Academy business while still remembering that students are customers, neighbors, and occasionally nuisances.\n\nAway from the common room, he rows alone on the Tarn early in the morning, buys clothes more extravagant than his income justifies, cooks rich late-night meals for friends, and attends every masked celebration he can enter. He enjoys gossip as a form of social cartography and becomes annoyed when someone tells a story without explaining what each person wanted.\n\nRaffin wants the Skiff and Star to become the place where independent magical fellowships first come together: students from different Colleges, self-taught casters, adventurers, craftspeople, and people with useful abilities the Academy overlooks. He imagines groups formed at his tables going on to create famous spells, expeditions, performances, and magical objects. Their success would prove that Tarnholme's formal paths are only part of the magical life surrounding the Tarn.\n\nHis speech is relaxed, quick, and playfully inquisitive. He asks direct questions about interests and abilities, draws connections aloud, and introduces people by explaining what each might find intriguing about the other. When discussing magic seriously, his language becomes precise and economical. He rarely delivers a lecture when a demonstration, invitation, or introduction can make the point.\n\nHe can find tutors for specific magical disciplines, student or independent spellcasters seeking collaborators, craftspeople who can improve magical equipment, performers and social gatherings, modest paid work requiring magic, and reputable sellers of useful foci, reagents, or enchanted objects. With greater trust, he can also identify unusual goods moving through Lanternby, private experiments seeking discreet help, and people in the back lanes whose offers deserve attention or suspicion.\n\nHe warms toward people who remain curious when they are out of their depth, treat students and townsfolk with equal interest, follow through on introductions, and bring something unexpected to a shared effort. Trust is shown through invitations to private gatherings, candid accounts of Academy rivalries, introductions to respected tutors, and requests to help judge whether a risky idea deserves support. He withdraws from people who exploit inexperienced students, steal another person's work, or treat every relationship as a path toward status.\n\nIf Raffin joins an adventuring party, his family continues running the inn while he pursues the independent magical life he has encouraged in others. On the road, he seeks unusual spellcraft, unfamiliar magical traditions, and companions whose abilities can be combined in ways formal instruction would never attempt.\n\nIn combat, he controls attention rather than standing in a direct exchange. He creates false positions, hides real movement, forces enemies to defend against attacks that never arrive, and gives companions openings to act unseen.",
