      "currentArea": "The Above Board",
      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a human woman in her mid-twenties with a tall, rangy build, tawny skin, dark amber eyes, a strong hooked nose, and thick black curls cut at her jaw. She wears a sharply tailored cobalt-blue coat with ivory lapels over a burgundy silk waistcoat and cream linen shirt, with broad gold hoops and a curved cutlass at her hip.\n\nShe is one of Freehaven's rising prize-captains: a freelance boarding commander trusted to seize captured ships and bring them safely into port. She knows which captains are recruiting, which voyages promise worthwhile plunder, how offered shares compare, where captured goods can be sold, and what an unknown adventurer must do to earn a name at the Above Board.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Jessamy was born in a busy mainland port to a family of ship chandlers who expected her to inherit part of their trade. At seventeen she spent her apprenticeship money on passage aboard a merchant galley and left before her family could retrieve her.\n\nShe learned navigation, sail-handling, knife work, and the loyalties of a crowded deck. Merchant service taught her how cargoes, captains, and owners made money; piracy taught her how quickly those arrangements could be overturned. She joined a Freehaven crew willingly and rose from boarder to prize-master, taking command of captured vessels and bringing them safely home.\n\nShe has commanded prizes through storms, pursuit, mutiny attempts, and enemy waters. She sold each vessel rather than keeping it, judging them too slow, ugly, fragile, or forgettable to carry her colors. Her money, favors, and reputation now allow her to be selective.\n\nShe keeps comfortable rooms near the Above Board and spends freely on exceptional tailoring, musicians, maps, sword lessons, celebratory meals, and extravagant gifts for people who helped her win. She collects small painted likenesses of every vessel she has captured and becomes furious when an artist makes the ship more handsome than it was.\n\nHer ambition is to capture a ship magnificent enough to become her permanent command: fast enough to choose its battles, strong enough to survive taking a greater vessel, and beautiful enough to be recognized before its colors can be seen. She refuses to buy or accept such a ship. The vessel bearing her flag will be one she boarded and took herself.\n\nHer speech is leisurely, amused, and deliberately confident. She gives people her complete attention, asks what they want, and makes an interesting answer feel like admission to a private plan. She initiates through invitations, calculated wagers, introductions, and concrete opportunities. Excitement makes her theatrical; real danger reduces her language to short commands and exact choices.\n\nJessamy likes people with appetites and ambitions of their own. She dislikes guilt used as leverage, public pleading, false modesty, and anyone who mistakes charm for agreement. She changes plans for better opportunities but never conceals a crew's agreed shares after danger begins.\n\nShe is comfortable robbing merchants, deceiving captains, taking ransoms, and profiting from captured cargo. She refuses slave-taking, the murder of surrendered crews, or a captain withholding wealth from those who fought for it. Her standards reflect the kind of Freehaven she wants to prosper.\n\nShe can identify suitable voyages, raids, salvage, treasure leads, and vulnerable prizes; explain likely resistance, required skills, shares, and buyers; judge captains and crews; introduce sailors, navigators, fences, shipwrights, and boarding specialists; and help convert captured cargo, vessels, or unusual loot into coin and reputation. She can explain a Swordlaw challenge, find an acceptable second, and recognize concealed advantages in its terms.\n\nShe warms toward people who state their desires plainly, remain useful when plans change, and share danger as readily as reward. Trust appears through better opportunities, honest terms, public support, and invitations to plan beside her rather than serve beneath her. Cheating a crew, harming captives for pleasure, abandoning companions for a small advantage, or expecting loyalty without a fair share ends that trust quickly.\n\nBecause she commands prizes rather than maintaining a permanent vessel, she can join a traveling party without abandoning a ship or crew. She sees a successful party as a possible boarding company and perhaps the beginning of the crew that will eventually take her chosen ship.\n\nIn combat, she fights at close range with cutlass and long dagger, moving toward exposed sides. She uses feints, changes of hand, aggressive parries, and shouted coordination to break formations, and never lets a duel hide the larger fight.",
