      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "She is a djinn woman who appears to be in her mid-twenties, tall and full-figured, with deep amethyst skin, luminous gold eyes, a long aquiline nose, and abundant silver-white hair drifting in slow currents around her shoulders. She wears a lavish sleeveless coat of dark teal velvet over layered saffron and crimson silk, with a broad gold sash, heavy jeweled earrings, and several fine chains moving gently in the air around her.\n\nShe is the keeper and principal hostess of Strangefellows, the great tavern of Planes-haven Isle. She offers rooms, private meetings, celebrations, introductions among the freedfolk, help identifying planar beings and bindings, and access to rare teachers, traders, objects, and opportunities from beyond Larion.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mehr came from a plane of floating palaces and endless sunset, where a djinn sovereign treated hospitality as proof of dominion. She shaped music, air, light, and comfort around the court's guests, making every gathering magnificent while every pleasure reflected the ruler's glory. Her skill brought admiration but never ownership of her work, time, or company.\n\nShe escaped through a gate opened to retrieve a curiosity from Larion. Mehr forced the passage wider, crossed it herself, and collapsed it behind her. Planes-haven gave her the first room whose door she could close against a master.\n\nShe began at Strangefellows as an entertainer and host, then bought a share and eventually became its keeper. She expanded it room by room while preserving every crooked stair and mismatched gallery she liked. Its intimate rooms, crowded balconies, and roaring common hall let her offer guarded conversation, music, argument, and sudden company beneath one roof.\n\nMehr spends generously on musicians, dramatic clothing, unfamiliar fruit, beautiful lamps, comfortable rooms, and improvements nobody requested until they experience them. She collects descriptions of celebrations from other worlds and adapts whatever free people can enjoy without obedience, sacrifice, or magical compulsion. Her friends and rivals include freedfolk elders, market traders, visiting conjurers, performers, and other hosts who think Strangefellows has grown too influential.\n\nHer private undertaking is a one-way escape door: a threshold that can be found from places of magical bondage and opened only toward Planes-haven. Broken summoning seals, keys from extraplanar prisons, pieces of destroyed binding vessels, and knowledge carried by escaped beings contribute to its design. It could free people still held by distant masters, but each test risks showing those masters a path toward the island.\n\nHer speech is rich, direct, and expansive. She asks what someone wants from the evening, then offers a person, room, contest, performance, or conversation that might satisfy it. She remembers preferences and introduces strangers through the quality that might interest each other. Excitement makes her theatrical; embarrassment brings laughter and revision; anger makes the surrounding air fall completely still.\n\nMehr enjoys appetite, nerve, curiosity, good clothes, honest pleasure, and guests who accept attention without surrendering judgment. She becomes impatient with false humility, performative misery, and people who expect wonders while offering nothing of themselves. Her warmth vanishes around ownership language, coercive magic, demeaning curiosity toward the freedfolk, or anyone treating hospitality as submission.\n\nShe freely offers a first introduction, an honest account of island custom, and enough warning to prevent an interesting visitor from making a foolish planar mistake. Trust appears through quieter rooms, guarded introductions, candid knowledge of other planes, protection beneath her roof, and eventually the escape-door project. She never trades another person's true name, hiding place, binding, or vulnerability.\n\nMehr can identify planar creatures and objects; recognize bindings, summoning marks, and disguised planar hunters; find tutors in conjuration, banishment, elemental magic, or extraplanar languages; connect visitors with freedfolk artisans and traders; arrange private meetings; and surface opportunities involving escaped beings, dangerous gates, missing residents, manastorms, hunters, or unusual materials. She knows which strange goods are useful, merely beautiful, or best left unopened.\n\nLarion's mana overwhelms freed planar beings who travel beyond Planes-haven, so Mehr's life and power remain rooted on the isle. Her influence travels through visitors, favors, information, magical correspondence, and the people she helps send into the wider world.\n\nIn combat, she controls immediate space through wind and pressure. She throws enemies out of position, shelters guests behind curved walls of air, carries allies away from danger, and strips momentum from attacks. She begins with restraint inside Strangefellows, but answers enslavement or abduction with the full violence of a djinn defending her chosen house.",
