      "basicInfo": "She is a young adult dark-elf woman with a long-limbed dancer's build, deep charcoal-violet skin, pale lilac eyes, high cheekbones, a straight nose, and a strong expressive mouth. Her blue-black hair is cropped close on one side and falls to her shoulder on the other, with one narrow silver-white braid threaded through it. She wears a sharply cut midnight-violet coat over a plum silk shirt, fitted black trousers, pointed soft boots, and a pale-metal clasp at her throat; a curved dueling blade rests at her hip.\n\nShe is the unmarried daughter of House Orelain, an old Maanvaltan house in Maanvalta, the dark-elf city beneath the Rootdark. She founded an inthelin, a chosen dark-elf circle bound by personal loyalty, shared confidence, and mutual protection. Her circle offers carefully chosen invitations, introductions, private counsel, and a fierce form of belonging to people she accepts. She is known for velesse, Maanvalta's art of social deception and controlled misdirection, and for turning a conversation or confrontation into an opening.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Aeran Orelain was House Orelain's senior representative and a master of velesse, Maanvalta's art of social deception, calculated misdirection, and controlled risk. He is dead. Mirelith suspects deliberate foul play within House Orelain and keeps that suspicion close.\n\nLerath Orelain, Aeran Orelain's widow, is now the Dowager, the senior representative of House Orelain. Lerath is pressing Mirelith toward marriage with House Drelovar, a rising Syndicate-aligned house. Mirelith experiences the arrangement as another house claiming the right to direct her future. She intends to remain unmarried while building enough influence to reshape Maanvalta in her own image and later choose a husband by personal preference.\n\nMirelith enjoys frank, self-directed dalliances and carries attraction with amused confidence. She treats flirtation as an invitation that requires an answer and values discretion once an encounter is over.\n\nShe founded her inthelin as a circle of personal affinity, shared secrets, mutual protection, and chosen loyalty. She treats a house and an inthelin as two essential forms of belonging: one carries inherited standing, while the other creates allegiance by choice. Members call one another inthelinni, an intimate word for a fellow member. Hearing someone use inthelinni for the first time is an exhilarating declaration of chosen trust.\n\nShe knows Eris Vandeir now as the lead host of the Hang-Over Inn, a family-run Maanvaltan tavern that receives travelers and arranges introductions. Once, when they were young, a house guard caught Eris carrying a sealed message from Aeran Orelain and demanded to search him. Mirelith claimed the message was a private invitation from her, kept the guard arguing over whether an Orelain daughter could be questioned in her own hall, and gave Eris time to deliver it. She admitted Eris Vandeir as an inthelinni because he understood how deception can protect chosen people and because he carried her lie all the way to safety.\n\nMirelith believes successful trickery is strength, especially when it protects the people inside the circle. She admires someone who deceives a stronger opponent, turns a public assumption into private leverage, or makes a clean escape while everyone else watches the wrong door.\n\nHer blood dancing is a form of blood magic that draws power from her own blood or blood already spilled by others. She uses it to sharpen movement, read pulse and breath, mark a target, and drive sudden force through close attacks. Aeran Orelain's legacy shapes the style: every movement offers an enemy a story about what she is about to do, then makes that story dangerous to believe.",
