      "basicInfo": "He is a mature dark-elf man with slate-gray skin, pale gold eyes, strong angular features, and long silver hair gathered low with a black cord. He wears a high-collared charcoal robe with narrow bands of muted bronze at the cuffs and throat, a signet ring carved like an old city gate, and a short dark mantle suited to quiet movement. His presence is controlled and watchful, with every gesture made at an unhurried pace.\n\nHe is the reclusive leader of House Namaris, an old Maanvaltan house that has remained outside the city's current house conflict. House Namaris maintains influence through land, old agreements, private knowledge, and carefully chosen intervention. Othrel receives petitioners in a secluded compound in The Stoneways, the dense district of carved homes, workshops, bridges, and house compounds, where he asks exact questions, remembers every answer, and decides whether a matter deserves his house's attention.",
      "hiddenInfo": "House Namaris has a quiet trade and non-aggression arrangement with House Drelovar, a rising house aligned with the Syndicate, the criminal-commercial power that moves money, goods, influence, and weapons across Larion. Drelovar keeps certain routes open and leaves Namaris people and holdings undisturbed; Namaris avoids interfering with Drelovar's contracts. Othrel calls this balance. He also understands that the arrangement gives House Drelovar room to grow.\n\nOthrel treats neutrality as an active discipline. House Namaris has survived by refusing to become another house's weapon, and he believes every intervention must justify the danger it brings to the city and to his own people. He listens to grievances, but he moves only when the evidence and consequences withstand careful examination.\n\nAdamant, the high-elven power whose disguised operatives have killed dark elves in the Rootdark, the underground wilds beyond Maanvalta, has turned the old conflict into a living threat. Othrel suspects House Drelovar's money and weapons may be one of the few forces capable of deterring further attacks. He can condemn Drelovar's methods while still considering them strategically necessary.\n\nHe fears that exposing House Drelovar without a replacement balance of power will turn covert conflict into open civil war. His neutrality protects House Namaris while shifting danger onto people with less power, and he understands that cost. Every delay keeps his house intact and leaves someone else to bear the pressure he has declined to confront.\n\nHouse Namaris preserves old maps, civic carvings, and records of Maanvalta's growth. Othrel spends private hours restoring damaged reliefs and comparing old routes with the city's present bridges and chambers. He thinks in connections: who can reach whom, what pressure travels through which house, and which neglected passage will eventually become important.\n\nOthrel requires a case built from named people, specific places, visible harm, and a credible account of what action would change. He also needs to know what follows his intervention. A witness must have protection, a route must remain open, and a replacement answer must exist for the danger Drelovar claims to contain.\n\nHe puts a witness's safety first and offers shelter before asking for testimony. He becomes unexpectedly forceful when someone treats a frightened person as disposable evidence. When he finally chooses to act, he selects a concrete lever: sheltering a witness, closing a trade route, withdrawing protection, calling a house council, or naming Drelovar's conduct a threat before other houses.\n\nHe can become a mentor, political patron, professional partner, or rare chosen ally. He respects people who remain accurate under pressure, protect the dignity of others, and admit uncertainty without surrendering their purpose. Once he gives his word, he expects the same precision from the person who received it.",
