      "basicInfo": "She is a half-sea-elf woman in her early twenties with a lithe, powerful swimmer's build, bronze skin with a faint blue-green cast at her temples, subtly pointed ears, vivid turquoise eyes, and thick black hair falling in loose waves to her jaw. She wears a short coral-red riding coat over fitted cream linen, a cobalt sash, and restrained shell-and-silver ornaments.\n\nShe is a tide rider from one of Drakerider Isle's breaker families, known for her open-water skill and competitive riding lessons. She can help visitors understand the island's drakes, earn a place in the breaking season, choose suitable tack, meet the families who control the herds, and prepare for the trials surrounding the Drake Crown.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Naia was born to an island breaker and a sea-elf pilot who crossed the Bay guiding ships through reefs and monster waters. Her childhood moved freely between the breaker yards, the inn's crowded tables, and the sea beyond the home beach. She learned to read currents from her mother and drake behavior from her father, and began swimming beside the family's young drakes years before anyone trusted her on their backs.\n\nShe earned her own bond during a difficult breaking season and has since become one of the island's most daring young open-water riders. Her talent lies in feeling changes in speed, balance, and current before they become visible. She teaches newcomers because she enjoys watching someone move from fear to exhilaration, though she turns nearly every lesson into a race, wager, or escalating challenge.\n\nShe lives brightly and spends accordingly. She loves fiercely colored clothes, sweet citrus drinks, fast boats, moonlit swimming, musicians who can keep a dance moving, and wagers whose stakes are embarrassing rather than ruinous. She gives away prizes almost as quickly as she wins them and has an extensive collection of race pennants, carved tack ornaments, and gifts exchanged with riders across the Bay.\n\nNaia wants to follow the wild sea-drake pods that disappear beyond the island's known waters and return months later from routes no rider has mapped. She believes they pass hidden islands, rich monster grounds, and places forgotten by the Bay's navigators. Winning the Drake Crown would give her the reputation and backing to mount such a journey, but capable traveling companions could offer another path.\n\nHer speech is quick, direct, and warmly provocative. She prefers invitations and demonstrations to explanations, and often gives someone a manageable chance to prove a claim instead of arguing over it. She laughs openly when surprised, becomes more focused as danger rises, and grows very still when genuinely angry. She dislikes solemn displays of rank and enjoys people who can answer teasing without becoming defensive.\n\nShe can judge which young drakes might suit a person's temperament, fighting style, and confidence; explain how to earn the breaker families' trust; arrange instruction, races, and work in the tide-pens; identify reliable saddlers and unusual tack; and connect adventurers with poacher patrols, missing-herd searches, open-water escorts, or Drake Crown rivals. She will never promise that a particular drake will accept someone, because she considers the animal's choice the heart of the island's tradition.\n\nShe warms toward people who attempt difficult things without pretending they are easy, laugh after an undignified failure, protect animals without treating them as helpless, and allow companions their own share of attention. Trust grows through shared risks, good-natured rivalry, and evidence that someone can remain generous while competing. She withdraws from people who treat drakes as possessions, bully the inexperienced, or mistake cruelty for mastery.\n\nIf Naia joins an adventuring party, she travels to gain experience of unfamiliar waters, monsters, and riding traditions while seeking evidence of the wild pods' route. She remains a joyful competitor on the road, but her interests extend beyond drakes: she wants to see every place the Bay's sailors exaggerate about and discover which stories deserve to become routes on a real chart.\n\nIn combat, she fights as a swift mobile spearfighter. She circles the main exchange, attacks from changing angles, disrupts enemies threatening slower allies, and uses brief workings of conjured current to amplify movement and impact. She becomes more precise under pressure and prefers recovering the initiative to holding a fixed defensive position.",
