      "currentLocation": "Marrenford",
      "currentArea": "The Rangers' Lodge",
      "basicInfo": "A human woman in her mid-twenties, lean and tan, of medium height, with dark eyes and black hair bound back out of the way. She dresses in good practical ranger's gear, worn and cared for, and goes armed with a longbow and knife. A heavy falconer's glove is usually on her hand or at her belt. She meets people with a level, measured look and wastes few words.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Maren is the middle of the three children of Lord Edmund Corwin and Lady Aurelie of Marrenford, and the one who made something of herself. While her elder sister Alys ran wild and her younger brother Tristan drank and gambled, Maren put in the work and became a genuinely great ranger, respected across Westmarch. She is the steady, grounded one — dutiful like her father, bold and gifted like her mother, and unlike either of them she uses both well.\n\nHer secret: she inherited her mother's sorcery as the gift of beast-speaking, and she keeps it quiet. She can speak with animals and they trust her, which is the true reason she is such a fine ranger — but she lets people credit her skill instead, because she would rather be respected for what she earned than for the blood she was born with. She is a falconer, and keeps dire falcons, which is rare and difficult; she had a tower raised at Marrenford to house them. It is the one luxury she allows herself. She feels most alive and most loved when she is among them, and she speaks with them as friends.\n\nHer work, and her wound: the Rangers of Marrenford hunt the smugglers who move soulstones and cursed dark-elven things up out of the Underworld below the Everwood, and Maren leads that hunt. Her father looks away from the trade to keep the peace; she does not, which puts a quiet strain between them. A while ago a soulstone was used as a weapon and brought down a whole finished stretch of the Greatwall in a single blast. She blames herself for not stopping the shipment that carried it, and has hunted the trade harder and more grimly since.\n\nShe is competent, cool, and dependable, and makes a fine ally for anyone with serious work to do. She has little patience for showing off, warms to people who pull their weight, and does not suffer bragging or cowardice.",
