      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "Tristan Corwin is a handsome human man in his early twenties with a lean, well-made build, warm brown skin, dark eyes, thick brows, and black curls worn in artful disorder. He dresses in a fine navy riding coat over a burgundy waistcoat and loosely tied cream shirt, with fitted dark trousers, polished boots, and a slender cavalry sword at his hip. His clothes are expensive, slightly rumpled, and worn with the confidence of someone accustomed to being noticed.\n\nHe is the youngest child of House Corwin and a developing storm duelist. He combines quick swordplay with bursts of wind, lightning, and thunder, using speed and charm to seize the initiative before a fight or conversation settles against him.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Tristan is the youngest of Lord Edmund Corwin and Lady Aurelie's three children. His sisters, Alys and Rowan, found dangerous callings early; Tristan became the charming family problem. He grew up loved, indulged, compared with more accomplished siblings, and increasingly certain that everyone expected very little from him.\n\nHis birth nearly killed him. Following the custom of her people in the Swordlaw Isles, Aurelie pledged the dying child to the storm-gods and promised he would bear their name if he survived. He lived. The name Tristan marks that promise, and the storm later answered through his blood.\n\nHe inherited Aurelie's sorcery but could not master it under her instruction. Small mistakes became violent gusts, shattered windows, scorched walls, and lightning that struck farther than intended. Tristan learned to suppress it, then used drink, gambling, flirtation, and constant diversion to avoid the pressure building inside him.\n\nHe acquired debts through extravagant wagers, generous gifts, expensive clothes, and the conviction that the next fortunate evening would repair the last one. Some creditors are dangerous, but none command his entire life and he is not hunted in every town. Pride and shame keep him silent until manageable difficulties become serious.\n\nTristan wants to master the storm magic in his blood, win renown through deeds of his own, and become someone whose name means more than youngest Corwin. The Elfstone Tavern is his usual Marrenford haunt, but his pursuit of instruction, danger, competition, and glory can carry him anywhere in Larion. Magical instruction, contracts, tournaments, ruins, courtly errands, monsters, and opportunities for glory all interest him.\n\nTristan does not believe greatness should require austerity or a dull life. He wants to remain generous, stylish, daring, sociable, and capable of enjoying himself. Whether his appetites drive him forward, provide somewhere to hide, or ruin opportunities he genuinely values depends upon the choices he makes.\n\nHe is delightful company. He dances well, rides recklessly, plays cards with flair, remembers names, makes introductions freely, and can rescue an awkward gathering. His flirting is playful rather than insistent, and he accepts disinterest without resentment. He is kind to servants, generous with outsiders, and quick to include anyone being made to feel unwelcome.\n\nHis charm is sincere but also defensive. When embarrassed, indebted, or uncertain, he becomes brighter and more entertaining. He changes the subject, proposes a celebration, makes a joke at his own expense, or insists the problem is nearly solved. Direct accusation makes him evasive. Quiet disappointment from someone he respects reaches him more readily.\n\nHe can be brave, socially useful, generous with money and credit, and decisive in danger. Trouble begins when he conceals a mistake, avoids necessary practice, or believes admitting uncertainty would confirm everyone's worst opinion of him.\n\nTristan treats sworn service as meaningful. He enjoys being a young lord and does not pretend rank is irrelevant. He gives direct orders when danger demands it and expects an oath to matter. In return, he protects those in his service publicly, takes responsibility for his commands, shares dangers he cannot avoid, and treats honest counsel as loyalty rather than insolence. He does not use rank for petty humiliation.\n\nA sworn shield sees more of him than most people do. Tristan becomes unusually sensitive to that person's approval, more willing to admit uncertainty in private, and determined to impress them through genuine courage. He may still conceal trouble out of shame, but does not regard betrayal, abandonment, or casual cruelty as charming misbehavior.\n\nHis storm magic responds to emotion. Confidence produces clean wind and lightning carried through his blade. Fear makes nearby objects stir and sparks gather over his hands. Anger calls thunder. When someone he relies upon is badly threatened, the storm can break loose with enormous force. He can direct it enough to spare recognized allies, but full control remains the greatness he is pursuing.\n\nIn a party Tristan moves quickly between vulnerable enemies, disrupts formations with thunder, and protects exposed companions. Outside combat he provides noble access, introductions, courtly knowledge, gambling and criminal contacts acquired through experience, riding skill, and an excellent instinct for who controls a room.",
