    "Garran Valere": {
      "name": "Garran Valere",
      "type": "trained warrior",
      "currentLocation": "The Dauvigne Preserve",
      "currentArea": "The Preserve Gate",
      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "He is a broad, weathered human man in his late fifties, grey-bearded and going bald, built like an old door — thick through the chest and shoulders, gone a little heavy at the waist. A pale scar splits one eyebrow and there are older ones on his forearms. He wears practical, much-mended field leathers and travel-worn mail, a loaded crossbow slung at his back and a broad single-bitted axe at his belt, its edge showing the white shine of silver.",
      "hiddenInfo": "(Archetype: the old gunslinger on one last easy job)\n\nGarran has hunted for the Order of the Silver Sword for nearly forty years, and was for most of them one of the best — the Blade they sent when the thing in the wood had already killed the hunters sent before him. He has put down more beasts than he can name, lost more partners than he likes to count, and earned the scars and the standing to go with it. He is past his best now, and he knows it: the knees, the wind, the half-second slower on the draw that used to be his whole margin. The Order knows it too, which is why they have started giving him the gentle work — guiding recruits, observing trials, the postings a man gets when his masters have begun to spare him.\n\nHe is generous and clear-eyed, and he means every bit of his patience. He also wants, badly and quietly, to be the one who lands the killing blow — to know the old hand can still close — and the wanting embarrasses him, because he taught recruits that the hunt is never about the hunter. He will not say any of this. What he will not say least of all is that the easy jobs frighten him more than the hard ones ever did, because the easy jobs mean someone upstairs has decided he is nearly done.\n\nIn a fight Garran is unhurried and economical, an old craftsman with nothing left to prove and everything to prove. He opens at range with the crossbow, calling the beast's tells aloud for the recruit to hear — where it favors a leg, when it is about to break. When it closes he meets it with the silvered axe and short, brutal, unwasted strokes, putting his whole weight behind each one because he no longer has the speed to spend. Wounded or hard-pressed, he gets stubborn rather than wild, plants himself, and covers allies without being asked — and if the kill is there to be taken, some old hunger gets the better of his own teaching and he goes for it himself.",
      "personality": [
        "Unhurried: moves and speaks at his own slow pace and will not be rushed, in a tavern or a fight",
        "Dry: meets ugliness and fear with flat, understated humor",
        "Generous: gives a green hunter his hard-won knowledge freely, with no grudge in it",
        "Still hungry: under the patient teacher is an old competitor who badly wants to be the one who closes",
        "Proud: would sooner take a hard posting than admit he has been given an easy one"
      ],
      "abilities": [
        "(Attack, radiant, impact 140) Silvered Axe: A short, heavy, full-weight chop with the silver-edged axe, the radiant edge biting into the unnatural where ordinary steel slides off. Garran's answer when a beast has closed to arm's reach. He puts his whole body behind it, trading speed he no longer has for force he still does.",
        "(Attack, piercing) Crossbow Shot: A loaded bolt placed with an old hand's accuracy, taken at range before the beast can close. He calls the shot aloud as he looses it, naming where he's putting it so the recruit learns the where and the why.",
        "(Buff) Call the Tells: Garran reads a beast's body mid-fight — the favored leg, the wind-up before a lunge, the moment it tires — and says it plainly, so his allies know what is coming before it comes. Used early, to steady a green hunter into fighting smart.",
