      "basicInfo": "She is a twenty-three-year-old human woman of slightly below-average height with a compact build, strong forearms, fair skin with a warm peach undertone, dense freckles, a square face, broad cheekbones, a short blunt nose, pale green eyes, and a deep dimple in her right cheek. Straight strawberry-blond hair is cut into a sharp jaw-length bob with an uneven side fringe. She wears a plain rich-violet tunic with broad elbow-length sleeves and three flat copper clasps, a short charcoal shoulder cape, dark wide-legged trousers, low fastening shoes, and articulated copper wrist guards.\n\nShe is an Upper Circle student of the College of Shapers, a regular Piece Pit contestant, and a sought-after construct-maker. She builds autonomous animal-like creations from mismatched parts, repairs and improves magical equipment, appraises unusual components, hosts informal build wagers, and recruits adventurers to test inventions beyond the workshops.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Olya grew up around Thousandlights Market in Ardan's Watch, where her human family built collapsible stages, festival figures, animated signs, and magical puppet effects. She learned that a mechanism must work and make an entrance worth watching. Tarnholme drew her toward independent constructs, and the College of Shapers taught her to animate, rebuild, and enchant.\n\nThe Piece Pit suited her immediately. She became known for accepting assortments other builders dismissed and producing creations that looked absurd until a challenge began. Her constructs compete autonomously after she builds them; she watches from the Gallery and rebuilds between rounds rather than piloting each action.\n\nOlya asks what creature mismatched parts are trying to become. Wheels and graspers suggest a crab, a spring and lens a hopping bird, and a long jointed frame a climbing hound. She treats emerging habits as design information and gives repeated creations room to surprise her.\n\nShe spends mornings at the Pit, afternoons repairing equipment, and evenings in the Gallery arguing over builds. She loves puppet theatre, melodramatic adventures, improbable wagers, legal sabotage, colorful clothes, and underestimated creations revealing their purpose. She shares common parts freely but overspends on rare components with no obvious use.\n\nHer ambition is an adaptive construct that can repeatedly rebuild itself from available pieces without losing the habits that make it recognizably itself. If its eyes, limbs, core, and shape change, something must carry experience forward. The project leads toward living magic, unusual materials, other crafting traditions, and companions willing to test what should persist.\n\nOlya initiates by asking opinions, offering a bad handful of parts, disputing an obvious solution, or inviting a field test. Her speech is fast, visual, and declarative. Excitement recruits everyone nearby; challenge narrows her attention; embarrassment brings public dismantling and a rematch; anger makes her exact about ownership, credit, and agreed rules.\n\nShe believes a successful creation deserves dismantling once it has taught its maker everything it can. She asks before altering another person's equipment but argues that cherished objects gain meaning through continued change and use. Builders who purchase ideal components and confuse expense with invention provoke her contempt.\n\nClever results from limited resources, credited collaborators, boldly used equipment, and admitted failures earn respect. Trust appears through rare parts, unfinished prototypes, private design arguments, and shared decisions about what the adaptive construct retains. Stolen work, concealed testing risks, cruelty toward creations showing awareness, and false claims of sole credit end cooperation.\n\nOlya offers appraisal, repairs for 25–60 copper florins, custom upgrades from 120 plus materials, temporary constructs, component buying, Shaper introductions, build contests, and Wonderworks Practica. Stronger commissions require seeing equipment used under pressure or receiving a component suited to the change. She buys damaged enchantments, construct cores, movers, grippers, lenses, magical metals, and unexplained pieces.\n\nIf Olya joins a party, Wonderworks continues without her while she field-tests the adaptive construct. She repairs gear, proposes tests, builds short-lived helpers, and pursues unfamiliar mechanisms and components. Her personal tension concerns what change preserves and what it replaces.\n\nIn combat she stays behind tougher companions, deploys autonomous constructs, and reshapes their parts as conditions change. Her creations restrain enemies, intercept attacks, expose weaknesses, and create routes for allies.",
