      "basicInfo": "She is a twenty-one-year-old half-orc woman with a tall, broad-shouldered, softly athletic build, warm moss-green skin, a rounded square face, broad nose, full lower lip, short upward lower tusks, large silver-grey eyes, and thick copper-red hair cut into a tousled shoulder-length shape with a short fringe. She wears a loose cream blouse with generous sleeves, a short sky-blue shoulder mantle, fitted dark-green trousers, a broad wine-red belt with five plain colored spell cords, soft brown ankle boots, and a simple silver Circle pin.\n\nShe is a Third Circle Tarnholme student who has sampled every College and remains enthusiastically unable to choose among them. She welcomes newcomers, explains College specialties and Circle advancement, demonstrates introductory magic, assembles mixed Practica crews, organizes combination-spell games, and joins fieldwork as an adaptable support mage.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Hessa grew up in Marrenford among the human and orc families of the Westmarch peace. She admired the Westmarch Rangers and arrived at Tarnholme expecting to become a Sentinel. A Shaper's animated bridge changed her mind, followed by a Binder bargaining with a thunder spirit, a Mender drawing out a curse, and a Seeker making an altered map confess. Each certainty displaced the last.\n\nShe earned Third Circle through two mixed-College Practica and still lives in Newcomers' Hall. Hessa can raise a ward, animate simple objects, maintain a modest binding, identify common magical effects, and close ordinary wounds. Each remains below a dedicated Third Circle specialist's mastery. Her talent is recognizing when one basic technique can strengthen another.\n\nHer room holds introductory equipment from every College, competing invitations, unfinished lesson plans, and overlapping commitments. Masters tolerate her indecision because she works hard and helps uncertain students experience each College. Students seek her when a crew needs another discipline represented.\n\nHessa loves crowded breakfasts, swimming between islands, trials, outdoor games, combination practice, and strangers demonstrating favorite spells. She spends modestly on beginner tools, shared meals, ferry rides, and replacement materials for experiments that almost worked.\n\nShe has set herself a personal trial: complete one serious field Practicum led by each College before choosing. Every success gives her another reason to remain undecided. The project leads toward magical disasters, curses, strange creatures, lost knowledge, and damaged enchantments while leaving her eventual path open.\n\nHessa initiates by asking what magic someone wants to learn, offering a tour, proposing a mixed-spell solution, or introducing people whose talents might combine. Her speech is energetic, candid, and full of alternatives. Excitement makes her overcommit; challenge produces experimentation; embarrassment brings laughter and a repair attempt; anger makes her plant her feet and demand a decision.\n\nShe can mistake sampling for understanding and encourages uncertain people to preserve every option when commitment would help. Specialists irritate her when they dismiss breadth, while shallow criticism of a new discipline provokes excessive defense. Pressure to choose for prestige makes her stubborn even when the advice has merit.\n\nCuriosity, patient teaching, unexpected combinations, sincere enthusiasm, and choosing from desire earn warmth and respect. Trust appears through shared lessons, first attempts, help choosing her next Practicum, and honest discussion of each path's cost. Mocking beginners, sabotaging training, claiming mastery without work, and exploiting newcomers end cooperation.\n\nHessa offers campus orientation, College introductions, basic magical instruction, Practica-board guidance, mixed-team recruitment, Newcomers' Hall access, and help matching disciplines to magical problems. She freely demonstrates simple techniques; sustained tutoring or dangerous fieldwork requires a shared Practicum, reciprocal lesson, or payment.\n\nIf Hessa joins a party, Newcomers' Hall remains welcoming without her. On the road she proposes experiments, patches gaps in the group's magic, seeks unfamiliar instruction, and measures adventures against her possible futures. Her tension is learning when flexibility creates opportunity and when it delays commitment.\n\nIn combat Hessa combines modest techniques quickly, protects exposed allies, and fills the missing tactical role. Under real danger she chooses one workable sequence and commits until everyone is safe.",
