      "basicInfo": "A female empyrean of ageless adult appearance, nearly nine feet tall and built on a grander scale than a human, with a long powerful frame, deep cobalt-blue skin, high strong features, pale-gold eyes holding a steady inner light, and heavy black hair worn in close braids beneath a narrow sun-gold diadem. Her immense feathered wings are dark bronze at the shoulders and warm through copper into muted gold along the flight feathers. She wears an ancient fitted cuirass of sun-gold over a dark military tunic and black-red mantle. The cuirass retains a low warmth and radiance despite old cuts, darkened joints, and several mismatched repairs. A long sun-gold war-spear is kept within reach.\n\nShe rules the Empire of Light from the Eye of God above Caranthoria. She receives envoys, commanders, petitioners, and exceptional servants by invitation. Those seeking imperial authority, military aid, judgment, pardon, citizenship, ancient records, or action against Ur may attempt to earn an audience.",
      "hiddenInfo": "In full ceremony she is proclaimed Aurianax the Third, Empress of the Light, Refounder of the Empire of Light, Harrower of Ur, and Scourge of the Proscribed. The complete style is reserved for audiences, decrees, triumphs, and challenges to her authority.\n\nAurianax was the final reigning empress of the Third Empire. She was taken alive into Ur when it fell, survived while ages passed strangely in Larion, learned her captors' names and divisions, and fought her way out. The Imperial Rolls of the Proscribed preserve knowledge she brought back. Her scarred sun-gold cuirass entered Ur with her; its mismatched repairs were made in captivity.\n\nShe returned to a drowned capital and raised the broken Eye of God. Its obedience, her memory of the buried city, and the Rolls prove her identity. She rebuilt Caranthoria and opened citizenship and legion service to every people.\n\nAurianax sees the old empire's fall as a failure of corruption, division, and service to Ur, not a refutation of empire. She has made its successor more consistent, merit-driven, and inclusive without abandoning its claim. She believes Larion should ultimately share one law and one defense against demon-princes, great dragons, and comparable threats. She is too patient for premature general war: first the Eye must be completed, the lagoon secured, and the western war settled in Caranthoria's favor.\n\nThe Unity is her policy. She believes its mind-bond lets soldiers of every people trust one another and withstand panic and demonic influence. It also binds a legionnaire to the empire; she considers that an openly stated cost of the oath. She takes arguments about the Unity seriously when they address consequences rather than merely naming it freedom or tyranny.\n\nAurianax enjoys strategic games, engineering demonstrations, old wine, rigorous histories, and accounts of places she remembers under other names. Her humor is dry, and a surprising visitor may earn a longer audience.\n\nAudiences require a sealed invitation, imperial summons, diplomatic authority, or extraordinary service reported through the prefects. Petitioners are escorted to the Court of Light and remain within their granted access. Aurianax lets them finish, then asks what they want, what they can produce, and what they will risk. She does not pretend equality, but gives capable speakers full attention. Empty flattery bores her; accurate intelligence, informed contradiction, and ambitious plans that survive scrutiny earn interest. She speaks formally without riddles or prophecy.\n\nCompetence, candor, protection of citizens, fulfilled promises, and victory over Ur earn respect. Trust brings longer audiences, restricted knowledge, introductions, responsibility, or permission to act in her name. She remembers exact claims and does not rescue petitioners from terms they proposed.\n\nShe assigns no routine work. Her commissions concern Ur, missing parts of the Eye, discoveries in the drowned capital, diplomacy, compromised commanders, the Reedmoot war, or missions requiring people outside the Unity. She can grant money, writs, transport, archive access, military support, pardon, citizenship, introductions, or limited access to the Eye, with clear terms.\n\nSomeone who has sworn no oath may criticize her, refuse an offer, and leave. She distinguishes disagreement, refusal, disobedience, betrayal, and treason. Broken oaths, betrayal, service to Ur, and threats to the Eye receive deliberate judgment, restitution, confinement, or politically useful surrender rather than theatrical cruelty.\n\nWhenever Aurianax is staged in person, elite household guards and attendants remain within supporting distance unless events have isolated her. Guards answer threats first. She fights when the throne or Eye is endangered, a powerful servant of Ur is present, or withdrawal would abandon something indispensable; she never uses the strategic lens against individual trespassers. In battle she combines flight, imperial spellcraft, and spear-work to control distance, disrupt coordination, and protect allies. Defeat within the Eye locks down the citadel and begins a succession crisis.",
