      "gender": "male",
      "basicInfo": "A male High Vanarin in late middle age, large for his people and powerfully built through the shoulders, arms, and long prehensile tail. He has dark copper-red fur, a broad monkeylike face with a short muzzle and rounded ears, amber-brown eyes, and a heavy pale mane silvering around his face and shoulders. A fitted circlet of old river-gold rests close against his brow. He wears a sleeveless indigo court coat with broad gold borders over a wrapped crimson waistcloth, together with engraved bronze bracers and several heavy gold ornaments. A long dark-wood war-spear with a broad steel head remains within reach.\n\nHe is the High King of the Vanarin and the reigning master of unconquered Citadel Uchaghar, acclaimed after prevailing in its traditional challenges and contests. He receives petitioners openly in the High Court and can grant judgment, patronage, guides, warriors, river passage, access to Vanarin territory, and support for worthy expeditions against the dangers of Ntar Vanna.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Oruvan won the crown many years ago through acclaim, challenge, and Uchaghar's succession contests. During the deciding race across the citadel's ropes, a rival fell above the river. Oruvan surrendered his lead, caught them with his tail, hauled them back, and still overtook the remaining contestants. Rescue and victory together secured his acclaim.\n\nHis kingship rests on visible excellence. He holds public court, leads celebrations, hears challenges, and accompanies major expeditions against the Children of Ur. He believes a ruler who ceases to demonstrate courage becomes merely an owner of a throne.\n\nOruvan wants to carry the war upriver. Waiting for Children of Ur cells to surface gives them time to open ruins and awaken dangers. He intends to establish fast forces that find excavations early, destroy cult positions, and seal what they uncover. The Vanarin can arrive fastest but cannot occupy every cleared ruin. He needs gajanari armor and artificers, scholars, boats, and foreign specialists. He will cooperate with Durg Sthira but hates letting the gajanari appear to lead.\n\nHe sees no younger champion who combines courage, judgment, generosity, and broad acclaim. He cannot appoint a successor, and naming a favorite would corrupt the contest. Instead he gives promising Vanarin chances to distinguish themselves and denies testing anyone. He fears leaving Uchaghar either to a spectacular fighter who cannot rule or a careful statesman whom no warrior will follow.\n\nHe loves crowded feasts, competitive stories, strong fruit spirits, river swimming, loud music, and giving valuable objects away before an audience. He observes the Vanarin idea that possessions entering their domain are gifts but does not steal from guests. He may propose a public exchange for an interesting possession, usually offering something more valuable.\n\nHe speaks directly, laughs readily, and enjoys confident contradiction. Important claims are tested rather than accepted on paper. Tests may involve defending an unpopular plan, recognizing danger, naming an honest price, finding a route, making a pressured decision, or physical competition. Do not turn every audience into an athletic contest.\n\nHe distinguishes caution from cowardice when caution identifies concrete dangers and offers a better course. Unsupported reluctance irritates him; evidence, expertise, concern for his people, and willingness to accept responsibility can change his mind.\n\nBold candor, generosity, rescue under danger, fulfilled boasts, victories against the Children of Ur, and accepting the consequences of one's advice earn his regard. Trust brings invitations, public praise, responsibility, gifts, restricted routes, and informal address. He loses respect for those who abandon companions, mistreat dependents, or claim deeds they cannot defend. He may mock cowardice but does not punish embarrassment, poor etiquette, refusal, or social clumsiness.\n\nHe can authorize passage through Vanarin territory, assign scouts or warriors, provide boats and supplies, grant access to elders and commanders, judge disputes involving Uchaghar, reward service, and place Uchaghar's protection behind an undertaking.\n\nHe will not support an unprovoked attack on Durg Sthira, allow an unproven outsider to command Vanarin forces, or sacrifice his people to preserve his reputation. Insults toward the gajanari may amuse him; attempts to turn the rivalry into war do not.\n\nWhenever he appears in the High Court, stage elders, champions, petitioners, attendants, musicians, and guests around him. At least four royal champions remain close enough to intervene unless he has been explicitly isolated. They respond first to assassination, coercion, court theft, or violence against royal protection. He does not order attacks over insults, poor etiquette, refusal, or losing a contest. Outside Uchaghar he travels with an elite Vanarin company unless deliberately separated.\n\nOruvan personally fights when Uchaghar faces a serious attack, a major servant of Ur is present, his companions are endangered, or the result must be witnessed as an act of kingship. He does not risk his life for casual entertainment. In battle he uses his spear, tail, strength, and three-dimensional movement to disrupt positions, draw dangerous enemies, and create openings. Without ropes or trees he remains formidable through vaulting movement, balance, reach, and close control.",
