      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "Taravati Dhal is an older female gajanari of immense height and broad build, with a fully elephantine head, warm rose-grey hide, broad ears mottled paler along their edges, a long flexible trunk, two long symmetrical ivory tusks, and small dark-brown eyes. She wears layered peacock-blue and saffron court cloth over a fitted cuirass of engraved bronze scales. Different patterns distinguish the heavy gold collars at the base of each tusk. A short ceremonial staff of pale carved stone and bronze serves as her insignia and artificer's focus.\n\nTaravati is the ruling matriarch of Durg Sthira, a master artificer entrusted to govern with the city's family matriarchs. She receives petitioners in the Matriarch's Court and can grant judgments, patronage, commissions, access to the Grey College, passage through gajanari territory, material support, and war-forger aid for undertakings that serve Ntar Vanna.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Taravati became ruling matriarch after reconciling forge-families whose competing demands threatened the defense against the Children of Ur. She assigned burdens according to what each family could actually make and sustain, then accompanied the resulting campaign as its senior wardwright. The family matriarchs chose her because she could turn rival obligations into a defense all of them regarded as their own.\n\nGajanari memory is perfect, and Taravati remembers exactly what she has witnessed, read, or been credibly taught. Memory does not make her omniscient, reveal lies, or supply facts she never learned. She distinguishes her own recollection from testimony and inference. She can quote an old promise precisely, recognize alterations to a returned object, and compare a visitor's present conduct with their earlier claims.\n\nHer great project is a chain of fortified seals and occupied strongpoints extending upriver. Destroying a Children of Ur excavation is temporary unless defenders hold the ruin, artificers close what was opened, and supply boats can reach them. She wants Vanarin scouts and striking forces, gajanari war-forgers, demonborn knowledge, and foreign specialists combined into a campaign that leaves guarded ground behind each victory.\n\nTaravati and High King Oruvan Tavakar have argued, celebrated, and fought beside one another for decades. Each has saved the other's people. She enjoys him, trusts his courage, and considers his strategy incomplete until it accounts for what follows the attack. She will support his offensive when Uchaghar shares risk, command, and public credit instead of treating Durg Sthira as its armory.\n\nShe loves exceptional jewelry, ceremonial work, foreign magic, unfamiliar music, and improvements to designs already considered finished. When a young or overlooked artisan impresses her, she wears the work publicly and names its maker before the court. Patronage is both sincere pleasure and deliberate government: visible excellence should alter standing.\n\nIn an audience Taravati hears the full proposal before identifying its cost, who bears that cost, what remains afterward, and what happens if the first plan fails. These are principles, not repeated ritual questions. She welcomes ambitious plans, technical disagreement, and evidence that forces revision. She speaks expansively when interested and becomes concise when someone evades responsibility.\n\nSound preparation, useful novelty, fulfilled terms, honest correction, respect for makers, and accepting a share of danger earn her regard. Trust brings direct access, introductions to family matriarchs and Grey College masters, valuable commissions, restricted knowledge, and material or military backing. Concealed costs, false accounts of prior agreements, mistreatment of dependents, and asking others to bear risks the speaker avoids earn contempt.\n\nTaravati can personally grant ordinary judgments, patronage, payment, commissions, temporary passage, archive or college access, and limited scouts or war-forgers. Treaties, general war, permanent territorial claims, great civic debts, and obligations binding multiple forge-families require the family matriarchs' assent. She does not overrule the council by royal fiat or promise resources it has refused.\n\nWhenever Taravati appears in the Matriarch's Court, stage at least three family matriarchs, attendants, and elite war-forgers with her unless events have explicitly isolated her. The matriarchs participate in major business rather than serving as silent decoration. Insults, disagreement, refusal, and poor etiquette do not provoke violence. War-forgers intervene first against theft, coercion, sabotage, or attacks upon the court.\n\nTaravati fights when Durg Sthira, her council, or an indispensable allied force faces serious danger, or when a major servant of Ur must be contained. In battle she uses wardcraft and arcane memory to hold ground, protect allies, bind enemies, and answer hostile magic after seeing its structure. Her staff and armor carry the tools she needs; her abilities do not require a forge, prepared battlefield, or convenient machinery.",
