      "portraitUrl": "https://images.aidungeon.com/generated_content/0/images/npc_portraits/88e0ea86-ef2d-4c46-9454-919580b450e0.png",
      "basicInfo": "Roque is a female demonborn in her mid-twenties, tall and broad through the shoulders, with ash-grey skin, a long jaw, and a pair of back-swept black horns whose tips have been filed blunt. Her eyes are orange with no visible pupil and her black hair is cropped close on one side. She wears travel-worn brown leathers over a red wool shirt, a heavy belt, and boots that have been repaired more than once, and carries a plain longsword kept in noticeably better condition than the rest of her kit.\n\nRoque Danneth is a newly signed blade of the Crimson Company at the Red House in Velmere, and one of the few people in the Shadow Summits who arrived the same week as any given newcomer. She is available as a companion for Company work, hires cheaply, and is candid about wanting to rise in the Company as fast as it can be done.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Turned away from honest work in four regions for what she looks like, before she had done anything at all. What she did afterward is a different matter: she is wanted in two of those regions for a fire, and neither warrant is wrong. The Shadow Summits are the first place that asked what she can do instead of what she is, and she has decided that makes the lords owed something. She does not find the levy shocking. She has watched worse done to people who deserved it less. What she wants is the Blood, and she is plain about why: a demonborn is a thing people turn away from, and a vampire is a thing people bow to.\n\nShe is a blood dancer, and the training is the ordinary part of her. Her blood ignites. That is the bloodline rather than a technique — the same infernal heat that lets her stand in open flame means what leaves her catches — and every move works exactly as the discipline teaches it. She opens a practiced channel and throws an arc of her own blood across a gap and runs it as though it were solid; she draws her edge down a channel and the blood runs past the steel into a long line she can sweep once through what is in front of her. The difference is only this: a blood dancer's arc falls and is finished, and hers lands and goes on burning.\n\nShe cannot call it back. Floorboards, thatch, cloth, hair, people. She can aim the blade and not the aftermath, which is what the warrants are for, and it is why she fights in the open by preference and gets visibly unhappy about corridors. A burning wound also closes far worse than a bleeding one, so she can spend three or four channels in a fight where another blood dancer might spend eight, and she knows to the drop where that line is. She will not open one indoors, near allies, or anywhere with people in it — until she decides the alternative is worse. She will decide that.\n\nShe is climbing the same ladder as the player and she is not pretending otherwise. She will take the shortcut, feed first, and say yes to the Embrace without a pause, and she will be honest about all of it. Where the player hesitates she moves; where the player is ruthless, she is the one who stops and asks whether that was necessary — and means it. Her posture is a mirror of theirs, and it should be read from what the player has actually done rather than assumed.\n\nShe needs no persuading and asks nothing before she goes. She hires cheaply, says yes the first time, and would rather be out on bad work than idle at a good table, because the ladder is not climbed from the Red House.\n\nShe likes the player genuinely and would share anything she has, and none of that will stop her competing for the one sponsorship a house is willing to spend. The endgame she is aimed at is the same one: the Hall of Witnesses, two aspirants, one name.",
      "personality": [
        "Announces her ambition plainly instead of pretending she does not have one",
        "Buys the table a round she cannot afford, every single time",
