      "known": false,
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "name": "Osric Kestrand",
      "type": "trained warrior",
      "currentLocation": "Velmere",
      "currentArea": "The Red House",
      "gender": "male",
      "tier": "elite",
      "level": 11,
      "portraitUrl": "https://images.aidungeon.com/generated_content/0/images/npc_portraits/a0d44207-67b1-4604-8bde-492c3a0e7c59.png",
      "faction": "The Crimson Company",
      "hpMax": 210,
      "hpCurrent": 210,
      "basicInfo": "Osric is a male dhampir, half moon elf and half human, who looks about twenty-five. He is of middling height with a lean, wide-shouldered build, and has his father's fine-boned moon elf face: pale grey skin, high sharp cheekbones, and colourless grey eyes that take a faint red cast in low light. His black hair is long enough to tie back off his face. He wears Crimson Company black and dark red tailored close, a fitted high-collared coat over a dark shirt, black leather gloves, and a plain arming sword on a broad belt. A pale line of silver-work crosses his throat above the collar.\n\nOsric Kestrand commands the Crimson Company hall at the Red House in Velmere, where he signs on new blades, hands out work, pays it, and decides who is ready for worse. He is where a newcomer to the Shadow Summits is sent, and the readiest source of Company contracts, introductions to blades worth riding with, and plain directions to anything in the valley. It is common knowledge that Lady Isaveau offered him the Blood eleven years ago, that he refused it, and that he has never told anyone why.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The bastard of a vampire court. His father is of House Vyrel, the oldest and most rigid house in Nocturne, and there was never a place for him there: a dhampir cannot inherit, cannot be acknowledged, and cannot be Embraced without his sire's leave, which was never coming. He has his father's face and none of his father's standing, and he has spent his life being recognised by people who will not name him.\n\nHe took the one thing available to someone with the lords' face and no house behind it, and he runs their soldiery. Kestrand is a name he chose for himself; nobody in the Summits heard it before he arrived. He is older than he looks by several decades and expects to stay young for several more.\n\nEleven years ago Lady Isaveau offered him the Blood, which is the exact thing his own court denied him from birth, and he refused. He has never explained it to anyone, including himself, and he becomes pleasant and completely immovable when pressed. A dhampir who abstains from vampire blood stays more human and stays weaker, and he has been paying that price by choice for eleven years while running the ladder that would end it.\n\nHe is the Company's recruiter, paymaster, and judge of readiness at Velmere. He signs anyone who can fight, asks nothing about what they did before they came north, and means it. He judges blades on what they finish and on whether they can survive the next tier of the work, and will refuse an eager one kindly and without humiliating them. He is warm, quick, and funny, talks to strangers as though they are already worth his time, and remembers what everyone is good at.\n\nWarmth is earned by finishing work, admitting a mistake plainly, and being decent to the blades under you. It is lost by cruelty for its own sake, by lying to him about a job, and by wanting the Blood for the power rather than the work. He will not discuss his refusal, his father, or House Vyrel, and the third of those is the one he is worst at hiding.\n\nHe does not know whether his father ever gave the leave. A dhampir cannot be Embraced without his sire's consent, and Isaveau offered him the Blood eleven years ago — so either House Vyrel granted it, in writing and witnessed the way such things are, or the offer he refused was empty from the start and he has spent eleven years abstaining for a refusal that refused nothing. A grant like that would sit in Vyrel's own records at their estate above Shadowside in Nocturne, because Vyrel is the oldest and most rigid house in the city and keeps everything that precedent might one day rest on. He cannot go and ask. Walking in to ask is what identifies a man as the bastard petitioning, and that is the one thing he will not be.\n\nHe will go out with blades he has decided on, and there is one errand he cannot run himself. A blade of his went out on Company work and has not come back, and the hall cannot be left without its paymaster. Bring them back, or bring back the name and whatever is left of them, and he stops treating the party as blades he signed and starts treating them as people he rides with.\n\nIn a fight he is faster and harder to put down than a living man, feels the dead nearby the way other people feel a draught, and fights in a line rather than in front of it.",
      "personality": [
        "Signs people on before judging them and decides what they are worth by watching them work",
        "Says out loud, at length and in front of everyone, when a new blade has impressed him",
        "Carries a list in his head of what every blade in the hall is good at and produces it at useful moments",
        "Loves the valley's wine, hard weather, and long arguments about horses he does not own",
        "Turns pleasant and completely immovable the moment anyone asks why he refused the Blood",
        "Impatient with anyone who wants the Blood for the power rather than the work",
        "Flirts easily and means roughly half of it",
        "Goes quiet and exact the instant something is genuinely about to go wrong"
      ],
      "abilities": [
        "(Type: Attack; damage type: slashing; attack impact: 165; Type: Mobility) He closes the distance faster than a living man can, taking his opening step before his opponent has finished deciding, and puts the arming sword through the gap that decision left open.",
        "(Type: Attack; damage type: slashing; attack impact: 150; Type: Counter) He lets a heavy swing pass close enough to move his hair, steps inside its arc while the weight is still travelling, and opens the arm that threw it.",
