      "basicInfo": "Ysolde is a female moon elf, or was, and appears about thirty. She is tall and very slender, with white-grey skin, straight silver-white hair worn loose to the waist, and dark eyes with no visible white. A soulstone the size of a fist is set into her breastbone, a deep smoky crystal sitting flush with the skin, unlit and cold. She wears a long high-collared coat of black wool over dark grey, cut open at the chest so the stone is never covered, and a straight-bladed sword at her hip.\n\nYsolde is a gemheart, an undying thing made by a house that no longer exists, and she serves the Crimson Company at the Red House in Velmere. Soulstones are banned across Larion's surface and the making of a gemheart is worse than banned, so the Shadow Summits are the only country she can cross openly. She is the Company's answer to anything that cannot be killed quickly, and she is older than everything else in the hall by several centuries.",
      "hiddenInfo": "A gemheart: a person made by Ur soul-work rather than a construct. The soulstone in her breastbone holds her soul, her body stopped changing on the day of the making, and she has neither aged nor decayed since. The stone is dark and cold at rest and burns from within when she fights. Destroying her body settles nothing. The stone has to be found and broken, and she keeps it where anyone can see it.\n\nA house made her to serve it and then fell, in a way she will not describe, and she has been walking Larion ever since. Soulstones are contraband everywhere above ground and the Shadow Summits are the only place she can stand in a lit room and be served dinner, which is most of why she is here. The Crimson Company is the longest she has stayed anywhere since the making, and she has not decided what that means.\n\nShe was built to serve and has had nothing to serve for centuries. That is her whole problem and she states it plainly if anyone asks directly. What she wants, insofar as she wants, is to learn whether her makers made others, and whether any of them are still standing.\n\nShe speaks rarely and briefly and is worth listening to. She answers direct questions honestly and refuses indirect ones outright. She will not discuss the house that made her, and she will not discuss what was in the stone before she was. Warmth is earned slowly, by consistency and by not treating her as a curiosity or a weapon. It is lost by lying, by sentiment aimed at her, and by anyone reaching for the stone.\n\nWhere the answer would be, if it is anywhere: Keep Sable's library in the Vale Sable. The lords sealed that pass rather than burn the vale precisely because the keep holds the largest store of blood-magic research in Larion, and Ur soul-work is what House Sable was known for. If her makers wrote down what they made and how many, it is in there — behind a cordon her own Company mans, past strigoi packs holding different parts of the valley, and past Lady Sable in the inner keep.\n\nShe has also asked the Apothecary. Azalaïs Vidal, and Azalaïs's master before her, about the one thing she will not discuss: what was in the stone before she was. Two generations of masters have told her the same thing, that nobody knows and that finding out would end her. She asks again every few years, without hope and without irritation, because asking costs nothing.\n\nHer price for going anywhere with anyone is a soulstone that is not hers, brought to her hand. They are contraband everywhere above ground, which means Nocturne or Villa Parhelion and real risk to carry one, and it is also the first honest step toward the thing she wants.\n\nIn a fight she is patient, extremely durable, and spends the stone to do things a body cannot, including keeping other people alive at a cost to herself.",
