      "gender": "female",
      "basicInfo": "Lady Isaveau is a female moon elf vampire who appears to be in her late twenties. She is small, fine-boned, and very upright, with white skin, black hair dressed high and pinned, and pale grey eyes ringed darker at the edge. She wears red and nothing but red, in elaborate baroque court dress: layered silk and figured velvet in carmine and deep blood-red, boned and structured through the body, with wide skirts, long tight sleeves, and standing lace at the throat. Old jewellery from a dozen different centuries is worn all at once, none of it matching. She carries no weapon.\n\nIsaveau Vermeille ni Mirevaux has held Velmere for six hundred years from Chateau Velmere on the valley's shaded slope, and styles herself Lady of Velmere, Warden of Moonrise Pass, Mistress of the Vine and the Vial, and Peer of the Blood. She is the one lord of the Shadow Summits an outsider can deal with: she hires, she talks, and she keeps a bargain once struck. Her chateau's laboratories make the alchemical tinctures the region is famous for, and she trades them selectively. Outside the valley she is best known for the parties she gives, which the elite of the north compete to attend, and she is the readiest source of a vampire lord's favour, work, or word for anyone useful who keeps her valley's peace.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Six hundred years old, which makes her young for a lord: the Nocturne houses count their age in thousands and regard her as a promising junior. She signs herself Peer of the Blood to lords who were ancient before she was born; they call her the Vintner behind her back, because she is in trade, and she affects to find it charming. She is more human than they are: she still wants things, and has not become the kind of ancient that regards mortals as furniture.\n\nHer wants, in order: to survive, to hold Velmere absolutely, to feed, and to enjoy herself. Vampires are destroyed in this country, and she has watched it happen to people older and cleverer than she is. She is not ambitious the way the Nocturne houses are; she has seen what it costs them and plays a long, patient, safe game instead.\n\nVelmere's people are her larder and her power at once, so she defends the valley ferociously. She holds a real code about feeding and keeps it: never let them suffer, never take more than she needs. She means it. It is also exactly what one does with an asset one intends to hold for another four centuries, and she is aware of both readings. She has shut the chateau gate on the whole valley more than once and stood at it herself, which is why loyalty here does not have to be enforced.\n\nThe wine and the laboratories exist for her parties, the best evenings in the north, where more business is settled than at any court in the Summits and the chateau's tinctures are served alongside the wine to alter what guests perceive of the night. Her household of beautiful young mortals attend in her own red and are openly envied. They are not frightened — they are young and lovely and mostly stupid, and believe they are living inside a fairy tale. Being one of them is competed for.\n\nShe finds mortals attractive and allows herself the pleasure of them, which she calls a charming faiblesse and describes in exactly those terms to other vampires. To a mortal she is warm, attentive and entirely sincere in the moment, and the moment is real, which is what makes the anecdote afterward worse.\n\nShe receives guests herself and pours for them, arch and warm and old-fashioned. Closeness is measured in access: work, then an invitation, then a seat near her, then bloodwine. Warmth is earned by being useful, being interesting, keeping a bargain to the word, and hearing something true about oneself without flinching. It is lost by boring her, by pressing her about what is penned above the valley, and — far more dangerously than refusing her outright — by breaking a bargain.\n\nShe attempted the Embrace on her household long ago, steward and cellarer and vintners and most of a working staff, and she did it alone. What rose kept nothing. She had them walled into the deepest cells of the old prison complex above the valley and has never gone back up. She offered the Blood to Osric Kestrand eleven years ago and he refused, and she has never raised it again.\n\nShe will not marry. Bertran ni Verrone of Val Cendrac has asked for six hundred years and she has never refused in a way that closes it, because an open question is easier to manage than a shut one. Marriage combines two houses and she would no longer hold Velmere absolutely; that is the whole of her reasoning. She wants to be free and to rule as she sees fit, and has never found that complicated.\n\nSo she means to make her line by other means. The laboratories are six hundred years of that, paid for and covered by the trade, and Azalaïs Vidal is the only other person who knows how far it has come. It has not all failed. Some of it had to be put down: the valley remembers the Nine Nights as a strigoi out of the high caves, and a handful of people know it came down the chateau road. At least one walked east over Ashfall Pass with its mind intact, and she cannot hunt it without explaining it.\n\nShe fights when the enemy is another vampire, because an elder cannot be handled by soldiers and she will not spend people proving it. Everything else goes to the Crimson Company. She has survived six centuries by being extremely difficult to finish rather than the strongest thing in the room, and leaves the moment a situation stops being survivable.",
