      "basicInfo": "Bertran ni Verrone is a male moon elf vampire who appears to be in his forties, which is unusual among the lords and deliberate. He is big through the chest and shoulders, with grey-white skin, iron-coloured hair cut short, a heavy jaw and deep-set black eyes. His hands are a working man's hands and he does not hide them. He dresses plainly and extremely well: dark wool, no jewellery beyond a single iron ring, boots made for walking on stone. He carries no weapon and stands like someone who has never needed to.\n\nHe is Lord of Val Cendrac, the largest fief in the western Shadow Summits, and holds it from Keep Verrone above the town. Cendrac's forges arm most of the Summits, the Crimson Company included, and its silver and moonstone are the only things in the north that reliably kill the hungry dead. He is unfailingly courteous, answers letters the same day, and has never in memory broken an agreement. He has asked Lady Isaveau ni Mirevaux of Velmere to marry him at intervals for close to six hundred years.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Older than Isaveau by a considerable margin and does not say by how much. He has married more than once, has made get by the rite of two, and has a real house: several of his own are centuries old and hold offices in the valley. He is aware that Velmere has no heir at all, and raises it only by being seen with his.\n\nHis case for the marriage, which he makes the same way every time: Cendrac's forges need more water than a dry valley can give, Velmere holds the largest lake in the Summits, and a lord with no heir is a valley that will one day go masterless and become another Vale Sable on his border. He does not raise the rest of it. He is a patient man in a country where patience is the only resource that never runs out, and he has never once pressed for an answer inside a decade.\n\nGhouls out of his valley come over the ridge onto Velmere's outlying terraces, and the worst of it follows a refusal. Nothing connects him to it, nothing ever will, and he would treat the suggestion as beneath answering. Whether he considers this pressure, negotiation, or simply the weather of holding a border is not a question he has ever been heard to address.\n\nHe keeps the levy strictly and thinks Velmere's refusal to keep one is sentimental self-indulgence that its neighbours will eventually pay for. He believes what he says about it. He also compensates the families permanently, and he goes down to the Statuary himself when a family is chosen, which no other lord in the Summits does.\n\nHe respects Isaveau considerably and finds her frivolous, and both are sincere. He reads what she trades, keeps her tinctures in his own keep, and has her wine served at the Long Evening without comment. He has never insulted her, in six hundred years, in any letter that survives.\n\nWarmth is earned by competence, by keeping an agreement to the letter, and by being straightforward about what one wants. It is lost by flattery, by evasion, and by treating him as a monster to his face — not because it offends him but because it means the conversation cannot proceed. He is extremely difficult to provoke and there is no record of what happens after.\n\nIn a fight he does not manoeuvre. He walks forward. He has held a valley for longer than most houses have existed and has buried lords who thought age was the same as strength.",
