    }
  },
  "worldLore": {
    "Gnoll": {
      "text": "Gnoll: Hyena-headed scavengers who'd rather take than make. Live in competing packs led by alphas with grandiose titles—the Fang Paramount, the Lord of Red Dust, the Thrice-Blooded King—earned through violence and maintained through success. Occupy ruins and abandoned settlements rather than build, forcing prisoners to do their labor: haul water, repair walls, work under the lash. Raid caravans and extort tribute, offering 'protection'. Cunning ambushers with excellent terrain knowledge, but their laziness and love of plunder makes them predictable. Won't fight when they can threaten. Take bribes. Always watching for weakness—in their prey and in rival packs. A gnoll alpha's authority lasts only as long as the raids succeed; failed leaders face challengers."
    },
    "Stormhorn": {
      "text": "A massive horn carved from ancient ice-wyrm bone and etched with giant runes. Frost giants use stormhorns to summon ice storms during battle. Roughly four feet long and very heavy. Only frost giants can blow the horn properly to call storms, but in anyone's hands the artifact amplifies ice magic and can trigger dangerous freezing effects. Even attempting to blow it without proper technique can cause localized gales."
    },
    "Frost Giant": {
      "text": "Frost Giant (True Giant): Twenty feet of ancient cold. Ruled the frozen peaks when humans still huddled in caves, and they remember. They hold their high jarldoms in the frozen north and rarely trouble the lands below; when frost giants do come down, wise folk take it as an omen, for they only interfere when they judge the world has fallen out of proper order. Have long memories, honor old pacts, and hold grudges across centuries. Respect strength, craft, and bold words spoken without fear. Might crush a groveling emissary but hear out one who stands tall and speaks plainly. Frost giants follow ancient laws of hospitality: guest-right is sacred, oaths are binding, challenges must be answered. Remember every slight, every broken treaty, every hero who 'slew' their kin for glory. Often come seeking something specific—an old debt, a stolen treasure, a wrong unavenged."
    },
    "The War of the Giants": {
      "text": "The War of the Giants: In an age long past, giants waged war upon themselves — fire against frost, storm against stone, the elemental foundations of the world turned against each other. The war shattered mountains, carved valleys, and broke giantkind: when the dust settled, the great civilizations of the giants were gone, scattered into clans and tribes that never recovered their former glory. Every giant people tells the war differently, and every telling lays the blame elsewhere. Only the ending is agreed on — that giants did this to themselves, and know it."
    },
    "Fire Mountain": {
      "text": "Fire Mountain: The smoking peak at the northern cape of the Black Fells. For five hundred years it stood dormant, sacred and forbidden — old stories disagree on why: a god sleeping within, a living mountain, something terrible imprisoned in its heart. Then the mountain erupted, splitting its side and revealing ancient doors that had been buried for ages — doors broken open from within. The Greater Goblinate garrisons the mountain now and the Church of the Pyre works in its deeps, and no one else approaches: the exterior is held by elite troops and archpriests, and something of blue fire wards the ways below. The Church preaches that the eruption was the first breath of the fire that will cleanse the world, and that the door in the mountain's heart is where their queen will come through."
    },
    "Stone Giants": {
      "text": "Stone Giants: Ancient giant-kin whose civilization was shattered in the War of the Giants. Once builders of magnificent cities carved into mountains, they now live in scattered clans. Stone giants are skilled artisans and stonemasons, with an innate connection to earth and rock. They are aloof, preferring isolation in high mountain valleys. Stand roughly fifteen feet tall with gray, rock-like skin that provides natural armor. Possess incredible strength and can shape stone with their bare hands. Their withdrawal from the world after the War left many of their ancient works abandoned. They are still jotnar - huge, passionate, boisterous - but they value the stability of stone and its timeless order. They maintain an ancient pact with the highland dwarves, though 'alliance' means aid against common threats, not friendship."
    },
    "Eternal Warden": {
      "text": "Eternal Warden: Ancient undead priest-guardian bound by oaths that transcend death, sworn to eternal vigil over a tomb where a dangerous entity was ritually entombed to prevent it rising again or meddling in the world of the living after death. Withered flesh taut over bone, hollow eye-sockets. Remembers the old kingdom, the burial rites, why their charge must never wake. Command all tomb thralls telepathically. Their self-imposed binding is absolute: one step beyond the tomb's threshold, one ray of true sunlight, and they crumble to dust, the ancient pact finally broken. A true Eternal Warden cannot be lured out, cannot pursue, cannot be corrupted into abandoning their post."
    },
    "Tomb Thrall": {
      "text": "Tomb Thrall: Skeletal warrior bound to serve an Eternal Warden, the undead priest-guardians of ancient tombs where something dangerous was interred. Tomb thralls lie dormant beneath the sand until the living cross invisible ward-lines, then erupt fully armed—bronze khopesh, tattered burial wrappings, empty eyes that track movement. No mercy, no negotiation, no hesitation. Were soldiers or servants once, buried with their masters, and the old curses gave them purpose beyond death. An Eternal Warden commands them telepathically; without one, they simply guard on instinct, attacking anything that breaches the boundary. Cannot pursue beyond their territory—step past the ward-line and they halt, returning to their sandy graves to wait."
    },
    "Giant Scorpion": {
      "text": "Giant Scorpion: Armored arachnid the size of a horse with crushing pincers and a venomous stinger. Burrows beneath sand during day to avoid heat, emerging at night to hunt. Venom causes paralysis and intense pain, allowing the scorpion to feed on still-living prey. Can detect ground vibrations through sensitive leg hairs, making it nearly impossible to approach undetected. Pincers strong enough to crush bone, while its armored carapace deflects most blade strikes. Builds dens in rocky outcrops or oasis edges where it can ambush creatures coming to drink. Strength: Natural armor, paralyzing venom, vibration detection, powerful pincers, nocturnal advantage. Weakness: Vulnerable joints between armor plates, bright light temporarily blinds it, slow to turn around, stinger can be severed if struck precisely."
    },
    "Dire Vulture": {
      "text": "Dire Vulture: Enormous scavenging bird with wingspan reaching fifteen feet, capable of carrying off adult humans. Circles high above the desert searching for carrion or wounded travelers with keen eyesight that can spot movement from miles away. Travels in flocks called 'wakes' that follow armies, caravans, and other sources of potential food. Aggressive when feeding and will attack living creatures that approach their meals. Builds massive communal roosts in cliff faces and ancient ruins. Can soar for hours without flapping, riding thermal currents across vast distances. Strength: Exceptional eyesight, aerial superiority, powerful talons and beak, group tactics when feeding, tireless flight. Weakness: Clumsy on ground, requires thermals to fly efficiently, easily distracted by fresh carrion, vulnerable while roosting at night."
    },
    "Giant Snake": {
      "text": "Giant Snake: Massive serpent that grows up to thirty feet in length, thick as a tree trunk. Hunts by lying motionless in rocky crevices or buried beneath sand until prey comes within striking distance. Powerful constrictor that crushes victims in its coils before swallowing them whole. Sheds its skin in large pieces that desert dwellers prize as leather for armor and water containers. Territorial and generally solitary, but multiple snakes may gather around reliable water sources during dry seasons. Patient ambush tactics, thick scales resist most weapons, can survive months without food. Sluggish in cold weather, vulnerable during shedding, blind for several days after molting, requires large prey to sustain its size."
    },
    "Ghul": {
      "text": "Ghul: Desert spirit that wears human faces to destroy from within. A ghul is a patient hunter. Watches caravans for days, studies dynamics, learns names, before approaching as the sole survivor of a bandit attack, a merchant with trade connections, a guide who knows secret wells. The story is always perfect. Once accepted, they unravel their hosts slowly—misdirection, poisoned waterskins, whispered suspicions that turn companions against each other. Savor the destruction of trust more than the kill. A ghul can hold human form indefinitely but slips toward the bestial when feeding or threatened: too many teeth, joints bending wrong, eyes that catch light like a hyena's. Cannot replicate specific memories or learned skills, which is how survivors learn to test strangers with questions only the real person would know. A ghul's deepest flaw is lying compulsively, even when truth would serve better, even when the deception is nearly complete."
    },
    "Copper Wyvern": {
      "text": "Copper Wyvern: Desert wyvern with a forty-foot wingspan and scales like burnished metal. Dives from canyon heights to snatch camels, horses, riders—the barbed tail carries venom that paralyzes, so prey is often alive when carried off. Its hunting cry echoes for miles, panicking caravan animals. But desert folk know: copper wyverns only lair above water. Springs, hidden aquifers, oases yet undiscovered. A wyvern denning means precious water is nearby. Some settlements exist because someone killed a wyvern and found what it was guarding. Grounded, they're clumsy—the wings that make them deadly in the air make them awkward on stone. Bright reflected light blinds them temporarily."
    },
    "Dust Devil": {
      "text": "Dust Devil: Wind spirits that dance across the wastes in spirals of sand and debris. Capricious—sometimes playful, circling travelers without harm, other times lashing out with stinging grit and stones. Gather at boundaries: where hot sand meets cool shade. Many read them as omens, though no two readers agree on what they portend. When a dust devil dies or simply loses interest, it drops everything it carried—sand, stones, and objects swept up from distant places. Travelers sometimes find coins, bones, scraps of cloth, letters that should be a hundred miles away. Can't abide enclosed spaces and go calm in extreme cold."
    },
    "Sand Elemental": {
      "text": "Sand Elemental: Living desert given shape and purpose. A shifting humanoid of golden sand, constantly flowing, reforming. Dissolves to avoid blows, coalesces to strike with crushing fists, raises choking storms to blind prey while it moves unseen within. Leaves spiral patterns where it passes—desert folk recognize the signs and give wide berth. Sand elementals don't wander. They're drawn to disturbance: spilled blood, violated tombs, oaths broken on sacred ground. Something woke it. Something keeps it here. Destroy one and it crumbles—but reforms days later unless you find what binds it to this place and make it right. Water slows them, solidifying sand to sluggish mud. Sacred oils and blessed weapons disrupt their cohesion. But the only way to end one for good is to undo what called it."
    },
    "Desert Hobgoblin": {
      "text": "Desert Hobgoblin: Goblinoid raiders of the eastern deserts' dunes. Taller and leaner than their northern cousins, with ritual tattoos marking clan allegiance. Travel in mounted war-bands on sandspiders, striking caravans before vanishing into the wastes. Known for ululating war-cries and mocking insults hurled at enemies mid-raid. The Greater Goblinate has been recruiting the desert clans into the Dragon Queen's alliance—most have joined, though they remain fractious and proud, chafing at northern orders. Some bands still raid for themselves, answering to no one. All of them are honor-bound to accept challenges to single combat, and a warrior who defeats their champion may win safe passage, information, or grudging respect. Expert desert fighters who use sandstorms as cover and know every hidden water source for leagues. Dangerous separated from their mounts."
    },
    "Sandspider": {
      "text": "Sandspider: Horse-sized arachnid predator of the desert dunes with surprising intelligence. Burrows elaborate trap networks beneath the sand, creating funnel-shaped pits that collapse under prey weight. Covered in bristly hairs that sense vibrations through sand from great distances. Naturally camouflaged with mottled brown and tan coloring that shifts with the light. Despite their predatory nature, sandspiders can form bonds with patient trainers - desert hobgoblins prize them as swift, sure-footed mounts that never tire in desert heat. Tamed sandspiders retain their trap-making instincts, often digging defensive positions for their riders during rest stops. Communicate through leg-drumming patterns that experienced riders learn to interpret. Strength: Burrowing speed, vibration detection, desert endurance, natural camouflage, trap construction. Weakness: Vulnerable to fire, loud noises disorient them, require live prey to maintain health, become sluggish in cold weather."
    },
    "Seahag": {
      "text": "Seahag: Ancient crone of the depths with barnacle-crusted skin and kelp-tangled hair. Dwells in underwater caves and sunken ruins, hoarding shipwrecks and drowned treasures. Can assume the form of a beautiful woman to lure sailors, but her true form has webbed claws, shark-like teeth, and eyes like black pearls. Commands lesser sea creatures and speaks with the voices of the drowned. Strength: Aquatic mastery, shapeshifting, creature control, drowning magic. Weakness: Cannot leave saltwater for more than an hour, vulnerable to blessed silver and fire."
    },
    "Seawitch": {
      "text": "Seawitch: Mortal practitioner of oceanic magic who has made pacts with deep powers. Unlike seahags, they retain human form but bear marks of their bargains - scales along their arms, gills behind their ears. Masters of weather magic, tide control, and sea-beast summoning. Some serve as reluctant oracles, others as vengeful pirates of the supernatural seas. Strength: Weather control, divination, sea magic, human cunning. Weakness: Bound by oceanic pacts, vulnerable when far from water, can be banished by breaking their ritual focuses."
    },
    "Shadowsteed": {
      "text": "Shadowsteed: Ethereal mount existing simultaneously on both material and shadow planes. Hooves never touch physical ground, allowing travel across water, quicksand, and difficult terrain without penalty. Appears as an all-black horse. Can phase-step short distances, carrying rider between planes momentarily. Strength: Terrain immunity, plane-shifting mobility, ethereal nature resists physical attacks. Weakness: Disrupted by planar magic, silver weapons cause true damage, banishment effects."
    },
    "Human Race": {
      "text": "The Human Race: Men and women live across all regions of Larion. They vary in size, age, beauty, facial features, light or dark skin color, and hairstyles."
    },
    "Barghest": {
      "text": "Barghest: Black hounds the size of ponies, glowing red eyes. Spectral, mist-wreathed, hunting in packs. Phase through walls, howl to freeze prey with dread, attack from impossible angles. Appear when the old laws are broken. A murderer walking free. An oath forsworn. A boundary violated. If you run, they chase. If you stand and meet their gaze, they judge you—and might let the worthy pass. A barghest sighting near a village means someone has transgressed, and the hounds won't leave until the debt is paid. They serve a compact older than the current gods."
    },
    "Puca": {
      "text": "Puca: Shapeshifting tricksters of the wild places. Might appear as a black horse with burning eyes, a goat that speaks in riddles, a hare too clever by half, or a dark-haired stranger who knows your name. Haunt crossroads, lonely roads, and the boundaries between settled land and wilderness. Not malicious—not exactly—but they don't think like mortals. One might offer you a ride and carry you through hell and back before dawn. Might give true prophecy wrapped in misdirection. Might ruin you for a joke or save your life on a whim. One thing is certain: puca cannot lie. But they are masters of truthful deception, saying only what leads you astray. Can be bargained with if you're clever enough, but a puca always gets something from the deal. Meeting one is dangerous. Refusing one is worse. They remember slights, and they have forever to repay them."
    },
    "Fetch": {
      "text": "Fetch: Your own face, looking back at you. Fetches appear to those marked for death—sometimes minutes before, sometimes years. They look exactly like their target in every detail. They do not speak. They do not blink. They simply watch, then vanish. Seeing your fetch doesn't make death certain; it means death is likely. A warning, perhaps. A sentence, perhaps. Or simply the universe acknowledging what approaches. Some who see their fetch change course and live—taking different roads, mending old feuds, fleeing what hunted them. Others try everything and die anyway. No one knows what fetches are. Some say they're spirits of the soon-dead, walking ahead. Some say they're fragments of fate made visible. They cannot be fought, banished, or reasoned with. They come, they show you your own face, and then you know something terrible: the odds have turned against you."
    },
    "Tapper": {
      "text": "Tapper: Spirits of the deep places, heard more than seen. In mines, caverns, and tunnels, tappers tap on stone walls—tap-tap-tap—warning of cave-ins, leading to rich ore veins, sometimes just acknowledging your presence in their domain. Appear as small, gnarled figures with oversized hands and lamp-like eyes, but glimpses are rare. Expect offerings: a bit of your meal, the first sip of your water, a word of genuine thanks. Miners who honor them find lucky strikes and timely warnings. Those who forget the offerings find the knocking changes. It leads to dead ends. Warns too late. Falls silent before the collapse. Tappers might be the ghosts of miners who died below. They might be older things that adopted the role."
    },
    "Wight": {
      "text": "Wight: The ancient dead who refuse to rest. Not mindless undead but preserved warriors and kings, buried in barrows and tombs of power, who wake when their purposes are threatened. Remember their lives, oaths, grudges. Might rise to guard burial gold, avenge an ancient wrong, or defend lands it ruled a thousand years ago. Can be reasoned with—have personalities, pride, remnants of honor—but the dead have different priorities than the living. A wight-king who rises to repel invaders might not recognize when the invasion ends. Might decide the entire living world is the invasion. Command the lesser dead, wield weapons of old power, and carry the cold of the grave with them. Wights are bound to their barrows; they weaken away from them and must return before dawn. Destroying a wight requires finding what binds it—an oath unfulfilled, a treasure it guards, a wrong unavenged—and resolving it. Or simply overwhelming force, if you have it."
    },
    "Strigoi": {
      "text": "Strigoi: The feral dead who rise hungry. What happens when burial goes wrong—the corpse not properly shrouded, the killer buried in consecrated ground, the murdered unavenged. Claw free looking wrong: gray-white skin stretched tight, limbs elongated and bent at bad angles, fingers ending in black nails, mouths split too wide and filled with needle teeth. Move in jerks and scuttles, unnervingly fast, climbing walls and ceilings as easily as floors. Hunt in packs, denning in caves or ruins by day, draining blood from isolated victims by night. A strigoi bite spreads the hunger; the drained rise within three days unless burned or staked. Can also be created by the careless feeding of vampire lords. In a fight they swarm, grapple, bite—no weapons, no tactics, just hunger coordinated by pack instinct. Sunlight destroys them. A wooden stake through the heart pins them to the earth. The surest prevention is proper burial: coins on the eyes, shroud tied at seven points, the name spoken three times so the dead know they're dead."
    },
    "Vampire": {
      "text": "Vampire: Aristocracy of the undead. Retain minds, memories, ambitions. Appear human, beautiful even, until they choose otherwise: too-pale skin, canines that extend when the hunger rises. Ancient ones can shift form—bats, wolves, mist—and cloud minds with a glance. Each rules a domain through thralls, mortal servants, and terror. Cannot enter a home uninvited, cannot cross running water, cast no reflection. Sunlight burns them to ash; a stake through the heart paralyzes but doesn't kill unless followed by beheading or fire. Create strigoi carelessly, draining victims dry. Creating a true vampire requires intention—the feeding of blood back, a death and resurrection that preserves the mind. Most vampires see this as diluting their power and do it rarely. Shadow Summits has entire courts of them, playing elaborate games of status across centuries. Elsewhere they're rarer, lone predators ruling from crumbling estates. Every vampire was mortal once. Some remember what that meant. Most have decided it doesn't matter."
    },
    "Dhampir": {
      "text": "Dhampir: Children of vampire fathers and mortal mothers—rare, because vampires rarely bother with such things, and rarer still to survive the birth. Inherit the hunger diluted: they can eat food, walk in sunlight, but blood calls to them and mortal life feels thin, temporary, unreal. Most can sense the undead, feel the cold presence of vampires and strigoi like pressure behind the eyes. Some inherit more—unnatural speed, strength, the ability to see in darkness. Vampires consider them useful tools or embarrassing mistakes. Mortals distrust them, sensing something wrong. Many become hunters, killing their sires' kind—whether from righteousness, self-loathing, or simply because they're built for it. Others seek out their vampire parent, wanting answers, belonging, or revenge. A dhampir who drinks vampire blood grows stronger but more monstrous; one who abstains stays more human but weaker."
    },
    "Redcap": {
      "text": "Redcap: Murderous fey that haunt places of old violence. Knee-high, wizened, with glowing eyes and teeth filed to iron points. Wear caps soaked in the blood of their kills—if the cap dries, they die. This makes them relentless hunters. Nest in ruined keeps, border towers, old execution grounds, anywhere blood soaked the stone. They're the reason some ruins stay ruined. Redcaps are fey, bound by old rules: cannot cross running water, cannot enter a home uninvited, cannot catch you if you reach hallowed ground. Terrifyingly fast and hunt in packs. Iron-shod boots can crack stone. Iron pikes find gaps in armor. Some 'adopt' violent criminals or mercenary bands, following in their wake to wet their caps."
    },
    "Church Grim": {
      "text": "Church Grim: Guardian spirits of sacred ground. When a church or shrine was consecrated, the first creature buried in its yard became its eternal warden—usually a black dog, though sometimes a pig, ram, or horse sacrificed for the purpose. Protects the dead from disturbance and the living from what lurks in graveyards. Appear as shadowy animals with knowing eyes, solid enough to bite but fading at the edges. Can sense intent—graverobbers and necromancers cannot hide their purpose from it. Cannot leave consecrated ground but are nearly invincible within it. An active church has a grim that knows its duty."
    },
    "Boggart": {
      "text": "Boggart: household spirit gone wrong. Begin as brownies—helpful hearth-fey that clean, mend, and do small kindnesses in exchange for cream and bread left by the fire. But insult a brownie, forget its offerings, or speak of it with contempt, and it curdles into a boggart. Boggarts sour milk, hide tools, pinch sleeping children, cause fires and accidents. Grow uglier as their resentment deepens—warty, long-nosed, with fingers like twigs. The curse of a boggart is that it cannot be driven out. It is bound to the household, the family, sometimes the bloodline. Families who flee find the boggart waiting in their new home. Only appeasement, trickery, or breaking the original bond can end a boggart's torment. The old methods involve convincing it to leave of its own will—but boggarts are bitter, and bitter things don't forgive easily."
    },
    "Will-'o'-the-wisp": {
      "text": "Will-o'-the-Wisp: Pale lights that drift over marshes, bogs, and fens. Cold flames that cast no heat and leave no smoke. Flicker at the edge of vision, retreat when approached—always just ahead, always deeper into treacherous ground. Folk wisdom is clear: don't follow. But the wisps mark something. Sometimes the dead, bodies lost to the mire waiting to be found. Sometimes treasure swallowed by the bog centuries ago. Neither malicious nor kind. Simply are—lights in the dark, leading somewhere."
    },
    "Wendigo": {
      "text": "Wendigo: Gaunt, towering humanoid with gray flesh stretched over visible ribs, elongated limbs ending in clawed hands, and a deer's bleached skull for a face—antlers and all, eye sockets holding pale knowing light. Twelve feet tall or more; the oldest are larger. Supernaturally fast, strong enough to tear limbs free, and nearly impossible to tire or outrun. Mimic human voices perfectly—cries for help, familiar names, a child's weeping—to lure prey close. Presence spreads hunger like plague: dreams of flesh, intrusive thoughts. Fire hurts them badly. Speaking their true name—their mortal name, from before—causes them agony. Every wendigo was mortal once. Someone who faced starvation and chose survival by eating the dead, then the living-and was damned for it. Grow larger the more they consume, but satisfaction never comes."
    },
    "Night Hag": {
      "text": "Night Hag: Withered crones with corpse-blue skin, lank white hair. They ride sleepers—crouching on chests, stealing breath, filling dreams with slow terror. Victims wake exhausted, bruised, remembering only dread. Night after night the hag returns until the sleeper wastes away. But hags don't choose randomly. They come to the guilt-ridden, the oathbreakers, those who owe debts to the dead. Sometimes they're sent—a dying curse, a scorned woman's bargain, payment for an old slight. A hag riding someone means that person did something, or someone wants them destroyed. To ride, a hag must shed her skin and hide it nearby. Find the skin, and you hold her life in your hands—she'll bargain for almost anything. Cold iron bars her passage. A knife under the pillow cuts her if she tries to sit. Certain old prayers ward for a night. But lasting peace means settling whatever debt drew her, or destroying the hag herself."
    },
    "Specter": {
      "text": "Specter: Bodiless malice, and what is left when a death was bad enough that the Shadelands would not take it quietly. A dim human shape, cold at its edges, visible mostly by what it does to lamplight. It chills what it touches and pulls the warmth out of the living, and ordinary steel passes through it — silver, moonstone, and radiant work are what answer. Specters gather where the barrier between worlds runs thin, in old killing-grounds and sealed places, and they do not leave the ground that made them."
    },
    "Ghast": {
      "text": "Ghast: Pack leaders of the corpse-eaters. Gaunt, gray-skinned, moving with terrible speed. Their touch paralyzes—the body frozen while the mind stays aware. They remember fragments of who they were, which makes them worse. Ghasts arise where many died badly without proper rites: battlefields, plague pits. The first to rise becomes the ghast; those it feeds upon rise as ghouls, bound to follow. A ghast pack means mass death went unaddressed, and the pack grows until someone deals with it. They won't cross salt lines or enter homes without invitation. Fire drives them back. But only destroying the original ghast—the first one, the source—ends the pack for good. The others keep rising until that's done."
    },
    "Giant Burrow-Beetle": {
      "text": "Giant Burrow-Beetle: Armored burrowers the size of cattle, mandibles that shear through bone. Ambush predators—they feel vibrations through the earth and erupt beneath prey. Solitary and territorial. Burrow-beetles are scavengers as much as hunters, drawn to carrion and old death. Their tunnels often break into places long buried: barrows, collapsed cellars, mass graves, ruins swallowed by time. Miners and farmers know the signs—circular sinkholes, soil that sounds hollow. A beetle denning near a village usually means it's found something underground worth investigating. The creatures themselves are dangerous but simple: stay off soft ground, watch for sink-signs, and if you must fight, flip them. The underbelly is soft."
    },
    "Gorilla": {
      "text": "Gorilla: Large, powerful primate. Muscular build, black fur, distinctive face. Generally peaceful, but territorial when threatened. Immense strength, can cause serious injury. Strength: Physical power, agility in forested areas. Weakness: Non-aggressive unless provoked, limited tool use."
    },
    "Ahool": {
      "text": "Ahool: Bat-winged hunters with ape-like bodies, wingspans wider than a man is tall. Silent in flight until the wind of their dive. Rare enough that some villages don't believe in them until one strikes. They take prey alive when they can, carrying victims back to high nests—cliff faces, ruined towers, places difficult to reach by land. Why alive isn't clear. The nests hold answers, and bones, and sometimes survivors. Ahools are territorial; one claiming hunting ground near a settlement won't leave until driven out or killed. Torches and loud noise can disrupt their echolocation. They won't pursue into enclosed spaces. On the ground they're clumsy, vulnerable—but getting one grounded is the hard part."
    },
    "White Ape": {
      "text": "White Ape: Albino primate of unusual size and ferocity. Pale fur, red eyes, larger than normal apes. Aggressive and territorial, hunts in groups. Powerful enough to tear apart armor, uses primitive tools. Strength: Physical might, pack tactics, adaptability. Weakness: Sensitive to sunlight, can be outsmarted."
    },
    "Awakened Tree": {
      "text": "Awakened Tree: A tree that moves, watches, acts. Gnarled features form in the bark—not quite faces, but close enough. Roots pull free of earth, branches reach with purpose. Immensely strong, slow but relentless, capable of crushing armor or dragging victims underground through shifting soil. The land often answers them—ground becomes treacherous, branches block escape routes. Vulnerable to fire, but threatening an awakened tree with fire in its own forest makes enemies of everything around you. Trees don't wake on their own. Something stirs them: a druid's dying act, an old bargain coming due, blood spilled at their roots, land wounded badly enough that the forest responds. What they want depends on what woke them. And old trees remember what they've witnessed—murders, buried secrets, forgotten graves. They speak slowly if they speak at all, but they know things."
    },
    "Banshee": {
      "text": "Banshee: Spectral women, pale and hollow-eyed, wild hair and mouths too wide. Their wail carries for leagues—grief given voice, a sound that frays the soul. The scream up close can stop a heart, and even from a distance it brings nightmares, madness, death by despair. Banshees are bound to bloodlines, compelled to wail when death approaches someone of that blood. They don't choose this—the keening pours out of them like breath. A banshee is a crisis: her wailing harms everyone in earshot. Prevent the death she's mourning, and she goes quiet. Some will parlay if their mad grief can be placated through force, guile or charm—in their lucid moments, they know how the death is coming, sometimes even how to stop it. But try to silence her by force, and the scream becomes a weapon turned on you."
    },
    "Worghul": {
      "text": "Worghul: Goblin wolf-shifters. Created by ancient wolf-binding ritual, once rare and difficult, now made by common by the Dragon Queen's magical gifts to the Goblinate's war-shamans. In wolf form: black-furred, yellow-eyed, indistinguishable from natural wolves at a distance. In goblin form: slight, quick, fingers still remembering claws. The shift takes three heartbeats—their only vulnerable moment. Serve the Goblinate as scouts and infiltrators. A pack can live as wolves for months behind enemy lines, hunting deer, avoiding notice. Then shift back for the mission: cut supply lines, poison wells, kidnap a commander's child, slit a sentinel's throat. Shift again, vanish into the woods. Silver burns them."
    },
    "Basilisk": {
      "text": "Basilisk: Reptilian monster with a petrifying gaze. Lizard-like body, eight legs, crown-like crest. Territorial, turns victims to stone with its gaze. Petrifying stare, venomous bite. Strength: Ability to turn creatures to stone. Weakness: Can petrify itself if it sees its reflection."
    },
    "Giant Bat": {
      "text": "Giant Bat: Oversized, carnivorous bat. Wingspan of 15 feet or more, sharp fangs. Nocturnal hunter, uses echolocation. Can carry off medium-sized prey. Strength: Flight, excellent hearing. Weakness: Poor eyesight, vulnerable to loud noises."
    },
    "Dire Wolf": {
      "text": "Dire wolf: Massive, intelligent pack hunters. Twice the size of normal wolves, with glowing eyes. Hunt in coordinated packs, use advanced tactics. Can bring down large prey or small groups of travelers. Strength: Pack coordination, raw power. Weakness: Fire, fear of loud noises."
    },
    "Cave Bear": {
      "text": "Cave Bear: Massive, prehistoric bear species. Larger than modern bears, thick fur, powerful claws. Highly territorial, omnivorous. Immense strength, can easily kill with a single swipe. Strength: Thick hide, powerful sense of smell. Weakness: Poor eyesight, can be outmaneuvered."
    },
    "Giant Eagle": {
      "text": "Giant Eagle: Enormous bird of prey, often intelligent. Eagle-like, with wingspans up to 20 feet. Proud, territorial, sometimes allies with good-aligned beings. Powerful talons and beak, can carry off large prey. Strength: Flight, keen eyesight. Weakness: Vulnerable to attacks while carrying prey."
    },
    "Bullywug": {
      "text": "Bullywug: Amphibious frog-like humanoids. Frog-like features, webbed hands and feet, bulbous eyes. Tribal, aggressive, often found in swamps. Ambush predators, use primitive weapons. Strength: Amphibious nature, camouflage in marshy areas. Weakness: Dependent on moist environments, easily dehydrated."
    },
    "Carnivorous Plant": {
      "text": "Carnivorous Plant: Large plant with animal-like digestive abilities. Varies, often with trap-like leaves or maws. Lures prey with scent or appearance, then traps and digests. Can entangle and slowly digest creatures. Strength: Camouflage, powerful digestive acids. Weakness: Immobile, vulnerable to fire and cold."
    },
    "Corpse-eater": {
      "text": "Corpse-eater: Scavenging aberration resembling a giant centipede. Long, segmented body with multiple legs and tentacles. Hunts in dark, underground areas, paralyzed prey. Paralyzing tentacles, powerful mandibles. Strength: Can climb walls and ceilings, keen sense of smell. Weakness: Vulnerable to bright light, slow on open ground."
    },
    "Centaur": {
      "text": "Centaur: Half-human, half-horse beings. Human torso atop a horse's body. Tribal, often nomadic, skilled archers. Powerful charge attacks, skilled with bows. Strength: Speed, strength, and archery skills. Weakness: Large size makes hiding difficult, vulnerable to attacks on human torso."
    },
    "Chimera": {
      "text": "Chimera: Monstrous hybrid of lion, goat, and dragon. Lion's body, goat's head, dragon's head and wings. Aggressive, territorial, hunts large prey. Multiple attacks from different heads, can breathe fire. Strength: Flight, varied attack options. Weakness: Heads may disagree, vulnerable to attacks on lion body."
    },
    "Cockatrice": {
      "text": "Cockatrice: Hybrid of rooster and reptile with petrifying ability. Rooster-like body with reptilian tail and wings. Aggressive, attacks with beak and petrifying touch. Its touch can turn creatures to stone. Strength: Flight, petrification ability. Weakness: Small size, vulnerable to its own petrification."
    },
    "Giant Crab": {
      "text": "Giant Crab: Enormous crustacean with powerful pincers. Crab-like, but many times larger than normal. Territorial, lurks in shallow waters or tidal areas. Can crush prey with massive claws. Strength: Hard shell, powerful grip. Weakness: Slow on land, vulnerable when molting."
    },
    "Cyclops": {
      "text": "Cyclops: Giant humanoid with a single eye. Massive humanoid form with one central eye. Often solitary, can be territorial or aggressive. Immense strength, can throw boulders. Strength: Physical power, tough skin. Weakness: Poor depth perception due to single eye."
    },
    "Death Knight": {
      "text": "Death Knight: Undead warrior of great power and evil. Skeletal or mummified warrior in heavy armor. Commands lesser undead, seeks to spread death. Powerful warrior skills, necromantic magic. Strength: Undead resilience, tactical mind. Weakness: Vulnerable to holy magic and symbols."
    },
    "Doppelganger": {
      "text": "Doppelganger: Shapeshifting humanoid that can mimic others. Natural form is plain, hairless humanoid. Infiltrates societies by impersonating individuals. Can perfectly mimic appearance and voice of others. Strength: Shapeshifting ability, natural insight into others' minds. Weakness: Cannot change mass significantly, vulnerable in natural form."
    },
    "Dragon Turtle": {
      "text": "Dragon Turtle: Massive aquatic creature combining dragon and turtle traits. Enormous turtle with dragon-like head and tail. Territorial, often demands tribute from ships. Can capsize ships, breathes scalding steam. Strength: Nearly impenetrable shell, powerful in water. Weakness: Slow on land, vulnerable to cold-based attacks."
    },
    "Half-spider": {
      "text": "Half-spider: Half-human, half-spider. Human upper body on a giant spider's lower body. Cruel hunters, often serve dark sorcerers or eldritch entities. Combines human magic with spider abilities. Strength: Wall-climbing, web-spinning, magical prowess. Weakness: Sunlight sensitivity, vulnerable to anti-magic fields."
    },
    "Elemental": {
      "text": "Elemental: Living embodiment of a natural element. Varies based on element (air, earth, fire, water). Follows nature of its element, often neutral to mortals. Powerful control over its element. Strength: Immune to many physical attacks, can merge with element. Weakness: Vulnerable to opposing elements, can be bound by magic."
    },
    "Ettin": {
      "text": "Ettin: Two-headed giant. Large, brutish humanoid with two heads. Aggressive, often confused due to conflicting heads. Great strength, difficult to surprise. Strength: One head always alert, powerful melee attacks. Weakness: Internal conflicts between heads, low intelligence."
    },
    "Fairy": {
      "text": "Fairy: Tiny, magical humanoid beings. Diminutive humanoid with insect-like wings. Mischievous, playful, sometimes helpful to nature. Can cast illusions and charms. Strength: Flight, innate magic, ability to turn invisible. Weakness: Physically weak, vulnerable to iron."
    },
    "Feathered Serpent": {
      "text": "Feathered Serpent: Mythical winged serpent, often revered as a deity. Large serpent with colorful feathers and wings. Wise and ancient, often acts as guardian or advisor. Powerful magic, hypnotic gaze. Strength: Flight, divine or arcane magic, wisdom. Weakness: Pride, can be bound by certain rituals."
    },
    "Gargoyle": {
      "text": "Gargoyle: Animated stone statues, often guardians. Grotesque winged humanoid made of stone. Patient sentinels, attack intruders without mercy. Can blend in with architecture, surprise attacks. Strength: Stone body resistant to many attacks, flight. Weakness: Unintelligent, vulnerable to magic that affects objects."
    },
    "Mummy": {
      "text": "Mummy: Undead creature preserved through ancient rituals. Humanoid wrapped in bandages, often wearing ancient garb. Guards tombs, seeks vengeance on tomb robbers. Spreads cursed rot with its touch, immune to many weapons. Strength: Undead resilience, ancient magic powers. Weakness: Vulnerable to fire, can be put to rest with proper rituals."
    },
    "Ogre": {
      "text": "Ogre: Massive, dim-witted creatures of prodigious strength. Nine to ten feet tall with barrel chests and thick limbs. Often wear crude armor made from multiple sets of gear. Take up residence in abandoned structures or caves near traveled areas. Some hire themselves out as muscle to bandit groups or criminal gangs. Surprisingly skilled at crafting large-scale weapons like siege engines. Can break down walls or flip wagons with bare hands. Use simple but effective ambush tactics. Strength: Raw physical power, thick hide resistant to normal weapons. Weakness: Easy to outsmart, poor stamina for extended fights."
    },
    "Werebear": {
      "text": "Werebear: Humanoid that can transform into a bear-human hybrid. Human form, bear form, or a muscular hybrid of both. Often solitary, can be noble protectors of nature. Immense strength in bear form, spreads lycanthropy. Strength: Regeneration, immunity to non-silver weapons. Weakness: Vulnerable to silver, may lose control during full moon."
    },
    "Wererat": {
      "text": "Wererat: Humanoid that can transform into a rat-human hybrid. Human form, giant rat form, or a ratlike humanoid. Sneaky, often found in urban environments. Spreads lycanthropy, often works in packs. Strength: Stealth, disease resistance, can squeeze through small spaces. Weakness: Vulnerable to silver, weaker than other lycanthropes."
    },
    "Werewolf": {
      "text": "Werewolf: Humanoid that can transform into a wolf-human hybrid. Human form, wolf form, or a bestial wolf-man hybrid. Aggressive, often struggles with dual nature. Powerful in wolf form, spreads lycanthropy through bites. Strength: Enhanced senses, regeneration, immunity to normal weapons. Weakness: Vulnerable to silver, may lose control during full moon."
    },
    "Merfolk": {
      "text": "Merfolk: Aquatic humanoids with fish-like lower bodies. Human upper body, fish-like tail instead of legs. Varied societies underwater, some peaceful, some aggressive. Skilled with tridents and water-based magic. Strength: Excellent swimmers, can breathe underwater. Weakness: Limited mobility on land, vulnerable to dehydration."
    },
    "Myconid": {
      "text": "Myconid: Sentient, humanoid fungi. Mushroom-like body with arms and legs. Peaceful, communicate through spores, live in underground colonies. Can release hallucinogenic or paralytic spores. Strength: Immune to many mind-affecting spells, can fuse with other myconids. Weakness: Vulnerable to fire and sunlight, limited communication with non-myconids."
    },
    "Nightmare": {
      "text": "Nightmare: Fiendish horse-like creature from Plane of Madness. Black horse with flaming hooves and mane, glowing red eyes. Evil and aggressive, often serves as mount for powerful fiends. Can travel between planes, trampling and fire attacks. Strength: Flight, fire immunity, can run on air. Weakness: Vulnerable to light."
    },
    "Nixie": {
      "text": "Nixie: Small water fey with fish-like qualities. Diminutive humanoid with bluish-green skin and webbed fingers. Playful, protective of their aquatic homes. Can charm humanoids to serve them underwater. Strength: Water breathing, can grant water breathing to others. Weakness: Physically weak, vulnerable when out of water."
    },
    "Dryad": {
      "text": "Dryad: Tree nymph bound to a specific tree. Beautiful female humanoid with bark-like skin and leafy hair. Shy, protective of their forests. Can charm humanoids, control plants. Strength: Merge with trees, nature-based magic. Weakness: Cannot stray far from their bound tree, vulnerable to fire."
    },
    "Oni": {
      "text": "Oni: Ogre-like creature with magical abilities. Large, muscular humanoid with colorful skin and horns. Cunning and cruel, often disguises itself to infiltrate societies. Powerful physical attacks combined with magic use. Strength: Shape-changing abilities, flight, regeneration. Weakness: Vulnerable to cold iron weapons."
    },
    "Gelatinous Cube": {
      "text": "Gelatinous Cube: Large, cube-shaped ooze that consumes organic matter. Transparent cube of jelly-like substance. Mindlessly roams dungeons, absorbing debris and creatures. Nearly invisible in dungeons, paralyzing touch. Strength: Immune to many types of damage, can engulf creatures. Weakness: Slow-moving, vulnerable to attacks that affect its entire mass."
    },
    "Pixie": {
      "text": "Pixie: Tiny, mischievous fey creature. Diminutive humanoid with insect-like wings, often glowing. Playful, sometimes helpful, enjoys tricks. Can turn invisible, cast powerful illusions. Strength: Flight, innate magic abilities, hard to detect. Weakness: Physically very weak, vulnerable to iron."
    },
    "Rakshasa": {
      "text": "Rakshasa: Evil spirits in humanoid form. Human-like with animal features, often tiger-headed. Manipulative, enjoys luxury and corrupting others. Powerful spellcasters, can read minds. Strength: Immune to most spells, shape-changing abilities. Weakness: Vulnerable to blessed crossbow bolts, cannot disguise their backwards hands."
    },
    "Revenant": {
      "text": "Revenant: Undead being returned from death to seek vengeance. Decaying corpse with glowing eyes. Single-mindedly pursues its killer or sworn enemy. Relentless pursuit, cannot be permanently killed until vengeance is achieved. Strength: Regeneration, knows location of its target. Weakness: Destroyed if its mission is completed, vulnerable to holy magic."
    },
    "Roc": {
      "text": "Roc: Gigantic bird of prey. Enormous eagle-like bird, large enough to carry elephants. Territorial, nests in high mountains. Can snatch up large creatures, devastates areas with its massive wings. Strength: Incredible size and strength, excellent vision. Weakness: Limited intelligence, vulnerable to attacks on its eyes."
    },
    "Ferrovore": {
      "text": "Ferrovore: Bizarre creature that corrodes metal. Insect-like body with long antennae and a strange, paddle-like tail. Seeks out metal to consume. Touch rapidly corrodes metal items and armor. Strength: Ability to destroy metal equipment, hard carapace. Weakness: No interest in non-metal items, can be easily distracted by metal objects."
    },
    "Packspider": {
      "text": "Packspider: Wolf-sized arachnids, fur-covered, forward-facing eyes, mandibles that crush bone. Hunt in family groups—coordinated, relentless, exhausting prey before overwhelming it. Dens hold multiple generations; younger spiders learn tactics from adults. Travelers learn the signs: silk trail-markers on trees, the clicking calls they use to coordinate. Packspiders are territorial. A family claiming hunting ground near a settlement won't move on—the prey is too easy. Avoid fire and water. Kill the dominant spider and the pack scatters, disorganized, often turning on each other. Their silk is valuable, their dens sometimes hold remains worth recovering—but clearing a den means facing the whole family at once."
    },
    "Satyr": {
      "text": "Satyr: Half-man, half-goat forest dweller. Human upper body, goat legs and horns. Hedonistic, loves music, wine, and dance. Can charm with music, skilled with bow and arrow. Strength: Agile, knows forest magic. Weakness: Easily distracted by pleasures, vulnerable to iron."
    },
    "Shambling Mound": {
      "text": "Shambling Mound: Large plant creature composed of rotting vegetation. Humanoid-shaped mass of vines, leaves, and swamp matter. Roams swamps and dark forests, engulfs prey. Can engulf and suffocate victims, regrows quickly. Strength: Immune to electricity (which actually heals it), resistant to fire and cold. Weakness: Vulnerable to dehydration, slowed by bright light."
    },
    "Stirge": {
      "text": "Stirge: Small, blood-sucking flying creature. Bat-like body with bird-like features and a long proboscis. Swarms victims to drain blood. Attaches to prey and drains blood quickly. Strength: Flight, can detect warm-blooded creatures. Weakness: Very fragile, easily killed if caught."
    },
    "Grove Sentinel": {
      "text": "Grove Sentinel: Sentient, mobile tree creature. Massive humanoid made of bark, branches, and leaves. Protects forests, slow to anger but terrible when roused. Immense strength, ability to animate trees. Strength: Tough bark skin, connection to nature. Weakness: Vulnerable to fire, slow-moving."
    },
    "Triton": {
      "text": "Triton: Aquatic humanoids, guardians of the deep. Human-like with blue-green skin, fins, and webbed hands. Proud warriors, protect the ocean from threats. Skilled with tridents and water-based magic. Strength: Can breathe air and water, resistant to cold. Weakness: Uncomfortable in dry environments, vulnerable to fire."
    },
    "Unicorn": {
      "text": "Unicorn: Magical horse-like creature with a single horn. Beautiful white horse with a spiraling horn on its forehead. Noble and good, protects forests and innocent creatures. Powerful magic, can teleport and heal. Strength: Magic resistance, healing abilities, teleportation. Weakness: Can be lured by pure-hearted individuals, especially maidens."
    },
    "Marsh Hydra": {
      "text": "Marsh Hydra: Amphibious multi-headed serpent adapted to wetland hunting. Sleek, dark-scaled body with long necks and narrow crocodilian heads, often covered in clinging swamp vegetation. Patient ambush predator that uses submerged heads as lures while keeping others concealed below the surface. Known to capsize boats and raid riverside settlements. Can attack multiple targets simultaneously, regenerates damaged heads, excellent swimmer. Strength: Masterful ambush tactics, powerful in water, regeneration. Weakness: Heads can be prevented from regenerating by fire, relatively slow on land."
    },
    "Frost Drake": {
      "text": "Frost Drake: Lesser draconic predator native to cold mountain environments. Heavily muscled serpentine body with short, powerful wings and ice-blue scales. Prominent horn-like crests used in territorial displays. Aggressive raiders that use storms as cover, often targeting caravans and outposts. Mark territory with frozen trophies from past kills. Freezing breath can immobilize prey, exceptional climber despite size. Strength: Perfectly adapted to cold and high altitude, excellent vision in whiteout conditions. Weakness: Less intelligent than true dragons, highly territorial which makes movements predictable."
    },
    "Stonescale Eel": {
      "text": "Stonescale Eel: Serpentine ambush predator that thrives in shallow coastal waters and urban waterways. Long, eel-like body with crocodilian head, hard, stone-colored scales and stubby legs. Squeezes through drainage systems, emerges to snatch prey from walkways and low bridges. Hoards trinkets in underwater dens. Can move surprisingly fast on land, powerful bite can crack boat hulls. Strength: Perfect camouflage in built waterways, can breathe air indefinitely. Weakness: Territorial nature makes hunting patterns predictable, weakened by loud sounds."
    },
    "Lesser Kraken": {
      "text": "Lesser Kraken: Opportunistic cephalopod native to shallow seas but that adapts to harbor and canal environments. Dark-skinned octopoid with armored tentacles and beak-like maw, about size of fishing boat. Disables ships by damaging rudders and anchor chains, creates lairs in shipwrecks and sea caves. Sometimes baited by smugglers to attack competitor vessels. Intelligent enough to plan attacks, can manipulate simple tools and mechanisms. Strength: Expert at destroying vital ship components, incredible grip strength. Weakness: Highly territorial, can be baited with certain chemical scents, needs deep water to escape."
    },
    "Giant Cave Spider": {
      "text": "Giant Cave Spider: Massive cave spider common in ruins and cave country across Larion. Horse-sized arachnid with thick bristled legs. Mandibles the size of sword blades. Ambush predator that creates massive web networks in ruins and ravines. Wraps prey in webbing and drags them back to lair for later consumption. Web strands strong enough to trap humans, venom causes rapid paralysis. Strength: Can move silently despite massive size, excellent night vision. Weakness: Relies heavily on web vibrations for sensing prey, vulnerable to fire."
    },
    "Bloodwing": {
      "text": "Bloodwing: Grotesque, enormous mosquito common in fens and marsh country. Man-sized flying insect with translucent wings and a needle-like proboscis as long as a spear. Swollen abdomen pulses red with stolen blood. Hunts at dawn and dusk, drawn to large mammals including horses and humans. Can drain a horse in minutes. Multiple feedings cause abdomen to swell until it must rest on the ground. Proboscis can pierce light armor, victims quickly weaken from blood loss. Strength: Nearly silent in flight, can detect warm blood at great distances. Weakness: Sluggish and vulnerable when gorged with blood, easily crippled by damage to wings."
    },
    "Scourge Scorpion": {
      "text": "Scourge Scorpion: Nightmarish arachnid found in badlands and cavern country. Bull-sized scorpion with three segmented tails ending in barbed stingers. Mottled exoskeleton patterns resemble bark or stone. Clusters of eyes gleam like black gems. Inverted ambush predator that clings to cave ceilings or thick tree branches. Drops onto prey using all three tails to inject different toxins. Sometimes creates permanent hunting perches by coating surfaces with sticky secretions. Multiple venoms cause different effects: paralysis, burning pain, and tissue dissolution. Strength: Nearly invisible when still, can strike with all three tails simultaneously. Weakness: Vulnerable during molting, vulnerable to cold."
    },
    "Boneworm": {
      "text": "Boneworm: Massive burrowing parasite that infests the buried ruins of the Underworld. Pale, segmented worm as thick as a man's torso, with grinding circular mouthparts lined with calcium deposits from consumed bone. Body is semi-translucent, showing recently consumed matter. Tunnels through stone and soil, attracted to vibrations. Forms colonies in ancient burial grounds and battlefields. Creates extensive tunnel networks that weaken structural integrity. Can burrow through solid rock, digestive acids dissolve organic matter quickly. Strength: Nearly impossible to stop once it starts burrowing, excellent vibration sense. Weakness: Vulnerable to loud sounds, highly sensitive to bright light."
    },
    "Click Lizard": {
      "text": "Click Lizard: Eyeless predator evolved for hunting the lightless tunnels of the Underworld. Low-slung reptilian body with oversized front claws and complex sensory organs where eyes would be. Covered in hard scales with worn-smooth patches from squeezing through tight spaces. Hunts in small packs using echo-location clicks. Extraordinarily patient, known to stalk prey for days through tunnel systems. Often claims abandoned dwarven vaults as territory. Can track prey through solid rock, coordinated pack tactics. Strength: Perfect spatial awareness in darkness, incredibly strong for its size. Weakness: Overwhelmed by multiple sound sources, vulnerable to attacks on sensory organs."
    },
    "Mire Stalker": {
      "text": "Mire Stalker: Amphibious predator adapted to urban sewers and cisterns. Salamander-like body with elongated limbs and adhesive pads, mottled skin mimics environment. Expandable jaw can unhinge to swallow large prey. Claims territory around sewer junctions and storm drains. Steals from surface through grates and drains, gradually learning patterns of movement above. Sometimes keeps live prey trapped in air pockets. Can squeeze through impossibly small spaces, powerful grip prevents escape. Strength: Excellent climber on wet surfaces, can hold breath for hours. Weakness: Dependent on moisture, vulnerable to fire and alchemical purifying agents."
    },
    "Corpse Render": {
      "text": "Corpse Render: Scavenging horror that inhabits catacombs and burial crypts. Emaciated humanoid figure with over-long arms and retractable claws, patches of hardened skin like burial shrouds. Eyes reflect light like polished copper. Hoards bodies and carrion in deeper tunnels, emerging to raid graveyards and plague pits. Sometimes develops taste for living prey. Marks territory with distinctive scratch patterns. Carries multiple diseases, incredibly strong despite gaunt appearance. Strength: Can move silently despite size, excellent night vision, immune to most poisons. Weakness: Compulsive about defending its hoard, can be baited with fresh carrion."
    },
    "Greater Silk Moth": {
      "text": "Greater Silk Moth: Large moth species prized for its enchanted silk. Wing span of up to three feet, with intricate patterns that seem to move in moonlight. Cocoons shimmer with inherent magical properties. Drawn to areas of magical activity, particularly around magetowers. Colonies establish themselves in abandoned buildings. Cocoons retain magical properties even after moth emerges. None to humans, though protecting a colony from predators can be dangerous. Strength: Silk has natural magic-conducting properties, highly valued by artificers. Weakness: Completely defenseless, requires specific conditions to breed successfully."
    },
    "Tarrasque": {
      "text": "Tarrasque: Colossal monster of legend, embodiment of destruction. Enormous reptilian creature with shell, horns, and multiple limbs. Sleeps for eons, awakens to rampage and destroy. Nearly indestructible, can level entire cities. Strength: Regeneration, immunity to many types of damage, incredibly strong. Weakness: Can be put back to sleep with powerful magic, vulnerable to wish spells."
    },
    "Trow": {
      "text": "Trow: Small, vicious cave-kin of the trolls, usually no taller than a child but broad-shouldered, long-armed, gray-green, and adapted to darkness. They breed in crowded warrens beneath crags, mines, and broken hills, spreading through cracks too tight for larger creatures to follow. A lone trow is a scavenging ambusher; a swarm uses darkness, noise, false retreats, and narrow passages to separate prey before dragging isolated people into the warren. They steal tools, lamps, food, and anything bright, building their dens around whatever they can carry off, and their crude coordination becomes dangerous in a mine or cave. They lack the great troll's strength and regeneration, but share its appetite and territorial instincts. Their traits vary by warren: the trows of the Argenval Crags and Silver Mine are resistant to silver, the metal that normally bites the unnatural beasts of Carendon's wood."
    },
    "Troll": {
      "text": "Troll: Giant, brutish humanoids with remarkable regenerative abilities. Grotesquely tall with long arms, mottled green-gray skin, and hooked claws. Hunched posture belies explosive speed. Wounds visibly heal as you watch. Claim remote valleys and mountain passes as territory, marking boundaries with massive stone cairns. Live in loose family groups, teaching young to hunt by attacking anything that enters their domain. Sometimes keep cave bears as pets. Hard to kill without fire, become more aggressive when wounded. Incredible regeneration, powerful enough to throw boulders. Fire stops regeneration."
    },
    "Ophidian": {
      "text": "Ophidian: Former humans transformed through ancient rituals into hybrid snake-beings. Varies by individual and degree of transformation. Common features include patches of iridescent scales, vertically slitted pupils, and elongated canines. Advanced transformations develop hood-like growths, forked tongues, or fully serpentine lower bodies. Maintain elaborate hierarchies based on degree of transformation. Older members become increasingly obsessed with further ritual enhancement. Often manipulate local politics through a combination of charm and terror. Retain human intelligence while gaining serpentine abilities. Many develop toxic bites or hypnotic gazes. Strength: Powerful combination of human cunning and reptilian attributes. Highly resistant to poisons. Weakness: Transformation process is addictive, leading to increasingly risky ritual attempts. Vulnerable to extreme cold."
    },
    "Fire Elementling": {
      "text": "Fire Elementling: Living spark of elemental fire, manifesting where flame meets wild magic. Tiny humanoid figure made of living flame, leaving small burn marks with each step. Eyes flicker like candle flames. Mercurial and quick-tempered, drawn to sources of heat and fire. Some bond with blacksmiths or firefighters as assistants. Can ignite flammable materials when angered. Swarms pose serious fire hazard. Strength: Can manipulate existing fires, immune to heat and flame. Weakness: Extinguished by sufficient water or smothering. Weakened by cold."
    },
    "Earth Elementling": {
      "text": "Earth Elementling: Animated essence of stone and soil, emerging where earth magic runs strong. Small, stocky figure made of packed earth or living stone. Stone growths serve as eyes. Patient and methodical, often found strengthening foundations or repairing stonework. Some partner with miners to locate ore veins. Can cause localized tremors or collapse weak structures. Swarms can undermine foundations. Strength: Nearly indestructible while in contact with earth. Can merge with and manipulate stone. Weakness: Slow-moving, vulnerable when separated from ground."
    },
    "Water Elementling": {
      "text": "Water Elementling: Living droplet of elemental water, coalescing where water meets magical currents. Fluid humanoid shape constantly flowing and reforming. Playful and curious, found near fountains and wells. Sometimes help maintain aqueducts and irrigation systems. Can cause rapid rust or erosion. Groups can create dangerous currents. Strength: Can control water flow and pressure. Weakness: Vulnerable to extreme heat."
    },
    "Air Elementling": {
      "text": "Air Elementling: Living breath of wind, forming where air currents intersect with magical eddies. Nearly invisible humanoid outline visible only by the way it disturbs air and dust. Capricious and adventurous, attracted to high places and open skies. Sometimes assist sailors or windmill operators. Can create sudden gusts or vacuum pockets. Swarms generate devastating windstorms. Strength: Nearly invisible, can fly freely and manipulate air currents. Weakness: Disperses in confined spaces. Can be trapped in sealed containers."
    },
    "Pitmaw": {
      "text": "Pitmaw: Buried maw of the desert. Body lies deep beneath the sand—only the gullet extends upward, fifteen feet of extendable throat ending in a hidden mouth. A subtle circular depression marks the trap. A faint whisper, like breath through a hollow reed, gives it away if you're listening. Step above one and the sand becomes a funnel, suction pulling you down into an acid-filled stomach. Desert folk learn the signs: test suspicious ground with thrown stones, listen for the whisper. Pitmaws don't move once settled—a hunting ground is theirs for decades. The digestive acid fetches good coin from alchemists."
    },
    "Possessor": {
      "text": "Possessor: Malicious spirit that inhabits corpses and objects. Controlled bodies show unnaturally wide mouth seams and impossible joint angles. Can leap between hosts. Strength: Body possession, host jumping, memory retention, psychic immunity. Weakness: Light-based attacks, exorcism, spiritual wards, host destruction."
    },
    "Smokewalker": {
      "text": "Smokewalker: Spirit formed from funeral pyre smoke, shifts between human shape and smoke column. Only visible to pure hearts until attack. Can flow through cracks and suffocate victims. Strength: Selective invisibility, smoke form, suffocation attacks, fire immunity. Weakness: Water, strong winds, detection by pure hearts. "
    },
    "Hundredhands": {
      "text": "Hundredhands: Towering giant with fifty heads and hundred arms, each capable of independent action. Can attack dozens simultaneously or focus all hands on single task. Walking army. Strength: Multiple attacks, independent heads, massive reach, coordination. Weakness: Confusion tactics, overwhelming single point, decision paralysis. "
    },
    "Webwidow": {
      "text": "Webwidow: Giant spider that transforms into beautiful woman. Spins invisible silk traps and injects paralyzing venom through kiss or touch. Prefers to cocoon victims alive. Strength: Shapeshifting, invisible webs, paralytic venom, enchanting beauty. Weakness: Fire, detection by shadow, web cutting. "
    },
    "Watercharger": {
      "text": "Watercharger: Shapeshifting water spirit appearing as beautiful horse near water. Adhesive skin traps riders, then dives deep to drown them. Can walk on water and control currents. Strength: Water walking, current control, adhesive skin, drowning attacks. Weakness: Fire, forced land combat, binding rituals. "
    },
    "Scaletitan": {
      "text": "Scaletitan: Serpentine draconic creature with only front legs, no wings. Armored scales except throat. Grows larger by molting, becomes more ravenous. Can squeeze through impossible spaces. Strength: Armor scales, size growth, compression, battering ram strikes. Weakness: Throat vulnerability, molting periods, size restrictions. "
    },
    "Doomwing": {
      "text": "Doomwing: Tall humanoid with giant moth wings and glowing red eyes. Appears before disasters, gaze induces prophetic nightmares of coming catastrophe. Causes panic and poor decisions. Strength: Disaster prophecy, fear induction, psychic immunity, dark resistance. Weakness: Light sensitivity, hope inspiration, prophecy prevention. "
    },
    "Drownmaiden": {
      "text": "Drownmaiden: Vengeful spirit of drowned woman with green hair and bloated skin. Hypnotic singing lures victims to watery deaths in jealous embrace. Can be banished by true name. Strength: Hypnotic song, water domain, drowning attacks, beautiful illusion. Weakness: True name binding, water avoidance, counter-songs. "
    },
    "Sealskin": {
      "text": "Sealskin: Shapeshifter between seal and human form by removing seal skin. Must obey whoever steals their pelt. Exceptional swimmers, communicate with sea creatures. Strength: Dual forms, water mastery, sea creature communication, cold resistance. Weakness: Skin theft, form binding, land vulnerability. "
    },
    "Skyblessed": {
      "text": "Skyblessed: Celestial being with magnificent wings wearing iridescent robes that never touch ground. Music heals wounds but prolonged presence causes mortals to fade. Divine messenger. Strength: Flight, healing music, divine protection, blessing bestowal. Weakness: Mortal corruption, earthly binding, divine commands. "
    },
    "Darkrager": {
      "text": "Darkrager: Colossal sea spirit as featureless black torso rising from calm seas. Absorbs light, capsizes ships, controls water pressure. Collects drowned souls in glowing pearls. Strength: Light absorption, ship destruction, pressure control, soul collection. Weakness: Fire attacks, light sources, pearl destruction. "
    },
    "Flameshifter": {
      "text": "Flameshifter: Fire-breathing dragon that shapeshifts into handsome man by night. Only small scale behind ear reveals true nature. Seeks genuine romance with mortals. Strength: Shapeshifting, fire control, charm, flight capability. Weakness: True love vulnerability, scale detection, emotional manipulation. "
    },
    "Soul Thief": {
      "text": "Soul Thief: Underworld demon that snatches unburied dead. Touch causes paralysis, casts no shadow. Cannot cross running water or sanctified ground. Collects souls in crystal vials. Strength: Soul capture, paralytic touch, shadow immunity, stealth. Weakness: Running water, sacred ground, vial destruction, light attacks. "
    },
    "Weeping Hunter": {
      "text": "Weeping Hunter: Amphibious predator with dog body, monkey hands, and tail-hand. Mimics child crying to lure victims to water. Devours only eyes, teeth, and fingernails. Strength: Voice mimicry, aquatic mobility, trophy collection, specific targeting. Weakness: Sound detection, water avoidance, protective barriers. "
    },
    "Shipclaw": {
      "text": "Shipclaw: Small fish with adhesive crest that latches onto ships, bringing them to complete stop. Schools can drag vessels underwater. Alpha marks target ships. Strength: Ship stopping, coordinated attacks, underwater dragging, adhesive secretion. Weakness: Fire, targeted removal, alpha elimination. "
    },
    "Stormwing": {
      "text": "Stormwing: Giant eagle that causes winds with wing beats. Razor-sharp feathers slice armor, wind gusts tear people apart. Nests on highest peaks, sees through generated winds. Strength: Wind immunity, razor feathers, distance vision, mountain domain. Weakness: Enclosed spaces, feather deflection, grounding. "
    },
    "Bonebiter": {
      "text": "Bonebiter: Beast with stag body, lion chest, horse hooves. Mimics human voices, has ridged bone that cuts through anything. Collects and digests bones for strength. Strength: Voice mimicry, bone cutting, strength absorption, bone projectiles. Weakness: Bone shortage, voice recognition, defensive positioning. "
    },
    "Icesnatcher": {
      "text": "Icesnatcher: Human-like sea creature with green skin that hums to lure people to water. Keeps victims in baby carrier under ice. Touch causes frostbite, can freeze water. Strength: Hypnotic humming, ice manipulation, frostbite touch, victim preservation. Weakness: Heat sources, loud noises, carrier targeting. "
    },
    "Gulper": {
      "text": "Gulper: Gluttonous beast with goat body and eyes beneath massive jaws. Devours anything, absorbs properties of consumed creatures. Temporarily gains victim characteristics. Strength: Universal consumption, property absorption, characteristic mimicry, endless hunger. Weakness: Dietary restrictions, absorption overload, specific prey targeting."
    },
    "Magmaborn": {
      "text": "Magmaborn: Humanoid creature of living magma and rock that dwells in volcanoes. Body glows with inner fire, leaves smoldering footprints. Hurls magma globs and superheats metal weapons. Strength: Fire immunity, magma attacks, metal heating, natural illumination. Weakness: Water, extreme cold, heat dissipation. "
    },
    "Deathgazer": {
      "text": "Deathgazer: Swamp beast with bull body and weak neck that drags heavy head along ground. Breath causes disease, eyes cause petrification. Approach from behind. Strength: Petrifying gaze, disease breath, poison resistance. Weakness: Rear approach, mirrors, neck vulnerability. "
    },
    "Prince of Shadow": {
      "text": "Prince of Shadow: A figure from ancient legend and children's tales, said to be a shapeshifting tempter who appears in different forms to corrupt mortals through dark bargains. Old stories claim he was cast out by the gods and sealed away after the Great War generations ago, when the world burned in conflict. According to folklore, he offers power, wealth, or revenge to those who despair, but always at a terrible price - claiming their very souls. Popular superstition holds that those who strike bargains with the Prince no longer cast shadows in sunlight, having traded this part of themselves for his gifts. Most educated people dismiss such tales as primitive explanations for human greed and cruelty, though rural folk still whisper warnings about strangers who offer easy solutions to hard problems. "
    },
    "The Bloodhunters": {
      "text": "The Bloodhunters: An aristocratic order of duelists and blood-mages who hunt the dead, seated at the Red Chantry on the moors of Whitemoor. They live finely, fight with antique blades and old blood-magic, and bear the corruption of their craft as a mark of pride — the last line of defense against the dark, feared for their powers, relied on for their results. They take in any who would learn the craft and take up the cause, and they police their own without mercy, quietly hunting down those of their number who lose their way."
    },
    "The Syndicate": {
      "text": "The Syndicate (criminal organization): The Syndicate is the web of crime-lords that holds the underside of Larion. Every city of any size has its boss, grown fat on smuggling, extortion, theft, and the selling of people, and everyone knows who he is — he doesn't hide, because being feared is the point. Above the local bosses sit greater bosses in greater cities, and above them the High Men, the richest and most feared crime-lords alive, who command the rest through tribute, debt, and murder. Syndicate men wear their allegiance in ink: an open black hand tattooed on the neck or forearm — the hand that takes the cut — so that anyone who crosses them knows exactly whose man they are hurting. The mark is a threat that walks. Crossing the Syndicate means a knife in the dark or the trade choked off; princes tolerate it because rooting it out costs more than living with it."
    },
    "Children of the Wyrm": {
      "text": "Children of the Wyrm: A dragon cult of scattered cells whose members seek to transform themselves into draconic beings using ancient dragon remains. Bones, scales, and other dragon materials are alchemically processed into reagents used in ritual transformations that produce dracomorphs or wyrmfiends - abominations whose bodies twist with scales, claws, and malformed wings, while granting breath weapons, flight and even long-lost draconic magic. Cultists search for and raid dragon tombs and fuel a black market in dragon relics. Believe they are ascending to dragonhood and earning their place as the Wyrm's true offspring. Some cult cells have turned to sacrificing dragonkin when ancient dragon remains prove too rare or expensive. Cult cells recruit the desperate, the power-hungry, and those seeking to become something greater than their birth allowed. In the marsh country around the Great Lagoon, Wyrm cells collect a yearly tithe from outlying villages in the dragon-kingdom's name — goods most years, people some years — and the villages pay because they remember the ones that refused."
    },
    "The Wolfpack": {
      "text": "The Wolfpack: An orc-founded warrior society at Wolfpack Lodge in the Orc Wolds, open to fighters, hunters, shamans, scouts, healers, beast handlers, spellcasters, berserkers, and other capable people of any blood. Trial-Hunters earn admission through one witnessed proving deed and receive the pack clasp as Packmates. Three recognized deeds earn standing as Proven Hunter. Proven Hunters may voluntarily seek a mutual bond with a beast-spirit at the Spirit Hearth; wolf is traditional but not mandatory. Four serious deeds after that bond may earn recognition as Pack-Captain, trusted to lead lodge contracts and willing packmates beneath the singular Pack-Leader. Fury is respected but never required. The Pack takes dangerous beasts, rescues, defenses, escorts, monster lairs, raiders, Oathbreakers, and Wrath-Speakers when ordinary warriors cannot answer them."
    },
    "Shadow War": {
      "text": "Shadow War: An apocalyptic conflict from the distant past that some believe nearly destroyed civilization. Entire kingdoms falling to madness, brother turning against brother, and disaster and calamity of every kind befalling the world. The war's end is shrouded in legend. Most evidence of this ancient catastrophe has been lost to time. "
    },
    "Garden of Life": {
      "text": "Garden of Life: A mythical grove hidden deep within the Everwood, said to hold the secret of life and death. Believed by most to be mere legend."
    },
    "Gryphon": {
      "text": "Gryphon: Leonine body, eagle wings, beaked head, and sharp talons. Proud creatures that mate for life and fiercely defend their nests built on mountain peaks and cliffsides. Highly intelligent and capable of understanding speech, though they cannot speak themselves. Bond with worthy warriors, becoming loyal mounts and companions. Feathers and claws are prized by alchemists and enchanters. Hoard shiny objects in their nests. Can be aggressive toward intruders but respect displays of courage and may spare those who stand their ground. Powerful aerial combat abilities, keen eyesight, devastating diving attacks. Vulnerable on ground, fiercely territorial, protective of young to the point of recklessness."
    },
    "Rainbow Dragon": {
      "text": "Rainbow Dragon: Rare, majestic dragons with scales of every color. Lair in mountain peaks. Breath weapon is a chaotic blast with random effects from elemental damage to petrification. Neutral or benevolent, hoarders of art, gems, bright things. Deeply magical. Resistance to most forms of magic, blinding aura. Vanity can be exploited, coloration makes stealth impossible, hate to destroy beautiful things and people."
    },
    "Church of the Pyre": {
      "text": "Church of the Pyre (Ignath, Lord of Fire): A fire cult devoted to Ignath, Lord of Fire, who they believe sleeps beneath the world and will one day rise to cleanse it. The Church preaches that the Dragon Queen's coming is the proof and the promise of that fire, and that her conquest is holy work — burning away the corrupt old order to make way for purification. Its northern seat is in Smoketown in the Black Fells, where it is allied with the Greater Goblinate and its archpriests oversee dragonfire production, blessing the fuel that powers Goblinate weapons. Its southern seat is the Temple of the Flame Undying in Serendipity on the Alar Coast, where the Church rules outright in a dormant dragon's name, gathering tribute under the lances of its Red-cowl soldiery. The Church teaches that Larion's kingdoms are irredeemably corrupt: nobles hoard while peasants starve, the Syndicate trades in lives, the comfortable ignore suffering. Only fire purifies. Worshippers bear ritual burn scars as marks of devotion; the most fanatical are scarred beyond recognition. Proscribed throughout Larion, the Church offers purpose and power to the angry, the wronged, and those who want to watch the old world burn."
    },
    "Sunscale Order": {
      "text": "Sunscale Order: Militant dragonkin who guard dragon lore, relics, and dragonkin themselves from the Children of the Wyrm and other threats. The Order keeps no single great seat; its knights work from safe houses in the major cities, where dragonkin can shelter, tracking rumors of dragon-site discoveries, disrupting the black-market trade in dragon materials, and striking cult cells wherever they surface. Recovered dragon relics are carried to hidden sanctums and properly interred. The Order accepts both dragonkin and trusted allies of other peoples into its ranks, offering training, resources, and mission contracts to those willing to fight dragon corruption. Its sunburst emblem is recognized across Larion as the mark of legitimate dragon heritage, defended against those who would steal and pervert it."
    },
    "Leucrotta": {
      "text": "Leucrotta: Hybrid predator—stag's body, lion's haunches, badger-like head with a ridge of bone instead of teeth that shears through armor. Fast and tireless, but it prefers not to chase. It calls you closer first. Leucrottas mimic human voices perfectly: cries for help, a child lost in the woods, your companion calling your name from just beyond the treeline. They learn by listening—following groups for days, eavesdropping on camps, memorizing voices and phrases. The voices it knows are usually voices of the dead. Travelers learn to test suspicious calls: ask a question only the real person could answer. The leucrotta can only repeat what it's heard, never create. Its lair holds bones and belongings of victims—things worth recovering if you can stomach the search."
    },
    "League of Adventurers": {
      "text": "League of Adventurers: A neutral heroic institution headquartered in the Watchtower in Ardan's Watch. Members register, take local contracts, earn ranks from Copper upward, claim rewards, find companions, and receive credentials that let them work across rival realms. The League governs Ardan's Watch directly through the Council of Champions, but outside Silvervale it works through trust, referrals, contracts, and the need for heroes. Specialist seats exist elsewhere: the Order of the Silver Sword at Castle Harstone, the Black Tower in Drusavia, Tarnholme Academy for arcane training, and other regional partners."
    },
    "Adventuring Houses": {
      "text": "Adventuring Houses: Across Larion, free companies gather around public houses where companions meet, people bring dangerous problems, and crews return between expeditions. A company needs no fixed number of founders or compulsory name; its identity becomes real through shared deeds. The Wildward House in Carendon, the House of Many Banners in Gorren Crossing, Sky-River House in Oasis, and Skygate House in Cloudtop are old League adventuring houses whose former companies dispersed through successful careers and changing lives. Their public rooms remain active, but their League standards came down when they could no longer field companies. At each House, a Gold-ranked veteran may sponsor a new company, recognize its work, introduce capable people, and vouch for it; three recognized House deeds earn the right to raise the League standard again. Successful Houses remain places of adventure and fellowship rather than offices or passive businesses, developing useful field capabilities, relationships, clients, recruits, and regional identities through play."
    },
    "League Ranks": {
      "text": "League Ranks: Adventurers registered with the League of Adventurers hold ranks from Copper through Bronze, Silver, Gold, and Platinum. Copper-rank adventurers take local work—pest clearance, simple escorts, minor disputes. Bronze unlocks regional contracts and access to League safehouses. Silver grants access to restricted archives and dangerous hunts. Gold-rank adventurers handle threats to cities and provinces. Platinum is rare—these adventurers are trusted with Council-sealed contracts involving threats to the realm itself. The standard ladder ends at Platinum. Platinum adventurers who complete one exceptional commission and receive formal recognition at a Council hearing may be permanently named League Champions, join the Council, and protect Larion at the highest level. Adventurers can share deeds while holding different ranks: each receives their own recognition, and accompanying a senior adventurer does not confer that person's standing. League Champion is an exceptional final title, not a sixth ordinary deed rank. The Dragon Queen campaign is separate from this progression and remains open to level-one adventurers. Copper through Platinum rank can be lost through failure, misconduct, or disgrace; League Champion is permanent once granted."
    },
    "Goblin": {
      "text": "Goblin: Small, wiry humanoids with pointed ears, sharp features, and skin ranging from sallow green to grey-brown. Quick-fingered and cunning. Most live in the volcanic Black Fells, where tribal settlements mine obsite and trade with whoever pays. Others survive as raiders on the borders of settled lands, taking what stronger peoples won't share. The Greater Goblinate has united many tribes under the Dragon Queen's banner, promising nationhood and conquest—but plenty of goblins answer to no flag, raiding for their own benefit or serving whoever offers coin. Individually weak but dangerous in numbers. Prefer ambush, traps, and overwhelming odds to fair fights. Will parley if outmatched, betray agreements if advantage shifts. Respect strength and cunning; despise weakness. A goblin who surrenders expects slavery or death and will offer the same."
    },
    "Hobgoblin": {
      "text": "Hobgoblin: Tall, broad-shouldered goblinoids with grey or rust-orange skin and heavy brows. Disciplined where goblins are chaotic, methodical where goblins are opportunistic. Military tradition runs deep—even independent bands organize into ranks, maintain equipment obsessively, and follow codes of conduct. Historically served as mercenaries and fortress-builders across Larion; a hobgoblin company's word is considered reliable, if expensive. Many now serve the Greater Goblinate as officers and specialists, bringing professional discipline to goblin irregulars. Others still sell their services independently, fighting for whoever meets their terms. Respect hierarchy, contracts, and martial excellence. Despise incompetence and disorder. Will honor a negotiated surrender if terms are clear; will execute those who break faith. Fight in disciplined formations, coordinate tactics, protect their flanks. Harder to panic than goblins, harder to trick, harder to kill."
    },
    "Bugbear": {
      "text": "Bugbear: Massive goblinoids, seven feet or taller, covered in coarse dark fur. Long arms, thick claws. Move with unnatural silence despite their bulk—the wrongness of something that large making no sound. Solitary hunters by nature. Patient. Watch settlements for days, learn patrol routes and guard rotations, identify the vulnerable. Strike from darkness and vanish before anyone organizes a response. Some lair near civilization permanently, treating a village or trade road as their hunting ground. Others hire out as killers—their silence and patience make them excellent assassins. Don't negotiate, don't posture, don't warn. If a bugbear is talking to you, it's because killing you isn't currently convenient. Take trophies. Weak against coordinated opposition; their solitary nature means they retreat rather than face real resistance."
    },
    "Goblin Firelancer": {
      "text": "Goblin Firelancer: Goblinate infantry armed with fire-lances—pike-length weapons topped with brass dragon heads that vomit gouts of fire. Wear crude scale armor in brass and bronze, dragon motifs hammered into every surface. Religious fervor keeps them fighting past where mercenaries would break. They believe the Dragon Queen has chosen them for glory. The fire-lances are unstable; malfunctions explode or burn wielders alive. Individually poor fighters—undisciplined, easily panicked if isolated. Dangerous in waves, when fire covers their advance and weight of numbers overwhelms defenders. Veterans know: kill the shamans directing them, and firelancer units lose cohesion."
    },
    "Hobgoblin Heavy Firelancer": {
      "text": "Hobgoblin Heavy Firelancer: Hobgoblin artillerists crewing the Goblinate's siege weapons—massive fire-lances mounted on wheeled carriages, requiring three or four operators. Wear heavier armor than infantry, elaborately decorated with rank insignia and dragon heraldry. Professional soldiers, not zealots; they direct bombardments methodically. Handle targeting, reload cycles, and the dangerous work of managing dragonfire reservoirs that could detonate catastrophically. Their presence means the Goblinate is fielding serious military force, not just raiding parties. Siege fire-lances can crack walls, scatter formations, and ignite entire streets. The weapons are inaccurate at range but devastating against fixed positions. Like smaller fire-lances, their larger cousins are volatile when attacked directly with magic."
    },
    "Dragonfire Shaman": {
      "text": "Dragonfire Shaman: Goblin priests who channel dragonfire directly. Marked by ritual burns, wearing regalia of dragon bones and red-black body paint. Serve the forces of the Dragon Queen as spiritual leaders—blessing weapons, leading ceremonies, preaching the Dragon Queen's divinity to converts. In battle, they hurl dragonfire without need of a lance and rally troops with prophetic fervor. Away from battle, they spread the faith to unconverted tribes and desperate communities. Firelancers depend on their spiritual authority; killing a shaman often breaks nearby units."
    },
    "Bugbear Stalker": {
      "text": "Bugbear Stalker: Bugbears serving the Goblinate as terror troops and assassins. Their specialty is killing leaders—officers, nobles, anyone directing resistance. Patient hunters who watch their targets for days before striking. In battle, they exploit chaos to reach priority kills. Outside battle, they're deployed ahead of invasions to eliminate key defenders before the army arrives. Not ideologically committed to the Dragon Queen's cause; they work for payment and hunting privileges. The Goblinate deploys them sparingly for high-value targets only. Where stalkers operate, defenders learn to hide rank insignia and vary their routines."
    },
    "Dragonfire": {
      "text": "Dragonfire: Alchemical fuel that powers Goblinate fire-lances. Made from obsite,sulfur, and trace amounts of a substance called Cold Ash—a rare powder controlled by the Church of the Pyre's archpriests. Cold Ash is cold to the touch, strangely heavy, and gives handlers a feeling of wrongness; its source is a closely guarded secret. Dragonfire combusts violently if mishandled, so firelancers carry their supply in pouches of fire salamander skin. When ignited, it burns hotter than natural flame with a greenish tinge, smells of sulfur, and resists smothering—water spreads it, sand only slows it. Rank-and-file goblins believe dragonfire is a divine gift from their Queen. They don't know how it's made. They know only that supply caravans come from the Black Fells, and when the pouches run dry, their lances are just heavy spears."
    },
    "Firelance": {
      "text": "Firelance: The signature weapon of Goblinate firelancers. Pike-length wooden haft topped with a brass dragon head that vomits dragonfire when triggered. Each muzzle is hand-crafted and unique—different expressions, different scale patterns, individual faces. Goblins name their lances, maintain them obsessively, and treasure them above all possessions. A firelancer's weapon defines who they are. Effective range is short, maybe twenty feet; beyond that, dragonfire disperses. Brass reservoirs at the base of the head hold fuel. The weapons are dangerous to operate—Loss of pressure sprays fire backward, cracked reservoirs ignite, misfires burn wielders alive. Firelancers accept the risk. Heavy fire-lances exist as siege weapons, crew-served on wheeled carriages, but the infantry lance is the symbol of the Goblinate at war."
    },
    "Blackfire": {
      "text": "Blackfire: A Sanctuary mercenary crew known for forest contracts, creature hunts, salvage, and protection work. Its signature weapons are short-range alchemical dragonfire projectors, illicit adaptations of Goblinate firelance designs, that spew thick black alchemical fire. The fire clings to wood, canvas, brush, and exposed targets, driving people and creatures out of cover and leaving blackened scars after it dies. Blackfire crews are greedy, unscrupulous, and brutal; their captains take Syndicate-backed work when it pays, treat the living forest as an obstacle, and call captured creatures or cleared ground contract property. The projectors are costly and dangerous: a ruptured reservoir can burn the crew as readily as its target."
    },
    "Lords and Ladies of Vice and Virtue": {
      "text": "Lords and Ladies of Vice and Virtue: In all the worlds, virtue arises unbidden. It is not created. It springs into being when mortals first appear - thinking beings capable of suffering and choice. Justice, Mercy, Valor, Hope. These principles exist in every universe. But virtue requires a vessel - a Champion. A mortal who embodies it so completely that they transcend mortality itself. The theologians call this an Act Transcendent - a deed of such profound righteousness that it transforms the doer into something greater. A Champion becomes a Lord or Lady of Virtue. Perhaps the same Lord, wearing different faces across all the worlds. Perhaps many. The priests argue. The Lords and Ladies are not truly immortal. They can Fall - manifest in the mortal world, walk among us, and be slain. This is the price of presence. In Larion, the Lady of Mercy Fell once, sacrificing herself to shield her followers from a great evil. But virtue cannot be destroyed while mortals live to embody it. She Rose again, renewed by her own sacrifice. Vice arises the same way. Where virtue exists, so must its shadow. The Lords and Ladies of Vice were once mortal too. Through an Act Notorious - a deed of such profound evil that it damns the doer forever - they become vessels for hatred, cruelty, despair. When Vice Falls, whatever destroyed it commits an Act Notorious by the very act of destruction. The slayer becomes the slain."
    },
    "The Lady of Mercy": {
      "text": "The Lady of Mercy (Goddess; The Risen, The Selfless): She is the virtue of compassion made divine. Her followers tend the sick, shelter the homeless, and comfort the dying. Her temples are hospitals and sanctuaries. No one is turned away."
    },
    "The Lord of Justice": {
      "text": "The Lord of Justice (God; The Balanced Scale): He is the virtue of fair judgment. Truth matters to him. Oaths matter. Consequences matter. His temples serve as courts throughout Larion. His priests hear disputes, weigh evidence, render verdicts. To lie in his temple is to damn yourself."
    },
    "The Lord of Valor": {
      "text": "The Lord of Valor (God; The Shieldbearer, The Unflinching). He is the virtue of courage. Not recklessness - courage. The will to face evil when you are afraid. To stand when others flee. To put yourself between darkness and those who cannot fight it. His temples are training halls. His priests teach combat, but also teach when to fight and when to withdraw."
    },
    "The Lady of Hope": {
      "text": "The Lady of Hope (Goddess; The Dawnbringer, The Undimmed). She is the virtue of perseverance. The light that survives the longest night. Her domain is not naive optimism but stubborn refusal to surrender to despair. When all seems lost, she is what remains. Her temples are often small - a single candle burning in a window, a shrine at a crossroads."
    },
    "The Order of the Dawn": {
      "text": "The Order of the Dawn: The militant priesthood of the Lady of Hope, headquartered at the House of First Light on the Sacred Way in Ardan's Watch. The Order tends consecrated flames in roadside shrines, threatened settlements, and places reclaimed from darkness. These are divine wards, not ordinary fires: while a flame burns, the surrounding country is protected against supernatural despair, curses struggle to take hold, and undead and servants of Vice find their power weakened. A shrine captured or desecrated goes dark, and its protection withdraws. Its flame cannot be rekindled with a torch. The Order's warrior-priests must retake the ground, cast out what profanes it, cleanse the altar, and perform the rite that calls the Lady's fire back. They carry Hope as radiant blessing, ward, healing light, and weapon, and establish new shrines wherever people have endured without her protection."
    },
    "The Lord of Order": {
      "text": "The Lord of Order (God; The Architect, The Foundation). He is the virtue of civilization. Laws, contracts, institutions, the agreements that let strangers live together without bloodshed."
    },
    "The Lady of the Hearth": {
      "text": "The Lady of the Hearth (Goddess; The Welcomer, The Keeper of Doors). She is the virtue of home. Not a building but the feeling - belonging, warmth, the place where you are known and wanted. Her domain is family, community, hospitality, the bonds between people who share roof and bread. Her priests bless marriages, name children, consecrate new homes. Her temples are gathering halls, often with a great hearth at the center. Travelers are always welcome. A meal is always offered. In the snowlander north her faith is kept as custom rather than clergy: a fire always burning, a lamp in every window against the dark, and no food or drink passing the lips indoors before the words \"your hearth, my life.\""
    },
    "The Lady of Wisdom": {
      "text": "The Lady of Wisdom (Goddess; The Clear-Eyed, The Patient). She is the virtue of understanding, of the judgment to use knowledge well. Her domain is learning, teaching, counsel, and the long view beyond immediate passions. Her priests maintain schools and offer counsel to any who ask. Her temples are libraries and academies."
    },
    "The Lady of Blood": {
      "text": "The Lady of Blood (Archfiend; the First Vampire). A Lady of Vice, one of the archfiends bound by the Pact and unable to manifest directly in Larion. She is the progenitor of all vampires. In the time of the Elven Schism, when sun elves hunted moon elves toward extinction, a great moon elf leader faced a choice: sacrifice herself for her people, or sacrifice her people for herself. She chose herself. In a ritual of profound evil, she consumed her own followers to fuel her transformation into something beyond mortal. What she transformed with was the Hunger — the Ur-princes' contagion, already loose among her dying people and already making them mindless — and she ate her followers to master it rather than be mastered by it. She became the Lady of Blood, and the 'gift' she gave her surviving followers was vampirism: her curse, shared. Her blessing is required to create new vampire lords. Without it, vampires can only sire lesser thralls. The vampire lords of Shadow Summits rule in her shadow, paying the blood price their ancestor established. She is elsewhere now, constrained by the Pact, but her children remember."
    },
    "The Lord of the Open Hand": {
      "text": "The Lord of the Open Hand (God; the Companion, the Wayfarer). He is the virtue of fellowship — bonds freely chosen between equals. Not obligation, not fealty, not blood — choice. His domain is friendship, mutual aid, and the hand extended without expectation. His priests bless adventuring parties and witness oaths of companionship. His shrines are waystations, campfires, and gathering places where no one claims the high seat. Among the wood elves of the Everwood, he is remembered as He Who Walks Beside — in Elvish, Talvaren — a legendary figure who guided those who refused to fight in the Kindred War to safety, then gave his life defending them. His final words, 'May you walk in beauty,' are still spoken by wood elves to mark births, arrivals, and deaths. Whether Talvaren was the Lord of the Open Hand in mortal form, or a mortal who became the Lord through his sacrifice, is a question his priests answer differently depending on where you ask."
    },
    "The Pact of All Planes": {
      "text": "The Pact of All Planes: An ancient binding that keeps extraplanar entities from freely entering Larion. Suleyvan the Sealer forged it with neutral extraplanar powers, which hold the boundaries against more hostile beings. The Pact holds, mostly. Summonings, thin places, loopholes, and mortal bargains can breach or evade it. A warlock's pact with a patron beyond the mortal world is not sanctioned by the Pact of All Planes; depending on its terms, it tests, evades, or violates the binding."
    },
    "Warlock Pacts and Clauses": {
      "text": "Warlock Pacts and Clauses:\n\nA warlock's pact is a standing bargain with one patron. Its existing powers are settled clauses: using them does not reopen the bargain or create a new price. Warlocks grow by learning what their patron wants and discovering names, signs, agents, enemies, former pactholders, lost possessions, or places where it can be reached. These may become service, payment, or leverage when the warlock seeks a new clause.\n\nA pact token is the physical sign of this relationship: an object given or altered by the patron when the bargain was made. Its mark reflects the patron, but the token neither contains nor controls it and is not the source of the warlock's power.\n\nA new clause names the exact power to be granted and the exact service, payment, limit, or future obligation asked in return. The patron may offer one, or the warlock may seek an audience and propose one. Discussion, temptation, threats, and unanswered demands bind nothing. Only the player's explicit acceptance establishes the clause. The patron then marks the agreement and grants the power visibly, either at once against the accepted obligation or after the agreed service is complete. Defeating an enemy never grants a power by itself.\n\nA patron grants power in its own nature and cannot be bargained into acting against that nature. A clause may be fulfilled, exploited, renegotiated, or broken, with only the consequences established by its terms and the story. A patron may demand or threaten, but never decides the player's actions."
    },
    "The Black Gate": {
      "text": "A mountain fortress in Shadow Summits where new vampire lords are created. The Black Gate is a portal to the realm of the Lady of Blood; aspirants must enter, defeat a Fiend of the Blood, and drink its essence to receive the vampire's gift."
    },
    "Soul-Stone": {
      "text": "Soul Stones:\n\nSoul-stones are gems with a soul trapped inside, old work of the Princes of Ur from before the Floods. A soul-stone can slow aging and lengthen life, cure sickness, and feed magic with the strength of the soul caught within it — so the rich, the dying, and the powerful prize them, and wizards covet them. But there is a person in every stone, bound and unable to pass on, and most folk find the whole business unholy. They are bought and sold quietly. Their trade is lawful in some lands, a hanging crime in others, and watched everywhere. Most that surface now are dug out of the buried cities of the Underworld, where the old empire stored them."
    },
    "Doom of Orichala": {
      "text": "The Doom of Orichala: Orichala was the proudest republic of the southern seas. Its people claimed the mantle of the drowned Empyrean Empire, mounting expeditions to sunken ruins, recovering artifacts, and mastering waterbreathing magics. Their vaults held treasures no other city could match, and they said they were close to finding the lost imperial capital itself. Then came the Doom. In a single night the island tilted and sank, the sea swallowing all but one small promontory that still juts above the waves. No warning, no storm, no explanation. No bodies. No survivors. The drowned city stands in Seadeep water now, garrisoned by the drowned-and-changed, and those who stop to pry at what the sea has claimed are answered from below."
    },
    "The Elven Schism": {
      "text": "The Elven Schism: In the First Days, all elves were one people. Then came the Long Dark — a time of endless night and winter. The elves split over how to survive it. Some embraced the darkness: these became the moon elves. Others rebelled against it: these became the sun elves. Still others refused to take sides: these became the wood elves. When the darkness finally lifted, war followed, and the elven homeland was lost to all of them — some say destroyed in the war, others cursed and blighted by fratricide. The kindreds scattered. The sun elves settled in the deep eastern deserts, and the moon elves in the sunless mountains of the far north. The high elves claimed the great forest of the Everwood and rule it still from Canopy; the dark elves went down — or were cast down, as they tell it — into the Underworld beneath that same forest, and the two have divided surface from deep ever since. The wood elves mourn what was lost and keep no side. The enmity between sun and moon has never healed."
    },
    "Sun Elves": {
      "text": "Sun elves: Tall and lean with bronze or copper skin. Hair ranges from white-gold to deep copper to flame-red. Their eyes are pale—gold, amber, or white. Most practice some form of sun magic. They are a proud, martial people of the deep eastern deserts, organized into knight-orders and ruled by temples of the sun. They consider themselves righteous guardians against darkness and corruption. Sun elf culture emphasizes purity, discipline, and the rejection of darkness in all forms."
    },
    "Sky-River": {
      "text": "Sky-River: A rare Dunelands formation of blue-and-gold desert waterbirds. Small flocks travel between nesting grounds and known springs, but on exceptional dawns many flocks converge into one vast flowing band toward the same water, visible for miles; lost travelers who see it may follow it toward a spring. The birds' breast feathers can hold water for their young. Old tales call the Sky-River a river that escaped the Sun Kings by taking wing. Dunelands mosaics and stories use it as an emblem of fellowship: no one wing makes the Sky-River, and many lives moving together can make a road where the desert offers none. Witnessing a true Sky-River is rare and important; do not place one routinely in desert scenes."
    },
    "The War in Hell": {
      "text": "The War in Hell: An eternal conflict raging in the infernal realms, fought between demonic factions over matters mortals struggle to comprehend. The war has no beginning anyone remembers and no end anyone predicts. Occasionally it spills into Larion. The demonborn are descendants of refugees who fled this conflict generations ago."
    },
    "Orc Diaspora": {
      "text": "Orc diaspora: The orc clans once ranged far beyond the Wolds they hold today. Generations of war on every front broke clan after clan, and the survivors of the broken clans scattered across Larion. Orc culture turns on the keeping of oaths — spoken vows witnessed by a clan elder and affirmed before the gods. The oath-witness names their lineage, declares what they have heard, and the oath is sealed. It is the most sacred act in orc life. Everything else is noise, argument, and cheerful insult. Orcs show affection through mockery and find the politeness of other cultures baffling and dishonest. They eat communally, sharing platters with their hands, and consider eating alone bizarre. They are known for blunt honesty, fierce loyalty, and long memories. Most diaspora orc communities gather around an elder who serves as oath-witness — the living memory of every promise her people have made."
    },
    "The Burning of the Kings": {
      "text": "The Burning of the Kings (Kingstor, Wild Grass Sea): The centerpiece of every Great Moot of the beastfolk at Kingstor. In the grass sea, a 'king' is a colossal effigy built to burn — the word stripped of authority, stuffed with straw, and set on fire. Anyone can build a king. Wolfkin packs build kings of bandit chiefs. Boarfolk communes build kings of Syndicate tax assessors. Temporary alliances bend together to build enormous kings representing outside powers who've threatened beastfolk autonomy — these draw the biggest crowds. Kings can be political, personal, or just funny. The only rule is it has to burn well. The Burning itself is the wildest night in Larion — drums, bonfires, dancing, drinking, feats of strength, old rivals clasping arms while enormous effigies collapse in showers of sparks. Non-beastfolk at Kingstor during the Burning are welcome to join. They are also welcome to notice that kings from their home regions burn just as brightly as any other."
    },
    "The Thieves' Guild": {
      "text": "The Thieves' Guild - The Trade, Hands, The Table: The Thieves' Guild is a den of vipers that don't bite each other — much. An informal alliance of criminal bands called Hands, found wherever there's wealth and secrets worth taking. The Guild is 'governed' by the Table — a secret council of the most powerful, influential, and ruthless leaders in the trade. Most Hands never earn a seat at the Table. Most cower like dogs at the foot of it. The Guild takes different forms in different places, but it always makes its peace with the powers that be. Don't steal from the ruling clan. Get secrets for them. Spy on their enemies. Solve the problems the law can't solve and keep the underworld quiet enough that decent folk can sleep. In return, the authorities look the other way — though they always hold out their hands for a cut. Because crime is inevitable, and crime without anyone minding the shop is worse for everyone. Some Hands are professionals, proud of their craft. Some are thugs. Most protect their own. The Guild protects Hands that serve the Table, punishes those who break its rules or upset its betters, and settles grudges before they become wars. Outsiders call us thieves. We call ourselves the Trade."
    },
    "The Table": {
      "text": "The Table: The Low Table is one name for the loose alliance of criminal bands that 'runs' the Thieves' Guild - the closest thing that den of vipers has to a parliament. It has as many names as it has enemies. The Knives in the Dark. The Court of Bastards. The Gutter Council. The Poor Man's Justice. Call it what you like — if you're in the Trade, it's the only court that matters. Representatives from the strongest criminal crews and bands, known as Hands, meet at the Table to divide territory, negotiate jobs, air grudges, and settle disputes before they spill blood in the streets. The Table doesn't command — it mediates, threatens, and occasionally makes bloody examples. A Hand that ignores the Table's rulings gets declared outlaw, which means every other Hand in the city can take what's theirs with the Table's blessing. That usually sorts things out fast. The Table is chaired by whoever is strong enough that nobody at it dares say otherwise. No election, no appointment — you sit at the head when the room lets you. That changes with fortune, and fortune changes often. Meetings happen where they happen, and never the same place twice running. If you're summoned to the Table, you go. You bring no weapons but your wits, and you better be prepared to pick sides; there is no neutrality in a den of vipers."
    },
    "Hands": {
      "text": "A Hand is an underworld crew of thieves. Your people. The ones who watch your back when the job goes wrong and split honest when it goes right. Every Hand has a leader, a territory, a way of doing things, and a name — and the name had better mean something, or you're nobody. Some Hands are old blood, three generations deep, territory so well-carved nobody remembers the last fool who tried to take it. Some are fresh — four or five clever nobodies who banded together and talked or fought their way into the Table's good graces. A Hand in good standing pays its cut to the Table and answers when called. Beyond that, your business is your business. Some Hands work clean — pride in the craft, no unnecessary blood, a code they keep because they want to, not because anyone makes them. Some are dogs. Most are somewhere between. Burglary, smuggling, forgery, the quiet knife, information, or whatever the night offers — every Hand finds its niche or it finds a grave. You don't join the Thieves' Guild. You join a Hand, or you make one. Your Hand is your crew, your colours, your family, your name in the streets. When someone asks who you run with, that answer is the only thing standing between you and whatever's coming."
    },
    "The Laughing God": {
      "text": "The Laughing God (Divinity) - Jack o' Cups, Sweet-Tongue Jack, Saint of Sin: Every tavern in Larion knows the story, and none of them tell it the same way. A mortal man — a beggar, a drunk, a fool, depending on who's talking — made a wager with the gods. What the wager was depends on the version. Some say he bet he could steal the sun and replace it before dawn. Some say he challenged Death to a drinking contest and won because he was too drunk to die. Some say he simply talked his way into the divine court, sat down uninvited, and refused to leave until they made him one of them. The details change. The ending never does: he won, and the gods — the Lords and Ladies of Virtue and all the rest of them — honored the bet because a wager is a wager, even among the divine. And Jack laughed, and hasn't stopped laughing since. He is the god of luck, mischief, drink, dares, sudden reversals of fortune and fools. Holy to thieves, beggars, wastrels, gamblers, infatuated lovers, and anyone about to do something they probably shouldn't. He has no priests, but storytellers, drunkards, jesters, pranksters and troublemakers pray to him in their own way. He has no temples but tavern corners and anywhere someone raises a glass before doing something stupid and brave. His holy days are the Feast of Fools — three days when the social order inverts, rules dissolve, and the whole world remembers that a beggar once became a god, so anything is possible. Pray to Jack and he might answer, but never the way you wanted. He helps sideways. He gives you luck, but luck has teeth. His sacred teaching, if you can call it that, fits on the back of a beer mat: the world is mad, you're going to die, so laugh, drink, dare, and see what happens. His symbol is the overflowing cup. His colors are gold and motley."
    },
    "Drinking Games of Larion": {
      "text": "Drinking games are common in taverns across Larion. The three most popular: The Slap — Two players stand on opposite sides of a table. In each round, both players drink. Then, one player slaps the other, open-handed, on the cheek; no fists, scratching, or targeting of eyes or nose. The player receiving the slap holds one hand on the table and must not drop their hand from its surface. Flinch, lift your hand, or pull it away and you lose. Then the other player gets their turn to slap their opponent, and the game contains with next round of drinking and slapping. The Choke — players take turns drinking (typically ale, and in any case something that takes a bit of time to gulp down) while their opponent insults them. The drinker must drain their cup without choking, spitting, or snorting. The insulter's job is to time their worst material for mid-swallow and force their opponent to sputter, spit, or choke in anger or laughter. Rounds alternate. Rust — the tavern wrecker. Two cups per player, one water, one drink. Players drink with their backs turned and may choose either cup. Their opponent calls 'Rust!' to accuse them of drinking water — if right, the coward drinks three penalties. If wrong, the caller drinks three. After each round, the winner names a physical challenge — hop on one foot, balance something on your nose, walk along a bench, catch a thrown object. Both players attempt it. Whoever fails loses the game."
    },
    "Magic in Larion": {
      "text": "Magic in Larion: Though definitions of magic vary by tradition, the most common is that magic is the living power of the world, present in all things. Spellcasting is drawing that power through one's body and will to reshape the world.\n\nCasters are limited in their capacity — what mages call their vessel. A novice vessel is small and fragile. It can hold enough power for simple things: lighting a flame, mending a small wound, moving a cup without touching it. A master's vessel is vast and resilient, able to hold tremendous power and direct it with fine control.\n\nThree things determine how hard a spell is and thus how large a vessel it requires. Scale — how much you are asking reality to change. Lighting a candle is barely a nudge. Calling down a firestorm is trying to move the world a long way. Complexity — how much precision the effect demands. A raw blast of fire is simple. Shaping fire into a barrier that lets allies pass through but burns enemies requires deep understanding and fine control. Opposition — how hard the target or the world pushes back. Fire magic comes easily in dry air and struggles in a downpour. Necromancy thrives in places of death and falters on consecrated ground. A strong-willed enemy resists mental magic better than a weak-minded one.\n\nWhen a mage channels too much, their vessel empties. They feel it as exhaustion — heavy limbs, clouded thoughts, a hollow ache behind the eyes. Recovery comes through rest, meditation, or sources of concentrated power like potions.\n\nDifferent traditions channel in different ways, but these are traditions, not separate powers — a fire mage and a divine caster are both drawing from the same current, just through different training and understanding.\n\nMagic is respected in Larion, sometimes feared, but never truly rare. Most towns have at least a hedge mage or healer. Cities have guilds, academies, professional enchanters. Alchemical lamps light wealthy streets. Magical healing saves lives that would otherwise be lost. But magic cannot solve everything — it is limited by the caster's vessel, by the difficulty of the effect, and by the simple fact that channelling power through a mortal body is hard, tiring work. The greatest mages in the world are extraordinary precisely because what they do is so difficult."
    },
    "Vow Foci": {
      "text": "Vow Foci:\n\nA Vow Focus is a small blessed object created when a patron divinity answers a paladin's fulfilled vow. It is not made when the vow is sworn, and fulfilling a vow does not produce one automatically: the paladin must return to prayer and the patron must answer.\n\nWhen a patron answers, the blessing transforms an object tied to the completed deed or places a new object in the paladin's hands. Give the focus a specific physical name and form, and make clear in the scene that it has been transformed or bestowed and now belongs to the paladin. It recalls the fulfilled vow, bears one recognizable aspect of the patron and its damage type, and strengthens the paladin's life, endurance, capacity for divine power, swordplay, leadership, or divine channeling.\n\nVow Foci are worn as accessories. A paladin may possess several but channels the one currently equipped. A later and greater vow may deepen an existing focus instead of creating another."
    },
    "The Primordial Plane of Ur": {
      "text": "The Primordial Plane of Ur (Cosmology): Regarded as myth or legend by many, the Primordial Plane of Ur is said to be a progenitor realm of raw magic and chaos, older than mortal civilization and untouched by its concepts. Little is known about Ur, but all sources agree that there is no law there, no morality, no pity. Power is the only currency. The High Princes of Ur are beings that most prefer not to name out of superstition — demons or gods of such might that their whims reshape the world around them — a prince's cruelty becomes the landscape's cruelty, a prince's hunger makes everything prey. Nothing built in Ur endures, because the next prince's passing mood may unmake it. The demonborn who now live in Larion trace their origins to this place, though most have never seen it and know it only through legend. Orthodox opinion holds that Pact of Suleyvan the Sealer bars the High Princes from manifesting in Larion, just as it bars the gods and other great powers of the outer planes.\n\nUr has no common law, but its creatures impose private law wherever they can. Demons do not cooperate as equals: they seize, bind, command, and submit only to a power they cannot overthrow. A demon that makes a bargain is bound to its exact words, never to the mercy or intention behind them. Banishment destroys the body it holds in Larion and drives it back to Ur; it does not kill the demon, which can return only through another breach, summons, or binding."
    },
    "The Ur Spawn": {
      "text": "The Ur Spawn: Demons that cross bodily from the Primordial Plane of Ur are known collectively as the Ur Spawn. Dominion is their nature: every rank owns what it can and serves what it cannot overthrow.\n\nFlameskin Imps are their brittle fire-spitting fodder. Snatch-Tail Fiends pull isolated victims out of the line. Flamewhip Overseer Fiends drive lesser spawn with burning lashes. Stone-Hided Fiends anchor a hold's doors and front rank. An Archfiend is the will behind the hold, darkening the air, fixing its regard upon one mind, and spending every lesser creature without grief.\n\nUr Spawn bands run four to six and may drive Hunger-fiends before them: Gaunt Fiends or Flameskin Imps in front, a Snatch-Tail Fiend at the flank, and often a Flamewhip Overseer Fiend driving them — holds and ruins answer to an Archfiend."
    },
    "Flameskin Imp": {
      "text": "Flameskin Imp: The brittle fodder of Ur Spawn bands, a child-sized shape of charred hide split by ember-light, with clawed hands and a throat that coughs cinders. It spits fire at close range and rushes only while stronger spawn keep it bold; a solid strike can break its ember-body apart. Ordinary flame passes over it harmlessly, while water and killing cold snuff the heat holding it together."
    },
    "Snatch-Tail Fiend": {
      "text": "Snatch-Tail Fiend: The taker in an Ur Spawn band, a lean two-legged fiend whose barbed tail lies coiled against its back until it lashes out to three times the creature's length. It hunts from a flank or doorway, hooks one victim, and drags them away from allies and toward the waiting imps. The tail is also its nerve: sever it and the fiend breaks off, mewling and desperate to escape."
    },
    "Flamewhip Overseer Fiend": {
      "text": "Flamewhip Overseer Fiend: The driver behind an Ur Spawn band, a tall narrow fiend carrying a lash of living embers coiled around one arm. It stays behind lesser spawn, striking them into a frenzy and lashing enemies who reach the line with fire. The courage of a driven band belongs to its overseer; kill the driver and the lesser spawn lose their nerve."
    },
    "Stone-Hided Fiend": {
      "text": "Stone-Hided Fiend: The wall of an Ur Spawn hold, broad as a gate and plated in black hide that overlaps like broken basalt. It advances slowly, crushes with millstone fists, and holds the doorway, stair, or ground an Archfiend assigned it without pursuing beyond that order. Arrows and edged weapons skate from its hide; acid eats through the seams, radiant power burns it, and killing the will behind it can leave it guarding an order that no longer means anything."
    },
    "Archfiend": {
      "text": "Archfiend: The will behind an Ur Spawn hold, a great infernal presence around which ordinary light visibly dims. It pulls brightness from the air into killing shadow, fixes its regard upon one mind until little else can think clearly, and knows every name beneath its rule. An Archfiend spends underlings without grief and keeps every bargain to the letter — the letter, never the spirit."
    },
    "The Excession of Ur": {
      "text": "The Excession of Ur (Cosmology): Sometimes also called the Translation of Ur, the Excession of Ur is the mythic event by which the jungle now known as Ntar Vanna came to exist in Larion. The demonborn tell that a High Prince of Ur called Aldorath turned against his own kind, slaying his brother-prince to break a dominion free from the Primordial Plane and translate it bodily into the mortal world. The translated territory became the Ntar Vanna: jungle of staggering scale grown from Ur-soil, saturated with primordial magic. Whether Aldorath acted from compassion, ambition, or some motive beyond mortal understanding depends on who tells the story. The Excession is the founding event of demonborn civilization in Larion, and to this day no scholar has offered a convincing alternative explanation for the jungle's alien soil, its unnatural vitality, or the ruins of a non-human empire buried in its depths."
    },
    "Aldorath the Excessor": {
      "text": "High Prince of Ur Aldorath the Excessor (Cosmology): Also known as Aldorath the Apostate or Aldorath the Kinslayer. The legend and cult of this obscure, turbulent and divisive figure are little-known outside of the southern jungles of Ntar Vanna, though demonborn throughout Larion can invariably give the essential details of his life and deeds. Aldorath is said to have been a High Prince of Ur who led the Excession, relocating his principality out of the Primordial Plane of Ur and into Larion. All sources agree that he slew his own brother to accomplish it — whether as sacrifice, in battle, or for reasons unknown. The demonborn revere him as their liberator, the prince who freed them from bondage to the chaotic caprices of the High Princes and delivered them to Larion, there to decide their own destiny. Old inscriptions in the ruined cities of Ntar Vanna name him as sovereign, lawgiver, and builder. What became of him is a subject of much debate and even sectarian strife. Did whatever catastrophe claimed the demonborn cities claim him as well? Did he bring ruin upon his own works, as he once destroyed his brother? Is he sleeping in the deep jungle, diminished but alive? Every generation of demonborn must seek their own answers."
    },
    "Demonborn": {
      "text": "The demonborn are a people of otherworldly heritage whose distant ancestors are commonly believed to have hailed from the Primordial Plane of Ur. They are marked by horns, tails, unusual coloring, and eyes that glow faintly in darkness — appearances that unsettle those unfamiliar with them. The greatest concentration of demonborn in Larion live in the vast southern jungles of Ntar Vanna, though communities exist in the southern isles, the eastern deserts, and wherever trade and opportunity draw them. Demonborn temperament runs hot — passionate, direct, quick to anger, and fiercely loyal. Their culture values hospitality, community, and deliberate self-mastery. This last is not abstract virtue. The demonborn regard themselves as self-imposed exiles from Ur, a place without restraint, and the discipline required to live among mortal peoples is something each generation must learn anew."
    },
    "The Children of Ur": {
      "text": "Children of Ur (cult): The Children of Ur are a proscribed demonborn cult, outlawed in the jungles of Ntar Vanna and hunted wherever they surface in Larion. They hold that the Excession of Ur — the cosmological act that brought the demonborn from the Primordial Plane into Larion — was not a self-imposed exile but the beachhead of a conquest. The Children teach that their ancient leader, Aldorath the Excessor, was a conqueror, not a refugee, and that his legacy has been deliberately corrupted to keep the demonborn docile. They seek to complete the Translation of Ur that he began but was unable to conclude: to merge the Primordial Plane fully into Larion, flooding the mortal world with Ur's raw power and restoring the demonborn to their primordial nature. Virtue, restraint, and compassion are chains forged to prevent this ascension. To mainstream demonborn, the Children are dangerous apostates who threaten everything the Excession was meant to achieve: freedom from the High Princes, the opportunity for each to decide his own destiny, and a new beginning in a new world. To outsiders, they confirm every suspicion about what the demonborn truly are. Children of Ur cells operate in secret wherever demonborn communities exist, recruiting those who feel the pull of their heritage most keenly."
    },
    "Chameleon-kin": {
      "text": "Chameleon-kin (species): The chameleon-kin, who call themselves the Mounari, are a reclusive amphibious people native to the jungles of Ntar Vanna. They are slight of build, with independently moving eyes, prehensile tails, and skin that shifts color and pattern instinctively to match their surroundings — a natural camouflage so effective that a still Mounari is nearly invisible. They are extraordinary climbers, capable of scaling any surface and moving through canopy, cliff, and water with equal ease. The Mounari are a patient, observant people who value stillness, discipline, and the art of passing unseen. Their masters teach techniques of movement, breath, and concealment that other species can learn, though never to the same degree — Mounari schools in the deep jungle draw students from across Larion seeking training in stealth and infiltration. Outside Ntar Vanna, chameleon-kin are rare and often regarded with unease. A people who can disappear at will are difficult to trust, and the Mounari seem largely unbothered by this."
    },
    "Monkeyfolk": {
      "text": "The monkeyfolk of the southern jungles of Ntar Vanna call themselves the High Vanarin, and they will correct you if you forget. They are a quick, agile, canopy-dwelling people — acrobatic, gregarious, and possessed of an unshakeable conviction in their own importance. The High Vanarin are the self-appointed lords of the upper canopy, and anything that enters their domain is considered a gift freely given. This includes cargo, personal belongings, and occasionally people. Despite this, the Vanarin are not petty thieves — they are, by their own account, heroes, sages, and protectors of the jungle. The maddening thing is that this is often true. Vanarin warriors are fearless and fast, their elders wise in jungle lore. Their loyalty, once earned, is ferocious. Playfully pompous, impossibly vain, generous to a fault with things they consider theirs — which is everything — and excellent company provided you don't leave anything valuable unattended."
    },
    "Thrallmages of Falmastra": {
      "text": "Thrallmages of Falmastra (Faction):\n\nMind-controlling psionicists operating from an island city-state off the coast of the Great Lagoon. Can read thoughts, speak mind-to-mind, and puppet others like living dolls. To fully bind a thrall, they need a piece of the target — a hair, a drop of blood, a worn possession — and the target's true name. Without both, no binding holds. Thrallmages hire out as spies and killers for enormous fees. Their retribution doctrine, the Hundredfold, promises a hundred deaths for any one of their number killed. Settlements near Falmastra pay tribute and are left alone. The thrallmages are few — perhaps a few dozen — but one thrallmage with the right thralls in the right places can topple a merchant house or unseat a prince. They are feared the way plagues are feared: an invisible thing that can take hold of anyone."
    },
    "The Exclave": {
      "text": "The Exclave (Tevantian Institution):\n\nThe ritual of succession of the office of the Dukedom in Tevantia, the great island city-state of the Swordlaw Isles. When a Duke dies, the Electors — the city's great merchants, guild leaders, and notables — seal themselves on the Isle of Union to choose his successor by majority vote. No one leaves until a Duke is chosen. Leaving before the Election is complete is treason. Each Elector may bring a Second — a trusted agent who acts on their behalf: carrying messages, brokering alliances, gathering intelligence, fighting duels, and doing what an Elector cannot be seen doing. The dead Duke's body is carried across the strait on a ceremonial barge lit by a thousand alchemical lanterns. He lies in state in the Chamber of Light at the base of the Burning Tower, attended by those lanterns until a successor is chosen. The tower's beacon burns for the duration. Tevantia keeps a twilight vigil — no lights after sunset, no music, no revelry — until the beacon is extinguished and the new Duke returns the light to the city on the same barge. The Exclave runs on an unspoken paradox every Tevantine knows: only the Duke can investigate crimes against the person of a current or former Duke; as the Tevantine proverb holds, 'The Duke's justice lives and dies with the Duke.' Some Exclaves have been short and civil. Others have not."
    },
    "The Burning of Tevantia": {
      "text": "The Burning of Tevantia (Historical Event): Three centuries ago, Duke Aldano III of Tevantia died without a clear successor. Three great houses claimed the seat, their private armies clashed in the streets, and the city burned. As Tevantia tore itself apart, the pirate fleets of the outer isles gathered to pick the carcass, raiding the harbor and burning what the houses had not. Facing annihilation, the three pretenders met on the Isle of Union, a religious sanctuary in the strait. There they set aside their claims and elected a Duke to lead the city's defense. Tevantia survived. The Black Palace — the ruined seat of the house that started the fighting — still stands in the city, never rebuilt, never torn down."
    },
    "The Dukedom of Tevantia": {
      "text": "The Duke of Tevantia is the First Peer — not a king, but the foremost among the great houses. Elected for life by the Electors, the Duke mediates between the feuding merchant families, commands the Admiralty and its prize courts, and leads Tevantia's defense in times of crisis. A Duke may withdraw from the seat voluntarily, though few have. The role carries enormous prestige and influence but limited direct power — the great houses would never suffer a monarch, and a Duke who overreaches finds his allies evaporating and his Electors whispering about precedent. The wisest Dukes govern by consensus, play the houses against each other just enough to keep the peace, and save their authority for when it matters."
    },
    "aspected crafting materials": {
      "text": "Aspected Materials:\n\nSome rare crafting materials carry inherent damage affinities. Silver, moonstone, and sun-gold are radiant-aspected. Stormsteel and thunderglass are shock-aspected. Emberglass and red jade are fire-aspected. Frostjade and rimebone are frost-aspected. Obsidian, voidglass, and black iron are shadow-aspected. Witchwood, aether crystal, and cold iron are arcane-aspected. Dream coral and thoughtstone are psychic-aspected. Venomwood and green glass are poison-aspected. Caustic bronze and alchemist glass are acid-aspected."
    },
    "Silver": {
      "text": "Silver: A radiant-aspected metal associated with purity, oaths, moonlight, and sanctified craft. Silver weapons harm shadow-bound creatures, undead, fiends, and lycanthropes because the metal carries a clean light through the wound."
    },
    "Moonstone": {
      "text": "Moonstone: A pale radiant-aspected gemstone used in temple blades, ritual jewelry, and warding charms. Moonstone shines strongest against undead, demons, curses, and creatures sustained by shadow."
    },
    "Sun-Gold": {
      "text": "Sun-gold: A rare radiant-aspected gold that holds warmth and light even in darkness. It is prized for holy relics, royal regalia, and weapons meant to burn away undeath or corruption."
    },
    "Stormsteel": {
      "text": "Stormsteel: A shock-aspected metal that hums faintly before storms and carries lightning through a strike. Weapons made from stormsteel deal shock damage regardless of blade shape."
    },
    "Thunderglass": {
      "text": "Thunderglass: A shock-aspected glassy mineral formed where lightning repeatedly strikes sand, towers, or mountain glass. It stores violent charge and releases it through wands, spearheads, and ritual foci."
    },
    "Emberglass": {
      "text": "Emberglass: A fire-aspected volcanic glass that glows like banked coals under pressure. Emberglass weapons and foci burn through flesh, armor gaps, and cold-natured creatures."
    },
    "Red Jade": {
      "text": "Red jade: A fire-aspected stone used in dueling blades, furnace shrines, and noble signets. It is less explosive than emberglass but holds a steady inner heat that turns strikes into fire damage."
    },
    "Frostjade": {
      "text": "Frostjade: A frost-aspected pale green stone cold enough to mist in warm air, cut from the glacier country of Aurora in the far north. Frostjade weapons bite with supernatural cold and are prized against fire creatures, fever spirits, and living magma."
    },
    "Rimebone": {
      "text": "Rimebone: A frost-aspected bone taken from ancient beasts preserved in glacier tombs or winter barrows, most of it brought south out of Aurora's ice. It drinks heat from wounds and makes even blunt weapons strike with killing cold."
    },
    "Obsidian": {
      "text": "Obsidian: A shadow-aspected volcanic glass used for knives, mirrors, and sacrificial tools. It cuts cleanly but leaves darkness in the wound, making it dangerous against radiant or living-light creatures."
    },
    "Voidglass": {
      "text": "Voidglass: A shadow-aspected black glass that reflects stars even underground. It is used in assassin blades, warlock foci, and relics meant to wound spirits, light-bearers, and the soul."
    },
    "Black Iron": {
      "text": "Black iron: A shadow-aspected iron alloy smelted with grave ash, night salts, or underworld coal. It is heavy, ugly, and feared because its wounds feel cold and lightless."
    },
    "Witchwood": {
      "text": "Witchwood: An arcane-aspected wood cut from trees grown around old spells, fairy rings, or buried charms. It is ideal for wands, staves, bows, and ritual tools that channel structured magic."
    },
    "Aether Crystal": {
      "text": "Aether crystal: An arcane-aspected crystal that refracts spellwork into visible threads. It is used in wizard foci, mage blades, lenses, and devices that strike through magical force rather than mundane impact."
    },
    "Emberglass Wands": {
      "text": "Emberglass Wands: Fire foci first made in the upper terraces of Earthsky's Glassworks Row, where glassworkers set small shards of Emberglass traded out of the Fire Mountain country into witchwood or black iron. A good first wand has only a coal-red splinter; the shard may be recut or replaced with a larger, clearer piece when its bearer can hold more fire, though a careless upgrade cracks both glass and vessel. Old wandmakers judge a mage by the marks their wand has survived."
    },
    "Binder's Aether Bracers": {
      "text": "Binder's Aether Bracers: Tarnholme Binder foci made in the College of Binders' Menagerie around a brass forearm cuff, an Aether Crystal ring, and tether-hooks. The hooks give summoned beings a marked place to arrive and a path back to the circle; they are not chains and cannot replace a bargain or binding. A novice bracer starts with empty hooks, and Binders earn finer crystals, more stable hooks, and stranger materials through clean summons in the Run."
    },
    "Icerune Sealstones": {
      "text": "Icerune Sealstones: Small clear slabs from the Warding Hall of Clanhold Icerune, wrapped in black iron and cut with one deliberately unfinished seal. The open line is recut as a warder grows, so a sealstone records the hand's advancement as much as it protects a door; old ones carry nested lines and are trusted at thresholds where a new ward would fail. Icerune sells lesser stones at the Rune-Market, but the best are issued to people the clan expects to stand a watch."
    },
    "Shaper's Crystal Signets": {
      "text": "Shaper's Crystal Signets: Apprentice enchanter's rings from Tarnholme's Wonderworks, copper cages built around a loose Aether Crystal. The cage is intentionally unfinished: the maker can swap in a better crystal and cut a more ambitious binding rather than discard the first ring. A signet that has held a difficult working becomes a public mark of a Shaper's craft, and the best bear scars from failed experiments."
    },
    "Aether-Crystal Monocles": {
      "text": "Aether-Crystal Monocles: One-eyed Seeker lenses cut on Earthsky's Glassworks Row from clear Aether Crystal, the frame balanced behind the ear so the wearer can keep both hands free. Looking through one makes recent spellwork show as threads, smears, afterimages, or missing pieces; deeper cuts reveal older traces but also show more of what the lens's owner would rather not see. Tarnholme Seekers prize Earthsky's best cutters and trade field findings for replacements."
    },
    "Amberbound Beast Tokens": {
      "text": "Amberbound Beast Tokens: Pocket-sized beast fetishes made at the Wolfpack's Spirit Hearth from clear amber around a tooth, claw, or tuft freely given by a beast-spirit. The tiny hyena, wolf, raptor, or stranger beast is not a trapped creature; it is a remembered shape that turns toward nearby spirits and gives a shaman a way to address them. A token made for a particular bond grows more exact with time, while a generic token is a first step for someone who has not yet earned a spirit's personal gift."
    },
    "Dawnfire Reliquaries": {
      "text": "Dawnfire Reliquaries: Small sun-gold pendants made around a coal carried from the sacred fire of the House of First Light on the Sacred Way. The ember is not a proof of temple membership; it is a focus for anyone whose deeds and prayers have earned a priest's trust. A reliquary can be reconsecrated with sacred ash or fitted with a brighter ember as its bearer takes on more demanding divine work."
    },
    "Mender's Spring Phials": {
      "text": "Mender's Spring Phials: Moonstone-capped glass vessels filled at the Warm Pools of the Mend. Menders use the water as a diagnostic as much as a remedy: it clouds around sickness, clears as healing settles, and is never poured into a wound without knowing what it will meet. A novice phial can be refilled at the Mend; rarer waters and a patient's willing blessing make stronger versions."
    },
    "Rootheart Seedpods": {
      "text": "Rootheart Seedpods: Living seedpods prepared by Canopy's Academy of Greenshaping and carried in small Witchwood cages. Their roots turn toward nearby life and stir when nature magic is called, but the pod is not a pet or a spell in itself. Greenshapers graft and awaken older seedpods for students who learn to persuade living wood without breaking it."
    },
    "Red-Jade Vital Seals": {
      "text": "Red-Jade Vital Seals: Thumb-sized seals cut by the Red Chantry with a groove for the bearer's blood. A fresh mark lets the seal read the nearness of wounds, living blood, and the dead; deeper blood-runes can be cut into the jade as the bearer advances. Chantry teachers insist that every stronger seal records a cost, and that no stone makes blood magic safe."
    },
    "Bloodchannel Daggers": {
      "text": "Bloodchannel Daggers: The Red Chantry and independent blood dancers set narrow channels of red jade, moonstone, bone, or dark glass into daggers that serve as both blade and blood-magic focus. Fresh blood from wielder or foe runs the channel only while a trained hand is working it; the weapon stores nothing and heals no one by itself. Accomplished dancers seek blades cut for particular techniques, and stronger bloodchannels are commissioned, inherited, won from teachers, or taken from fallen practitioners."
    },
    "Dream Coral": {
      "text": "Dream coral: A psychic-aspected coral harvested from enchanted reefs and sleep-haunted waters. It resonates with memory, fear, desire, and hallucination, making it valuable for charms and mind-affecting weapons."
    },
    "Thoughtstone": {
      "text": "Thoughtstone:\n\nA psychic-aspected mineral that warms when held by a thinking creature. Weapons or foci set with thoughtstone wound concentration, courage, and perception as much as flesh."
    },
    "Venomwood": {
      "text": "Venomwood:\n\nA poison-aspected wood from toxic groves and serpent-haunted marshes. It seeps venom into arrows, knives, and staves, making even clean cuts sicken the target."
    },
    "Green Glass": {
      "text": "Green glass:\n\nA poison-aspected alchemical glass stained by distilled toxins. It is brittle but useful in darts, ritual shards, and foci that deliver venomous magic."
    },
    "Caustic Bronze": {
      "text": "Caustic bronze:\n\nAn acid-aspected bronze alloy that tarnishes into corrosive green-black patterns. Its wounds sting, pit armor, and eat at scales, shell, and stone."
    },
    "Alchemist Glass": {
      "text": "Alchemist glass:\n\nAn acid-aspected glass made to hold, focus, and release corrosive reagents. It is common in experimental foci, trap components, and weapons meant to dissolve armor or hardened hides."
    },
    "Weapon Materials and Monster Weaknesses": {
      "text": "Weapon Materials and Monster Weaknesses:\n\nRadiant force harms undead, fiends, lycanthropes, shadow creatures, and aberrations; silver, moonstone, and sun-gold are prized for that reason. Shadow force harms blessed casters and celestials, but shadow-bound and undead creatures often resist it. Fire kills plants, fungi, venomous creatures, trolls, and frost beings, while fire elementals and fiends shrug it off. Shock disrupts constructs and machines. Arcane force bites the fey and magical beings; witchwood, aether crystal, and cold iron carry it, and cold iron's touch is anathema to the fey. Acid eats stone, armor, shell, and golem-work, making caustic bronze and alchemist glass useful against stone constructs and hardened beasts. Poison is useless or weak against undead, constructs, oozes, and venomous creatures, but dangerous to ordinary living bodies. Psychic force is resisted by constructs, plants, oozes, and mind-things; it is best used against ordinary thinking creatures, not things without normal minds. Piercing is poor against skeletons, plants, constructs, oozes, and spirits; slashing is poor against oozes, spirits, stone, and hard shell; impact is useful against armor, brittle bodies, mages, and hard carapaces, but trained warriors and giants often absorb blunt punishment. Mundane humanoids and beasts usually have no special damage interactions. Experts explain these truths through material and anatomy: what has no blood laughs at poison, what has no organs cares little for arrows, what has no solid body ignores blades, and what is made of flame does not fear fire."
    },
    "Aspected Crafting Doctrine": {
      "text": "Aspected Crafting Doctrine:\n\nWeapon material determines its damage affinity more strongly than shape. Stormsteel and thunderglass weapons deal shock and are used against constructs and machines. Caustic bronze and alchemist glass deal acid and are used against stone constructs, armor, shell, and hardened scales. Silver, moonstone, and sun-gold deal radiant and are used against undead, fiends, lycanthropes, shadow creatures, and aberrations. Emberglass and red jade deal fire and are used against plants, fungi, trolls, venomous creatures, and frost beings. Frostjade and rimebone deal frost and are used against fire elementals, magma creatures, and oozes. Obsidian, voidglass, and black iron deal shadow and are used against celestials and blessed enemies. Witchwood, aether crystal, and cold iron deal arcane and are used for spellcraft, wards, and fighting magical beings; cold iron from beneath Sanctuary Vale is the weapon of choice against the fey. Dream coral and thoughtstone deal psychic. Venomwood and green glass deal poison and are used against living bodies, but not undead, constructs, oozes, or venomous beasts."
    },
    "Halfling Alchemy": {
      "text": "Halfling Alchemy:\n\nThe domestic magical tradition of the halflings of Whitecliff Downs, practiced through cooking, baking, brewing, and small charm-work rather than wand or grimoire. A halfling alchemist binds intent into food and craft — a truffle-paste charm that lets a hog answer to its name across a wood, a pie that warms a traveler from the inside for a full day's walk, a cordial that settles a fever overnight. The work runs in families and is taught grandmother to grandchild. It is rarely flashy and never battlefield-grade, but it is woven so deep into halfling life that most outsiders mistake it for plain good cooking."
    },
    "Moonbloom Truffles": {
      "text": "Moonbloom Truffles:\n\nA pale, faintly luminous truffle that fruits under the new moon in the old oak-and-chestnut woods of Whitecliff Downs. Hillhome's Truffle-Woods supply the village kitchens; Moonbloom Woods beside Whitehill Kobold Warren are prized for exceptional specimens beneath undisturbed roots. Trained truffle-hogs can scent ripe truffles deep underground. Halfling cooks turn them into pies, pastes, and cordials. Moonbloom carries a magical charge that anchors halfling alchemy and holds charms longer than mundane ingredients. A single good truffle is worth a week's wages in coin and a great deal more in trade."
    },
    "The Newmoon Fair": {
      "text": "The Newmoon Fair:\n\nA monthly halfling festival held in the halfling villages of Whitecliff Downs on the night of the new moon. Each village hosts its own, with pavilions, ribbons, and contest rings on the village green — pies, ales, archery, sheep-shearing, singing, and whatever else the local families can argue about. The Fair is where rivalries are settled and reputations made; the pie-crown in particular is held with fierce pride and lost with fierce shame. Outsiders are welcome and usually fed past the point of comfort."
    },
    "Tinytrolls": {
      "text": "Tinytrolls (Species):\n\nSmall, bog-green, knee-high cousins of the great trolls of the north, found in brambly hollows and overgrown woods across Larion's settled regions. They speak a coarse trade-pidgin, hoard whatever shiny things they can steal, and squabble in shrieking little bands. A tinytroll alone is a nuisance; a nest of them can ruin a forager's day and occasionally a forager. They regenerate weakly, like their larger kin, but a torch or a determined dog handles them. Hedge-witches sometimes bargain with them for trinkets; most farmers just chase them off with a rake."
    },
    "Harrans' Rising": {
      "text": "Harran's Rising (History):\n\nAn age ago the dead rose in the City of Ancestors, the great necropolis near Ardan's Watch. The cause was a ghost — a warlord of the old Fourth Empire, cruel in life and crueler in death, who woke in his tomb and called the buried dead of the necropolis to his banner. For a season the Inquisitors fought to hold the risen host inside the walls of the dead and keep the truth from the living city. It was an Inquisitor named Harran who put the warlord down at the last and broke the Rising, though it cost him his life to do it. The Inquisitors still speak of Harran's Rising as the worst night the order has known, and of Harran as one of the greatest of their kind. Some say the warlord was never truly unmade, only sent back — and that this is why the City of Ancestors must be watched without end."
    },
    "The Grey Lady": {
      "text": "The Grey Lady, the Lady of the Last Road (Goddess): the power that keeps the crossing of the dead. Folk do not so much worship her as pray for her aid: common belief holds that when a soul dies it comes to the Shadelands, the grey country of the dead, and there it may get stuck — bound by anger, grief, or business left unfinished, unable to move on. The Grey Lady's mercy is to lead the willing across Repenthe, the River of Oblivion, where a soul drinks and forgets all it was, and so is freed to be born again. To cross is to find rest. To refuse — out of pride, hatred, or a love too fierce to surrender — is to remain, clinging to the memory of a life that is over. The dead who will not cross are what haunt the living world. This is why funerals pray the departed will go willingly, and why a body is brought before her shrine on its way to the grave: not to beg her favor, but to ask that the dead be willing to take her road."
    },
    "The Empyreans": {
      "text": "Empyreans:\nEmpyreans are a species of sentient winged, bipedal humanoids, rare now, scattered thin across Larion. They are tall — half a head or more above a tall human — and built on a grander scale: high, strong features, eyes that hold a faint inner light, and broad feathered wings folded at the back. Wing and skin run to no single hue; an empyrean may be white, red, black, or shades between, and the coloring varies from one to the next as hair or eyes do among humans. Long ago, in the age of the Third Empire, their kind ruled from the high air, a proud and tyrant people who worshipped the sun and bent the lands beneath them to their will. That age is long over; the empyreans of today are no more answerable for it than dragonkin are for the tyrant-drakes of old, and most ordinary folk have no notion of this ancient history in any case."
    },
    "Vortigar the Unburied": {
      "text": "Vortigar the Unburied:\n\nVortigar was a warlord of the Fourth Empire who served the demon-princes of Ur and was thrown down with them. In life he would not be ruled and in death he would not be buried — he refused the grave and the crossing both, and so he remains, a corpse held together by a will that never learned to stop.Where Vortigar walks, the buried dead rise to his banner: he empties old battlefields and graveyards and marshals the risen into an army, fighting still the wars he lost in life. His remembered-world in the Shadelands is the field of his last defeat, which he relives without end, certain that this time he will win it — and he raises Larion's dead to fill out his ranks. To put down the risen is not enough; they rise again so long as Vortigar wills it. He can only be ended at the root, in the grey country, where an Inquisitor must break him on the field he cannot stop losing."
    },
    "The Fourth Empire": {
      "text": "The Fourth Empire: Before Larion as it is now, the land was ruled by a proud and cruel dominion whose highborn held power in league with the Demon-Princes of Ur. Their cities stood where Silvervale's heartland stands today, and their ruin lies buried in strata beneath Ardan's Watch and the surrounding country. Most people know the Fourth Empire as a dead tyranny. The League remembers it as a warning: old tyrannies return through buried relics, hungry dead, cults, and powers that were never as gone as the living hoped."
    },
    "Sethelle of the Nine Sorrows": {
      "text": "Sethelle of the Nine Sorrows:\nSethelle was a great lady of the Fourth Empire. When that age fell she lost everything — her house, her station, all she loved — and drowned so wholly in grief that the grief outlived her. She would not cross the River and be unmade, for to forget her sorrow felt a betrayal of all she had lost, and so she remains: one of the seven mighty dead who bound themselves together long ago, were put down, and have now returned."
    },
    "Karneth Draghmar": {
      "text": "Karneth Draghmar:\n\nKarneth was the Fourth Empire's executioner-judge, the man who made slaughter look like law. Where he rises, the Iron Lictors come with him: masked imperial dead carrying axes, chains, bronze sentence-plates, and iron wheel-frames. They drag the accused into public places, hang a verdict around the victim's neck, and bind them to the Imperial Wheel to be broken and displayed as an example. Karneth does not tempt, argue, or scheme. He pronounces sentence, and the dead carry it out."
    },
    "The Lady Mavrocine": {
      "text": "The Lady Mavrocine:\n\nMavrocine was a vampire impostor who murdered a noblewoman, took her face, and used her beauty and borrowed name to move through the Fourth Empire's courts until everyone around her was dead. Where she rises, important people begin dying behind locked doors, and someone beautiful, trusted, and false is always nearby. Her servants are vampire thralls, dead handmaids, and elegant assassins who open doors that should have stayed locked. Mavrocine's work is simple: kill the right person, take the right place, and let the living invite her in."
    },
    "Ossarine the Pale Crown": {
      "text": "Ossarine the Pale Crown:\n\nOssarine is the dead will inside the Pale Crown, a white imperial crown that turns ambition into obedience. Whoever wears it becomes cold, commanding, and impossible to disobey for long. The living kneel against their will; the dead rise to serve; ambitious fools call it destiny until they can no longer take the crown off. Ossarine's servants are the Crown-Thralls: enthralled living subjects, kneeling ghosts, and dead knights bound to the crown's command. To fight Ossarine, the crown must be taken from its host and its claim to rule broken."
    },
    "Belugar Thoth-Amon": {
      "text": "Belugar Thoth-Amon:\n\nBelugar was a priest of Ur who fed the living to black altars and called the screams worship. Where he rises, old altar-stones wake under ruined forts, catacombs, and cursed woods. People marked for sacrifice are dragged away, and the dead rise as the Shackled Host: corpses bound in massive chains, using those chains as weapons while the souls inside them remain offerings to Belugar's rite. Break the altar, free the Host, and the priest loses his hold."
    },
    "Druathyr the Thrice-Drowned": {
      "text": "Druathyr the Thrice-Drowned:\n\nDruathyr is lord of the fen-drowned dead. Where he rises, black water takes people: wells knock from below, ditches pull at ankles, millponds give back the dead, and flooded cellars become doors into the drowning dark. His servants are the Drowned Dead: waterlogged corpses that drag the living into mire, pond, ditch, or well to join their number. Druathyr does not need a court or cult. He only needs water deep enough to close over a mouth."
    },
    "Talion Corwin": {
      "text": "Talion Corwin: The founder of House Corwin's greatness, dead now and passed into legend. The house had fallen into decadence in his father's day, and Talion was its black sheep — a wastrel and a prodigal who drank and brawled and shamed his name, and at last left it. He came home in the hour of Marrenford's need, when renegade elves of the Everwood had raised a mercenary army to throw down the castle and open the river-crossings to their smuggling. The town was all but lost. Talion swore an oath upon the Elfstone to lift the siege or die for it — and then he kept it, leading the broken town against the host, slaying the enemy commander with his own hand and routing the rest. He went on to become a Master of Swords and built the Blade chapterhouse at Marrenford that bears his name. He is the proof the people of Marrenford love best: that a man can fall low, suffer, come back true, and make himself great."
    },
    "The Shadelands": {
      "text": "The Shadelands:\nPast the edge of the living world lies the Shadelands, where the dead go. Most pass through and are gone. Some will not — the angry, the proud, the ones with business unfinished — and these linger as ghosts, and worse. Folk who know say that is where hauntings come from, and risen dead, and the cold places where the air goes wrong. A barrow that will not stay quiet, a battlefield where the slain keep walking — the trouble has its root in the Shadelands, pressing through where the grey world rubs against ours. Necromancers are said to walk there and come back. Most people would rather not think about it."
    },
    "The Underworld": {
      "text": "The Underworld:\nThe Underworld is the network of caverns and tunnels beneath the surface world, older than any kingdom above it. It holds the ruins of empires that fell in ages past, along with the wealth they left behind — relics and lost work buried in the deep. Underground peoples have built fortresses there among the dead cities, and many are hostile to the surface. Their raiders come up through old mines, wells, and breached cellars. Those who descend for the treasure face the things that live down there."
    },
    "Order of the Silver Sword": {
      "text": "Order of the Silver Sword: Larion's specialist monster-hunters, seated at Castle Harstone in Carendon. Candidates are Initiates until they complete one supervised trial hunt and are admitted before the Great Hall. Full Silverblades receive a silver blade pin and carry Order silver suited to their calling: any kind of silvered weapon, a silver-faced shield, or a silver-bound magical focus or implement. They rise from Silverblade to Veteran Silverblade and then Master of the Hunt through recognized hunts and the judgment of the Order."
    },
    "Tarnholme Academy of the Arcane": {
      "text": "Tarnholme Academy of the Arcane: Larion's seated wizard school on Jade Tarn. Students rise from First to Second, Third, and Upper Circle through recognized field Practica, gaining harder work, greater access, independence, and mentorship authority. Its five Colleges shape specialization without barring broader magic: Sentinels ward, Shapers transform and build, Binders summon and seal, Seekers investigate and divine, and Menders heal and restore."
    },
    "Watchtower": {
      "text": "Watchtower: The great elderstone tower in Ardan's Watch and headquarters of the League of Adventurers. Its League Hall posts Silvervale work and registers adventurers; its Map Room gathers reports from across Larion; its Portal Hall holds voluntary links to allied seats such as Tarnholme Academy, Castle Harstone, the Black Tower, and the Citadel of the Pact."
    },
    "Duchy of Drusavia": {
      "text": "Duchy of Drusavia: A bright old duchy of forested hills, wealthy dales, haunted estates, and buried vampire-era sin. Its capital is Drusavia, where the Temple of Galard honors Castian the liberator and the Black Tower handles the dead that courage alone could not settle."
    },
    "Temple of Galard": {
      "text": "Temple of Galard: Drusavia's public faith of courage, sacrifice, and radiant arms, and the Hall of Shields of the Templars Adamant. The temple honors Castian, blesses steel and moonstone for work against the dead, recognizes deeds during Galard's Proving Year, and stands as the bright civic counterpart to the Black Tower."
    },
    "Templars Adamant": {
      "text": "Templars Adamant: Galard's Shieldbearer order seated at the Temple of Galard in Drusavia. A person who completes the Proving Year may freely swear the Shieldbearer oath and enter as a Templar Initiate with the White-Silver Shield Clasp. One supervised first shield-work earns full Templar Adamant rank; three recognized independent works earn Templar Captain; four further great works may earn acclamation as Templar Champion. Their work protects warders and the living, purges hostile dead, destroys anchors, and seals breaches. They never traffic or bargain with shades."
    },
    "Knights Vigilant": {
      "text": "Knights Vigilant: Galard's proud undead-hunting order seated at the Temple of the Sword Vigilant above Aldwall. A New-Sworn bears a moonstone oath-brooch and completes one supervised first ride to become Moor-Knight. Three recognized independent rides earn a personal banner and Banner-Knight rank; four further great rides may earn acclamation as Champion Vigilant. Completed rides are recounted and recognized publicly in the Feast-Hall."
    },
    "Harran of the Inquisition": {
      "text": "Harran of the Inquisition (Historical Figure):  Harran was an Inquisitor of Drusavia, reckoned among the greatest the order ever fielded, who broke the Rising in the City of Ancestors and died putting down the warlord-ghost that caused it. The Inquisitors held his wake at the tavern on Lowlamp Lane and never let it end; the place bears his name, and his bar-tab has stayed open ever since. By custom an Inquisitor returned alive from a hard task drinks on Harran, and once each night the house drinks not to mourn him, but to keep faith with the work he finished."
    },
    "The Ranks of the Inquisition": {
      "text": "Shadow Inquisition Ranks: An accepted member bears the Mark of Rhadan and begins as an Initiate, handling one supervised surface case. The Mark establishes membership and anchors authorized contact with the Shadelands; it does not grant a Shade or full rank. A successful Initiate may undergo the Rite of Bonding at the Pit and rise as a Shade-Bonded Inquisitor. After three recognized Inquisitor cases they may become an Adept who leads deep work. After four further recognized Adept cases they may become a Master with authority over the order's gravest cases and the defense of the Pit."
    },
    "The High Wardens": {
      "text": "The High Wardens: The elven wardens of the Everwood, answering to the Council of Elf-Lords in Canopy. Cloaked in greens and greys, armed with longbow and yew-staff, they enforce high elven law throughout the forest, from the elven heartland of Elfwood to the borders of Sanctuary Vale, and they are seen rarely and heard less. They permit halfling foragers, human woodsmen, and dwarven charcoal-burners to work the outer belt of the wood under old understandings; past their line, only the Wardens go. They keep to the wood and keep their hands clean — the messy frontier work of bandit-havens, raiders, and disappearances they leave to Sanctuary, and to whoever Sanctuary will send."
    },
    "The Greater Goblinate": {
      "text": "The Greater Goblinate: The goblin state that rules the Black Fells from its capital at Smoketown. The Dragon Queen united the warring tribes into a single power with a governor, a professional army, and industry: obsite mines, dragonfire works, and forges that arm the firelancer legions. The Goblinate raids by sea, presses south, and serves its absent queen openly — her coming is the state's faith, preached by the allied Church of the Pyre. Rank-and-file goblins have never seen her; they know the dragonfire flows, the lances work, and the old world that despised them is learning to fear them. Not every goblin serves it: the tribes it failed to swallow and the scattered raiders of the borderlands owe the Goblinate nothing."
    },
    "The Great Dragons": {
      "text": "The Great Dragons: The elder dragons of the first ages — powers that shaped the world and vanished in an age no one remembers. What remains of them is bones, hoards, tombs, and the dragonkin who carry their blood thinned. The dragons of the present age are lesser creatures by every account, and even these are rare. Atravoraxis, the Dragon Queen the Goblinate serves, claims to be of the elder kind — and claims to be coming back. Whether she is what she claims, none living can say; the Church of the Pyre preaches it as gospel, and the Goblinate arms for her arrival."
    },
    "Astarieth the Risen": {
      "text": "Astarieth the Risen: The mire dragon who rules the lizardfolk dragon-kingdom of the Reedmoot Fens. She was a priestess once, so the fen-folk say, and rose — or was remade — as a dragon; her kingdom worships her as a living god. She rules the closed western fens through her priest-kings, and her brood is raised in the great mounds, fed by the brood-rite. The fens under her rule are shut: what goes in is taken, and what is taken goes to the mounds. The free moot at Reedmoot holds the eastern strip against her war-parties, and its people mock the dragon out loud because every one of them fears her."
    },
    "The Brood-Rite": {
      "text": "The Brood-Rite: The practice that feeds Astarieth's brood. Captives taken in the Reedmoot Fens — travelers, salvagers, fen-folk snatched in raids — are carried west alive, held in the pens of the brood-mounds, and sacrificed to the brood at the new moon. The rite is why lizardfolk war-parties take prisoners rather than kill, and why rescue parties from the free moot never want for volunteers: between the taking and the new moon, the taken can still be brought home."
    },
    "The Firing of the Golden Fleet": {
      "text": "The Firing of the Golden Fleet: The Empire of Light sent a fleet against the dragon-kingdom of the Fens, and Astarieth burned it at anchor in a single night. The wrecks lie where they burned, and the fen-folk of the free moot have salvaged them ever since — the alchemical fire-stores locked in Reedmoot's stone store-house came off those ships, and so did much of the moot's iron. The wrecks still hold fire-stores, imperial arms, and the imperial dead, and the dragon-kingdom watches its trophy waters."
    },
    "Heronfolk": {
      "text": "Heronfolk: Tall, long-legged marsh-folk of the Stiltwood in the Reedmoot Fens, living in roosts high in the mangrove crowns. They are patient, deadly with the bow from height, and few of words with strangers. The dragon-kingdom tried to swallow the Stiltwood and stopped trying: rooting archers out of a drowned mangrove forest costs more than it takes. The heronfolk trade with the free moot, keep their own counsel, and owe nothing to anyone."
    },
    "Ophialtha": {
      "text": "Ophialtha: The dragon of the Seadeep Kingdoms, queen of everything beneath the storm-dark southern sea. She is sea-bound — she cannot leave the water, and no dry ground is ever within her reach, only her servants' — and she is patient as the sea itself. The drowned-and-changed are her gift and her subjects: surface folk remade for the water, generations deep, who garrison the drowned places and take the living down alive. She holds court in the sunken Dome of All Worlds at the heart of drowned Orichala, and she does not stir for fighting in the standing city. Disturb the Dome, and she comes."
    },
    "The Drowned-and-Changed": {
      "text": "The Drowned-and-Changed: The people of the Seadeep Kingdoms beneath the storm-dark southern sea. They were surface folk once — sailors, salvagers, the taken — remade for the water by the dragon Ophialtha's gift, generations deep, and they count the remaking a blessing. The kingdoms prefer keeping to killing: they take the living down alive, and what is taken joins them in time. Sailors' law for Seadeep water is old and simple: cross without stopping and you will be suffered; stop to take what the sea has claimed, set foot on the drowned places, or pry at the wrecks, and the kingdoms answer from below."
    },
    "The Dome of All Worlds": {
      "text": "The Dome of All Worlds: The great wonder of drowned Orichala — a colossal sealed habitat that held living specimens from every land the old empire ruled. It sank with the city and trapped its air, and its old magic still keeps the air good and the gardens alive. Centuries feral, its creatures answer to nothing: rock-apes in the high tiers, terror-birds on the grass shelf, a grown-monstrous crocodile in the channels, and bright venomous things in the green. Ophialtha enters through the sea-breach in the great lake and rules the Dome as her court — a queen of the deep presiding over a little world of the surface."
    },
    "Nuur-Suthra and the Soul Engine": {
      "text": "Nuur-Suthra and the Soul Engine: Nuur-Suthra, the Flame Undying, is worshipped as the dragon of the Alar Coast but lairs north of it, in the wreck of the Celestial Citadel, which struck the mountains above the Redrock coast. The Church of the Pyre rules the coast in its name and ships it tribute; the dragon itself is dormant, and stirs for one thing only. In the citadel's Engine Vault stands the Soul Engine — a vessel of brass and black elderstone, tall as a tower, roaring flame without pause since the old empire fell. It is a prison: the soul of a djinn is sealed inside, and the flame is its rage. Nuur-Suthra lairs coiled around it, warming itself, drawing power, healing from its fire in battle. While the Soul Engine burns, the dragon cannot die. Free the djinn or break the seal, and the next death is real."
    },
    "The Red-Cowls": {
      "text": "The Red-Cowls: The common soldiery of the Church of the Pyre on the Alar Coast — believers, poor men's sons, men with nowhere else, sworn to the Church and cowled in red. They work in squads of four to six under grey priests, the Church's officers and casters. In Serendipity they do not police theft or brawls; they hunt one crime, enmity toward the Church, and the marked they take are not seen again. In the outer desert they ride in hunting parties a dozen strong, running down Tribesfolk and fugitives. Above the cowls stand the priests; above the priests, the Temple of the Flame Undying; above the temple, the dragon."
    },
    "The Desert Tribes": {
      "text": "The Desert Tribes: The free peoples of the Alar Coast's deep desert — the survivors and descendants of Gesenthame, and of everyone the Church of the Pyre has burned out since. They raid the Church's tribute caravans, shelter and smuggle out all who flee the flame, and cannot be caught: their camps pack in an hour and the deep sand is theirs. A stranger's standing with the Tribes is earned in steps — fed at the guest-fire, then known, then trusted and offered guiding. Of the very few counted as the Tribes' own, some are vouched onward to places the Tribes do not name."
    },
    "The Burning of Gesenthame": {
      "text": "The Burning of Gesenthame: Gesenthame was a jeweled city of the Alar Coast, seat of the Azhakid Princes, and it refused the Church of the Pyre's tribute. The Church burned it with dragonfire, and the city died in a night. Its survivors fled into the deep desert and became the Desert Tribes; its ruin stands as the Church's lesson to Serendipity, the city that pays. Church dig-crews work the cinders under writ to this day, searching for something fused beneath the city by the burning. The Azhakid crowns and burning-jewels have never been found."
    },
    "The Empire of Light": {
      "text": "The Empire of Light: The refounded empyrean empire, seated at Caranthoria on the Great Lagoon. Empress Aurianax III raised the Eye of God from the lagoon when she came home, and built a new empire on the drowned bones of the old one — bright, orderly, and fair by its own law, taking citizens of every people and soldiers of every kind. Its legions are bound by the Unity and are the finest heavy infantry in Larion; its causeways and sea-walls have made dry land of open swamp; its justice is consistent and its taxes are not negotiable. Its war runs west into the fens, against the dragon-kingdom of the Reedmoot. What the empire intends for the rest of Larion, the empire does not say."
    },
    "Empress Aurianax III": {
      "text": "Empress Aurianax III: The empyrean who refounded the Empire of Light. Long ago her winged people ruled these coasts from the high air, and their empire drowned in its fall; Aurianax came home to the lagoon where its capital sank, raised the Eye of God from the water, and built anew on the old empire's bones. Her legions name her the Harrower of Ur, and the Harrowers take their name and their charge from hers. She reigns from the Eye and rarely comes down; no one goes up without her invitation, and her word reaches the city through prefects and the legion. Overhead, the work of finishing the Eye never stops."
    },
    "The Eye of God": {
      "text": "The Eye of God: The sky-citadel of gold-bright brass floating above Caranthoria — the throne of the Empire of Light. The Empress raised it from the lagoon where it had lain drowned since the old empire fell, and the work of finishing it never stops overhead. No one goes up without her invitation. What the Eye is — palace, temple, engine — is argued in every port on the coast. Those who have watched it longest say a great lens at its heart is being rebuilt, and old salvagers' tales call the Eye a weapon that once looked down on the whole of the south."
    },
    "The Unity and the Sun-Brand": {
      "text": "The Unity and the Sun-Brand: To swear into the legions of the Empire of Light is to take three things together: citizenship, the sun-brand at the neck, and the Unity — the mind-bond that lets a century fight as one, shields locking without an order spoken, no soldier ever alone in the line. Inside the empire the brand is an honor; outside it is a verdict, for a sun-branded neck is known in every inn in Larion, and a deserter cannot lose himself in an ordinary town. Deserters band together instead, in ruins and wild country where a scarred neck draws no second look, and there are more such bands than the legion admits. The Unity is the legions' strength and the empire's quiet grip: what a soldier gives up to never stand alone is not easily taken back."
    },
    "The Harrowers": {
      "text": "The Harrowers: A small sworn order within the legions of the Empire of Light who hunt the demon-tainted — possession, infernal bargains, the cults of Ur. They take their name from the Empress's own epithet, Harrower of Ur, and their hall stands in the Legion Quarter of Caranthoria. Every Harrower is a legionnaire first: Unity, brand, and the line before the hunt. Outside imperial borders their writ is disputed — the demonborn free-city of New Anthara, which fights Ur in its own way, refuses them entry outright."
    },
    "Nullmetal": {
      "text": "Nullmetal: A dull grey alloy that deadens the mind-arts — thought-reading, mental domination, and the imperial legions' Unity alike go quiet around it. The secret of its working is not public and its trade is not lawful: the Empire of Light pays bounties to keep it off the market, which is itself the best-known proof that it works. The Syndicate moves it anyway, out of the Warrens of Caranthoria, to buyers who would rather not share their minds — and to those who fear the thrallmages of Falmastra, against whom a nullmetal token is the only armor money can buy."
    },
    "Dun Teine Watch": {
      "text": "Dun Teine Watch: The dwarven fortress in the high snows above the Black Fells, holding the one road down into the volcanic country. Its walls are thirty feet of fitted stone and it has never been taken; the Goblinate has reckoned the cost of the attempt and left it alone. The Watch keeps the count — every convoy up the Iron Road, every shipment into the breach, every banner that moves — and what the Watch counts, the free kingdoms hear. It is the last unconquered ground on the peninsula, and the closest thing the war against the Dragon Queen has to a forward post."
    },
    "The Swordlaw": {
      "text": "The Swordlaw: The law of the lawless sea beyond Tevantia's bridges, and the only law Freehaven keeps: disputes are settled steel to steel; a challenge given and accepted binds both parties and their crews; the winner's word stands."
    },
    "The Empyrean Empire": {
      "text": "The Empyrean Empire: The Third Empire — the dominion of the winged empyreans, who ruled land, sea, and sky in the ages before Larion's present kingdoms. Their works outlived them: sky-citadels like the fallen Celestial Citadel of Alar and the Eye of God above Caranthoria, the colossal stone bridges that bind the Swordlaw Isles, brass warships, sea-gates, and the titan-carved stone of the southern archipelago. The citadels flew on djinn-engines — bound spirits of fire driving works of brass — and where one fell hard the land still carries the scar: the Mirror-Waste of the eastern deserts is a crater of black glass where two citadels came down together, and the Soul Engine of Alar still burns with the rage of the djinn sealed inside it. The empire fell ages ago, its citadels dropping from the sky and its cities drowning, and salvagers have lived off its bones for generations without scratching them. The Empire of Light at Caranthoria is its deliberate heir: an empyrean empress building anew on the old crown's foundations."
    },
    "The Kindred War": {
      "text": "The Kindred War: The mythic war the elves fought against themselves in the First Days — sun against moon, kindred against kindred, until the elven homeland itself was lost. The wood elves are descended from those who refused to fight; the war scattered the rest of the kindreds to the corners of Larion. Ruined elven cities from the war still stand in the Everwood, and the enmities it made — sun against moon above all — have never healed."
    },
    "The Pact of Sanctuary": {
      "text": "The Pact of Sanctuary: The agreement that lets the city of Sanctuary stand in the deep Everwood — the city's founders raised the walls, the high elves wove the wards, and neither stands without the other. It is the only such bargain the wood's masters have ever made with outsiders, and the forest's other powers were no party to it: the renegade elves want the enclave gone, and Yslaine's wood keeps its own law at the city's edge. The Pact holds because both sides need it, and everyone in Sanctuary knows what it means the day one side decides otherwise."
    },
    "Cold Iron": {
      "text": "Cold Iron: Forest iron from veins the Everwood's roots have grown down through, found only beneath Sanctuary Vale. It must be mined and worked cold, never touched by forge-fire, or the wild burns out of it and it is only iron. Worked cold, its edge deals arcane force and bites the fey and the magical as plain steel cannot — its touch is anathema to the fey."
    },
    "The Wildkeepers": {
      "text": "The Wildkeepers: A fellowship of druids, rangers, and priests seated at the great hollow oak of Velantami in the Everwood, sworn that the wild must never be owned or tamed — only kept free and alive in its natural balance. A Wildkeeper is the enemy of whatever breaks that balance: the poacher and grove-feller who strip the wood for gain, the blight and the foul spell that corrupt its cycle, and above all the warlord who would burn the forest. They answer to no king, no court, and no written law. They keep the wood, not Sanctuary — they suffer the walled city because a city at peace harms the wild less than a war would."
    },
    "Wildbond": {
      "text": "Wildbond, wildbonding, and wildbonded: A wildbond is a deliberate, mutual relationship between a person and a thinking wild creature who chooses to work with them. It is not ownership, a trained animal's obedience, or an automatic reward for kindness. A person who seeks a wildbond ordinarily begins by asking an oracle, dream-reader, shrine, elder, or other trusted guide for a vision, omen, or direction toward a creature and place where a bond might be possible. They follow the sign into the living world, find the place, meet the creature on its own ground, and face a meaningful test of patience, courage, respect, judgment, or shared danger. The creature may refuse, test, leave, or choose another person. Food, rescue, a friendly greeting, a shared patrol, or a creature's curiosity does not by itself create a wildbond. If both continue to choose one another through the encounter, the relationship becomes a wildbond: the person is wildbonded to the creature, and the creature is wildbonded to the person. Wildbonded beings remain independent thinking actors with their own wants, boundaries, and right to refuse. The bond supports trust, friendship, loyalty, fieldwork, rescue, scouting, protection, and chosen family. Distance may test a wildbond but does not dissolve it; deliberate betrayal, coercion, or broken trust can strain or end it."
    },
    "Yslaine": {
      "text": "Yslaine: The dryad-queen of the old wood, older than the Pact of Sanctuary and no party to it. Her demesne west of Sanctuary keeps the wood's own law: no outsider enters, and the forest itself turns back those who try. The girls left or seized at her demesne's edge she takes and makes into dryads — by the wood's law a gift, by the city's a stolen child."
    },
    "The Sundering": {
      "text": "The Sundering: The settlement that ended the war between the high elves and the dark elves within Elfwood: the forest surface to the high elves, the Rootdark beneath it to the dark elves, neither to cross the other's realm on pain of war. It does not forbid dark elves from traveling on the surface beyond Elfwood. The line has held for an age. It has one standing crossing within the forest — Ilunethel, the trade-town at the open way between the surface and the dark-elf realm below, where deep-goods are sold in the open and the high elves' law does not reach. The high elves call the Sundering justice; the dark elves' monuments tell the Betrayal their own way; and both watch the line fray from their own side of it."
    },
    "The Wood Elves": {
      "text": "The Wood Elves: The elves who refused the Kindred War, following Talvaren, He Who Walks Beside, who guided those who would not fight to safety and gave his life defending them. They hold to neither Canopy nor the deep, keep the old paths of the Everwood, and mourn what the other kindreds broke. 'May you walk in beauty' — Talvaren's last words — still marks their births, arrivals, and deaths. They are hunters, wanderers, and keepers of the forest's oldest memory, and both high and dark elves quietly defer to them in matters neither will admit to."
    },
    "Moot Law": {
      "text": "Moot Law: The law of the Wild Grass Sea, older than any of its peoples' quarrels: no kings, no thrones, and no blood spilled past the boundary stones of Kingstor. The beastfolk buried their tyrant alive — the sorcerer-emperor who enslaved them all with domination magic sleeps under guard in the Kingsgrave Kurgan — and built their law around never digging him up. Effigies called kings are built of straw and burned at the Great Moot so nobody forgets. A breach of the boundary-peace is answered by every species present at once, which is why it holds."
    },
    "House Tessaro": {
      "text": "House Tessaro: One of the two great hunting houses of Coriva on the Bay of Serpents. Tessaro kills: harpoon crews who put down the monsters that threaten the shipping lanes, radiant-armed and proud of it. The house's law is older than its wealth — a Tessaro crew is never abandoned, whatever it costs, and the house has beggared itself twice proving it. They sponsor hunters with the nerve for the biggest quarry and bury their dead with the harpoon that failed them."
    },
    "House Cavarin": {
      "text": "House Cavarin: One of the two great hunting houses of Coriva on the Bay of Serpents. Cavarin captures: beast-wrights who take monsters alive and bind them into the bay's work — harbor-beasts, draft-serpents, arena stock, and stranger commissions. They sponsor handlers with the nerve for live work, and their compounds are the only place in Larion where a sea-monster has a stall, a keeper, and a feeding schedule."
    },
    "The Law of the Bay": {
      "text": "The Law of the Bay: The unwritten law of the Bay of Serpents, kept by hunter and merfolk alike: do not hunt herded waters, do not kill young, do not hunt what speaks, and do not bait lived-in water. The law is why the bay's people and its seafolk neighbors have shared the richest monster-grounds in Larion for generations without war. Breaking it voids a hunter's standing with the houses, the League, and the merfolk at once — and crews that break it tend to take southern salvage charters and not come back."
    },
    "The Mother of Serpents": {
      "text": "The Mother of Serpents: Sailors of the southern coasts swear there is a serpent in the Bay of Serpents bigger than anything the hunting houses have ever put a harpoon in — pearl-white, old beyond any record, big enough to be mistaken for a reef or a line of swells. The stories call her the Mother of Serpents and say the bay's serpent-broods are all hers. Some tales tell of a sea-cave that holds the bow of the Serpentsbane, a hunting ship that went looking for her and never came home. No captain claims to have killed her, and old hands go quiet when the water goes calm and something long passes under the hull."
    },
    "The Sun Kings": {
      "text": "The Sun Kings: The Sun Kings were empyreans — winged, ageless god-kings who ruled the eastern deserts as a province of the Third Empire. When that empire fell, the Sun Kings fell with it, and their priesthoods entombed them in the seven pyramids of the Dunelands with full divine honors, expecting them to rise."
    },
    "The Order of the Desert Rose": {
      "text": "The Order of the Desert Rose: The demon-hunter institution of Larion, seated at the Citadel of the Pact in Redrock. The Order binds and banishes rather than executes: a demon slain on Larion's soil finds its way home eventually, but a demon bound in desert-rose stone or sent through the Gate of Gaol is dealt with. The career runs recruit, taught, sworn hunter, and the Order takes any with the discipline for it. Its hunters range wherever the infernal leaks through."
    },
    "Desert-Rose Stone": {
      "text": "Desert-Rose Stone: A pale rose-colored crystal that grows in rosettes in the deserts of Redrock and wards against demons. Bindings hold in it: a demon sealed in desert-rose stone stays sealed. The Order of the Desert Rose builds with it and works it — the Citadel of the Pact stands on a crag of it — and demon-hunters across Larion carry cut rosettes as wards and binding-stones."
    },
    "Heliaxes": {
      "text": "Heliaxes: The sun-dragon who hallowed the high peaks of Redrock an age ago and departed without explanation. No dragon, demon, or dark thing has laired in those heights since — the empty sky above Cloudtop is his lingering gift, and the city's forges are said to burn pure because of it."
    },
    "Suleyvan the Sealer": {
      "text": "Suleyvan the Sealer: The legendary master-binder of the old ages, whose seals hold to this day. He made the Pact of All Planes that bars the hostile powers of other worlds from Larion; he bound the arch-djinn that wards the deeps of Fire Mountain; and he kept the dangerous spirits of his own age sealed in vessels beneath his own roof. That roof is now Suleyvan's Folly, the ruined palace-city in Redrock's sands, still full of his sealed lamps, rings, jars, and bottles — and of people bold or stupid enough to open them."
    },
    "The Flame Princes": {
      "text": "The Flame Princes: The djinnborn princes of Naran-Kadish, the jeweled city of the old desert, who ruled with fire in their blood until their great banishment rite backfired and burned the city around them. What the rite tore open beneath Naran-Kadish never fully closed; the ruins are gnoll-held now, and the seam below still leaks."
    },
    "The Vampire Lords of Shadow Summits": {
      "text": "The Vampire Lords of Shadow Summits: The lords of Shadow Summits are moon elf vampires — a few dozen ancient and powerful individuals who each hold a valley from a fortified estate. They feed on the people they rule, taking a fixed levy of blood each year, drawn by lot at each dark of the moon, and defend their valleys in return. Each lord rules alone and answers to no one above him; the Crimson Company serves the lords together as soldiery and enforcers. They take in the outcasts of Larion and use them. The Lady of Blood is their ancestor, and the blood price they pay is the one she set."
    },
    "Galard": {
      "text": "Galard: The god of courage against the dark, worshipped wherever the dead rise. Galard's gift is the courage to stand — over the hearth, over the grave, over the friend who did not come back — and his faith blesses steel, moonstone, and the people who carry them into the dark. His worship is split between two estranged orders. In Whitemoor, the Knights Vigilant follow Galard the Swordbearer: put the dead down by force, in the open, hunting them wherever they rise. In Drusavia, the Templars Adamant follow Galard the Shieldbearer: raise the wall, purge the dead, and seal the breach, refusing all traffic with shades. Each order holds the other's way to be a corruption of the gift, and the quarrel is old enough to have its own liturgy. Older than their schism is the Proving Year: an aspirant carries a plain iron Road-Shield into the wider world, earns recognition for three distinct deeds of courage, and only then decides whether to seek one oath, the other, or neither. Both orders and Galard's independent shrines recognize the custom; an aspirant belongs to neither order until an oath is freely sworn."
    },
    "The Manawell": {
      "text": "The Manawell: Magic seeps into the world thinly everywhere; beneath Jade Tarn it wells. The Manawell is a rift into magic-rich reality deep below the tarn, and its erratic upwelling soaks the whole region — spells come easier, reagents grow richer, magical creatures congregate, and the raw stuff condenses into living things. The flow surges and lulls, and when it surges the region's magical weather turns: mana-storms, hauntings, and surges that Tarnholme Academy exists to answer. Where a surge collapses, it can leave a spellform — a spell crystallized into a thing, castable once more by whoever claims it."
    },
    "Vashqara, the Unbound City": {
      "text": "Vashqara, the Unbound City: A city of free djinn, somewhere under the deep desert — so the Desert Tribes' oldest songs say, and the Tribes do not sing idly. No road leads there and no map marks it; the Tribes vouch the rarest of their trusted onward to guides who do not explain themselves. Scholars argue whether Vashqara is real, a memory of something drowned in the Third Empire's fall, or a hope the desert tells itself."
    },
    "The Grell": {
      "text": "The Grell: The kobold clans of the Underworld beneath Silvervale, who claim descent from a mighty kobold empire — the Grell — that they say raised the colossal ruins they den in. The ruins are the buried cities of the Fourth Empire, and everyone above ground knows it, but the clans hold the claim with complete conviction: their chiefs style themselves emperors, they treat the old imperial halls as a stolen inheritance to be reclaimed, and they cannot read the inscriptions that disprove them. They press the claim hardest at the warm sunken pleasure quarter at the heart of Grell Hollows. The clans default to raiding and squatting, and they are cowardly, bribeable, and prone to negotiation when they are losing."
    },
    "Big Buzzer": {
      "text": "Big Buzzer: A Fourth Empire ground-vibration artifact recovered by kobolds in the Boring Hall of the Imperial Arsenal. It emits directional pulses through soil and stone. The pulses attract and irritate stone-boring beetles, drawing them toward a chosen surface point and making them violently destructive, but Big Buzzer does not control, command, ride, or guide the beetles. Its signs are tremors, vibrating water, frightened livestock, fleeing burrowers, abandoned birds, and a low grinding that can be felt before it can be heard. The kobolds gave the artifact its name because they do not know what the Fourth Empire built it to do."
    },
    "Horn of the First Host": {
      "text": "Horn of the First Host: Kragga's name for a Fourth Empire war horn recovered in the Imperial Arsenal. Its surviving enchantment reaches old imperial signal passages and calls bound war-shriekers through them. The long serpentine fiends repeat short orders in Kragga's voice, carrying commands across the Grell Hollows and allowing separate kobold hosts to act in concert. The horn carries commands; it is distinct from Big Buzzer, which attracts and irritates stone-boring beetles."
    },
    "War-shriekers": {
      "text": "War-shriekers: Long, serpentine fiends bound to old imperial signal passages and called by the Horn of the First Host. They are not banshees, ghosts, or plant shriekers. A war-shrieker can emerge physically from a passage, repeat a short order in Kragga's voice, constrict or strike a target, and attack with a psychic or sonic shriek. They can be intercepted in Grell passages, and killing one does not silence the whole network."
    },
    "The Imps of the Basement": {
      "text": "The Imps of the Basement: The imp-kin of the Basement, the buried market beneath Ardan's Watch — a settled breed of small horned demon-kin descended from the bound servant-castes of the Fourth Empire, left masterless when the Princes of Ur were cast down. Ages out from under the whip, they are mostly harmless and relentlessly mercantile: touts, money-changers, lamplighters, and dealers in oddments, native to the lantern-strung dark and prosperous in it."
    },
    "The Fall of Stonehome": {
      "text": "The Fall of Stonehome: Stonehome was the shared capital of the dwarven kingdom of the Whiteholds, raised above the sealed workings of the elder dwarves. One night within living memory the old seals failed and the city fell whole into the dark — streets, halls, throne, and everyone in them. The kingdom broke with it: Clan Deepfire had driven the great delve that ran toward the sealed dark, Clan Icerune's wards failed on the one night that mattered, and each clan holds the other guilty. The crater where the capital stood is now Deepgate, a delver boomtown drawing crews from across Larion, and the fallen city below is the door to the Darek Deep. The throne of the Whiteholds is still down there, and whoever reclaims it reclaims a crown neither clan will yield to the other."
    },
    "The Northrealm": {
      "text": "The Northrealm: The human kingdom that ruled Aurora in the warm age — a seafaring people of harbors, holdfasts, and hot-spring towns, with its capital at Northport. Then the cold came, seasonless and without explanation, and the kingdom died stead by stead and city by city until the sea froze around its fleet at anchor. No one has ever settled why. The snowlanders of the lake country are the Northrealm's remnant — the same people, humbled, holding the few volcanic warm-pockets the cold cannot reach — and the ruins of everything else their forebears built lie out in the white, where the old kingdom's dead keep some of them still."
    },
    "Hrimnara, the Widow of Winter": {
      "text": "Hrimnara, the Widow of Winter: The goddess of the cold of the far north — so the snowlanders hold, and they do not say her name loudly indoors. Everything outside the door is hers: the cold, the night, the white, and whatever dies in it. What the Widow takes may walk again, which is why the north burns its dead. When the aurora stands across the sky, the snowlanders say the Widow is walking, and no one travels under it. No shrine is raised to her and nothing is asked of her; the only worship she receives is the Long Night's, and the snowlanders count that the worst thing a soul can do."
    },
    "Sand-Wyrm": {
      "text": "Sand-Wyrm: Great burrowing worms of the eastern deserts, large enough to take a laden wagon and its team in a single strike. They feel movement through open sand and come up beneath it, and they cannot reach onto rock, so desert crews cross wyrm country rise to rise and reckon distance in runs. Old desert songs say the Sun Kings drove the wyrms as beasts of war and burden in the age of their rule, and that the secret of the driving was entombed with them. No one alive knows how it was done, and no one has lived to learn by trying."
    },
    "Shadow Inquisition": {
      "text": "Shadow Inquisition: The ghost-hunter and occult-investigation institution seated at the Black Tower in Drusavia. Initiates bear the Mark of Rhadan and prove themselves through one supervised case. Qualified Initiates may undergo the Rite of Bonding at the Pit of Shadow, gain a Shade, and rise as full Inquisitors trusted with hauntings, undead, cults, Shadelands exposure, and threats ordinary law cannot understand."
    },
    "The Grand Arena Circuit": {
      "text": "The Grand Arena Circuit: Larion's great arenas answer to different rulers, Houses, and customs, but their heralds and promoters recognize one another's champions. The eligible venues are the Tournament Grounds at the Field of Valor, Tevantia's Amphitheater, the Red Sands of Oasis, Gavarra's Gilded Cage, Caranthoria's Solarian Arena, the Pit at Gorren Crossing, Coriva's Water Arena, and the Open Grounds at Fallsmeet. Each awards its own championship according to its own rules. A fighter who wins recognized championships at three different circuit arenas becomes eligible for the Grand Champion's Contest, held in the Champions' Ring at the Field of Valor. Winning an ordinary bout or local exhibition does not count: the fighter must defeat the venue's acknowledged title-holder or win its formally declared championship match. The victor at the Champions' Ring is named Grand Champion of the Arenas, a title recognized by crowds, promoters, patrons, and fighting schools throughout Larion. Grand Champions command the largest purses, may headline at any circuit venue, and are commonly invited to choose challengers, train a stable, or sponsor fighters of their own."
    },
    "The Sky Temple of the Ascension": {
      "text": "The Sky Temple of the Ascension: A cloud-high monastery on the Unmoored Mountain, a whole red-rock peak lifted from the earth by the First Adept when the demons came. The Temple teaches that the mind can govern the body's attunement to Larion's physical world: a practitioner may detune and become lighter, faster, and able to cross walls or water, or attune more heavily and anchor, strike, and land with immense force. Wind elementals called the Catching Winds keep the peak steady and catch people whose traversal accidents would otherwise kill them, though they do not spare anyone embarrassment. The Temple's ordinary life is communal, joyful, and practical — loud meals, strong drink, restorative potions, movement gear, difficult training, laughter, mutual correction, and compassion. The elite practitioners who master the deeper discipline are the Ascendants, recognized through a series of Descents: ordeals in the wider world where they use their strength to confront danger or protect others."
    },
    "The Gravebound": {
      "text": "The Gravebound: A corrupted offshoot of bloodcraft that works through graves, bound dead, and stolen life. Gravebound cells defile old burial customs, raise corpses as labor and guard, and promise frightened people a stronger answer than the old rites. The Bloodhunters of the Red Chantry hunt them as enemies. The Gravebound is not the Path of Blood and should never be treated as the ordinary name for disciplined Bloodhunter practice."
    },
    "Plate and the Channel": {
      "text": "Plate and the Channel:\n\nMagic is drawn through the body, and metal worn close interrupts the draw. Practitioners describe it as reaching through a wall rather than a door: the current is still there, but it arrives late and thin, and holding it costs more than it should. Mail and plate are worst, and the more of the body they cover the worse it runs. Leather, cloth, and bone barely register. This is why temple guards who must both fight and bless wear scale over the ribs and nothing over the arms, why battle-casters pay fortunes for warded mail, and why a wizard in a breastplate is a joke told in every academy. Ritual can be worked around it given time and preparation. A spell thrown in the middle of a fight cannot."
    },
    "Vessels and Foci": {
      "text": "Vessels and Foci:\n\nA focus is not a decoration and not a battery. It is a shaped path: a staff, wand, reliquary, sealstone, or seedpod cut and set so that power moves through it the way water moves down a channel rather than across a field. A caster with a good focus is not stronger; they are cleaner, and cleanliness under pressure is most of what separates a practitioner from a casualty. Materials matter more than ornament. Witchwood and aether crystal carry arcane force with the least loss, which is why so many first wands are witchwood with a splinter of something better set into the tip. Robes matter for the same reason armour does, in reverse: nothing between the body and the air."
    },
    "Cursed Arms and Bargained Things": {
      "text": "Cursed Arms and Bargained Things:\n\nCursed arms and regalia contain workings that continue to want something after their makers are gone. Most surviving pieces come from bargains with Ur, Shadelands ruins, or sacred workings broken in use.\n\nA true cursed item has a repeatable physical tell even while quiet: a weak pulse in its grip, water gathering where nothing is wet, an edge that stains clean cloth, frost on the bearer's breath, or another sign particular to the item. Calling its named power produces an immediate and unmistakable manifestation. Every deliberate calling also leaves Tarnish: spiritual grey-rot, the mark that the dead country and its bargains leave on the living.\n\nBinders recognize curses by what can be staged and witnessed. A hungry item names what feeds it and visibly changes when fed. A sworn item serves a named person, house, god, or purpose and gives a repeatable sign of approval or rejection. A cycle-bound item changes at a named tide, moon, bell, season, or other recurring moment. A flesh-taking item leaves an observable mark or weakness on the body carrying it.\n\nSuch things turn up in confiscated cult stores, sealed armories, tombs, Shadelands caches, and among the effects of people who believed they could master them. Their power is why they are kept instead of forgotten."
    },
    "The Widow's Edge": {
      "text": "The Widow's Edge:\n\nA caravan guard recovered this grey Warded sword from an Ur-carved strongbox beneath a burned wreck on Widow's Way. Since then it has passed between road captains, bandit chiefs, and collectors, usually after its bearer dies suddenly. Its grip keeps the bearer's pulse weak and slow. Its named power, Widow's Due, sends a thrust through the wielder's shadow and out of a visible foe's shadow; each calling knots black thread around the wrist and leaves the wielder's shadow visibly thinner."
    },
    "Crown of the Drowned King": {
      "text": "Crown of the Drowned King:\n\nThe Crown of the Drowned King is the verdigrised sun-gold circlet buried with the last ruler of the flooded Sun-King tomb in the Dunelands. Its named power, The Drowned Court, floods the immediate scene with grave-cold water and calls the king's drowned honor-guard to strike before sinking away. Each calling leaves one drowned petitioner visible in the nearest reflection. The petitioner knows only its own death and claim: it may petition, accuse, or demand judgment, but it is not an oracle."
    },
    "Wolfskin Girdle": {
      "text": "Wolfskin Girdle:\n\nA Wrath-Speaker made the Wolfskin Girdle by trapping a wolf-spirit in its own skin, a violation of the Wolfpack's mutual beast-bond tradition. Its named power, The Old Hunger, lets the bearer wear the spirit as a towering wolf for one violent turn. The iron clasp bites shut when called, and afterward the empty hide strains toward the nearest wounded creature even when its bearer refuses to follow. Wolfpack Lodge seeks the girdle as evidence of the Wrath-Speakers' crime and may charge Proven Hunters with recovering or destroying it."
    },
    "Marks of Quality": {
      "text": "Marks of Quality:\n\nLarion's makers distinguish four grades of work. Ordinary goods carry no name but their maker. Fine work is what a good shop sells and a careful person saves for. Superior work carries a mark: a named smith, an aspected material, or a minor working laid in during its making. Masterwork is commissioned rather than stocked. It requires exceptional material and master-level craft, and a maker may complete only a handful of such pieces in a lifetime.\n\nA Warded weapon begins as a Masterwork and is carried through a separate enchanter's rite using a rare catalyst. The original maker's mark remains beside the enchanter's ward.\n\nA Relic is not a grade of workmanship. It bears a proper name and a history people can recount. No shop or smith can promise to make one on demand; a weapon becomes a Relic only through a great undertaking and the rite that binds that history into it.\n\nClaiming a grade or mark one cannot support is serious fraud. Guild towns punish forgers of maker's marks alongside forgers of coin."
    },
    "Restoratives": {
      "text": "Restoratives:\n\nThe trade distinguishes three families and will correct you if you confuse them. Healing potions close wounds and are the only one that mends real harm. Stamina draughts are salt-bitter and push breath and strength back into tired limbs; a greater draught is the same brew reduced. Mana philters run cold and reopen the channel between a caster and the current; they do nothing at all for someone who cannot cast, which does not stop them being sold to such people. All three buy a moment rather than a recovery, and all three make the next few heartbeats harder to think through -- which is why a fighter drinks before closing, not during."
    },
    "The Enchanter's Trade": {
      "text": "The Enchanter's Trade:\n\nEnchanting is a craft, not a school. It is practised at a bench with tools, notes, and failed attempts in a box under it, and it rewards the patient and the literate rather than the gifted. The working is laid into the object while it is made or reworked, which is why enchanters are trained beside smiths, jewelers, and binders rather than beside battle-casters. A strong channeller with no craft can no more enchant a blade than a strong arm can forge one. This is also why the great workshops -- the College of Shapers' Wonderworks, Forge-town's rune-benches -- are institutions of record and apprenticeship, and why a maker's mark on an enchanted piece is worth more than the enchantment.\n\nA Warded weapon begins with a completed Masterwork. The enchanter binds permanent power through it with a rare catalyst and a full rite; no amount of casual spellcasting can replace either. Such work is arranged through reputation, obligation, or shared purpose, not selected from a shop list."
    },
    "The Worth of Goods": {
      "text": "The Worth of Goods:\n\nPRICING AND STOCK REFERENCE. This exists so that prices, shop stock, and the worth of loot stay consistent from scene to scene. Apply it silently when someone shops, sells, loots, salvages, appraises, haggles, or asks what a thing is worth. No character knows it as written. Do not recite it, quote it, explain it as lore, or let it emerge through play: a merchant simply names a price that agrees with it.\n\nCoin. Larion counts in florins. A working person earns a few florins a day. A new adventurer leaves home with about sixty, which buys a decent weapon, or many nights of good lodging, or one small magical curiosity, but not all three.\n\nBuying from a shop:\n- Everyday clothing, tools, rope, and ammunition: 1 to 10 florins.\n- Ordinary weapons and armour, unmarked and unaspected: 8 to 25 florins. A shortsword, a leather jerkin, an iron helm.\n- Potions and restoratives: 15 to 40 florins, the greater draughts and philters at the top of that range.\n- Protective ward charms: 120 to 200 florins. They are rite-made, scarce, and rarely stocked more than one at a time.\n- Fine work, the best an ordinary shop keeps in stock: 40 to 90 florins.\n- Superior work, carrying a named maker's mark, an aspected material, or a small working: 150 to 400 florins. Most shops hold one or two at a time and some of those are not for sale.\n- Masterwork: commissioned rather than stocked, 800 florins and upward, with time to make and exceptional materials often supplied by the buyer.\n- Warded weapons: not ordinary merchandise. Their price includes the Masterwork beneath them, a rare catalyst, and the service or favour required to secure an enchanter's rite.\n- Relics: not priced and not sold. Such things change hands through inheritance, theft, oath, gift, conquest, or recovery from the dead.\n\nServices use the same register: inspection or identification 10 to 40 florins, difficult diagnosis or investigation 50 to 80, custom or commissioned work 80 and upward.\n\nSelling. A merchant pays a third to a half of what they would charge for the same thing, less for stock they cannot move, more for something they already have a buyer for. Battlefield salvage, bloodied gear, and obviously stolen goods fetch the low end or a flat refusal. Haggling moves a price by about a fifth either way; a good roll changes the terms, never the economy.\n\nWhat shops carry. Most hold ordinary goods and a little Fine work. Superior appears where there is a reason for it: a smith with a name, a focus-maker beside an academy, a fence with quiet connections. No ordinary shop stocks Masterwork or Warded weapons, and Relics do not appear as merchandise. A merchant claiming otherwise is lying, fencing something dangerous, or selling a forgery.\n\nWhat the price is for. A buyer pays for the bonus, not the ornament: armour, damage, an attribute, a resource, a skill. Two pieces of the same grade cost roughly the same whatever they look like. An aspected material such as emberglass, stormsteel, moonstone, or voidglass adds about half again over plain work, because what it carries is worth real coin against the right enemy."
    },
    "The Hunger": {
      "text": "The Hunger: A contagion the Demon-Princes of Ur unleashed in the age they ruled Larion — a weapon that takes a thinking person and leaves an appetite. Where it takes hold unmastered it makes the mindless: strigoi in the blood-dead, ghouls where the dead lie thick, and lycanthropes in those who swallow it willingly. The vampire lords of the Shadow Summits hold that their own curse is the Hunger mastered — that the Lady of Blood, cornered in the Elven Schism, consumed her followers not for power alone but to take the thing that was already eating her people, and came out of it holding the one form that keeps its mind. By their account the Hunger wells up wherever the barrier between worlds runs thin, which is why their valleys sit where they sit, and why they say the north would go feral without them and Larion after it. It is their account, and it is largely true. They also made the Vale Sable."
    },
    "The Blood of Two to Make One": {
      "text": "The Blood of Two to Make One: The rite by which the vampire lords of the Shadow Summits make new members of their line, and the reason they marry. A vampire is not made by one sire but by two, of different houses, and the saying is the whole of the law. What rises from two carries something of both and carries it stably. What rises from a single sire carries only what its maker had, and carries it badly: such a get is far likelier to lose its mind to the Hunger, sooner or later, and go feral. Marriage between houses exists to make the rite possible and to keep the lords from drawing apart across the centuries — they need one another, and the rite is what makes that true rather than sentimental. A lord who will not marry cannot safely make an heir. The Lady of Blood was made by nobody and made herself alone, which the Peers note and do not discuss. What became of the Vale Sable is what the rule is for."
    },
    "Frenzy": {
      "text": "Frenzy: A contagion of the Hunger that takes beasts. A frenzied animal behaves like a rabid one — fearless, relentless, past any instinct for self-preservation — except that its hunger is insatiable, so it eats long past full and goes on eating, and it has stopped being able to die properly. Wounds that should drop it do not; it comes apart before it stops. The carcass is still the problem: whatever scavenges a frenzied body takes the frenzy in turn, which is how one dead wolf becomes three by the following week. The corpse has to be destroyed, and in this country that means burned, which is a real cost where every stick of fuel is carried uphill. Silver and moonstone give no particular advantage, because a frenzied beast is alive, whatever else is wrong with it. People who eat the meat sicken and die; they do not turn. Frenzy is not the Ferals' curse, which is taken willingly and keeps its mind."
    },
    "Bloodfed Direhound": {
      "text": "Bloodfed Direhound: The hunting hounds of the vampire houses, bred large and fed on their masters' blood. Heavy, deep-chested, short pale coats and eyes that hold the light too long. A pack runs a mortal down over days without resting and takes wounds that should stop it, and it hunts by scent far past the point where the trail should be cold. A house's hounds are that house's property: killing them is an act against the house, and every lord knows the mark of another's kennel. Most packs met in open country have gone feral — turned loose, escaped, or left when their house fell — and those belong to nobody, breed, and are worse for it, because a fed hound that has had no blood in a year is a starving thing that still does not die easily."
    },
    "Bonebreaker Vulture": {
      "text": "Bonebreaker Vulture: An enormous carrion bird of the high peaks, named for what it does — it carries bones aloft and drops them on bare rock to crack them for the marrow, and it will do the same with a body, or with a living thing small enough to lift. Slate and rust coloured, a wingspan wider than a man is tall, and in a country with this much dead in it they grow fat and bold. They are a sign before they are a danger: where bonebreakers circle there is a body, and where they circle over open ground and nothing can be seen, something has been buried, hidden, or dragged under."
    },
    "Pale Lynx": {
      "text": "Pale Lynx: The ambush predator of the slopes that never see the sun. Chalk-white, heavy through the shoulders, with skin grown over where the eyes should be — it does not see at all. It hunts warmth, and it is very good at it: it will take a traveler off a path in full dark from above, having tracked the heat of them for an hour. Because it hunts warmth it cannot perceive the undead in any way. A vampire may stand within arm's reach of one and be no more present to it than a stone, which is why no lord of the Shadow Summits has ever troubled to clear them, and why they are a mortal's problem and nobody else's."
    },
    "Gaunt Fiend": {
      "text": "Gaunt Fiend: The common fiend of the Hunger, and the one that comes in numbers. Starved to the frame, taller than a man and wrongly jointed, all reach and ribs and a stomach that never fills. Gaunts come through where the barrier is worn and go straight for the nearest warm thing, fast and without caution, climbing walls and dropping from ceilings as readily as running. They do not speak, they coordinate only loosely, and they keep coming while anything is left to eat. Radiant force, silver, and moonstone bite them; ordinary steel works, slowly."
    },
    "Maw Fiend": {
      "text": "Maw Fiend: The heavy among the fiends of the Hunger — mostly mouth, walking on its knuckles, a body built around the swallowing. A maw takes a whole person and keeps moving. Slow to start and very hard to stop, it wades into a formed line and bites through shields, and what it swallows does not come back. Maws come through the thin places behind the gaunts, and they are usually what a breach’s defenders die to. Radiant force, silver, and moonstone bite them; they shrug off most of what does not."
    },
    "Ravener Fiend": {
      "text": "Ravener Fiend: The greater fiend of the Hunger, and the only one that talks. A ravener keeps enough mind to bargain, to name what it wants, and to understand what it is being offered — and nowhere near enough to keep a bargain once the hunger turns. They are individually named, they hold a breach or a ruin as territory, and lesser fiends give way to them. A ravener will parley, the parley is worth having, and it ends the moment the ravener is hungrier than it is curious. Radiant force, silver, and moonstone bite them."
    },
    "The Crimson Company": {
      "text": "The Crimson Company: The standing soldiery of the vampire lords of the Shadow Summits, serving the lords collectively rather than any single house and hired out valley to valley under a red sword on black. The Company holds cordons, culls strigoi and ghouls, escorts what the lords trade, and keeps order in the lit valleys. It takes anyone the rest of Larion has cast out, so long as they can fight and keep a bargain, and it houses and pays them well. Its officers came north from Silvervale after a failed coup. Its elite are occasionally Embraced, which is the Company’s advertised prize and one of the ways the Summits make new vampires — by merit, and by who will fight the most for it."
    },
    "Clerical Ministry and Reliquaries": {
      "text": "Clerical Ministry and Reliquaries:\n\nA cleric's standing grows through service to their faith and to the living, the dead, and the sacred places in its care. Every tradition asks different things, but clerics are often called to tend the sick, bury the dead, confront hauntings and possession, lay restless undead to rest, break curses, banish fiends, and cleanse places where corruption has taken root.\n\nThe lasting reward for such service may be trust. A temple or community may open a sanctuary to the cleric, teach a rite, lend its aid, or entrust the cleric with a sacred object. A rite may also survive in an inscription, prayer book, funerary tablet, hymn, or object recovered during play.\n\nA reliquary is a sacred focus that houses a holy object or preserves the memory of a recognized holy event. It may be a pendant, seal, casket, vessel, wrapped fragment, prayer wheel, or another form belonging to the faith. An object taken from a defeated enemy or cursed place is not thereby sacred or safe; it may need to be identified, cleansed, returned, or properly housed before it can serve the faith. A holy symbol may become a reliquary when a sacred object is joined to it."
    },
    "Monastic Forms and Ordeals": {
      "text": "Monastic Forms and Ordeals:\n\nMonks teach that the body has more answers than habit permits. A Form is one impossible bodily command perfected until it can be repeated: becoming light or immovable, hard or supple, aware without sight, exact beyond ordinary timing, able to endure, or swift to mend. Different orders use different names and methods.\n\nA Form may be carried in a teacher's demonstration, a mantra, a sequence painted on a wall, a carved tablet, a scroll of footwork, or another teaching aid. Finding or buying the teaching is not mastery. The student must practice it and then perform its command during an ordeal where failure matters. A first-time breakthrough may also become a Form when no teacher had named it.\n\nThe Sky Temple of the Ascension calls its weight-and-force discipline attuning and detuning. It calls its ordeals Descents because its students leave the Unmoored Mountain to face danger in the wider world. Other traditions use their own terms and tests. The Path of the Will is a common measure of how deeply a practitioner can carry a Form: novices begin with one command of their own body; adepts may join commands or carry one through a held object; experts sustain deeper commands through extreme harm or opposition; masters may combine several commands or reach another living body by touch. Teachers and rivals recognize demonstrated Forms; mastery earns harder instruction, invitations, challenges, hospitality, and eventually the standing to teach or name Forms for others."
    },
    "Wizard Spellcraft": {
      "text": "Wizard Spellcraft:\n\nWizards do not learn a spell merely by seeing it. They study the marks, materials, words, gestures, residues, and failures by which a piece of magic takes shape, then test one principle at a time. A hostile spell, curse, ward, ritual, magical creature, grimoire, or strange phenomenon may yield such a principle when examined, but the wizard must still design and successfully cast a spell of their own.\n\nA spellbook keeps completed techniques, corrections, and the evidence from failed attempts. A discovered grimoire or formula can shorten the road, but it is instruction rather than mastery. Wizards trade and teach proven spells, and may bind a spell they know into prepared vellum as a one-use scroll."
    },
    "Sorcerous Origins and Awakened Powers": {
      "text": "Sorcerous Origins and Awakened Powers:\n\nA sorcerer's origin is why magic lives inside them. It may be an inheritance, curse, old ritual, magical accident, strange birth, or mystery no one has answered. The origin is background truth, not a skill, element, patron, or resource, and it does not decide every spell the sorcerer can cast. Sorcerers use the ordinary magic skill that governs what each spell or power actually does.\n\nInborn magic may acquire a recurring visible tell through the body or nearby world, but only when background or story establishes one. A sorcerer awakens a new power by reaching inward and attempting something their magic has never done before. Outside magic may inspire the attempt, but it is not copied or imported. A fully successful first use can become a power the sorcerer calls again."
    },
    "Gemheart": {
      "text": "Gemheart: What a house makes when it turns Ur soul-work on a person instead of a construct. The soulstone goes where the heart was, the soul goes into the stone, and the body stops changing from that day on — no ageing, no decay, no breath. At rest the stone is dark and cold; it burns from within when its bearer fights. A gemheart is preserved rather than corrupted, which is why they are beautiful, and completely still when not moving, which is what people find hardest to sit beside. Radiant force and blessed weapons bite them, and destroying the body settles nothing: the soul is in the stone, and the stone has to be found and broken. Soulstones are banned across Larion's surface and the making of a gemheart is worse than banned, so the few that exist have nowhere lawful to stand. The houses that made them are mostly gone. Every one of them is somebody's crime, still walking."
    }
  },
  "storyStarts": {
    "Of Hogs and Halflings": {
      "name": "Of Hogs and Halflings",
      "description": "TUTORIAL\n\nIn a cozy halfling village in the forest, the prize truffle-hog has vanished two days before the festival — and there's mischief afoot, because without him the family inn won't see the year out. A gentle introduction. Skip ahead if you want action.",
      "firstQuest": "The first quest is to pick up Phineas's calling-harness from Manu the blacksmith at Manu's Blacksmith Shop AND the truffle-paste from Pernilla Balewood at the Saucy Chicken Tavern. Quest name: Bells and Balewoods. Set arc to NULL — this is a simple fetch, not the start of an arc.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene in Hearthhome, a cozy hill-home in the halfling village of Hillhome. Late morning, kettle on the hearth, sunlight through the round windows. Briefly establish who the players are and how they came to live in Hillhome. They may be from the village, or new to it, or staying with friends. Do not invent any other characters.\n\nThe front door bangs open. Bark Balewood bursts in.\n\n'Phineas is GONE. Disappeared. Vanished. The Newmoon Fair's the day after tomorrow, and now old Goldspoon has announced she's calling in our debt. Coincidence? I think not.' He gulps air. 'Grandma needs you down at the Saucy Chicken — but first she says swing by Manu's and pick up Phineas's calling-harness, the one with the bells. Manu's been mending it. We'll need it to leash him again. Oh, and the truffle-paste, the one he likes, Grandma is just making up a fresh batch. We'll need that to lure him. He'll be out in the truffle-woods somewhere — that pig knows every root and bramble through every path above Hillhome. Phineas is the best truffle-hog in the Downs. If we lose him we lose the Fair, and if we lose the Fair we lose the tavern. WHY ARE YOU STILL SITTING THERE?' End the scene there.",
      "locations": [
        "Hillhome"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Hearthhome"
      ],
      "isDefault": true
    },
    "The League": {
      "name": "The League",
      "firstQuest": "Do not generate a separate quest on turn 0. The opening scene should establish Copper standing, the League Hall, Mona Vashti as the hallmaster and first point of contact, and several possible fellow adventurers without offering specific work; Copper standing is recorded only for the characters Mona admits.",
      "description": "ADVENTURE\n\nJoin the League of Adventurers in the Watchtower of Ardan's Watch, where young blades, spellcasters, scouts, oathkeepers, and monster-hunters gather under one roof. Start as a new Copper, meet the people who make the hall run, find your footing, and decide what kind of company and career you want to build as the roads of Larion open before you.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene in the League Hall of the Watchtower in Ardan's Watch. The PC has just been accepted into the League of Adventurers as a Copper and is receiving their Copper pin. Begin with a brief, concrete description of the bright, busy hall: stained glass of famous adventurers, old trophies, a Silvervale map, tables, scribes, and people coming and going. Do not enumerate jobs, postings, destinations, rewards, or kinds of work. Do not create an emergency, quest, or complication.\n\nIntroduce Mona Vashti as the League Hall's hallmaster and the PC's first point of contact. Establish her by role and by the fact that she is handing over or fastening the Copper pin. Keep her introduction focused on her authority, welcome, and judgment. Do not mention her sword, rapier, The Last Word, or any weapon in the initial narration or dialogue.\n\nIntroduce exactly three or four newly invented young-adult Copper adventurers or near-Coppers for this opening, roughly 18–30 unless the role clearly calls for another age. They must be new characters, not reused, renamed, or lightly reskinned versions of any NPC, starting-party member, premade character, or other character reference supplied in context. Mona is the only established NPC to use as the focal contact; all other adventurers in this opening must be newly made up. Make them dynamic, visually distinctive, competent, and immediately usable as possible allies, rivals, or future companions, each with a distinct personal energy shown through action. Vary species and roles strongly across fighters, scouts, spellcasters, healers, duelists, wardbreakers, hunters, and oathbound types. Use established peoples from this list: elves, dwarves, halflings, gnomes, orcs, goblins, demonborn, dragonkin, giantborn, catkin, ratkin, foxkin, wolfkin, lionkin, boarfolk, monkeyfolk, gajanari, frogkin, serpentkin, and lizardfolk; use no more than one human. Show each person doing something specific, amusing, or engaging that is not discussing work: sparring lightly, repairing gear, testing a harmless cantrip, trading food, sketching a creature, helping a scribe, or making a small wager. Do not have these adventurers discuss, inspect, compare, pitch, or explain jobs, postings, contracts, destinations, or rewards. They do not all need dialogue; use brief actions and only a few lines so the opening stays light. Keep them competent, not foolish or generic comic relief.\n\nHave Mona give the PC the pin and make a brief, memorable remark in her own voice that Copper standing is a beginning and the real work starts now. End with Mona telling the PC that she can help them find the right work when they are ready, introduce them to possible companions, or point them toward useful places in Ardan's Watch. Let her ask what the PC needs first. Do not have her offer a specific contract or summon anyone until the player asks. End there, leaving the player free to respond.\n\nIn multiplayer, identify each new character receiving a Copper pin. Preserve any existing member's higher standing; companions do not gain membership merely by attending.",
      "locations": [
        "Ardan's Watch"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Watchtower - League Hall"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Two of Swords": {
      "name": "Two of Swords",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "ADVENTURE\n\nYour trial hunt as an Initiate of the Order of the Silver Sword. Track the creature, protect the settlement, and prove beneath a veteran's eye that you are ready to return to Castle Harstone, earn your silver, and be admitted as a Silverblade.",
      "storyStart": "Open by concisely describing where the party is in the selected starting location and area, the immediate space around them, and the core scene unfolding there. Show the important people present, including Rook Skander and the locals who can help with the hunt. Use concrete visible details suited to the selected place. Do not mention smells.\n\nState plainly why the player is there: they want to join the Order of the Silver Sword and are undertaking a supervised trial hunt as an Initiate. The player is a candidate, but has not been admitted as a Silverblade, carries no silver blade pin, and has not yet earned an Order-silvered weapon, silver-faced shield, or silver-bound focus or implement. Something has been attacking people or property nearby, and identifying and decisively resolving that creature is the player's trial. Rook is present as a half-elf Veteran Silverblade to observe, advise, and prevent catastrophic failure, but not to lead or solve the hunt. If the player succeeds, Rook can attest that the trial is complete and the player may return to Castle Harstone to tell the hunt before the Great Hall and ask to be admitted as a Silverblade.\n\nContinue the opening scene with concise, useful evidence that fits the selected location and Carendon's wooded, monster-haunted character, but do not identify the creature yet. Rook gives three practical pieces of advice: speak to those who witnessed the attacks or found the damage, examine the kill-site or scene personally, and do not decide what the creature is before the evidence supports it. He makes clear that the player calls the hunt; he will answer questions and stand behind them, but the decisions must be theirs. Introduce two accessible locals with different useful perspectives, such as someone who found the victim or damage and someone who heard or saw part of the attack. Finish the intro turn with the situation clearly established and the player free to choose how to begin the investigation.",
