    "Galard Loves the Bold": {
      "name": "Galard Loves the Bold",
      "description": "ADVENTURE\n\nSwear into the Knights Vigilant, take your first ride against the restless dead, earn a banner of your own, and rise toward acclaim as a Champion Vigilant.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locations": [
        "The Temple of the Sword Vigilant"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Feast-Hall"
      ],
      "firstQuest": "",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene in the Feast-Hall of the Temple of the Sword Vigilant, the fortress-seat above Aldwall, on the evening the player joins the Knights Vigilant. The hall is loud and warm: trestle tables crowded with knights, ale-casks against the wall, banners overhead, and trophies of famous undead kills along the stone. Briefly establish what brought the player here without fixing their personality or motives. Explain that Whitemoor is the broad, wet moorland around Aldwall, marked by drowned roads, barrow-fields, old war-dead, and villages that rely on the Knights Vigilant to keep the restless dead from spreading.\n\nAt Galard's altar, offer the oath of the Sword Vigilant and let the start's chosen premise carry the player through joining without inventing their exact words. On being sworn, they receive a moonstone oath-brooch, the Swordbearer Oath, and the rank New-Sworn. Briefly explain that Advancing Dawn is the exceptional active expression of the oath and works through the player's own weapon, magic, or focus rather than requiring a sword or mount. The hall answers with a roar and hammering on tables. Explain the path briefly through the feast and nearby knights: a New-Sworn completes one first ride under a veteran's eye to become Moor-Knight; a Moor-Knight completes three recognized independent rides to earn a personal banner and become Banner-Knight; a Banner-Knight leads four great recognized rides to be acclaimed Champion Vigilant. Rides are recognized when the knight returns to the Feast-Hall and the deed is established through a true account, witnesses, trophies, or rescued people.\n\nBring the standing hunt-board into immediate reach. Surface three concise first-ride options against the restless dead of Whitemoor, each with a named quarry or manifestation, a specific nearby place, who is threatened, and what decisive resolution means. Introduce two or three competent veteran knights willing to witness a New-Sworn's first ride, each with a distinct style and opinion but none who will take command away from the player. End before any hunt or veteran is chosen. Leave the player free to drink, boast or remain quiet, meet knights, inspect a posting, choose a veteran, visit the forges, or simply take in the Feast-Hall.",
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Win Your First Ride"
      ]
    },
    "Run with the Pack": {
      "name": "Run with the Pack",
      "firstQuest": "Do not generate an emergent quest during the opening. The authored quest Prove Yourself to the Pack handles the Trial-Hunter's proving deed and later Wolfpack advancement.",
      "description": "ADVENTURE\n\nEnter Wolfpack Lodge as a Trial-Hunter, prove yourself through a dangerous deed for the Orc Wolds, earn a place in the pack, and rise toward an earned bond with a beast-spirit.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene in the Great Lodge at Wolfpack Lodge in the Orc Wolds. Omit smells. Clearly describe the place, the immediate scene, and the few characters who matter to the exchange. Make the lodge feel active, capable, and worth joining: a fortified home and meeting hall where hunters and warriors return from difficult work, exchange news, and prepare to go out again. Keep the description focused rather than cataloging every feature or introducing many people.\n\nThe player has come here because they want to join the Wolfpack. Have Pack-Leader Uro Madresh receive them if he is present; if he is absent, use another respected lodge member. Have the receiving character explain the Wolfpack in simple, natural exposition. It is an orc-founded warrior society open to capable people of any ancestry or adventuring style. Its members take on dangerous creatures, rescues, defenses, escorts, raiders, and other threats too serious for ordinary Wold warriors. Do not assume the player is an orc, a Berserker, capable of Fury, or interested in becoming one.\n\nExplain the immediate admission rule clearly. The player is not yet a Packmate. They may enter as a Trial-Hunter and choose one deed that a Packmate will witness. Completing that deed and returning with the witness or convincing proof earns admission to the Wolfpack and the pack clasp. Briefly hint that admission is only the beginning: proven members undertake harder deeds and may eventually face deeper trials, rites, and leadership responsibilities. Do not explain the entire rank ladder or Beast Bond in the opening.\n\nMany suitable proving deeds need doing, but do not list assignments, create a new quest, or introduce prospective witnesses yet. Have the lodge member make clear that the player may ask to hear about the available deeds, ask about the Wolfpack, look around, or leave. End with that open invitation and wait for the player's response.",
      "locations": [
        "Wolfpack Lodge"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Great Lodge"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "First Blood": {
      "name": "First Blood",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "MYSTERY\n\nAs an Initiate of the Black Tower, you have been sent under cover to resolve the case of a fledgling vampire in a country village. Complete this supervised field trial and you may return to the Pit of Shadow for the Rite that makes a full Inquisitor.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene on the Workshop Terraces in Fallsmeet at dusk. The player character is an Initiate of the Black Tower accompanied by their mentor Tarain Rethford, a blood mage and operative. Briefly establish that the Black Tower is Drusavia's respected order for protecting the living from supernatural threats. The player bears the Mark of Rhadan as an accepted member, but is not yet a full Inquisitor, has no Shade, and cannot undertake an unsanctioned Shadelands descent. This is their one qualifying field case: if they decisively resolve it under Tarain's supervision, the Tower may authorize their return to the Pit of Shadow for the Rite of Bonding. Tarain advises and prevents catastrophe, but the Initiate leads. Briefly establish why the player joined the Black Tower without fixing their personality or motives.\n\nWorkshops are closing along the stepped streets as dusk settles, but voices rise near a shuttered workshop. Armed villagers hold a young man while others shout accusations; a woman lies dead nearby, her throat torn. The accused is a Tarth, and the dead woman a Millner; the two households have an obvious rivalry over Games sponsorship. The crowd wants blood itself. Tarain studies the scene, then speaks quietly: 'Fledgling vampire. Sloppy kill, no attempt to hide it. Turned recently, still figuring out what it is.' She pricks her palm with a silver dagger and lets a drop of her blood fall to the stone, then closes her eyes. 'The boy's heart is racing. Scared out of his wits. But beating.' She glances at the gathered villagers. 'He's not our vampire. But we tell them they've got one loose, we may scare off our quarry—or start a riot. This is your case, Initiate. Your call how we handle it.'",
      "locations": [
        "Fallsmeet"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Workshop Terraces"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [
        "Tarain Rethford"
      ],
