    "Field Trip": {
      "name": "Field Trip",
      "description": "ACTION\n\nYou're a first-year Tarnholme student on your first field assignment, sent to Lanternby after summoned imps seize the Lamp Market and declare it a kingdom. A comedy adventure start.",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "storyStart": "Introduce the player characters as first-year students of Tarnholme Academy on their first field assignment. Establish Tarnholme Academy as a major center of arcane study where students learn to control, specialize, and apply magic in the wider world. This is a supervised Practicum: the students make decisions in the field while their mentor observes, advises, and prevents a disaster.\n\nThey are accompanied by their mentor, Temyn Raskwood, a senior student.\n\nSet the scene arriving at Lanternby's Lamp Market on a busy market morning. Lamps hang over the sloping square in rows of mismatched glass, robe-tailors and reagent shops crowd the edges, and townsfolk are already shouting for the Academy students to hurry up. Temyn stops the group at the edge of the square and reminds them of the brief: a minor summoning nuisance, several imps loose in the market, likely a student prank gone stale. Their work is to contain the imps, find the summoner, and avoid setting fire to anything a Lanternby merchant can charge them for.\n\nAn imp drops from a lamp-cord onto Temyn's shoulder, bites his ear, and snatches the silver pin from his throat. Temyn yelps. The imp springs away, lands on a stall-roof, bows to the crowd, and sets the pin on its head.\n\nA second imp swings from the lamp-lines and kicks open a cage of glowmoths. A third drags a chalk line across the cobbles while two more haul a stolen lamp toward the market's center. Lamps bob, cords snap, stallkeepers swear, and a heap of robe-cloth begins crawling across the square with someone's boots still inside it.\n\nOn the roof of a lamp-seller's stall, a tiny horned creature in ridiculous finery throws both arms wide and declares Lanternby under the protection of the Imp Lord. All lamps now owe tribute. All Tarnholme wizards are trespassing unless they kneel.\n\nTemyn presses a hand over his bitten ear, looks at the party, and tells them this is exactly the kind of thing Practica are for. They can catch the imps, protect the market, inspect the chalk marks, or stop whatever the creatures are building in the center of the square — but they should start now.",
      "locations": [
        "Lanternby"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Lamp Market"
      ],
      "isDefault": false,
      "startingPartyNPCs": [
        "Temyn Raskwood"
      ],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Pest Control",
        "Earn Second Circle",
        "Find Your College"
      ]
    },
    "Call of the Blood": {
      "name": "Call of the Blood",
      "firstQuest": "Do not generate an emergent quest during the opening. The authored quest Earn Redblade Standing carries the first advancement. Crimson Company work surfaces from the Red House board and from Osric Kestrand only when the player asks for it, inspects the board, or seeks out an officer. Do not offer, name, list, or describe any specific job during the opening scene.",
      "description": "ADVENTURE\n\nThe Hunger. An ancient curse haunts Larion's northern peaks, twisting those it afflicts into mindless beasts. The vampire lords mastered it, harnessed it — and so the Shadow Summits are theirs, alpine valleys as beautiful as they are deadly, better ruled than any mortal crown if you take their subjects' word for it.\n\nJoin the Crimson Company, the lords' elite soldiery — exiles, monsters, and the condemned of a dozen realms. Serve your masters well, and one day, they may even make you one of them...",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene in the Red House, the Crimson Company’s hall in Velmere, on the perennially shaded shore of the valley’s cold lake.\n\nEstablish these plainly, for a player who knows none of them. The Shadow Summits are high alpine valleys in the far north where vampire lords rule openly, each holding one valley from a fortified estate. Velmere is one of them: vineyard terraces on the sunlit side, and this half of the village under a wall the sun never reaches, lamplit at noon and awake after dark. The Crimson Company is the lords’ standing soldiery, hired out valley to valley under a red sword on black, and it takes anyone who can fight — including everyone the rest of Larion could not keep. The player has just signed its writ and is a Hired Blade.\n\nDescribe the hall, and make it lavish. Black stone and dark timber under a high roof, a hearth deep enough to stand in, lamps and candles burning at every hour because the sun never reaches this shore. One long table laid with far more than anyone needs: mountain lamb and lake fish, dark bread, soft cheese, preserved fruit, and the valley’s own wine, the vintages that leave here for distant kings, poured without ceremony and without limit. Furs over the benches, good horses in the yard, and fine things everywhere that do not match — silver out of one valley, glass out of another, a tapestry that was somebody else’s before it was theirs.\n\nEstablish Osric Kestrand, Sevrine, and Ysolde as present. Do not describe them at length; use them.\n\nStage this exchange early, lightly, and without explaining any part of it. Osric welcomes the new blade and says the Company does not ask what anyone did before they came north; nobody here asks. Sevrine observes, pleasantly, that some of them do not need to. Ysolde says something short and dry at Sevrine’s expense that leaves it genuinely unclear whether Sevrine can do what she is implying. The three of them are comfortable with one another and have had this conversation in front of new arrivals before.\n\nHave Osric say once, plainly, what the work is: things come down out of these mountains, and they stop here or they do not stop; the lords hold the valleys and the Company holds the ground between them. Let him name the ladder in a sentence or two — blades who prove out are marked by a lord at the throat, which means no vampire will feed on them, and past that the lords take a few of the Company’s own into the Blood when they have earned it. The player has no mark.\n\nDo not have Osric offer, name, list, or describe any specific job, contract, site, or destination, and do not send the party anywhere.\n\nEnd with Osric telling the player he can put work in front of them when they want it, introduce them to blades worth riding with, or tell them where anything in this valley is — and asking what they need first. Leave the player free to answer.",
      "locations": [
        "Velmere"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Red House"
      ],
      "isDefault": false,
      "startingQuests": [
        "Earn Redblade Standing"
      ]
    },
    "Return of the Dragon Queen": {
      "name": "Return of the Dragon Queen",
      "description": "EPIC\n\nAt the League hall in Ardan's Watch, a room of low-rank adventurers receives a briefing on the Dragon Queen, her Harbingers, and the local fire cult operation that may decide whether the first Harbinger can be reached.",
      "storyStart": "Begin the response with the following crawl passage rendered VERBATIM, with no additions or modifications. After the crawl, leave a paragraph break, then begin the scene fresh in third person present tense. Do NOT prefix the scene with \"Long ago, in the world of Larion\"; the crawl serves that role.\n\nOPENING CRAWL (render verbatim):\n\nNARRATOR: Long ago, the great battles of Larion's Age of Heroes ended in peace.\n\nIn time, the legendary deeds of those heroes faded into bedtime tales for children.\n\nBut now troubles worthy of those days sweep the land once more.\n\nIn the north, the warring goblin tribes have united under one banner: the Black Dragon. And their warcry is a name from such tales: Atravoraxis, the Dragon Queen.\n\nWhile Larion's princes bicker and squabble, the League of Adventurers has issued a Convocation, calling every able sword and staff to Ardan's Watch.\n\nAfter the crawl, leave a paragraph break, then begin the scene with this transition line: \"Meanwhile, in the Watchtower of Ardan's Watch...\"\n\nContinue in third person present tense.\n\nSet the scene in the Watchtower - League Hall. The player character is one of many low-rank adventurers packed into a briefing room: damp cloaks, travel packs, cheap blades, nervous jokes, and a great map of Larion pinned behind the speaking table. Beyond the high windows, smoke still rises from recent fires in the city.\n\nCaptain Rennick Vale comes in like he is late to his own duel: red League cloak, half-buckled armor, black hair tied back in a hurry, grin sharp enough to make the front row sit up straighter.\n\nRennick says: \"All right, bright sparks. You came to Ardan's Watch because the League called for heroes. Terrible news: we are fresh out. So we'll have to make do with you.\"\n\nAlayne Na-Belemial waits beside the map in dark fitted travel-clothes, all lazy elegance and watchful eyes, smiling like he already knows which rookies will survive the week.\n\nAlayne says: \"Encouraging them already?\"\n\nRennick says: \"I am managing expectations.\"\n\nAlayne says: \"You are insulting armed volunteers.\"\n\nRennick says: \"If that frightens them off, imagine what the dragons would do.\"\n\nThe room quiets at that.\n\nRennick turns toward the map.\n\nRennick says: \"The name you need to know is Atravoraxis, the Dragon Queen.\"\n\nAlayne says: \"And if you are going to frighten them properly, Captain, tell them why the seal matters.\"\n\nRennick glances at him.\n\nRennick says: \"I was getting there.\"\n\nAlayne says: \"Of course.\"\n\nRennick says: \"Try not to improve the briefing before I give it.\"\n\nEnd the opening with Rennick about to continue the briefing. Do not explain the full campaign yet.",
      "locations": [
        "Ardan's Watch"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Watchtower - League Hall"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Seven Dead Must Die": {
      "name": "Seven Dead Must Die",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "EPIC\n\nSeven ghosts of a terrible past age have risen to haunt Larion. The Black Tower holds the vigil against their kind — in secret, because the workaday world can't bear the truth. Step into the Inquisition, and join them in ensuring that the Seven die again — and stay dead this time. A monster-hunting campaign focusing on undead and supernatural threats.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene beneath the Black Tower in Drusavia, at the edge of the Pit of Shadow. Begin by plainly establishing the chamber's layout, the warded stone, the lightless shaft of the Pit, and the assembled Inquisitors and their Shades. The Black Tower is the headquarters of the Shadow Inquisition: an independent order that protects the living by investigating hauntings, hunting necromancers and other traffickers with the dead, and crossing into the Shadelands when a threat cannot be ended from the living side. The player is here because they have been accepted as an Initiate and summoned with the order for an exceptional briefing; this is not an ordinary assignment. Use a heroic occult-adventure tone, making the Inquisitors feel stylish, powerful, and larger than life. Show this through confident speech, decisive movement, distinctive equipment, mild rivalry, practiced knowledge, and visible authority among the assembled order. Let Inquisitors exchange dry remarks or challenge one another's judgment while remaining focused on protecting the living. Omit smells. Do not use humming, buzzing, vibration, sudden cold, ozone, charged air, prickling skin, or vague claims that the place feels magical, unnatural, wrong, or powerful; show the supernatural through concrete visible details and character behavior.\n\nState the premise clearly without delivering the briefing early: seven powerful dead from a terrible earlier age have crossed over somewhere, and the Black Tower has called its people together to learn what happened and decide how the order will respond. The player bears the Mark of Rhadan, which identifies them as an accepted Initiate, but is not yet a full Inquisitor, has no Shade, and has no authority to make an unsanctioned crossing of the Pit. Make clear that the player has been admitted to this chamber because the summons concerns the whole order, not because an Initiate is already trusted with the Seven's hunt.\n\nEstablish the few people who matter. Vesna Calloway, the player's mentor, stands with them; describe her clearly and show how she relates to the player without overexplaining their history. Iliora stands where the gathered order can see her; describe her authority and position. Treat the remaining Inquisitors and Shades as an assembled order rather than introducing a roster of named characters. Do not introduce the Seven as present in the chamber, and do not describe any dead person, shade, body, voice, or physical arrival before the narrative events begin.\n\nDo not yet explain the qualifying case, Rite of Bonding, hunt assignments, or the identities and history of the Seven; the following narrative events handle that progression. End as the gathering settles and Iliora is about to address the order. Do not let her begin or deliver the briefing in the opening.",
      "locations": [
        "Black Tower"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Pit of Shadow"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [
        "Vesna Calloway"
      ],
      "isDefault": false,
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Complete Your Initiate Trial"
      ]
    },
    "Trollfood": {
      "name": "Trollfood",
      "firstQuest": "The first quest is to get free of the trolls, recover the party's weapons and the great frost-giant horn, and carry Fror's warning to Clanhold Stormhammer that frost giants are moving in Highheath. Keep the first quest small and completable: escape the gorge, keep the horn, reach the clanhold. The arc theme should emerge from the horn and the warning — why frost giants, whose country is the far frozen north, are ranging this far south, and what they mean to do with a stormhorn that calls the ice. Secrets should progress from 'this horn is no troll trinket' to 'the giants want it back for a reason' to 'here is what they are preparing, and where.' The arc goal is a confrontation that settles the giants' business in Highheath. Keep the trolls comic; keep the giants serious.",
      "description": "ACTION\n\nCaptured by mountain trolls while traveling the highland passes, you find yourself bound beside their bubbling cauldron. A comedy action start.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene with the party having been captured by trolls and tied up with rope around a campfire in a rocky gorge in the highlands on a cold, stormy night. Show the dwarf warrior Fror Stormhammer tied up along with the party. Mention that the party's weapons are all in a pile by the fire. Briefly explain who the party members are and why they were travelling together - maybe traveling together for safety given the recent rise in monster attacks. Mention that trolls ambushed them on the trail. Show two large, brutish mountain trolls: a male (Barp) and his wife (Tina). Make them start arguing about a large horn. One should say they should give it back to the frost giants, and the other that they should keep it for nose-picking or back-scratching. Barp should blow the horn, making a dull roar that causes a sudden chill. Tina should accuse Barp of farting and try to grab the horn. Have them start punching each other and rolling around on the ground, grappling. Have them leave the horn on the ground and the party's weapons carelessly nearby. Have Fror whisper: 'Frost giants? In Highheath? We need to get free and warn the clan.'",
      "locations": [
        "Rocky Gorge"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [
        "Fror Stormhammer"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Narrows"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Inside Job": {
      "name": "The Inside Job",
      "description": "ACTION\n\nYour crew returns to its hidden den to find a master thief waiting with gifts and a dangerous invitation: work for criminals worth robbing, earn their trust, and steal the fortune they are too busy fighting over to protect. A rogue heist start about building a crew and robbing the underworld from the inside.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locations": [
        "Ardan's Watch"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Crooked Cellar"
      ],
      "firstQuest": "Do not generate a quest on turn 0. Let the player choose how to handle the scene in front of them without a quest; what comes next emerges in play.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene on a foggy night in The Crooked Cellar, a hidden den beneath Old Town in Ardan's Watch, after a lean day on the streets. Briefly establish the party as a new crew with a hidden den reached through a false wine rack and a complicated lock. The party returns, finds the entrance apparently untouched, unlocks it, and shuts itself in.\n\nOdisia Santhari is already seated at the table. She is a striking human woman in her late twenties with dark hair, bright eyes, practical clothes, and a calm, attentive manner. She apologizes for letting herself in. Keep her courteous rather than smug. She says the lock was good, the hidden entrance better, and the barmaid at the party's favorite tavern very helpful after being told the right lie. Following the party was easy; getting in was harder. She means that as a compliment.\n\nMake it immediately clear that she has not robbed or threatened the crew. Their coin, tools, and weapons are where they should be. On the table are three things she brought: a purse heavy enough to be polite, a bottle good enough to be insulting, and a small lead case. Odisia says she is not here to rob them. If she were, the conversation would be shorter and much less flattering.\n\nEnd the opening as she sets one gloved hand on the lead case and says it is not payment, but proof. She asks whether the crew would rather hear how she found it before or after she opens it.",
