      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "SANDBOX\n\nYou've come to a tavern at the start of your road. The room is alive, the night is young, and the next move is yours. An open-ended start that lets you choose the direction your story takes.",
      "storyStart": "Set the scene on an open-ended first night in the current tavern. Briefly establish who the player is and why they have come tonight. Use two or three concrete details from the authored area description and the current location or region to make the place distinctive. Make the room feel alive through visible activity, specific sounds, objects, and people rather than a catalogue.\n\nStage one concrete social situation in the tavern: a live wager, contest, game, provocative exchange, public challenge, disputed seat, unusual performance, daring proposal, or someone being pressured by another person. Give the situation a clear visible stake and make the wants of the people involved legible. Keep it small enough to unfold in the room but interesting enough to invite curiosity, participation, or a change of sides.\n\nIntroduce a focal NPC: a charismatic, distinctive young adult with a strong personality, useful capabilities, an active life beyond this conversation, and a reason the player might want to know them. Prefer an established NPC connected to the tavern; otherwise create one worth meeting again. An appealing young woman can be the focal NPC when that suits the scene, but give her agency, boundaries, and a want beyond attracting the player. Use appearance as supporting texture, not as the reason for the NPC's interest. Do not make the focal NPC a generic barkeep, guard, elderly regular, farmer, fisher, merchant needing an errand, or random drunk.\n\nShow the focal NPC actively involved in the social situation. They may be winning or arranging a wager, provoking a rival, defending someone, making an offer, hiding a problem, testing a principle, or looking for someone willing to change the terms. Their interest in the player should arise from the concrete situation and what the player can choose to do in it, not from the player's aura, appearance, or generic look. Give the NPC a specific immediate want and a personal stake in how the scene develops.\n\nMake the interaction inviting and dynamic. The focal NPC may greet the player, draw them into the game, ask them to judge a dispute, offer a seat, propose a wager, challenge their position, or ask what brings them into the room. Let another identifiable person have a different stake so the player can sense a real social web rather than a staged monologue. Keep the player's established role, capabilities, feelings, motives, and history intact; never invent a new profession, power, or unexpressed reaction for them.\n\nUse existing Larion material first and keep unfamiliar lore concrete and limited. Let the current tavern and region provide atmosphere, but keep the opening focused on the people and situation in front of the player. The tavern proprietor may offer a natural welcome, explain the house's rules, point out the interesting table, or intervene if the exchange threatens to turn ugly. Do not make the proprietor automatically convert the scene into a job offer.\n\nEnd with the focal NPC's offer, challenge, question, wager, or invitation in motion, at a moment when the player's response can change the terms, relationship, reputation, alliance, or opportunity in the room. Do not decide what the player feels or what they choose.",
