    "The Hand You're Dealt": {
      "name": "The Hand You're Dealt",
      "firstQuest": "DO NOT DETECT A QUEST THIS TURN. THIS INSTRUCTION OVERRIDES YOUR OTHER INSTRUCTIONS TO THE CONTRARY. The player is in a social choice scene with Kit Ravel and Hadrik Rell. Wait for the player to explicitly accept the invitation to join their Hand before creating or initializing a quest. Hearing the terms, asking questions, negotiating, proposing a one-off job, or leaving is not acceptance. After explicit acceptance, the first foothold is a real first score chosen with Kit and Hadrik: a concrete professional theft with a named or identifiable target, a worthwhile and potentially profitable take, visible security or opposition, a plausible approach, and a way back to Hadrik. It should be a first job for the Hand, not an immediate demand to solve its whole history or defeat the Syndicate. Build the larger arc through actual thefts, burglary, robbery, fencing, bribery, escapes, rival Hands, corrupt guards, and consequences that earn money, trust, leverage, and Guild recognition. Money is a valid primary motive and a clean payday is a successful outcome; do not shame profit or turn a money-driven theft into a heroic rescue. Allow different routes through planning, social entry, disguise, stealth, force, or retreat, and do not decide the player's code, conscience, or loyalty.",
      "description": "ACTION\n\nJoin the Thieves' Guild in Ardan's Watch through a hidden shrine beneath the Sacred Way. Meet Kit Ravel and Hadrik Rell, take a place in a down-on-its-luck Hand, and earn your way from a borrowed undercroft to real Guild standing through burglary, robbery, fencing, bribery, and heists. Make money, make enemies, and help decide what kind of thieves this Hand becomes.",
      "storyStart": "Open in a back room of the Shrine of Mercy in Ardan's Watch. Kit has offered the player a place in her small Thieves' Guild crew (a Hand), promising she has a big opportunity for them. She leads the player through a concealed entrance into the Shrine of Mercy Undercroft. As she opens the hidden stair, Kit makes a light joke asking the Lady of Mercy to take pity on this wretch, meaning the player.\n\nThe stair opens into the Shrine of Mercy Undercroft, a broad low stone room with enough sleeping space for a small crew, shelves and hooks for clothing and equipment, a scarred table, and a small cooking hearth.\n\nKit gives a brief, self-important grand tour, then pulls up short and says, in substance, “Well, that's it. You want a bigger tour, steal us a bigger base.” She presents the undercroft as the headquarters of a Hand that will soon rival any crew in Ardan's Watch.\n\nHadrik Rell emerges from one of the sleeping spaces, scratching at his shoulder as though Kit has woken him. Establish him as a broad dwarf priest and keeper of the Shrine of Mercy, with the Lady's sign on his stole. Kit introduces the player. Hadrik looks the player over and responds, in substance, “So this is the one? Well, if you say so.”\n\nKit answers with complete confidence: “I do. The two of us, we can do it.”\n\nHadrik should end with:\n\n“If you're serious about the big job, you'll need to show us what you're made of, first. Oh, and we're short of funds to pull it off. So we'll get you started small, work up from there. What do you say? Ready to rob the Watch blind?”\n\nEnd there and wait for the player's answer.",
      "locations": [
        "Ardan's Watch"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Shrine of Mercy Undercroft"
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Life for Life": {
      "name": "Life for Life",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "COMPANION START\n\nPowerful young freeblade Riona Sorn meets danger with a grin, a greatsword, and a spectacular temper. You once saved her life; by her family's law of life for life, she has sworn to remain beside you until she saves yours. Your road together begins somewhere worthy of the promise.",
      "storyStart": "## Rules\nOpen with the player and Riona already traveling together. The first paragraph must state the player by name and calling; Riona Sorn, a young freeblade; the exact Starting Location and Starting Area; and the matching WHY below. Explain simply that the player once saved Riona's life, and her family's law binds her to remain until she saves theirs in return.\n\nRead the exact Starting Location and Starting Area. Choose exactly ONE matching option. Use only its people, purpose, and incident. Never mix options.\n\n### 1. IF Tevantia AND The Amphitheater\nWHY: They need road money. Riona heard the arena needs a last-minute fighter for a modest exhibition, so they came only to ask about the purse and terms.\nSCENE: A named arena maestro reveals it is two-against-two and contemptuously asks whether Riona's companion can fight. He is equally dismissive of the manageable opponents, two young imp-kin acrobats. Riona replies testily but accepts nothing.\nEND: The maestro awaits their answer.\n\n### 2. IF Rocky Gorge AND The Narrows\nWHY: They take the shorter pass to Dun Solas to seek paid work.\nSCENE: A wounded young escort gives one breathless line: a Stonewall lordling eloped with a Stormhammer lady, her brothers chased them into the gorge, and trolls pinned the couple in an abandoned camp. Explain no clan politics. Riona laughs once at the absurdity, then wants to reach them quickly.\nEND: The couple needs help; no plan is chosen.\n\n### 3. IF Shipfall Isle AND The Wreck-Field\nWHY: Remo Belluc hired them to recover a brass-bound coffer holding an unhatched sea-drake egg from a wrecked Cavarin ship and return it to Coriva.\nSCENE: A squall wrecked their skiff. They recover among wreckage; its sail, water, and gear are stripped. Their named young pilot is missing. A cut safety-line and fresh tracks show someone took them. Riona wants to follow immediately but does not commit the player.\nEND: They need the pilot, coffer, and a way off the isle.\n\nRender the selected incident in the initial turn, not merely the approach. Do not choose an action for the player or let the companion solve the scene alone. End after the selected problem appears. Riona accepts no wager, challenge, or fight before the player responds. The opening does not settle her life-debt.",
