    "A Young Lord's Oath": {
      "name": "A Young Lord's Oath",
      "firstQuest": "",
      "description": "COMPANION START\n\nHandsome young lord Tristan Corwin is a charming rake with storm magic in his blood and greatness on his mind. You have sworn to serve as his personal shield and companion. Wherever his hunt for mastery and glory leads, trouble is rarely far behind—and turning back is no longer only his decision.",
      "storyStart": "## Rules\nOpen with the player and Tristan already traveling together. The first paragraph must state the player by name and calling; Tristan Corwin, a young nobleman and storm-duelist; the exact Starting Location and Starting Area; and the matching WHY below. Explain simply that the player swore to serve as Tristan's personal shield and companion, while Tristan seeks mastery of his storm magic and renown earned through his own deeds.\n\nRead the exact Starting Location and Starting Area. Choose exactly ONE matching option. Use only its people, purpose, and incident. Never mix options.\n\n### 1. IF Marrenford AND The Elfstone Tavern\nWHY: They leave for Clanhold Stormhammer so Tristan can seek instruction. He promised a farewell breakfast and claimed their horses and money were arranged.\nSCENE: A named young half-orc cardsharp arrives with Tristan's saddle and signed marker. He lost his horse to her last night, meant to win it back by breakfast, and hid this; he wagered nothing of the player's. In one amused line she demands an exact painful-but-payable sum or the horse. She will negotiate, not forgive it. Tristan charms and minimizes but orders no solution.\nEND: Stop before they settle the debt.\n\n### 2. IF Cloudtop AND The Condor Aerie\nWHY: They accepted an Edict to find two Copperstone miners missing beneath the Bonecrags. A named young eaglekin rider prepares their condor.\nSCENE: The bird accepts the player but refuses Tristan; its feathers rise and brass harness sparks near his storm magic. The amused rider says rank and charm mean nothing to a condor: he must quiet the charge first. His embarrassment worsens it.\nEND: They must get Tristan aboard; the miners await below.\n\n### 3. IF Shipfall Isle AND The Stormspire\nWHY: They sailed for Caranthoria to seek a storm-duelist, but Shipfall's weather wrecked them and Stormcallers captured them.\nSCENE: On the sacrificial ledge, their hands are bound, equipment within reach, with a named young sailor beside them. Two captors remain. A merchant ship approaches. One says their blood will call the storm that wrecks it. Tristan quietly says that if one hand is freed, he may turn the gathering lightning on the captors, but cannot promise control.\nEND: Stop before sacrifice or escape begins.\n\nRender the selected incident in the initial turn, not merely the approach. Do not choose an action for the player or let the companion solve the scene alone. End after the selected problem appears. Tristan may propose something daring but does not attack or commit the player before they respond.",
