    "Construct": {
      "name": "Construct",
      "description": "Made, not born. Constructs are artificial beings — bodies of stone, metal, wood, or ceramic animated by a soul bound through enchantment. Who made them and why varies: some were forged as laborers, some as guardians, some as experiments. Whatever the original purpose, the art of making them is believed to be lost, and their kind is rarer by the day. Every construct faces the same question sooner or later — what am I for now? The answer is theirs to decide, which may be more freedom than most of their creators intended.\n\nNo two constructs look alike. A body of riveted iron plates moves differently from one carved in smooth granite or assembled from lacquered hardwood. Some are roughly humanoid, others only approximate it. Eyes glow or don't. Joints click or hum or grind. Faces range from expressive carved features to featureless masks to bare metal with nothing resembling a face at all. Size varies with construction but most fall within human range. They don't need to breathe, eat or sleep, though some learn to mimic such behaviors for the peace of mind of those around them. But, like the other races, they can be hurt and die when their bodies sustain too much damage.\n\nPeople don't know what to make of constructs, and it shows. Are you a person or a thing? Most folk land on person eventually, but the hesitation is always there. In big cities, they're rare but common enough to be accepted without open fear. In rural places, where most people have never seen one, reactions may be more varied and extreme: fear, revulsion, or even worship.",
