      "requirements": [],
      "traitNarrativeEffects": "A cleric is a keeper of a faith's rites and responsibilities. They know the ordinary forms of its prayers, blessings, funerary rites, and consecrations; recognize its sacred signs and profanations; and may speak for its temple or tradition where that standing is acknowledged. People of the faith may seek them for healing, burial, counsel, hauntings, curses, possession, restless dead, or places made profane. Meaningful service may earn access to a guarded rite, a sacred place, or an object entrusted by the faith."
    },
    "Half-elf": {
      "name": "Half-elf",
      "description": "Caught between two worlds and at home in both — or neither, depending on who you ask. Half-elves carry enough of each parent's blood to belong everywhere and nowhere. Among humans they're exotic, graceful, touched by something older. Among elves they're blunt, hasty, burning through a life that's long by human measure and embarrassingly short by elven. Most half-elves stop trying to pick a side early and learn to be whatever the room needs instead. It makes them very good with people.\n\nElven heritage softens human features without replacing them. Pointed ears, slightly angular bone structure, and eyes a shade too bright to be fully human. They age slowly — a half-elf at sixty might pass for a human at thirty — but they do age, visibly, which sets them apart from their elven parent's people. Builds vary widely depending on the human side. Hair and coloring tend toward the elven parent but nothing is guaranteed.\n\nHalf-elves fit in. That's the short version. They pick up local customs fast, adjust their manner to match their company, and rarely give offense without meaning to. People find them easy to talk to and hard to pin down — friendly without being fully knowable. Elves consider them charming but shallow. Humans consider them elegant but distant. Half-elves have heard both opinions enough times to stop caring, or at least to fake it convincingly.",
