    "Ratkin": {
      "name": "Ratkin",
      "description": "Survivors first, everything else second. Ratkin are small, quick, and very hard to get rid of — a description they'd take as a compliment. They live in the spaces other races overlook: crawlways, storm drains, wall cavities, the gaps between buildings where the light doesn't reach. Most cities have a ratkin population whether they know it or not. They're scavengers, informants, couriers, fixers — people who make a living in the margins and know more about how a city actually works than anyone sitting in a guildhall. Ratkin don't build empires. They outlast them.\n\nSmall and lean, standing three and a half to four feet tall. Coarse fur in shades of brown, grey, black, or dull tawny. Pointed faces with bright dark eyes, prominent front teeth, and round ears that swivel toward sound. Hairless tails as long as their bodies, used for balance in tight spaces. Narrow builds that can squeeze through gaps that look impossible. Their hands are small but extraordinarily quick — a ratkin can strip a lock, re-stitch a seam, or empty a pocket faster than most people can follow.\n\nNobody trusts a ratkin on sight. That's the reality, and most ratkin stopped being hurt by it a long time ago. Merchants watch them, guards move them along, and polite company pretends they aren't there. In the underworld they're valued — good ratkin informants and couriers are worth their weight in coin. Above ground, earning trust takes time and proof. Ratkin who've done it tend to inspire fierce loyalty precisely because everyone knows what it cost them to be taken seriously.",
