    "Monk": {
      "name": "Monk",
      "description": "Main Attributes: Attunement, Wisdom, Dexterity, and Strength\n\nMonks train until breath, balance, attention, and motion answer as one. Some orders teach through meditation and prayer, some through endurance, some through repeated movement until the body learns before thought can interfere. The result is not serenity as decoration. It is practical control: a hand in the right place, a step taken at the right time, a body that refuses to be hurried by fear.\n\nA trained monk can fight unarmed because every joint, breath, stance, and fall has been made useful. They close gaps, redirect force, read tension, and strike where balance fails. Outside combat, that same discipline lets them climb, wait, listen, endure, and act cleanly when everyone else is reacting. The most dangerous monks are not the loudest ones. They are the ones who arrive already centered.\n\nMonks teach that the body has more answers than habit permits. Through Forms—commands perfected until breath, flesh, bone, balance, and sense answer the will—they make their own bodies exceed ordinary mortal limits for a single deed. Different orders pursue different limits: impossible weight or lightness, hardness or suppleness, sense without sight, perfect timing, endurance, or swift mending.",
