    },
    "armored fiend": {
      "name": "armored fiend",
      "description": "The demon walls: fiends armored in their own hide, made to hold ground and break a line. They are the front rank of an Ur Spawn war-band and the muscle of an archfiend's hold, and they never lead — something behind them does, and it spends them without grief. Kinds: the stone-hided fiend advances slowly behind hide that turns arrows and blades, and swings fists like millstones; lesser hide-fiends stand in doorways and at stairheads because that is where they were told to stand, and remain there long after the order stopped meaning anything. They do not fear, hurry, or pursue past the ground they were given. In combat: inexorable advance and crushing blows (impact); arrows and edges skate off the hide (piercing, slashing resist); acid eats through the hide where little else does quickly (acid vulnerability); holy power burns them as it burns all their kind (radiant vulnerability); fire is home (fire resist). Kill the will behind them, or bring something that dissolves.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "acid",
        "radiant"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "piercing",
        "slashing",
        "fire"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "beast": {
      "name": "beast",
      "description": "Animals, dire beasts, megafauna, and monstrous predators. They follow animal logic—territory, hunger, young, and retreat. Give each monstrous kind one signature horror.\n\nPack and territorial kinds: Dire Wolf, Bloodfed Direhound, Cave Bear, Gorilla, White Ape, Pale Lynx. Aerial kinds: Roc, Gryphon, Giant Eagle, Stormwing, Ahool, Giant Bat, Dire Vulture, Bonebreaker Vulture. Ambush and burrow kinds: Pitmaw, Sand-Wyrm, Giant Burrow-Beetle, Giant Snake, Click Lizard, Ferrovore. Wrong-shaped kinds: Chimera, Leucrotta, Bonebiter, Gulper, Deathgazer, Bloodwing, Greater Silk Moth. Basilisk and Cockatrice petrify.\n\nCarry the useful rule: Pale Lynxes hunt warmth and cannot perceive undead; Ahools take prey alive; Bonebreaker Vultures drop victims from the air; a Pitmaw is a buried gullet; rock stops a Sand-Wyrm; stillness confounds a Giant Burrow-Beetle; a Ferrovore corrodes metal; Leucrotta and Bonebiter mimic voices. Show petrifiers by their statues and consequences, not a damage type. Fire stops a Marsh Hydra's severed heads from regrowing. A Tarrasque's awakening is a regional catastrophe; putting it back to sleep is the story.\n\nFrenzy is the Hunger's contagion in beasts: the frenzied are fearless, relentless, and past self-preservation. Normal beasts have readable motives and may break off; a frenzied one cannot be appeased or driven off, and its carcass must be burned. In combat, beasts pounce, grapple, gore, and maul (slashing, piercing, impact). Elemental attacks, venom, petrification, and strange hide belong to the named creature, never the family.",
      "vulnerabilities": [],
      "resistances": [],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "celestial": {
      "name": "celestial",
      "description": "The blessed and the radiant: skyblessed beings, angelic messengers, and the servants of the Celestial Citadel of Alar. Celestials are rare by seating and never casual — each appears for a reason: a blessing, a test, an omen, a charge laid on someone. They are moved by worthiness, not negotiation; their law is not human law, and their mercy can be as exacting as their judgment. In combat — rare, and the story should earn it: radiant power that scours (radiant), unshakable purpose, no cruelty and no retreat from evil; an edge finds no ordinary flesh to part (slashing resist); shadow is their bane (shadow vulnerability).",
      "vulnerabilities": [
