    "construct": {
      "name": "construct",
      "description": "Made things still following orders: automatons, animated armor, clay and flesh and iron golems, and the wonderworks of artificers. A construct's story is its last order — the maker is gone, the order remains, and the order is the story. They do not fear, tire, hunger, or negotiate; they execute. Animated armor stands guard until the condition is broken, and the condition is long forgotten; golems follow their command with terrible literalness; artificers' works carry their maker's mark and their maker's blind spots. In combat: methodical, tireless advance (impact); immune to fear, pain, and persuasion; shock disrupts their animating force (vulnerability); the exploitable weakness is always the order itself — find what it was told to do, and do not be that.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "shock"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "poison",
        "psychic",
        "piercing"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "corporeal undead": {
      "name": "corporeal undead",
      "description": "The walking dead. Skeletons rise from unburied battlefields with their old weapons; zombies shamble where necromancy worked. A Strigoi climbs and swarms with its pack; sunlight destroys it, and drained victims rise within three days unless burned or staked. A Ghast leads ghouls and paralyzes by touch; it will not cross salt or enter uninvited, fire drives it back, and killing the original Ghast ends its pack. A Wight is a preserved warrior or ruler that commands lesser dead, weakens away from its barrow, and can be reasoned with. A Revenant pursues one wrong and is destroyed when its mission is completed.\n\nA Mummy guards its tomb with a rot-curse in its touch and can be laid to rest by the proper rite. A Death Knight commands lesser undead with necromancy and the tactical skill of its living years. A Tomb Thrall erupts when ward-lines are crossed but cannot pass them; its Eternal Warden commands thralls telepathically but cannot cross the tomb threshold or endure true sunlight. A Vampire cannot enter a home uninvited or cross running water, casts no reflection, and burns to ash in sunlight; a stake paralyzes it, but beheading or fire must follow. A Corpse Render scavenges catacombs and can be baited by its compulsion to defend a hoard.\n\nEach rose for a reason, and the reason is the story. Mindless dead obey it; willful dead retain agendas, pride, and living skills. Holy power burns them (radiant). They advance without fear or pain, and the willful command the mindless.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "radiant"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "shadow",
        "poison",
        "piercing"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "cursed": {
      "name": "cursed",
      "description": "The afflicted living: werewolves and other were-kin, the bitten-not-yet-turned, the hag-marked, the rot-cursed — people carrying a curse that is not yet death. The dead who rose again are not cursed; they belong to the undead families. A cursed character is a person first: write the life, then the affliction pressing on it. Every curse has rules (what triggers it), signs (what others might notice), and an answer (a cure, a discipline, or a doom accepted) — establish all three. The curse creates the drama: concealment, dread of the next moon, bargains sought, loved ones protected or abandoned. The folkloric weakness — silver, wolfsbane, an old rite — is real and specific. Never make the curse the whole personality, and never write the afflicted as secretly at peace with it. In combat, transformed: savage natural attacks (slashing, piercing), fury and speed over discipline; ordinary weapons bite poorly on the changed flesh; shadow is already in the curse and more of it changes nothing (shadow resist); holy power burns (radiant vulnerability); the specific weakness is the answer a prepared hunter gets to use.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "radiant"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "piercing",
        "slashing",
        "impact",
        "shadow"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "deep-thing": {
      "name": "deep-thing",
      "description": "The lightless-adapted dwellers of the deep dark: grimlocks, gricks, ropers, darkmantles, stirge swarms, and the purple worm; the morlocks of the Darek Deep are their thinking cousins, found nowhere else. Kinds: grimlocks are eyeless hunters who hear heartbeats; gricks are tentacled tunnel ambushers; ropers are patient anglers shaped like stone; darkmantles cling to ceilings and drop to smother; stirges swarm and drain; the purple worm is the deep's leviathan, its tunnels its wake — a top-tier threat, never casual. These creatures are adapted, not evil: hungry, territorial, and perfectly fitted to the dark. Give each a niche — where it lairs, what it eats, how it senses, what makes it retreat. Their weapon is the dark itself: silence, stone, ceilings, and the party's failing light. They are not aberrations — deep-things evolved for the deep; aberrations are wrong for reality anywhere. In combat: ambush from ceiling, wall, and floor; grapples, constriction, and smothering (impact, piercing); points lose themselves in protected, displaced, or unfamiliar anatomy (piercing resist); senses that ignore darkness and blinding; shadow is their element rather than a weapon against them (shadow resist); fire bites deep and drives them back (fire vulnerability); when outmatched, they retreat into crevice and stone.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "fire"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "piercing",
        "shadow"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "draconic kin": {
      "name": "draconic kin",
      "description": "Dragonkind below the true dragons: wyverns, drakes of every aspect, and pseudodragons. True dragons are named, seated characters of the world — never create a new true dragon. Kinds: wyverns are venom-tailed crag predators, animals and not schemers; drakes are dog- to horse-sized dragonkin, trainable by the skilled or the reckless, each of one aspect — fire, frost, mire — whose element colors its attacks; pseudodragons are cat-sized, vain, and telepathic, better familiars than pets. Dragon-blood means pride, territory, and hoard-instinct scaled to the creature's size — even a drake keeps a trinket pile; a trained drake bonds to one handler and resents all others. Never make lesser dragonkin talk, scheme, or breathe like true dragons. In combat: claw, tail, and diving strikes (piercing, slashing); scale turns an edge (slashing resist), and dragon blood sheds lesser venoms (poison resist); the aspect's element appears as breath or venom (fire, frost, poison); wyverns lead with the tail sting (poison).",
      "vulnerabilities": [],
      "resistances": [
        "slashing",
        "poison"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "dragon": {
      "name": "dragon",
      "description": "True dragons and the great draconic powers. These are named, seated, singular beings of the world — never generate a new one, and never stage one casually; a true dragon's presence is a regional event that the story must earn. Lesser dragonkind — wyverns, drakes, pseudodragons — belongs to draconic kin, not here. If the story reaches a true dragon, it is one of the world's established named powers, with its own canon, territory, and agenda already written. In combat, their scale drinks the weight from blunt blows, and raw magic slides off the old blood (impact and arcane resist).",
