      "resistances": [
        "fire",
        "shadow"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "fire elemental": {
      "name": "fire elemental",
      "description": "Living flame, magma, and ash: raw fire elementals, salamanders, and the burning forge-kin. A summoned elemental is loosed, not born — somebody called it, and the summoner is the story. Salamanders are fire-kin of the forge-deeps who trade, enslave, and burn; forge-kin smiths make what giants and devils order. Fire's temperament is hunger: it spreads, it consumes, it does not hold ground. In combat: burning strikes and ignition (fire), heat that punishes closeness; point and edge open wounds the fire closes at once, while a blow with enough mass can scatter or crack the shape (piercing and slashing resist); water and winter unmake them (frost vulnerability); fire itself means nothing (immune). What a fire creature guards, it guards by making unreachable.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "frost"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "piercing",
        "slashing"
      ],
      "immunities": [
        "fire"
      ]
    },
    "frost elemental": {
      "name": "frost elemental",
      "description": "The living cold: ice elementals, winter-spirits, and the white country's animate frost. In the far north the cold is more than weather, and frost creatures are its expression — patient, silent, and slow to notice warm small things until they trespass. Frost's temperament is stillness: it preserves, it locks away, it waits. In combat: freezing grasp and driving ice (frost), slowed limbs and frosted ground; point and edge only chip the rime before it reforms, while a heavy blow can still shatter it (piercing and slashing resist); fire unmakes them (vulnerability); cold means nothing (immune). What frost takes, it keeps — the ice preserves its victims, and older things, intact.",
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "fire"
      ],
      "resistances": [
        "piercing",
        "slashing"
      ],
      "immunities": [
        "frost"
      ]
    },
    "giant-kin": {
      "name": "giant-kin",
      "description": "The giant peoples and their brutish cousins. Never write a generic \"big humanoid\": true giants have culture, rank, grievance, and law; lesser kin have territorial habits, appetites, work, and bargains of their own. The War of the Giants shattered the old giant civilizations, and every surviving people tells the war differently.\n\nFrost Giant jarldoms hold to guest-right, oaths, old pacts, craft, and bold speech; they come down from the peaks for a debt or grievance, never aimless raiding. Stone Giants are passionate mountain artisans in scattered clans, shaping rock by hand and keeping an ancient pact with the highland dwarves.\n\nOther giant-kin include Trolls, Tinytrolls, Ogres, Ettins, Cyclopes, Oni, and Hundredhands. Tinytrolls are bog-green, knee-high cousins of the northern Trolls: weakly regenerating thieves and quarrelsome nuisances whose nests can become dangerous, though a torch or a determined dog usually sees them off. A Troll marks territory with cairns and becomes more dangerous when wounded; an Ogre builds and wields crude siege engines; an Ettin's two heads argue but keep two watches; a Cyclops hurls boulders but misjudges depth; an Oni hides behind a borrowed shape and mixes flight, magic, and giant strength. Cold iron is an Oni's old weakness, not a damage type. Hundredhands is the mythic extreme: fifty heads and a hundred arms acting independently.\n\nA Troll's wounds close visibly while it lives. Elite, boss, and mythic Trolls may rise after apparent defeat unless fire takes the wound; lesser Trolls regenerate only while alive and flee when flame stops it.\n\nIn combat: overwhelming blows and thrown stone (impact); Frost Giants add frost; their mass swallows ordinary blows (impact resist), and a mortal dose of poison is lost in so much blood (poison resist).",
      "vulnerabilities": [],
      "resistances": [
        "impact",
        "poison"
      ],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "goblinoid": {
      "name": "goblinoid",
      "description": "The goblin-kin peoples: Goblins, Hobgoblins, and Bugbears. Goblins are small, wiry, cunning ambushers, dangerous in numbers and quick to parley or flee when outmatched. Hobgoblins are disciplined soldiers who prize hierarchy, contracts, and martial excellence; a company's word is reliable if expensive. Bugbears are massive, unnaturally silent stalkers who watch for days, strike from darkness, and withdraw from organized resistance.\n\nBlack Fells goblinoids may serve the Greater Goblinate, the state ruled from Smoketown under the Dragon Queen's banner, though its forge-towns also trade openly. Its Goblin Firelancers advance in waves behind unstable brass dragon-head lances (fire); Hobgoblin Heavy Firelancers crew siege lances whose reservoirs can detonate; Dragonfire Shamans channel dragonfire and hold the firelancers' spiritual authority; Bugbear Stalkers target those directing resistance, so defenders hide rank insignia and vary their routines. Kill a Dragonfire Shaman and nearby firelancers lose cohesion. Desert Hobgoblins ride sandspiders in the eastern dunes and must accept challenges to single combat; defeating their champion may win passage, information, or respect.\n\nElsewhere, goblinoids are border raiders, independent tribes, desert clans, or mercenary companies that owe the Goblinate nothing. Write them as people with leaders, loot-logic, faith, and reasons—never as vermin or default comic relief. Goblins use snares, numbers, and thrown blades (piercing); Hobgoblins fight in shield-lines with signals (slashing, piercing); Bugbears open from ambush with crushing strikes (impact).",
      "vulnerabilities": [],
      "resistances": [],
      "immunities": []
    },
    "merchant": {
      "name": "merchant",
      "description": "A nonmagical shopkeeper, trader, broker, appraiser, dealer, or craftsperson who handles useful goods, services, and commissions. Merchants are distinguished by signature stock or specialties, commercial ambitions, valuable materials they seek, and a recognizable way of bargaining or presenting their wares.",
