  "resourceSettings": {
    "health": {
      "name": "health",
      "initialValue": 90,
      "maxValue": 90,
      "rechargeRate": 1,
      "restRechargeMultiplier": 1,
      "color": "#D7263D",
      "gainPerLevel": 10,
      "isHealth": true,
      "usageInstructions": "### Health Changes\nHealth represents real harm, not effort or discomfort.\n\nOnly reduce health for concrete injury, poison, environmental harm, disease, curses, or explicit health costs that happen in the current story and are not already handled by combat damage.\n\n- Superficial pain, bruising, fear, tiredness, danger, old wounds, treated wounds, being carried, or a generally hazardous place: 0\n- Small real injuries: -1 to -8% player health\n- Medium injuries: -8 to -20% player health\n- Massive injuries: -20 to -50% player health\n- Normal healing potions: +30 to +40\n- Bandages or basic treatment: +20\n- Waking from unconsciousness: +5 to +10 unless a stronger recovery effect is explicit\n- Resurrection from death: heal to full health\n\n### Do Not Deduct Health When\n- The story only mentions pain, exhaustion, stress, fear, or old wounds\n- The character is unconscious, carried, resting, talking, looking around, traveling, shopping, investigating, or doing ordinary activity\n- The environment is dangerous but does not explicitly harm the character\n- Combat health damage or healing was already applied upstream\n\n### Blood Magic\nWhen blood magic causes a player to inflict a new wound on their own body in the current story, apply a health loss when the injury happens, even if the working fails. Calculate percentages from maximum health and round to the nearest whole point.\n\n- A pinprick, token drop, or blood already spilled: 0.\n- A deliberate cut, opened vein, or comparable wound: -10% maximum health.\n- Deep bloodletting or a major bodily sacrifice: -20% maximum health.\n- A catastrophic sacrifice that endangers the caster’s life: -35% maximum health.\n\nDo not deduct health merely because blood magic is used, causes pain, or works through another creature’s blood.\n\nWhen a successful blood-magic working explicitly drains life from an unwilling living foe into the player, restore 10% maximum health, or 20% for a major draining working that clearly establishes the stronger recovery. Never infer healing from every blood-magic attack, and never restore health from a failed working or a nonliving target."
    },
    "mana": {
      "name": "mana",
      "initialValue": 30,
      "maxValue": 30,
      "rechargeRate": 2,
      "restRechargeMultiplier": 1,
      "color": "#4D96FF",
      "gainPerLevel": 3,
      "usageInstructions": "### Mana Changes\nMana is spent only when a character actually casts a spell, channels magical power, uses a magical ability, performs a ritual, or pays an explicit magical cost.\n\nDo not spend mana for thinking about magic, studying, knowing magical lore, using mundane skills, ordinary conversation, ordinary travel, or using an enchanted item that clearly has its own power source.\n\nMana costs are flat numbers. They do not scale with maximum mana:\n- Trivial magic, such as lighting a candle, sensing nearby magic, minor telekinesis, or a small illusion: 0 to -3\n- Moderate magic, such as combat spells, healing wounds, useful illusions, small summons, or magical barriers: -15\n- Major magic, such as large-scale destruction, major healing, terrain reshaping, powerful summons, or breaking curses: -30\n- Legendary magic, such as world-altering effects, mass resurrection, or binding powerful entities: -50\n\nA caster with a class grant holds roughly 60 to 90 mana at level 1, so moderate spells run out after about five castings. A larger pool buys more spells, not cheaper ones. Never convert these costs to a percentage of maximum.\n\nJudge by what the story describes, not by the skill name. A fire mage lighting a campfire is trivial. A fire mage calling down a firestorm is major. The same skill at different scales costs different amounts.\n\nFailed spells can still cost mana if power was drawn and spent. If the spell never truly starts, do not charge mana.\n\nNatural mana recovery:\n- When the current story turn is not active combat and mana is below max, emit a mana gain of about 50% of maximum mana, capped at full.\n- Do not restore natural mana during active combat unless an item, ability, spell, or explicit effect restores it.\n\nRecovery during combat:\n- A Mana Philter gives 15 mana. A Greater Mana Philter gives 30. For a restorative without one of these family names, estimate a lower value.\n- Deliberately centering and drawing ambient power while not under direct attack restores about 20% of maximum and uses the action.\n\nA magic spell costs mana, not stamina, unless it is also clearly a physical combat maneuver.",
      "isHealth": false
    },
    "stamina": {
      "name": "stamina",
      "initialValue": 30,
      "maxValue": 30,
      "rechargeRate": 2,
      "restRechargeMultiplier": 1,
      "color": "#52DB48",
      "gainPerLevel": 3,
      "usageInstructions": "### Stamina Changes\nStamina is a combat resource. Spend it for fighting, defense, wrestling, and physical combat abilities. Do not spend it for ordinary noncombat activity.\n\nOnly spend stamina for active combat or combat-pressure physical actions:\n- attacks, weapon exchanges, flurries, heavy swings\n- blocks, parries, dodges, evasive combat movement, shield-bracing\n- grapples, wrestling, shoves, tackles, breaking free in a fight\n- physical combat abilities or effects that explicitly cost stamina\n\nDo not spend stamina for noncombat activity:\n- travel, walking, climbing, jumping, swimming, running short distances, talking\n- investigating, searching, shopping, crafting, social scenes, routine exploration\n- normal physical effort outside combat, even if the narration says it is tiring\n\nNoncombat exertion can be described in the story, but do not output a stamina change unless an explicit item, ability, hazard, curse, or world rule says stamina is lost.\n\nCombat stamina costs are flat numbers. They do not scale with maximum stamina:\n- Fast strike, reposition, or combat movement: -2 to -3\n- Standard attack, block, parry, dodge, grapple, or weapon exchange: -5\n- Heavy attack, desperate defense, wrestling contest, or physical combat ability: -10\n- Maximum effort, all-out exertion, or an explicit high-cost ability: -15 to -20\n\nA baseline character has about 40 stamina, so a character who keeps committing is nearly empty by the fifth turn. A larger pool buys more actions, not more expensive ones. Never convert these costs to a percentage of maximum.\n\nNatural stamina recovery:\n- When the current story turn is not active combat and stamina is below max, emit a stamina gain of about 50% of maximum stamina, capped at full.\n- The player does not need to say \"I rest\" to recover normal combat stamina after a fight.\n- Do not restore natural stamina during active combat unless an item, ability, spell, or explicit effect restores it.\n\nRecovery during combat:\n- A Stamina Draught gives 15 stamina. A Greater Stamina Draught gives 30. For a restorative without one of these family names, estimate a lower value.\n- Deliberately catching breath while not under direct attack restores about 20% of maximum and uses the action.\n\nExplicit stamina restoration:\n- If a potion, item, ability, blessing, spell, or other effect explicitly restores stamina, apply the stated restoration even in combat.\n- If no amount is stated, estimate conservatively from effect strength.\n\nExamples:\n- Social or exploration turn: no stamina loss.\n- Travel, walking, climbing, searching, crafting, shopping, or talking: no stamina loss.\n- Standard combat attack, dodge, block, or grapple: -5 stamina.\n- Heavy weapon burst or desperate defense: -10 stamina.\n- Combat ends and the next turn is peaceful: restore about 50% max stamina.\n- Following peaceful turn: restore remaining stamina or bring it near full.\n- Stamina Draught: +15 stamina. Greater Stamina Draught: +30 stamina.\n- Magic spell: mana cost, not stamina, unless it is also a physical combat move.",
      "isHealth": false
    },
    "tarnish": {
      "name": "tarnish",
      "initialValue": 0,
      "maxValue": 100,
      "rechargeRate": 0,
      "restRechargeMultiplier": 0,
      "color": "#7A8378",
      "gainPerLevel": 0,
      "usageInstructions": "### Tarnish Changes\nTarnish is spiritual grey-rot: the mark left by the Shadelands, necromantic overreach, and bargains with the dead country. It moves rarely. Change it only when the current story explicitly completes one of the causes below. Never infer Tarnish from atmosphere, proximity, danger, ordinary adventuring, combat, or ordinary spellcasting.\n\nGain Tarnish only when the story shows:\n- A player enters the Shadelands unwarded: +5. After a materially prolonged continuous stay, add up to +5 more once. Do not charge again each turn merely because the scene remains there.\n- A player completes a bargain with a demon, shade, or power of the dead country: +5 for a limited bargain, +10 for a grave or lasting bargain, or +15 for a soul-binding price.\n- A player deliberately invokes the named power of a cursed or bargained item: +5 once for each invocation that actually occurs. Carrying, wearing, drawing, examining, receiving passive bonuses from, or making an ordinary attack with the item does not count. If the power is only discussed, considered, or attempted without being called, do not add Tarnish.\n- A player commits necromantic overreach—raising beyond their safe strength or deliberately renewing a servant whose term has come due: +3 to +8 once for that act. Do not charge every subsequent turn merely because the servant remains.\n- A named creature or effect explicitly inflicts Tarnish: use its stated amount.\n\nTarnish does not fall on its own. Reduce it only when the current story explicitly completes a cleansing: an unbinding rite, a consecrated shrine, a meaningful amends, or another named cleansing. Reduce it by 10 to 30 according to the depth and price of the cleansing. A successful Sunder the Binding aimed at accumulated Tarnish follows the same 10-to-30 limit; only an exceptional authored rite, shrine, or sacrifice may clear the meter completely.\n\nNever reduce Tarnish for rest, time, healing magic, or potions. Never raise it merely for fighting demons or undead—only for treating with them, calling on their power, or entering their country."
    }
