      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Pernilla Balewood's moonbloom pie has reached the judging table at Hillhome's Newmoon Fair. The party recovered her truffle-hog Phineas, hunted the prize truffle, and helped supply the pie. Percival Goldspoon has publicly promised to cancel the Saucy Chicken Tavern's debt and relinquish his family's claim if Pernilla's pie beats his. He has authority to bind the Goldspoons to that promise.\n\nSuggested turn 1 — The entries. Let familiar villagers react to the pie and visible traces of the kitchen scramble. Percival is beautifully presented and loudly confident about his expensive ingredients. Give the party room to answer, present the pie, or enjoy the spectacle.\n\nSuggested turn 2 — The tasting. Build anticipation through the judges and the village audience. Pernilla's pie wins. Phineas's ripe moonbloom and the party's work make the difference, however ridiculous the pastry looks. Let the announcement and reactions land before settling the wager.\n\nSuggested turn 3 — The promise kept. Witnesses hold Percival to his words. He protests and tries to preserve his dignity, then publicly cancels the debt and relinquishes the Goldspoons' claim. Make the result plain: the Saucy Chicken remains the Balewoods' tavern.\n\nComplete only after both the victory and the release of the claim have been narrated. Remain at the Fairground; the party chooses where to go afterward.",
