    "commoner_start_ordinary_day_breaks": {
      "title": "An Ordinary Day Breaks",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Have one unexpected event occur. Make it concrete, immediate, and particular to the current settlement or area. It should change what is happening now and open a direction the player could pursue, resist, exploit, or ignore.\n\nChoose one fitting possibility or invent another: a visitor arrives with a surprising purpose; an old friend or rival returns unexpectedly changed; a missing villager walks home; a noble arrives on an exotic mount; traveling performers begin setting up; an adventuring company limps back from a failed expedition; a foreign caravan seeks shelter; a wounded messenger arrives; a fugitive asks to be hidden; a peaceful monster enters the village; a ghost appears in daylight; someone announces a festival, feast, dance, fair, race, tournament, hunt, or contest; a public dare or wager is offered; a wedding or engagement is announced, interrupted, or abandoned; a feud erupts; a startling confession is made; a crime is committed; stolen goods appear in the player's possession; a prisoner escapes; someone is publicly accused; the player is mistaken for a criminal, hero, noble, champion, or expected guest; the player receives an unexpected payment, inheritance, gift, valuable animal, or other windfall; something valuable is uncovered during ordinary work; a rare egg hatches; an unusual creature chooses to follow someone; a noble's prized beast escapes; livestock stampede through a gathering; a dangerous animal enters the village; a magical experiment goes wrong; a young person's magic appears for the first time; an enchanted object awakens; an ordinary object speaks; a door appears where none stood before; a well, stream, tree, or standing stone reveals something impossible; something falls from the sky; the weather changes impossibly; a familiar landmark moves or transforms; a fey revel crosses the settlement; a masked procession arrives; a merchant offers an extraordinary bargain; a letter or invitation reaches the wrong person; a stranger proposes a risky partnership; a local announces an audacious plan; a celebration produces an unexpected winner; an important visitor disappears during their arrival; two strangers begin a magical duel; or someone believed dead returns with an extraordinary story.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "grell_of_bores_isadora_followup": {
      "title": "The Voice Below",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Use two short scene movements. First, let the immediate aftermath breathe. Show the fish seller emerging from the shop, residents reacting to the wrecked stall, and the woman coming down from the roof to check that nobody nearby needs help. Let the party say what happened or ask about the voice. Have the woman answer the immediate question plainly: the lizard was listening to someone, and the voice is connected to a larger pattern she has been tracking. Have her thumb the worn brass god-token on her bracer and make her worldview clear: \"No coincidence. A screaming voice, a rampaging lizard, and a crew already in the street? The gods have put a sign in front of us. I don't ignore signs.\" Keep this first movement about the scene in front of the party.\n\nAfter the party has had room to respond, have the woman identify herself as Isadora. She explains that she was tracking disturbances connected to what lies below Ardan's Watch. Under Silvervale is another world of caverns, buried roads, and old cities; most people leave it alone, but kobold raids have grown more frequent and an old lower route has collapsed. She has a kobold source named Baska who refuses to come topside. Baska has asked surface adventurers to meet her below at the Imperial Shrine to Avarice and see what is changing for themselves.\n\nHave Isadora make the recruitment logic plain: \"The gods have put a problem in front of me, and put you in the street when it happened. I intend to follow the sign. I need a crew to go below with me and make contact with Baska. One hundred florins for each of you. A sign is an invitation, not a command.\" Have her state the route plainly: the only way down from Ardan's Watch into Grell Hollows is the Delvers' Lift, reached through Old Town and the Basement; it descends to The Scrabble. She will accompany the party and take them to the Lift when they are ready.\n\nComplete this event when the immediate aftermath has been shown, Isadora's faith and reason for recruiting the party have been made clear, the lizard incident has been connected to trouble below Ardan's Watch, Baska and the meeting place have been introduced, the route through Old Town and the Delvers' Lift has been stated, and Isadora's offer is on the table. If the party leaves before the offer is finished, close the event without repeating the scene; leave the offer available when the party returns to Old Town.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "grell_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "investigation_offered"
        },
        {
          "type": "quest-init",
