    "dq_muckside_dull_knives": {
      "title": "Muckside: The Dull Knives",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "A gang called the Dull Knives holds the Way Down: a cracked stone ramp under a collapsed counting-house, the only dry descent into the Muckwarren anyone local will admit exists.\n\nThey are Muckside locals, not Pyre cultists: Hask Drown-Tongue, a half-orc brute with a boat-hook and a voice like wet gravel; two ratkin brothers with crossbows up in the broken rafters; and a handful of hard-faced river trash with knives, cudgels, and lantern-oil bottles. They hate outsiders, hate the clean city across the river, and hate anyone who comes down here acting like Muckside owes them answers.\n\nHask blocks the ramp and makes it ugly. He jokes that the party will come out softer after the sump-leeches have kissed the marrow, and his people laugh like they have heard men scream down there before. He does not say who paid him, but he keeps glancing toward the dark below when the Pyre is mentioned, and one of his crew is plainly more frightened of the underways than of the party.\n\nRun this as a hard gate, not a polite conversation. The Dull Knives try to keep the high ground and the exit: crossbows from above, knife-fighters pressing close, oil thrown to make fire and smoke on the ramp. They want the party beaten, robbed, and driven off, not honorably killed.\n\nIf they start losing, Hask orders the ramp fired and tries to collapse loose masonry across the Way Down, trapping the party above or forcing a dangerous descent through smoke and falling stone. If Hask falls or breaks, the gang's courage goes with him: the ratkin bolt first, the others scatter, and one frightened survivor can be made to point the way into the Muckwarren.\n\nThe challenge is over only when the party gets past the Dull Knives and down into the underways: by breaking them, frightening them off, turning one of them, sneaking through, forcing another entrance open, or surviving the attempted collapse.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "dq_muckside_sump_leech": {
      "title": "Muckside: The Chained Sump-Leech",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Past the Dull Knives' way down, the underways narrow into a drowned brick passage ending at an old plague-door: iron-banded stone, swollen with age, built to seal the crypt road below Muckside.\n\nThe door is guarded by the thing the Dull Knives joked about. A gargantuan pale sump-leech fills the flooded trench before it, thick as a wagon and long enough that most of its body vanishes back into the black water. Old iron bands have been sunk around its body, with heavy chains running from the bands to wall-rings on either side of the passage. The chains are long enough for it to reach the whole flooded floor before the door. Bones, boots, and half-dissolved gear show how it has been fed.\n\nIt is not loyal to anyone. It is hungry, hurt, and trained by pain. A hooked prod and a bucket of salted offal lie on a high dry ledge where someone has been driving it back from the door when cultists need to pass.\n\nRun this as a physical obstacle with danger, not a simple monster fight. The leech surges from the water with shocking speed, trying to fasten on and drag someone under. The flooded trench makes footing bad. Anyone who falls or gets pulled in is in real trouble. The chains can be used against it, cut, jammed, shortened, or broken. The prod, salt, fire, blood, noise, or food can distract or drive it. A clever party can slip past, calm it, free it, kill it, or turn it loose behind them.\n\nIf the party handles it badly, the leech thrashes hard enough to crack the old brickwork and flood the passage deeper, making the door harder to reach and the fight uglier. If it is badly wounded or freed, it panics and tears at anything nearby, including the chains, the ledge, and the door fittings.\n\nThe challenge is over only when the party gets past the chained leech and opens or bypasses the plague-door. Beyond it, the air turns dry and dead, and the old plague crypts begin.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "dq_muckside_plague_ward": {
      "title": "Muckside: The Plague-Crypt Ward",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Beyond the plague-door, the water and rot fall away at once. The party enters the old plague crypts, dry as bone and sealed off from the living city for generations. Niches line the walls, still occupied. The air is dead, powdery, and close, and every sound carries too far.\n\nThe route deeper ends at a sealed archway of iron-banded stone. Faded civic ward-signs cover it from floor to lintel, old protection marks raised to keep the plague dead from being carried across the river to the City of Ancestors. A bronze guardian statue stands before the arch, broad and faceless, holding a staff in each hand across the sealed way.\n\nThe Pyre cult has not broken this ward. They have learned how to pass it. Fresh heat-marks on the stone, wax drips from red candles, and a line of burned handprints suggest the cultists use a rite or phrase to make the guardian stand aside. The old ward is still doing its original work; the cult is exploiting it.\n\nRun this as a ward-and-guardian challenge. The party can study the signs, reconstruct the old civic warding, search for the cult's bypass rite, trick the guardian, dispel or break the magic, disable the statue, or fight it if it wakes. Rough tampering should animate the guardian: bronze joints grinding, both staffs lowering, the statue moving with slow crushing force to drive intruders back from the arch.\n\nThe guardian is not evil and not clever. It is an old protective machine obeying an old command. It blocks, crushes, shoves, and pins; it tries to force intruders away from the arch rather than chase them through the crypts. Radiant or civic authority symbols may confuse it; Pyre fire-marks may briefly command it; brute force can break it, but loudly and dangerously.\n\nThe challenge is over only when the party gets through the sealed arch. Beyond it, the crypt walls descend toward the older Hall of Skulls, and heat begins to breathe up from below.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "dq_muckside_altar_chamber": {
      "title": "Muckside: The Pyre Altar",
      "targetTurns": 4,
      "beats": "Beyond the sealed arch, the crypt passage descends into older stone. The air grows hotter with every step. The walls change from plague niches to stacked skull-pillars and old sarcophagi worked into the supports. Red light pulses from below.\n\nThe party reaches the Hall of Skulls and finds what the Pyre has made of it. A stone altar stands at the center of the vast crypt, carved over with fire-sigils and split by a vertical seam of living flame. An iron ring of braziers surrounds it, each brazier chained to a skull-pillar. Smoke climbs upward through cracks in the ceiling, feeding the false-fire ward above Muckside and over the roads to the City of Ancestors.\n\nBrandrek the Ashen leads the rite. He is calm, gaunt, and intense, with burned hands folded over a fire-blackened blade. Two Pyre cultists tend the braziers and keep the working alive. Brandrek does not rant. He speaks with terrible certainty: Ardan's Watch is corrupt, the dead city is sealed for a reason, and the Dragon Queen's fire will make the old world clean.\n\nRun this as a boss encounter built around the altar. When the party enters, the braziers flare and a ring of fire seals the chamber. Brandrek calls a fire elemental out of the altar seam. The cultists try to keep the braziers burning, relight any that go out, and throw themselves between the party and the altar. Brandrek fights to hold attention and punish anyone who goes for the working.\n\nThe altar is the real objective. Defeating Brandrek does not end the threat if the altar still burns. The party can destroy the altar by breaking the braziers, severing the chains, disrupting the fire-sigils, turning the elemental against the seam, using water, stone, or warding magic, or physically cracking the altar while the rite is unstable.\n\nIf the fight goes badly for the cult, Brandrek tries to overfeed the altar, making the chamber hotter and more dangerous in order to finish the burn or kill everyone inside. If Brandrek falls, the cultists panic unless the altar is still clearly winning. If the braziers are broken or the altar seam is disrupted, the false-fire working begins to collapse.\n\nThe challenge is over only when the Pyre altar is permanently destroyed, extinguished, purified, shattered, disabled, or otherwise made unable to feed the ward. When it falls, the fire in the chamber is sucked inward, the smoke above Muckside dies, and the way toward the City of Ancestors can finally stay open.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "dq_muckside_altar_reached",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "sdm_summons": {
      "title": "Seven Dead: The Summons",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "At the rim of the Pit of Shadow, Iliora the Veiled addresses the gathered Inquisitors: something has broken loose, and the whole order has been called for it.\n\nShe turns to the Pit. A faint image appears above the dark shaft: first one distant figure, then another, until seven dim images hang in the air like glimpses seen through deep water. They are only images shown by the Pit; no body, shade, voice, or physical presence enters the chamber. Iliora names each as it appears: Vortigar the Unburied, the warlord who refused the grave, who empties battlefields and graveyards and marshals the risen into armies to refight the wars he lost. Sethelle of the Nine Sorrows, the great lady whose grief outlived her. Karneth Draghmar. The Lady Mavrocine. Ossarine the Pale Crown. Belugar Thoth-Amon. Druathyr the Thrice-Drowned.\n\nSeven of the mighty dead — lords and sorcerers who served the demon-princes of Ur in the age of the Fourth Empire. Long ago they were stopped, put down, and bound to the Shadelands, the land of the restless dead. Now they have crossed over again. Iliora states plainly: 'We do not know where they are - only that they have crossed over, somewhere.'\n\nLet the gathered Inquisitors react — grim recognition, fear mastered, old grudges surfacing — but keep the scene at the Pit and keep Iliora in command of it. The seven faint images remain only as distant signs above the Pit and fade when she is done naming them; they are not tracked characters, arrivals, or people the party can interact with in this chamber.\n\nThis event is complete when all seven of the named dead have been named before the gathered order and Iliora has made clear that their locations are unknown.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "sdm_charge"
        }
      ]
    },
    "sdm_charge": {
      "title": "Seven Dead: The Charge",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Still at the Pit of Shadow, Iliora the Veiled tells the order what the return of the Seven means and what must be done: the Seven must be hunted down and ended — each by the means its nature demands — and the dead they leave rising in their wake must be put back to rest. This is the work of the whole order now; every full Inquisitor will carry a piece of it. Initiates will support the work through lesser cases until they have proven themselves and undergone the Rite of Bonding.\n\nQuestions and reactions from the gathered Inquisitors are welcome; Iliora answers plainly and without comfort. Keep the scene at the Pit.\n\nThis event is complete when the order's charge — hunt the Seven, and settle the risen dead they leave behind — has been stated plainly to the gathering.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "sdm_dismissal"
        }
      ]
    },
    "sdm_dismissal": {
      "title": "Seven Dead: The Dismissal",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "The gathering breaks and the Inquisitors disperse to their tasks. As they do, Vesna Calloway claps the player on the shoulder and tells them the truth without ceremony: hunting the Seven will have to wait, because the player is not ready — that is Inquisitor work, and the player is an initiate. For the time being, Jett has asked for help with smaller related threats in and around Drusavia; the player can find him in the Hall of Shadow for work.\n\nEnd with the player free to move and act as they choose.\n\nThis event is complete when Vesna has redirected the player to Jett in the Hall of Shadow and the gathering has dispersed.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "quest-init",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "Complete Your Initiate Trial"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "See Jett in the Hall of Shadow for work.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_great_hall_practica_board": {
      "title": "Tarnholme: The Board Crowd",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "The Great Hall is in its welcome-dinner and board-crowd mode: food, noise, College tables, newcomers in the center, older students circling, and masters watching. The Practica board draws students because it is where work, rank, gossip, rivalry, and College courtship meet.\n\nStage a short social spark around the board. A few lively students from different Colleges debate which upper students are bold enough to take hard posted work, tease each other about College styles, and size up the newcomers. Keep it character-first, not a list of jobs.\n\nEnd with the player still in the Great Hall, able to approach a student, ask about a College, inspect one posting, talk to a master, meet other newcomers, or keep enjoying the dinner.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academyGreatHallOrientationDone",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Stay in the Great Hall, meet the students around the Practica board, and decide who to talk to next.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_bastion_first_visit": {
      "title": "The Bastion: Sentinel Eyes",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the player's first Academy visit to the Bastion, make the College of Sentinels feel alive and recruiting without forcing a choice. The route from the Ringstead crosses stepping-stones, swaying spans, and leaping-stones over the green shallows, while Sentinel students shove, ward, dodge, and sometimes dump each other into the Tarn for educational reasons.\n\nHave a Sentinel student or junior mentor notice the newcomer and offer a quick test: cross a span under warded gusts, stand three touches in a practice ring, hold a shield while someone tries to break focus, or ride harnessed on a patient sky-skate. Keep it playful, physical, and public.\n\nThe pitch should be plain: Sentinels are the ones everyone calls when something has gone wrong and needs wards raised fast. If the player shows nerve, steadiness, combat sense, protective instinct, or the ability to laugh after landing wet, someone says in-world that the Bastion could use a student like that.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academySentinelsCourted",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_wonderworks_first_visit": {
      "title": "The Wonderworks: Bad Parts, Good Hands",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the player's first Academy visit to the Wonderworks, make the College of Shapers feel alive and recruiting without forcing a choice. The Ringyard is cluttered with crates, wet workbenches, abandoned frames, and half-finished constructs; above the Piece Pit, the Claw deals out a bad hand of movers, grippers, eyes, cores, and oddments for the day's challenge.\n\nHave a Shaper student or junior mentor pull the newcomer into a small build problem: make a useless-looking heap cross a line, carry a prize, climb a stair, sort a pile, or survive a rival's legal sabotage. The rules are clear: loose theft is a foul, but a part stolen and attached immediately is fair play.\n\nThe pitch should be plain: Shapers build the devices the Academy depends on, and they win by making impossible pieces useful before anyone else has finished complaining. If the player shows invention, practical craft, comfort with tools, or delight in ugly solutions, someone says in-world that the Wonderworks could use a student like that.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academyShapersCourted",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_menagerie_first_visit": {
      "title": "The Menagerie: A Thing That Listens",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the player's first Academy visit to the Menagerie, make the College of Binders feel alive and recruiting without forcing a choice. The Bound Ring is marked with double iron circles, the Habitats hold little sealed climates, and the Run waits below the galleries with the day's creature-class and task chalked above it.\n\nHave a Binder student or junior mentor offer a controlled test with safeguards already in place: speak a command through a circle, coax a minor elemental across the wrong terrain, persuade a proud little creature to fetch instead of preen, identify what a called thing wants, or help close a slipping binding before it becomes embarrassing.\n\nThe pitch should be plain: Binders do not merely blast dangerous things. They call, bind, bargain, command, and banish; they make the frightening thing listen and send it home whole. If the player shows nerve, respect for boundaries, command, empathy, bargaining instinct, or precision under pressure, someone says in-world that the Menagerie could use a student like that.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academyBindersCourted",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_stacks_first_visit": {
      "title": "The Stacks: A Question With Legs",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the player's first Academy visit to the Stacks, make the College of Seekers feel alive and recruiting without forcing a choice. The Ringstead opens beneath weathered librarian statues, the Lower Galleries climb away in shelf-canyons and bridges, and students hurry between reading-courts with books, maps, copied inscriptions, and arguments.\n\nHave a Seeker student, librarian statue, or junior mentor hand the newcomer a question that points outward: identify a charm-mark, name the thing that left a tooth, translate a warning, match a torn map to a place, find which book knows a curse, or spot the missing premise in a confident student's theory. The answer should lead somewhere practical, not end as trivia.\n\nThe pitch should be plain: Seekers turn mysteries into directions. The useful answer tells someone where to go next and what to fear when they get there. If the player shows curiosity, pattern sense, skepticism, field-minded scholarship, or hunger for the real answer, someone says in-world that the Stacks could use a student like that.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academySeekersCourted",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "academy_mend_first_visit": {
      "title": "The Mend: Who Needs Help First",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the player's first Academy visit to the Mend, make the College of Menders feel alive and recruiting without forcing a choice. The Garland Arch opens onto lawns and warm pools, students from every College soak bruises and spell-burns, the Long Table is always laid, and the Quiet Ward waits behind a watched gate for dangerous cases.\n\nHave a Mender student or junior mentor test the newcomer gently but seriously: notice who around the pools is hiding pain, choose which patient needs help first, identify whether a wound is physical, cursed, poisoned, or still magically moving, or steady someone frightened by a harmless but uncanny side effect.\n\nThe pitch should be plain: Menders close wounds, draw poison, break curses, and treat the strange harms magic leaves behind. Their first discipline is noticing the hurt person before the impressive spell. If the player shows care, triage, patience, courage around strange injuries, or refusal to treat a rival as less deserving, someone says in-world that the Mend could use a student like that.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "academyMendersCourted",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "occasion_open_arrival": {
      "title": "Occasion: Something Is Happening Today",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "The party has just arrived in this place, and today, something is happening here. Choose a public occasion suited to this location, drawn from what its own character and description would plausibly hold: a festival, a market day, a contest or race, an arena bout, an election or moot, a wedding, a launching, a returning hero, an auction, a seasonal rite. It should be the kind of thing an outsider could attend and would be curious to attend, and it should already be underway or visibly imminent — decorations going up, crowds drifting one way, criers announcing, wagers being laid.\n\nIntroduce exactly one named local with a visible want connected to the occasion — a competitor who needs a partner, a vendor short-handed on the biggest day of the year, an official trying to keep order, a proud parent, a bettor in too deep, a performer missing something. A stranger with an agenda, friendly and specific, whose want the party could help with, ignore, or complicate. They should speak to the party or within earshot of them.\n\nMake it plain, diegetically, when the occasion's main moment will happen: the final heat at noon, the vote at sundown, the bout after the drums.\n\nTone rules for the whole occasion: it invites and never obligates. No quest offers, no forced participation, no danger, no crisis. The party may ignore all of it and do something else entirely; that is a fully supported choice. If the party leaves the location, let the occasion recede without comment and wrap up offscreen.\n\nThis event is complete when the occasion has been established in the narration and the named local has spoken to the party or within their earshot.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "occasion_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "main"
        },
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "occasion_main"
        }
      ]
    },
    "occasion_open_homecoming": {
      "title": "Occasion: Home Has Its Own Plans",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "The party has just come home to this place after time away, and today, something is happening here. Choose an occasion suited to this location, drawn from what its own character and description would plausibly hold: a fair, a harvest, a wedding, a moot, a market day, a launching, a contest, a funeral feast, a seasonal rite. It should be the kind of thing the whole place turns out for — and the kind of thing a returnee is immediately expected to join.\n\nIntroduce exactly one named local with a visible want connected to the occasion — family or an old friend of a party member. Their want should be warm and personal: a hand needed with the preparations, a seat saved at the table, an old role the returnee is expected to resume, a piece of news they have been saving, a small worry they've been carrying. Let the region's chronic pressures color their talk if it fits — thin catches, mine trouble, a hard season — as background and conversation, never as an attack or a crisis. They should speak to the party directly; they know them.\n\nMake it plain, diegetically, when the occasion's main moment will happen.\n\nTone rules for the whole occasion: it invites and never obligates. No quest offers, no forced participation, no danger, no crisis. The party may ignore all of it and do something else entirely; that is a fully supported choice. If the party leaves the location, let the occasion recede without comment and wrap up offscreen.\n\nThis event is complete when the homecoming and the occasion have been established in the narration and the named local has spoken with the party.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "occasion_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "main"
        },
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "occasion_main"
        }
      ]
    },
    "occasion_main": {
      "title": "Occasion: The Main Moment",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "The local occasion established earlier reaches its main moment, around the party, whether or not they take part: the race is run, the vote is called, the bout is fought, the feast is served, the rite is performed. Stage it as living background if the party is busy elsewhere in the location, or as the scene itself if they have joined in.\n\nThe main moment produces exactly one visible upset, decision, or change that bystanders react to: an unexpected winner, a scandal, an announcement nobody saw coming, a reversal of fortune, a public refusal, a surprise arrival. Keep it local in scale — this is the place's story, not the world's.\n\nIf the party engaged with the named local and their want, weave that want through the upset — helped, thwarted, or transformed by it. If the party ignored them, show that person acting on their want anyway, and let it succeed or fail on its own terms.\n\nTone rules for the whole occasion: it invites and never obligates. No quest offers, no forced participation, no danger, no crisis. The party may ignore all of it and do something else entirely; that is a fully supported choice. If the party leaves the location, let the occasion recede without comment and wrap up offscreen.\n\nThis event is complete when the occasion's main moment has occurred in the narration and its upset or outcome has been shown.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "occasion_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "aftermath"
        },
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "occasion_aftermath"
        }
      ]
    },
    "occasion_aftermath": {
      "title": "Occasion: The Place Exhales",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "The occasion winds down and the place exhales. Show the consequences of the main moment's upset in small, concrete ways: who is celebrating, who is sulking, what is being packed up or repaired, what people are saying about it. The named local's business resolves on-screen — with the party's help acknowledged if they gave it, or without them if they didn't.\n\nEnd with one soft open door: a single thing the party could look into if they chose, arising naturally from the occasion or the upset — phrased as world texture (a rumor, a departure, a curiosity, an unanswered question), never as a job offer or a request. Do not press it; mention it once and let it lie.\n\nTone rules for the whole occasion: it invites and never obligates. No quest offers, no forced participation, no danger, no crisis. The party may ignore all of it and do something else entirely; that is a fully supported choice. If the party leaves the location, let the occasion recede without comment and wrap up offscreen.\n\nThis event is complete when the occasion has wound down and ordinary life has resumed around the party.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "occasion_done",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "occasion_active",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "house_work_offer": {
      "title": "Work for the House",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the player's adventuring House, use the authored Gold-ranked veteran who belongs here: Hugh Merrivant at the Wildward House, Tula Vereskar at the House of Many Banners, Calia Delassar at Sky-River House, or Rielle Mazarane at Skygate House. Do not replace the veteran with a generated keeper or another dispatcher.\n\nHave the veteran introduce one named external client or bring forward one concrete request from a named person. Fit it closely to the actual city, region, nearby locations, factions, and the veteran's established knowledge. During the proving stage, the work should be serious, exciting, and reasonably achievable by a new company. After the standard has been raised, it may carry larger regional stakes. If the House is renowned, ordinary offers may be difficult and consequential, but only a separately established defining commission can complete the House's legacy.\n\nState the exact place, the active danger or opportunity, the people threatened or interested, what is known, what decisive success means, and a concrete offered reward. The reward may be an exact amount of coin, a specifically named useful item, access, instruction, transport, materials, or another practical benefit. Make the request field adventure, dangerous investigation, rescue, protection, recovery, monster or outlaw work, supernatural trouble, exploration, or negotiation under pressure. Never make it routine supplies, food, drink, repairs, accounting, licensing, paperwork, or passive management.\n\nLet the client and veteran answer questions. Present only one offer and do not treat it as accepted. End with the player free to negotiate, accept, decline, seek companions, place another existing mission under the House's banner, or ask for time.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "house_raise_standard": {
      "title": "Raise the Standard",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the player's home House, create a proud, interactive homecoming after its third recognized deed. Use the authored Gold-ranked veteran and the House's established community. Let clients, rescued people, witnesses, trophies, recovered proof, companions, or public consequences from the three actual deeds establish what the company has accomplished. Refer only to deeds that truly occurred.\n\nBring out the House's old League standard and make its return concrete. The veteran explains that the House can field a proven company again and publicly recognizes it as an active League adventuring house. Let the player, their companions, the veteran, or people helped by the company physically raise the standard according to the scene; do not prescribe a speech, attitude, company name, or personal ritual.\n\nIf the player does not already hold Bronze or higher League standing, the veteran also publicly names them Bronze standing as the captain who restored the House. Preserve any higher League standing already held. Present one specifically named piece of useful field gear suited to the player's actual class, equipment, and demonstrated style, stating its exact form, material, and practical property clearly enough for the item system to create it.\n\nKeep the ceremony celebratory, stylish, and socially alive. The veteran remains the House's resident anchor and does not automatically join the traveling party. This event is complete when the House has been formally recognized, its League standard has been raised, and any required Bronze recognition has occurred.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "house_win_renown": {
      "title": "A Name Across the Region",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the player's home House, create an earned homecoming after three further recognized deeds beyond the raising of the League standard. Use the actual deeds and their visible consequences. Show people from beyond the immediate city seeking the House, capable adventurers discussing its company, and serious clients treating its judgment and promises as meaningful.\n\nThe authored Gold-ranked veteran recognizes that the House now carries regional renown. If the player does not already hold Silver or higher League standing, the veteran also publicly names them Silver standing in the same scene. Preserve any higher standing already held. Let one established relationship, companion, regular, patron, rival, or person helped by the company contribute when appropriate. Do not invent a compulsory sponsor, headquarters facility, rival tavern, administrative title, or management problem.\n\nMake clear that ordinary work can continue and Silver-level deeds may now build toward Gold, but the House's next defining step is a major regional commission whose outcome will establish what it is remembered for. Do not choose or begin that commission in this event. This event is complete when the House's regional renown has been clearly established and any required Silver recognition has occurred.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "house_legacy_commission": {
      "title": "The Defining Commission",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the renowned adventuring House, use the authored Gold-ranked veteran to bring forward one defining regional commission. Connect it strongly to the House's city, region, identity, past deeds, established relationships, and unresolved opportunities. It should matter to the region and be worthy of a renowned company, but it must remain playable rather than becoming an immediate world-ending crisis.\n\nIntroduce a named client, faction representative, community, or directly affected person. State the exact location or route, the active threat or opportunity, what is known, the people and interests at stake, what decisive success means, and a concrete reward or lasting access. The commission should admit meaningful choices in method, alliances, priorities, or consequences while retaining a clear field objective.\n\nDo not decide the House's legacy in advance. The legacy must emerge from how the player handles the commission and what visibly changes afterward. Do not begin travel or mark the commission accepted. End with the player free to question, negotiate, accept, decline, seek companions, or postpone it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "house_legacy_homecoming": {
      "title": "The House's Legacy",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the player's home House, create a major interactive return after the defining regional commission has been decisively completed. Ground the scene in the commission's actual outcome, the people affected, the player's choices, and the visible change produced in the region. Do not replace those consequences with a generic celebration.\n\nUse the authored Gold-ranked veteran, established companions, clients, rivals, regulars, and relationships when appropriate. Let the House's community name what this commission revealed about the company and why people will remember it. The player may embrace, challenge, reinterpret, or ignore those judgments; do not prescribe their speech or values.\n\nRecognize the House as legendary in a way suited to the selected region. Its future remains open to further adventures, relationships, recruits, and great commissions; do not retire the company, turn the player into a passive administrator, or imply that its story is over. This event is complete when the defining commission's consequences and the House's lasting legacy have been clearly established.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "tavern_company_founders": {
      "title": "The Adventuring House",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the player's home tavern, let the keeper take the commitment seriously and help turn aspiration into a playable choice. Establish the familiar Larion model briefly: the keeper runs the public house and receives petitioners; the player leads the company in the field. No charter, League approval, founder count, accounting, or routine business plan is required.\n\nAsk naturally what the player wants the company to be called, while allowing them to use the tavern's name or defer with a deliberately provisional name. Do not invent the final name. Ask what kinds of work or principles matter only as an open invitation; do not require a mission statement or prescribe morality.\n\nSurface three competent possible field associates already plausibly in the tavern or introduced by the keeper. Vary species, role, experience, and desired work. Each should be capable of becoming a companion, recurring company member, rival, or useful contact, but none is assigned and the company does not require recruiting any of them before it can exist. Existing party members may be treated as potential founders if appropriate.\n\nEnd with the player able to establish the company under a chosen, tavern-derived, or provisional name; talk further with candidates; invite someone; defer recruitment; or reconsider the project.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "tavern_company_first_client": {
      "title": "The First Client",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "At the newly founded company's home tavern, bring in one named external client with the first request. The keeper has screened them enough to believe the problem is real but does not promise the company's help. Fit the client and job to the actual home region and established local lore.\n\nThe request must require going into the field and be reasonable for a new company: a dangerous local rescue, escort, investigation, missing person, monster or outlaw problem, recovery, threatened route, supernatural disturbance, or tense practical negotiation. It must not be repairing the tavern, pouring drinks, collecting routine supplies, bookkeeping, licensing, or paying ordinary bills. State the place, people at risk or interested, what is known, what successful completion means, and a concrete payment or reward opportunity.\n\nLet the client and keeper respond to questions. If possible, let one previously surfaced associate show interest in the work without automatically joining. End with the offer open and the player free to negotiate, accept, decline, seek companions, or ask for time. Do not begin travel or mark the contract accepted.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "companyOfferOpen",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "tavern_doors_open": {
      "title": "The Old Adventuring House",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "During the first one or two exchanges, let the keeper explain the tavern's dormant legacy plainly and conversationally. This used to be an adventuring house: people brought dangerous problems here, its company went into the field to answer them, and the company returned to these tables afterward. The old company is gone or scattered, so that part of the house has fallen quiet. Do not add a detailed history, tragedy, mystery, debt, enemy, or unfinished contract.\n\nExplain the practical division in equally simple terms. The keeper can continue running the tavern, receiving people who need help, and keeping the house functioning. If the player gathers a company, the player leads its important work in the field. Refounding the house does not require a charter, League approval, a fixed number of founders, routine business management, or serving drinks. A company name, companions, clients, facilities, and reputation can emerge later through play; do not present all of them now.\n\nDo not introduce prospective recruits, a first client, a rival house, an inherited obligation, facility choices, or the full progression ladder in this event. End with the keeper asking plainly whether the player wants to gather a company and make this an adventuring house again. Hearing the explanation is not itself a commitment. The event is complete when the premise and the keeper/player roles are clear and the player has a clean opportunity to answer, ask questions, look around, or decline.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "tavern_ambitions_surfaced",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "tavern_the_incident": {
      "title": "Reopening Night: The Incident",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "One social complication arrives during reopening night, tied to the house's possible future as an adventuring base. Choose what best fits: members of a returning crew dispute credit or payment for a dangerous job; someone claims the previous owner promised help that never came; a rival adventuring house sends a boast, challenge, planted heckler, or attempt to recruit someone in the room; a client publicly accuses a local adventurer of abandoning a contract; or a beloved regular causes a scene about whether the new owner has any right to lead people into danger.\n\nThe room watches how the owner handles it. Give the complication a face, name, and reasonable grievance beneath the bad timing. It may be resolved, deflected, investigated, charmed, negotiated, or left standing. Violence occurs only if the player starts it. Do not turn this into licensing, inventory, drink pricing, supplies, or ordinary business administration.\n\nThis event is complete when the complication has been handled or consciously left unresolved and the room has registered something about the new owner's judgment.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "tavern_last_call"
        }
      ]
    },
    "tavern_last_call": {
      "title": "Reopening Night: Last Call",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "The night settles toward last call. Show who stayed late, which adventurers or locals are still talking, and how the room now regards the owner after the social complication. Do not discuss takings, stock, supplies, or routine management.\n\nAs the keeper closes the room alongside the owner, lay out the house's central possibility: it could become the seat of the player's own free adventuring company. The keeper can receive petitioners and maintain a request wall while the player leads work in the field. The company needs no charter or fixed founder count; it needs the player's commitment, a name when the player is ready to choose one, people willing to ride under it, and a first deed that makes the name mean something. This is an invitation, not an automatic decision.\n\nAlso surface the existing pressures as adventure-facing roads: an old obligation or debt that may be cleared through a dangerous recovery, investigation, escort, or confrontation; a rival adventuring house or company tavern competing for clients, recruits, patrons, and reputation; and the keeper's still-undecided loyalty, which will be earned through leadership, promises kept, people brought home, and what sort of house the player builds.\n\nThis event is complete when the company possibility and those three roads are clear, while the player remains free to pursue, combine, defer, or refuse them.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "tavern_owner",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "tavern_ambitions_surfaced",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        }
      ]
    },
    "castle_harstone_tava_missing_silverblade": {
      "title": "A Missing Silverblade",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "If Tava Rell is here and it is appropriate, have the player overhear a conversation, or be directly involved in one, about a Silverblade who went missing during a Wolvesholt contract. Keep the exchange practical and immediate: someone is asking whether the hunter is late, injured, hiding, or in trouble, and Tava can explain what is known so far or ask whether the player is willing to hear more. Do not force the player to accept the work.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "castle_harstone_tamsin_gate_commission": {
      "title": "The Gate Commission",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "If Tamsin Dain is here and it is appropriate, have the player overhear a conversation, or be directly involved in one, about a serious Order commission being prepared as part of the work leading toward the Gate beneath Castle Harstone. The exchange may concern a dangerous monster contract, a missing hunter, a required specialist, or whether a crew is ready for the next step. Let Tamsin speak plainly about the work and its stakes, but do not force the player to accept a commission.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "tevantia_two_swords_vianne_opportunity": {
      "title": "A Name Worth Backing",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "If Vianne Damarque is here and it is appropriate, have the player overhear or enter a conversation in which she is matching one dangerous opportunity with someone capable of claiming it. Keep the exchange light and specific, with only one opportunity in view. Let Vianne notice the player and ask what kind of deed they would want their name attached to, without assuming their ambition, offering a menu of work, starting a quest, or forcing their involvement.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "arena_bout_call": {
      "title": "The Sanctioned Bout",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Continue from the specific sanctioned arena match the player has accepted. Preserve the already announced opponent or opposing team, exact purse, permitted weapons or magic, victory condition, surrender rules, and any local complication. If any one of those terms was not yet stated, have the arena official state it plainly before the gates open. Do not replace the accepted match with a different hook, chore, delay, investigation, or backstage crisis.\n\nCall the entrants, bring them onto the fighting ground, and let the bout begin. Use the current arena's established character: honorable varied contests at the Field of Valor; formal House sport at Tevantia; sand, heat, and weapon schools at Oasis; cage spectacle and wagering at Gavarra; disciplined judged contests at Caranthoria; rough agreed fights at Gorren Crossing; or wet platforms, nets, teams, and manageable sea creatures at Coriva. Scale all opposition to a fair but meaningful challenge for the fighter's current standing. Never use an impossible enemy or huge swarm.\n\nThe player chooses tactics, conduct, persona, and whether to surrender; do not prescribe their actions, attitude, boasts, mercy, or showmanship. Officials enforce the declared rules. The opponent fights competently and wants to win, but does not become a mortal enemy merely for competing. Complete the event when the bout has decisively ended by its announced terms.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "arena_bout_aftermath": {
      "title": "After the Bout",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Resolve the immediate public aftermath of the sanctioned bout whose outcome was just established. The official names the winner or result, enforces the announced terms, and pays the exact purse or contracted losing share already established for this match. Do not substitute vague coin, praise alone, or a new unrelated reward, and do not treat the opponent's personal equipment as loot. If the performance changed the player's arena standing, have the official or promoter state the new standing plainly.\n\nLet the crowd, opponent, promoter, possible sponsor, or existing rival react in a way grounded in what actually happened, without choosing the player's arena persona for them. A defeat may produce a rematch or training opportunity, but never erase established renown. End with the player free to celebrate, recover, speak with someone present, inspect future matches, or leave.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "arena_title_acclamation": {
      "title": "A Circuit Champion",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Stage the immediate, interactive acclamation after the player has just won a formally declared championship match. Name the exact arena and local title. Have the herald announce the victory, pay the exact championship purse established before the bout, and present a distinctive, usable championship prize shaped by this venue. Name the prize precisely—its item type, material, quality, and any magical or damage aspect—so the item system can create it. The prize may be a weapon, armor piece, focus, fighting implement, or other useful item suited to the player's actual build; do not assume a sword or heavy armor.\n\nMake the venue's culture visible in the award: sacred contest at the Field of Valor, House attention at Tevantia, Red Sands weapon schools, Gavarran wagers and sponsorship, Solarian ceremony, Gorren honor, or Corivan hunting Houses. Sponsorship, patronage, challengers, and stable offers are opportunities, never obligations. Do not prescribe the player's speech or public persona. If this is the player's third distinct circuit championship, have the heralds plainly recognize their eligibility for the Grand Champion's Contest in the Champions' Ring at the Field of Valor.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "arena_grand_invitation": {
      "title": "The Grand Champion’s Contest",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "At the Champions' Ring, have the Tournament Masters publicly recognize that the player holds championships from three distinct eligible circuit arenas. Present the Grand Champion's Contest as an immediate, prestigious opportunity, not an automatic fight: name the proposed opponent or qualifying final, state the exact rules, state a great exact purse of at least 1,500 copper florins, and name the non-coin champion's prize or commission that awaits the victor. The challenge must be severe but winnable and suited to the player's established build and history.\n\nLet prior rivals, sponsors, companions, or representatives of the player's three conquered arenas appear when appropriate, but keep the player central. End with the Masters awaiting acceptance. Do not prescribe the player's answer or begin the contest until they accept.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "arena_grand_champion_acclamation": {
      "title": "Grand Champion of the Arenas",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Stage an epic but interactive acclamation in the Champions' Ring immediately after the player's victory in the Grand Champion's Contest. The Tournament Masters proclaim the player Grand Champion of the Arenas before the gathered circuit heralds, name the three or more championships that brought them here, pay the exact announced purse, and present the already announced champion's prize. If that prize was not previously specific enough, define it now as one singular named Masterwork weapon, armor piece, focus, fighting implement, or other primary adventuring item fitted to the player's actual build, with exact material, magical aspect where appropriate, and a clear powerful property. It must be usable, not merely ceremonial.\n\nMake the new privileges concrete: the Grand Champion may headline at any circuit arena, choose or accept challengers, command the greatest purses, train or sponsor fighters, and establish a stable if desired. These are options, not assigned duties. Let rivals, companions, sponsors, and crowds respond to the actual career the player built. Do not supply the player's speech, emotions, future plans, or arena persona.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "league_champion_council_hearing": {
      "title": "A Seat at the Council",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Stage a public League celebration and Council hearing in a League authority space for the Platinum candidates whose contribution to the completed exceptional commission is being presented. Senior guildmasters, chaptermasters, Council delegates, fellow adventurers, and local witnesses honor what they achieved. Name each qualifying recipient as a League Champion, grant that person a Council seat, and charge them with protecting Larion at the highest level. Companions may be celebrated without receiving the title; preserve their individual standing. A later hearing may recognize a contributor who was not invested earlier. Ground the ceremony in the actual commission and the candidates' history, leaving their words, feelings, and future priorities to them. Complete the event when this hearing concludes.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "leagueChampionHearingComplete",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-clear"
        }
      ]
    },
    "maanvalta_mirelith_request": {
      "title": "Mirelith's Request",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Use two narrative turns. Each turn covers one beat and ends with room for the player's response.\n\nTurn 1 — Mirelith's suspicion: Mirelith explains that she suspects House Drelovar arranged or enabled the attack in the Dims and is using the lockdown to search outsiders, isolate rivals, and control movement. Drelovar has money and ties to powerful outsiders, and is using the threat of surface-elf attacks to control the city.\n\nTurn 2 — The favor and protection: Mirelith asks the player to meet Sethira Drelovar, who married into House Drelovar. There are rumors of tension between Sethira and her husband, so her wish to meet someone from Mirelith's inthelin is promising and probably not a trap. Sethira wants to meet at the Stonetome, Maanvalta's dark-elf museum. Meeting her carries risk, but a good-faith effort earns House Orelain's protection during the crisis: a pass granting movement through the city, useful introductions, lodging at the Hang-Over Inn, and Eris's fine cuisine. End with Mirelith giving the player the path to The Stonetome.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "maanvalta_war_beneath_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "awaiting_sethira_at_stonetome"
        },
        {
          "type": "quest-init",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "What Sethira Knows"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Go to The Stonetome and meet Sethira Drelovar.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "maanvalta_sethira_arrival": {
      "title": "Sethira at the Stonetome",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Use one narrative turn.\n\nAt The Stonetome, show Sethira Drelovar arriving with her attendants. She sends them away, then stands before the stone diorama depicting the high-elven Betrayal and the dark elves' descent toward the Sundering. She waits there alone.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "maanvalta_war_beneath_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "awaiting_sethira_engagement"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Approach Sethira Drelovar when you are ready.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "maanvalta_sethira_information": {
      "title": "What Sethira Knows",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Use two narrative turns. Each turn ends with room for the player's response.\n\nTurn 1 — The warning: Sethira explains that House Drelovar has money and ties to powerful outsiders, and is using the threat of surface-elf attacks to control Maanvalta. She says: “I know enough to suspect, but they treat me as an outsider, not their blood. They'll kill anyone sniffing around, but I can tell you who has grudges against them worse than mine…”\n\nTurn 2 — The name: Sethira names a once-promising scion of House Drelovar who caused a scandal when he chose to join the Unseen, the cross-house brotherhood of spies and assassins. The Unseen have secluded themselves in their island monastery, the Needle, during the crisis. Getting in to see him won't be easy. Sethira explains that she has heard rumors the family considers him a thorn in their side, but dares not act against the Unseen. End with the player needing to find a way into the Needle.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "maanvalta_war_beneath_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "sethira_information_complete"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Stay alert. Someone has found Sethira at the Stonetome.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "maanvalta_sethira_assassination": {
      "title": "The Knife at the Stonetome",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Use one narrative turn.\n\nExactly two dark-elf assassins enter The Stonetome together and move to attack Sethira Drelovar. They carry kris-like, serpentine blades of black steel. Illusion magic shrouds their faces and obscures their features. End when they begin their attack.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-string",
          "key": "maanvalta_war_beneath_stage",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "awaiting_mirelith_reunion"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Resolve the attack on Sethira, then find Mirelith when you can.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "maanvalta_mirelith_recognition": {
      "title": "The Word Is Inthelinni",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Use three narrative turns. Each turn ends with room for the player's response.\n\nTurn 1 — Recognition: when the player next meets Mirelith, she asks what happened at The Stonetome and lets the player's account establish Sethira's fate. She recognizes that the player stood in danger for her friend, even if Sethira died. Mirelith names the player inthelinni, the word for a fellow member of her inthelin, her chosen circle of personal loyalty. She offers the dagger and says the player can join their blood to the inthelin's. End the turn there.\n\nTurn 2 — The rite: if the player accepts, narrate the blood rite briefly. If the player declines, have Mirelith make a light joke about outsiders and their squeamish ways. She remains amused and welcoming.\n\nTurn 3 — The larger path: Mirelith says their ultimate goal is to convince Othrel Namaris, the reclusive leader of House Namaris, that House Drelovar threatens Maanvalta's survival. More than one person's information will be needed to convince Othrel to act, but the lead from the Unseen scion is a good place to start. Complete the event after this objective is clear.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "maanvalta_inthelin_recognized",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
