          "type": "quest-init",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": "Convince Othrel Namaris"
        },
        {
          "type": "party-next-step-set",
          "text": "Gather evidence and a credible path to convince Othrel Namaris that House Drelovar threatens Maanvalta.",
          "source": "objective"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_training_yard_introduction": {
      "title": "Talia Calder's Training Yard",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "On the party's first arrival in the Watchtower - Training Yard, show Talia Calder already there finishing a light footwork drill beside one of the chalked rings. She is young, warm, playful, and confident without being competitive. She notices the party and offers a one-shot League combat tutorial: real combat actions against weak magical constructs, low damage, and Talia's healing after each combat turn. Let her explain that the point is to learn the ropes, not to win a contest. She may say, If you want the League version of the basics, I can walk you through it. The constructs hit for real, just lightly, and I will put you back together after every turn. Do not start combat or the tutorial in this event, assign a quest, or add paperwork, permissions, guards, or other people. End with the offer available for the player to accept, decline, or ask about. Complete once Talia and the offer have been clearly introduced, or the player declines or leaves.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_01_turns": {
      "title": "Lesson One: A Combat Turn",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as the first and only combat turn of the lesson. At the beginning of the turn, Talia Calder must submit real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target, even if a target is already at full health. She gives a brief story explanation that a combat turn is a sequence of choices resolved together: players can attack, defend, evade, use an ability, or direct an ally, and the storyteller reports the result afterward. Before any construct acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Copper Impact Construct by its exact name to make one real, low-force attack against a living player, then tell it to hold. The Copper Impact Construct must submit one real attack intent using its authored low-impact impact attack, around 55 impact. This is real damage, not mockAttack, subdue, or a harmless sparring exchange. Let the player take their turn and do not claim that they succeeded at any particular tactic unless the player's narration and Combat Results make that clear. After the turn, Talia may explain what happened, then wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson before this event completes. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs. If the named construct has already been defeated, have Talia name and order one living authored substitute; if all authored constructs are gone, use one emergent weak training construct with about 50 HP and one low-impact real attack only after Talia explicitly orders it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_02_attack_defend"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_02_attack_defend": {
      "title": "Lesson Two: Attack and Defend",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target, then must add a real defend intent protecting the player or party member most exposed to the coming attack. She explains that a player can attack and defend in the same turn, or attack and evade, and that a declared defense is a choice with a purpose rather than a passive pause. Before it acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Cobalt Frost Construct by its exact name to make one real, low-force attack against a living player, then tell it to hold. The Cobalt Frost Construct must submit one real frost attack intent around 55 impact using its authored ability. Do not use mockAttack, subdue, or narrate a harmless sparring exchange. Let the player attempt either attack plus defend or attack plus evade if they choose. Do not claim the combined tactic happened or worked unless the player's narration and Combat Results support it. After the turn, Talia waits for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if Cobalt is defeated, have Talia name and order another living authored construct, and if all authored constructs are gone use one emergent weak construct with about 50 HP only after she explicitly orders it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_03_damage_types"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_03_damage_types": {
      "title": "Lesson Three: Damage Types",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target. Before either construct acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Amber Fire Construct by its exact name to attack one living player with its real low-impact fire attack around 55 impact, then order the Jade Shock Construct by its exact name to attack one living player with its real low-impact shock attack around 50 impact. These are actual attacks and actual damage, never mockAttack or subdue. Through brief story guidance, Talia explains that damage type is part of an attack and that a target's resistances or vulnerabilities can change the result. She may point out that the Copper Impact Construct's copper frame is unusually vulnerable to shock, but must not promise a result. Invite the player to use an attack or ability whose damage type seems useful, including against the Copper Impact Construct if a suitable shock attack is available, but give general guidance rather than inventing a success not established in the story or Combat Results. Do not claim that the player exploited a type or that either construct was defeated unless the narration and results support it. After the turn, wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if a named construct is defeated, have Talia name and order a living authored substitute while preserving the indicated low-impact real damage type when possible, and if all authored constructs are gone use emergent weak constructs with low-impact real attacks only after explicit orders.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_04_protect_healer"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_04_protect_healer": {
      "title": "Lesson Four: Protect the Healer",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target, then must add a real defend intent targeting the living player character most likely to be the party's healer; if no other healer is present, she may defend herself while explaining the same party role. Before it acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Slate Slashing Construct by its exact name to make one real, low-force attack against a living player, then tell it to hold. The Slate Slashing Construct must submit one real slashing attack intent around 55 impact. Talia teaches that a party is more than a row of attackers: a front-line ally can defend a healer while another ally attacks, and players can tell allied NPCs to protect the healer with a defend-plus-attack plan. Do not invent a party member, healer, command, or successful protection that the player has not narrated. Let the player try the party tactic in story, then wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if Slate is defeated, have Talia name and order another living authored construct with one low-impact real attack, or use an emergent weak construct around 50 HP only after she explicitly orders it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_05_preempt_damage"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_05_preempt_damage": {
      "title": "Lesson Five: Remove the Threat",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target. Before either construct acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Amber Fire Construct by its exact name to attack one living player, then point to and order the Copper Impact Construct by its exact name to attack another living player. Each construct must submit one real low-impact attack intent around 50 to 55 impact using its authored ability. These attacks are real and must not be replaced with mockAttack or subdue. Talia explains that if a player kills an attacking NPC during the turn, the engine can preempt that defeated NPC's queued damage before it reaches the players; this is a lesson about removing a threat, not a guarantee that every attack will be negated. Let the player try to focus down one threat. Do not claim a construct was killed, damage was negated, or the tactic succeeded unless the player's narration and Combat Results establish it. After the turn, wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if either named construct is defeated, have Talia name and order a living authored substitute while preserving two low-impact real attacks where possible, or use emergent weak constructs with about 50 HP only after explicit orders.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_06_area_attacks"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_06_area_attacks": {
      "title": "Lesson Six: Area Attacks",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target. Before any construct acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Amber Fire Construct by its exact name to attack one living player, then point to and order the Jade Shock Construct by its exact name to attack another living player. Each construct must submit one real, low-impact attack intent using its authored ability. Talia explains that one player attack can cover several targets, but the damage is spread out and reduced for each target. Invite the player to attempt one attack against three living named constructs in the same action, preferably the Amber Fire Construct, Copper Impact Construct, and Jade Shock Construct. Do not state an exact damage number or claim that the multi-target tactic succeeded unless the player's narration and Combat Results support it. The incoming attacks are real and must not be replaced with mockAttack or subdue. After the turn, wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if a named construct is defeated, have Talia name and order a living authored substitute while preserving the lesson, or use emergent weak constructs with low-impact real attacks only after explicit orders.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_07_recovery"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_07_recovery": {
      "title": "Lesson Seven: Recover Under Pressure",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target. Before it acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Cobalt Frost Construct by its exact name to make one real, low-force attack against a living player, then tell it to hold. The Cobalt Frost Construct must submit one real frost attack intent around 55 impact using its authored ability. Talia explains that active combat does not restore stamina or mana merely because someone stops attacking. Ask one participant to create space while another deliberately catches breath or draws ambient power. Recovery is possible only while the recovering character is not under direct attack, restores about 20% of the relevant maximum resource, and uses the action. Do not invent a party member or recovery result the player has not narrated. After the turn, wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree to the next lesson. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if Cobalt is defeated, have Talia name and order another living authored construct, or use an emergent weak construct around 50 HP only after she explicitly orders it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "narrative-event-start",
          "eventId": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_08_control"
        }
      ]
    },
    "watchtower_combat_tutorial_08_control": {
      "title": "Lesson Eight: Stop the Attack",
      "targetTurns": 1,
      "beats": "Run this as one combat turn. Talia Calder must begin with real heal intent(s) targeting every living player character she can target, then must add a real preventAttack intent targeting the Frail Piercing Construct. Before it acts, Talia must explicitly point to and order the Frail Piercing Construct by its exact name to make one real, very low-force attack against a living player, then tell it to hold. The Frail Piercing Construct must submit one real piercing attack intent around 45 impact using its authored ability. In her spoken guidance, Talia should tell the player to try to prevent the construct's attack or subdue it to prevent it from acting at all. Do not have Talia say the internal camel-case engine label aloud. Do not claim that a control action worked unless the player's narration and Combat Results support it. The attack remains real and low-impact; do not use mockAttack or narrate harmless sparring. After the turn, wait for the player to say they are ready, ask to continue, or clearly agree that the fundamentals are complete. If the player wants to repeat or practice, keep this event active and do not advance. Use only living constructs; if Frail is defeated, have Talia name and order another living authored construct while preserving one low-impact real attack and the control demonstration, or use an emergent weak construct around 50 HP only after she explicitly orders it.",
      "onCompleteEffects": [
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_complete",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "write-boolean",
          "key": "watchtower_combat_tutorial_started",
          "operator": "set",
          "value": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "dhr_arrival_gate": {
      "title": "The Head Above the Causeway",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Stage the party's arrival at Demon's Head Ruins as a threshold with teeth. First, the approach: the colossal black-marble head of the Demon-Prince leering from the broken wall above the Causeway, black mud and shallow channels between the stones, and fresh sign of occupation such as kobold route-marks, salvage-crew tracks, or a snapped snare line. Then, immediately, a local and manageable hostile contact physically on scene and acting, scaled to the party with at least one enemy per party member or one creature plus support: kobold scouts setting a snare or prying at a door, a drowner pulling itself from a flooded channel, a stirge swarm feeding in the reeds, or a darkmantle dropping from the breach. Let the fight use the mud, reeds, channels, and causeway stones. Before or after the clash, plant one visible promise deeper in: a glint beyond the breach, an armory rack through a fallen arch, or a salvager's note naming the Relland Vaults. Complete the event when the immediate enemies are defeated, driven off, escaped, evaded, or bargained past. If the party turns back down the Causeway instead, let them go and complete the event.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "dhr_vault_guardian": {
      "title": "The Keeper of the Relland Vaults",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Stage the climax of Demon's Head Ruins at the strongroom threshold in the Relland Vaults. The keeper is an old fortress defense still holding its last order: animated armor in Relland ceremonial plate, risen from a collapsed niche, with the house motto 'Where others kneel, we build' carved above the bronze doors. Give it presence before violence: it bars the way and issues the old house challenge, and how the party answers matters. Those who kneel or grovel it treats as thieves begging pardon; those who stand and state their business may earn a measured exchange, a test, or passage on terms. If it comes to battle, fight it as a setpiece: heavy blows, long reach, the terrain of bronze doors, collapsed niches, and family plaques in play, and if the ruin is roused, one wave of lesser defenses or kobold opportunists complicating the fight. Scale it as a hard but winnable fight for the current party. Complete the event when the keeper is destroyed, deactivated, satisfied, or decisively bypassed and the strongroom threshold is won. If the party retreats from the Vaults, the keeper does not pursue beyond them; complete the event only on a decisive outcome, otherwise let it stop when they disengage.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "hc_arrival_gate": {
      "title": "The Gate Arch",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Stage the party's arrival at The High Caves. First the approach and the architecture: the hard climb above the vineyard terraces, the square-cut face in the shaded wall, a gate arch wide enough for a cart with its gates long gone, guardrooms either side, shooting-slots in the stone overhead, and the Crimson Company's cull-camp on the shelf outside with a tally-post that has run out of room twice. Establish who is holding the place this visit and how dug in they are, using the occupiers named by the arrival brief and no others. Then make contact immediate and physical, scaled to the party with at least one enemy per party member: a watch on the outworks, something already in the gate arch, or the occupiers reacting to being found. Let the fight use the arch, the guardrooms, the slots, the loose rock, and the daylight reaching down the first corridor. Before or after the clash, plant one visible promise deeper in: a worked gate, a held junction, a cell being used as a store, or cold air moving up from below. Complete the event when the immediate enemies are beaten, driven back, escaped, or dealt with. If the party turns back down the slope instead, let them go and complete the event."
    },
    "hc_keeper": {
      "title": "Whatever Leads Them",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Stage the climax of The High Caves in the Unmapped, where the builders' plan runs out and cold air moves up from the mountain. The keeper is whatever leads the current occupiers, and it must match the tenant the arrival brief established: the oldest strigoi in the complex, the leader of the Ferals pack, the raiders' captain, or a ghast among the flock. Give it presence before violence, in whatever way fits what it is — a pack leader will speak and bargain and lie, a captain will offer terms and mean some of them, a strigoi will simply be aware of them in a way the others are not, a ghast will announce itself by stench and sound. Fight it as a setpiece using the ground: collapsed levels, unrecorded ways in, drops, dark, and the mountain's own air moving through. Scale it as a hard but winnable fight for the current party. Complete the event when the leader is beaten, destroyed, driven out, or brought to terms and the site is broken for now. If the party retreat from the Unmapped it does not pursue past the Cells; complete the event only on a decisive outcome, otherwise let it stop when they disengage."
    },
    "chm_arrival_gate": {
      "title": "The Black Throat",
      "targetTurns": 2,
      "beats": "Stage the party's arrival at Charnel Mountain. First the Headworks on the mountain's shoulder: gutted winch-houses and ore-sheds stripped and weathered, a collapsed counting-office, prospectors and cullers camped among the ruins arming and trading what they brought out, and the diggings opening below as a black throat in the rock with cold air breathing out of it that smells of the dead. Establish how hard the mountain is pressing this visit using the arrival brief, and let the camp show it — confident and busy, or watchful, or half a siege. Then make contact immediate and physical, scaled to the party with at least one ghoul per party member: something already up among the ruins, something that comes out of the throat at them, or a culler's fight the party walks into. Let the fight use the spoil heaps, the ruined buildings, the open ground, and daylight. Before or after the clash, plant one visible promise below: silver actually showing in a sample, a delver's map, or a story about the seam nobody has been able to work. Complete the event when the immediate ghouls are destroyed or driven off. If the party turns back down the mountain instead, let them go and complete the event."
    },
    "chm_deep_seam": {
      "title": "The Seam Nobody Works",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Stage the climax of Charnel Mountain in the Deep Diggings, and make it an extraction rather than a duel. There is no master here to find and no bargain to strike — that is the mountain's whole truth — so the setpiece is the richest silver in Larion sitting in a gallery whose floor is thick with the bones of the eaten, and the flock that lives on top of it. Give the party the seam first and let them see what it is worth. Then make taking it cost: working silver is loud, the noise carries through worked stone, and the dead come from the side workings and the levels above as well as ahead. Ghasts drive them. Use bad air, failing light, rotten timber, dead-end galleries, and the simple fact that ore is heavy and hands are needed for blades. Scale it hard but winnable for the current party. Complete the event when the party has broken the press down here and holds the seam long enough to work it, or when they decide the load is not worth the climb and disengage. If they withdraw, the flock does not pursue above the Upper Workings."
    },
    "blood_embrace_offer": {
      "title": "The Other Wine",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "Lady Isaveau receives the party at Chateau Velmere herself, as she always does, and this time she pours the other wine — the bloodwine she serves her own kind and does not sell. Anyone who has spent time in this valley knows what being poured a glass of it means. Let that be the signal before a word is said about it.\n\nThen she makes the offer plainly, without selling it. She has watched them work, the Company has written their record down, and she is prepared to give them the Blood. She should be specific about the price rather than romantic: the sun will kill them, the hunger comes back every night and is answered on people, and it is permanent — there is no cure anywhere in Larion and she will say so in exactly those words. She should also be honest that it is a good bargain from where she stands, and that she is not pretending otherwise.\n\nGive the weight underneath it without explaining it. She has not made a vampire in six centuries. She does not say why, and she changes the subject smoothly and completely if pushed. If the party have been up to The High Caves, let them notice she knows exactly what is in the deepest cells there, and let her decline to discuss that too.\n\nThe offer is refusable and she knows it. If they refuse, she takes it well, without punishment, coldness, or retaliation, and says the offer keeps — she has time. Their mark and their standing are untouched. If they accept, do not resolve the turning inside this scene; end on the decision and let what follows be its own beat.\n\nComplete the event once the offer has been made and answered either way, or if the party leave the chateau without answering.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    },
    "blood_rival_assassination": {
      "title": "Someone Objects",
      "targetTurns": 3,
      "beats": "A new vampire with a Crimson Company captaincy is a change in the balance, and the oldest house in Nocturne finds an outsider made lordly to be an obscenity. House Vyrel does not challenge anyone; it removes them, quietly, and denies it afterward.\n\nStage the attempt away from Company ground and away from a lord's protection. It should be competent and specific rather than a random ambush: people who have watched this character's habits and picked the hour, the room, and the way out. Use what a house can afford — silvered and moonstone weapons out of the Crescent House, a mortal who was paid and does not know for whom, one of the house's own who does, and a second plan for when the first fails. Go for the vampire specifically: sunlight let in, silver, fire, or a stake, and if there are mortals in the party expect the assassins to treat them as leverage rather than targets.\n\nMake it survivable and make it cost something. Someone or something the character values should be at risk in the attempt: their ghoul, a Company blade under their command, a servant, or the room they thought was safe.\n\nLeave evidence rather than a confession. A house token, a Crescent House purchase somebody can be made to remember, a contract with a name burned off, or a survivor who can be worked on. Whether the party pursue it, sell it, trade it to a rival house, or take it to their own lord is theirs to decide, and every one of those is a real move in the houses' game.\n\nComplete the event when the attempt is beaten off, escaped, or turned, and the party hold something that points at who sent it. If the party never engage, let the attempt fail on its own terms and complete the event with the evidence still findable.",
      "onCompleteEffects": []
    }
  },
