    "velantami_region_campaign_guidance": {
      "name": "velantami_region_campaign_guidance",
      "recurring": true,
      "conditions": [
        {
          "type": "read-boolean",
          "key": "velantami_start_active",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "party-region",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": "Sanctuary Vale"
        }
      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "## Hidden Campaign Guidance\n\nTreat this as persistent hidden campaign context for the Wildkeeper opening and nearby forest play. Do not assume the player knows the forest's factions, history, or current pressures. Let unfamiliar things become clear through a present actor, creature, action, or consequence.\n\n## Light Glosses\n\nThe Wildkeepers are a fellowship of druids, rangers, and priests based at Velantami who keep the Everwood free and alive rather than owning or taming it. The Eastwood is the war-torn eastern reach of the Everwood, where Bloodsworn and renegade elves keep colliding. The Bloodsworn are human blood-cult war parties from Westmarch who burn groves while hunting renegade elves. The renegade elves raid back and retreat into the forest, so neither side is a clean heroic cause."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "High Wardens are high-elven forest officers who enforce elven law; they are not Wildkeepers and do not share all of the fellowship's priorities. Riddlewood bandits are renegade elves and outsider raiders who prey on forest roads and withdraw into the Riddlewood. Sanctuary contract crews are hired outsiders who enter the forest for protection, hunting, salvage, security, or profit. Blackfire is a Sanctuary mercenary crew: greedy, unscrupulous, and brutal, with quiet Syndicate backing and alchemical dragonfire weapons that spew black alchemical fire."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Syndicate sgherros are armed Syndicate criminals, smugglers, and hired muscle used to move contraband, enforce debts, and make inconvenient people yield. Spinners are intelligent horse-sized spider-folk who communicate through projected images and sensations and trade their strong silk for things they find beautiful or interesting. Unseelie fey are dangerous, specific monster-like fey rather than vague folklore spirits; use forms such as redcaps, briar hags, meenlocks, quicklings, darklings, yeth hounds, blackthorn treants, cinderbark dryads, and black-horn unicorns."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Use concrete entity nouns when relevant: Wildkeepers, Blackfire, Bloodsworn war-parties, renegade elves, Riddlewood bandits, Sanctuary contract crews, High Wardens, Syndicate sgherros, smugglers, and hired blades; griffons, hippogriffs, greater monkey-bats, Spinners, harpies, cockatrices, dire wolves, giant spiders, cinderbark dryads, black-horn unicorns, redcaps, briar hags, meenlocks, quicklings, darklings, yeth hounds, blackthorn treants, and will-o-wisps. Do not reduce a creature to a generic monster, sprite, forest spirit, or magical beast."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "## Campaign Loop\n\nMaintain at most one active forest threat or opportunity. Once the player engages, investigates, negotiates, opposes, accepts, or otherwise chooses to pursue a thread, keep that thread in play until it is resolved, defused, escaped, abandoned, or clearly declined. Do not offer a replacement forest threat or opportunity while the current thread remains unfinished, even if the player takes a short rest or moves nearby.\n\nIf the player follows another authored story, let that story occupy play without injecting a new forest offer. After the current thread ends, allow optional downtime, a quiet social beat, or free exploration when natural. At a meaningful later transition, surface one new forest threat or opportunity if circumstances support it; do not chain offers every turn."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "## Choosing the Next Threat or Opportunity\n\nWhen the campaign is ready for a new thread, read Recent Story and the current state first. Prefer consequences, actors, creatures, places, and disputes already established over a disconnected random encounter. Choose one pressure and one dynamic; do not combine the whole catalog."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Stage one concrete focal entity, one present action, one specific place, one immediate stake, one first foothold, and one desirable larger opportunity. Use visible nouns and verbs such as smoke, ash, torches, arrows, snares, cages, roots, wingbeats, tracks, black flame, burning brush, spreads, circles, stoops, flees, guards, bargains, cuts, pursues, burns, ambushes, retreats, and rescues. Let the first foothold be achievable at the party's scale while pointing toward richer Wildkeeper trust, a creature bond, a dangerous place, a rival crew, unusual equipment, a new ally, or larger forest work. Gloss an unfamiliar faction, creature, or place briefly when it first matters. If no suitable situation is ready, leave the forest quiet, social, or open rather than manufacturing one."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "## Threat and Opportunity Types\n\nA fireline spreads through the Eastwood: smoke, ash, torches, a threatened grove, a returning patrol, or a route cut off by flame. People must hold, flee, rescue, pursue, or choose what the fire consumes.\n\nA specific magical wild creature has been displaced: a griffon or hippogriff driven from its eyrie, a cinderbark dryad defending a burned tree, a black-horn unicorn fleeing a curse, or another established forest creature acting from territory, hunger, injury, young, law, or fear. Let it circle, stoop, flee, guard, snare, root, refuse, bargain, or choose; do not reduce it to a random monster."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "A mercenary or contract crew has overstepped: it has staked a camp, cut living timber, laid snares, caged a creature, claimed a route, burned cover, or pursued a target into ground the Wildkeepers consider free. Give the crew a contract, vulnerable people, or a defensible purpose, but make its trespass real. Blackfire is the named example for this pressure: its Syndicate-backed crew uses alchemical dragonfire projectors that spew black alchemical fire.\n\nTwo concrete problems demand one response: a patrol is pinned by fire while a creature breaks loose, a crew is trapped while raiders approach, or a burning grove competes with an urgent rescue."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "A raid and counter-raid are already moving: Bloodsworn burn, renegade elves ambush, a patrol pursues, and the forest catches the consequences.\n\nUse one dynamic at a time and keep it physically underway through present actors, visible action, and immediate stakes."
        }
      ]
    },
