    "reward_substantial_victory": {
      "name": "reward_substantial_victory",
      "recurring": true,
      "conditions": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "query": "The latest story response newly and decisively resolves a substantial fight or challenge in the party's favor. The party defeated, drove off, captured, outwitted, cleansed, sealed, rescued through, or otherwise overcame meaningful opposition or danger. This is the new moment of victory or resolution, not a later mention of an earlier success, a minor routine action, an unfinished fight, a temporary pause, or merely escaping without overcoming the challenge."
        }
      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Surface a concrete reward or reward opportunity for the substantial victory that just occurred. Do not make defeated enemies inexplicably empty-handed, make nearby containers worthless, or end with praise alone. Ground the reward in the defeated threat, current location, region, and established lore. Humanoids may leave weapons, armor, tools, supplies, copper florins, documents, keys, captives, or stolen goods. Monsters may yield established hides, scales, glands, venom, bone, teeth, organs, aspected matter, or crafting materials. Ruins may reveal a cache, working relic, sealed room, valuables, or appropriate equipment. Rescues and noncombat victories may earn payment, information, access, standing, hospitality, training, an introduction, or a favor. Environmental victories may expose a resource, safe route, hidden place, or shortcut. Prefer materials named in the location lore."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Make the reward proportionate and useful. Prefer something that fits at least one current player character's equipment, skills, class, crafting, or progression when the fiction supports it; otherwise give something clearly valuable or saleable. Ordinary victories give modest goods, materials, or florins. Serious victories may give Fine or occasionally Superior items, valuable materials, or meaningful access. Masterwork rewards require major accomplishments. Never create a Legendary item unless established story and lore clearly justify it. Surface the reward where players can inspect, harvest, accept, recover, or take it. Do not put anything into inventory before the story says a player takes or receives it."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "NAME every physical reward specifically in the narrative. Do not say only 'a sword,' 'a better weapon,' 'some armor,' 'a useful item,' 'some materials,' 'a little coin,' or 'some loot.' Give enough detail for the item system: exact item type, material, quality when above mundane, and magical or damage aspect when nonstandard. Follow the world's naming logic naturally: quality, exotic material, item type, and 'of [damage type]' where applicable. Identify an ordinary steel longsword as such. Identify an aspected reward with a complete name such as a Superior Emberglass Dagger of Fire rather than 'a glowing dagger.' Name harvested monster materials precisely and state a concrete florin amount instead of 'a few coins.' The prose need not print mechanical fields, but it must support a strong item definition."
        }
      ]
    },
