    "shadow_summits_bestiary_context": {
      "name": "shadow_summits_bestiary_context",
      "recurring": true,
      "conditions": [
        {
          "type": "party-region",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": "Shadow Summits"
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "query": "The current scene involves or approaches danger from creatures: the party is fighting, hunting, tracking, fleeing, or guarding against a monster or beast; the story has mentioned creature signs such as tracks, kills, remains, cries, or warnings; characters are seriously discussing dangerous creatures nearby or a contract, hunt, or bounty on one; or the party is moving through wild, ruined, underground, or abandoned ground where hostile creatures could plausibly appear. This is NOT satisfied when the party is safely socializing, trading, resting, or celebrating with no creature threat present or discussed."
        }
      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Bestiary guidance for the Shadow Summits (narrator-only). Apply ONLY where the scene calls for creatures; never force one into a calm scene. Use the region’s own, never generic monsters: strigoi, the vampire-dead gone mindless, denning in high caves and lost valleys, sluggish by day, fierce after dark; ghouls and ghasts overflowing Charnel Mountain’s diggings in flocks; the Ferals’ werewolves and beast-shifters running the high wilds as organized packs; gaunt fiends, maw fiends, and named ravener fiends where the barrier runs thin; gargoyles on old keeps and estates. Living danger is the Crimson Company, house agents, and Syndicate men from Villa Parhelion. Silver and moonstone are the answer, moved as contraband by the Crescent House and Parhelion. Show sign before the creature. In the lit valleys these are rumor, trophy, contract, or raid talk, not street encounters. Vampire lords are people and politics, never random encounters. If nothing fits, use cold, altitude, or human danger."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "More of the Shadow Summits' own, to be used as readily as strigoi and ghouls. WIGHTS: the Schism-era dead in the old prisons, sealed cells, and dead houses — preserved, armed, still holding rank, and commanding the lesser dead around them. REVENANTS: someone a lord or a house had killed, back for exactly one name and stopping at nothing until it is paid; they talk, and can be reasoned with about everything except the one thing. SPECTERS: bodiless cold where the barrier runs thin, in old killing-grounds and sealed places, and ordinary steel passes through them. DIRE WOLVES: the living animal of this country, running the passes in winter and taking stock off the high terraces — sometimes the answer to a killing is just a wolf. Do not let strigoi and ghouls stand in for all of these: a den, a barrow, a haunting, and a wolf pack are four different jobs."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "FRENZY is available on any living beast of the Summits and is not a species of its own. A frenzied wolf, bear, boar, or dire wolf behaves like a rabid animal whose hunger is insatiable: it eats past full and goes on eating, it has no fear left, and it has stopped being able to die properly — wounds that should drop it do not, and it comes apart before it stops. Silver and moonstone give no edge, because it is alive. The corpse must be burned, or whatever scavenges it goes frenzied in turn. Use frenzy to make an ordinary report of an animal turn out to be something far worse, and let a party that killed the thing and walked off without burning it learn what that costs."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Three more of the region's own. BLOODFED DIREHOUNDS: the hunting packs of the vampire houses, fed on their masters' blood, running a mortal down over days and taking wounds that should stop them. A house's own hounds are its property and killing them is an act against that house; most packs met in open country have gone feral and belong to nobody. BONEBREAKER VULTURES: enormous carrion birds that carry bones aloft and drop them on rock to crack them — where they circle there is a body, and where they circle over open ground and nothing can be seen, something has been hidden. PALE LYNX: a chalk-white ambusher of the sunless slopes with skin grown over its eyes, which hunts by warmth and therefore cannot perceive the undead at all — lethal to the living, and a vampire is no more present to it than a stone."
        }
      ]
    },
