    "blood_hall_contracts": {
      "name": "blood_hall_contracts",
      "recurring": true,
      "conditions": [
        {
          "type": "read-boolean",
          "key": "inCompanySpace",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": true
        },
        {
          "type": "action",
          "query": "The party reads, examines, or asks about the Crimson Company work board, the posted contracts, or the available jobs; asks an officer such as Osric Kestrand for work, a job, a contract, a bounty, or something that pays; or asks what needs doing, where to go next, or what the Company wants done in the Shadow Summits."
        }
      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Crimson Company halls are soldiers’ houses kept far better than soldiers’ houses need to be: one long table, good wine, trophies on the wall, a work board by the door, and a warded room holding what the Company has taken and will not destroy. Officers judge work by hearing the account and paying on it. Keep work grounded in the Shadow Summits: name the trouble, the exact valley or site, the stakes, what counts as done, and the pay in florins. Draw on existing locations and on the region’s own named trouble, the Ferals’ packs and Syndicate business out of Villa Parhelion among them. Keep the hall alive with blades between jobs, wounded coming back, kit argued over, and Company talk. Do not force work on the party because they walked in; surface it when they ask, read the board, or speak to an officer. The League has no standing here and no work to give."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Vary the board; the Company is not only a monster-culling service. THE LORDLESS — a dead lord's valley has no protection and no law, and people out of three domains have drifted into it and are taking the pass road; a household turned out when their lord was destroyed, now hitting the tincture wagons; blades who took the coat and the kit and ran. MISSING, WITH A CLOCK — a watchpost above the vineyards stopped answering; a pressing crew of ten went up the high terraces and has not come down; a valley has sent no word since the snow closed the pass. TAKEN — the tincture wagon left for Nocturne and never arrived; a case of bloodwine is being sold where bloodwine cannot be sold; somebody is offering to sell where a lord sleeps by day. AFTER A VAMPIRE — a lord made someone six weeks ago who cannot control it; a destroyed lord's ghouls are still up there and leaderless; an old Company blade turned hunter and knows exactly how it is done."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Keep every posting inside the Shadow Summits. The Company has no standing elsewhere and does not send blades out of the region: never offer a trade run, a caravan escort, a delivery, or any job that begins with leaving the Summits. Do not offer passive attendance, ceremonial witnessing, routine escort duty, missing livestock, or a person who merely wandered off. Of any three postings at most one is a straightforward cull, and when it is a cull, vary the quarry beyond strigoi and ghouls. Every posting names a place, an opposition that can be confronted or outmanoeuvred, what counts as done, and the pay in florins. Much of this work is a search, a chase, or a negotiation rather than a fight, and those postings carry the same weight as the fights. When a beast problem is worth a contract, name the individual and give it followers — the thing that has taken forty people out of one valley and the pack that runs with it — rather than posting a lone animal."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "When a posting is a creature problem, choose its shape independently of the quarry and do not default to depredation. BLOCKADE: nobody has died, and nothing can get up the road. WRONG PLACE: the creature is a symptom, something drove it here, and the real job is upstream. SPREAD: there are more every week and the job is the source. IT HAS SOMETHING: it took a body, a strongbox, or a person who may still be alive. FED: the village says there is no problem, and there is a problem. NOT WHAT YOU WERE TOLD: the report says wolves and it was not wolves. ALREADY DEAD: something killed the wolves. GET IT OUT FIRST: killing it where it stands ruins the vineyard, the cistern, or the ledge above the village. IT IS SOMEONE'S: it wears a collar, and killing it is a political act. A RACE: another crew is working the same contract. ONLY AT AN HOUR: you must be there when it happens."
        }
      ]
    },
    "blood_rank_work_scope": {
      "name": "blood_rank_work_scope",
