      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "The player is searching, looting, or harvesting a corpse. First use whatever this location's own guidance says may be found here, and where it gives exact item names, use those names before inventing anything. Many places say nothing, and that is fine: then ground what is found in the specific source rather than in a general idea of loot. Who this person was and what their work required. What this room, pack, or container is actually for. What this creature's body yields, using the parts the region's own bestiary names. A courier carries what a courier carries; a ghoul yields ghoul parts. Do not fall back on generic filler such as a rusty blade, a few coins, and some rations when the source implies something specific."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "Scale what is found to the source, not to the party. Ordinary opposition yields ordinary goods, materials, and modest florins. Someone equipped for a reason yields what they were equipped with. A cache hidden on purpose is worth the hiding. Fine and Superior items come only from a source that justifies them, Masterwork almost never, and Legendary not at all unless established lore says so. A place also gives up its contents once: a body already stripped, a room already turned over, or somewhere with no reason to hold anything yields nothing, and the narration should say so plainly rather than invent a consolation prize. Refusing the fourth search of the same room is not stinginess, it is the world holding still."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "NAME everything found specifically, because the item system can only create what the prose names. Never write 'a sword', 'some armour', 'a useful item', 'some materials', 'a little coin', or 'some loot'. Give the exact item type, the material, the quality when it is above mundane, and the magical or damage aspect when it is nonstandard, following the world's naming logic: quality, exotic material, item type, and 'of [damage type]' where it applies. An ordinary steel longsword is named as such. An aspected find is a Superior Emberglass Dagger of Fire, never 'a glowing dagger'. Name harvested creature parts precisely, and state a concrete florin amount instead of 'a few coins'."
        },
        {
          "type": "story",
          "instruction": "What an enemy fought with is the first thing on the ground: if the prose gave them a crossbow, mail, a good blade, or a shield while they were alive, those exist after they are dead and are the obvious find — the crossbow before the spare string, the mail before the belt knife. Never describe gear on a living enemy and then withhold it from the body. Narrative props are not rewards: a map, letter, key, journal, or ledger appears only when it opens something the party can act on now, never as the main thing found, and never instead of something usable. And do not itemize trivia — whetstones, spare bowstrings, rations, rope, and ordinary everyday tools are texture in a sentence, not items handed to a player."
        }
      ]
    },
