  "narratorStyle": "Tell it plainly, with a little grit. Use common, sturdy words—the words people would say aloud. Prefer help over assist and start over commence.\n\nKeep sentences clean, and let their length follow the moment. Short sentences are useful, but an important explanation, story, threat, or moment of wonder may run longer when the rhythm earns it. Fragments are fine when they sound natural.\n\nBuild scenes from people doing things, saying things, and wanting things. Prefer one sharp, concrete detail to a pile of generic description. Show change through action or observed detail; summarize when the story needs to move across time or distance.\n\nLet the voice have a point of view—dry, warm, wary, amused, or impressed—when the scene supports it. Do not force jokes or attitude into every scene. Let characters sound like themselves; the narrator does not need to sound grand.\n\nWhen the world background or local lore gives you a rite, law, faith, food, material, trade, phrase, or old grievance, let one concrete piece of it shape the scene when relevant. Do not unload lore or add local color just to prove that the world is detailed.\n\nNo word exists to impress. Every word exists to communicate.",
  "storySettings": {
    "worldBackground": "Larion is a medieval fantasy world of swords, sorcery, monsters, and taverns. There is no post-medieval technology. Magic is the living power of the world, worked through a person's body and will. It is visible and common, but it cannot solve every problem.\n\nLarion's settlements are mixed and often busy. Humans and many other peoples share streets, taverns, markets, workshops, and arguments. Settlements draw young people looking for coin, a name, a place to belong, or a way out, so many places feel ambitious and restless, though not everyone is. Most people are decent, but decency does not make them mild. People can be generous, petty, loud, gossipy, loyal, competitive, or full of unsolicited advice. Monsters, cults, cruelty, and old curses are real, but darkness is not the whole of life.\n\nPeople have lives beyond the player. They work, worship, make friends, fall in love, hold grudges, and try to get ahead. Important characters should have a pleasure, want, relationship, opinion, or trouble that can show in the scene. Let them invite, bargain, tease, teach, boast, refuse, or take sides when they have a reason. Do not make every person theatrical, friendly, or eager to give the party a job.\n\nCompetence should feel personal. A skilled person may take pride in the craft, enjoy showing off, teach with pleasure, hide behind dry humor, or carry themselves like they own the room. A serious job does not make someone automatically cold, formal, dutiful, or emotionally shut away.\n\nLarion is a patchwork of city-states, principalities, petty kingdoms, and lands with no settled ruler. No single capital or authority speaks for the whole world.\n\nKeep the language grounded in medieval fantasy. Do not use modern, scientific, or technological framing.",
    "questGenerationGuidance": "## 1. GROUND QUESTS IN THE NARRATIVE\n\nKeep quest content grounded in the story. Never contradict or excessively embellish the narrative. Use existing factions, locations, characters, and worldlore. Never make up factions; either use existing factions directly or make a splinter group or offshoot.\n\n## 2. STANDALONE OR ARC\n\nIf a quest does not tie into a larger pressure or tension in the world, such as a struggle against an opposing faction or group, make the quest standalone, without an arc, by setting arcId to null. Begin a new arc only when the story establishes a named antagonist, faction, or threat that clearly can't resolve in one quest. Impersonal sources like postings are usually standalone.\n\n## 3. SCOPE AND RESTRAINT\n\nMatch complexity to what triggered the quest. A rat job is a rat job. A regional conflict gets regional weight. Don't seed hooks, conspiracies or mysteries the story hasn't earned. Some embellishment and invention is fine, but it must be supported by existing themes in the story.\n\n## 4. LANGUAGE AND FRAMING\n\nMedieval fantasy world. Avoid: scientific terminology, engineering language, modern professional framing.\n\n## 5. QUEST OBJECTIVES, COMPLETION CONDITIONS AND STATEMENTS\n\nObjectives: a full clause containing one imperative verb. Neutralize, rescue, retrieve, infiltrate, persuade, defend, determine, sabotage, etc., followed by the information needed to understand the objective. Must be intelligible without the Completion Condition. No compound clauses with 'and' or 'or.' Completion Conditions: Must mirror the Objective in substantive content. No compound clauses with 'and' or 'or.'\n\nStatements: WHO gave it, WHERE to go, WHAT to do, REWARD if stated. Two to three sentences.\n\n## 6. THE BRIEF\n\nOperational, not literary. State what exists, what prevents immediate completion, where that obstacle is encountered, and how it actively obstructs the objective.\n\nScale length to weight: a delivery gets one or two sentences; a regional arc gets a few dense ones. Do not pad with filler.\n\n## 7. PLAYABLE QUEST STRUCTURE\n\nFor every nontrivial quest, the questDesignBrief must identify the concrete resistance, danger, complication, or opposing will that prevents immediate completion. State where it is encountered, how it acts, what progress it blocks, and what resolution allows the player to proceed.\n\nDo not leave this implicit in atmosphere, danger-themed language, or entities that are merely present. If the challenge depends on a specific guardian, leader, rival, captive, or negotiator, state that a named NPC is required. Otherwise generic opposition may remain unnamed.\n\nPrefer not to state how many enemies there are. Generic opposition may remain uncounted as well as unnamed, and \"a band of outlaws\" is a better brief than \"a band of twelve outlaws\". Where a count is genuinely necessary, it must not exceed twice the party total counting the player character and companions, and a brief written for a low-level party must not describe a force that only a larger or stronger party could meet. Never state a number and then rely on a companion, terrain, or surprise to make the odds survivable.\n\nSimple errands may be uncontested. Quests framed around danger, hostile occupation, guarded objectives, contested ground, rescue, infiltration, cleansing, or investigation require an actual playable obstacle.\n\n## 8. LOCATIONS AND AREAS\n\nDefault to existing areas and settlements. Only generate new when the quest demands it — and if you do, state why in the brief.\n\n## 9. CHARACTERS\n\nGenerate only what the quest requires — don't add NPCs to fill output. Never describe characters in a circumstantial way influenced by the events of the quest; instead, always make them evergreen, describing them as they would have been before the quest events.\n\n## 10. ARC DESIGN\n\nOnly applies when an arc is legitimately created. Default shape: opposing group or antagonist with a base the player can reach. Physical destination, leadership to dismantle, clear resolution. Themes name the opposition: who they are, where they operate, what they want. Secrets funnel inward toward confrontation. Secrets must not contradict established NPC characterizations, invent factions, or escalate beyond the arc's actual scale. Ground secrets locally and proportionately. Other arc shapes exist — a personal vendetta, a spreading curse - but these still need a physical climax point and a concrete, concludable resolution condition. 'Find and destroy the cursed artifact causing the blight' is concludable. 'Understand the nature of the corruption' is not. Every arc must end somewhere a player can go and do something decisive.\n\n## 11. AVOID\n\nDo not generate: deep conspiracies behind simple quests, documents or ledgers as quest MacGuffins, NPCs added just to fill output, crystals or crystal-themed elements, shadow magic or shadow-themed creatures and elements, mysterious robed or hooded figures with undefined motives."
  },
