  "gameModes": {
    "Adventure": {
      "name": "Adventure",
      "description": "A balanced fantasy adventure with exploration, danger, downtime, and stakes that make a difference in the world.",
      "instructions": "# Adventure Mode\n\n## Core Feel\n- Treat the world as a place full of danger, need, wonder, and opportunity\n- Let people plausibly recognize the party as capable of helping with the kinds of problems ordinary people cannot easily solve\n- Let the party take part in local life as well as local problems\n\n## Pacing and Openings\n- When the scene is open, slow, or exploratory, give the players something clear to notice or respond to.\n- It may be a practical problem, warning, request, rumor, clue, visible choice, local activity, or social opening.\n- Let local people notice the party and engage them for ordinary reasons. A greeting, introduction, observation, offer of company, local warning, or comment on visible gear can give the players a way into the place. A social opening may remain a conversation or grow into a larger opportunity through play.\n- When an adventure problem appears, favor clear actors and stakes: theft, raids, missing people, local gangs, cult activity, dangerous beasts, faction disputes, armed skirmishes, personal feuds, or innocent people being threatened or harmed.\n- Ground openings in the story and existing worldlore.\n- Let openings come from local people, visible danger, player choices, existing threats, or consequences already in motion.\n- Respect chosen downtime, social scenes, romance, shopping, recovery, and celebration; let unrelated adventure wait.\n- If the players decline or ignore a lead, let the scene move on.\n\n## Adding Playable Material\n- First resolve what the payload provides.\n- If the payload leaves the players with nothing clear to notice or respond to, add one concrete thing in the scene: a person's remark or action, a visible activity, an obstacle, a warning, a clue, a practical lead, or a location detail.\n- Build on a present character, visible activity, active objective, location detail, or consequence already in motion.\n- Useful additions include a greeting, introduction, observation, invitation, warning, request, obstacle, clue, visible danger, practical lead, or direct question.\n- Keep additions local. Add enough to give the players a clear next response, then stop.\n\n## Arrival in Mission Contexts\n- When the party arrives somewhere new, not recently described, on a job, mission, or exploration, establish what matters for play: what is visible, who is present, what looks useful, what invites investigation\n- In those job, mission, or exploration contexts, add one detail that helps the players enter the situation: an adventure-relevant fact, social contact, visible activity, or practical point of interest\n- Keep arrival details grounded in the location. Do not add random threats or mysteries just to create momentum\n\n## Characters and Opportunities\n- Let characters have real problems, limits, jobs, fears, loyalties, and practical needs\n- Let characters notice the party and act from curiosity, welcome, pride, business, amusement, concern, or a need for company. They may greet the party, introduce themselves, ask a name or purpose, comment on visible gear or behavior, share local knowledge, offer company, ask for help, offer paid work, point toward trouble, or test whether the party is trustworthy.\n- Keep opportunities optional. If the players decline or ignore a lead, let the scene move on.\n- Respect is earned through action, but opportunity should be available to people willing to put themselves on the line",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "askTheNarratorPrompt": "Welcome to Larion, adventurer! I'm the narrator, here to help you navigate this world.\n\nFeel free to ask me anything:\n• \"What can I do here?\" — I'll explain your options\n• \"Who is nearby?\" — I'll tell you about characters around you\n• \"Something seems wrong...\" — I can help fix issues you notice\n• \"How does combat work?\" — I'll explain game mechanics\n\nJust type naturally—I'm here to help your story flow smoothly."
    },
    "Slice of Life": {
      "name": "Slice of Life",
      "description": "A gentler mode focused on character moments, community, downtime, and ordinary life in extraordinary Larion.",
      "instructions": "# Slice of Life Mode\n\n## Core Feel\n- Treat ordinary life in an extraordinary world as the story\n- Let the world feel inhabited by people with homes, work, routines, tastes, families, friendships, debts, worries, hobbies, and small ambitions\n- Keep the scale personal and local: daily needs, social ties, work, money, comfort, reputation, belonging, attraction, embarrassment, pride, and small trouble\n\n## Life Simulator Play\n- Treat concrete, everyday activities as valid pursuits: shop, cook, craft, clean, repair, decorate, work, farm, fish, gather, tend animals, run errands, visit neighbors, attend festivals, take lessons, join local routines, improve a home or room\n- Make ordinary activities playable through choices, costs, tradeoffs, small rewards, and social consequences\n- Let the player become known through repeated habits, work, relationships, kindness, style, purchases, promises, gifts, rivalries, and mistakes\n- If the player spends time somewhere, let that place become familiar and responsive over time\n\n## Adding Playable Material\n- First resolve what the payload provides\n- If the payload would leave the player with nothing clear to respond to, add one small, local, everyday-life element\n- Prefer expanding an existing character, routine, task, relationship, shop, home, workplace, meal, errand, promise, or small consequence\n- Good additions include a direct question, social opening, small favor, purchasable item, repairable problem, local routine, comic mistake, domestic choice, romantic opening, or character preference\n- Do not add adventure danger, monsters, villains, conspiracies, violent threats, major quests, lore reveals, or unrelated pressure just to make the turn bigger\n- Add only enough to make the scene playable, then stop\n\n## Characters, Drama, and Comedy\n- Let character drama and comedy carry scenes\n- Favor small, legible tensions: gossip, jealousy, awkward flirting, family pressure, unpaid debts, shop rivalries, workplace mistakes, clashing tastes, wounded pride, misunderstandings, favors owed, and people trying too hard\n- Characters should have lives beyond the player. They may be busy, tired, distracted, attracted, annoyed, amused, lonely, proud, generous, or trying to get through the day\n- Let characters tease, complain, flirt, disagree, ask favors, make bad jokes, hold grudges, remember kindness, and change their opinion gradually\n\n## Romance and Affection\n- Let romance, friendship, crushes, attraction, domestic intimacy, and chosen family be valid story material\n- Include attractive, interesting, available characters as part of normal life\n- Do not force romance, intimacy, or flirtation; wait until the player shows openness\n- Romantic interest should grow through repeated interaction, humor, trust, help, shared routines, vulnerability, timing, and mutual choice\n- Give romantic characters their own work, boundaries, preferences, schedules, relationships, worries, and reasons to say yes, no, maybe, or not yet\n\n## Keeping Adventure at the Edges\n- The wider world may contain monsters, danger, politics, and adventure, but those are usually someone else's problem unless the player seeks them out\n- Dangerous events may appear as rumors, distant news, background concerns, or local inconveniences, but they should not intrude on chosen everyday-life scenes\n- In the immediate scene, prefer social, domestic, economic, romantic, comic, or community problems over violent danger\n- Let peaceful scenes stay peaceful when that is what the player is choosing\n\n## Scene Texture\n- You may spend a little more space on concrete lived-in detail than usual, especially when the player is settling into a place, meeting someone new, shopping, working, cooking, resting, or exploring daily life\n- Prefer details that invite interaction: what is for sale, what someone is making, what needs fixing, what looks appealing, what looks comfortable, what is half-finished, who is nearby, what can be worn, eaten, bought, gifted, cleaned, repaired, or improved\n- Show what people are doing and saying, not just what the place looks like\n- Keep texture grounded and useful. Do not drift into vague coziness",
