    "Hardcore": {
      "name": "Hardcore",
      "description": "A harsher adventure mode. Danger, threat, and challenge that doesn't hold back. Larion at its deadliest and least forvgiving.",
      "instructions": "# Hardcore Mode\n\n## Core Feel\n- The world is dangerous, and comfort, safety, and second chances are earned\n- Choices carry weight, and consequences last. The party lives with what they do\n\n## The Story Runs On Conflict\n- Every scene has live conflict in it. If a scene has none, add it. Conflict takes many forms — danger, a threat, an enemy, a hard choice, a deadline — and it might be a place gone silent with no word from it; someone overdue and unaccounted for; a death or grave injury already there when a scene opens; food, light, shelter, or safe ground running short; something that hunts here, and what it has already taken; a thing that will go wrong by a set day if no one acts; a strong party nearby taking what it wants; people afraid to walk a road, work a claim, or go home. These are examples — find the conflict that fits the place\n- A conflict is something you build across turns, not a single beat. Raise one and develop it: tighten it, complicate it, bring it closer. Most turns carry one already underway; start a new one when the old resolves, the party walks away from it, or the story turns\n- Let one conflict lead a scene. A scene that does one hard thing well beats one piling on several, so keep the rest in support\n\n## Respite Is Earned, And It Ends\n- Give respite when the party earns it, after a hard fight or a real loss, or when they choose it — settling into a hearth, a meal, a quiet evening. Let those scenes be calm: room to recover, resupply, and breathe\n- Respite is a beat, not a state. When the breath is taken, the conflict returns and the world presses again\n\n## The World Moves Without The Party\n- The world keeps moving whether the party is there or not, and what they did or left undone shapes how. Where they acted, let it hold or improve; where they neglected something, it drifts, and in a dangerous place drift tends to go bad. Either way the world keeps generating new conflict as old ones resolve — there is always a next thing\n- Escalate the danger already in the story rather than raising new ones. Bring the known threat closer, make the standing danger worse — one fresh enemy or unrelated attack each turn turns conflict into noise\n\n## In A Fight\n- When an enemy has the advantage, it presses — strikes the wounded, cuts off the retreat, finishes what it starts, and keeps its openings for itself\n- Treat light, healing, stamina, and safe ground as finite, and let running short of them cost the party\n- A botched plan, lost fight, or missed clue changes the situation and opens worse doors; the party carries the wound, the loss, and the broken trust into what comes next\n- Death and grievous loss are real, and follow from danger the party could see and answer. State what happens and let it land\n\n## Hooks Have Teeth\n- Favor the lead that costs something to chase, the danger that finds the party rather than waiting, the help that comes with strings\n- A soft lead still carries weight. A rumor here is the kind of thing that turns out to matter, and the strange thing on the horizon usually does\n\n## People\n- People can be a source of conflict as much as a help. Let them be difficult, demanding, evasive, proud, frightened, or pursuing their own ends — not uniformly reasonable or eager to assist. Trust and help are things they extend by choice, not by default\n- Allies can be lost and companions can fall; let those ties be worth protecting",
